[{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q1","surface":"track","en":"How does the Java Memory Model (JMM) work: happens-before, volatile, and visibility?","bodyEn":"Scope label Java SE/JLS guarantee: happens-before, volatile, and final-field rules are portable language/runtime contracts. HotSpot/OpenJDK implementation detail: barrier instructions and optimization strategies are implementation choices; verify them on the target vendor/build. The Java Memory Model (JMM) defines which values a read is allowed to observe and which ordering guarantees exist between actions in different threads. Without a happens-before path, conflicting accesses form a data race; “it worked in a test” does not make their result portable. Important happens-before edges include: program order within one thread monitor unlock → a subsequent lock of the same monitor write to a volatile field → every subsequent read of that field in the synchronization order actions before Thread.start() → actions in the started thread all actions in a thread → another thread successfully detects its termination through join() Happens-before is transitive. It provides visibility and ordering for the actions before/after those edges; it is more precise than saying “flush the CPU cache.” class Worker { private int data; private volatile boolean stop; void shutdown () { data = 42 ; stop = true ; // release/publication edge } void run () { while (!stop) Thread. onSpinWait (); System.out. println (data); // 42 after observing stop == true } } The volatile read that observes the published write also observes data = 42, which precedes that write. “Latest value” means a value permitted by the volatile synchronization order—not an unsynchronized wall-clock promise while writes race. Removing volatile permits the loop to keep reading an old value or be optimized in ways that never observe shutdown. Visibility is not compound atomicity volatile int count; count++ is still a read, calculation and write that can lose updates. Use AtomicInteger/CAS, a lock, or ownership that removes the race. Volatile is appropriate for independent state such as a cancellation/config reference when all invariants fit one atomic read/write. JITs map JMM guarantees to architecture-specific barriers and instructions; portable code should not assert one fixed “store-store/store-load” recipe for every JVM/CPU. Use VarHandle acquire/release/opaque modes only when their weaker semantics are deliberately proven and benchmarked. Final fields receive special initialization safety when construction completes without this escaping. That protects the initialized final state; it does not make later mutation of reachable objects thread-safe or safely publish arbitrary non-final state. Keep atomicity, visibility and ordering separate. CAS/ABA details are in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q3).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q1"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q2","surface":"track","en":"Heap vs stack, escape analysis, and TLAB — why do they matter for performance?","bodyEn":"Stack: one stack per thread, holding frames (local variables, references). Heap: shared objects, managed by the GC. Escape analysis (JIT): if an object never escapes its method/thread, the JVM can: eliminate the allocation through scalar replacement; HotSpot C2 does not replace it with an ordinary stack allocation or drop its lock through lock elision → reduces GC pressure. TLAB (Thread-Local Allocation Buffer): each thread gets its own private allocation buffer inside Eden for fast, contention-free allocation. Takeaway most objects \"die young\" (the weak generational hypothesis) — that's why the GC splits generations and allocation in Eden is extremely cheap. Escape analysis cannot eliminate the allocation when an object: gets stored into another object's field gets returned to the caller is passed into a method that isn't inlined → it then still goes to the heap. The -XX:+PrintEscapeAnalysis and -XX:+EliminateAllocations switches are HotSpot build/product details, not Java SE diagnostics. Verify their availability on the target build. To determine whether an optimization happened, prefer JFR allocation events, compiler logs, and a controlled benchmark. HotSpot/OpenJDK implementation detail: allocating in Eden often uses a bump-the-pointer fast path; TLABs let a thread allocate locally before it needs a shared slow path. TLAB layout, refill behavior, and collector interaction can differ by build. Verify them with runtime evidence rather than treating them as a Java SE guarantee. Real-world consequence: hot loops that create garbage objects (autoboxed Integer, iterators, lambda captures) push the allocation rate up → more frequent minor GCs. measure allocation rate (MB/s) in the GC log reduce it by reusing buffers, using primitives, and avoiding boxing on the hot path","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q2"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q3","surface":"track","en":"Comparing GCs: Serial, Parallel, G1, ZGC, Shenandoah — when do you pick which?","bodyEn":"Choose a collector from the workload's SLO, allocation profile, heap size, and CPU budget—not from a universal ranking. Record Why JDK/vendor/build; heap/live set; allocation rate runtime and GC workload CPU quota; memory limit container headroom target p99/p999; method warm-up, steady-state window, traffic shape, repetitions Collector Optimizes for Typical fit Main trade-off Serial simplicity and small footprint small heaps, single-core utilities stop-the-world collection Parallel throughput batch and offline jobs longer stop-the-world pauses G1 (default) balanced throughput and pause goals the starting point for most services pause target is a goal, not a guarantee ZGC very low pause time large heaps and tail-latency-sensitive services concurrent-work CPU and memory overhead Shenandoah very low pause time latency-sensitive deployments whose JDK vendor ships it concurrent-work overhead and vendor availability Start with the JDK default, record allocation rate, live-set size, CPU, pause distribution, and end-to-end p99/p99.9, then load-test a candidate with production-shaped traffic. Do not compare only maximum GC pause: a collector can shorten pauses while consuming CPU that increases application latency. The mechanisms matter: G1 divides the heap into regions and selects collection sets to pursue -XX:MaxGCPauseMillis. Humongous allocations, remembered-set work, and evacuation failure are useful investigation signals. ZGC uses colored pointers plus load barriers so most marking and relocation work runs concurrently. Shenandoah uses forwarding/indirection pointers and barriers for concurrent evacuation; it does not use ZGC's colored-pointer design. Neither “sub-millisecond” pauses nor throughput cost is a contractual constant across heaps, JDK builds, hardware, and workloads. Report a result as an example with provenance: workload, JDK/vendor, collector flags, heap, hardware, run duration, and latency/CPU before and after.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q3"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q4","surface":"track","en":"GC tuning: generational behavior, collector-specific events, and signs of a memory leak?","bodyEn":"Terminology depends on the collector. A generational collector may report young, mixed, concurrent, remark, relocation, or full-collection events. “Minor” and “major” are legacy shorthand, not a universal mapping. Use the names emitted by the target collector. Reserve Full GC for an event the collector or log defines that way. Generational heap: Young (Eden + 2 Survivor spaces) and Old. Young-collection events reclaim young regions; frequency and pause depend on the collector in a generational collector, survivors may be promoted to old regions a collector-defined Full GC can reclaim broadly and pause for a long time; do not infer this from “major” alone Signs of a memory leak: repeated collector-defined full-collection events where old-region occupancy does not shrink heap occupancy climbs steadily until OOM Common causes: a static collection holding references forever, a ThreadLocal never removed, unbounded listeners/caches, a ClassLoader leak (redeploy). Tools: heap dump + MAT, GC logs, jcmd. Record Why JDK/vendor/build; heap/live set; allocation rate runtime and GC workload CPU quota; memory limit container headroom target p99/p999; method warm-up, steady-state window, traffic shape, repetitions Fintech trap an unbounded cache is the most common source of OOM in high-traffic services — always set a max size + eviction policy. Leak investigation workflow: enable -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError + GC logging open the heap dump in Eclipse MAT → check the dominator tree + \"Leak Suspects\" trace the GC root holding the reference Four classic culprits: unbounded cache (no max size/eviction) ThreadLocal not remove()d in a thread pool (object lives as long as the thread does) listener/callback never unregistered ClassLoader leak on hot redeploy (Metaspace bloats) Distinguish a leak (old-region occupancy keeps rising across collector-defined full-collection events) from under-provisioning (frequent collection but reclamation still works). Citing allocation rate + promotion rate is a strong signal.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q4"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q5","surface":"track","en":"What are JIT compilation, the interpreter, C1/C2, tiered compilation, and warmup?","bodyEn":"The JVM starts by interpreting bytecode, while also counting how \"hot\" each method is. Hot methods get compiled to machine code by the JIT: C1: fast compile, light optimization C2: slower compile, deep optimization (inlining, escape analysis, loop unrolling) Tiered compilation combines both Warmup: a JIT-based service changes performance as profiling and compilation progress. a steady-state benchmark must control and report warmup; a startup/cold-path benchmark intentionally measures it Native Image removes JIT warmup and often improves startup/footprint, while peak throughput and latency depend on workload, build options and profile-guided optimization—measure both deployment modes Tiered compilation has 5 levels: 0 interpreter → 1/2/3 are C1 (different profiling levels) → 4 is C2. Two mechanisms rarely mentioned: OSR (On-Stack Replacement): compiling mid-flight while a long-running loop is already executing deoptimization: when a speculative assumption (type profiling, a never-taken branch) is invalidated → falls back to the interpreter then recompiles (uncommon trap) A full code cache (-XX:ReservedCodeCacheSize) makes the JIT stop compiling → silent performance degradation. A megamorphic call site (many receiver types) can't be inlined → slower. Use JFR, compilation logs or jcmd evidence to inspect compilation; match the benchmark lifecycle to the production question instead of warming every benchmark by rule.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q5"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q6","surface":"track","en":"JVM memory areas versus HotSpot process memory: why can RSS exceed -Xmx?","bodyEn":"The JVM specification describes logical run-time areas; the operating system measures an implementation process. They answer different questions. -Xmx caps the Java heap, not every byte reserved, committed or resident by HotSpot and its native libraries. Record Why JDK/vendor/build; heap/live set; allocation rate runtime and GC workload CPU quota; memory limit container headroom target p99/p999; method warm-up, steady-state window, traffic shape, repetitions The specification is portable semantics; the process map is vendor, collector, JDK and operating-system dependent. Number What it means Common mistake heap used live and garbage objects currently occupying committed heap treating it as process RAM heap committed memory currently available to the heap without growing assuming all committed pages are resident heap reserved / -Xmx maximum heap address-space budget using it as a container memory limit RSS / PSS resident process pages; PSS apportions shared pages expecting NMT totals to equal it exactly Thread type changes where the stack cost appears. Platform threads have native stacks whose reservation is influenced by -Xss; reservation is not the same as committed or resident bytes. Since Java 21, virtual-thread stacks are growable stack-chunk objects in the GC-managed heap, not one native stack per virtual thread. Escape analysis does not mean HotSpot moves ordinary objects onto a stack. C2 can scalar-replace a non-escaping object, eliminating the allocation, and can elide an uncontended lock. Current HotSpot documentation explicitly distinguishes that from stack allocation; see the corrected treatment in (01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q2). Direct and foreign memory are also different ownership models. NIO direct buffers use native memory behind a heap wrapper and are associated with the direct-memory limit. The Java 22 FFM API uses Arena lifetime to control native segments. Do not assume -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize caps arbitrary FFM or JNI allocations (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q5). Start with HotSpot's own native allocation accounting java -XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary -jar app.jar jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory baseline jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary.diff Then reconcile with the operating system cat /proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup pmap -x <pid> RSS/NMT troubleshooting checklist: compare RSS/PSS with heap used/committed and the cgroup current/max values plus OOM events inspect NMT summary/diff, platform-thread count/-Xss, direct-buffer metrics, metaspace/code cache, and JNI/FFM/native allocator usage reproduce near peak concurrency, then correlate the first growing category with the process timeline NMT tracks HotSpot/JVM native allocations, not every third-party/JNI allocation, mapped file, or shared page. Its total will not equal RSS. Diagnose a widening RSS - NMT committed gap with OS mappings and native tooling instead of increasing -Xmx. Container sizing is a sum, not one flag Budget heap plus metaspace, code cache, platform-thread stacks, direct/foreign buffers, GC and compiler structures, native libraries, mapped pages and safety headroom. Verify the sum under peak concurrency and the chosen collector; do not copy a fixed percentage across services.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q6"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q1","surface":"track","en":"Traditional thread pools vs Virtual Threads (Project Loom) — what's the core difference?","bodyEn":"Scope label Java SE contract: Thread and concurrency APIs define observable synchronization and lifecycle semantics. HotSpot/OpenJDK implementation detail: virtual-thread carrier scheduling, pinning behavior, diagnostics, and ergonomics depend on the target JDK/vendor/build. A platform thread is scheduled by the OS and normally maps 1:1 to it. A virtual thread is a Java Thread scheduled by the JVM over a smaller set of carrier threads. When supported blocking I/O parks a virtual thread, the JVM can unmount it so the carrier runs another task. Model Programming style What must be bounded bounded platform-thread pool simple blocking code threads, queue, and downstream resources event loop/reactive non-blocking pipeline event-loop work and downstream concurrency virtual thread per task simple blocking code at high I/O concurrency downstream concurrency; virtual threads are not the scarce resource Virtual threads, final since Java 21, improve the scalability of thread-per-request code; they do not make CPU work faster, make a blocking protocol non-blocking, or increase database capacity. Create one per task rather than pooling them, and put explicit semaphores, connection pools, queues, or rate limits at scarce dependencies. Admission is not capacity A virtual-thread task is cheap to park, not free to admit. It still consumes heap/stack chunks, file descriptors, deadlines and queue budget, competes for CPU, and waits on DB/HTTP/provider capacity. Put a bound at each scarce resource and measure queue age. Version-aware pinning guidance On JDK 21–23, blocking while holding a synchronized monitor can pin a carrier; moving that I/O outside the critical section or using ReentrantLock can help. JDK 24's JEP 491 removed monitor-related pinning, so “replace every synchronized” is obsolete advice on JDK 24+. Native/foreign calls and class-initialization edge cases can still hold a carrier. The old -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads diagnostic was removed in JDK 24; use JFR, thread dumps, and carrier-utilization evidence instead. Virtual threads preserve ordinary stack traces, try/catch, ThreadLocal compatibility, and debugger behavior, which makes migration of blocking JDBC/HTTP code incremental. That does not make reactive I/O obsolete: event loops remain useful for streaming, protocol engines, explicit backpressure, and ecosystems designed around non-blocking APIs. The operational question is not “how many virtual threads can the JVM create?” but “how much concurrency can each downstream safely accept?” A virtual thread waiting for one of 20 database connections is cheap, yet 50,000 waiters still consume memory, deadlines, and queueing budget. See the coding and diagnostic details in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q1"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q2","surface":"track","en":"What do structured concurrency and ScopedValue (Java 21/25) solve compared to ThreadLocal?","bodyEn":"Structured concurrency makes concurrent subtasks share a lexical lifetime: the parent forks related work, joins it once, handles failure centrally, and cannot accidentally leave child tasks running after the scope closes. StructuredTaskScope is still a preview API in JDK 25; the JDK 25 shape uses StructuredTaskScope.open(...), not the old public ShutdownOnFailure/ShutdownOnSuccess constructors. ScopedValue, finalized in JDK 25, carries immutable context such as a request ID or principal through a bounded dynamic scope. Code can read a binding but cannot mutate it from arbitrary layers, and child tasks inherit the binding under the structured-concurrency rules. Separate responsibilities StructuredTaskScope controls task lifetime, cancellation, failure, and joining. ScopedValue carries context. Neither is a drop-in replacement for every ThreadLocal: keep thread-confined mutable state local, and use explicit method parameters when the dependency is part of the business API. In JDK 25, StructuredTaskScope.open() uses a fail-fast policy: a failed subtask causes sibling cancellation and join() propagates failure. A custom Joiner expresses policies such as “first successful result.” Because this is preview, compile and run with --enable-preview, pin the target JDK in examples, and expect source changes between previews. They did change. JDK 26 (JEP 525) renamed anySuccessfulResultOrThrow() to anySuccessfulOrThrow() and made allSuccessfulOrThrow() return a list. JDK 27 (JEP 533, the seventh preview) makes the default join() throw a checked ExecutionException, so the JDK 25 example below does not compile on 27 until it catches one. ScopedValue.where(KEY, value).call(...) binds a value only while the operation runs. This avoids the set()/remove() discipline and thread-pool leakage risk of mutable ThreadLocal. It also makes ownership visible, but large objects and accidental retention can still be costly—scope values narrowly. Compare task coordination tools in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q2) and the JDK 25 API in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4). Runnable JDK 25 preview — two child tasks inherit one immutable trace context: import java.util.concurrent.StructuredTaskScope; public class FanOut { private static final ScopedValue<String> TRACE = ScopedValue.newInstance(); static String fetch(String name) { return name + \":\" + TRACE.get(); } public static void main(String[] args) { ScopedValue.where(TRACE, \"trace-42\").run(() -> { try (var scope = StructuredTaskScope.open()) { var user = scope.fork(() -> fetch(\"user\")); var order = scope.fork(() -> fetch(\"order\")); scope.join(); System.out.println(user.get() + \" \" + order.get()); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); throw new RuntimeException(e); } }); } } save as FanOut.java javac --release 25 --enable-preview FanOut.java java --enable-preview FanOut user:trace-42 order:trace-42 Impact and trade-off: Benefit: lexical scope exposes child lifetime, failure propagation and context cleanup; leaving it cannot leak an orphan task. Preview cost: StructuredTaskScope in JDK 25 needs --enable-preview, and its API may still change. Boundary: ScopedValue replaces one-way immutable context, not a mutable ThreadLocal cache.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q2"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q3","surface":"track","en":"How does ConcurrentHashMap work, and is it really faster than Hashtable/synchronizedMap?","bodyEn":"Instead of locking the whole map, CHM locks at the bucket/bin level: CAS when a bin is empty locks the bin's first node on collision reads are nearly lock-free In current OpenJDK/HotSpot implementations, long bins may be converted from a linked list to a red-black tree (O(log n)) around an implementation threshold; verify the target build instead of treating that threshold as a Java SE contract. Result: writers to different bins may proceed independently, but this is not a universal speed claim. Hashtable, synchronizedMap, and CHM have different synchronization, iteration, null-handling, and compound-operation contracts. Benchmark the target access pattern, key skew, read/write ratio, and contention. Note: size() is only an estimate; compound operations need compute()/merge() to stay atomic. Compound operations are where mistakes usually happen: ConcurrentHashMap<String, List<Seat>> holds = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(); // WRONG: both operations are individually atomic, but the combination isn't if (!holds. containsKey (id)) { // T1 and T2 both see false holds. put (id, new ArrayList<>()); // T2 overwrites T1's list → the held seat is lost } holds. get (id). add (seat); // RIGHT: a single call — CHM holds the bin lock while the function runs holds. computeIfAbsent (id, k -> Collections. synchronizedList ( new ArrayList<>())) . add (seat); init is lazy; resize happens via transfer one bin at a time, with multiple threads helping resize concurrently (each thread claims a range of bins via transferIndex) size counting uses baseCount + a CounterCell array, LongAdder-style, to reduce contention → size() is only approximate treeify triggers when a bin has ≥ 8 elements and the table has ≥ 64 buckets (below 64, it resizes instead of treeifying) A common race: a compound \"check-then-put\" must use computeIfAbsent/merge to stay atomic — an easy mistake when using CHM as a cache. This exact \"check-then-act\" trap is the classic thread-safety question in LLD rounds (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q3"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q4","surface":"track","en":"synchronized vs ReentrantLock vs StampedLock — when do you pick which?","bodyEn":"synchronized: the simplest intrinsic monitor; automatic release and strong JVM optimization, but no timed/interruptible acquisition or multiple conditions. ReentrantLock: tryLock, timeout, interruptible acquisition, optional fairness, and multiple Conditions; always unlock in finally. StampedLock: optimistic reads plus read/write modes; not reentrant and deliberately lower-level. // 1. synchronized — prefer this for a short, unconditional critical section synchronized (lock) { balance += amount; } // 2. ReentrantLock — timed admission and explicit failure policy if (lock. tryLock ( 200 , TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) { try { balance += amount; } finally { lock. unlock (); } } else { throw new BusyException(); } // 3. StampedLock — copy state, validate, then fall back long stamp = sl. tryOptimisticRead (); long snapshot = balance; if (!sl. validate (stamp)) { stamp = sl. readLock (); try { snapshot = balance; } finally { sl. unlockRead (stamp); } } These mechanisms do not share one implementation: synchronized is a JVM intrinsic monitor; ReentrantLock is built on AbstractQueuedSynchronizer (AQS); StampedLock has its own stamped read/write synchronizer. Choose semantics first and benchmark only when contention matters. Biased locking was disabled in JDK 15 and is no longer a modern selection criterion. The JIT can still coarsen or eliminate locks. Fair ReentrantLock reduces starvation risk but can materially reduce throughput. StampedLock optimistic reads must copy the fields needed, then call validate(stamp) and retry under a read lock. Do not dereference a mutable object graph before validation. It is not reentrant and has no Condition, so it should not be the default. On JDK 21–23, blocking I/O inside synchronized could pin a virtual-thread carrier; JDK 24 removed monitor-related pinning. Use ReentrantLock when you need its semantics, not as a blanket JDK 24+ pinning workaround. See the concrete coordination examples in (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q1) and (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q4"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you compose an async pipeline with CompletableFuture, and how is it different from Future?","bodyEn":"Future only has get() (blocking). CompletableFuture lets you compose non-blocking pipelines: thenApply/thenCompose (chaining) thenCombine (merging two stages) allOf/anyOf exceptionally/handle (error handling) You can specify an executor to control which thread runs it. With Java 21+, many cases are simplified with virtual threads + blocking code instead. Production trap: the non-Async variant of thenApply runs on whichever thread completed the previous stage — if that's an I/O/Netty thread, you accidentally block it → use thenApplyAsync(fn, executor) with an explicit executor. The default ForkJoinPool.commonPool is shared JVM-wide → one slow task starves the whole app, and its size is cores-1 (poor fit for I/O). swallowed exceptions: you need an exceptionally/handle at the end of the chain, or errors silently \"disappear\" Java 9+ adds orTimeout/completeOnTimeout cancel() does NOT interrupt a task that's already running With Java 21, many CF pipelines can be replaced with virtual threads + blocking code for easier reading/debugging. For runnable CompletableFuture failure and timeout exercises, see (23-java-concurrency-coding.async.the-modern-toolkit.q1). Network retry/timeout budgets belong to (15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q5); API idempotency belongs to (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q5"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q6","surface":"track","en":"Concurrency, multithreading and parallelism — what is the real difference, and how does it change the design you pick?","bodyEn":"They answer three different questions, and an interview rewards keeping them apart: Concurrency is a property of the structure: several tasks are in flight at once and their steps interleave, so correctness has to hold for every interleaving. It is about coordination. Multithreading is one mechanism for that structure: the OS or the JVM schedules several threads of execution. Event loops, coroutines, virtual threads, separate processes and async I/O are other mechanisms for the same thing. Parallelism is a property of execution: two things run in the same instant, which needs more than one core. Concurrency Parallelism Answers how do overlapping tasks coordinate? how fast can the work finish? Limited by shared state, locks, ordering cores, and the serial fraction (Amdahl) Fails as race, deadlock, lost update no speed-up, or negative scaling from contention Needs threads? no — one event loop is concurrent yes — something must run at the same instant A single-threaded event loop is concurrent and never parallel. A parallelStream over an immutable array is parallel with almost no concurrency hazard, because nothing is shared for the interleaving to corrupt. Why the distinction changes the design: for I/O-bound work the limit is waiting, so raising concurrency — more threads, or non-blocking I/O with fewer threads — raises throughput. For CPU-bound work the limit is cores, and threads past that number buy context switches and cache pressure instead of throughput. The one-line version \"Concurrency is about dealing with lots of things at once; parallelism is about doing lots of things at once\" — Rob Pike. Concurrency is how you write it; parallelism is what the hardware gets to do with it. Little's Law turns it into a number. Requests in flight L = λ × W: 500 rps at 200 ms latency means 100 concurrent requests, whatever the pool size says. That figure is the concurrency you must support. A thread count is only one way to pay for it, and a blocking pool smaller than L queues the difference (15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q1). Choosing a mechanism is then a resource question, not a philosophical one: blocking threads — simplest to read, debug and profile; each carries a stack, so the price of concurrency is memory event loop — concurrency is cheap, but one blocking call stalls every task on the loop and stack traces stop explaining anything (15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q3) virtual threads — thread-per-request code at event-loop-shaped cost, which is what changed the default answer in Java 21 (01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q1) Parallelism has its own ceiling. Amdahl caps the speed-up at the serial fraction, and the Universal Scalability Law adds a coherency term — which is why throughput can fall when you add threads to one contended counter (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q5).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q6"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q7","surface":"track","en":"Virtual threads are cheap, so can I remove every executor and downstream limit?","bodyEn":"No. A virtual thread makes blocking-style tasks cheaper to represent; it does not make CPU, database connections, sockets, file descriptors, memory, or a provider quota infinite. Start with an admission budget for every scarce dependency: CPU-bound work needs a bounded executor or semaphore sized from available processors and measured service time. A database pool, HTTP connection pool, file descriptor limit, and external rate quota each need their own bound; the number of virtual threads must not be used as a substitute. Queue memory needs a maximum size, age/deadline policy, and a rejection or fallback path. A huge number of parked tasks can still exhaust heap and retain request data. JDK 24 delivered the monitor changes described by JEP 491, so blocking on ordinary synchronized monitors no longer creates the old broad virtual-thread pinning problem. That does not remove pinning at native or foreign-function boundaries, long CPU sections, or any downstream resource bottleneck. Measure carrier utilization, blocked-task age, pool wait time, queue age, CPU saturation, and cancellation latency rather than counting threads. StructuredTaskScope in Java 25 is still a preview API. Where it is enabled, its value is the bounded lifetime and sibling cancellation of a task tree, not a replacement for admission control. Propagate a monotonic deadline, preserve interruption, cancel children on failure, and close every resource. Test overload, a slow database, a stuck native call, cancellation during I/O, and a deployment shutdown. The design is sound only when cheap concurrency is paired with explicit bulkheads.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q7"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q1","surface":"track","en":"The equals()/hashCode() contract — what happens if you violate it?","bodyEn":"Scope label Java SE/JLS contract: equals/hashCode semantics and the equality/hashCode contract are portable. HotSpot/framework implementation detail: object layout, generated implementations, persistence proxies, and identity behavior around frameworks depend on the target runtime and library. Contract: equal per equals ⇒ same hashCode (the reverse isn't required). Violating it: two \"equal\" objects land in different buckets in a HashMap/HashSet → wrong lookups, silent duplicates. equals must be: reflexive, symmetric, transitive, consistent. Use the same set of fields for both; for JPA entities, prefer a business key over the id (the id is null before persisting). Violating the contract loses elements from a HashSet: class Booking { Long id; // null until persisted public boolean equals (Object o) { / compares by id / } public int hashCode () { return Objects. hash (id); } } Set<Booking> set = new HashSet<>(); Booking b = new Booking(); set. add (b); // hashCode computed while id = null → bucket X em. persist (b); // DB assigns id = 1001 → hashCode changes → should now belong to bucket Y set. contains (b); // false — the object is still in the set, just in the wrong bucket set. remove (b); // removes nothing at all, the element stays stuck forever A classic JPA/Hibernate bug — using a DB-generated id in equals/hashCode: before persisting, id=null → every entity is \"equal\" after persisting, id changes → the object already in a HashSet ends up in the wrong bucket, lost from the set Fix: use an immutable business/natural key or assign a UUID at the application layer before persisting or use a constant hashCode (poor distribution but correct) Be careful with Lombok's @EqualsAndHashCode, which by default pulls in every field (including lazy associations → triggers a query/LazyInitException). Rule: base equals/hashCode on an immutable set of fields that identify the business entity, using Objects.equals/hash.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q1"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q2","surface":"track","en":"Immutability and the String pool — why does String's immutability matter?","bodyEn":"String's immutability gives us: thread safety stable map keys enables a string pool (interning) that reuses literals → saves memory Immutability in general reduces concurrency bugs and makes code easier to reason about. It's a core principle behind value objects in DDD. literals are interned at compile time into the string pool (which has lived in the heap since Java 7) new String(\"x\") creates a new object; .intern() puts it back into the pool (be careful interning large amounts of runtime strings — it holds references and bloats the pool) Java 9's \"Compact Strings\" stores a Latin-1 byte[] instead of char[] → half the memory for ASCII strings concatenating in a loop should use StringBuilder (each + creates a new object) The basis for DDD value objects (Money, Currency) — records are the modern way to create an immutable carrier; remember to defensive-copy any mutable-type fields. Trivia: the string pool is a fixed-size hash table (-XX:StringTableSize) & 8594; interning too many runtime strings causes collisions & bloat. G1 has -XX:+UseStringDeduplication to merge char[]/byte[] arrays with identical content. StringBuffer is Java 1.0's StringBuilder with every method synchronized. One builder shared between threads is almost never the design, so StringBuilder is the default and StringBuffer survives in old APIs. since Java 9, a + b + c in one expression compiles to an invokedynamic call into StringConcatFactory (JEP 280), which sizes the result once. The loop is still the trap: every += iteration builds a whole new String, so the characters copied grow as O(n²).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q2"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q3","surface":"track","en":"Checked vs unchecked exceptions — the modern backend philosophy for error handling?","bodyEn":"Checked: recoverable errors, must be declared/handled. Unchecked (RuntimeException): programming errors or unrecoverable conditions. The modern trend (Spring): prefer unchecked. avoids throws spreading everywhere wraps infrastructure errors as domain exceptions handles them centrally at the boundary (@ControllerAdvice) Wrapping correctly — keep the cause: // WRONG: swallows the root cause, the real error's stack trace is gone try { gds. issueTicket (pnr); } catch (IOException e) { throw new TicketingException( \"issue failed\" ); // e is lost } // RIGHT: translated into a meaningful business exception but the cause is preserved try { gds. issueTicket (pnr); } catch (IOException e) { throw new TicketingException( \"issue failed: \" + pnr, e); } // TicketingException is checked → by default it does NOT roll back, must be declared explicitly @Transactional (rollbackFor = TicketingException. class ) Transaction implication Spring by default only rolls back on unchecked exceptions. A checked exception does NOT auto-rollback without rollbackFor. At the architectural level: wrap infrastructure errors (SQLException, IOException) into a meaningful domain exception (e.g. InsufficientBalanceException) → upper layers stay decoupled from technical detail. Spring's @Repository automatically translates JPA exceptions into DataAccessException (unchecked, categorized) handle centrally at the boundary via @ControllerAdvice, returning problem+json (RFC 7807) never swallow an exception (catch, log, and ignore) — fail fast Remember the rollback rule: only unchecked/Error auto-rolls back; checked exceptions must declare rollbackFor — a \"money didn't roll back\" bug that's very common in fintech.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q3"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q4","surface":"track","en":"ClassLoaders and the delegation mechanism — when do they cause problems?","bodyEn":"ClassLoaders load classes using parent-first delegation (Bootstrap → Platform → Application). Real-world problems: ClassLoader leaks on redeploy (a web app holds a static reference to an old class → Metaspace bloats) version conflicts between libraries Understanding this helps you debug NoClassDefFoundError, LinkageError, and dependency hell. parent-first delegation is safe (prevents spoofing core classes), but SPI/JDBC/JNDI need the thread-context classloader to load implementations from a child classloader (reversing the delegation direction). Metaspace leaks on hot-redeploy — an old web app instance is kept alive via: a static field a thread that never stopped a ThreadLocal holding a class a JDBC driver still registered with the old classloader → the whole ClassLoader can't be GC'd → Metaspace bloats until it OOMs on \"Metaspace\". Tools: -verbose:class, heap dumps. Multiple versions of the same library → LinkageError/NoSuchMethodError, inspect with mvn dependency:tree. In K8s this is avoided entirely with immutable containers + restarts instead of hot-redeploy.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q4"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q5","surface":"track","en":"What is off-heap / direct memory used for?","bodyEn":"ByteBuffer.allocateDirect allocates outside the GC heap. used for high-performance I/O (Netty, mmap) avoids copies, reduces GC pressure In exchange: you must manage its lifecycle manually (easy to leak without releasing it) harder to debug Many databases/brokers (Kafka) rely on the OS page cache + zero-copy following this same principle. Mechanism: a DirectByteBuffer allocates outside the heap, released indirectly via a Cleaner/PhantomReference when the GC collects the Java wrapper object. if the object lives long / the GC doesn't run → native memory never gets returned → a 'leak' even though the heap has plenty of room bounded by -XX:MaxDirectMemorySize Applications: Netty's pooled allocator (avoids GC + copying) Kafka: OS page cache + zero-copy sendfile (no copy through user space), mmap for the index/log the Foreign Function & Memory API (finalized in Java 22) — the modern, safer replacement for sun.misc.Unsafe Go deeper: off-heap trades \"manual lifecycle management\" for \"reduced GC pressure\" — worth it for latency-critical systems.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q5"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q6","surface":"track","en":"Which java.time type belongs where, and why must LocalDateTime never store when something happened?","bodyEn":"LocalDateTime is a wall-clock reading with no zone attached. 2026-03-29T02:30 is not a moment in time until someone says where — and in some zones that reading never existed. Type What it is Use it for Column Instant a point on the UTC timeline when something happened timestamptz OffsetDateTime instant + fixed offset API payloads, keeping the caller's offset timestamptz ZonedDateTime instant + zone rules future local events, DST-aware maths timestamptz + zone id LocalDateTime date + time, no zone a reading a human typed, before interpretation timestamp — rarely correct LocalDate / LocalTime calendar date / clock time birthday, opening hours date / time Duration / Period elapsed seconds / calendar amount timeouts vs \"one month later\" interval or numeric // wrong: the moment depends on wherever this JVM happens to run order.setPlacedAt(LocalDateTime.now()); // // right: store the instant, render in the user's zone at the edge order.setPlacedAt(Instant.now(clock)); var shown = order.placedAt().atZone(ZoneId.of( \"Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\" )); // // DST: adding a day is not adding 24 hours, and ZonedDateTime knows that var zdt = ZonedDateTime.of( 2026 , 3 , 28 , 10 , 0 , 0 , 0 , ZoneId.of( \"Europe/Berlin\" )); zdt.plusDays( 1 ); // 29 Mar 10:00 local — 23 real hours later zdt.plus(Duration.ofDays( 1 )); // 29 Mar 11:00 local — exactly 24 hours The default zone is not a decision, it is an accident LocalDateTime.now(), Instant.toString() in logs and new Date() all read the JVM default. It differs between your laptop, CI and the container, so the bug appears only in one of them. store UTC, convert at the boundary. One conversion in, one out, and the domain never asks what time zone it is in. the exception is a future local commitment: \"the meeting is at 09:00 Berlin time next October\". Store the local time plus the zone id, because governments change DST rules and the correct instant is not knowable yet. timestamptz in PostgreSQL does not store a zone — it stores a UTC instant and converts on the way out. timestamp stores the reading verbatim and is the one that loses information. Date, Calendar and SimpleDateFormat are legacy, and SimpleDateFormat is not thread-safe — a shared static instance corrupts output under load. DateTimeFormatter is immutable and safe. casting or wrapping a timestamp column in a function inside WHERE stops the index being used; compare against a computed range instead (05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q5). the zone database ships with the JDK and changes several times a year. Long-lived containers with a stale tzdata quietly disagree with reality (02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q1).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q6"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q7","surface":"track","en":"Which clock do you read in production, and what breaks when the machine's clock moves?","bodyEn":"There are two clocks and they answer different questions. Using the wrong one produces bugs that only appear when NTP corrects the machine. Wall clock Monotonic clock API Instant.now(), System.currentTimeMillis() System.nanoTime() Means what time humans think it is ticks since an arbitrary origin Can jump yes — NTP steps, manual changes, DST for local views no, never goes backwards Comparable across machines roughly, within skew no, meaningless outside one JVM Use for timestamps, expiry, scheduling latency, timeouts, rate limiting windows // wrong: an NTP correction mid-request can report a negative duration long t0 = System.currentTimeMillis(); // … work … metrics.record(System.currentTimeMillis() - t0); // // right: elapsed time comes from the monotonic clock long t0 = System.nanoTime(); // … work … metrics.record(Duration.ofNanos(System.nanoTime() - t0)); // // and the wall clock is a dependency, not a static call @Bean Clock clock () { return Clock.systemUTC(); } // tests substitute it // abridged: propagate one monotonic deadline, do not reset timeout per hop long deadline = System.nanoTime() + budget.toNanos(); long remaining = deadline - System.nanoTime(); if (remaining <= 0 ) throw new TimeoutException(); downstream.call(Duration.ofNanos(remaining)); Clock-jump failure test: inject a wall Clock and a controllable monotonic deadline source. Jump wall time forward and backward during a request, then assert that: expiry happens once remaining budget never increases cancellation reaches the child, and the resource closes If the deadline changes with the wall-clock jump, the wrong clock leaked into the timeout. What clock movement actually breaks: expiry checks — a token, lock lease or idempotency window judged against a clock a few seconds ahead of the issuer's (13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q1) ordering by timestamp — two services writing \"the latest wins\" cannot agree when their clocks differ by more than the gap between events scheduling — a job pinned to local time runs twice or not at all on the DST changeover night The habit that makes time testable Inject Clock and never call Instant.now() inside domain code. It costs one constructor parameter and turns \"wait until midnight\" into a one-line test (26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q1). give every expiry a skew allowance and validate on the side that owns the truth. A few seconds of leeway is standard for token validation; ordering guarantees need a version or sequence number instead (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q5). in PostgreSQL, now() returns the transaction start time and is identical for every row in that transaction; clock_timestamp() advances. Choosing wrongly makes rows written seconds apart look simultaneous. application time and database time are two clocks. Pick one authority per field and write it down, otherwise created_at ends up populated by whichever layer happened to touch it. fixedRate schedules from the previous start time and can pile up if a run overruns; fixedDelay schedules from the previous finish. Cron with a zone is a third behaviour again. never compare nanoTime values from two JVMs, and never persist one — the origin is arbitrary and resets with the process.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q7"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q8","surface":"track","en":"Why can `com.acme.Order cannot be cast to com.acme.Order` happen, and how do you prove which loader is at fault?","bodyEn":"At runtime a class is identified by its binary name plus the loader that defined it, never by the name alone. Load the same bytes through two loaders and the JVM holds two unrelated types: instanceof is false, the cast throws, and the message prints the same name twice. Modern JDKs print the diagnosis rather than hiding it — each side carries its module and defining loader: java.lang.ClassCastException: class com.acme.Order cannot be cast to class com.acme.Order (com.acme.Order is in unnamed module of loader 'app'; com.acme.Order is in unnamed module of loader com.acme.PluginLoader @1b6d3586) Where the second copy comes from: the same jar sits on the parent classpath and inside a child loader — a plugin, WEB-INF/lib next to the container's shared lib, an OSGi bundle Spring Boot DevTools reloads application classes in a fresh restart loader while a library singleton still holds an instance built by the previous one a static registry, cache or ThreadLocal outlives a redeploy and hands the old type back two artifacts publish the same package because a shade/relocate step was skipped Four lines settle it — compare identity first, then the loaders and the jars: Object row = registry. lookup ( \"order\" ); Class<?> found = row. getClass (), expected = Order. class ; System.out. println (found == expected); // false System.out. println (found. getName (). equals (expected. getName ())); // true System.out. println (found. getClassLoader ()); // who defined it System.out. println (found. getProtectionDomain (). getCodeSource ()); // from which jar The same rule duplicates static state Two loaders mean two copies of every static field, so a \"singleton\" initialises twice and the halves of the app disagree about a cache or a counter — with no cast anywhere to make it visible. The fix is ownership, never a cast. Decide which loader owns the shared type and let parent-first delegation do the rest (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q4): the shared API or DTO lives in the parent only; the child ships an implementation, not a second copy of the contract across a plugin or restart boundary, talk through an interface the common parent loaded — or a serialized form (JSON, bytes) that carries no class identity at all never cache instances of application classes in a class that outlives the restart loader; with DevTools, exclude the package or move the cache inside the restarted graph mvn dependency:tree / gradle dependencies finds the duplicate artifact; relocate it rather than shipping one package twice Reading the evidence. -Xlog:class+load=info prints every loaded class with the source: it came from, so a duplicate shows up as two sources for one name. A heap dump answers the other half: how many java.lang.Class instances exist for that name, and which loader keeps each one alive — the same question a Metaspace leak asks.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q8"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q9","surface":"track","en":"The four OOP pillars — which Java mechanism actually implements each, and where does each one leak?","bodyEn":"Encapsulation is not \"private field + getter/setter\". It is: the object never becomes visible in a state where its invariant is broken. Validate in the constructor, hand back copies of mutable fields, and delete the setter that lets a caller reach an invalid state. Abstraction names what a caller may ask for and hides how. interface for the contract; abstract class only when subtypes genuinely share state or a fixed sequence of steps. Inheritance is the strongest coupling Java has — a subclass depends on the superclass's implementation, not just its signature, so a change upstream can break it silently. Prefer composition; use sealed when the set of subtypes is closed and you want the compiler to check every switch. Polymorphism is one call site, many implementations, picked by the JVM from the object's runtime class. interface vs abstract class — the split that decides most designs: interface abstract class Instance state none — only static final constants fields, and a constructor that runs before the subclass body How many a class implements many extends exactly one Bodies default , static , private methods (Java 8/9+) any method, any access modifier Reach for it when a capability unrelated types can offer ( Comparable , a port) shared state + a template of steps subtypes fill in Overload is compile-time, override is run-time — and mixing them up is the classic trap: class Repo { void save (Object o) { / generic path / } } class UserRepo extends Repo { void save (User u) { / never reached below / } } Repo r = new UserRepo(); r. save ( new User()); // Repo.save(Object) — overload resolved from the STATIC type Repo // override, by contrast, is resolved from the object: class Base { Base() { init (); } void init () {} } class Child extends Base { private final List<String> items = new ArrayList<>(); @Override void init () { items. add ( \"x\" ); } // NPE: Base's ctor runs first, items is still null } The one-line version Encapsulation protects the invariant, abstraction names the contract, inheritance shares the implementation (at a price), polymorphism defers the choice to run time. SOLID is what you get when the last two are applied with discipline. an overridable call from a constructor is the bug above: the JVM dispatches to Child.init() while Child's field initialisers have not run. Make such a method private, static or final, or move the work out of the constructor. @Override is not decoration. It is the only compile-time proof that you overrode rather than accidentally overloaded — the UserRepo above would not compile with it. only instance methods dispatch. static methods are hidden, not overridden, and fields are shadowed: both resolve from the static type, so neither is polymorphic. A private method compiles to invokespecial and a static one to invokestatic — no dispatch instruction at all. final is the one people get wrong: a final method still compiles to invokevirtual. Nothing can override it, so the JVM resolves it to a direct call — but that happens at run time, not in the bytecode, which is why final is a correctness contract rather than a speed trick. what dispatch costs: invokevirtual reads the object's class vtable, but the JIT profiles each call site. Monomorphic (one type seen) inlines to a direct call; bimorphic inlines both behind a type check. megamorphic (three or more types) falls back to a real vtable lookup and stops inlining — which is why a hot loop over a single implementation runs as fast as a static call, and why an over-abstracted one does not. covariant return types are allowed on an override (a narrower return), parameters are not — widening a parameter silently turns it into an overload. the pillars are the mechanism; the principles that say when to reach for them are (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q2), and how to cite them in an interview without reciting is (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q1).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q9"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q10","surface":"track","en":"Is Java pass-by-value or pass-by-reference — and what does that mean for objects, arrays and \"pointers\"?","bodyEn":"Java is always pass-by-value. For an object argument, the value that gets copied is the reference — a handle that points at the object — never the object itself. So a method can change the object the caller sees, but it can never make the caller's variable point somewhere else: void rename (User u) { u. setName ( \"B\" ); } // visible to the caller: same object void replace (User u) { u = new User( \"C\" ); } // invisible: only the local copy moved void swap (Integer a, Integer b) { Integer t = a; a = b; b = t; } // swaps two copies What a reference is, and is not: an opaque handle the JVM manages: no pointer arithmetic, no address-of operator, no way to free memory yourself. null is the reference that points at nothing; dereferencing it is the NullPointerException whose name still remembers pointers. an object lives while something reachable references it; the GC owns the rest (01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q2). The interview sentence Java copies every argument; for an object the copy is a reference, so you can mutate the shared object but you cannot rebind the caller's variable. the JLS says it directly: on invocation, \"the values of the actual argument expressions initialize newly created parameter variables\" (§15.12.4.5). Java has no ref or out parameter. arrays are objects, so void fill(int[] a) mutates the caller's array; a = new int[3] inside the method does not. immutable types make the difference invisible: a String or Integer parameter cannot be mutated, so the method can only affect its own copy — which is why swap(Integer, Integer) is the classic trick question. to hand back more than one value, return a small record. An out-parameter through a mutable holder (AtomicReference, a one-element array) works, but it hides the data flow from the reader. defensive copies follow from the same rule: a constructor that stores a caller's List stores a reference to it, so the caller can still change your \"private\" state (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q9). with compressed oops (HotSpot's default for heaps under about 32 GB) a reference field takes 4 bytes instead of 8 — a JVM detail, never a number your code can read.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q10"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q1","surface":"track","en":"How does HashMap work? Hashing, buckets, collisions, treeify, resize?","bodyEn":"Scope label Java SE contract: collection API behavior and HashMap correctness contracts are portable. OpenJDK/HotSpot implementation detail: hash spreading, bin layout, treeification, and object sizes can vary by target build and flags. HashMap = an array of buckets + a hash function. The put(k, v) flow: compute hash(key) → pick a bucket (index = hash & (n-1)) bucket empty → place the node there collision (same bucket) → append to a linked list; if the key equals, overwrite instead Treeify: in current OpenJDK/HotSpot implementations, a bucket with ≥ 8 elements and a table with ≥ 64 buckets may become a red-black tree (O(log n) instead of O(n)). The threshold and tree-bin mechanics are implementation details, not Java SE guarantees. Verify the target build before tuning around them. Resize: when size exceeds capacity × load factor (default 0.75) → capacity doubles and everything is rehashed. What a single put walks through: static int spread ( int h) { return h ^ (h >>> 16 ); // mixes high bits into low bits, reducing collisions } map. put ( \"VN8H2K\" , booking); // 1. h = spread(key.hashCode()); i = (n - 1) & h → n = 16, so i falls in 0..15 // 2. bucket i empty → place a new Node there, size++ // 3. bucket i has a key → compare hash then equals: if it matches, overwrite the value, size unchanged // 4. different key → append to the end of the list; if the list is ≥ 8 AND the table ≥ 64, treeify // 5. size > 16 0.75 → resize doubles capacity; each node moves to i or i + 16, no full rehash Trap a broken hashCode/equals \"loses\" a key inside the map (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q1). Using a mutable key and changing a field after inserting it means you can no longer find it. Why capacity is a power of 2: it lets hash & (n-1) replace modulo (faster). HashMap also spreads the hash (h ^ (h >>> 16)) to mix high bits into low bits, reducing collisions. not thread-safe → concurrent puts can lose data (Java 7 even had an infinite loop during resize); use ConcurrentHashMap instead (01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q3). allows one null key (bucket 0); Hashtable doesn't. iteration order is not guaranteed → use LinkedHashMap for insertion order, TreeMap for sorted order.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q1"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q2","surface":"track","en":"ArrayList vs LinkedList — how do they differ and when do you choose which?","bodyEn":"ArrayList LinkedList Structure dynamic array doubly-linked list get(i) O(1) O(n) add at end O(1) amortized O(1) add/remove in the middle O(n) — shifts the array O(1) if already at the node Memory compact (just the array) costly (2 pointers/node) In practice: almost always use ArrayList. good cache locality (contiguous array), fast index access LinkedList only wins when adding/removing a lot at both ends — and even then ArrayDeque should be used instead Takeaway LinkedList's O(1) assumes you're already at the right node; getting there still costs O(n). That's why LinkedList rarely wins in practice. ArrayList.add is amortized O(1): when full it grows ~1.5× (newCap = old + (old >> 1)) and copies into a new array → occasionally O(n) but O(1) on average. If you know the size in advance, use new ArrayList (capacity) to avoid resizing. removing from the middle requires System.arraycopy to shift the tail → O(n). neither is thread-safe → use CopyOnWriteArrayList (read-heavy) or Collections.synchronizedList. iterating while removing → use Iterator.remove(), otherwise you get ConcurrentModificationException (fail-fast).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q2"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do HashSet / LinkedHashSet / TreeSet differ?","bodyEn":"All three store no duplicate elements; they differ in ordering and cost: HashSet: no order, O(1) add/contains — backed by HashMap LinkedHashSet: preserves insertion order, O(1) — backed by LinkedHashMap TreeSet: sorted by Comparator/natural order, O(log n) — backed by a red-black tree (TreeMap) Choosing: just need fast dedup → HashSet need stable insertion order → LinkedHashSet need sorted order / range queries (headSet, subSet) → TreeSet HashSet LinkedHashSet TreeSet Iteration order not guaranteed insertion order sorted add / contains O(1) O(1) O(log n) Memory per element lowest plus 2 pointers plus 3 pointers and a color bit Allows null one null one null no Requires of elements equals + hashCode equals + hashCode Comparable/Comparator Range queries no no headSet, tailSet, subSet HashSet is just a HashMap with a dummy value → every property (hashing, collisions, load factor) matches HashMap's. TreeSet doesn't allow null (elements must be comparable); HashSet allows one null. broken equals/hashCode causes HashSet to hold \"silent duplicates\" (two equal objects in two buckets) — same root cause as item 1.11. TreeSet dedupes using compareTo, not equals — if two objects aren't equals() but compareTo returns 0, TreeSet silently drops one of them. A Comparator inconsistent with equals is a silent bug.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q3"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q4","surface":"track","en":"Cheat sheet: which collection for which situation?","bodyEn":"Need Use Because Index-accessed list ArrayList get is O(1), cache-friendly Queue / Stack / Deque ArrayDeque faster than the old LinkedList/Stack Fast key→value lookup HashMap O(1) Map that keeps insertion order LinkedHashMap + can double as an LRU cache Sorted map/set TreeMap / TreeSet O(log n), range queries Concurrent map ConcurrentHashMap bin-level locking (01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q3) Rule of thumb: default to ArrayList + HashMap; only switch when you have a measurable reason (ordering, sorting, concurrency). A few points senior interviewers like to ask: LinkedHashMap with accessOrder=true + overriding removeEldestEntry gives you a few-line LRU cache. Arrays.asList() returns a fixed-size list (add/remove throw UnsupportedOperationException). a plain array (T[]) is the fastest and most compact but fixed-size and not generic-friendly → only use it on hot paths/primitives (int[] avoids boxing). pre-sizing a large HashMap/ArrayList's initial capacity avoids repeated rehashing/resizing.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q4"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q5","surface":"track","en":"HashMap vs ConcurrentHashMap — and what do initial capacity, load factor and threshold actually control?","bodyEn":"Both are the same skeleton: an array of bins, a hash function, and the same two knobs (16 and 0.75). They differ only in how that array is guarded — and that one difference leaks into the constructor, size(), iteration and null handling. Aspect HashMap ConcurrentHashMap Thread safety none — two concurrent puts can lose an entry per-bin; safe for any number of writers Write path plain array write CAS into an empty bin, otherwise synchronized on the bin's head node Read path plain field reads volatile reads, no lock at all null key / value one null key, null values allowed neither — both throw NullPointerException Iterator fail-fast — ConcurrentModificationException weakly consistent — never throws, may miss concurrent writes size() exact, one int field an estimate summed from baseCount + CounterCell[] Resize one thread rebuilds the whole table threads that find a resize running help transfer bins Resize threshold capacity × loadFactor , your value always 0.75 — sizeCtl = n - (n >>> 2) Custom load factor honoured for the map's whole life only sizes the first table, then ignored new …(16) gives you 16 bins, grows at 12 entries 32 bins, grows at 24 entries The three numbers, precisely: initial capacity = 16 — table.length, the number of bins. Always rounded up to a power of two, and the array is allocated lazily on the first put, not in the constructor. load factor = 0.75 — how full the table may get before it grows. threshold = 16 × 0.75 = 12 — the entry count for the whole map that triggers resize(). Cross the twelfth entry and capacity doubles to 32 and the threshold to 24. Threshold counts entries in the map, not entries in one bin. Twelve entries all chained into a single bin still resize the table at 12 — and resizing does not help them, because they collided on their hash, not because the table was full. Do these decide the bucket index? Only the capacity does. // 1. spread — fold the high 16 bits down, so the mask cannot throw them away static int hash (Object key) { int h = key. hashCode (); return h ^ (h >>> 16 ); } // // 2. index — a mask, not a modulo: n is a power of two, so (n-1) is all-ones // n = 16 -> n-1 = 0b0000_1111 -> only the low 4 hash bits survive // n = 64 -> n-1 = 0b0011_1111 -> the low 6 bits survive i = (n - 1 ) & hash; Load factor and threshold never appear in that formula. What they decide is when n changes — and n is in the formula. So they steer the index one step removed: every growth widens the mask by one bit, and one more hash bit starts participating. // \"VN8H2K\".hashCode() = -1759086559 -> spread 0x9726EF07 -> low bits ...0000_0111 // \"VN9J4L\".hashCode() = -1759054783 -> spread 0x97266367 -> low bits ...0110_0111 // // n = 16 mask 0b001111 both -> bin 7 collision // n = 32 mask 0b011111 both -> bin 7 one extra bit was not enough // n = 64 mask 0b111111 bin 7 and bin 39 they finally split // // Resize never recomputes a hash. (hash & oldCap) == 0 keeps the node at i, // anything else moves it to i + oldCap: 0x67 & 32 != 0 -> 7 + 32 = 39 Two consequences worth naming. A power-of-two capacity exists so & replaces % — one instruction instead of a division, and no negative index from a negative hashCode. And because the mask only ever reads the low bits, keys differing only high up would collide forever; h ^ (h >>> 16) is what brings those bits into reach. Tuning the load factor: Load factor Memory Collisions When it makes sense low — 0.5 worse: resizes earlier, so the array is larger and emptier fewer; chains stay at one or two nodes a small, lookup-dominated hot map where the footprint is noise default — 0.75 balanced ~61% of bins empty, ~30% hold exactly one node leave it alone unless you measured a reason high — 1.0 and up better: a smaller array holds the same entries more, and they worsen fast; above 1.0 collisions are guaranteed by pigeonhole very large, rarely-read maps where footprint beats lookup latency \"High saves memory\" is true and usually still a bad trade, because the array is the cheap half of the map: // 1000 entries, 64-bit HotSpot with compressed oops // lf 0.75 -> need cap x 0.75 >= 1000 -> cap = 2048 bins // lf 1.00 -> need cap x 1.00 >= 1000 -> cap = 1024 bins // // the array holds references, 4 bytes each: // 2048 x 4 = 8 KB vs 1024 x 4 = 4 KB -> saved 4 KB // the Node objects are the real cost, and they do not change: // 1000 x 32 B = 32 KB vs 1000 x 32 B = 32 KB -> saved 0 // // 4 KB out of 40 KB = 10%, paid for by doubling average bin occupancy // from 0.49 to 0.98 entries per bin. Rarely worth it. Implementation scope: compressed oops, object headers, field sizes, and the Node byte count vary with JDK build, architecture, and flags. Confirm them with JOL or a target-runtime measurement; these figures are not API guarantees. The physical layer — what those numbers look like in memory: Trap new HashMap<>(1000) does not reserve room for 1000 entries: it rounds to 1024 bins and the threshold is still 1024 × 0.75 = 768, so the map resizes at the 769th entry anyway. Sizing takes a capacity, not a count — use HashMap.newHashMap(1000) (Java 19+) or new HashMap<>((int) (1000 / 0.75f) + 1), both of which land on 2048 bins and never resize. Current OpenJDK detail — why 8 is the treeify threshold. With a decent hashCode bin occupancy follows a Poisson distribution — at load factor 0.75 the mean is about 0.5 entries per bin. The threshold, 64-bin guard, and tree-bin mechanics are implementation details; inspect the target build before using them as diagnostic evidence. The table in HashMap's own source: a bin is empty 60.65% of the time, holds one node 30.33%, two nodes 7.58%, and eight nodes about 6 times in 100 million. Treeifying is not a normal event; a bin that reaches 8 is evidence of a weak hash or a deliberate collision attack, not of a table that is merely full. It also only treeifies once the table is ≥ 64 bins — below that HashMap resizes instead, because a tiny table's collisions are crowding rather than clustering. ConcurrentHashMap computes 0.75 without touching a float. sizeCtl = n - (n >>> 2) is n minus a quarter of n. That is exactly why a custom load factor handed to CHM's constructor only sizes the first table and is then forgotten — after the first resize the density is hard-wired. new ConcurrentHashMap<>(16) allocates 32 bins, not 16. The constructor evaluates tableSizeFor(1 + 16 / 0.75) = tableSizeFor(22) = 32, so the map genuinely holds 16 entries before growing. HashMap given the same argument gives 16 bins and resizes at 12. Same number, opposite meaning — CHM pre-divides by the load factor for you. A HashMap's threshold field means two different things over its life. new HashMap<>(64) stores threshold = 64 — the capacity to allocate — and only when the first put triggers resize() does it become 64 × 0.75 = 48. One field, reused, which is why reading a fresh map's threshold in a debugger is confusing. Chains are a cache problem before they are a complexity problem. Eight nodes is eight dependent loads that cannot be prefetched, and a main-memory miss costs on the order of 100× an L1 hit. That is why treeify pays despite a TreeNode being ~56 B, roughly two Nodes. The put/treeify/resize walkthrough lives in (01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q1); the bin-locking detail and the computeIfAbsent race are in (01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q3).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q5"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q6","surface":"track","en":"A class is used as a HashMap key — why must it override both `equals` and `hashCode`, and what exactly breaks when only one of them is there?","bodyEn":"get reaches your key in three steps, and each step uses a different half of the override: hash(key) — take key.hashCode() and spread it with h ^ (h >>> 16) index = (n - 1) & hash — which bucket to walk inside the bin — e.hash == hash && (e.key == k key.equals(e.key)) Step 2 decides where to look; step 3 decides whether it matched. Miss either override and the map still answers, with the wrong answer and no exception anywhere. Overridden What the lookup does Symptom neither identity hash, identity comparison only the exact instance is ever found — correct if that is what you meant only equals two equal keys hash to different buckets step 3 is never reached: get returns null and put adds a duplicate only hashCode right bucket, then identity comparison works with the original instance, fails with an equal copy — the \"passes locally\" bug both bucket, then value comparison correct — as long as the fields it reads cannot change A key that mutates is worse than a missing override: Map<Point, String> map = new HashMap<>(); Point p = new Point( 1 , 2 ); map. put (p, \"here\" ); // hash of (1,2) chose bucket X p. setX ( 99 ); // entry stays in X, the hash now points at Y map. get (p); // null — it looked in Y map. remove (p); // removes nothing; the entry is stuck for the map's lifetime What to use as a key Prefer a record, String, enum or boxed number: a record derives both methods from its components and cannot mutate, which deletes the whole class of bug instead of documenting it. A constant hashCode is correct and still wrong. The contract only demands equal ⇒ same hash, so return 1 never loses an entry — but every key lands in one bin, and lookups degrade to a scan or to a tree once the bin holds eight entries in a table of at least 64. Correctness and distribution are separate properties, and the contract covers only the first (01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q1). == before equals is why the bug hides. Step 3 tries reference equality first, so a test that reuses the same instance passes while production — where the key is rebuilt from a request or a database row — does not. Not every map cares. TreeMap and TreeSet ignore both methods and order by compareTo/Comparator, which brings its own surprise when the ordering disagrees with equals (01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q3). IdentityHashMap uses == on purpose. JPA entities are where this is lost most often — a database-generated id inside hashCode changes at persist time. The contract item works that case through in full (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q1).","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q6"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q7","surface":"track","en":"ConcurrentHashMap is thread-safe; when is a multi-step cache or deduplication operation still wrong?","bodyEn":"Thread-safe individual map operations do not make an entire business workflow atomic. The classic race is if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, value): two callers can observe the gap and both perform the work. A get followed by an update across two keys has the same problem, and ConcurrentHashMap does not provide a table-wide transaction or a global ordering guarantee. Use the narrowest correct mechanism: compute, computeIfAbsent, or merge can make one-key state transitions atomic according to the API contract; keep the mapping function short, deterministic, and free of recursive updates or slow I/O. An immutable value object plus one atomic replacement is easier to reason about than mutating a value stored in the map. A multi-key invariant needs an external lock/actor/serialized command, or a durable database constraint and idempotent command. A JVM map cannot be the authority for a process that must survive restart. Cache stampede protection still needs expiry, failure, and eviction policy; a map operation alone does not make a remote refresh safe. Do not turn this into a universal speed claim against Hashtable or synchronizedMap. The contracts and contention pattern differ; benchmark the target key distribution, read/write ratio, hot-key skew, allocation, and critical-section time. Test duplicate callers, a mapping failure, reentrancy, eviction during refresh, and restart/rebuild of the durable source.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q7"},{"id":"01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q8","surface":"track","en":"Comparable vs Comparator — what does `sort` actually rely on, which algorithm runs, and how does a bad comparator break it?","bodyEn":"Two ways to order a type: Comparable is the type's natural order, one per class (compareTo): String, BigDecimal, LocalDate. Comparator is an external order you pass in, as many as you need: by price, then by date. Both return a negative, zero or positive int, and a sort relies on nothing else. What makes it work is the contract: the sign flips when the arguments swap, the order is transitive, and equal elements compare equal to everything else alike. // compose instead of hand-writing compare() Comparator<Order> byRank = Comparator . comparing (Order::status) . thenComparing (Order::createdAt, Comparator. reverseOrder ()) . thenComparing (Order::id); // a unique tie-breaker makes the order total ──── (a, b) -> a.amount() - b.amount() // overflows: MIN_VALUE - 1 is positive (a, b) -> Integer. compare (a.amount(), b.amount()) // correct Which algorithm runs — implementation notes rather than spec, but stable for years: Call Algorithm today Stable? Arrays.sort(int[]) and other primitives dual-pivot quicksort irrelevant — equal primitives are indistinguishable Arrays.sort(Object[]) · List.sort · Collections.sort TimSort, an adaptive mergesort yes — the spec requires it Stability is what makes multi-pass sorting work: sort by date, then stably by status, and orders inside each status stay in date order. A comparator that lies fails late TimSort may notice an inconsistent comparator mid-sort and throw IllegalArgumentException: Comparison method violates its general contract! — only for some inputs, usually first in production. the usual liars: subtraction overflow (above), comparing double with < (NaN is neither), sorting on a field that changes during the sort, and random tie-breaks. \"consistent with equals\" matters for sorted collections: TreeSet/TreeMap use compare alone, so new BigDecimal(\"1.0\") and \"1.00\" are one element in a TreeSet and two in a HashSet (01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q3). nulls are a policy, not an accident: Comparator.nullsFirst(…) / nullsLast(…) state it; a bare compareTo throws NPE at the first null. the stability guarantee covers objects only. The JDK's note says sort(Object[]) \"does not have to be a MergeSort, but it does have to be stable\"; primitives have no identity, so their sort need not be. cost: O(n log n) comparisons in the worst case, and TimSort runs close to O(n) on input that is already mostly sorted — which real data often is. ArrayList.sort sorts its backing array in place; a list without its own sort falls back to the default, which copies to an array, sorts, and writes back. asked \"what does sorting rely on\", answer with the contract: a sort is only as correct as the total order you hand it.","contextEn":"Java Core & JVM internals","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm#question-01-java-core-jvm.collections-data-structures.q8"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q1","surface":"track","en":"How did Java 8 change the way Java is written?","bodyEn":"Java 8's turning point was a set of final Java SE language and library features that changed everyday code: Feature Status Practical boundary Lambda expressions, default methods, streams final Java SE 8; choose the pipeline or a loop by readability and workload Optional final Java SE 8; makes absence explicit but does not prevent every null bug java.time final Java SE 8; use the immutable, timezone-aware types for new code Many estates still run Java 8, but it is not a universal enterprise default. Whether to stay depends on the selected JDK distribution's support and security policy, framework/dependency compatibility, and migration budget. Streams are lazy until a terminal operation; parallelStream() uses the common ForkJoinPool, so treat it carefully for I/O or web-container work. Optional is useful at an API boundary, especially as a return type for an absent value. It is not a blanket replacement for validation, fields, or parameters. java.time gives immutable types such as Instant and ZonedDateTime ; keep local business time and UTC instants distinct. Default methods can evolve an interface, but conflicting defaults and behavioral compatibility still need review. Lambda translation uses invokedynamic and LambdaMetafactory as an implementation detail. Do not turn that detail into a universal class-count or performance promise. Pitfall: nested streams can be harder to read and debug than a plain loop. Keep the abstraction that makes the invariant easiest to review.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q1"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q2","surface":"track","en":"What does Java 11 bring that matters (compared to 8)?","bodyEn":"Java 11 is best understood as a release with both final features and migration boundaries: Feature or change Status Migration meaning var for local variables final Final in JDK 10 and available on Java 11; it is compile-time inference, not dynamic typing Standard HttpClient final Java 11 API with HTTP/2 and asynchronous operations; test timeout and cancellation behavior Single-file source launch final JDK 11 tool behavior; it does not replace a production build/reproducibility policy Java EE and CORBA modules removed from the JDK Add and pin required dependencies; test JAXB, JAX-WS, CORBA, reflection, and packaging explicitly G1 default, ZGC, and Epsilon implementation/vendor-specific Verify the selected distribution, collector availability, flags, and container ergonomics Do not use the release number to predict migration pain. An 8→11 hop can expose removed modules and stronger encapsulation, while a later hop can expose agents, bytecode tools, drivers, TLS defaults, or container assumptions. Treat every hop as a test matrix: Gate Evidence before rollout Removed modules and APIs dependency scan, compile, startup, and representative integration tests Strong encapsulation, agents, and reflection agent attach/startup tests plus reflective and proxy-heavy paths Serialization, JDBC, and TLS wire/schema compatibility, driver matrix, certificate, and protocol tests Runtime and container limits GC/thread/heap behavior under the production cgroup and failure budget Preview or incubator flags compile and run with the exact pinned flags, or remove the feature from the target Use 02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q7 for the support-policy decision. The migration conclusion is evidence from this matrix, not “8→11 is always the hardest” or “11→17→21 is only dependency bumping.”","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q2"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q3","surface":"track","en":"How did Java 13–16 lay the groundwork for 17 (text blocks, switch, records)?","bodyEn":"Java 13–16 were transition releases: several features were previewed, then finalized on a later release. Feature Status timeline What to carry forward Switch expressions preview in JDK 12–13; final in JDK 14 value-producing switch and arrow cases; verify syntax against the target JDK Text blocks preview in JDK 13–14; final in JDK 15 multi-line SQL/JSON/HTML is clearer, but escaping and formatting remain application concerns Records preview in JDK 14–15; final in JDK 16 record components are final references, not recursively immutable objects Sealed classes preview in JDK 15–16; final in JDK 17 the permitted hierarchy can support exhaustive analysis once the compiler knows its variants Pattern matching for instanceof preview in JDK 14–15; final in JDK 16 less manual casting; null behavior and flow scoping still matter Helpful NullPointerException final in JDK 14 diagnostic detail is useful evidence, not a substitute for fixing the invariant A preview feature is not a stable production contract. --enable-preview must match the compiler and runtime, and the source can change before finalization. A final feature still needs a target-runtime and library compatibility test. This is why Java 17 contains several final forms of work that began earlier; “introduced in 17” and “finalized in 17” are different statements. Keep the JEP status beside the feature when writing a migration plan.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q3"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q4","surface":"track","en":"Java 17 (LTS) — what's the key feature set?","bodyEn":"Java 17 provides a strong final baseline, but its guarantees have precise boundaries: Feature or behavior Status in Java 17 Boundary Records final A shallowly immutable carrier: component references are final, while referenced objects may still mutate Sealed classes final Restricts permitted direct subclasses; it does not define serialization, validation, or business-state rules Pattern matching for instanceof final Reduces casts; flow scope, null, and type tests remain part of the contract Pattern matching for switch and record patterns preview or not yet available in the Java 17 target Use the status of the exact target JDK; both were final in Java 21 JDK-internal encapsulation platform/runtime behavior Older reflection and agent assumptions can fail; test the actual libraries and flags Java 17 is a vendor-supported LTS choice for many distributions, but support dates, patch channels, and framework compatibility are distribution- and date-specific. Pin those inputs instead of calling one release the universal recommendation. sealed + record + pattern matching is an ADT-like modeling style, not a Java feature formally named “algebraic data type”: the compiler can reason about a known sealed hierarchy; a record supplies a concise carrier, but deep immutability requires defensive copies or immutable components; exhaustiveness does not settle null , serialization, persistence, or compatibility when a hierarchy evolves; state-machine variants such as Held Paid Issued Cancelled still need explicit transition and validation rules. Java 17's encapsulation changes and the Java 17 baseline for Spring Boot 3 are migration facts to verify alongside the dependency and agent matrix.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q4"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q5","surface":"track","en":"Java 21 (LTS) — why is it a 'game-changer' for concurrency?","bodyEn":"Java 21 made several concurrency and data-model features final; other names in the same discussion were still preview or version-specific. Feature Status in or around Java 21 Boundary Virtual Threads (JEP 444) final Useful for blocking-style concurrency; CPU work and downstream capacity still need bounds Pattern matching for switch (JEP 441) final Guarded patterns do not cover values that fail the guard Record patterns (JEP 440) final Deconstruction follows the record's declared components and their null/type behavior Sequenced Collections (JEP 431) final Standardizes first/last/reversed operations; it does not change collection capacity Generational ZGC implementation/vendor-specific Check the selected build, collector flags, and measured pause/CPU trade-off Structured Concurrency preview Preview in Java 21 and still preview in JDK 25; isolate flags and source usage Scoped Values preview in Java 21 Final in JDK 25; do not back-port the later status to a Java 21 target Foreign Function & Memory API not final in Java 21 Final in JDK 22, so keep that milestone separate An exhaustive Java 21 switch must include an unguarded fallback for a variant whose guard is false: sealed interface Event permits Refund, Charge {} record Refund(String id, long amountCents) implements Event {} record Charge(String id, long amountCents) implements Event {} switch (event) { case Refund( var id, var amount) when amount > 0 -> refund (id, amount); case Refund( var id, var amount) -> throw new IllegalArgumentException( \"amount\" ); case Charge c -> charge (c); case null -> throw new IllegalArgumentException( \"event\" ); } The migration value is larger than a feature checklist: Virtual threads can keep blocking JDBC/HTTP code readable, but CPU-bound work needs bounded parallelism and the database, partner quota, queue, deadline, and memory budgets remain finite. Monitor-related pinning changed in JDK 24; native calls and other blocking paths still require an audit on the target runtime. A guarded pattern covers only values satisfying its guard, so the second Refund arm is required. Scoped Values became final in JDK 25, while Structured Concurrency remains preview there. Keep those lifecycle statuses separate in build and rollout policy. For a Spring Boot blocking service, enabling virtual threads can be a small configuration change; capacity tests decide whether it is safe.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q5"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q6","surface":"track","en":"Java 25 (GA 9/2025) — what matters most for production and high-load systems?","bodyEn":"Java 25 should be read feature by feature. Treat its LTS position, support duration, patch channel, and cost as properties of the selected JDK distribution rather than universal Java guarantees. Feature or change Status Production boundary Scoped Values (JEP 506) final Immutable, bounded context propagation in JDK 25; test scope lifetime and inheritance Compact Object Headers (JEP 519) final feature, build/flag-specific Header layout and heap savings depend on compressed references, fields, alignment, and the selected build; measure them Opt-in in 25; the default from JDK 27 (JEP 534) Generational Shenandoah product feature; implementation/vendor-specific Availability and default differ by distribution; select and benchmark it against the target collectors AOT command-line ergonomics and method profiling implementation/toolchain-specific Profiles are optimization artifacts tied to a build and representative training workload JFR sampling and method tracing additions implementation/version-specific Verify the exact JDK build, event support, overhead, and operational export path KDF API, module imports, compact source files, and flexible constructor bodies final features in JDK 25 Still run compiler, framework, and style/tooling compatibility tests Structured Concurrency (JEP 505) preview Requires the matching preview policy and can change before finalization Still preview in JDK 27 (JEP 533), and its API changed in 26 and 27 Foreign Function & Memory API final since JDK 22 It is not a new Java 25 finalization; native boundaries still need security and failure tests Production positioning Canary product features and compare application SLOs, CPU, RSS, GC behavior, readiness, and rollback time. A feature marked final can still be a poor choice for a particular workload, while a preview feature needs an explicit compatibility and rollback policy. Compact Object Headers do not justify a universal “8-byte object” or heap-saving claim: the actual layout depends on the object and build. Use JOL or heap measurement on the target distribution. Generational Shenandoah may suit a short-lived-object workload, but select it explicitly and benchmark it against G1/ZGC with the same traffic and cgroup limits. AOT method profiles come from a training run. If class paths, feature flags, or traffic differ from production, HotSpot still profiles and optimizes online; the cache is not a replacement for the JIT. Structured Concurrency uses StructuredTaskScope.open(...) in the JDK 25 preview and needs --enable-preview ; Scoped Values are final. Keep those statuses distinct in documentation and CI.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q6"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q7","surface":"track","en":"Why do enterprises stick to LTS releases instead of the latest version every 6 months?","bodyEn":"“LTS” is a support commitment made by a JDK vendor, not a Java-language guarantee with one universal end date. Policy input What to record Support owner vendor/distribution, architecture, support channel, security policy, and end date Update cadence CPU/PSU rollout, canary, rollback, and emergency-patch procedure Compatibility scope framework, agents, libraries, drivers, base image, and operating-system support Upgrade lead time next-JDK CI signal, migration test budget, and production rollback rehearsal Enterprises often choose a supported LTS to simplify certification, vulnerability patching, compliance evidence, and fleet testing. That does not mean freezing the runtime: security and CPU/PSU updates still need a tested rollout path. Vendor designations and free/commercial windows differ, so verify them for the actual distribution. The migration risk is accumulated distance Jumping from 8 directly to 21 or 25 combines removed APIs, strong encapsulation, library changes, TLS defaults, and build-tool upgrades. Regularly testing the next JDK keeps the dependency graph and operational playbook ready. There is no universal “never fall more than two LTS releases behind” rule; derive the deadline from support, security exposure, framework support, and migration lead time. Before adopting a target JDK, require evidence for each gate: Gate Minimum evidence Removed modules/APIs dependency inventory, compile, startup, and integration tests Strong encapsulation, agents, and reflection agent startup, proxy, reflective access, and serialization tests JDBC, TLS, and wire compatibility driver matrix, certificate/protocol checks, and representative contract tests Container/runtime limits CPU, heap, native memory, threads, GC, and shutdown tests under cgroup limits Preview/incubator use exact compiler/runtime flags, isolated source boundary, and rollback plan A non-LTS feature release can be reasonable when the team can upgrade frequently and needs a delivered feature. LTS is a maintenance strategy, not proof that an application is secure or stable.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q7"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q8","surface":"track","en":"How do you read Java release notes without accidentally shipping a preview or vendor-specific feature?","bodyEn":"Classify every feature before using it in a production recommendation: a final Java SE API has a specification and compatibility contract a preview feature requires the matching compiler/runtime preview flags and may change or disappear an incubator module is an experimental API with a different adoption and migration risk a HotSpot diagnostic flag or vendor default is not a Java language guarantee For a pinned service, record the JDK distribution, exact patch version, compiler and runtime flags, base image, architecture, and whether preview modules are allowed. Compile and run with the same policy in CI, verify the feature with a small compatibility test, and make the release gate fail when the runtime is older or preview flags are missing. Use the official JEP/API/release note rather than a blog title as the status source. For example, StructuredTaskScope is documented as a preview API in Java 25, while JEP 491 is a delivered Java 24 HotSpot change. Those facts have different rollout rules. A release table should therefore say final, preview, incubator, or implementation-specific next to each feature, then link the migration and rollback test. “Java 25 supports it” is incomplete unless the exact feature status and target distribution are named.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q8"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q9","surface":"track","en":"Java 26 and 27 shipped after the Java 25 LTS. What changed that matters in production, and should a service on 25 move?","bodyEn":"Neither is an LTS release. JDK 26 reached GA on 17 March 2026 and JDK 27 on 15 September 2026. Oracle's roadmap plans the next LTS as Java 29 in September 2027, and each non-LTS release is superseded by the next feature release. A fleet standardised on 25 usually stays there and treats 26 and 27 as the early-warning build — what changes there is what the next LTS will change. Change Release · status What it changes in production G1 is the default collector in every environment (JEP 523) 27 · final A JVM that saw one CPU or less than 1792 MB used to pick Serial On 27 a small pod gets G1 unless a flag pins Serial Compact object headers by default (JEP 534) 27 · final Headers shrink from 96 to 64 bits on 64-bit platforms (opt-in since JEP 519 in 25) -XX:-UseCompactObjectHeaders turns it off Warnings when deep reflection mutates a final field (JEP 500) 26 · final Default --illegal-final-field-mutation=warn , one warning per module deny is the planned future default HTTP/3 in java.net.http.HttpClient (JEP 517) 26 · final Opt-in with HttpClient.Version.HTTP_3 ; HTTP/2 stays the default Falls back to HTTP/2 or 1.1 unless HTTP_3_URI_ONLY is set AOT object caching with any GC (JEP 516) 26 · final The AOT cache from JEP 483 now works with ZGC as well Faster startup no longer rules out a low-pause collector Hybrid post-quantum key exchange in TLS 1.3 (JEP 527) 27 · final The JDK client offers X25519MLKEM768 first by default jdk.tls.namedGroups overrides the list JFR redacts secrets inside the process (JEP 536) 27 · final Arguments, environment variables and system properties that look secret are written as [REDACTED] Tuned with -XX:FlightRecorderOptions Structured Concurrency, Lazy Constants, primitive types in patterns 27 · preview Seventh, third and fifth previews Structured Concurrency changed shape again in 26 and 27 Should a service on 25 move? Usually not in production: a non-LTS release is superseded within six months, so a fleet on 26 or 27 has to take every feature release after it. Run 27 in CI instead, so the new defaults surface before the next LTS makes them yours. What to try on 27 before it matters: the full test suite under --illegal-final-field-mutation=deny, which finds serializers, mocking and injection code that still writes final fields small containers: a pod with one CPU or under 1792 MB moves from Serial to G1, so compare RSS, pause time and CPU at the same limits memory and tooling with compact headers on: heap size, heap-dump readers, JVMTI agents and anything that reads object layout TLS peers and middleboxes against the larger hybrid key share Preview code breaks between releases. The JDK 25 structured-concurrency example in (01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q2) does not compile on JDK 27, because join() now throws a checked ExecutionException. Pin the JDK for every preview example and re-check it on each release (02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q8). Which collector did ergonomics pick? Ask the JVM at the limits it will run under, rather than reading the release notes: JDK 21/25 inside a 1-CPU or < 1792 MB container: \"Using Serial\"; JDK 27: \"Using G1\" java -Xlog:gc -version the effective flags of a running JVM jcmd <pid> VM.flags keep the choice explicit instead of inheriting a new default java -XX:+UseSerialGC -jar app.jar Serial was chosen for small machines because it has the least overhead. JEP 523 expects G1 not to regress footprint, throughput, latency or startup there, but that is a claim to measure on your own pod, not a guarantee — G1's remembered sets and regions cost native memory that a 512 MB limit notices. Compact headers help most where there are many small objects. The saving is per object, so measure it with the real heap rather than quoting a percentage.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.release-timeline.q9"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q1","surface":"track","en":"Virtual Threads vs Goroutines — how do the models differ?","bodyEn":"Goroutines and virtual threads are runtime-managed concurrency abstractions that can multiplex many activities onto OS threads, but there is no single contract saying they block, cancel, or consume memory in the same way. Dimension Goroutine Virtual thread Scheduling Go runtime scheduler; version, GOMAXPROCS , preemption, and cgroup behavior matter JVM scheduler mounts virtual threads on carriers; JDK and executor policy matter Blocking integration Many standard blocking paths cooperate with the runtime; syscalls and cgo paths need verification Supported blocking APIs can park the virtual thread; native calls and library behavior need verification Cancellation/context context.Context is an explicit convention; goroutine lifetime is not automatic cancellation Interruption and structured scopes can express cancellation; propagation still needs a contract Stack and memory Stack growth, runtime metadata, heap, and cgo allocations are implementation-dependent Continuation, heap objects, carrier state, and ThreadLocal usage are implementation-dependent Native boundary cgo can change scheduling, pointer/lifetime rules, and thread consumption JNI/FFM and monitor behavior can change pinning and failure boundaries by JDK Coordination Channels, mutexes, atomics, and ownership are chosen for the invariant Shared memory, locks, concurrent collections, interruption, and scopes are chosen for the invariant Admission Bound goroutines with queues, semaphores, connections, and provider quotas Bound virtual-thread work with executors, semaphores, connections, and provider quotas Channels and structured scopes solve different problems: one helps coordinate data/ownership, the other helps manage task lifetime. Neither is a direct substitute for the other's cancellation or backpressure semantics. Go scheduler behavior is tied to the pinned Go version and runtime settings; do not use a historical stack-size slogan as a capacity promise. A virtual thread may park on a supported blocking operation, but a pinned carrier or a native/library boundary can still consume scarce OS-thread capacity. context.Context , interruption, and structured scopes only work when the called library propagates them. Test cancellation at the database, HTTP, queue, and response boundaries. The count of goroutines or virtual threads is not service capacity. CPU, memory, sockets, database connections, queue depth, and external quotas need explicit bulkheads.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q1"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should Java and Go performance be compared for a real backend workload?","bodyEn":"A fair Java-versus-Go comparison starts with functional equivalence and a workload card, not a language slogan. Dimension Java variables Go variables Report Runtime JDK distribution/version, HotSpot or OpenJ9, GC, JIT, flags Go version, GC settings, GOMAXPROCS , cgroup limits exact toolchain and image Request payload, serialization, TLS, fan-out, CPU/blocking mix, cancellation the same protocol, client behavior, payload, fan-out, and cancellation functional-equivalence checks Concurrency platform/virtual executor, queue/bulkhead, connection pool goroutine admission, channel/worker limits, scheduler, connection pool in-flight work and downstream saturation Memory heap/live set, direct/native memory, code cache, thread/carrier state heap/live set, goroutine stacks, runtime metadata, cgo/native allocations RSS, cgroup memory, and OOM margin SLO cold readiness, warm p50/p99/p999, throughput, CPU, errors/timeouts the same cold/warm and steady-state measures distribution, not one favorable run Correctness ordering, idempotency, deadlines, cancellation, numeric precision, schema the same invariants and failure schedule contract pass before performance wins Keep framework, database, cache, deployment topology, client, data distribution, quotas, and target latency equivalent. “Startup” means process-ready plus dependency readiness; “memory” means RSS/container usage, not only heap; “throughput” includes errors and tail latency. Benchmark validity Warm JIT-based services to the state production reaches, avoid coordinated omission in the load generator, use realistic dependencies or faithful fakes, and repeat enough trials to make the interval and variance useful. A collector or runtime change can move tail latency without fixing queueing, CPU throttling, network, or dependency limits. Publish the harness, commit, configuration, dataset, hardware, runtime flags, raw results, and date before generalizing the result. Decide from the measured bottleneck: If CPU is material, compare representative hot paths and include warm-up and allocation behavior. If database or partner latency dominates, schema, caching, batching, connection limits, and deadlines may matter more than language microbenchmarks. Price capacity only after fixing region, replicas, autoscaling, SLO, and operational ownership; report cost per successful business operation rather than a universal runtime discount.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q2"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q3","surface":"track","en":"When do you pick Java, when do you pick Go? What's the polyglot model?","bodyEn":"There is no universal language winner. Choose a runtime and deployment target from the service's constraints, then verify the boundary with a contract and capacity test. Decision signal Java candidate Go candidate Domain complexity large stateful domain, mature JVM library, or an existing Java estate small focused service where a compact dependency surface is valuable Deployment shape JVM or Native Image, depending readiness, observability, and dynamic-feature needs native-binary deployment when its toolchain and runtime contract fit the service Concurrency bottleneck virtual threads or bounded executors, with explicit downstream bulkheads goroutines or bounded workers, with explicit downstream bulkheads Team and operations existing JVM skills, libraries, incident tools, and support ownership existing Go skills, libraries, incident tools, and support ownership Evidence matched workload, SLO, RSS/CPU, migration and patching results the same workload, SLO, RSS/CPU, migration and patching results A polyglot boundary can be justified when each side has a clear owner and a stable HTTP/gRPC or messaging contract. It is not evidence that one language wins overall: two stacks also mean two build, observability, dependency, hiring, and incident-response surfaces. Closing point Present the measured bottleneck, capacity headroom, operational owner, and rollback path. Cost or density numbers are useful only with their workload, date, and deployment assumptions. Choose Java for domain and ecosystem constraints when the team can support the JVM or Native Image target. Choose Go for a focused service or native deployment when tests show the binary, scheduler, memory, and operational model meet the SLO. Keep the boundary explicit: protocol, schema, deadlines, retries, idempotency, telemetry, and ownership. Revisit the choice when the workload or support contract changes; do not infer it from company name lists or an unsourced velocity percentage.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q3"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q4","surface":"track","en":"GraalVM Native Image — which Java weakness does it address relative to Go?","bodyEn":"GraalVM Native Image is an alternate Java deployment target. It AOT-compiles a reachable application into a native executable, which may improve startup/readiness or density for a particular workload; it is not a guaranteed drop-in equivalent to a HotSpot JVM or a Go binary. Potential benefit Condition to verify Startup and readiness measure process-ready plus dependency readiness under the same image, probe, and cold-start conditions Memory and density measure RSS/cgroup memory at idle and load; do not infer it from Java heap alone Scale-to-zero behavior include image pull, initialization, TLS/resource loading, and autoscaling delay in the SLO Peak throughput compare with a warmed-up JIT using the same protocol, data, concurrency, and error budget The trade-off is a closed-world build and a separate operational artifact: Failure boundary Required check Reflection, dynamic proxies, and resources declare reachability metadata and run end-to-end paths, not only a successful build JNI, FFM, and native libraries test loading, pointer/lifetime assumptions, platform images, and injected native failures Build-time initialization check time-sensitive state, configuration, security providers, and side effects moved to build time PGO and optimization train on representative production paths; treat the profile as build/workload-specific Operations test metrics, profiles, logs, graceful shutdown, patching, debug symbols, and rollback Native-image release gate A native build is not ready until reachability/metadata tests, representative integration and load tests, TLS/resource loading, startup/readiness, graceful shutdown, observability, and rollback pass for the target image and architecture. Native Image can be a good fit when startup or density is an SLO, while a warmed JVM can be a better fit when peak throughput, dynamic framework behavior, or fast build iteration dominates. PGO can help only when its training paths represent production. Treat Native Image as a separate deployment contract. A passing compilation does not prove reflection, serialization, resource loading, security-provider, JNI/FFM, or failure-path compatibility.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q4"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q5","surface":"track","en":"What's the future of Java: Project Loom, Valhalla, Panama?","bodyEn":"The names Loom, Valhalla, and Panama mix delivered features, preview APIs, and active project work. Keep their status separate before making a roadmap claim. Area Status anchor Safe production statement Loom Virtual Threads final in JDK 21; Structured Concurrency preview in JDK 25 Use the final virtual-thread API; isolate preview structured-concurrency code and flags Valhalla active project / status can change Value/primitive classes are a project direction, not a shipped Java 25 contract Panama Foreign Function & Memory API final in JDK 22; later work has its own status Use the final FFM contract for the selected JDK; do not label every future Panama feature final The useful direction is clear—better concurrency, data layout, and native interop—without treating the direction as a benchmark or compatibility guarantee. Delivered facts belong to a pinned JDK and JEP: Virtual Threads are final in JDK 21 and FFM is final in JDK 22. Preview APIs need matching compiler/runtime flags, an isolated source boundary, and a migration plan. Valhalla remains an active project/unknown from this unit's evidence. Do not promise flat layout, no headers, lower RAM, or cache gains before the target feature and workload exist. Panama is not one status: distinguish FFM final in JDK 22 from later incubator, preview, or project work. This wording keeps the Java-versus-Go comparison honest: a delivered runtime contract can be tested; a project goal cannot.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q5"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q6","surface":"track","en":"Records, sealed classes, and pattern matching combine to create what programming style?","bodyEn":"Records, sealed classes, and pattern matching support an ADT-like modeling style in Java; they do not create one Java feature formally named “algebraic data types.” Construct What Java guarantees What it does not guarantee record final component fields and generated data-oriented methods deep immutability, validation of every value, or immutable referenced collections sealed hierarchy a declared set of permitted direct subclasses known to the compiler that persistence, serialization, reflection, or another module will evolve safely Pattern matching for switch concise dispatch and exhaustiveness checks for the known type hierarchy coverage of null , future variants, or values outside a guard without explicit cases This style is useful for domain and state-machine modeling with less boilerplate, but “ADT-like” is the accurate label. A Java record is a shallowly immutable carrier, and a sealed hierarchy is nominal and closed only within the rules of the compiled model. For a booking or payment state machine, define one record per state and make the switch cover every permitted state, null , and any guarded fallback. Adding a permitted subtype is a source- and deployment-compatibility event, not merely a new data class. Also decide the serialization, persistence, default-value, and evolution rules explicitly. A compiler exhaustiveness check protects one compiled boundary; it does not make illegal states impossible across JSON, databases, reflection, or independently deployed services.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q6"},{"id":"02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q7","surface":"track","en":"What must a fair Java-versus-Go backend benchmark hold constant?","bodyEn":"Hold the question constant before comparing the languages. Use the same protocol, payloads, data distribution, client, TLS settings, serialization, retry/deadline policy, database and cache, connection-pool limits, container CPU/memory quotas, and deployment topology. Measure both cold start and warmed steady state; a JIT warm-up, a build-time native image, and a pre-warmed Go process answer different operational questions. Report throughput and p50/p95/p99 latency together with CPU, RSS/cgroup memory, allocation/GC or runtime pauses, startup/readiness, error/timeout rate, connection-pool wait, queue age, and downstream saturation. Run multiple trials with a declared ramp, duration, sample size, hardware, compiler/runtime version, and statistical treatment. A lower median with a worse tail or a faster server that overloads the database is not a win. Virtual-thread or goroutine count is not capacity. Bound CPU work, database connections, sockets, memory, external quota, and in-flight requests in both implementations. If Java Native Image is compared with a Go binary, label it as a separate deployment target and test reflection, resource loading, TLS, shutdown, observability, and patching. The result should select a workload and operating model, not declare a universal language winner.","contextEn":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go#question-02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q7"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q1","surface":"track","en":"How does the IoC/DI container work? Bean scopes and lifecycle?","bodyEn":"The container inverts control: you declare beans, the container creates/wires/manages their lifecycle. DI removes manual new calls → easier to test (mock), easier to swap out. Scope: singleton (default): 1 instance/context prototype: a new instance every time it's fetched web scope: request/session Lifecycle: instantiate → DI → @PostConstruct → use → @PreDestroy. Trap injecting prototype into a singleton → it's only created once. You need ObjectProvider/@Lookup to get a fresh instance each time. The container has two phases: BeanDefinition: reads metadata (can be modified via BeanFactoryPostProcessor) instantiation: DI, BeanPostProcessor wraps the AOP proxy Prefer constructor injection (immutable, easy to test, detects circular dependencies at startup) over field injection (hides dependencies, hard to test, forces reflection). a circular dependency between two constructor-injected singletons → fail-fast (good); fix by splitting responsibilities, don't use @Lazy to hide a design smell request/session scope needs a scoped proxy to be injected into a singleton","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q1"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q2","surface":"track","en":"How does @Transactional actually run? Why does self-invocation lose the transaction?","bodyEn":"When transaction management is configured in the default proxy mode, Spring uses an AOP proxy (JDK dynamic proxy if there's an interface, CGLIB if it's a class). The proxy opens/commits/rolls back the tx around the method. The logic lives in the outer proxy, not in the bean's own bytecode. Self-invocation: calling this.method() within the same bean → goes straight to this, bypassing the proxy → the annotation has no effect. fix: split into a separate bean, self-injection, or AspectJ weaving similarly: private/final methods can't be proxied Code where the transaction is silently lost: @Service class BookingService { public void confirmAll (List<Long> ids) { for (Long id : ids) { confirm (id); // = this.confirm(id) → doesn't go through the proxy → NO transaction } } @Transactional public void confirm (Long id) { / ... / } } // Fix: split into another bean, so the call leaves and re-enters through the proxy @Service class BookingConfirmer { @Transactional public void confirm (Long id) { / ... / } } Money trap by default only RuntimeException/Error trigger rollback. A checked exception does NOT auto-rollback unless rollbackFor is set. JDK dynamic proxy (with an interface) vs CGLIB (subclass — can't proxy final/private/static) advice sits on the outer proxy → every self-invocation bypasses it correct fixes: split into another bean, AopContext.currentProxy() (needs exposeProxy), or AspectJ weaving (also intercepts self-calls) In the default proxy mode, imperative transaction-resource synchronization is bound to the current thread through TransactionSynchronizationManager (ThreadLocal). A reactive transaction manager carries context in Reactor instead; it is not a ThreadLocal continuation. @Async and parallelStream start work outside the imperative boundary unless the worker opens its own transaction. A reactive chain must use a supported reactive manager and preserve Reactor context. Critical fintech trap: calling an external API (PSP/GDS) inside a tx → holds the DB connection too long → pool exhaustion + prolonged locking.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q2"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q3","surface":"track","en":"Propagation REQUIRED / REQUIRES_NEW / NESTED — when to use each?","bodyEn":"Propagation Behaviour Use when REQUIRED joins the existing tx or creates a new one default — shared commit/rollback REQUIRES_NEW suspends the outer tx, opens an independent one audit log must persist even if the business tx rolls back NESTED savepoint within the current tx partial rollback back to a savepoint REQUIRES_NEW typically needs a second physical connection in a JDBC pool → watch the pool; other resource managers can have different mechanics. Isolation can also be configured here (fintech usually uses READ COMMITTED/REPEATABLE READ + deliberate row locking). The propagation annotations are intercepted only when the call enters through a Spring proxy in the default proxy mode. Imperative managers bind resources to the current thread. Reactive managers carry a transaction in Reactor context and must support the requested propagation. Do not copy this JDBC table to a reactive flow without checking that manager's contract. Three nested methods and the resulting rollback: @Transactional // TX-1 opens here public void pay (Long bookingId) { ledger. debit (bookingId); // REQUIRED → runs within TX-1 audit. log ( \"PAY_ATTEMPT\" , bookingId); // REQUIRES_NEW → TX-2, commits separately promo. apply (bookingId); // NESTED → savepoint within TX-1 throw new PaymentFailedException(); // TX-1 rolls back } // Result: the debit is rolled back, promo is lost too (its savepoint sits inside TX-1), // but the audit row STILL remains — that's exactly why REQUIRES_NEW exists. @Transactional (propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW) public void log (String type, Long id) { / ... / } @Transactional (propagation = Propagation.NESTED) public void apply (Long id) { / an error here only rolls back to the savepoint / } REQUIRES_NEW suspends the outer tx and typically opens a second physical connection in a JDBC pool → it can multiply HikariCP pressure and deadlock when outer workers hold connection A while waiting for connection B. NESTED uses a JDBC savepoint (not every DB/driver supports it well) OTA deep dive: writing audit/outbox entries should share the tx (REQUIRED) to stay atomic with the business operation REQUIRES_NEW only makes sense when the audit event must be saved even if the business operation fails Resource-allocation bound, not a normal pool-size recommendation: for C_outer concurrent outer JDBC transactions and at most C_nested simultaneously-held REQUIRES_NEW connections per outer transaction, use P_needed ≥ C_outer × (1 + C_nested) + reserve as a conservative demand bound. Measure C_nested, cap outer concurrency, and check the result against the database and fleet budget. If it cannot fit, split the workflow, use an outbox, or avoid nested physical transactions.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q3"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q4","surface":"track","en":"Spring AOP: how does the proxy work, and what are its limitations?","bodyEn":"AOP separates cross-cutting concerns (tx, logging, security, retry) from business logic via advice wrapped around a pointcut. Spring AOP is proxy-based (runtime): it only intercepts calls made through the proxy to a Spring bean's public methods it does not intercept self-calls, private methods, or objects created with new If you need more power (constructors, fields, self-calls) → use AspectJ (compile-time/load-time weaving). Why private and final methods can't be advised: @Service class PricingService { @Cacheable ( \"fares\" ) public Fare lookup (String route) { / ... / } // works: called from outside through the proxy @Cacheable ( \"fares\" ) private Fare lookupRaw (String route) { / ... / } // silently does nothing @Cacheable ( \"fares\" ) public final Fare lookupFinal (String r) { / ... / } // silently does nothing } // CGLIB generates a subclass at runtime — whatever can't be overridden can't be advised: // class PricingService$$SpringCGLIB extends PricingService { // public Fare lookup(String r) { ...advice...; return super.lookup(r); } // OK // private/final methods can't be reached by the subclass → advice can never be inserted // } AspectJ inserts bytecode directly → can intercept constructors, field access, self-calls, non-Spring objects, but the build is more complex (weaver/agent) ordering multiple aspects via @Order (important when tx + retry + logging overlap) performance: the proxy adds one extra call layer (negligible); an overly broad pointcut makes reasoning harder Deep dive: typical cross-cutting concerns besides tx are retry, rate-limit, audit, metrics — but don't hide business logic inside an aspect.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q4"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do Spring application events behave around a transaction, and when does an event have to become an outbox row?","bodyEn":"ApplicationEventPublisher is an in-process call, not a message bus. By default the listener runs synchronously, on the caller's thread, inside the caller's transaction — so a listener that throws rolls the publisher back. Listener Runs Failure does Use for @EventListener at publish, same thread and transaction rolls back the publisher work that must be part of the same unit @TransactionalEventListener(BEFORE_COMMIT) just before commit aborts the commit last-moment validation, extra writes @TransactionalEventListener (AFTER_COMMIT, default) after commit succeeds is lost — the commit already happened notifying the outside world AFTER_ROLLBACK / AFTER_COMPLETION after the outcome is known logged only cleanup, metrics @Async listener on another thread never reaches the caller slow, optional side work // publishing inside the transaction, acting after it commits @Transactional public void confirm (OrderId id) { var order = orders.get(id); order.confirm(clock); events.publishEvent( new OrderConfirmed(id)); // queued until commit } // @TransactionalEventListener // AFTER_COMMIT by default void on (OrderConfirmed e) { mailer.send(e.id()); } // crash here and nobody is notified The trap in the default: AFTER_COMMIT fixes \"published before rollback\", but the work is now outside the transaction and outside any retry. A process crash between commit and listener loses it silently. A write performed by that listener also needs a transaction of its own (REQUIRES_NEW), or it joins one that is already finishing. The line where events stop being enough Optional reactions (cache eviction, metrics, or resendable email) can use an event. Required reactions write an outbox intent in the same local transaction. Topic 09 q3 owns Outbox/Saga/idempotency; topic 08 q7 owns broker delivery. This item owns Spring listener timing and the crash window. events keep modules decoupled inside one deployable, which is their real value; they are not a substitute for a broker between services (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3). an @Async listener loses everything thread-bound: the transaction, the security context and the MDC. Propagate what it needs explicitly, or the log line for that side effect has no trace id. ordering between listeners is @Order, not luck — but relying on listener order usually means the flow belongs in one explicit service method. listeners are proxied like anything else, so publishing from a private method or self-invoking a listener behaves the way @Transactional does (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q4). in tests, a published event with no assertion is invisible. Capture events with a recording listener and assert on them, the same way you would assert on a message (26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q1).","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q5"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q6","surface":"track","en":"Spring Cache turns caching into one annotation. Which of its defaults will hurt you in production?","bodyEn":"The abstraction hides a cache manager, a key generator and a serialization format — three decisions you now own without having made them. Provider dimension Local cache Shared cache Decision and test Topology Caffeine is per instance; pods hold separate copies Redis or another shared provider adds a network and availability boundary choose acceptable divergence and failure mode TTL and eviction configure per cache name; process restart clears it provider TTL, memory policy and eviction are shared but operationally mutable test stale windows, expiry and eviction under load Serialization in-process objects avoid a wire format but still need version-safe code payload schema, class allowlist and compatibility must be explicit test old and new payloads during rolling deploys Invalidation write on one pod does not evict another automatically pub/sub, versioned keys or explicit eviction can coordinate copies test commit, rollback, replay and missed invalidation Stampede and sync sync=true may coalesce supported local loads only annotation-level sync does not automatically lock across nodes load-test one hot key and bound origin concurrency Tenant and authority key include tenant, user or authorization dimensions when the value depends on them shared topology makes a missing dimension a cross-tenant leak assert key isolation and never treat a hit as authorization Failure behavior define fail-open or fail-closed behavior when local memory is full define timeout, outage, stale-read and circuit policy for the remote cache inject provider failure and observe the business fallback // the annotation is a proxy call, so this self-invocation is not cached at all public Fare quote (String route) { return lookup(route); } // bypasses the proxy @Cacheable (value = \"fares\" , key = \" route\" , sync = true ) public Fare lookup (String route) { … } // // eviction inside a transaction that may still roll back @Transactional @CacheEvict (value = \"fares\" , key = \" route\" ) // runs at method exit public void updateFare (String route, Fare f) { … } // rollback ⇒ cache already gone The consistency problem the annotation cannot solve: eviction happens in the application while the commit happens in the database, and the two are not atomic. Between them another request repopulates the cache from the old row, and the stale value now outlives the update. A cached value is a copy with its own lifetime Once a value is in Redis it survives your deploy. Change the shape of the cached object without changing the key and the new code will fail deserializing the old payload, on every instance at once. version the cache name or the key when the payload shape changes: fares:v3:{route}. It is the cheapest deploy-safety measure in caching. evict after commit, not during: publish the eviction from a transactional event listener so a rollback leaves the cache untouched (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q5). a local Caffeine cache is per instance, so ten pods hold ten opinions. Either keep the TTL short enough that divergence is acceptable, or use a shared cache and accept the network hop (10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3). do not cache what you cannot invalidate — permissions, balances and prices at checkout are read from the source of truth, however tempting the annotation looks. the abstraction is worth it for read-through of stable reference data; for anything requiring precise invalidation, explicit cache code is clearer than annotations spread across the call graph.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q6"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q7","surface":"track","en":"Why does a Spring transaction work in MVC but disappear in a reactive or asynchronous call?","bodyEn":"The transaction boundary follows the execution model. In the imperative stack, Spring commonly binds transaction resources and synchronization to the current thread while the proxied method runs. A call through @Async starts on another executor after the caller may have returned, so it does not inherit the same transaction or resource safely. Passing a JDBC connection or EntityManager into that task is a lifecycle bug, not context propagation. Reactive transactions use a reactive transaction manager and Reactor context rather than assuming a thread-local continuation. The context must travel through the reactive chain; blocking JDBC work on the event loop is still a resource and latency failure. Proxy rules still matter: self-invocation, private/final methods, or direct construction can bypass advice in the default proxy mode. Choose the boundary explicitly: keep one local transaction around the state change write an outbox or durable job for work that must survive a crash let the worker open its own short transaction Propagate only an intentional correlation/authorization context under a tested policy; never copy secrets or a live connection. Test commit, rollback, cancellation, retry after an unknown outcome, scheduler shutdown, executor rejection, and a reactive cancellation path. “It returned a Future” is not proof that the database work is atomic or durable.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q7"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q1","surface":"track","en":"How does Spring Boot auto-configuration work under the hood?","bodyEn":"Boot scans auto-config classes (declared in ...AutoConfiguration.imports), applying them conditionally via @Conditional: @ConditionalOnClass — is the library on the classpath? @ConditionalOnMissingBean — has the user not already defined one? @ConditionalOnProperty — enabled by a property? → 'HikariCP is on the classpath ⇒ auto-configure a DataSource'; if you declare your own bean, Boot steps aside. Bottom line 'convention over configuration' = auto-config + starters. Debug with --debug to see the conditions report (matched/unmatched). Boot 3.x and later read auto-configuration from META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports; older Boot lines used spring.factories for this mechanism. The Boot 4/Spring 7 examples below are a separate generation from Boot 3 defaults, so pin the target line before relying on metadata or ordering. Boot then orders configurations with @AutoConfigureBefore/After/Order and evaluates @Conditional checks against the classpath, user beans, properties, and web type. writing a company-wide custom starter = an auto-config module + OnMissingBean (so other teams can override) + @ConfigurationProperties Trap: wrong auto-config ordering causes wrong bean overrides; too many conditionals slow down startup Condition report as a contract: run java -jar app.jar --debug and inspect matched/unmatched conditions in the ConditionEvaluationReport. A protected /actuator/conditions endpoint can complement startup logs. In tests, use ApplicationContextRunner to assert the expected auto-config and back-off behavior.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q1"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q2","surface":"track","en":"What is a starter dependency and why is it convenient?","bodyEn":"A starter (e.g. spring-boot-starter-web) is a POM 'bundle' that gathers compatible dependencies for a single purpose (web = Spring MVC + Tomcat + Jackson...). One line instead of declaring and aligning versions for dozens of libraries. Versions are aligned by Boot's parent BOM. Interview trap The follow-up question is almost always 'how does a starter differ from auto-configuration?'. A starter is just a POM that pulls in dependencies, it contains no configuration logic; the actual auto-configuration lives in spring-boot-autoconfigure with @Conditional classes (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q1). Proof: pull in a starter and exclude auto-config, it still runs; conversely you can declare the dependency yourself and auto-config still kicks in. a starter is a 'meta' POM (little/no code) that bundles compatible dependencies + activates the matching auto-config transitive versions are aligned by the spring-boot-dependencies BOM → declaring a dependency needs no version upgrading Boot means upgrading the whole 'basket' of libraries that have been tested together (reduces jar hell) writing your own company-payments-starter standardizes config/observability across services (platform engineering) Trap: overriding a transitive version in the BOM must be done carefully (can break tested compatibility); use dependency:tree to check.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q2"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q3","surface":"track","en":"Deep-dive POM: dependencyManagement, BOM, scope, transitive dependencies, exclusion — what do you need to know?","bodyEn":"dependencyManagement / BOM: centralizes version declarations (import BOM with scope import); child modules inherit them scope: compile (default), provided (supplied by the container), runtime (JDBC driver), test, import transitive dependency: a dependency of a dependency; conflicts are resolved by 'nearest wins' exclusion: excludes an unwanted transitive dependency distinguish plugin (build-time) from dependency (the app's libraries) Golden command: mvn dependency:tree to inspect and untangle conflicts. Bottom line transitive + nearest-wins + BOM are the key to avoiding 'jar hell' and NoSuchMethodError caused by version mismatches. nearest-wins resolves conflicts (shallowest depth wins, NOT the highest version) → can sometimes pull in an unexpectedly old version, so pin it explicitly in dependencyManagement to 'pin' it <scope>import</scope> in dependencyManagement imports a BOM (e.g. Spring Cloud) optional=true stops a transitive dependency from propagating further; provided for things supplied by the container (servlet/lombok) dependency:tree -Dverbose + dependency:analyze (used-but-undeclared / declared-but-unused) Deep dive: runtime NoSuchMethodError/NoClassDefFoundError is usually caused by mismatched transitive versions → pinning the BOM + exclusions fixes 'jar hell' at the root.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q3"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q4","surface":"track","en":"Maven vs Gradle — what's the difference, and when to pick which?","bodyEn":"Maven Gradle Syntax declarative XML DSL (Groovy/Kotlin) Speed measure dependency resolution, plugins, compilation, tests and cache hits measure the same stages; incremental, configuration and task caches help only when inputs are cache-safe Customization strict convention flexible build logic Good for simple projects, easy onboarding large / multi-module projects Gradle can avoid repeated work with incremental build, build cache, configuration cache and the daemon, but the gain depends on task inputs, plugins, repository latency, cache hit rate and CI isolation. Maven's determinism, strict convention and broad plugin ecosystem can make a build easier to inspect, but plugins, repositories and project structure still determine its performance and failure modes. Deep dive: pick Gradle for monorepos/multi-module projects needing speed & complex build logic (Android/Kotlin); pick Maven for simplicity and stability. Both support reproducible builds & BOMs (supply-chain security). Build measurement table before choosing: Dimension Record for both tools Controlled comparison Dependency graph resolution time, graph size, dynamic or changing modules same lock/BOM, repository mirror and network cache Plugins and tasks configuration, compilation, code generation, tests and packaging same source, JDK, plugin versions and worker limits Repository and cache download bytes, local/remote hit rate and cache misses compare cold and warm runs separately Build state daemon, incremental and configuration-cache invalidation repeat after a clean checkout and an unchanged rebuild CI runner CPU/RAM, queue time, parallelism and network record p50/p95 over representative commits, not one demo Use the report to choose the tool for this repository; never turn one warm build into a universal Maven-versus-Gradle percentage.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q4"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q5","surface":"track","en":"Spring profiles & externalized config — best practices for managing configuration?","bodyEn":"Separate config from code (12-factor): application-{profile}.yml, env vars, secret managers. Priority order (highest → lowest): command-line args env vars application-{profile}.yml application.yml defaults in code Never hardcode secrets in the repo; use Vault/Secrets Manager + a config server for multiple services. do NOT commit secrets to the repo/image; inject them at runtime via env/volume from Vault/AWS Secrets Manager/K8s Secret (+KMS) profiles for environment (dev/staging/prod) and for features (@Profile) for multiple services → Spring Cloud Config/Consul + dynamic refresh (@RefreshScope) to change config without redeploying Trap: a profile-specific override silently causes 'works fine locally, breaks in prod'; storing sensitive config in a ConfigMap instead of a Secret config is a kind of API too — version it and audit changes (GitOps)","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q5"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q6","surface":"track","en":"@Async and @Scheduled — what should you watch out for?","bodyEn":"@Async runs a method on a different thread pool (also proxy-based → self-calls have no effect); configure a dedicated executor, don't rely on the default unbounded pool @Scheduled for cron/fixed-rate jobs; with multiple instances you must avoid duplicate runs (ShedLock/distributed lock or leader election) Production trap: @Async defaults to SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor (spawns a new thread every time, NOT bounded) → under high load this explodes thread count/OOMs. you must define a dedicated ThreadPoolTaskExecutor (core/max/queueCapacity) remember queueCapacity must fill up before the pool grows threads toward max (a surprising behaviour) @Async is also proxy-based → self-calls have no effect, and it loses tx/security/MDC context (needs a TaskDecorator to propagate MDC/trace id) @Scheduled across N instances runs N times → use ShedLock/distributed lock or leader election Deep dive: Java 21 — a virtual-thread executor (newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor) for @Async scales I/O-bound work well without pool tuning.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q6"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q7","surface":"track","en":"Spring Boot Actuator & observing an application?","bodyEn":"Actuator exposes operational endpoints: /health (liveness/readiness groups) /metrics (Micrometer → Prometheus) /info, /loggers... Integrates with Micrometer + OpenTelemetry to export metrics/traces. Remember to protect sensitive endpoints (auth) + only expose what's needed. Actuator + Micrometer is a facade that exports metrics to Prometheus/Datadog/OTel (swap the backend without changing code) the /actuator/health liveness vs readiness groups map directly to K8s probes: readiness includes dependencies (DB, broker), liveness only means 'the process is alive' (avoids a restart loop when the DB is slow) custom HealthIndicator for business dependencies (GDS/PSP); protect /env, /heapdump with auth wire up Micrometer Tracing + OTel → trace_id flows across services; export custom business metrics (booking_failed_total) to alert on SLOs Operational trap: high tag cardinality (userId, bookingId) 'blows up' the Prometheus time-series → only tag bounded dimensions (status, endpoint). Use exemplars to jump from a metric to its trace.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q7"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q8","surface":"track","en":"Validation (Bean Validation) and centralized error handling?","bodyEn":"Use @Valid + annotations (@NotNull, @Size, custom constraints) to validate at the boundary (controller/DTO). Catch errors centrally with @ControllerAdvice + @ExceptionHandler → return a standard error structure (RFC 7807 problem+json). Separate syntactic validation (at the boundary) from business rules (in the domain). syntactic/format at the boundary: DTO + @Valid + JSR-380 (@NotNull, @Email, @Positive), validation groups for create vs update business rules in the domain: e.g. 'seat count ≤ passenger count' belongs to the aggregate, not an annotation custom constraints for reusable rules (valid airport code) centralized error handling with @RestControllerAdvice → problem+json with field errors Trap: validating on the client and then trusting it — you must re-validate on the server (never trust input) cross-field validation (return date > departure date) uses a class-level constraint; don't cram complex business rules into an annotation","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q8"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q9","surface":"track","en":"How do you configure HikariCP in Spring Boot for a high-traffic service?","bodyEn":"Treat HikariCP as a bounded admission-control boundary, not a knob that makes the database faster. With spring-boot-starter-jdbc or spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, Spring Boot brings HikariCP onto the classpath and prefers it when auto-configuring a pooled DataSource. Configure the pool only after defining a fleet-wide connection budget and a deadline hierarchy. spring: datasource: hikari: pool-name: booking-write maximum-pool-size: 12 illustrative; derive from measured capacity + fleet budget connection-timeout: 1500 milliseconds waiting to borrow; not connect/query time validation-timeout: 500 must be < connectionTimeout max-lifetime: 1740000 only if infrastructure max age is known to be 30 min keepalive-time: 120000 only when idle paths otherwise expire connections The numbers above are a configuration-shape example, not defaults or a recommendation. In particular: derive maximumPoolSize from the database's measured sustainable concurrency and the aggregate budget across peak replicas, rollout surge, jobs, migrations, and every DataSource; if minimumIdle is left unset, Hikari defaults it to maximumPoolSize, effectively keeping a fixed-size pool; set it lower only when fewer idle sessions are worth slower refill during a burst; connectionTimeout bounds only the wait for a pool slot. JDBC connect, socket/read, statement, transaction, lock, and HTTP-request deadlines are different controls; maxLifetime retires a physical connection after it is returned; keep it below a known maximum-age limit. keepaliveTime pings only idle connections and must be lower than maxLifetime; leakDetectionThreshold reports a connection checked out for too long. It is evidence of a possible leak or long transaction, not proof of either; prefer JDBC4 Connection.isValid(); do not add connectionTestQuery unless the driver actually lacks JDBC4 validation. Transaction boundary Never perform remote HTTP, broker, filesystem, or user-waiting work after a transaction has acquired a connection. A 100 ms SQL operation inside a 5 s transaction occupies a pool slot for roughly 5 s, so the bottleneck is hold time rather than query time. Watch active, idle, pending, acquisition timeouts, acquisition duration, usage duration, and creation failures through Actuator's jdbc.connections and Hikari-specific metrics. A growing pending queue means demand is waiting; it does not tell you whether the cause is a small pool, a saturated database, a lock, a leaked connection, or a slow external call inside the transaction. Generic sizing logic lives in (25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q1), while exact Hikari parameter semantics are in (25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q3). The Spring lifecycle and engine-specific resource effects are covered in (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q12), (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q13), and (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q14). Fleet budget worksheet: let B_db be the database connection/process budget. Subtract administrative, replication, other-application and failover reserves. Require Σ(replicas_i × poolMax_i) + nested-connection headroom + jobs ≤ B_db − reserves. The per-pod pool value is an allocation bound, not a normal-size recommendation. Test maxSurge and the largest REQUIRES_NEW demand before choosing it.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q9"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q10","surface":"track","en":"Testing strategy for a Spring Boot service: test pyramid, slice tests, Testcontainers, contract tests?","bodyEn":"Follow the test pyramid: lots of unit tests (JUnit 5 + Mockito, pure domain logic, milliseconds) fewer: integration tests on top: a handful of e2e tests Spring slice tests (@WebMvcTest, @DataJpaTest) load only part of the context → fast test real infrastructure (DB, Kafka) with Testcontainers (Docker) instead of H2, to stay close to production contract tests (Spring Cloud Contract/Pact) keep provider–consumer compatibility from breaking when APIs change Anti-pattern: cramming every test into @SpringBootTest (loads the whole context & 8594; slow, brittle) instead of using slice/unit tests. H2 lies: its dialect/locking/data types differ from MySQL/Postgres & 8594; tests pass green but production fails; Testcontainers runs the real engine inside Docker. Flaky tests are usually caused by time/order/thread dependencies → replace Thread.sleep with Awaitility, isolate data per test fintech/OTA: test idempotency (calling twice with the same key & 8594; one effect) + concurrency (two threads debiting money/booking a seat at once & 8594; correct final balance) property-based testing (jqwik) for money calculations (BigDecimal, rounding); measure quality with mutation testing (PIT), not just coverage % Runnable integration boundary — let the real PostgreSQL constraint answer: @Testcontainers @SpringBootTest class BookingDbIT { @Container @ServiceConnection static PostgreSQLContainer postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer(\"postgres:18-alpine\"); @Autowired JdbcTemplate jdbc; @Test void duplicateReferenceIsRejected() { jdbc.execute(\"create table booking(reference text primary key)\"); assertThat(jdbc.update(\"insert into booking values (?)\", \"B-42\")).isEqualTo(1); assertThatThrownBy(() -> jdbc.update(\"insert into booking values (?)\", \"B-42\")) .isInstanceOf(DataIntegrityViolationException.class); } } test deps: spring-boot-testcontainers, testcontainers-postgresql ./mvnw -Dtest=BookingDbIT test @ServiceConnection supplies connection details to Boot; the test does not copy a random mapped port into properties. The container is the Testcontainers 2.x type, which is no longer generic (26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q1). Benefit: it exercises real DDL, driver error translation and database semantics. Cost: Docker startup and schema setup are slower, so keep fast domain tests and reserve this layer for boundaries where an in-memory substitute could lie. Boundary matrix — each layer proves only part: Boundary Test It does not prove Domain unit/property tests for rules and money proxy, driver, database or serialization behavior Spring proxy/context proxy, self-invocation, commit/rollback and async/reactive-context integration tests real database locking or broker delivery Web/JPA slice controller binding, validation, repository mapping and error shape full auto-config, network, failover or production topology Real infrastructure Testcontainers with the target database/driver and failure injection all replicas, rollout surge or every provider version Public contract JSON snapshots, compatibility and provider-consumer contract tests authorization correctness unless fields and principals are asserted Operational failure timeout, cancellation, pool exhaustion, crash-after-commit and shutdown tests recovery in an untested deployment configuration Use this boundary map with the integration and failure-test guidance in (26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q2).","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q10"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q11","surface":"track","en":"What platform baseline and migration plan should a Spring service use in the Boot 4 generation?","bodyEn":"Version banner (reviewed 2026-08-23): this unit uses the Spring documentation's Boot 4.x/3.5 lines, Spring Framework 7.x, current Hikari/JDBC documentation, MySQL 8.4, current PostgreSQL, and Oracle Database 26ai as evidence scopes. These are not the service's target versions. Pin exact Java, Boot, Spring, Hikari, JDBC-driver and database versions in the build. Boot 4/Spring 7 examples are separate from Boot 3 defaults. Support windows set the deadline (spring.io, as of 2026-10-25): Boot 3.5: open-source support ended on 30 June 2026 Boot 4.0: open-source support ends on 31 December 2026 Boot 4.1: open-source support runs to 31 July 2027; 4.2 is planned for November 2026 A service still on 3.5 gets no open-source patches unless it buys commercial support, so moving to Boot 4 is a security deadline, not a modernisation project. Pick one production JDK—often JDK 25 when the organization and vendors support it—and test that exact runtime. A library may need a lower --release target than the service that consumes it. Generation change What to verify Jakarta EE 11 level Servlet 6.1, JPA 3.2, Bean Validation 3.1, container/provider compatibility, and any remaining javax. dependencies Jackson 3 default tools.jackson package/group changes, changed defaults, custom modules, persisted JSON, and public wire-contract snapshots; annotation packages remain a documented exception JSpecify nullness compiler/static-analysis configuration, application annotations, generated code, overrides, generic types, and Kotlin interop Java 25 runtime agents, bytecode tools, native libraries, container image, GC/heap flags, CDS/CRaC/AOT assumptions, and preview features kept behind an explicit policy Migration sequence: Record the current BOM, direct/transitive dependencies, Java runtime, removed/deprecated APIs, serialization fixtures, and operational baselines. Upgrade through a supported path; let Boot dependency management own versions unless a documented reason requires an override. Compile with all warnings visible, run unit/slice/contract/integration tests, and compare generated OpenAPI, JSON, database mappings, security rules, startup, memory, and latency. Canary with rollback compatibility for database, messages, and stored JSON. Patch to the latest supported maintenance release rather than freezing on .0. JSpecify describes nullness; it does not insert runtime validation or make existing business data non-null. Jackson 2 and 3 can coexist temporarily for migration, but dual stacks increase ambiguity and dependency risk—give the bridge an owner and removal date. Do not assume a major Spring upgrade requires adopting every new feature; first preserve behavior, then introduce API versioning, resilience, AOT, or new observability capabilities as separately measurable changes.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q11"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q12","surface":"track","en":"How does Spring Boot wire HikariCP, and what happens from @Transactional to Connection.close()?","bodyEn":"Spring Boot's DataSourceAutoConfiguration creates a pooled DataSource only when JDBC classes and a usable driver are present and the application has not supplied its own DataSource. Boot prefers HikariCP when it is available. Common credentials and URL bind from spring.datasource.; implementation-specific properties bind from spring.datasource.hikari.. Defining a custom DataSource makes the relevant auto-configuration back off, so multiple data sources also require explicit pool names, transaction managers, @Primary decisions, and metrics tags. The imperative request path is approximately: HTTP request -> TransactionInterceptor -> JpaTransactionManager / DataSourceTransactionManager -> HikariDataSource.getConnection() -> Hikari proxy Connection -> JDBC driver + socket/TLS -> database session/backend Connection.close() -> close tracked statements / roll back unfinished work / reset dirty state -> return the physical connection to Hikari's idle collection -> physically close only on eviction, failed validation, retirement, or pool shutdown getConnection() borrows an idle physical connection or creates one while the pool is below maximumPoolSize. When every connection is in use, the caller waits up to connectionTimeout. The object returned to application code is a proxy: calling close() releases it to the pool instead of closing the database socket. Spring's transaction manager or Hibernate binds the borrowed connection to the current imperative transaction, so repository calls inside that boundary share it until commit/rollback and cleanup. This has three important consequences: Transaction duration is connection hold time once a connection has been acquired. Query speed alone cannot explain pool pressure. Hikari must reset observable state such as auto-commit, read-only, transaction isolation, catalog/schema, and network timeout before reuse. Application code must still close ResultSet/Statement/Connection deterministically; pool cleanup is a safety net, not an ownership model. spring.datasource.connection-fetch=lazy in the Boot 4.1 generation can wrap the auto-configured pool so a transaction that never creates a JDBC Statement never borrows a physical connection. It does not make SQL non-blocking or increase database capacity. What exists in application memory per physical connection? Hikari keeps pool bookkeeping and proxy state; the driver owns protocol/session objects, prepared-statement metadata or caches when enabled, authentication/TLS state, and socket buffers/file descriptors. Active ResultSet, LOB buffers, ORM entities, Hibernate's first-level cache, and DTO materialization usually dominate request memory, but they are request/transaction state rather than an idle Hikari entry. A larger pool indirectly permits more of that active work to exist concurrently. Hidden connection retention @Transactional around a remote call, a streaming response, an unbounded cursor, or slow application computation can retain a connection long after SQL stops. Open EntityManager in View extends the persistence context and permits late SQL during rendering, which can hide N+1 queries and acquire connections outside the intended service boundary; prefer spring.jpa.open-in-view=false plus explicit fetch plans/DTOs. Leaving minimumIdle unset gives Hikari a fixed-size target because its default equals maximumPoolSize. That favors burst responsiveness but makes every pod try to maintain its full allotment, which matters during cold starts and rolling deployments. An elastic pool (minimumIdle lower than max) reduces idle sessions but can create a connection storm when traffic jumps; measure authentication/TLS/session-setup latency and rate-limit rollout surge. Choose startup behavior deliberately with initializationFailTimeout: a service that cannot do useful work without the database may fail fast, while a component designed to start degraded may let the pool retry in the background. On graceful Spring shutdown, the HikariDataSource bean closes and physical connections are released; kill -9, OOM-kill, or a partition cannot rely on that lifecycle, so database-side idle/session limits and TCP keepalive remain necessary. The proxy and transaction mechanics above connect directly to (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q2). Pool size and virtual-thread count are independent: cheap waiting threads do not create extra database capacity.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q12"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q13","surface":"track","en":"Why does the same Hikari maximumPoolSize have different memory and connection effects on MySQL, PostgreSQL, and Oracle?","bodyEn":"maximumPoolSize = N means up to N physical JDBC connections for that pool, but a \"connection\" is not the same server-side object in every engine. It also has no universal MB cost: idle-session baseline, active statement memory, driver caches, TLS/socket buffers, execution processes/threads, parallel workers, and server configuration all vary. Engine Server execution/session model Memory and limit implications Inspect / scale deliberately MySQL 8.4 The classic handler associates a connection with a server thread; optional thread-pool/proxy products can change scheduling. Each connection needs thread/session state such as a stack, network buffers and statement-digest memory. Sort/join/read/result buffers grow for active statements rather than being one fixed pre-allocation. max_connections is a slot ceiling, not a throughput target. Threads_connected , Threads_running , Performance Schema and sys.memory_by_thread_by_current_bytes . Preserve an administrative path instead of consuming every slot. PostgreSQL 18 Each client connection has a separate backend OS process unless an external pooler/proxy multiplexes clients. Every backend has private state, and PostgreSQL also sizes some shared resources from max_connections . work_mem is per sort/hash operation—not per connection—and several plan nodes, parallel workers and sessions can multiply it; temp_buffers is session-local and allocated on demand. pg_stat_activity , OS RSS/PSS and pg_backend_memory_contexts for the current backend. A pooler can reduce backend count, but transaction pooling restricts session state. Oracle 26ai Dedicated mode gives a user process its own server process; shared server uses dispatchers and a smaller server-process set; DRCP pools server processes/sessions across middle tiers. Dedicated sessions place session memory in PGA; shared server moves UGA into SGA shared/large pool; DRCP changes the count that owns server/session memory. Capacity is constrained by PROCESSES , SESSIONS , PGA/SGA targets and workload—not just a JDBC pool value. V$SESSION , V$PROCESS_MEMORY , PROCESSES / SESSIONS and dispatcher/DRCP views. For RAC FAN, planned draining or Application Continuity, evaluate Oracle UCP instead of assuming a generic pool provides those events. Useful snapshots—run with appropriate privileges and correlate them with application pool metrics: -- MySQL: connected vs executing, then current allocation by server thread SHOW GLOBAL STATUS WHERE Variable_name IN ('Threads_connected','Threads_running'); SELECT user, current_allocated FROM sys.memory_by_thread_by_current_bytes ORDER BY current_allocated DESC LIMIT 10; -- PostgreSQL: session state; the memory-context view describes this backend SELECT state, count() FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE backend_type = 'client backend' GROUP BY state; SELECT name, total_bytes FROM pg_backend_memory_contexts ORDER BY total_bytes DESC LIMIT 10; -- Oracle: session/server mode and aggregate process-memory categories SELECT server, status, COUNT() FROM v$session WHERE type = 'USER' GROUP BY server, status; SELECT category, SUM(allocated) FROM v$process_memory GROUP BY category; On the JVM side, every physical connection adds at least a Hikari entry, driver object graph, socket/file descriptor, and possibly per-connection prepared-statement metadata/cache. The expensive amplification is often indirect: N active connections permit N concurrent result sets, ORM persistence contexts, LOB reads, JSON mappings, and database work areas. Therefore JVM heap, native/kernel socket memory, and database memory must be measured separately under the same concurrency. Source scope and unresolved target: these rows are scoped to the named documentation lines, not every release, driver or topology. If the application is not pinned to these targets, treat the DB-specific answer as unresolved and verify the exact server and driver manuals before sizing. MySQL 8.4 server system variables PostgreSQL current connection settings and pgJDBC documentation Oracle Database 26ai PROCESSES Do not multiply a folklore constant Statements such as \"one PostgreSQL connection costs 10 MB\" or \"Oracle always costs more\" erase engine version, extensions, session settings, query shape, shared/dedicated mode and allocator behavior. Measure idle baseline, then the delta for representative active queries and parallelism; use PSS rather than summing process RSS when shared pages matter. Connection-local caches multiply silently. A prepared-statement cache of K entries on each of N connections can create roughly N×K client entries plus server statements/cursors, while Hibernate's first-level cache is per persistence context and follows transaction/request cardinality instead. Size each cache from hit rate and memory/cursor limits, not because a blog calls it \"best practice.\" Database-side multiplexing changes semantics. PostgreSQL transaction-pooling proxies, Oracle shared server/DRCP, and vendor proxies reduce backend/process churn, but session variables, temporary tables, prepared statements, advisory locks, package state, and failover behavior may no longer have connection affinity. Verify the application's state assumptions before inserting another pooling layer, and choose one client-side pool rather than accidentally wrapping a pool inside another pool. Finally, an idle connection is not free, but an active query usually has a much larger and more variable footprint. max_connections/PROCESSES answers \"can another session log in?\"; load testing answers \"how many active queries meet the SLO without CPU, I/O, lock or memory collapse?\" Those are separate limits.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q13"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q14","surface":"track","en":"How should you budget connections and align Hikari, JDBC, and database timeouts for autoscaling, rolling deploys, and failover?","bodyEn":"Start with two ceilings: the database's hard session slots after administrative, replication, HA, maintenance, and other-application reserves; and its measured safe active-query concurrency for this workload. The lower operational boundary wins. Then calculate the worst live fleet, not today's replica count: fleet connection ceiling = Σ(max live instances during rollout/failover × every pool maximum) + batch workers + schedulers + migration clients example only: (8 replicas + maxSurge 2) × (12 primary + 8 replica) + 4 jobs = 204 The arithmetic exposes risk; it does NOT prove that 204 is safe. Count read/write pools, tenant-specific pools, sidecars, canaries and old/new versions during a rollout. If the ceiling exceeds the assigned budget, reduce per-pod pools or rollout surge, cap replicas, consolidate tenant pools, or introduce a semantics-compatible server/proxy pool. Raising the database's hard connection setting without CPU, memory, I/O and lock evidence can convert a clean \"too many connections\" rejection into a slow cluster-wide collapse. Timeouts are layered contracts, not synonyms: Control What it bounds Failure meaning Hikari connectionTimeout Waiting to borrow an existing/new pool connection Local admission queue exceeded its budget; no SQL necessarily ran Driver connect/login timeout DNS/TCP/TLS/authentication and physical session creation Cannot establish a new physical connection Statement/database query or lock timeout SQL execution, lock wait, or server cancellation policy The operation exceeded its DB budget; transaction may require rollback Driver socket/read timeout Network read waiting for database bytes Transport is silent; the driver may close or invalidate the connection Spring transaction timeout Transaction-manager lifetime/resource deadline; it may propagate to JDBC statements Can mark/roll back the transaction when enforced; it does not interrupt arbitrary Java or remote calls HTTP/request deadline End-to-end service budget, including response serialization Must be outside the inner budgets and leave time to return a controlled error maxLifetime / keepaliveTime Connection lifecycle/idle liveness, not request execution Retirement or health maintenance; neither cancels a slow query Allocate inner budgets so pool wait + database work + application work fit inside the transaction/request deadline; reserve time for cleanup and error propagation. Configure driver properties explicitly and verify their units: MySQL Connector/J uses milliseconds for connectTimeout/socketTimeout pgJDBC uses seconds for the similarly named properties Oracle documents oracle.jdbc.ReadTimeout in milliseconds and has separate Oracle Net connect/keepalive controls Copying the same numeric map across drivers can therefore change a 2-second intent into 2 milliseconds or leave it effectively unbounded. Diagnose before increasing the pool: Observed together Likely class of problem Next evidence/action active=max, pending/acquire timeouts rise, usage duration rises, DB CPU modest Long transaction, remote I/O inside transaction, lock wait, streaming result, N+1, or leaked checkout Trace connection usage; inspect thread dumps, transaction spans, lock waits and slow queries; use leak detection only as supporting evidence active=max and DB CPU/I/O/locks plus query p99 are saturated Database is the bottleneck Tune query/index/model, shed/load-limit work; a larger pool usually adds queueing and contention total remains below max while creation failures rise DNS/TLS/auth, firewall, exhausted DB slots, listener/proxy or failover problem Inspect the actual driver exception and DB listener/session counters; pool sizing is not the first fix many idle connections across pods; outage begins during scale/rollout minimumIdle , maxSurge, multiple pools or tenant cardinality multiplied the fleet Recompute the worst-case ceiling; slow/rate-limit startup and preserve administrative reserve Failover is a transaction event A dead connection cannot preserve an in-flight local transaction. MySQL's autoReconnect is not a safe transaction-recovery strategy; Hikari can replace a broken connection for later work, but the caller must handle the failed unit. Retry only at an idempotent business boundary and treat a lost response after commit as an unknown outcome to reconcile, not proof that rollback occurred. Align maxLifetime a little below a documented proxy/database maximum age, with Hikari's per-connection attenuation preventing mass retirement. Use keepaliveTime or TCP keepalive when infrastructure removes idle connections; do not confuse an idle timeout with maximum age. A low minimumIdle reduces steady idle sessions but makes recovery and scale-out depend on connection-creation capacity, so test a database restart and a full rollout, not only steady load. For Oracle RAC event-driven invalidation, service draining and FAN/Application Continuity, Oracle UCP may be the appropriate client pool; Spring Boot supports choosing a pool implementation. Pick one pool intentionally rather than stacking UCP under Hikari. For PostgreSQL/MySQL proxies or multi-host drivers, verify read/write routing, DNS refresh, session state and transaction outcome semantics under real failover. The operational dashboard should correlate jdbc.connections/hikaricp active-idle-pending-acquire-usage-creation metrics with database sessions, active queries, CPU/I/O, locks, query p99, JVM heap/native memory, pod count and deployment surge. That correlation distinguishes an application queue from a database saturation event. See the incident-level aggregate-budget trap in (25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q2) and query-side remediation in (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4). Budget equation to keep visible during rollout: define B_effective = B_db − administrativeReserve − otherAppReserve − failoverReserve. Require Σ(replicas_i × poolMax_i) + jobs + nestedConnectionHeadroom ≤ B_effective at the worst live replica count, including maxSurge. This is a resource-allocation bound, not a recommendation for normal pool size. Load-test active-query capacity separately.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q14"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q15","surface":"track","en":"How does HikariCP interact with transaction isolation, and how do you prevent connection state leaking between requests?","bodyEn":"HikariCP is a pool, not an isolation layer. A physical connection can serve many requests, so the isolation setting belongs to a transaction or workload contract, not to whichever request borrowed the connection last. Set a global default only when every transaction can safely use it. Otherwise use @Transactional(isolation = ...) or JDBC setTransactionIsolation before the first statement, and verify that the transaction manager owns the connection. Prefer transaction-scoped database settings where supported, such as PostgreSQL SET LOCAL. Do not leave session-level SET commands, read-only flags, schema changes, or catalog changes for the next borrower. Close the logical connection on every path. Hikari returns it to the pool and resets the standard JDBC state it tracks; it cannot reset arbitrary session variables, temporary tables, or application side effects. Pool sizing and isolation solve different bottlenecks. maximumPoolSize limits simultaneous physical database work; connectionTimeout limits waiting for a slot. A larger pool cannot fix lock contention or a database that is already saturated. When debugging, correlate Hikari active, pending, and usage-duration metrics with database locks, active queries, and transaction traces. If a request sees the wrong isolation, inspect transaction boundaries and session-state leakage before changing pool size. A safe test matrix uses two concurrent transactions, the deployed driver and database version, commit and rollback paths, and a connection returned to the pool between cases. Assert both visibility behavior and state reset. Session-state leakage test: borrow a connection, set isolation/read-only/schema and one arbitrary session variable, create any temporary object used by the application, then close it. Borrow again through the same pool. Assert standard JDBC state and application-owned session state are reset or explicitly cleaned; verify the temporary object and transaction outcome cannot affect the next borrower. Repeat on success, rollback, exception, cancellation, pool eviction and the exact deployed driver/database versions. Hikari's standard reset is a safety net, not proof that arbitrary SQL/session variables were undone.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q15"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q16","surface":"track","en":"How do you make a Spring Boot dependency upgrade reproducible instead of trusting a green build?","bodyEn":"A green compile proves only that one resolution and test path succeeded. Pin the Java, Spring Boot, Spring Framework, plugin, build-tool, driver, database, and container versions that the service supports. Use the official BOM or dependency constraints, commit the lock/resolution policy where the build tool supports it, reject dynamic versions, and inspect the resolved dependency tree for convergence, exclusions, duplicate APIs, and known vulnerabilities. Make repository and artifact trust explicit: use approved repositories, checksum/signature/provenance verification where available, an SBOM, and one promoted artifact rather than rebuilding every environment. Test the upgrade with: startup/condition-report checks real database semantics through Testcontainers or an equivalent environment public JSON/contract tests and migration/rollback tests a canary A build cache may reduce time, but it must not be treated as a correctness oracle; cache keys and task inputs must be reproducible. Measure build performance as a workload: cold versus warm daemon, dependency resolution, configuration, compilation, annotation/code generation, tests, packaging, cache hit/miss, CI runner and network. Gradle configuration cache and task cache require cache-safe build logic, so profile and fix invalidation rather than copying a flag. Record the before/after report, rollout owner, rollback artifact, and dependency/security review. The upgrade is complete only when runtime behavior and reproducibility—not only compile time—are preserved.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q16"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q17","surface":"track","en":"How do you route read-only transactions to a replica in Spring, and why does the obvious `AbstractRoutingDataSource` version keep hitting the primary?","bodyEn":"The idea: one DataSource that picks primary or replica per transaction, keyed on @Transactional(readOnly = true). The trap: the routing key is read when the connection is fetched. The transaction manager fetches it in doBegin, before the read-only flag is published to TransactionSynchronizationManager — so every transaction routes to the primary, and nothing fails to tell you. The fix, built in since Spring Framework 6.1.2: LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy defers the physical fetch to the first statement, and can route read-only transactions itself: @Bean @Primary DataSource dataSource (DataSource primary, DataSource replica) { var proxy = new LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy(primary); proxy. setReadOnlyDataSource (replica); // used inside readOnly = true transactions return proxy; } ──── @Transactional (readOnly = true ) List<Order> history (CustomerId id) { … } // replica @Transactional Order place (PlaceOrder cmd) { … } // primary What routing does not give you: read-your-writes. A user who places an order and lands on \"my orders\" reads a replica that may not have it yet. Decide that per read, not per bean: route that read to the primary, or wait until the replica has replayed past the write (06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q2). Make the replica refuse writes on its own readOnly = true is a hint, not a guard. Point the replica pool at a read-only standby or a SELECT-only user, so a mislabelled method fails loudly instead of diverging. the older recipe is still everywhere: subclass AbstractRoutingDataSource, return a key from isCurrentTransactionReadOnly(), and wrap that in LazyConnectionDataSourceProxy. It works for the same reason; the 6.1.2 property removes the subclass. per the proxy's Javadoc, Connection.setReadOnly is left untouched and auto-commit and isolation are expected to match the primary — so configure the replica's Hikari pool with readOnly=true and the same defaults. an inner readOnly method called from a writing transaction joins it and runs on the primary connection already bound — the safe direction. REQUIRES_NEW with readOnly = true opens a second connection, from the replica pool; size both pools for that (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q14). replica lag belongs on a dashboard before it becomes a bug report, and a reporting query that runs for minutes on a replica can delay replay itself (06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q1). split reads only when primary read load is measured to be the constraint: by the tier yardstick a replica is not needed at T1 and worth it at T2–T3 once reporting reads compete with writes.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q17"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q18","surface":"track","en":"`@Scheduled` with fixedDelay, fixedRate or cron — and when do you need Quartz or a job table instead?","bodyEn":"The three triggers: fixedDelay: wait n after the previous run finished. The gap is guaranteed; the cadence drifts. fixedRate: start every n from the previous start. A slow run delays the next one; runs of the same task still never overlap. cron: wall-clock times in a named zone. A time missed while the app was down, or while the last run overran, is simply skipped. @Scheduled (fixedDelay = 30 , timeUnit = TimeUnit.SECONDS) // poll a table @Scheduled (cron = \"0 0 2 \" , zone = \"UTC\" ) // sec min hour day month weekday @Scheduled (fixedRateString = \"${jobs.heartbeat:PT1M}\" ) // interval from config Two defaults that surprise: Spring Boot's scheduler has one thread (spring.task.scheduling.pool.size=1): one slow job delays every other job in the application. nothing is persisted: a restart forgets what ran, and every replica runs every job (25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q4). When Quartz earns its place: jobs and triggers stored in the database (spring.quartz.job-store-type=jdbc), a cluster where exactly one node fires each trigger, and an explicit misfire policy for the runs the cluster missed while it was down. Need Reach for an in-process tick: poll, refresh, clean up @Scheduled , plus a lease once replicas > 1 schedules created at run time, per tenant or user, that survive restarts Quartz JDBC JobStore, clustered a backlog of work items with retries and an audit trail a job table drained with SKIP LOCKED cross-system workflows with timers that last days a workflow engine, not a scheduler Quartz clustering trusts the clock Quartz requires node clocks within a second of each other, and a non-clustered instance started on the same tables corrupts the schedule. Both are documented; both happen in staging. Quartz decides which node fires a trigger by taking a database row lock. isClustered=true and a unique instanceId per node (AUTO) are the minimum; clusterCheckinInterval decides how fast a dead node's jobs are recovered. misfire is the question @Scheduled never asks. A trigger later than misfireThreshold (60 s by default) applies its instruction — fire once now, or do nothing until the next time. Choose per job; the \"smart policy\" default is not a decision. @DisallowConcurrentExecution keeps one job key from running twice at once across the cluster; without it, a run that outlasts its interval overlaps itself. Boot auto-configures a SpringBeanJobFactory, so jobs get dependencies injected; but a job is instantiated per execution, so state belongs in the JobDataMap or the database, never in fields. with spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true, Boot runs @Scheduled on a virtual-thread scheduler, so one slow job no longer blocks the rest — the overlap and duplicate-run questions remain. Quartz's eleven QRTZ_ tables ship as DDL per database; let Flyway own them like the rest of the schema (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q7). cron is zone-sensitive: schedule in UTC or name the zone, and decide what 02:30 means on a DST night (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q7).","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q18"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.bean-lifecycle-request-flow.q1","surface":"track","en":"The full bean lifecycle: what are the 8 stages, and where do the intervention hooks sit?","bodyEn":"The container doesn't just new an object — it runs 8 stages for every bean. Knowing the exact order is how you answer 'when does the AOP proxy get created' and 'why has @PostConstruct already seen its dependencies'. Instantiation — calls the constructor (constructor injection happens right here) Populate properties — sets fields/setters: @Autowired, @Value Aware callbacks — BeanNameAware → BeanFactoryAware → ApplicationContextAware BeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeInitialization — runs for every bean Initialization — @PostConstruct → afterPropertiesSet() (InitializingBean) → init-method BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization — where the AOP proxy gets wrapped Ready to use — the singleton sits in the container, serving requests Destruction — @PreDestroy → destroy() (DisposableBean) → destroy-method Trap Prototype beans: the container creates them and lets go — it never calls the destroy callback. Resources (files, sockets, executors) in a prototype bean must be closed manually. @PostConstruct runs in step 5, before the proxy is wrapped in step 6 → this at that point is the raw bean; calling your own @Transactional/@Async/@Cacheable method from within @PostConstruct has no effect if you need to run after the context is fully ready, use ApplicationRunner/CommandLineRunner or @EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class) BeanFactoryPostProcessor modifies the BeanDefinition (metadata — before instantiation); BeanPostProcessor modifies the instance (after instantiation). Confusing the two is a classic mistake BPPs are initialized very early → don't inject business beans into a BeanPostProcessor: that bean gets created before the infrastructure is ready and permanently loses its chance to be post-processed (no proxy → loses @Transactional) @PreDestroy only runs on a graceful context shutdown (SIGTERM + shutdown hook); kill -9/an OOM-kill skips it → don't put critical business logic in a destroy callback destruction order is the reverse of initialization order; to control start/stop order use @DependsOn or SmartLifecycle (with getPhase()) Where each hook sits among the 8 stages: @Component class GdsClient implements InitializingBean, DisposableBean { GdsClient (HttpPool pool) { / ... / } // 1 · constructor injection @Autowired void setClock (Clock c) { } // 2 · populate field/setter @PostConstruct void warmUp () { // 5 · after Aware and BPP.before this . refreshCache (); // @Cacheable here has NO EFFECT: proxy not wrapped yet } public void afterPropertiesSet () { } // 5 · right after @PostConstruct public void destroy () { } // 8 · only runs on a graceful context shutdown } // The proxy (@Transactional, @Async, @Cacheable) is wrapped in stage 6 — AFTER stage 5. // If you need to run once everything is truly ready: @EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class).","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.bean-lifecycle-request-flow.q1"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.bean-lifecycle-request-flow.q2","surface":"track","en":"How does a request flow through DispatcherServlet? Filter vs Interceptor vs ArgumentResolver vs MessageConverter?","bodyEn":"DispatcherServlet is the front controller: it doesn't handle business logic, only dispatches. 1 HandlerMapping — finds the handler matching @RequestMapping (path, method, header, produces/consumes) 2 HandlerExecutionChain — runs Interceptor.preHandle() in registration order 3 HandlerAdapter — InvocableHandlerMethod invokes the controller method 3.1 ArgumentResolver — deserializes JSON → DTO, @Valid, @RequestParam, @PathVariable 3.2 → 3.4 — Controller (@RestController) → Service (@Transactional) → Repository 3.5 ReturnValueHandler + 3.6 HttpMessageConverter — Jackson serializes the object → JSON 4 postHandle — only runs when there is no exception 5 HandlerExceptionResolver — maps exceptions → HTTP status (@ControllerAdvice) 6 afterCompletion — always runs, even on error → cleans up MDC/ThreadLocal Filter vs Interceptor: a Filter belongs to the Servlet spec, wrapping outside DispatcherServlet — it sees the raw request/response and can short-circuit (Security returns 401, rate limiting returns 429). An Interceptor belongs to Spring MVC, sitting inside DispatcherServlet, so it knows which HandlerMethod is about to run. Trap An exception thrown inside a filter (e.g. a broken token in the Security filter) does not go through @ControllerAdvice — it has already left DispatcherServlet. For a consistent JSON error body you must configure AuthenticationEntryPoint/ErrorController. @Transactional commits at step 3.3, but Jackson serializes at 3.6 — after the tx has already closed → touching a lazy collection at that point throws LazyInitializationException. Fix with a fetch join/DTO projection, not with spring.jpa.open-in-view (defaults to true: keeps the EntityManager open for the whole request → holds the connection longer and masks N+1 queries) a filter that sets MDC/correlation id must call MDC.clear() in a finally — worker threads are reused, so without clearing, this request's log lines carry the previous request's traceId filter order: @Order/FilterRegistrationBean.setOrder; Spring Security is actually one filter (FilterChainProxy) containing an internal chain of sub-filters produces/consumes participate in handler selection and determine which HttpMessageConverter is used; duplicate mappings for the same path fail immediately at startup (AmbiguousMappingException) ArgumentResolver/ReturnValueHandler are customized via WebMvcConfigurer — the standard place to implement @CurrentUser or a standardized response envelope","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.bean-lifecycle-request-flow.q2"},{"id":"03-spring-boot-deep-build.bean-lifecycle-request-flow.q3","surface":"track","en":"The JSON boundary is a contract. Which ObjectMapper decisions become breaking changes, and what leaks if you serialize an entity?","bodyEn":"Every field name, date format and number representation your service emits is part of the API. Jackson makes those choices by default if you do not. Decision Default risk What to set Field naming Java camelCase leaks into the contract pick one strategy explicitly and freeze it Unknown properties fails on a field the producer just added ignore inbound, so a new field is not an outage Dates epoch numbers instead of ISO-8601 register the java.time module, write ISO strings Big numbers a long id or BigDecimal loses precision in JavaScript serialize ids and money as strings Nulls null and \"absent\" look the same to the reader omit nulls, or model absence explicitly Unknown enum values a new value breaks old readers read unknown as a fallback constant // the DTO is the contract; the entity is an implementation detail record OrderView (String id, // string: JS loses precision past 2^53 String totalAmount, // string: money never a JSON float String currency, Instant placedAt) { } // ISO-8601 via the java.time module // @Bean Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilderCustomizer json () { return b -> b.failOnUnknownProperties( false ) // inbound: tolerate new fields .serializationInclusion(NON_NULL); } Serializing a JPA entity straight out can create three classes of bugs: it exposes fields no caller should ever see it triggers lazy loading during serialization — an N+1, or an exception once the transaction has closed it welds the storage schema to the public contract, so renaming a column becomes a client-breaking change Tolerant inbound, strict outbound Ignore unknown fields you receive so a producer can add one safely; never emit a field you are not prepared to keep. Removing an emitted field is the breaking change nobody notices until a client parses it. null versus absent matters most for PATCH: {\"note\": null} means clear it, {} means leave it alone, and both deserialize to null in a plain DTO (17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q3). polymorphic typing is where a JSON contract becomes a security decision — bind a sealed set of subtypes, never let the document name a class (13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q3). mapping DTOs is not busywork, it is the seam that lets storage and contract evolve independently (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2). always UTF-8, explicitly. String.getBytes() without a charset reads a platform default that differs between your laptop and the container. for large payloads, stream rather than materialise: a 200 MB response built in memory is a GC event and a p99 spike, not a serialization problem (15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q1). write a round-trip test per DTO — serialize, deserialize, compare. It catches format drift far earlier than a consumer does (26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q4). Entity exposure is configuration- and integration-dependent, so “never safe” is not a substitute for evidence. Prefer an explicit DTO/projection and a serialized-field allowlist. Add regression tests for payload size, lazy-load query count, PII/secret redaction and authorization by principal/tenant. Also test unknown/null/renamed fields and old-client compatibility; fail the test when an entity field appears accidentally.","contextEn":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build#question-03-spring-boot-deep-build.bean-lifecycle-request-flow.q3"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q1","surface":"track","en":"REST principles, HTTP method idempotency, and correct status codes?","bodyEn":"REST: resource + representation + operations via standard methods, stateless. Idempotency: GET/PUT/DELETE/HEAD should be idempotent (calling repeatedly gives the same result) POST is not (creates something new each time) → payment POST needs an Idempotency-Key Status codes: 200/201/204 success 400 bad input · 401/403 auth · 404 409 conflict · 422 is a common validation convention (document your 4xx policy) · 429 rate limit 5xx server error Using the right status code helps the client retry sensibly. PUT is idempotent (sets absolute state) but be careful with 'increment/decrement'-style PUT (not idempotent) distinguish safe (GET/HEAD don't change state) vs idempotent (calling multiple times = calling once) status codes are a contract with the client/monitoring: 409 (seat already held), a documented 422 validation policy, 429 + Retry-After, and 503 (degraded) returning 200 for everything with the error embedded in the body breaks retry/alerting/circuit-breaker logic ETag + If-Match for optimistic concurrency over HTTP Deep dive: business error codes in the body plus the correct status help the client automate retries — important for an OTA's B2B partner API. The hands-on build: going from a use case to resources and naming the URI (17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q1), then mapping non-CRUD actions like cancel/refund onto REST (17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q2).","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q1"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q2","surface":"track","en":"REST vs gRPC — what's the difference and when to use which?","bodyEn":"REST/JSON gRPC Format text (JSON) binary (Protobuf) Transport HTTP/1.1-2 HTTP/2 multiplexing Contract OpenAPI (optional) .proto generates code Streaming limited (SSE) 4 kinds, bidirectional Good for public API, browser/mobile internal low-latency microservices In practice, a gateway translating REST/JSON at an edge to gRPC for selected internal calls is a common topology, not a law. Browser support, public tooling, proxies, streaming, observability, organizational boundaries, or contract governance may lead to REST end to end, gRPC with grpc-web/transcoding at the edge, or another protocol. Choose per boundary and measured contract; gRPC is hard to use directly from a browser without grpc-web or a proxy. Bottom line REST often fits the public edge and gRPC often fits selected internal low-latency or streaming calls, but the boundary is workload- and organization-dependent. Protobuf supports compatible evolution through field numbers when its field-evolution rules are followed. Protobuf's wire representation can reduce bytes or CPU for some schemas, but the result depends on payload shape, compression, runtime, and client/library; benchmark the deployed path before claiming a win. safe evolution still depends on field-number rules: add compatible fields, and never reuse or reinterpret removed numbers. Gotcha: gRPC is hard from a browser (grpc-web/Envoy); L7 load balancing needs a proxy that understands HTTP/2 (sticky streams) propagate the deadline through every hop and retry only under an explicit idempotency policy and bounded budget (15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2), (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4) OTA deep dive: REST gateway toward partners/B2C, gRPC internally between search/pricing/inventory to cut latency & load","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q2"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q3","surface":"track","en":"What are gRPC's streaming modes?","bodyEn":"Four kinds: unary (1-1) server-streaming (1 request → many responses) client-streaming (many requests → 1 response) bidirectional (both directions at once) Good for large data feeds, telemetry, chat, real-time sync. Kind Signature Used for Unary 1 request → 1 response ordinary RPC calls Server-streaming 1 → N returning results incrementally: flight search, log tailing Client-streaming N → 1 batch upload, pushing metrics Bidirectional N ↔ N chat, two-way sync, real-time control server-streaming: returns multiple results incrementally (e.g. streaming flight search results — whichever airline responds first gets shown → faster UX than waiting to gather everything) client-streaming: uploading/batching metrics bidirectional: real-time sync/chat streaming uses HTTP/2 flow control to bound in-flight bytes, but a framework write returning is not proof that the peer received the data deadline expiry or cancellation can leave partial work, so propagate cancellation, check it in application code, and define retry/idempotency semantics (15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2), (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4) Gotcha: a long-lived stream holds connection resources → set a deadline/timeout; leaks if the client walks away and the server doesn't detect the cancellation if it's really just 1 request-1 response, unary is simpler — don't reach for streaming just because it 'sounds cool'","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q3"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q4","surface":"track","en":"What API versioning strategy avoids breaking clients?","bodyEn":"URI versioning (/v1/...) — explicit, widely used Header/media-type versioning — cleaner URLs but harder to debug Principles: backward-compatible changes (adding an optional field) → no new version needed classify the change against real clients: additive changes may remain compatible, while changing meaning, removing fields, or changing error semantics can be breaking a URI or header version is only a routing signal; contract checks, client observability, a parallel rollout, and a deprecation policy are what make retirement safe favor backward-compatible evolution: add optional fields, don't change the meaning of existing fields, don't remove fields still in use (tolerant reader) when a breaking change is unavoidable: run /v1 and /v2 in parallel, with a deprecation policy (Sunset header, partner notice, a deadline) URI versioning is easiest to debug/route for a public API; header/media-type versioning keeps URLs clean but is harder to test. Neither strategy makes an incompatible schema safe by itself. OTA B2B deep dive: partners change slowly → support old versions for a long time; use consumer-driven contract tests (Pact) to know who still depends on which field before removing it. The real process for retiring a version — announcement, Sunset header, measuring who's still calling it before turning it off (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4).","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q4"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you design pagination/filtering for a large list API?","bodyEn":"Use cursor/keyset pagination when the workload has a stable total order (a sequential column plus a unique id) and only next/previous navigation is needed. Large OFFSET can get slower and drift as rows change, but arbitrary page jumps or a search-engine token may justify another strategy. Return a 'next' cursor. Whitelist filtering/sorting to avoid injection and un-indexed queries. a large OFFSET scans-then-discards N rows → linearly slower, plus 'drift' when data changes between pages keyset/cursor relies on a sequential column plus an id tie-breaker: WHERE (created_at,id) < (?,?) ORDER BY created_at DESC,id DESC LIMIT n → stable & fast, uses the index well return an encoded (opaque) cursor with an expiry/freshness policy; bind it to the filter, sort, and tenant scope, and reject invalid or expired cursors. Whitelist filter/sort fields. OTA: read-heavy lists (bookings/transactions) should use keyset + a covering index; avoid an expensive total COUNT() Keyset limitation: you can't jump straight to 'page N' (only next/prev). If arbitrary page jumps are required, use offset/page number or Elasticsearch's search_after; for mutating data, document freshness/snapshot semantics because stable ordering alone cannot promise a repeatable snapshot. The DB side of this same question: why COUNT() is expensive and how keyset pagination performs on a table with tens of millions of rows (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q2).","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q5"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q6","surface":"track","en":"GraphQL vs REST — when does each fit?","bodyEn":"GraphQL: the client picks the fields, combining multiple resources in a single query → reduces over/under-fetching, good for complex UIs/many clients. Trade-offs: caching is harder risk of heavy queries (needs depth/complexity limits) N+1 at the resolver (needs deliberate batching/identity-map controls; DataLoader is a common tool, not a specification requirement) REST is simpler, with better HTTP caching for most CRUD use cases. REST GraphQL Who picks fields server client Round-trips for a complex screen many one HTTP/CDN cache built-in: ETag, TTL must build yourself: persisted queries Load limiting per endpoint per query depth and cost N+1 at the DB layer an extra layer at the resolver; use deliberate batching/identity-map controls Fits public APIs, B2B, CRUD multi-client apps, UIs aggregating many sources GraphQL: generic HTTP caching is harder because query shape matters (persisted queries and a query-aware cache can help), while heavy/deeply recursive queries need depth/complexity limits and cost-based rate limits. resolver N+1 needs deliberate batching or an equivalent data-access plan; no particular library is mandatory. REST: caches well (CDN/ETag), simple, clear status codes OTA deep dive: GraphQL fits mobile/web apps aggregating multiple sources (flights + pricing + seats + loyalty) in one round-trip; but B2B partner APIs + webhooks should still be REST. Don't use it for plain CRUD — the operational cost isn't worth it.","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q6"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q7","surface":"track","en":"How do you benchmark REST and gRPC fairly, and what does flow control actually protect?","bodyEn":"Do not turn a serialization anecdote into a universal multiplier. Define the same workload on both paths: unary versus streaming, payload-size distribution, compression, TLS, connection reuse, concurrency, client/server language, CPU limits, and p50/p95/p99 targets. Measure goodput, wire bytes, CPU/GC, queueing, cancellation, and error rate with the same topology. Protobuf and HTTP/2 may help an internal call, but a gateway translation, proxy, small messages, or a downstream bottleneck can dominate. A benchmark without these controls is a local observation, not an architecture rule. For streaming, HTTP/2 flow control bounds bytes in flight per stream and connection; it is not a durable queue and not a delivery guarantee. A write accepted by the framework may still be buffered. Use bounded application buffers, explicit deadlines, cancellation propagation, and a read strategy that cannot deadlock a bidirectional stream. Ownership Use q3 for the four streaming shapes and 15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2 for transport behaviour. The decision is evidence-led: choose the protocol that meets the measured contract with the smallest operational surface.","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q7"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q1","surface":"track","en":"Reactive programming (WebFlux): what are Mono/Flux, the event loop, and backpressure?","bodyEn":"WebFlux is built on Reactor: Mono (0-1 element), Flux (0-N) non-blocking processing on a small number of event-loop threads (Netty) when there's no data, the thread isn't held → handles high I/O load with few threads Backpressure: a slow consumer tells the producer 'send only so much' (request(n)), avoiding memory overflow — a feature reactive streams have built-in that blocking models don't. Gotcha just one blocking call in a reactive chain blocks the event-loop thread handling that task. Enough such calls can stall available capacity and collapse tail latency; keep the chain non-blocking end to end (R2DBC driver, WebClient, etc.) or isolate blocking work explicitly. Reactor has both cold publishers and hot publishers. Many factory chains are lazy/cold until subscription, while shared sinks, processors, and some adapters can be hot. forgetting to subscribe = a cold chain never runs; verify the source and sharing policy before reasoning about execution. flatMap runs concurrently and doesn't preserve order; concatMap preserves sequential order backpressure strategies under overload: buffer/drop/latest/error publishOn (switches the downstream thread) vs subscribeOn (switches the source thread) Fatal gotcha: a blocking call (JDBC, RestTemplate) blocks the Netty event-loop thread assigned to it; enough blocked workers can stall unrelated requests and tail latency. Prefer non-blocking dependencies (R2DBC, WebClient), or use subscribeOn(boundedElastic) with queue, deadline, and concurrency limits to isolate unavoidable blocking work. Deep dive: reactive stack traces are hard to read → turn on Hooks.onOperatorDebug()/checkpoints while debugging. Executable proof that a blocking call left the event loop: @Test void blockingWorkUsesBoundedElastic() { Mono<String> result = Mono.fromCallable(() -> { Thread.sleep(20); // stand-in for blocking JDBC return Thread.currentThread().getName(); }) .subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic()) .timeout(Duration.ofMillis(200)); StepVerifier.create(result) .assertNext(name -> assertThat(name).contains(\"boundedElastic\")) .verifyComplete(); } add reactor-test, then run the test with Maven or Gradle Put subscribeOn immediately after the blocking source; placing it later can leave subscription work on the event loop. Benefit: Netty workers stay available. Limit: boundedElastic moves and bounds blocking work; it does not make JDBC non-blocking. Its queue can still saturate, so keep a deadline, downstream concurrency limit and pending-work metrics.","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q1"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q2","surface":"track","en":"Reactive (WebFlux) vs Virtual Threads — which should you pick in 2025–2026?","bodyEn":"Three ways to handle 'lots of I/O with few OS threads': WebFlux (Reactor) Vert.x Virtual Threads Model reactive streams event loop + callback/Future blocking style, JVM unmounts on supported I/O Code Mono/Flux, harder to read callback/Verticle, prone to callback hell sequential, easiest to read Backpressure built-in (request(n)) manual (pause/resume/fetch) no reactive demand signal; use bounded queues/semaphores Blocking code unsafe on the event loop; isolate it unsafe on the event loop → executeBlocking/worker blocking I/O is supported; still bound CPU, pinning, and downstream Ecosystem Spring reactive its own Vert.x ecosystem, polyglot reuses JDBC/Hibernate Debugging operator context/checkpoints; async traces need discipline event-loop context and async traces sequential stack style, but inspect pinning and executor saturation Spring Boot is not a fourth I/O model: it packages and configures Spring MVC or WebFlux. With a Spring Boot release that supports the feature (for example, Boot 3.2+ on Java 21+), spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true can make the Servlet/Tomcat/Jetty path use virtual threads; it does not turn MVC into WebFlux. For a 2025–2026 decision: choose from the workload and dependency contract, not from a popularity ranking: existing imperative Spring MVC + JDBC/JPA with mostly request-response and I/O waits → try MVC + virtual threads, then measure end-to-end non-blocking clients/drivers, streaming, or a real reactive demand contract → WebFlux explicit event-loop/multi-reactor control, polyglot services, or Vert.x integrations → Vert.x Bottom line Pick the model by dependency and semantics: virtual threads simplify blocking-style request-response code WebFlux makes reactive streams and backpressure explicit Vert.x gives direct event-loop/toolkit control None of them removes the need to bound database connections, HTTP concurrency, broker consumers, or partner quotas. WebFlux & Vert.x are both event-loop-based: a blocking call stalls the event-loop thread handling that task; enough blocked workers can stall the service. Isolate it on a bounded scheduler, executeBlocking, a worker verticle, or another explicit executor. Vert.x is lightweight, polyglot, has a distributed event bus — strong for gateways/high-throughput; but callback/Future style is prone to callback hell (mitigated with RxJava/Mutiny) Reactor is built for streaming composition (time/window operators) with backpressure built in; Vert.x backpressure must be handled manually (ReadStream.pause/resume/fetch) Virtual threads let teams write sequential blocking code and reuse JDBC/Hibernate. They have no reactive demand signal, so use connection pools, semaphores, bounded queues, and deadlines to protect scarce resources for Kafka/RabbitMQ listeners, listener threading and broker semantics are independent: a Spring listener may use platform or virtual threads, while a Vert.x callback arrives on an event loop Kafka bookkeeping is a committed offset; RabbitMQ bookkeeping is a consumer ack. Neither is business completion unless it follows the idempotent side effect Keep reactive/Vert.x for a true stream, backpressure, or event-loop contract; use MVC/virtual threads when imperative blocking dependencies make the simpler model the safer fit. The shared rule remains: never block the event loop.","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q2"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q3","surface":"track","en":"Reactor: error handling and commonly used operators?","bodyEn":"Operators: map/flatMap (transform/chain), zip (combine), filter, retryWhen (retry with backoff), timeout. Error handling: onErrorResume (fallback), onErrorMap (convert exception), doOnError (side-effect logging). Errors propagate down the chain → handlers must be placed at the right point. onErrorResume (fall back to another publisher — e.g. cached pricing when the GDS errors out) onErrorReturn (a default value); onErrorMap (convert to a domain exception) retryWhen(Retry.backoff(n, duration).jitter(...)) (backoff+jitter, filtering which error types are retryable) timeout + fallback to avoid hanging; doOnError is side-effect only (logging), not handling combine with resilience4j (circuit breaker/bulkhead) at the reactive layer Deep dive: fanning out to multiple GDSs in parallel → flatMap + a per-call timeout + onErrorResume(empty) so 'one airline failing doesn't take down the whole search'.","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q3"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q4","surface":"track","en":"WebSocket vs SSE — how do you choose for real-time features?","bodyEn":"SSE WebSocket Direction one-way server→client full-duplex, both ways Transport HTTP (text/event-stream) ws:// (upgraded from HTTP) Reconnect automatic (Last-Event-ID) must be coded yourself Good for feeds, notifications, price pushes chat, collaborative apps, games Choose based on whether you need two-way communication: SSE is cheaper and simpler if you only need server push. SSE is one-way, with auto-reconnect via Last-Event-ID to resume — enough for 'push real-time price/seat/booking status updates' SSE gotcha: browser HTTP/1.1 per-origin connection limits (often six) are user-agent-specific; HTTP/2 multiplexing changes the constraint, and proxy buffering can still block the stream. test the actual browser, HTTP version, and proxy before choosing a connection budget (disable buffering in Nginx when appropriate). WebSocket gotcha: doesn't auto-reconnect (the client must code reconnection & state recovery itself), needs heartbeat ping/pong to detect dead connections, an LB/proxy idle-timeout silently kills the connection & 8594; keep periodic pings scaling: sticky sessions are needed only when connection state is local. A shared pub/sub layer or externalized session/resume state can remove that dependency; test LB routing and reconnect behaviour (see 16.9).","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q4"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q5","surface":"track","en":"What benefits does a contract-first approach with OpenAPI/Protobuf bring?","bodyEn":"Define the API contract first (OpenAPI for REST, .proto for gRPC): generates client/server stubs, mocks, contract tests lets FE/BE teams work in parallel reduces misunderstandings The contract is the shared language between BA/FE/designers. contract-first → a 'shared language' for BE/FE/partners: generates stubs, mock servers; contract tests catch breaking changes in CI before deploy FE/BE work in parallel off the spec; versioning & docs are auto-generated (Swagger UI) OTA/B2B: the spec is a commitment; use consumer-driven contracts (Pact) to know which fields partners depend on before changing them Gotcha: the spec drifts from the code if it's generated once and then hand-edited. A contract-first gate should: lint the schema and run breaking-change checks compile generated clients/servers execute provider/consumer contract tests run a runtime integration test Either generate from the spec or fail when generated output is stale. The contract is exactly a Published Language in DDD terms — the shared language at the boundary between two bounded contexts, so it must be treated as a real contract, not something edited on a whim (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3). The contract's lifecycle after it's signed off: adding an optional field breaks nobody; changing the meaning of a field or removing it requires a retirement process with a Sunset header and measuring who's still calling it (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4).","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q5"},{"id":"04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q6","surface":"track","en":"How do you keep a reactive service safe when a downstream or stream is slower than the producer?","bodyEn":"Backpressure is a signal to reduce demand, not permission to create an unlimited queue. Put a bound on every buffer and concurrency gate, then choose the overflow contract deliberately: wait within a deadline drop only data that is explicitly disposable return a degraded result reject with a retryable error Propagate cancellation when the client disconnects or the deadline expires. A reactive operator can preserve a signal while an adapter, broker client, database pool, or bounded-elastic scheduler still saturates; instrument queue depth, pending demand, in-flight work, downstream latency, cancellation, and dropped or rejected items. Never block the event loop to make a slow dependency look synchronous. Isolate unavoidable blocking work on a bounded scheduler, cap its queue, and protect the dependency with a bulkhead. If the consumer needs a replayable history rather than live flow control, use a durable cursor or job contract instead of relying on a live stream. request(n) controls demand between reactive publishers; it does not make a database, network, or business side effect cancellable automatically. Cancellation and partial-work semantics belong in the adapter contract.","contextEn":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux#question-04-rest-grpc-webflux.reactive-webflux.q6"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q1","surface":"track","en":"What is an index? What index data structures exist and what are they used for?","bodyEn":"Index = an auxiliary structure that finds data fast without a full scan. Trade-off: costs storage + slows down writes (index must be updated). Index families: B+Tree: default RDBMS — equality + range + sort Hash: O(1) equality, useless for range/sort LSM-tree: sequential writes (Cassandra, RocksDB) — write-heavy Bitmap: low-cardinality columns in a data warehouse Inverted index: full-text search (Elasticsearch/Lucene) GiST/GIN (Postgres): JSONB, full-text, geo Bottom line there is no 'best' index — pick based on access pattern. B+Tree is the most balanced choice for OLTP. every index slows down INSERT/UPDATE and costs storage (write amplification) → don't index carelessly; weigh the read/write ratio OTA: the booking table needs a composite B+Tree for history; searching flights/destinations uses Elasticsearch (inverted); 'seats remaining' can use a bitmap/partial index Bottom line: 'there's no best index, only an index that fits the pattern.'","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q1"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q2","surface":"track","en":"The B+Tree algorithm: why is lookup O(log n), and what are page split and fanout?","bodyEn":"B+Tree is balanced: every leaf sits at the same depth. each node = 1 page (e.g. 16KB in InnoDB) holding many keys → very large fanout (hundreds) the tree is only 3–4 levels deep for millions/billions of rows → a lookup takes only a few page reads data lives at the leaves; leaves are chained into a linked list → range scans are cheap Page split: when an insert fills a page, it splits in two → costs I/O + causes fragmentation. inserting sequential keys (auto-increment) → limits splits inserting random keys (UUID v4) → splits everywhere Bottom line 'short tree + high fanout + linked leaves' is why B+Tree is fast for both point lookups and range queries. A sequential PK avoids splits. Quantifying it: a 16KB InnoDB page, BIGINT PK 8B + pointer 6B → fanout ~1000. 3 levels ≈ 1000³ = 1 billion keys → every data point is ~3 page reads from the root (upper levels are usually already in the buffer pool) this is why adding an index turns a scan of millions of rows into a handful of page accesses Write amplification: every secondary index adds extra writes on INSERT/UPDATE and can trigger page splits → a table with many indexes writes noticeably slower. random keys (UUIDv4) → splits and fragmentation drive down the buffer pool hit ratio (measure with SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS) comparing B+Tree (good reads) vs LSM (good writes) is a great closing point Seeing splits instead of arguing about them: -- Split/merge counters, per table. Watch the delta over a load test, -- not the absolute value. SELECT name, count_star FROM performance_schema.events_waits_summary_global_by_event_name WHERE name LIKE '%btr_search%' ; -- -- The blunt but decisive measure: how fat is the index on disk? SELECT table_name, index_name, ROUND (stat_value 16 / 1024 , 1 ) AS size_mb FROM mysql.innodb_index_stats WHERE stat_name = 'size' AND table_name = 'bookings' ; -- PRIMARY (BIGINT auto-inc, 10M rows) ~ 380 MB -- PRIMARY (CHAR(36) UUIDv4, 10M rows) ~ 1100 MB <- splits + a 36B key Two effects compound and people usually credit only the first: the UUID key is 4.5x wider (36B as CHAR(36) vs 8B), and random insertion order leaves pages ~50–60% full instead of the ~93% InnoDB reaches on sequential inserts. Every secondary index carries a copy of that PK, so the cost multiplies by index count. Store UUIDs as BINARY(16), and prefer a time-ordered id (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q3).","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q2"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q3","surface":"track","en":"What's the difference between a clustered and a secondary index in InnoDB? What are the consequences for choosing a PK?","bodyEn":"Clustered index = the PK; leaves hold the entire row (one per table) Secondary index: leaves hold the indexed value + the PK → after looking up the secondary index you must 'lookback' to the clustered index for the remaining columns Consequences for choosing a PK: a short, sequential PK (BIGINT auto-increment) beats a random UUID because: every secondary index stores the PK → a long PK bloats every index a UUID causes page splits If you must use a UUID, consider UUIDv7 (time-ordered). since secondary indexes store the PK: the bigger the PK, the more every secondary index bloats → costs buffer pool RAM, lowers the hit ratio; changing a PK value is extremely expensive → a PK should be immutable OTA: the booking table is queried heavily by (user_id, created_at) → a covering index (user_id, created_at) INCLUDE (status, pnr) (Postgres) so 'booking history' doesn't need a lookback when the optimizer would have to look back too many rows, it may abandon the index for a full scan → a covering index saves p99","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q3"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q4","surface":"track","en":"Composite index (a,b,c): leftmost-prefix rule and covering index?","bodyEn":"Index (a,b,c) can be used for leftmost prefix: a, a,b, a,b,c, or a + range(b) NOT for b alone or b,c after a range column, the columns that follow can no longer filter Covering index: contains every column the query needs (including SELECT) → Using index, no lookback. Column order: equality columns first, range/sort columns after. Postgres uses INCLUDE to add non-key columns to the leaf (covering without growing the key) OTA: (user_id, status, created_at) serves 'a user's active bookings over time'; add INCLUDE(pnr, amount) so the list doesn't need a lookback → saves p99 Nuance: MySQL 8.0+ has index skip scan, which can sometimes use (a,b,c) for a condition only on b when a has low cardinality — but don't rely on it, still design around leftmost. MySQL 8 also has descending indexes for mixed-direction ORDER BY. key_len tells you how much of the index was really used: CREATE INDEX ix ON bookings(user_id, status, created_at); -- user_id INT NOT NULL(4) · status CHAR(10) utf8mb4 NULL(41) · created_at DATETIME(5) -- -- key_len=4 -> only user_id used EXPLAIN SELECT FROM bookings WHERE user_id= 7 ; -- -- key_len=45 -> user_id + status EXPLAIN SELECT FROM bookings WHERE user_id= 7 AND status= 'ACTIVE' ; -- -- key_len=45, NOT 50: a range stops the prefix, created_at only filters rows EXPLAIN SELECT FROM bookings WHERE user_id= 7 AND status> 'A' AND created_at> '2026-01-01' ; Read key_len before believing an index \"works\": it is the only field that proves how many columns deep the prefix actually went.","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q4"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q5","surface":"track","en":"In which cases does an index NOT get used even though the column has one?","bodyEn":"'Has an index' ≠ 'index gets used'. Cases where the index is skipped: a function/expression on the column: WHERE YEAR(d)=2026 (rewrite as a range, or use a functional index) leading wildcard LIKE '%x' implicit type conversion (a string column compared to a number, mismatched collation) — a trap with string-typed account codes low selectivity (a boolean column) → the optimizer picks a full scan OR across different indexed columns; !=/NOT IN over a large set Always verify with EXPLAIN. Five common production traps: OR can sometimes be rescued by index merge but usually performs worse than a UNION ALL of two separately indexed queries IN (...) that's too large → the optimizer switches to a full scan sort/group order that doesn't match the index order → Using filesort skewed statistics after loading a large batch of data → the optimizer mis-estimates → run ANALYZE TABLE parameter sniffing/plan cache picking a bad plan for a skewed-distribution value Final verdict: EXPLAIN ANALYZE to compare actual rows vs estimated — a large gap means bad statistics or column correlation. Implicit conversion — the trap that survives code review: -- account_code VARCHAR(20), indexed -- Bind an int and MySQL casts the COLUMN, not the literal: EXPLAIN SELECT FROM accounts WHERE account_code = 10023 ; -- type: ALL key: NULL rows: 2841190 -- Warning: Cannot use index 'ix_code' due to type or collation conversion -- EXPLAIN SELECT FROM accounts WHERE account_code = '10023' ; -- type: ref key: ix_code rows: 1 Run SHOW WARNINGS straight after EXPLAIN — MySQL names the index it refused to use and why. In JPA this bites when the entity field is Long but the column is VARCHAR; the same applies to a utf8mb4_general_ci column joined against utf8mb4_unicode_ci.","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q5"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q6","surface":"track","en":"How do selectivity and cardinality affect the optimizer's decisions?","bodyEn":"Cardinality = number of distinct values Selectivity = cardinality/total rows (closer to 1 = 'filters more strongly') The optimizer uses these statistics to estimate row counts → picks an index/plan. stale/skewed statistics → picks a bad plan → run ANALYZE TABLE to refresh a low-selectivity column is less useful to index on its own the optimizer relies on histograms/statistics to estimate row counts, then picks the index/join order/method stale/skewed statistics (after a bulk load, after a large delete) → picks a bad full scan or nested-loop → ANALYZE (Postgres)/ANALYZE TABLE (MySQL) a skewed-distribution column (status='SUCCESS' at 99%) needs a histogram so the optimizer knows 'SUCCESS' isn't selective while 'FAILED' is parameter sniffing/plan cache keeps the plan that's optimal for this value but bad for another → monitor estimated vs actual rows (EXPLAIN ANALYZE)","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q6"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q7","surface":"track","en":"You inherit a table keyed by a random UUIDv4 and inserts keep getting slower — what happens in InnoDB versus PostgreSQL, and what can you change without replacing the key?","bodyEn":"The B+Tree half is the same everywhere: a random key lands on a random leaf, so inserts split pages all over the tree instead of packing the right edge (05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q2). What differs is what else each engine pays for that. InnoDB PostgreSQL Row placement the PK is the table, so rows scatter with the key the heap still appends; only the index scatters Secondary indexes each one stores the PK — a 36-byte key bloats all of them entries carry a 6-byte row pointer, not the PK The hidden cost buffer-pool churn: the working set becomes the whole tree full-page writes — the first touch of a page after a checkpoint copies all 8 KB into WAL What to measure buffer pool hit rate; index size in mysql.innodb_index_stats idx_blks_read vs hit in pg_statio_user_indexes; WAL bytes per commit Four changes, in increasing order of disruption: Store 16 bytes, not 36. BINARY(16) in MySQL, the native uuid type in PostgreSQL — never CHAR(36)/varchar. Same values, less than half the bytes in every index that carries the key. Change the generator, not the column. UUIDv7 (RFC 9562) puts a millisecond timestamp in the high bits, so new rows append at the right edge again. Same 128-bit column: existing v4 rows stay valid and nothing has to be migrated. Split the public id from the clustered key. Keep the UUID as a unique secondary key and cluster on a BIGINT — the InnoDB answer when the UUID has to stay in URLs and payloads. Blunt the write amplification. A larger max_wal_size / checkpoint_timeout in PostgreSQL means fewer checkpoints and so fewer full-page writes; REINDEX CONCURRENTLY reclaims bloat that already exists. Do not \"fix\" it by shuffling bytes MySQL's UUID_TO_BIN(uuid, 1) reorders the time fields of a version 1 UUID; a v4 has no time fields to move, so on random values the swap flag buys nothing at all. Order the changes by what they cost the application. Steps 1 and 2 touch storage and generation only — no API change, no downstream contract broken. Step 3 changes what a foreign key points at, so every join, report and integration follows it. A time-ordered id leaks its creation time by design. Anyone holding the id knows when the row was created, and two ids sort in creation order. That is exactly what the index wanted, and exactly what a password-reset token or a bearer secret must not have — those need an unguessable random value with no ordering at all. Measure your own before and after. Insert throughput at a fixed concurrency, plus index size and WAL/redo volume for the same batch, is enough to show whether the key really was the constraint. Numbers copied from someone else's benchmark describe their hardware and their fill factor (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q3).","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q7"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q1","surface":"track","en":"The 4 isolation levels and their corresponding anomalies? MySQL vs Postgres defaults?","bodyEn":"Level Prevents Remaining anomaly READ UNCOMMITTED — dirty read READ COMMITTED dirty non-repeatable + phantom (default PG/Oracle) REPEATABLE READ + non-repeatable phantom (InnoDB blocks it via next-key) SERIALIZABLE every anomaly most expensive dirty = reading something not yet committed non-repeatable = re-reading a row and getting a different value phantom = re-reading a set and getting a different row count Default: MySQL = RR, Postgres/Oracle = RC. RC (PG/Oracle) takes a new snapshot per statement → sees the latest committed data but is non-repeatable within a tx RR (MySQL/InnoDB) snapshots once for the whole tx; InnoDB also blocks phantoms with next-key locks on a locking read SERIALIZABLE is expensive (predicate locks/aborts) Going deeper for fintech: most money-moving logic runs at RC/RR + deliberate row locks (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE / conditional update) for contention hotspots, instead of raising isolation globally (expensive + more deadlocks). Choose isolation per transaction, don't flip on SERIALIZABLE 'just to be safe'. Version baseline The MySQL examples in this topic target InnoDB on MySQL 8.4 LTS. “MySQL 8” is not a sufficient production specification: record the exact server release, storage engine, isolation setting, sql_mode, topology, and relevant session overrides. As of 2026-10-25 the newer LTS line is MySQL 9.7 (GA April 2026), the last sequentially numbered release; later releases use calendar versions such as 26.7. 8.4 is still an LTS line, and 8.4 → 9.7 is a supported upgrade — skipping an LTS line is not. Innovation releases and other engines may expose different features or optimizer behavior, so verify claims against the deployed version.","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q1"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q2","surface":"track","en":"MVCC in InnoDB, and why doesn't MySQL's RR suffer phantoms the way the ANSI standard predicts?","bodyEn":"Every row has a trx_id + roll_pointer pointing into the undo log (older versions). On read, InnoDB builds a read view to pick the visible version: RR: builds the read view once, at the first SELECT (a consistent snapshot for the whole tx) RC: rebuilds it on every statement Phantoms: a plain SELECT → already immune thanks to the snapshot a locking read (FOR UPDATE, INSERT/UPDATE) → next-key lock locks the gap itself, blocking new rows from being inserted into the range Bottom line InnoDB's RR prevents phantoms via snapshot reads (plain reads) + gap/next-key locks (locking reads). A notable difference from the ANSI standard. a read view holds m_ids (active trxs), min/max_trx_id, creator_trx_id; a version is visible if the transaction that created it committed before the view was captured older versions live in the undo log; undo is only purged once no read view still needs it Trap: a transaction left open too long (a report running for 30 minutes at RR) blocks purging → undo bloats, the history list grows long, dragging down performance. Postgres stores tuple versions directly in the table → needs VACUUM to clean up dead tuples; autovacuum falling behind → 'table bloat' operationally: avoid long-running reads on the primary; push them to a replica/snapshot","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q2"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3","surface":"track","en":"Record / gap / next-key locks, and optimistic vs pessimistic locking?","bodyEn":"Lock types (InnoDB): Record lock: locks an index record Gap lock: locks the gap between records (prevents inserts → prevents phantoms) Next-key = record + gap Pessimistic vs Optimistic: Pessimistic (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE): locks immediately — fits high contention (e.g. hot inventory/balance) Optimistic (a version column): no lock, retry on conflict — fits low contention/distributed systems UPDATE account SET balance=balance-?, version=version+ 1 WHERE id=? AND version=?; -- 0 rows => retry gap locks only apply at REPEATABLE READ, not at READ COMMITTED (so RC deadlocks less but allows phantoms) a subtle source of deadlocks: a missing index forces UPDATE ... WHERE to scan and place a next-key lock over a large range → adding an index to narrow the locked range is a non-obvious way to 'reduce deadlocks' Hot row contention that's extremely high (e.g. a shared promotion voucher): both optimistic and pessimistic approaches choke → architectural solutions: shard the balance (split it into N sub-accounts that sum together) or funnel through a sequential per-account queue Why the version column usually lives on the aggregate root rather than being scattered across tables: the aggregate is the natural unit for optimistic locking (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2).","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q4","surface":"track","en":"Deadlocks: how do they happen, how does InnoDB handle them, and how do you prevent them?","bodyEn":"Deadlock = two transactions locking in opposite order, waiting on each other in a cycle. InnoDB detects the cycle (wait-for graph) and rolls back the transaction with the cheaper undo cost (error 1213); check SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS. Prevention: lock in a consistent order (always lock the smaller id first) — eliminates most transfer-style deadlocks keep transactions short; don't make network calls or wait on the user while holding a lock good indexes so fewer rows get locked (missing index → gap locks spread wide) prepare for idempotent retries at the app layer InnoDB rolls back the victim with the cheaper undo cost (error 1213); check the LATEST DETECTED DEADLOCK section. Four ways to reduce deadlocks: lock in a consistent order (lock the smaller account id first — eliminates the A→B vs B→A transfer deadlock) keep transactions short, no network calls/waiting on the user while holding a lock good indexes so fewer rows get locked (missing index → next-key locks spread over a wide range, more deadlocks) idempotent retries at the app layer (deadlocks are normal under high concurrency) Going deeper for OTA: transferring money/exchanging tickets between two accounts must order its locks; seat-hold uses a conditional update (doesn't hold a lock for long). The same problem at the application layer: dining philosophers, the 4 Coffman conditions, and ordering resources to break the wait cycle (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q3). Reproduce a real InnoDB deadlock in two MySQL clients: -- setup once CREATE TABLE deadlock_demo(id int PRIMARY KEY, balance int) ENGINE=InnoDB; INSERT INTO deadlock_demo VALUES (1,100), (2,100); -- client A -- client B START TRANSACTION; START TRANSACTION; UPDATE deadlock_demo UPDATE deadlock_demo SET balance=balance-1 SET balance=balance-1 WHERE id=1; WHERE id=2; -- wait until both first updates have completed UPDATE deadlock_demo UPDATE deadlock_demo SET balance=balance+1 SET balance=balance+1 WHERE id=2; WHERE id=1; -- one client receives ERROR 1213; inspect the victim and lock order SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS\\G Then change both transactions to lock id=1 before id=2: one may wait, but the cycle disappears. Operational effect: consistent lock ordering, short transactions and selective indexes reduce deadlocks. Recovery cost: retry the entire idempotent transaction with bounded jitter. A blind retry loop increases contention and can repeat an external side effect.","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q4"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q5","surface":"track","en":"Reading EXPLAIN: which columns to check first? What are the warning signs that something needs optimizing?","bodyEn":"Columns to check first: type: const > eq_ref > ref > range > index > ALL (ALL on a large table = red flag) key: the index actually used (NULL = none used) rows: estimated rows scanned Extra: Using index (covering, good); Using filesort/Using temporary = expensive, needs optimizing Use EXPLAIN ANALYZE (MySQL 8) to get real execution timings. In practice grab a real query from RDS pre-prod, run EXPLAIN, and be able to say 'rows went from 2M to 1.2k after adding an index' — far more convincing than theory alone. rows estimate way off from reality (EXPLAIN ANALYZE) → wrong statistics/column correlation Using temporary; Using filesort on a pagination query → revisit the index for ORDER BY, switch to keyset pagination type=index (full index scan) on a large table → the index isn't selective enough Postgres: EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) shows shared hit/read (real I/O) Tip: save a good plan as a baseline; when p99 spikes, diff the plans to catch a plan regression. One level deeper — EXPLAIN ANALYZE: estimated vs actual rows, and what loops and buffers tell you (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2).","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q5"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q6","surface":"track","en":"What common query optimization techniques exist beyond indexing?","bodyEn":"Beyond indexing: avoid SELECT (reads extra columns + breaks covering) keyset pagination instead of a large OFFSET batch instead of looping queries avoid functions on WHERE columns rewrite correlated subqueries as JOINs/window functions deliberate denormalization for read-heavy paths covering indexes; partitioning for pruning push heavy aggregation to a replica/OLAP Always measure with EXPLAIN ANALYZE + the slow query log. Interview trap Rattling off a list of techniques is a mid-level answer. A senior-level answer starts with measuring: which query dominates total time according to pg_stat_statements, what its plan looks like, and only then picking a technique. A 200ms query that runs once an hour is not worth touching, while a 20ms query running 5000 times a second is the real culprit — getting this backwards is the most common mistake. measurement tools: EXPLAIN ANALYZE + slow query log + pg_stat_statements OTA: a search/pricing screen with extremely heavy reads → cache + a dedicated read model (lightweight CQRS) instead of optimizing each query against the transactional table Principle: measure first, optimize whatever takes the most time, don't guess. Topic 18 contains the full treatment: Slow-query investigation: (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q1) Sargable WHERE clauses: (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q1) COUNT() and pagination on very large tables: (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q2) Pre-aggregation versus OLAP for heavy aggregation: (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q3)","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q6"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q7","surface":"track","en":"LSM-tree vs B+Tree — why do write-heavy databases choose LSM?","bodyEn":"First, separate the axes. SQL versus NoSQL describes a logical data model, API, query language, and transaction contract. B+Tree versus LSM-tree describes the physical access path: how keys, pages, sorted runs, logs, and background I/O are arranged. The axes cross: MySQL/InnoDB and PostgreSQL expose B-tree access paths, but their row layout is different. Cassandra and RocksDB use LSM-shaped components; Bigtable stores tablets as SSTable-based ordered data. MongoDB is NoSQL but its default WiredTiger table structure is B-tree-based. CockroachDB is SQL, while its Pebble key-value layer is LSM-based. So “SQL equals B+Tree” and “NoSQL equals LSM” are useful first guesses only, never a product rule. Original ByteByteGo visual, kept unchanged as a teaching aid. Open the source guide and the CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 license . The logos are examples, not a universal taxonomy. Physical question B+Tree LSM-tree Persistent shape Mutable, multi-level pages; separator keys route to child pages. Sorted memtable plus immutable SSTables or sorted runs. Foreground write Find and mutate a leaf page; a full page can split and propagate upward. Append to WAL or commit log, update a sorted memtable, then flush later. Point read Walk root to leaf; a secondary index may need a second row lookup. Check memtables and newest runs first; indexes and filters prune files. Range read Follow linked leaves in key order. Merge sorted sources; overlap and compaction policy affect the work. Background work Checkpointing, page split, merge, vacuum, or cleanup depending on engine. Compaction rewrites and merges sorted files, often consuming CPU and disk bandwidth. Main pressure Random page writes, split churn, and maintenance for every secondary index. Read, write, and space amplification plus compaction backlog. Local schematic connecting the three physical ideas: WAL, memtable/SSTable, and compaction. Compare it with the RocksDB Overview and Google Cloud Bigtable overview . B+Tree: pay at mutation time A B+Tree is a multiway tree balanced by page or block; it is not a binary tree with only two children. an internal page holds separator keys and page IDs a leaf holds a key plus a row pointer or record, and leaves are commonly linked so a range scan can continue in key order Because fanout is large, a point lookup usually travels from root to internal pages to a leaf; upper pages are often already in the buffer or cache, so there is no fixed I/O count for every database. “In-place write” means that the engine logically mutates an existing access path. When a page fills, a page split can create two pages and push a separator into the parent; the split can reach the root. WAL or redo, checkpointing, filesystem copy-on-write, and SSD behavior can turn that logical operation into several physical writes. Random keys usually have worse locality, and every secondary index adds another structure to maintain. Row layout depends on the engine: InnoDB normally stores the row in the leaf of the clustered primary-key index; a secondary leaf carries the secondary key and primary key, so a lookup may need a second step. PostgreSQL separates the heap table from its indexes; an index leaf points to a heap tuple. An index-only scan can avoid the heap fetch only when visibility information and other conditions allow it. LSM-tree: move work to the background A typical LSM pipeline is: append a record to the WAL or commit log for crash recovery; update a sorted memtable in RAM; when it fills, flush it into an immutable, sorted SSTable; compact sorted runs, dropping old versions or tombstones when it is safe. The flush path can be more sequential than a random foreground page update, but “an LSM only writes sequentially” is too simple: WAL, metadata, cache misses, and compaction still create I/O, and compaction reads and rewrites data repeatedly. The benefit is that many writes can be batched before they touch the on-disk structure. A point read usually checks the newest memtable, immutable memtables, and then SSTables from newest to oldest. At L0, files can overlap, so one key may require checking several files. In a leveled layout, higher levels are usually non-overlapping by key range, allowing a block index and Bloom filter to quickly rule out files that definitely do not contain a key; a Bloom filter is a probabilistic filter, not the final answer. Updates and deletes in an LSM usually create a new record or tombstone instead of editing every older copy in place. Compaction must preserve the version visible under the snapshot or transaction contract, and it can remove a tombstone only when no reader or level still needs it. That is why write amplification, read amplification, and space amplification are separate costs. Compaction policy is a tuning knob, not a footnote: leveled compaction limits overlap to make reads and space more predictable, at the cost of rewriting more data; size-tiered or universal compaction groups similarly sized runs, often reducing foreground rewrite pressure while allowing more read or space amplification; when compaction cannot keep up with ingest, L0 grows, pending bytes rise, and the engine may throttle or stall writes. Treat this as intentional backpressure, not as “the database randomly got slow.” An LSM also does not imply eventual consistency or weak ACID. RocksDB is a library; transaction, replication, quorum, and durability semantics come from the database layer around it and its WAL/fsync configuration. Choose by workload, not by label Choose B+Tree when you need many relational secondary indexes, point lookups and range scans with predictable latency, or a mixed read/write workload with good page locality. Consider LSM when ingest is large, key writes can be batched, append/update volume is high, and the system can budget CPU and I/O for compaction. Do not turn “ledger uses B+Tree, logs use LSM” into a law. Benchmark key distribution, hot keys, range scans, value size, warm and cold cache, p95/p99, crash recovery, and actual compaction rate. Measure WAL/redo bytes, page splits or bloat, L0 file count, pending compaction bytes, write stalls, read amplification, space per record, cache hit rate, restore time, and tail latency. If the invariant is an SQL transaction or constraint, an LSM does not replace the transaction boundary; that is a separate logical decision. Bottom line “B+Tree pays when it mutates a page; LSM pays when it reads many runs and compacts.” Use that sentence to ask the next questions: which workload, which amplification, which latency, and who operates the backpressure? Primary references: MySQL InnoDB physical structure · PostgreSQL B-tree · PostgreSQL page layout · RocksDB SST lookup · Cassandra storage engine · WiredTiger B-tree · CockroachDB Pebble","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q7"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q8","surface":"track","en":"ACID — what does each letter actually promise, and what inside the engine enforces it?","bodyEn":"Atomicity — all of the transaction or none of it. Enforced by the undo log: a ROLLBACK, or a crash mid-transaction, replays the undo records backwards until the row images are what they were before. Consistency — the transaction moves the database from one valid state to another. This is the odd letter out: the engine only enforces what you declared (PK, FK, UNIQUE, CHECK, NOT NULL). Everything else that \"valid\" means is your code (05-db-core-index-lock.modelling-the-schema.q1). Isolation — a concurrent transaction does not see another's half-finished work. Enforced by MVCC plus locks, and it is the letter you tune: the isolation levels are exactly that dial (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q1). Durability — once COMMIT returns, the write survives a crash. Enforced by the redo log / WAL: the log record is written and fsynced before the commit is acknowledged, and the dirty data pages are flushed later. Who really does the work: InnoDB (MySQL) PostgreSQL A undo log (rollback segments) no undo — the commit log ( pg_xact ) marks the transaction aborted and its tuples become invisible C declared constraints only declared constraints only I MVCC read views + record/gap/next-key locks MVCC snapshots + row locks; SSI at SERIALIZABLE D redo log, innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit WAL, synchronous_commit Durability is a setting, not a law innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit = 2 or 0 (and synchronous_commit = off) acknowledge the commit before the log reaches disk. Throughput goes up and you lose roughly the last second of already-acknowledged transactions on power loss — a legitimate trade for a metrics table, never for a ledger. the C in ACID is not the C in CAP — a favourite interview trap. ACID's C means \"your declared invariants still hold after the transaction\"; CAP's C means linearizability across replicas. so a single-node ACID database is perfectly consistent in the ACID sense while a stale replica serves an old read (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q4). A and D you get for free; I is what you pay for. That is why only isolation ships with levels — and why READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ is a real decision while \"atomicity level\" is not a thing. durability is per node unless you make it otherwise. With async replication a transaction is committed and durable on the primary and simply absent on the replica; a failover then loses it. semi-sync/synchronous replication is what extends D across machines, at the cost of the replica's round trip on every commit (06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q1). fsync per commit would cap throughput at the disk's flush rate, so engines use group commit: concurrent commits share one flush. This is why commit latency barely moves as concurrency rises, and why a single-threaded write loop is the slowest way to load data. ACID stops at the database boundary. Two services writing their own databases in one business operation are not atomic no matter how each transaction is configured — that is what Saga, Outbox and TCC exist for (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3). an application-level try/catch that swallows an exception without rolling back leaves the transaction open, holding its locks and its undo history until the connection is reaped. Atomicity is only as good as the code that decides when to roll back.","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q8"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q9","surface":"track","en":"How do you choose an isolation level for a real workflow instead of memorizing the anomaly table?","bodyEn":"Start with the invariant, then choose the narrowest isolation that protects it. Isolation controls which committed versions a transaction may observe; it does not replace a unique constraint, row lock, or conditional write. READ UNCOMMITTED permits dirty reads in InnoDB; PostgreSQL treats it as READ COMMITTED. Use it only when approximate diagnostics are acceptable. READ COMMITTED takes a fresh statement snapshot; it is the PostgreSQL default. A second SELECT in one transaction can therefore see a different committed value. REPEATABLE READ keeps one consistent snapshot after the first consistent read; it is the InnoDB default. Locking reads and write conflicts still need the right indexes and retry handling. SERIALIZABLE asks the engine to make concurrent transactions appear ordered. It may wait or abort with a serialization/deadlock error, so retry the entire idempotent transaction, not only the last statement. For a money or inventory invariant, prefer READ COMMITTED or REPEATABLE READ plus a unique constraint, SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, or one conditional UPDATE that checks the available state. Use SERIALIZABLE for a measured hotspot when the invariant is hard to encode otherwise, and budget for retries. Keep isolation per transaction or workload, set it before the first query, and test the deployed engine and version. Never add a remote call while holding the transaction open. A production review should: name the invariant; identify the read/write race; choose the constraint, lock, or conditional write; choose isolation; then load-test contention and retry 40001 or deadlock errors with bounded jitter.","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q9"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.modelling-the-schema.q1","surface":"track","en":"Why is a database constraint stronger than the same rule enforced in application code?","bodyEn":"Application checks run once per code path, on one instance, in one language. A constraint runs on every write, from every service, script and manual UPDATE, and it holds while two transactions race. -- the check-then-insert race: both pass the SELECT, both insert SELECT count () FROM users WHERE email = 'a@b.c' ; -- 0 in both sessions INSERT INTO users(email) VALUES ( 'a@b.c' ); -- 0 becomes stale here -- -- the constraint decides, and one transaction is told it lost ALTER TABLE users ADD CONSTRAINT users_email_key UNIQUE (email); Constraint What it guarantees Typical use NOT NULL the column always has a value everything that is not genuinely optional UNIQUE no duplicates, even under concurrency email, idempotency key, natural identifiers FOREIGN KEY no orphan rows ownership between tables in one database CHECK a value-level invariant amount > 0, status IN (…), ends_at > starts_at Partial unique index uniqueness under a condition one active row per key while keeping history The unique index is also the cheapest idempotency mechanism you have: insert the key first and let the duplicate fail, instead of asking whether it exists (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1). Where the rule belongs Business rules that need context stay in the domain. Invariants that must be true of the data itself — no matter who writes it — belong in the schema, because the schema is the only thing every writer passes through. constraints have a cost and it is worth knowing: a foreign key requires an index on the child side for cascading and takes a lock on the parent row during writes, which shows up under contention (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3). translate the violation, do not leak it. A unique-violation error code becomes a domain conflict and a 409, not a stack trace containing the constraint name (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q8). deferrable constraints are checked at commit rather than per statement, which is what makes circular references and reordering possible inside one transaction. sharding removes cross-shard foreign keys and cross-shard uniqueness — you get them back only by routing the key to one shard, which is why the shard key is a modelling decision, not an infrastructure one (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q1). a rule enforced only in code is a rule that a backfill script, an ops fix or a second service written later will not know about.","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.modelling-the-schema.q1"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.modelling-the-schema.q2","surface":"track","en":"Which schema decisions are hardest to reverse later — keys, soft delete, enums, and the nullable-everything habit?","bodyEn":"Adding a column is easy. Changing what a row means is not, because every query, index and integration already depends on it. Decision Cheap now Expensive later Natural key as PK no extra column the \"immutable\" business value changes and every FK follows Soft delete flag one boolean every query, unique index and report must respect it forever Enum stored as ordinal compact reordering the Java enum silently rewrites the meaning of old rows Everything nullable never blocks an insert no invariant is provable; every reader handles null JSONB for real fields schema-free velocity no constraints, no plan stability, ad-hoc keys across versions Keys. Prefer a surrogate primary key and put a UNIQUE constraint on the natural identifier. You keep the business rule without betting referential integrity on a value people rename. Physically, a random UUID as a clustered key scatters inserts across the tree; time-ordered ids avoid that (05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q3). Soft delete is the decision people regret most: it changes the meaning of every subsequent query. If you keep it, make it structural rather than a convention — a partial unique index so a deleted row does not block reuse of the key, and a filtered view, so forgetting the predicate is impossible. -- uniqueness that applies only to live rows CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_live ON users(email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ; -- -- store the enum as text, and constrain it status text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ( 'PENDING' , 'PAID' , 'CANCELLED' )) Deleted is a state, not an absence Once rows can be \"sort of there\", reads must say which they mean. Archiving to a history table keeps the live table honest and keeps the audit obligation satisfied (13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q3). keep audit columns from day one: created_at, updated_at and the actor. Reconstructing them afterwards is impossible, and they are what makes an incident timeline possible. JSONB earns its place for genuinely open-ended payloads — a webhook body, per-tenant settings. It stops being reasonable the moment you filter, join or constrain on a field inside it (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q4). model money as numeric with an explicit currency column, never a float and never \"assume VND\" (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q4). normalise first, denormalise deliberately with a written reason and a plan for keeping the copies in step (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q6). every one of these is reversible with an expand-and-contract migration; what makes them expensive is that consumers you do not control already read the old shape (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q7).","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.modelling-the-schema.q2"},{"id":"05-db-core-index-lock.modelling-the-schema.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do you keep a transactional source of truth and a derived projection from becoming two competing databases?","bodyEn":"Name the authority before naming the projection. The authority is the only place allowed to accept the business mutation and enforce the invariant; a cache, search index, materialized view, feed, or OLAP table is a read model unless the design explicitly makes it authoritative. Write path: commit the mutation and its version in the authority; do not rely on a best-effort second write publish the change through the appropriate transactional outbox or database-log/CDC boundary (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q7), (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) include an event identity, aggregate/key, operation, source version or position, schema version, and delete/tombstone meaning apply the projection idempotently, advance its watermark only after the durable effect, and expose lag/freshness Rebuild path: keep a snapshot boundary plus replayable changes, or document a separate backfill source. Build a new version, compare counts/checksums and domain invariants, then swap it. Never write business truth back from a stale projection just because it is convenient. A search result can be stale; a balance, entitlement, price, or inventory decision cannot silently use it. If the authority is unavailable, return an explicit unavailable/unknown result or a domain-safe fallback rather than inventing consistency from cache state. A projection is not made authoritative by being fast Speed, denormalisation, and a successful CDC pipeline do not transfer ownership of an invariant. Record the owner, freshness budget, repair procedure, and the action that must re-check the source of truth.","contextEn":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock#question-05-db-core-index-lock.modelling-the-schema.q3"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q1","surface":"track","en":"What's the difference between async/semi-sync/sync replication? What problems does replication lag cause?","bodyEn":"Async: primary commit returns immediately, replica applies later → has lag (reading from the replica shows stale data) Semi-sync: primary waits for ≥1 replica ack before committing → reduces lag/increases durability, trades off write latency Sync: waits for every replica → safest, slowest Lag consequence: 'I just transferred money, why hasn't my balance changed?' — from reading a lagging replica by mistake. Two kinds of lag: apply lag: replica replays slowly transport lag: network Measured with Seconds_Behind_Master (coarse) or a heartbeat table (more accurate). Semi-sync trap: if the only ack-ing replica dies, the primary can degrade to async → thresholds and alerting must be configured Aurora separates storage/compute → a 'replica' reads the same volume → millisecond lag (very different from binlog replication) OTA: 'history/loyalty' can read from a replica freely; 'wallet balance / just-created booking' must use read-your-writes back to the writer. Measuring lag honestly: -- Postgres: bytes behind, per replica. write/flush/replay are NOT the same; -- replay_lag is the one a stale read actually sees. SELECT client_addr, state, sync_state, pg_wal_lsn_diff ( pg_current_wal_lsn (), replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind, write_lag, flush_lag, replay_lag FROM pg_stat_replication; -- -- Heartbeat table: the only lag number that stays honest when the -- primary is idle (Seconds_Behind_Master reads 0 with no traffic). SELECT NOW () - ts AS real_lag FROM repl_heartbeat WHERE id = 1 ; Seconds_Behind_Master lies in two directions: it shows 0 on an idle primary even when the replica is far behind, and it measures the apply delay only, missing transport lag entirely. A heartbeat row updated once a second on the primary makes lag a wall-clock number you can alert on and put in an SLO.","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q1"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do you guarantee read-after-write consistency when reading from a replica?","bodyEn":"Read-your-writes: after a write, route that user's reads to the primary for a few seconds Version/LSN check: only read from a replica that has applied up to the user's commit position (Aurora supports this) Sticky/monotonic reads: keep the same replica as long as it's recent enough Fintech balances and core data read from the primary; only reports/older history/analytics get pushed to a replica. read-your-writes: route the just-written user's reads to the primary within a window of a few seconds (sticky by user/session) LSN/GTID gating: after writing, get the log position, and only read from a replica that has applied up to it (Aurora/Postgres) monotonic reads: use the same replica for one session so time doesn't 'go backwards' Trap: a load balancer round-robins across replicas with different lag → the user sees data jump around. Rule: data the user expects to see immediately after writing → primary. Runnable PostgreSQL lower-bound token — wait for replay, then fall back to primary: -- same primary session, immediately after COMMIT SELECT pg_current_wal_flush_lsn() AS required_lsn; -- e.g. 0/16B6C50 -- on the chosen replica SELECT pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() >= '0/16B6C50'::pg_lsn AS caught_up; -- f = wait with a short deadline, or route this read to primary -- t = the replica has replayed at least the required lower bound The flush LSN can include unrelated concurrent WAL, so it is a safe but sometimes conservative lower bound—not an exact “my row” position. Carry it only for the workflow that needs monotonic reads. Benefit: most later reads may still use replicas. Cost: token propagation and waiting consume latency; primary fallback consumes write-node capacity. Safety: never wait indefinitely or compare LSNs from unrelated timelines after failover.","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q2"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q3","surface":"track","en":"Master-slave vs multi-master — what's the trade-off?","bodyEn":"Single-primary Multi-master Writes 1 place multiple places Write conflict none must be resolved (LWW/CRDT/app) Weakness bottleneck + SPOF (needs failover) complex, easy to lose consistency Good for most systems (especially ledgers) high-availability multi-region Fintech rarely uses multi-master for a ledger. single-primary: simple, no write conflicts, but bottleneck + SPOF → needs automatic failover (Patroni/Orchestrator/RDS Multi-AZ) with the risk of split-brain (two primaries) if fencing is weak → needs quorum/STONITH multi-master: writes in multiple places (low latency across regions) but must resolve write conflicts (LWW loses data, CRDT works for mergeable types, or the app resolves it) Fintech/OTA: a ledger is almost never multi-master; multi-region is usually single-primary-per-region + partitioned by region (data residency) + replicated reads. Active-active is reserved for data that tolerates eventual consistency (catalog, session).","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q3"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q4","surface":"track","en":"Case: six nodes are added to a Cassandra/ScyllaDB cluster and some customers' balances start coming back empty. What are the candidates, and how do you separate them?","bodyEn":"Start with the symptom, because it is the clue: a missing row is not an error. A read served by a replica that never received the write returns no rows, so the application renders an empty balance instead of failing. The question is therefore always \"which replica answered, and why did it have nothing?\" Candidates, cheapest to check first: The consistency math — safety needs R + W > RF. At RF = 3, QUORUM on both sides overlaps (2 + 2 > 3); ONE/ONE does not, and a topology change widens the window in which that shows. Check the driver's default consistency level, not only the statement in front of you. A node that joined without streaming — auto_bootstrap: false, copied from a \"first node in a brand-new cluster\" runbook, lets a node claim its token ranges and serve reads with nothing behind them. The hole appears immediately and never heals on its own. Six at once — nodes are meant to bootstrap one at a time, each showing UN in nodetool status before the next begins. Forcing parallel joins past the consistent-range-movement guard lets ownership move before the data does. nodetool cleanup run too early — cleanup deletes what a node no longer owns, so running it before streaming and repair have converged removes rows the new owner never received. Expired hints — a replica down longer than the hint window (three hours by default) is not caught up by hinted handoff, and only a repair fixes it. The decisive test is per key, not per cluster: -- which replicas own this customer? nodetool getendpoints ledger balances 8f3c1a2e -- then ask with one replica, and watch the coordinator work CONSISTENCY ONE; TRACING ON; SELECT FROM ledger.balances WHERE customer_id = 8f3c1a2e; -- ONE finds nothing but ALL returns the row => a replica is missing data, -- rather than a write that never happened Raise consistency before you debug Moving reads and writes to LOCAL_QUORUM costs latency and stops customers seeing an empty balance, so the investigation can proceed without the incident continuing underneath it. Recovery order matters: repair first, cleanup after. nodetool repair -pr on every node, or a scheduled repair service, rebuilds what the new replicas are missing; only then does cleanup remove what the old nodes no longer own. A node that joined with auto_bootstrap: false is not repaired but replaced — decommission it and let it bootstrap properly. Know what your version promises about topology. The one-node-at-a-time rule exists because ownership used to change before the data did. ScyllaDB has moved topology changes onto Raft so the cluster coordinates joins itself, and Cassandra is moving the same way. Confirm what your release actually does rather than inheriting either rule from a blog post. The real defect sits upstream of the database. A mutable balance cell in an eventually consistent store has no safe read-modify-write: two writers at QUORUM still overwrite each other, and last-write-wins resolves that by timestamp, silently. Money wants an append-only ledger with the balance derived from it (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3), or a lightweight transaction (Paxos, IF with SERIAL) paying for compare-and-set. Either way, the reconciliation that should have caught this before a customer did is not optional (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6).","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q4"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q1","surface":"track","en":"What's the difference between partitioning and sharding? How do you choose a shard key?","bodyEn":"Partitioning: splits 1 table into multiple partitions within the same DB (range/hash/list) → partition pruning, managing old data Sharding: splits data across multiple independent nodes → scales writes & storage beyond a single machine A good shard key: high cardinality, evenly distributed (avoids hotspots), matches the primary query pattern. Fintech typically shards by account_id so one user's transactions stay on the same shard (no cross-shard). Three concepts that often get asked together: Partition = splitting a table within 1 node (range by month for booking to prune + drop old partitions fast) Sharding = splitting across multiple nodes (by user_id) Composite index = a multi-column index serving a query pattern (not splitting data) A table can both be range-partitioned and have a composite index within each partition. Shard trap: using flight_id as the shard key → hotspot on popular flights; user_id distributes evenly but 'all bookings for one flight' becomes scatter-gather. Bottom line: the shard key follows the hottest query, accepting a trade-off for secondary queries (push them to a separate read model).","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q1"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q2","surface":"track","en":"Why is resharding painful? How does consistent hashing help?","bodyEn":"Resharding has to move data + change the mapping while the system is running. Consistent hashing: maps keys & nodes onto a hash ring. adding/removing a node only remaps a small fraction of keys (instead of nearly all of them with modulo) virtual nodes help balance the load Tip: over-provision many 'logical shards' up front so scaling out later is easy. What you lose when you shard Cross-shard JOIN/transaction, global AUTO_INCREMENT, aggregation requires scatter-gather. Don't shard early — optimize indexes/replicas/cache first. each physical node gets only 1 point on the ring → skewed distribution, and losing a node dumps all its load onto exactly one neighboring node assign each node ~100–200 vnodes spread around the ring → losing a node spreads the load, adding a node 'steals' a little evenly from every node (the Cassandra/DynamoDB technique) Limitations of plain consistent hashing: still needs rebalancing of data + handling replicas (replicate to the next N nodes on the ring) if range queries are a core requirement → hashing destroys locality → use range sharding + a directory instead of hashing","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q2"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q3","surface":"track","en":"Generating global IDs once you've sharded: Snowflake vs UUID?","bodyEn":"Sharding loses global auto-increment → you need a distributed, unique ID. Snowflake UUIDv4 UUIDv7 Structure timestamp+machine+seq (64-bit) random 128-bit time-ordered 128-bit Index-friendly good (time-sequential) poor (page splits) good Needs coordination machine id + clock sync no no Snowflake 64-bit = timestamp(41b)+machine(10b)+sequence(12b) → time-sequential (B+Tree-friendly, fewer splits), sortable, ~4096 ids/ms/node; needs clock sync + handling clock-skew/leap seconds UUIDv4 is random (no coordination needed, but destroys locality → splits, bloats secondary indexes); UUIDv7 is time-ordered and fixes that or a ticket server/hi-lo scheme handing out id ranges OTA: booking_id/pnr should be time-sortable (Snowflake/UUIDv7) for good write-index behavior + convenient keyset pagination + leaking less volume information (avoiding strictly sequential ids so competitors can't guess booking volume).","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q3"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q4","surface":"track","en":"Explain CAP and PACELC. Which side does fintech lean toward?","bodyEn":"CAP: when there's a Partition you must choose Consistency or Availability (P is unavoidable) PACELC: if Partitioned, choose A/C; Else (normal operation) you still trade off Latency vs Consistency Fintech: core ledger/balance = CP (better to refuse than get the money wrong) peripheral (feed, displayed history) = AP/eventual Bottom line don't answer CAP in the abstract — say explicitly 'this part is CP, that part is AP'. when Partitioned → choose A or C; Else (no partition) → you still trade off Latency vs Consistency (reading the primary is strongly consistent but slower/farther away, reading a replica is fast but possibly stale) examples: DynamoDB = PA/EL (favors availability+latency), Spanner = PC/EC (favors consistency, pays for it in latency via TrueTime) Fintech/OTA states this per component: ledger/balance/seat hold = CP/EC (better to refuse/be slow than get money wrong/double-sell) search/availability/feed/loyalty = AP/EL (fast, tolerates staleness)","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q4"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q5","surface":"track","en":"What is distributed consensus (Raft/Paxos) used for — at what depth do you need to know it?","bodyEn":"Consensus (Raft/Paxos): multiple nodes agree on a value/ordered log despite node failures (leader election, replicated log). It underlies etcd, the Kafka controller (KRaft), Consul, and distributed databases. A senior engineer needs to understand the concepts (quorum, leader, log replication, why an odd number of nodes) rather than implement it from scratch. Raft (easier to understand than Paxos): leader election + replicated log + quorum (majority of N/2+1, hence an odd number: 3 tolerates losing 1, 5 tolerates losing 2) an entry commits once a majority has written it → consistent despite losing a minority underlies etcd (K8s), Consul, Kafka KRaft (replacing Zookeeper), CockroachDB/TiDB why an odd number: avoids ties + optimizes fault tolerance/cost why quorum: any two majorities always overlap (no two valid leaders at once) consensus is often 'hidden' inside infrastructure (leader election for a scheduler/distributed lock) — knowing when it's in play is enough","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q5"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q6","surface":"track","en":"N+1 in JPA/Hibernate — how do you detect and fix it?","bodyEn":"N+1: 1 query fetches a list of N parents → each access to a lazy association fires 1 extra query. Fix depending on context: JOIN FETCH/@EntityGraph (deliberate eager loading) default_batch_fetch_size/@BatchSize (batches into IN → N/k+1) DTO projection for read-heavy screens Don't go EAGER globally; watch out for LazyInitializationException. detect: turn on spring.jpa.show-sql/Hibernate statistics, or a datasource-proxy that counts queries in dev — N+1 shows up as 'one list query → N child queries' fix: JOIN FETCH/@EntityGraph, @BatchSize/hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size (N+1 → N/k+1), or DTO projection (fetch only the needed columns) Trap: JOIN FETCH on multiple collections at once → cartesian product bloats the result set; fetch one collection per query. OTA: a booking list screen with segments + passengers is prone to N+1 → use projection/2 batched queries instead of repeated lazy loads. The same problem viewed from the application layer (batching, fetch size, statement timeout) is covered in 18.8 if the domain is separated from the JPA entity, most N+1 issues disappear along with lazy loading (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2). The three fixes, and the one that throws at startup: // 1. @EntityGraph - declarative, no JPQL, composes with Pageable @EntityGraph (attributePaths = { \"segments\" }) Page<Booking> findByUserId (Long userId, Pageable page); // 2. JOIN FETCH - explicit, but pagination happens IN MEMORY @Query ( \"select distinct b from Booking b join fetch b.segments where b.userId = :u\" ) List<Booking> findWithSegments ( @Param ( \"u\" ) Long u); // 3. Two List collections at once -> MultipleBagFetchException, at STARTUP @Query ( \"select b from Booking b join fetch b.segments join fetch b.passengers\" ) // org.hibernate.loader.MultipleBagFetchException: cannot simultaneously fetch multiple bags Fix 3 by fetching one collection per query and letting Hibernate stitch the second into the same persistence context, or by making the collections Set instead of List. Pair @EntityGraph with hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size=100 — the graph handles the association you know about, batch fetching covers the ones you forgot.","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q6"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q7","surface":"track","en":"What is CDC (Change Data Capture) / Debezium used for?","bodyEn":"CDC reads a DB's binlog/WAL → emits a stream of 'data that changed' without the app needing to dual-write. Debezium pushes into Kafka, used for: syncing to other systems updating a cache/search index implementing Outbox efficiently (reading the outbox table via binlog) Guarantees at-least-once → consumers must be idempotent. CDC reads the transaction log (MySQL binlog / Postgres WAL logical decoding) → emits a stream of 'row changed' events WITHOUT the app dual-writing (solves the atomic DB↔event problem) Debezium → Kafka: syncs search/cache, data lake, implements Outbox (reading the outbox table via binlog instead of polling) at-least-once + order preserved by PK → consumers must be idempotent Traps: schema changes, the initial snapshot (reading the whole table) is resource-intensive, 'tombstones' on delete. OTA: CDC from the booking DB to update the 'seats available' search index, sync loyalty/analytics, materialize a read model — decoupling services while staying eventually consistent.","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q7"},{"id":"06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q8","surface":"track","en":"How do you choose a partition or shard key, and migrate to a better key without losing correctness?","bodyEn":"Start from the access pattern and invariant, not from the hash function. Score a candidate key against: read locality: can the common query be answered in one partition, or will every request fan out? write distribution: does one tenant, product, account, or time bucket become hot? tenant isolation and privacy: can a large tenant consume the failure and capacity budget of smaller tenants? growth and lifecycle: does the partition stay bounded, and can old data be archived or dropped? cross-partition rules: which uniqueness, ordering, counters, or transactions become harder? migration cost: can the key be changed with an online backfill and a repairable cutover? Each key shape trades something: a time bucket can bound an ever-growing partition but creates boundary queries a tenant key preserves locality but can concentrate a large tenant a salted or bucketed key spreads writes but may require bounded fan-out and loses a simple total order Consistent hashing helps placement changes; it does not solve a single hot key or a cross-shard invariant. Safe migration: DISCOVER -> BACKFILL -> DUAL_VERIFY -> DUAL_READ/CUTOVER -> DRAIN -> RETIRE. record the source position/checkpoint and make copies idempotent apply writes after the snapshot boundary compare counts/checksums and semantic invariants, including deletes/tombstones ramp traffic by tenant or cell Keep the old route for rollback until the new route has passed a measured stability window. Global uniqueness and ordering need an explicit owner: a database constraint on one authority, a namespace/ID strategy, or a workflow that accepts eventual reconciliation. A shard map that only answers “where is this key?” is not a correctness design. Measure skew, p95/p99 partition size, hottest-key share, cross-partition fan-out, replication/repair lag, and migration error classes. Average load can look healthy while one tenant or key is already over its budget.","contextEn":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling#question-06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q8"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q1","surface":"track","en":"SQL vs NoSQL — how do you choose based on the problem?","bodyEn":"SQL (relational): strict schema, strong ACID, complex relationships, flexible querying (JOIN, aggregate) — the standard for financial/transactional data NoSQL: flexible schema, easy horizontal scaling, optimized for a specific access pattern, usually BASE/eventual Criteria SQL NoSQL Schema strict, constrained, migrations flexible, schema-on-read Consistency strong ACID BASE/eventual (depends) Relations/JOIN strong weak, favors denormalization Scale scale-up + replicas; sharding is hard horizontal scale-out Good fit for ledgers, transactions catalogs, sessions, logs, time-series In practice it's usually polyglot persistence: each service picks the DB that fits its job. Avoid the binary 'which one is better' — reason in terms of access pattern + consistency requirements. OTA polyglot: booking/payment use Postgres (ACID), price & availability are cached in Redis, search logs go to Elasticsearch, events go through Kafka. The physical layer is a separate axis. SQL versus NoSQL describes the logical model, API, and transaction contract; B+Tree versus LSM describes how one storage engine arranges pages, sorted runs, logs, and I/O. InnoDB and PostgreSQL commonly expose B-tree paths, Cassandra and RocksDB use LSM-shaped components, and the labels still cross: a NoSQL product such as MongoDB/WiredTiger can use B-trees, while a SQL product such as CockroachDB can sit on an LSM-based Pebble layer. Read the physical walkthrough at (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q7).","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q1"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q2","surface":"track","en":"The types of NoSQL and their typical use cases?","bodyEn":"Type Example Use case Document MongoDB profile/catalog/CMS (nested JSON) Key-Value Redis, DynamoDB cache, session, counter Wide-column Cassandra, HBase heavy writes, time-series, IoT Graph Neo4j social, fraud, recommendation Document (Mongo): reads a whole aggregate in one go (itinerary/profile), flexible schema, but cross-document relationships/transactions are weak Key-Value (Redis/DynamoDB): extremely fast by key (cache/session/counter/feature flag); non-key queries are weak Wide-column (Cassandra): massive writes, scales linearly per node, modeled around the query (denormalize, no JOIN); tunable consistency Graph (Neo4j): multi-hop relationships (fraud rings, recommendations, connecting-flight itineraries) OTA: Cassandra/Scylla for clickstream/search logs; Redis for price caching & seat holds; graph for 'find the optimal connecting itinerary'. Choose by access pattern, not by how 'hot' the technology is.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q2"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q3","surface":"track","en":"ACID vs BASE — the philosophical trade-off?","bodyEn":"ACID (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability): prioritizes correctness — the RDBMS standard, mandatory for ledgers BASE (Basically Available, Soft state, Eventual consistency): prioritizes availability & scale, accepts eventual consistency — common in distributed NoSQL This is the design-philosophy-level expression of CAP/PACELC. ACID = a property of a transaction (correctness); BASE = a distributed-systems philosophy (availability + eventual consistency) 'Eventual consistency' is not a vague 'it'll be correct if you wait long enough' — you must define the convergence window, the conflict-resolution mechanism (LWW/CRDT/version vector), and the acceptance boundary Fintech/OTA — a clear dividing line: ledger/balance/seat hold = ACID (absolute consistency) history/loyalty/notifications = BASE The key question: 'where does a DECISION about money get made based on this data?' → that part must be ACID.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q3"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q4","surface":"track","en":"CAP: what do Consistency, Availability and Partition Tolerance actually promise—and does SQL automatically choose C while NoSQL chooses A?","bodyEn":"CAP applies to a distributed service whose replicas communicate over a network; it is not a label attached to every SQL or NoSQL database. The three guarantees are: Letter Guarantee What it does not mean C — Consistency every successful read behaves as if there were one current copy (linearizable) not the same as ACID's “valid state” invariant A — Availability every request to a non-failed node eventually receives a non-error response the response may be stale; it is not an uptime/SLA promise P — Partition tolerance the service keeps operating while messages between nodes are lost, delayed, or split by a network partition it does not mean that every kind of node or disk failure is harmless When a partition separates replicas, a system cannot keep all three guarantees. “Pick two” is a useful shorthand, not a deployment recipe: in a real distributed system P is usually an operating assumption, so the choice is what to sacrifice during the partition. During a partition What the system does Cost CP waits for a quorum or rejects a conflicting operation, preserving a single ordering of writes some requests fail or block, so availability falls AP continues serving each reachable side and reconciles replicas later reads can be stale and concurrent writes can need conflict resolution SQL versus NoSQL is not a CAP classification: a single-node Oracle or MySQL instance has no replica-to-replica partition to tolerate; a distributed SQL database can use quorum/CP behaviour, while a relational read replica can still be stale; NoSQL engines range from AP/eventual to strongly consistent or tunable, depending on the engine, topology, and read/write policy. CAP-C is usually discussed as linearizability (a read sees the newest completed write in real-time order); ACID-C means that a transaction preserves the application's constraints and invariants. CAP-A means a reachable, non-failed node answers; ACID-D means a committed change survives failure. They are different axes, so “SQL is consistent” and “NoSQL is available” are both too broad. PACELC adds a second question: else (when there is no partition), do we trade latency for stronger consistency? Define the consistency contract per operation, then test failover and recovery rather than assigning a permanent CAP badge to a product.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q4"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q1","surface":"track","en":"Why do large enterprises still favor Oracle — from features down to physical storage?","bodyEn":"Oracle is expensive but still chosen for core systems because of: RAC (Real Application Clusters): multiple instances sharing storage → HA & scale, transparent failover powerful PL/SQL; a mature optimizer; partitioning, materialized views, Flashback, Data Guard (DR) deep physical administration: tablespaces, ASM, redo/undo, RMAN backup enterprise support + contracts + compliance certification — decisive in banking/insurance Bottom line enterprises pay for HA, mature operational tooling, and 'a single throat to choke' when something breaks — not just SQL features. RAC (instances sharing storage via Cache Fusion) → HA + scale, transparent failover Data Guard (DR standby), RMAN backup, Flashback (restore a past state — invaluable for financial investigations), ASM, partitioning & parallel query, an extremely mature optimizer/PLSQL the core value: HA + tooling + support contracts + compliance — downtime/errors mean lost money + legal exposure Trend: new systems shift to Postgres (cost), but legacy core banking stays on Oracle because of migration risk & operational features. The whole picture: how buffer cache, undo, redo and SCN serve a read and a locking read is (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q11).","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q1"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q2","surface":"track","en":"Why is MySQL/InnoDB widely used, and where does it fit best?","bodyEn":"MySQL/InnoDB is a pragmatic OLTP default because it combines ACID transactions, MVCC, clustered primary-key storage, secondary indexes, foreign keys, crash recovery, replication options, mature drivers, and broad managed-service support. MySQL now publishes two release tracks. 8.4 is an LTS series aimed at a stable feature set and longer support; Innovation releases deliver features and behavior changes more frequently. Pin the track and upgrade path explicitly—“MySQL 8” is no longer a sufficient production target. Good fit Investigate before choosing transactional web/SaaS workloads with well-understood access paths complex analytical SQL, extension requirements, or specialized index/operator needs teams that value operational familiarity and managed offerings write topology, failover semantics, replica lag, backup/restore RTO and licensing/support schemas that benefit from relational constraints hot clustered keys, large secondary indexes, and cross-region consistency requirements InnoDB's clustered index means every secondary-index leaf stores the primary key. A short, stable primary key can reduce all secondary indexes; a random or ever-growing hot key changes page locality and write behavior. Its redo log provides durability, undo records support MVCC/rollback, and the buffer pool controls most read performance—these are concrete mechanisms, not “cheap but good enough.” Do not transfer cloud-product claims to upstream MySQL. Aurora, Cloud SQL, RDS, and self-managed MySQL have different storage, replication, failover, observability, and cost models. Benchmark the selected product and topology, including failover and restore, rather than comparing only SQL features.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q2"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q3","surface":"track","en":"How does PostgreSQL differ from MySQL, and why is it on the rise?","bodyEn":"stricter SQL standard compliance; advanced features: JSONB (indexable), recursive CTEs, powerful window functions, partial/expression/GiST/GIN indexes, materialized views a rich set of extensions: PostGIS (geo), TimescaleDB (time-series), pgvector (AI embeddings) → a 'do-it-all' database MVCC works differently from MySQL: stores tuple versions inside the table → needs VACUUM to clean up dead tuples Bottom line Postgres wins when you need advanced features + JSON + extensions (especially pgvector for AI). MySQL wins on simplicity & ubiquity. JSONB (binary, GIN-indexable — a hybrid relational+document model), recursive CTEs, window functions, partial/expression indexes extensions: PostGIS, TimescaleDB, pgvector (a major reason Postgres is rising in the AI era) MVCC stores tuple versions inside the table → requires VACUUM/autovacuum; falling behind → table/index bloat + transaction ID wraparound (requires a vacuum freeze) OTA: Postgres + pgvector for semantic recommendations/search, JSONB for trip-search-result snapshots, PostGIS for 'airports near you'.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q3"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q4","surface":"track","en":"When can MongoDB's document model make reads efficient, and what does it trade away?","bodyEn":"MongoDB can make a known aggregate read efficient when data that is read and changed together is embedded in one bounded document. One indexed lookup can then return the aggregate without application-side joins or several network round trips. That advantage comes from data modelling and access-path locality, not a rule that MongoDB is faster than SQL. Trade-offs are workload-specific: embedding can duplicate data and make fan-out updates or document growth expensive; referencing keeps one source of truth but reintroduces multiple reads or $lookup; a document has a size limit, and broad projections waste cache/network if the aggregate is too large; relational databases can be equally fast for indexed point reads and are often clearer for ad-hoc joins, constraints, and set-based analysis; MongoDB supports transactions and configurable consistency; it is wrong to classify it as inherently weak-consistency storage. Compare equivalent models Measure the same business response, indexes, durability/consistency settings, dataset larger than memory, concurrency, and network topology. Comparing one denormalized MongoDB read with N unindexed SQL queries measures schema/query design, not database-engine speed. WiredTiger provides MVCC, compression, cache management, and document-level write concurrency. Performance still depends on index selectivity, working-set fit, document shape, update amplification, checkpoints, and storage latency. Good candidates include bounded catalog/product views, user preferences, and immutable snapshots. Money ledgers or inventory can also be stored in MongoDB only if the chosen schema, unique constraints, transaction boundary, write/read concerns, audit trail, and operational recovery prove the invariant. Choosing an RDBMS is often simpler, but it is an engineering decision rather than a product label. Runnable mongosh example — one embedded document makes the hold atomic: db.inventory.insertOne({_id:\"sku-1\", available:1, version:7, holds:[]}) db.inventory.updateOne( {_id:\"sku-1\", available:{$gte:1}, version:7}, {$inc:{available:-1, version:1}, $push:{holds:{id:\"h-42\", expiresAt:new Date(Date.now()+300000)}}} ) // matchedCount: 1, modifiedCount: 1 // run the same update again: matchedCount: 0; it cannot oversell this document The atomic single-document update rechecks its predicate, so the expected version prevents a lost update. Benefit: inventory, version and hold move together without a multi-document transaction. Cost: one popular document becomes a hot serialization point; holds cannot grow without bound, and duplicated data needs an update policy. Exit criterion: reference or bucket children when cardinality or lifecycle no longer fits one aggregate.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q4"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q5","surface":"track","en":"MongoDB: sharding and the consistency model?","bodyEn":"Sharding and consistency are separate design axes. A shard key determines data placement and whether a query can target one shard. Evaluate cardinality, frequency, monotonicity, query routing, zone requirements, document growth, and the ability to refine or reshard later; a poor key creates hotspots or scatter-gather work. readPreference chooses which replica-set member may serve a read. The default is primary. readConcern controls consistency/isolation. The default local concern can return data that is later rolled back, even when reading from the primary—so “primary by default means strong consistency” is false. writeConcern controls acknowledgement/durability. A majority write combined with the right read concern/session semantics gives stronger guarantees at availability/latency cost. // Example policy for an invariant-sensitive operation; tune to the actual contract session. withTransaction (work, TransactionOptions. builder () . readConcern (ReadConcern.SNAPSHOT) . writeConcern (WriteConcern.MAJORITY) . readPreference (ReadPreference.primary()) . build ()); majority reads return majority-committed data but do not by themselves mean “the newest value anywhere.” linearizable reads have stricter real-time semantics for eligible single-document reads on the primary and should use maxTimeMS. Causally consistent sessions are the tool for guarantees such as read-your-own-writes across related operations. Multi-document transactions are a supported correctness mechanism, not something to ban. They add coordination and can reduce throughput, so keep them short and measure them; model a bounded aggregate into one document when that naturally preserves the invariant, and use a transaction when the invariant genuinely crosses documents. Avoiding a necessary transaction is not an optimization.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q5"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q6","surface":"track","en":"Redis: why is it fast, what data structures does it offer, and how does persistence work?","bodyEn":"Redis is fast because it's in-memory + a single-threaded event loop (no lock contention) + data structures tuned for the job. Data structures: string, hash, list, set, sorted set (leaderboards!), stream, bitmap, HyperLogLog. Persistence: RDB (snapshot) AOF (append log) → a trade-off between durability and performance. Used for: caching, sessions, rate limiting, lightweight queues, distributed locks, leaderboards. single-threaded data path → one O(N) command (KEYS , SMEMBERS on a large set) blocks everything → avoid it; use the SCAN cursor persistence: RDB (loses data between two snapshots) vs AOF (everysec is the common balance); use both for high durability HA: Redis Sentinel (failover) or Redis Cluster (16384 hash slots + replicas) lock/dedup: TTL expiring mid-operation → breaks correctness → use a fencing token OTA: seat-hold TTL (SET seat NX PX), short-TTL price/availability caching, token-bucket rate limiting, sorted sets for 'trending routes/inventory counts'. This exact TTL mechanism is a common LLD question: implementing your own key-value store with TTL using lazy expiration + sampling, just like Redis does (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q4).","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q6"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q7","surface":"track","en":"A decision framework: how do you choose a DB for a specific use case?","bodyEn":"Ask in sequence: does the data have relationships? does it need strong ACID? access pattern: by key / range / full-text / graph? read/write ratio & scale? strong or eventual consistency? Quick map: ledger/transactions → RDBMS (Postgres/MySQL/Oracle) cache/session → Redis catalog/CMS → MongoDB time-series/heavy writes → Cassandra/Timescale search → Elasticsearch AI embeddings → pgvector Often you combine several — polyglot persistence. Question framework: (1) relational & needs JOIN? (2) needs strong ACID (money/booking)? (3) primary access pattern? (4) read/write & scale (beyond a single node)? (5) strong or eventual? Map: ledger/booking → RDBMS; cache/session/seat-hold → Redis; flight/destination search → Elasticsearch; large clickstream/logs → Cassandra/Timescale; AI embeddings → pgvector; analytics → columnar (ClickHouse/BigQuery). Mistake: forcing one DB to do everything, or adding a new DB just because it's 'trendy' (raises operational cost).","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q7"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q8","surface":"track","en":"Oracle vs MySQL/InnoDB: when does the enterprise premium buy something real?","bodyEn":"Oracle Database and MySQL with InnoDB are both relational SQL systems, and both can provide ACID transactions. Compare the engine, topology, durability settings, workload, and operating contract—not the product names alone. Term note — High Availability / Disaster Recovery (HA/DR): HA keeps the service available through instance or node failures. DR restores service and data after a site-level or regional failure. Their targets are usually expressed as RTO (recovery time objective) and RPO (recovery point objective). Dimension Oracle Database MySQL + InnoDB Process and memory Memory: SGA/PGA Log writer: LGWR Database writers: DBWn; checkpoint coordination: CKPT Process: mysqld Memory: InnoDB buffer pool and log buffer Logical → disk Hierarchy: tablespace → segment → extent → block Datafiles contain data/index blocks; control files describe the database. Hierarchy: tables/indexes → pages The clustered primary-key index stores the row; tablespaces include .ibd , system, undo, and temporary space. Transaction and recovery Rollback/read consistency: undo Recovery: online and archived redo, checkpoints, RMAN, and Flashback Concurrency/rollback: undo + multi-version concurrency control (MVCC) Recovery: redo + doublewrite Replication/PITR: a separate binary log High availability / disaster recovery (HA/DR) HA: RAC + Cache Fusion DR: Data Guard with Maximum Protection, Maximum Availability, or Maximum Performance (default) HA: Group Replication/InnoDB Cluster or a managed-service equivalent Replication/DR: binlog replicas, often asynchronous; the exact guarantee depends on topology Integrity and isolation Integrity: declarative constraints, automatic locking, and multiversion read consistency Default isolation: READ COMMITTED; also SERIALIZABLE and READ ONLY Integrity: primary/unique/foreign-key constraints, row locks, and MVCC; CHECK is enforced since 8.0.16 Default isolation: REPEATABLE READ Cost and operations Deep enterprise tooling, support, and certification Higher licence/option cost and DBA complexity Low entry cost, familiar drivers, and many managed offerings Enterprise support and HA still carry operational cost The engines use different names and files, but the write-ahead principle can be read as three separate paths: WRITE — transaction is running SQL statement ↓ locking + MVCC visibility ↓ change page in memory + create undo ↓ generate redo records (write-ahead logging principle) ──────────────────────────── COMMIT — synchronous critical path COMMIT ↓ flush required redo to durable storage ↓ ACK success to client ──────────────────────────── PAGE WRITE — asynchronous dirty pages stay in buffer ↓ checkpoint / DBWn / background writer ↓ datafile pages Crash recovery is a second, separate flow: CRASH ↓ REDO roll forward durable log records since the last checkpoint ↓ UNDO roll back transactions without a commit record Redo can therefore restore changes from an incomplete transaction before undo removes them. “Redo only committed work” is an oversimplification. Data integrity has separate layers: Entity rules: primary key and NOT NULL Referential rules: foreign keys Domain rules: types, CHECK, and UNIQUE Business invariants: a transaction boundary plus the required lock or isolation level Redo, checksums, backup, and point-in-time recovery protect the physical copy. They do not replace constraints. MySQL/InnoDB durability settings: The guarantees here are for InnoDB. A non-transactional table, disabled foreign_key_checks, relaxed flush policy, or asynchronous replica changes the real contract. innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=1 (default): write and flush redo at every commit; this is the fully durable transaction setting. innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=2: write redo to the OS cache at commit; survives a mysqld crash, but not necessarily an OS crash or power loss. innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=0: write and flush periodically; even a process crash can lose recent commits. The interval is approximately one second, not a guaranteed maximum. Use sync_binlog=1 with innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=1 when the binary log must follow the fully durable contract too. Oracle commit behaviour has two independent axes: WAIT NOWAIT: whether COMMIT waits until redo is persistent—the durability decision IMMEDIATE BATCH: whether redo is offered immediately or batched for group commit The default is WAIT IMMEDIATE; COMMIT_WAIT and COMMIT_LOGGING apply when the clause is omitted. In either product, read the selected configuration instead of inferring durability from the brand. ACID is a transaction contract: Atomicity: commit everything or roll everything back. Consistency: move from one valid, invariant-preserving state to another. Isolation: control what concurrent transactions can observe. Durability: a successful commit survives the failures covered by the log, storage, and configuration. ACID-C is not CAP-C, and ACID-D is not CAP-A; see (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q4). When Oracle's premium can be justified: RAC/Data Guard materially reduce the required RTO/RPO. Mature recovery, audit, compliance, partitioning, parallel execution, PL/SQL, or optimizer behaviour matters to the workload. Vendor SLA/certification or an existing Oracle estate reduces operational and migration risk. The organisation accepts separately licensed options, support cost, and DBA complexity. When MySQL/InnoDB is often the better fit: Web/SaaS OLTP has clear access paths and a well-defined transaction boundary. The team prioritises lower cost and managed-cloud operations. The chosen topology passes the same durability, failover, and backup/restore tests. The benchmark keeps schema, indexes, durability policy, dataset, and RTO/RPO comparable. Aurora, RDS, Cloud SQL, and self-managed MySQL are different products. Do not transfer one topology's storage, replication, or failover claims to another. Use Oracle when the invariant is mission-critical and the organisation values High Availability / Disaster Recovery, recovery, compliance, and support enough to pay for that ecosystem. Use MySQL/InnoDB when the same required guarantees can be proven with a simpler topology and lower total cost. Start with the invariant and access pattern; choose the brand after recovery and failover testing. The whole picture: how Oracle's reads use undo and SCN on top of this write path is (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q11).","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q8"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q9","surface":"track","en":"When does a search engine earn its place next to the database, and what must it never own?","bodyEn":"Relational full-text search is fine until you need relevance ranking, fuzzy matching, faceting and aggregation over millions of documents at low latency. That is the point where an inverted index stops being optional (05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q1). Relational LIKE/full-text Search engine %term% full scan, no index indexed by token Relevance none, or bolted on scoring is the core feature Typos, synonyms, stemming manual analyzers Facets over millions of rows heavy aggregation designed for it Transactions and correctness ACID near real-time, no transactions The index is a read model, not a database. It is derived data — rebuildable from the source at any time — which is exactly what makes it safe to reindex, and exactly why it must never be the system of record (12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q4). Feeding it. Writing to the database and the index in the same request is a dual write: one of them fails and the two drift apart forever. Feed it from the outbox or from CDC so the index follows a log rather than a best effort (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q7). Near real-time is visible to users A document is searchable after a refresh, not at commit. \"I just saved it and search cannot find it\" is the expected behaviour — read the detail page from the database, not from the index. reindex behind an alias: build the new index, verify, then flip the alias atomically. Mapping changes are not in-place edits, and pretending otherwise turns a schema tweak into downtime. decide what the index stores: enough to render a result list, no more. Storing the whole document tempts people to read it as truth and doubles the data you must protect (13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q2). deep pagination has the same cost problem it has in SQL; use search_after rather than large offsets (04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q5). a search cluster is an operational commitment: heap and shard sizing, snapshots, version upgrades, and a reindex path. Do not add one for a feature a well-chosen index would serve (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q7). the same shape covers analytics: derive a read store from the transactional one, keep the derivation replayable, and never let reports run on the primary.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q9"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q10","surface":"track","en":"How do you make a search, cache, or OLAP projection rebuildable and freshness-aware?","bodyEn":"Treat the transactional model as the authority and every derived store as a versioned projection. The projection contract needs more than a connection string: a snapshot boundary and a replay source event identity, aggregate/key, source version or log position, and the ordering scope schema version, upsert semantics, delete/tombstone handling, and retention a watermark/freshness metric and a policy for stale, paused, or failed states a repair path that can replay a range or rebuild a new version Search mapping or materialized table: build version N+1 beside N, backfill it, catch up from the recorded position, compare counts and domain samples, then swap an alias or controlled route. Cache: define invalidation/version/TTL and accept that eviction or loss is normal; do not pretend cache persistence is source durability. OLAP: define late-arriving data, correction, lineage, and access control rather than running reports on the OLTP primary. CDC is useful because it supplies a replayable change stream, but at-least-once delivery still requires idempotent/version-checked projection writes. A healthy consumer process is not proof that it has applied every source change. Never let a stale derived store authorize a critical mutation Search can find a candidate, cache can accelerate a read, and OLAP can answer an aggregate. Charge, grant entitlement, decrement inventory, and settle money against the authority or an explicitly authoritative design.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q10"},{"id":"07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q11","surface":"track","en":"Oracle at a glance: how do buffer cache, undo, redo, SCN, MVCC and isolation serve a SELECT and a SELECT FOR UPDATE?","bodyEn":"In one line: a plain SELECT reads the past without locking; SELECT ... FOR UPDATE works on the present and locks it. The SCN picks which past, undo rebuilds it, and redo makes a commit durable. One plain SELECT by primary key, from parse to result, with a consistent read check at every block. Its labels are in Vietnamese. Writes, FOR UPDATE and isolation are covered below. Open it for full size. Memory and disk. The buffer cache holds current blocks plus consistent read (CR) clones. DBWn writes dirty blocks to datafiles later; LGWR writes the redo log buffer to the online redo log. Detail: (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q8) A write. The row is locked until COMMIT or ROLLBACK. Undo keeps the old value, and one redo record covers both the data block and the undo block. The commit is LGWR's write of that redo and the commit SCN; data blocks follow later. Detail: (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q8) A plain SELECT. It takes a snapshot SCN, then finds each block in the cache or reads it from a datafile. If the block holds anything newer than the snapshot, Oracle copies it and applies undo to build a CR clone. No row lock, no dirty read. Detail: (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q2) is the same idea in InnoDB. SELECT FOR UPDATE. It finds the rows, checks their current version and locks them until the transaction ends. By default it waits for a held lock; NOWAIT, WAIT n and SKIP LOCKED change that. Detail: (28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q2), and (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3) for pessimistic vs optimistic. Isolation. READ COMMITTED, the default, takes a snapshot per statement. SERIALIZABLE and READ ONLY take one per transaction. A serializable update of a row changed by a transaction that committed after it began fails with ORA-08177. Detail: (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q1), (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q9) Specific to Oracle, with no other card: MVCC is not a component: it is SCN + undo + CR clones. Readers and writers do not block each other; writers of the same row do, and FOR UPDATE is the one read that locks. Row locks are recorded in the data block's interested transaction list (ITL), and under normal circumstances Oracle does not escalate them to block or table level. A resumed statement sees the committed row. Oracle's own example has a blocked UPDATE end with 0 rows because the new row no longer matched its predicate. Snapshot too old (ORA-01555): undo a long reader needs was overwritten. Raise undo_retention. Commit cleanout: an ITL entry can outlive the commit. A later session checks the undo segment header and cleans the block, which generates redo. Bottom line Reads go back in time through SCN and undo, writes go forward through redo, and locks only stand between statements after the same row.","contextEn":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","href":"/topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines#question-07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q11"},{"id":"08-message-queue.the-two-models.q1","surface":"track","en":"The RabbitMQ model: exchange, queue, binding, routing key?","bodyEn":"A producer publishes to an exchange. The exchange routes the message to one or more queues through a binding and a routing key. Exchange types: direct: exact routing-key match topic: wildcard routing, such as booking..created fanout: broadcast to every bound queue headers: route by message headers RabbitMQ is sometimes summarized as a smart broker, dumb consumer. Treat that as shorthand only: the broker owns routing and delivery state, while the consumer still owns validation, authorization, deduplication, retries and business effects. Durability is a chain, not one switch. Every link must match the failure domain you designed for: durable topology and persistent-message intent queue type and quorum/replica placement storage and publisher-confirm policy A quorum queue can improve survival of a defined node failure. It does not guarantee survival of correlated storage loss, deletion, expiration or every acknowledgement ambiguity. Publisher confirm means the broker accepted the publish under its configured contract. A consumer ACK only advances the broker's delivery boundary; neither proves that a database write, payment or other business effect completed. Bound in-flight work with prefetch: a larger value can improve batching, but can also increase memory, unfairness and redelivery cost. For the full delivery vocabulary, use the four-boundary model in (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1).","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.the-two-models.q1"},{"id":"08-message-queue.the-two-models.q2","surface":"track","en":"The Kafka model: topic, partition, offset, consumer group?","bodyEn":"Kafka is a retained distributed log: a topic is split into partitions each record has an offset within its partition consumers in the same consumer group share partition ownership, so at most one active member normally processes a partition at a time Records remain available according to retention and compaction policy rather than disappearing simply because a consumer read them. Different groups can consume independently and replay by choosing offsets within the retained window. An offset is a consumer cursor, not proof that the business effect committed. Commit after the protected effect for an at-least-once flow; committing first trades duplicates for possible loss. A key can keep related records in one partition, but it does not create global order, and adding or migrating partitions requires an explicit ordering/version strategy. Each guarantee covers one boundary: producer acknowledgement or a Kafka transaction covers the producer/broker or Kafka-internal boundary replication and retention decide whether replay is still available the consumer's offset commit and its business side effect are two more, separate boundaries Kafka transactions can provide exactly-once read-process-write behavior inside their supported Kafka scope, not an arbitrary database or external provider.","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.the-two-models.q2"},{"id":"08-message-queue.the-two-models.q3","surface":"track","en":"RabbitMQ vs Kafka — when do you use which?","bodyEn":"RabbitMQ Kafka Primary abstraction exchange → queue → delivery topic → partition → retained log Routing exchange and binding rules producer partitioning, usually by key Work ownership competing consumers and ACK/requeue consumer groups and committed offsets Replay queue/DLX/retention design retained records and independent offsets Ordering scope queue/delivery lane, affected by redelivery and concurrency one partition, affected by key, retries and handlers Throughput workload-dependent; measure payload, batching, replication, consumers and retention Bottom line Choose from the workload contract. RabbitMQ is often a good fit for routed commands and discrete tasks; Kafka is often a good fit for retained event streams, replay, fan-out and partition-scoped order. A system may use both, but each flow must name its owner, retention, failure domain and recovery path. Kafka 4.2 narrowed the queue gap. Share groups (KIP-932, production-ready in 4.2) let consumers take records from the same partition without exclusive assignment, with per-record acknowledgement — ACCEPT, RELEASE, REJECT — and a delivery-count limit. They trade away ordering: use them for work processed one record at a time, not for a stream whose order matters. Before Kafka 4.4 added dead-letter queues for share groups (KIP-1191), a record past the limit was archived rather than copied anywhere — so on 4.2 and 4.3 poison handling still needs its own path. A broker choice does not choose delivery semantics for you. Publisher confirmation, broker retention/replication, consumer ACK or offset, and the business effect remain distinct. See (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1) before making a durability or exactly-once claim.","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.the-two-models.q3"},{"id":"08-message-queue.the-two-models.q4","surface":"track","en":"Message queue vs pub/sub: how do delivery ownership and failure isolation differ?","bodyEn":"The decision is about delivery ownership, not the transport brand: Shape Who owns a delivery? Use for Queue / competing consumers One active consumer in the group handles a task One command or unit of work Pub/sub Each subscription or consumer group owns its reaction One fact, many independent reactions In RabbitMQ, one queue with multiple consumers is queue-like; a fanout exchange with a queue per subscriber is pub/sub-like. In Kafka, consumers in the same group share work, while different groups have independent cursors. Give each independent subscriber its own queue or group so one subscriber's lag, retry policy or poison message does not block another. Bound in-flight work, retry with a defined policy, and use a dead-letter/quarantine path where appropriate. The shape still does not promise at-most-once or exactly-once. Each signal proves one thing: a publisher confirm means broker acceptance retention and replication determine whether recovery is possible an ACK or offset records a consumer decision only the consumer's durable business transaction proves its protected effect Use a stable event identity and a versioned contract so duplicates and permitted reordering are safe.","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.the-two-models.q4"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1","surface":"track","en":"What do the delivery semantics at-most-once / at-least-once / exactly-once mean?","bodyEn":"Delivery semantics describe what may happen to one logical delivery within a named boundary. They do not, by themselves, guarantee disk durability, infinite retention, producer correctness or an external business effect. Use four boundaries: Producer → broker acceptance: did the broker accept the publication? Examples are a RabbitMQ publisher confirm or a Kafka producer acknowledgement. Broker retention/commit: can the record be recovered after a consumer crash? Examples are queue durability/replication, Kafka retention and offsets, or a provider's visibility/retention policy. Broker → consumer delivery: did the consumer acknowledge or commit its cursor? A RabbitMQ ACK, Kafka offset or visibility completion is this boundary. Consumer → business effect: did the protected database state or external effect commit? This needs a local transaction, inbox/business key, provider idempotency or reconciliation as appropriate. Semantic Typical protocol behavior Application consequence at-most-once acknowledge/commit before processing or do not retry a failure can lose work; duplicates are traded for loss at-least-once redeliver until acknowledgement within availability and retention limits the handler can run more than once; the effect needs deduplication or reconciliation exactly-once within a scope atomically commit input position and output/state, or deduplicate under a defined protocol no duplicate committed result inside that scope; arbitrary external systems remain outside it The crash window decides which failure you get: ACK before the effect: a crash loses the work. Effect committed, crash before ACK: the message is redelivered, so the effect must be safe to repeat. Kafka transactions can provide a scoped internal guarantee; RabbitMQ plus a database inbox can make a database effect converge, but the ACK and database transaction are still separate. For the consumer-side pattern, see (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6) and (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q2). What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: Which broker and version, and which defaults you pinned — retries, acks, prefetch, min.insync.replicas — because \"at-least-once\" is a property of a configuration, not of a product. What the primary workload is: commands, durable domain events, replayable analytics, or an ordered per-aggregate stream. The right answer for one is wrong for another. The loss you can tolerate, the cost of a duplicate, the oldest-message age you can live with and the replay window — the numbers that decide queue versus log. Which external effects sit inside the guarantee. \"At-least-once plus idempotent consumer\" is falsified by any effect with no stable business identity, no durable dedup or reconciliation, or an irreversible side effect fired before the record commits. Who owns the DLQ, how long it is retained, who may redrive it, and the maximum age before it is an incident.","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do RabbitMQ Publisher Confirms and consumer ack/prefetch work?","bodyEn":"RabbitMQ exposes separate producer and consumer boundaries: Publisher confirms report whether the broker accepted a publish according to the selected topology, queue type and durability/replication contract. A confirm is not a processing acknowledgement. Returns with mandatory report that a message could not be routed. A message can be returned and still receive a positive publisher confirm, so handle both signals. Consumer ACKs tell RabbitMQ that the consumer accepts responsibility for that delivery. An unacknowledged message may be redelivered; an ACK does not prove that an external effect was correct. Prefetch bounds unacknowledged deliveries. Tune it from handler time, payload size, fairness and memory; raising it is not a substitute for capacity or backpressure. Durability is a chain: durable topology, persistent-message intent, queue replication, confirm policy, storage and the tested failure domain. A quorum acknowledgement does not promise survival of every correlated node/storage loss, operator action or expiration. Choose the RabbitMQ data structure for the workload: a quorum queue gives replicated queue semantics, while a stream is an append/replay shape for retained backlog or fan-out. A stream is not a drop-in replacement for task-queue acknowledgement, per-message work ownership, or the application's external-effect contract. Crash windows matter: a database effect committed before ACK can be delivered again, while ACK before the effect can lose work. Couple the consumer's deduplication/inbox record with its local effect where the flow is at-least-once; the broader idempotency contract belongs in (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q2). Publisher confirms also do not make a database write and publication atomic. Use the queue-specific Outbox boundary in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3).","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q2"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q3","surface":"track","en":"How does Kafka guarantee durability: replication, ISR, acks?","bodyEn":"Kafka durability is defined per partition and acknowledged record: a partition has a leader and replicas; the ISR is the set currently considered in sync acks=0 does not wait, acks=1 waits for the leader, and acks=all waits for the current ISR under broker semantics min.insync.replicas can reject an acks=all write when the ISR is too small instead of silently weakening the policy replication factor controls placement count; rack/zone awareness, election policy and storage health shape the failure domain A policy such as replication factor 3, min.insync.replicas=2, acks=all and producer idempotence may be appropriate for a tested single-broker failure model. It is not a universal no-loss recipe: correlated zone/storage loss, retention expiry, operator error and lost acknowledgement responses still need an explicit recovery contract. Producer idempotence can prevent duplicate log appends from supported retries, but it does not make a consumer's database or external call exactly once. Committing an offset after an effect gives at-least-once behavior; committing before the effect risks loss. Kafka transactions can atomically join consumed offsets and produced Kafka records inside their supported Kafka boundary. Monitor ISR shrink, under-replicated/offline partitions, produce errors/latency, retention and consumer lag, then rehearse the broker/zone failure you claim to tolerate.","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q3"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q4","surface":"track","en":"Ordering: how do the two brokers guarantee it?","bodyEn":"Ordering must be scoped to a key and an observable effect; neither broker gives a free global order. Kafka RabbitMQ Broker order append order within one partition queue/delivery order, subject to queue features and redelivery Parallelism different partitions progress independently competing consumers and handlers run concurrently; single-active-consumer narrows the lane Application risk key changes, partition migration, producer retries, rebalances and parallel handlers requeue/redelivery and concurrent handlers can complete effects out of order Use a stable aggregate key only when every event sharing the invariant uses the same partitioning contract. Delivery order is not completion order: producer idempotence and supported in-flight settings can preserve partition order under retries, yet application handlers can still reorder effects a RabbitMQ queue can narrow delivery order, but a crash/redelivery or asynchronous database work can still change completion order When stale or out-of-order state is harmful, carry an aggregate sequence/version and apply it with an atomic condition. Define gap, duplicate, replay and partition-migration handling. A global single partition sacrifices throughput and availability and still cannot order arbitrary external systems atomically. Test two producers for one key, retries, rebalance/failover, delayed old events, poison-message bypass and DLQ replay. The broader messaging concurrency card is (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q2).","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q4"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q5","surface":"track","en":"DLQ and poison messages — how are they handled?","bodyEn":"A poison message is one that keeps failing and can block useful work if it is retried forever. A DLQ or dead-letter topic is a quarantine state, not proof of reliability. classify failures before retrying: transient dependency failure, throttling, schema/contract error, authorization error and invalid business input have different actions use bounded attempts with exponential backoff and jitter; keep retry work out of the hot delivery path when it would block a partition or queue after quarantine, record the original identity, attempt/error metadata and first-seen age; assign an owner, retention, alert threshold and redrive authorization validate code/schema/configuration before replay, rate-limit the redrive, and make the consumer effect safe for duplicates or reconcile unknown outcomes RabbitMQ commonly uses retry queues/TTL and a dead-letter exchange; Kafka commonly uses retry topics and a dead-letter topic. These are topology choices, not universal semantics. Quorum queues can now hold the retry themselves (as of 2026-10-26). Since RabbitMQ 4.3, delayed retry (delayed-retry-type, -min, -max as a policy) holds a returned message for min(min × delivery count, max) before redelivery: linear back-off with no TTL queue. RabbitMQ 4.4 adds delayed delivery: a message whose x-opt-delivery-time is in the future is enqueued now and delivered at that time. Neither replaces the delivery limit (default 20 on quorum queues): past it the message is still dead-lettered, so the DLX is still needed. Monitor oldest quarantined age and recovery outcome, not only queue size. Consumer deduplication is covered in (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6); broader API/business idempotency belongs in (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1).","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q5"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6","surface":"track","en":"Idempotent consumer — how do you implement dedup?","bodyEn":"At-least-once delivery can run a handler more than once. Use a stable event or command identity, not a delivery tag or offset that changes when the message is redelivered. Two common designs are: make the business operation naturally idempotent, such as an UPSERT or an absolute-state update with a version condition insert the event ID or business key into an Inbox/processed table with a unique constraint in the same local transaction as the protected business effect; a duplicate then becomes a no-op or an explicit conflict The deduplication window must cover the retry, retention and approved replay window, or the old event can become new work again. The broker can suppress some duplicate publications inside its own scope, but that does not make a database write, payment call or email exactly once. Beyond the consumer: external provider idempotency: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) timeout/unknown outcomes and reconciliation: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1) where eventual consistency is acceptable with money: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6) the idempotency-key contract: (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1) This topic owns the message-to-consumer boundary; keep complete payment, Saga and API idempotency workflows in their canonical topics.","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7","surface":"track","en":"The Outbox pattern with a message queue — why is it needed?","bodyEn":"A database commit followed by a broker publish is a dual write. A crash between the two can leave durable business state with no publication, or a publication with no matching state. An Outbox makes only the local boundary atomic: In one database transaction, commit the business state change and an integration-event intent in an Outbox table. A polling relay or CDC connector reads pending rows and publishes them. The relay records progress only after its own publication/confirmation policy; a crash can make the relay publish the same event again. Consumers therefore need stable event identity and an Inbox/idempotent effect. A publisher confirm helps establish broker acceptance for a relay attempt. It does not atomically include the relay's database bookkeeping, consumer ACK, downstream database, payment provider or other business effect. Do not describe Outbox plus confirm as end-to-end exactly once. Where each part is explained in depth: outbox mechanics and the dual-write failure: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3) consumer deduplication: (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6) Saga/compensation: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2)","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q8","surface":"track","en":"Event-driven architecture & event sourcing — concepts and trade-offs?","bodyEn":"Event-driven architecture communicates through asynchronous facts or commands. It can decouple deployables and absorb bursts, but it also spreads the flow across consumers, introduces lag and eventual consistency, and requires tracing, replay controls and failure ownership. Event sourcing makes an append-only event history the authoritative state for a bounded aggregate; current state is rebuilt by replay, often with snapshots and separate read models. It is not automatically a perfect audit trail: completeness, immutability, retention, authorization, privacy/delete policy, schema evolution and replay correctness still have to be designed. Choose it where history, reconstruction or temporal queries are a real product invariant, such as a ledger-like domain. Do not event-source ordinary CRUD merely because a broker is available. Distinguish internal domain events from public integration events so a private aggregate refactor does not silently break consumers; the modeling boundary is covered in (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3).","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q8"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q9","surface":"track","en":"Your consumer group rebalances every few minutes and reprocesses messages. Which settings cause that, and how do you stop it?","bodyEn":"Kafka liveness has two different clocks: the heartbeat thread answers session.timeout.ms the application poll loop must return within max.poll.interval.ms A handler can keep heartbeating while processing a batch, yet still be removed when the poll interval expires. Uncommitted offsets then move with the partition and records are delivered again. Setting Controls First response --- --- --- max.poll.records records returned per poll lower it when batch work is slow max.poll.interval.ms maximum time between polls raise only when work is genuinely bounded and long session.timeout.ms / heartbeat.interval.ms member liveness tune for the network and client version fetch.min.bytes / fetch.max.wait.ms fetch batching versus latency adjust from measured workload assignment strategy rebalance behavior use a supported cooperative/static strategy when its rollout contract fits group.protocol which rebalance protocol runs consumer (KIP-848) moves assignment to the broker; the client default is still classic The usual fix is bounded work: reduce the batch move slow work off the poll thread with an explicit pause/claim model apply downstream backpressure A larger timeout alone can hide a stuck consumer. Commit after the protected effect and make redelivery safe; no rebalance setting creates exactly-once business effects. Kafka 4.x changed the protocol, not just its settings. The classic protocol rebalances behind a group-wide synchronization barrier: one member that joins, leaves or stalls disturbs every member, and cooperative assignment only shortens the pause. KIP-848, generally available since Kafka 4.0, moves assignment to the broker's group coordinator and reconciles members incrementally, so a member whose assignment does not change is not interrupted. A client opts in with group.protocol=consumer. session.timeout.ms and heartbeat.interval.ms are then not supported — the broker's group.consumer. settings own liveness — and a server-side assignor (group.remote.assignor) replaces the client strategy. max.poll.interval.ms still applies: a consumer that stops polling still loses its partitions. See (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q1) and (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6).","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q9"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q10","surface":"track","en":"An event is a published contract you cannot recall. How do you design and evolve one without breaking consumers you do not control?","bodyEn":"An event can remain in retention and be read by consumers that did not exist when it was published. Design the contract for replay, not only for today's caller. publish a fact such as OrderConfirmed separately from a command such as ConfirmOrder choose a payload shape deliberately: enough data for an independent consumer, but no private aggregate structure or undeletable PII key by the entity whose order matters, and state that order is only within a partition or delivery scope give every event a stable identity and schema version; do not use a body-level version field to hide an uncontrolled compatibility break Compatibility mode is a policy of a particular registry/subject and deployment sequence, not a universal property of all messages. Additive fields are commonly safer; removal, rename, enum narrowing and semantic changes need a migration plan. Test old and new consumers against the actual wire format. If compatibility cannot be maintained: publish a new version/topic dual-publish for a bounded migration window move consumers retire the old contract A compacted log is a state projection with tombstone and retention semantics, not a substitute for deletion/privacy design. Replay also needs authorization, rate limits and safe effects. Contract-test the fields consumers actually read; the compatibility test boundary is (26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q4), and the data-protection implications are in (13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q3).","contextEn":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","href":"/topics/08-message-queue#question-08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q10"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q1","surface":"track","en":"How should you choose local ACID, 2PC/XA, or a Saga?","bodyEn":"Start with the invariant, not the pattern name. Local ACID first: keep data that must change atomically in one authority and one database transaction. 2PC/XA: use it only when every participant, coordinator, latency budget, and recovery owner are deliberate and supported. Saga: use local transactions plus explicit forward recovery or compensation when the boundary is genuinely distributed. 2PC is not universally wrong. While prepared and unresolved, participants can hold locks or other resources; a coordinator or participant failure leaves an in-doubt recovery problem. That makes it a bounded option, not a good default for long human or provider calls. Bottom line A service boundary alone is not a reason to distribute a transaction. Name the authority, failure budget, recovery owner, and user-visible states before choosing the pattern (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q5). 2PC can provide atomic commit within its protocol scope, but not unlimited availability. A Saga does not provide atomic intermediate states; it coordinates local commits toward a declared desired state under eventual consistency. Each local transaction remains atomic only within its own authority. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: Which provider contract the example assumes — Stripe and Adyen differ on idempotency keys and retries — or say the pseudocode is provider-neutral. The real reconciliation SLA for payment, inventory and booking. Without it, \"eventual\" is a hope, not an operational contract. Whether 2PC is actually available in the target databases and drivers and has a recovery owner; if not, 2PC stays a bounded comparison, never runnable guidance. The retention window for late retries and callbacks. If it outlives the idempotency-key TTL, the recommendation is falsified until a durable business reference exists. Whether the ledger can prove a zero-sum posting invariant under crash and replay. One failing property test falsifies any \"exactly-once money movement\" wording.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q1"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2","surface":"track","en":"Saga: choreography vs orchestration? Compensating transaction?","bodyEn":"Saga splits a long business operation into local transactions. It coordinates toward a declared desired state; a failed step uses a compensating transaction, which is a new business operation, not a database rollback. Compensation can fail, be delayed, be rejected, or be impossible after an irreversible effect. Choreography: services react to events without a conductor; useful for small, stable flows, but ownership and recovery can become hard to see. Orchestration: a durable workflow state store drives steps, timers, retries, and compensation; it improves visibility but adds a control-plane dependency. A replicated workflow engine changes the crash mode, not the business invariant. Every step and compensation needs an idempotent command and explicit states such as PENDING, COMPENSATING, and RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED. Put irreversible work after a declared pivot when possible. Generic Saga trade-offs are cross-referenced in (25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q3); the OTA flow belongs in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5). Boundary Intermediate states are observable. Do not let a user or another workflow spend against a balance, hold, or payment that has not reached the authority's settled state.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3","surface":"track","en":"The Outbox pattern, and why is dual-write dangerous?","bodyEn":"Dual write means committing business state and publishing externally as separate steps. A failure between them creates drift. Transactional Outbox writes the integration event intent in the same local transaction as the business change. The outbox closes only the state & 8596; publish-intent boundary. A relay or CDC connector may publish at least once, and can crash after publish before marking progress. The consumer therefore needs an Inbox, a unique event ID, or an equivalent idempotent effect in the same local transaction as its state change. Outbox does not prove that a broker delivered, a consumer applied, or a PSP settled the effect. Boundary This unit owns the workflow meaning and recovery boundary. Broker confirms, acknowledgements, partitions, and replay mechanics belong to (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q2), (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6), and (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7). Retain an event ID, relay error/age metrics, repair and redrive policy, and a versioned integration contract. Put public integration events in the outbox, not every internal domain event (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3).","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q4","surface":"track","en":"How does TCC (Try-Confirm-Cancel) differ from Saga?","bodyEn":"TCC is a protocol shape, not an automatic synonym for a Saga or a seat hold. Try reserves a resource. Confirm commits the reservation. Cancel releases it when the workflow cannot continue. Saga TCC Order perform, then compensate reserve, then confirm or cancel Visibility intermediate business state is observable reservation is observable and may expire Isolation depends on compensation and authority can be stronger for the reserved resource Cost local op plus compensation three idempotent phases per participant Each phase needs an owner, idempotency, empty-rollback handling, expiry, and recovery policy. Try, Confirm, and Cancel must each tolerate duplicate or late calls under that contract. A seat hold or PSP authorize/capture flow may be TCC-like only when its provider contract actually exposes those phases; do not infer it from similar names. The concrete OTA race and hold invariant are in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2). TCC can reduce exposure of an uncommitted resource, but it does not remove timeouts, stale reservations, provider failure, or reconciliation.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q4"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q5","surface":"track","en":"Two services need to stay consistent without a distributed transaction — what are the options?","bodyEn":"Choose the authority and atomic boundary first: Local ACID when the invariant is genuinely shared: keep both ledger legs or the inventory transition in one bounded context and database. Provider atomic contract or 2PC/XA only when its participant, latency, availability, and recovery contract is explicit and acceptable. Saga + Outbox when the split is real: accept observable intermediate states, idempotent steps, compensation, and reconciliation. Eventual projections for search, notifications, and reporting; revalidate against the authority before a commit decision. Idempotency belongs at each command, consumer, and provider boundary; these are different keys and contracts. Reconciliation is a control loop, not proof that the online invariant was safe. Key takeaway Service boundaries should usually follow consistency boundaries, but participant count alone is not the decision rule. Ownership, reversibility, risk, latency, and recovery evidence matter. The end-to-end money-transfer comparison lives in (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q4). If a booking, payment, or ledger operation crosses a real external authority, model PENDING, UNKNOWN, COMPENSATING, and RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED rather than pretending a local rollback can undo it.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q5"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q6","surface":"track","en":"Distributed lock (Redis/Redlock) — when to use it, and what are the risks?","bodyEn":"A lock coordinates ownership of an attempt; it is not the authority for money or inventory correctness. A short lease can prevent duplicate scheduler work: SET key value NX PX ttl. Risk: a pause, partition, or slow operation can outlive the TTL, so an old worker and a new worker may overlap. Use an ownership token, safe release, renewal bounds, and fencing tokens when the resource can reject stale writers. Fintech Let the database or provider authority enforce the invariant with a unique constraint, version, conditional write, or documented idempotency contract. A lock only reduces contention. See (28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q1) and (28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q3). Redlock and other multi-node locks have implementation- and timing-dependent safety claims. Do not turn a lock expiry into a second charge, ledger posting, or event; use durable state and reconciliation instead.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q6"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1","surface":"track","en":"Real-world scenario: a call to a PSP/bank times out and you don't know whether the money was already deducted?","bodyEn":"Unknown outcome means there is not enough evidence to call the operation either failed or succeeded. Claim one local payment attempt and stable merchant reference. Use a provider idempotency key when that provider documents its scope and retention. On timeout, store PENDING or UNKNOWN; do not create a new logical charge with a new key. Use the provider's status inquiry or verified callback when available. If evidence remains incomplete, keep an owned unknown queue and reconcile it against provider records or settlement. Move to SUCCEEDED, FAILED, COMPENSATING, or RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED only through legal, audited state transitions. Bottom line A timeout is a missing observation, not proof of failure. The local state machine, provider contract, and reconciliation SLA decide when the user can be told the outcome. CREATE TABLE payment_attempt( id uuid PRIMARY KEY, idempotency_key text UNIQUE NOT NULL, provider_ref text UNIQUE, status text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('CREATED','SUBMITTED','PENDING','UNKNOWN','SUCCEEDED','FAILED', 'RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED')) ); -- claim one local attempt, then call the provider outside this DB tx UPDATE payment_attempt SET status='SUBMITTED' WHERE id=:id AND status='CREATED'; COMMIT; -- timeout: preserve uncertainty, do not mark FAILED UPDATE payment_attempt SET status='UNKNOWN' WHERE id=:id AND status='SUBMITTED'; -- inquiry/callback/reconciliation applies one legal transition UPDATE payment_attempt SET status='SUCCEEDED', provider_ref=:ref WHERE id=:id AND status IN ('PENDING','UNKNOWN','SUBMITTED'); The provider contract is integration-specific; not every PSP or bank offers equivalent idempotency, inquiry, callback, or retention semantics (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1). Make the unknown queue operational by storing unknown_since, next_inquiry_at, reconciliation_deadline, owner, and a risk tier. Age/risk buckets, not a magic timeout. Tie each bucket to the provider's response and settlement SLAs: within the documented window: back off and inquire past it: escalate to settlement or manual review when policy requires Until an authority proves the outcome, client expiry does not authorize a second charge. Track unknown age, retry owner, callback verification, inquiry attempts, settlement drift, and manual escalation. Auto-void or refund is a policy after evidence and eligibility, not a blind timeout handler.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2","surface":"track","en":"Idempotency in payments: how do you implement it airtight?","bodyEn":"Treat idempotency as three separate boundaries: Command claim: scope the key to tenant, account, operation, or endpoint; store a request fingerprint and reject the same key with a different payload. Local result: commit the claim, business transition, final result, and any Outbox row in one local transaction. Outbox is an additional intent row, not a replacement for the key/result record. Provider command: reuse the provider key or merchant reference only under that provider's documented contract. INSERT INTO idempotency_keys(scope, key, request_fingerprint, status, response) VALUES (?, ?, ?, 'IN_PROGRESS' , NULL ); -- UNIQUE(scope, key) A duplicate with the same fingerprint returns the stored result, polls an IN_PROGRESS attempt, or receives a documented conflict. A different fingerprint is a conflict, not a retry. Keep records for longer than the client/provider retry and callback windows, or retain a durable business reference; a short cache TTL is not a correctness boundary. Before production, pin the provider's behavior: API/version and account/region key scope and retention payload-mismatch and concurrent-reuse behavior status inquiry webhook authenticity/replay behavior settlement/reversal semantics Stripe and Adyen document different idempotency details; copy neither as a universal default. Do not promise that an HTTP method, broker delivery mode, or local key alone prevents an external duplicate. Name the boundary and test concurrent duplicates, late callbacks, result replay, payload mismatch, and retention expiry (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1).","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3","surface":"track","en":"Double-entry bookkeeping — why is it the gold standard for a ledger?","bodyEn":"A posting is a controlled batch of signed entries: for one currency or asset, the entries must balance according to the ledger's invariant. A normal transfer has debit and credit legs; issuance, fees, FX, and reversals use explicit accounts and policies rather than silently breaking the equation. The ledger is append-only: do not destructively update or delete a posted entry. Correct a mistake with an authorized reversing or adjustment entry, preserving actor, reason, time, and source reference. Commit both legs and the posting identity atomically in the ledger authority, or leave the posting pending. Make the posting command idempotent with a unique business key. Derive a balance from entries, or update a materialized balance in the same local transaction and reconcile it periodically. Check the zero-sum invariant per posting batch and currency; it is not a single sum across unrelated currencies. Bottom line The ledger is the book of record; workflow state, locks, caches, and projections must not silently become a second money authority (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q4). Separate pending, posted, reversed, and disputed states. Restrict administrative adjustments, keep an immutable audit trail, and reconcile ledger postings against provider or settlement evidence.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q4","surface":"track","en":"Why not use float for money? What's the correct way to store it?","bodyEn":"Binary float/double cannot represent many decimal fractions exactly, so repeated arithmetic can drift. Use a representation with an explicit domain policy: DECIMAL(p,s) or BigDecimal for decimal amounts, with scale and rounding chosen deliberately. Integer minor units only when the currency and operation have a fixed, validated exponent; do not assume every currency is cents-based. Store the currency code, scale, and, for FX, the rate/source/time and rounding result. Never add unlike currencies. Validate currency metadata and business rounding rules against the chosen ISO 4217/domain source; HALF_EVEN is an example policy, not a universal law. The representation does not prove provider settlement, chargeback, tax, KYC/AML, PCI, or other legal/compliance obligations. Keep those as integration-specific contracts and owners rather than inferring them from DECIMAL or BigDecimal. new BigDecimal(&quot;0.1&quot;) .add( new BigDecimal(&quot;0.2&quot;)) .setScale( 2 , RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN); // example: 0.30 Test tax, fee, FX, split-payment, reversal, and reconciliation rounding at the currency boundary. The storage type prevents one class of error; it does not choose the accounting policy for you.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q4"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you prevent double-spend / balance races under high load?","bodyEn":"Do not read a balance, decide in application code, and write later. Put the linearization point in the authoritative database transaction: Use a conditional update such as UPDATE account SET available_minor=available_minor-? WHERE id=? AND currency=? AND available_minor>=?; zero rows means the condition was not met. Use optimistic version checks with bounded retry, or a short pessimistic row lock when contention and isolation require it. Use unique constraints for one logical debit or posting, and append both ledger legs atomically. Revalidate any stale projection at the final authorization boundary. A distributed lock may reduce contention, but it cannot replace these guards (28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q1). Bottom line Money correctness must be anchored to the ledger/account authority: conditional state transition, uniqueness, balance invariant, and durable idempotency. Queueing or sharding can protect throughput, but they are not proof of correctness. For a hot resource, serialize or partition attempts only as a capacity tactic. If a worker loses ownership or a request times out, preserve UNKNOWN and reconcile; never retry by creating a second logical debit.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q5"},{"id":"09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6","surface":"track","en":"Eventual consistency in fintech: where is it acceptable, and where is it forbidden?","bodyEn":"Eventual consistency is acceptable for a projection whose staleness is explicit and cannot authorize money movement: displayed history, notifications, reports, analytics, and reward views. At the final decision boundary, use the authoritative transaction or conditional write for available balance, limits, ledger posting, anti-double-spend, and inventory or hold confirmation. A read may begin the request from a projection, but it must be revalidated before commit. Do not turn a freshness preference into a universal slogan The requirement is strong correctness at the invariant boundary, not that every read in a fintech product must synchronously query one database. Define freshness, PENDING, UNKNOWN, and escalation behaviour for each projection. For OTA or commerce, search availability may be stale while final hold/payment revalidation is authoritative (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4). For money, reconcile ledger, provider, Outbox/relay, and projection drift with an owner and an age SLA. Observe unknown/pending age, reconciliation lag, duplicate/replay rate, provider status drift, ledger imbalance, and projection freshness. Test concurrent retries, late callbacks, provider timeout, replay, reconciliation repair, and the boundary that prevents a stale projection from authorizing money.","contextEn":"Transactions across services (fintech)","href":"/topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech#question-09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1","surface":"track","en":"How would you lead a 45-minute system-design interview from requirements to a defensible design, and decide which assumptions, capacities, risks, and trade-offs deserve a deep dive?","bodyEn":"7 steps: Clarify requirements: functional + non-functional (scale, p99 latency, availability, consistency) Capacity estimation: read/write QPS, storage, bandwidth API design: a few core endpoints Data model: tables/collections, indexes, SQL vs NoSQL High-level diagram: client → LB → service → cache/DB/queue Deep dive into 1-2 critical components Bottleneck & trade-off: pain points, how to scale, what you're trading off Bottom line lead the conversation, keep surfacing trade-offs and confirming assumptions. What's judged is the thinking and reasoning, not a perfect diagram. start by clarifying functional + non-functional requirements (QPS, p99 latency, availability, consistency, read/write ratio) — don't dive straight into drawing boxes keep stating trade-offs & confirming assumptions ('assume 10M bookings/day, p99 < 200ms, strong ledger consistency — correct?') deep-dive into 1-2 core components The interviewer grades your thought process (decomposition, trade-offs, bottlenecks), not a pretty diagram. Close with 'where's the bottleneck, how would it scale, what's the trade-off' — that's what sets senior apart. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: Which limiter authority and failure policy the application needs: local best-effort, Redis with bounded overage, a gateway service, or strongly consistent — and the maximum overage the business can reconcile after a failover. Whether the guarded commands are idempotent. If not, say what stops a retry after a timeout from performing the business action twice. The trusted proxy chain behind an IP-based key, and how NAT and shared-IP users are treated. The Redis version, client and cluster topology the code targets — script and function behaviour is version-sensitive. The measurements that would falsify the design: p99 decision latency above the endpoint budget, key skew making one shard hot, a failover losing more than the agreed overage. If the authoritative resource cannot enforce fencing or version checks, downgrade every \"the limiter preserves correctness\" claim to \"it reduces load\".","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/design-review-framework#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2","surface":"track","en":"Given daily volume, peak-to-average ratio, payload size, replication, and retention, how do you estimate read/write QPS, storage, bandwidth, and the first capacity threshold that changes the architecture?","bodyEn":"Use 86,400 seconds as the arithmetic constant; the other values are assumptions until measured. Assumption table (illustrative, not a benchmark): Input Illustrative assumption Measure before sizing Volume 10M/day → 115.7 average QPS peak/average, p95/p99 burst, retry attempts Payload 500 B logical record wire bytes, headers, compression Storage 1.8 TB/year raw index, metadata, replicas, retention Capacity no portable QPS number code path, hardware, runtime, DB, p99 target Average QPS = daily volume / 86,400; peak QPS = average × the stated peak-to-average factor. Bytes/s = peak QPS × measured request/response bytes. Storage = records × logical size × index/metadata/replica/retention factors. reads ≫ writes can justify cache/replicas only when freshness and authorization boundaries allow it. Measurement exercise: Replay a representative read/write mix and payload distribution against one candidate instance and database. Sweep arrival rate and concurrency until p99, errors, queue age, connection wait, CPU/memory, or I/O crosses its budget. Repeat with retry amplification and dependency-failure injection; record the safe operating point, burst duration, and rollback threshold. Bottom line Use the arithmetic to find the first bounded resource; treat every capacity number as a workload-specific result. Keep R_sustained, R_peak, payload, retries, replication, and retention as explicit inputs; include headers and compression in byte estimates. A statement such as “one instance handles a few thousand QPS” is not portable without the code path, hardware, runtime, dependency shape, and tail-latency target. The architecture changes when a bounded resource reaches its safe limit: bytes, file descriptors, connections, CPU, memory, queue age, DB locks/I/O, or provider quota.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/design-review-framework#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3","surface":"track","en":"For a read-heavy service, which caching pattern and invalidation contract would you choose, and how would you prevent stale decisions and cache stampedes?","bodyEn":"A cache is a bounded, disposable copy; choose it only after defining what may be stale and which decision must read the source of truth. Cache-aside (lazy): the application reads the cache; on a miss it reads the database and populates the cache. It is the common default for derived reads. Write-through: update the cache and database in the write path. It can reduce stale reads, but the two stores do not share one transaction; define retry and recovery when either write fails. Write-behind: accept a cache write first and persist later. It improves write latency only when loss, replay, and ordering are explicitly acceptable. Invalidation contract: commit the source-of-truth change first, record an outbox or invalidation event atomically with that change, and let consumers delete or version cache entries. TTL limits the damage from a missed event; it is not proof of freshness. Stampede/thundering herd: when a hot key expires, many requests can rebuild it at once. Add TTL jitter, request coalescing (single-flight), refresh-ahead, and a bounded rebuild budget. The race, drawn out. It is two threads and four steps, and every cache-aside deployment has it: time reader (cache miss) writer (committed change) ---- --------------------- ------------------------ t0 GET price:42 -> miss t1 SELECT ... id=42 -> 100 // reads the OLD row t2 UPDATE price=120; COMMIT t3 DEL price:42 // deletes nothing t4 SET price:42 = 100 // writes the OLD value back // // the cache now holds 100 for a full TTL, and both sides did exactly // what they were told to. No retry and no ordering fixes this, because // the reader's SELECT legitimately happened before the write. A versioned key removes the race instead of narrowing it. The reader can no longer write into the key the next reader will look at: // the version is authoritative, tiny, and cheap to read long v = versions. get ( \"price\" , id); // 42 -> 7 Price p = cache. get ( \"price:\" + id + \":v\" + v, // price:42:v7 () -> db. load (id)); // // the write bumps the version inside the same transaction as the row, // so readers move to price:42:v8 the moment the change is visible. // The stale v7 entry is unreachable and simply ages out. Delayed double-delete — delete, wait, delete again — only shrinks the window. It is a mitigation with a tunable failure rate, and it should be described that way rather than as a fix. Bound the rebuild, not just the expiry. Single-flight collapses concurrent misses for one key; a cold cache is many keys missing at once, which is a different failure: Control What it protects against What it does not TTL jitter keys loaded together expiring together a hot key that legitimately expires alone Single-flight N concurrent misses on one key becoming N origin reads N different keys missing at once Refresh-ahead the expiry cliff on a key that is always hot keys whose next read is unpredictable Rebuild budget a cold start or failover flooding the origin nothing — this is the backstop the other three assume exists The rebuild budget is a semaphore around the origin call, sized from what the database can serve unaided. If it is full, serve stale or fail fast — that decision is the contract, and it belongs in the design rather than in whatever the code happens to do. Fintech For a monetary or authorization decision, read a strongly consistent ledger or policy source. A cache may support display data only when the product explicitly accepts its staleness. cache-aside plus invalidation after a committed write is a useful default for derived reads, not a cross-store transaction. Include tenant, authorization dimension, and schema version in the key where they affect the result. a read-miss fill can race a committed write. Use a versioned value or a post-commit invalidation path and retain a short TTL as recovery. Delayed double-delete is only a best-effort mitigation, never a correctness guarantee. a hot key needs deliberate routing: coalesce rebuilds, use a small local cache for the hot subset, or replicate only when writes and invalidation reach every replica. Randomly reading key 1..N without that contract lowers the hit rate instead of adding capacity. OTA: cache browsing results for seconds. When a supplier provides a feed, webhook, or poll result, normalize it through an invalidation pipeline; before a hold or charge, revalidate price and availability with the authoritative inventory or supplier boundary. Recommendation boundary: Cache-aside, versioned keys, TTL jitter, single-flight, and delayed double-delete are recommendations for derived reads, not correctness guarantees. Before a correctness-critical mutation, revalidate price, inventory, policy, ledger, or authorization against its authority; make invalidation loss observable and repairable.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/traffic-caching-building-blocks#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q4","surface":"track","en":"When should traffic be routed at L4 versus L7, and how do protocol behavior, connection reuse, health checks, and session or cache locality affect that choice?","bodyEn":"L4 L7 Layer transport connection (IP/port) application protocol, commonly HTTP Routing signal address and connection state path, host, header, cookie, or RPC metadata Useful controls low-overhead connection distribution TLS termination, request routing, canary, WAF, and request-aware health checks Choose the layer from the routing and operational decision you need, not from a belief that one layer always scales better. Round-robin, least-connections, weighted routing, and consistent hashing are policies with different fairness, locality, and failure behavior. Drain connections and use health checks that represent the work the backend can actually serve. Bottom line Stateless request handling makes horizontal scaling easier, but state still exists in databases, caches, queues, connections, and long-lived streams. Name where it lives and how it recovers. L4 balances connections. L7 can inspect and route individual HTTP requests or RPC metadata. Neither removes the need for capacity, connection draining, and backpressure. gRPC/HTTP2 multiplexes many RPCs over one connection. an L4 proxy normally selects a backend per connection, so one busy client connection can be concentrated. the answer is topology-dependent: use an HTTP/2-aware L7 proxy, or gRPC client-side balancing with enough backend connections; do not claim L7 is the only solution. use consistent hashing only when locality is worth its skew and failure consequences. Sticky sessions are a compatibility choice, not a substitute for moving durable session state out of one instance. OTA: an L7 gateway can centralize authentication and coarse rate limits, while internal gRPC clients still need deadlines, connection management, and service-level authorization.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/traffic-caching-building-blocks#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q4"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q5","surface":"track","en":"Which responses may a CDN safely serve for this workload, how should cache keys, TTLs, and purge behavior be defined, and where must the origin remain authoritative?","bodyEn":"A CDN places content (static & cacheable) close to the user (edge) → reduces latency & load on the origin. controlled via Cache-Control/TTL, cache key, invalidation/purge edge compute for lightweight logic not a fit for personalized/frequently-changing data unless configured carefully CDN caches static & cacheable content at the edge near the user (PoP) → reduces latency + origin load + absorbs large spikes/DDoS at the static layer Cache-Control/TTL, cache key (including select query params/headers), purge on change, edge compute (Workers/Lambda@Edge) OTA: CDN for assets (destination images, JS/CSS), can cache popular search results (hot routes) with a short TTL at the edge; but personalized price/seat & checkout are not cached at the CDN. Consider stale-while-revalidate for fast UX. Three things determine edge hit ratio, ranked by how often they're gotten wrong: cache key (one extra utm_ param or an Accept-Language header fragments the cache — normalize the query string before computing the key), TTL, then Vary. Two directives worth remembering: stale-while-revalidate (serve the old version immediately while refreshing in the background — fast UX) and stale-if-error (if the origin is down, there's still a page to serve).","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/traffic-caching-building-blocks#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q5"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q6","surface":"track","en":"For a service running on ten pods, when do you use an L1 local cache, a shared L2 cache, or both—and how will you bound stale reads, invalidation loss, deploy compatibility, and origin fallback?","bodyEn":"Choosing where the cache lives is a consistency decision disguised as a performance one. Local (Caffeine) Distributed (Redis) Multi-level Read latency nanoseconds, in-heap a network round trip local hit, else Redis Consistency N pods, N versions one shared version bounded by the local TTL Invalidation hard — must reach every pod one delete delete plus a broadcast Capacity bounded by heap, duplicated one dataset, sized independently hot subset local If it dies nothing — the pod restarts cold a dependency outage for everyone degrades to the level below The local-cache trade you are actually making: with a 60-second TTL and ten pods, a value updated now can be served stale by nine of them for up to a minute. That is fine for reference data and unacceptable for a balance or a permission (10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3). To keep local caches honest you need a fan-out: publish an invalidation on a Redis pub/sub channel that every pod subscribes to, or make the key carry a version so a new value simply lands under a new key. // multi-level: local absorbs the hot keys, Redis holds the truth Fare quote (String route) { return local.get(route, k -> // L1: short TTL, per pod redis.get( \"fares:v3:\" + k, () -> // L2: shared, longer TTL db.load(k))); // source of truth } A rolling deploy makes both versions share one cache While the rollout runs, old and new code read the same Redis. Change the shape of a cached value without bumping the key prefix and the new pods fail deserializing old entries — on every request that hits a warm key. treat the cache as a dependency that can fail and decide fail-open or fail-closed per use. Falling through to the database is right for a product page and wrong for a rate limiter that would then stop limiting (10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q4). a cold restart after an eviction is a stampede against the database. Warm critical keys on startup, or coalesce misses so one loader wins (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q1). keep values small and boring: Redis executes commands on a single thread, so one huge value or one KEYS scan adds latency to every other client (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q6). hot keys defeat a distributed cache the same way they defeat a shard; a small local cache in front of exactly those keys is the standard answer (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q2). the annotation-level version of these decisions — TTL placement, key design, eviction on rollback — lives in (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q6).","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/traffic-caching-building-blocks#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q6"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7","surface":"track","en":"How should a capacity estimate include bytes, concurrency, retries, and the first architectural threshold?","bodyEn":"Start with a workload table, not a single QPS number: average request rate = daily volume / 86,400; peak rate = average × the stated peak-to-average factor; ingress or egress bytes/second = peak requests/second × the measured payload distribution, including headers and compression assumptions; retained storage = records × logical size × index, replica, metadata, and retention factors; in-flight work is approximately arrival rate × latency (Little's law), then split it across connection, thread, worker, database, and downstream pools; retry amplification = original work plus every retry attempt, multiplied again when several layers retry the same failure. The first architecture threshold is whichever bounded resource reaches its safe operating limit first: network bytes, file descriptors, connection acquisition, CPU, memory, queue age, database I/O/locks, or a downstream quota. State the limit, target utilization, burst duration, and what happens at saturation. A fixed claim such as “one instance handles X QPS” is not portable without payload, code path, latency target and failure assumptions. Interview contract Show one optimistic, one peak, and one retry-amplified scenario. The point is to expose the threshold that changes the design, not to pretend a rough estimate is a benchmark.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/design-review-framework#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q1","surface":"track","en":"Design a token-bucket limit for a named API: what is the key, refill rate, burst allowance, client response contract, and atomic state in single-node and distributed deployments?","bodyEn":"The bucket holds up to B tokens, refilling at r tokens/second. each request consumes 1 token; out of tokens → reject/wait allows burst up to B (flexible), average rate limited to r the most common choice for APIs pseudo now = time() tokens = min(B, tokens + (now-last)r) last = now if tokens >= 1: tokens -= 1; allow() else : reject() token bucket allows bursts (good for APIs with spikes); leaky bucket smooths output (protects a weak downstream) distributed needs atomicity: store (tokens, last_refill) + compute lazy refill inside a single Redis Lua script (atomic read-compute-write) — don't split into a GET then SET (race condition) calling Redis on every request adds latency → you can use a local per-instance token bucket with a quota = total/N (less accurate under uneven load) Return a documented machine-readable rejection. Use 429 for a caller-facing rate rejection. Retry-After is an API policy choice, and emit X-RateLimit-Remaining only when its value and reset semantics are truthful; do not promise these headers at every internal layer. The whiteboard version of this question is (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1). The full token bucket implementation, including synchronization when multiple threads grab tokens concurrently, is in the LLD round (22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q3). Algorithm contract — complete these fields before writing code: Field Contract Key / authority policy + tenant + subject + route; name the store/slot that owns one transition Clock / refill monotonic local clock or one server authority; state precision and skew rule Burst / response capacity B, token cost, allow/reject/wait, 429 and retry semantics Atomicity / expiry refill + consume + expiry in one transition; expiry is a tested cleanup bound Failover / overage fail-open/closed/local mode, maximum allowed overage, and reconcile path The contract distinguishes an algorithm guarantee from a deployment guarantee: a correct token transition does not promise global accuracy when authorities partition or lose acknowledged state.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/distributed-rate-limiter#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q1"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q2","surface":"track","en":"Compare token and leaky buckets for an API and a quota-bound downstream: which arrival and output behavior, queue bound, latency, and drop policy make each appropriate?","bodyEn":"Token bucket Leaky bucket Burst allowed (up to B) no (steady output) Output accumulates then spends forced steady, fixed rate Over limit reject/wait queue full → drop Good for APIs with natural spikes protecting a weak downstream (smoothing) token bucket = 'allow accumulation then spend it'; leaky = 'force a steady rate regardless of what accumulated' OTA: fighting price-scraping bots → token bucket per-IP/key (blocks abuse but lets real users burst a bit) protecting the GDS (a hard quota from the provider) → leaky bucket smooths requests to the GDS so it never exceeds quota real systems typically use both at once: token bucket at the entry point (metered per client) and leaky bucket at the exit point (metered against the provider's quota). They limit two different things, so one doesn't substitute for the other. Code for both, including synchronization when multiple threads grab tokens concurrently, is in the LLD round (22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q3).","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/distributed-rate-limiter#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q2"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q3","surface":"track","en":"For a per-user limit, how do fixed windows, sliding-window logs, and sliding-window counters differ in boundary overshoot, accuracy, state cost, and an atomic distributed implementation?","bodyEn":"Algorithm Accuracy Cost Issue Fixed window counter low cheap (1 counter) double burst at the boundary Sliding window log exact expensive memory (O(n)) — Sliding window counter near-exact cheap (2 counters) balanced choice fixed window double burst: 100/minute can allow ~200 within the 2 seconds straddling the boundary sliding window log stores every request's timestamp → exact accuracy but O(n) memory sliding window counter interpolates between the current & previous windows (weighted) → near-exact, cheap Choose based on requirements: billing/hard quotas need accuracy (sliding counter/log); coarse abuse prevention only needs a fixed window. Distributed setups need atomicity (Redis Lua). Sliding-window-counter and token bucket code side by side, plus how to nail down thread-safety when the interviewer asks (22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q3).","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/distributed-rate-limiter#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q3"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q4","surface":"track","en":"How would you implement a Redis-backed shared rate limiter so refill and consumption are atomic, keys expire safely, Cluster key placement and time source are explicit, and outage behavior is deliberate?","bodyEn":"Shared state in Redis can coordinate stateless instances, but the design must say exactly what is atomic, where the key lives, and what happens when the limiter is unavailable. for a fixed window, increment and expiry must be installed as one operation; a crash between separate INCR and EXPIRE calls can leave an unbounded counter. for token or sliding-window algorithms, one short Redis Lua script can read state, calculate refill or window weight, decide, update state, and set expiry atomically on the serving primary. atomicity is per script execution, not a global guarantee across regions, caches, or a failed primary. Keep scripts short because they block other work on that Redis shard. Bottom line The critical operation is check plus consume plus expiry as one state transition. A separate GET followed by SET is a race, but a Lua script is only correct when its key, time, and failure policy are also explicit. use a stable key such as policy plus tenant plus subject plus route. In Redis Cluster, every key touched by one script must be in the same hash slot; a per-subject bucket is simplest, and multi-key designs need deliberate hash tags. choose one time authority. Redis server time avoids disagreement among application clocks, while a local fallback needs a documented bounded overshoot. Do not silently mix both. choose fail-open, fail-closed, or a smaller emergency local limit from the threat and business invariant; a login endpoint may need brute-force protection even while a broad public read API prefers availability. record the maximum tolerated overage and the reconciliation path, and measure and alert on every fallback. return 429 and a documented retry delay when the policy denies a request. Emit limit or remaining headers only when they describe the actual policy and are safe to expose. apply coarse edge limits, gateway policy limits, service business limits, and downstream concurrency guards for different purposes rather than treating Redis as the only protective layer. Runnable Redis Lua token bucket — save as token_bucket.lua: local rate = tonumber(ARGV[1]) local capacity = tonumber(ARGV[2]) local cost = tonumber(ARGV[3]) local t = redis.call('TIME') local now = t[1] 1000 + math.floor(t[2] / 1000) local state = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts') local tokens = tonumber(state[1]) or capacity local previous = tonumber(state[2]) or now tokens = math.min(capacity, tokens + math.max(0, now-previous) rate / 1000) local allowed = 0 if tokens >= cost then tokens = tokens - cost; allowed = 1 end redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now) redis.call('EXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(capacity / rate 2)) return {allowed, math.floor(tokens), now} Run one key with rate 10/s, capacity 20, cost 1: redis-cli --eval token_bucket.lua 'rl:{tenant-42}:checkout' , 10 20 1 first result: 1 (allowed), 19 (tokens left), server time TIME avoids client-clock skew, one script makes refill/consume/expiry atomic, and {tenant-42} makes Cluster slot ownership explicit. Benefit: every gateway sees shared state. Cost: Redis latency joins admission; a long script blocks server work; and a hot tenant serializes decisions on one shard. Measure script p99, key skew, and fallback/error rates before increasing concurrency. Failure boundary: define fail-open/closed per resource; at cell scale prefer per-cell allocation over a global Redis round trip. Durability and expiry boundary: Redis replication is asynchronous by default. A primary can acknowledge a consume before the replica has it; failover may forget accepted tokens. Treat this as bounded overage, not exact quota accounting. math.ceil(capacity / rate 2) is a cleanup heuristic, not a safety proof. Test idle expiry, refill precision, clock skew, failover, and resharding. On MOVED, ASK, timeout, or an unknown script outcome, retry only a decision that is safe to repeat; otherwise record UNKNOWN and reconcile before repeating the business command.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/distributed-rate-limiter#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q4"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q5","surface":"track","en":"Which limits belong at the edge, gateway, service, and downstream boundary, and how do rate limit, quota, concurrency limit, and overload shedding differ in keying, response, and policy?","bodyEn":"Multiple layers: edge/CDN: blocks coarse DDoS (by IP) API gateway: per API key/IP, plan quotas service: per user/endpoint, business rules downstream guard: protects DB/PSP/GDS Return a documented machine-readable error shape. A caller-facing rate decision commonly uses 429; include retry guidance only when the request is safe to retry and the delay is meaningful. Distinguish: rate limit (abuse prevention) vs quota (plan/billing limit) vs throttling/backpressure (protects the system under overload) vs concurrency limit (semaphore/bulkhead) OTA: per-key rate limiting on search fights price-scraping bots; quota for B2B partners per contract; leaky bucket protects the GDS; a concurrency limit (semaphore) to the PSP. Set limits at multiple layers for different purposes, not just one place. The further out you block, the cheaper it is: blocking at the CDN costs almost nothing, whereas by the time a request reaches the service it has already passed the gateway and occupied a connection in the pool. The trade-off is that the further out you go, the less business context you have — the edge doesn't know which plan this user is on. Define one machine-readable error shape, but do not force every layer to emit identical headers. 429 is the usual caller-facing rate rejection; RFC 6585 permits Retry-After, so send it when the retry delay is known and retry is safe. A dependency timeout or overload may instead surface as 503/504, and clients must not retry those blindly. X-RateLimit- or newer RateLimit fields are compatibility choices: document their units and reset semantics, and omit a value you cannot calculate truthfully. The gateway can map internal rejection reasons to the public contract. Decision table: Control Key / unit Response / policy Metrics Rate subject + route; requests/time 429; burst and overage policy decision p99; allow/reject Quota tenant + plan; period units contract rejection; usage authority usage, reset, reconcile Concurrency dependency + class; in-flight wait or reject; reserve critical in-flight, permit wait Backpressure queue/stream; buffer capacity slow producer; bounded buffer queue fill, oldest age Shedding priority class; accepted work degrade or reject bulk rejection, deadline, critical success Protocol boundary: RFC 2697 and RFC 2698 define marker terminology and token-rate limits; they are not a complete HTTP API policy. RFC 6585 is the source for 429 Too Many Requests. Retry-After and any response fields still need an API contract. The RateLimit header fields in the reviewed source are an Internet-Draft, not a final RFC. Treat RateLimit-/RateLimit fields as version-sensitive and provide a compatibility fallback.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/distributed-rate-limiter#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q5"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q6","surface":"track","en":"When the shared limiter is unavailable or partitioned, how do you choose fail-open, fail-closed, or local fallback?","bodyEn":"Classify the endpoint by the invariant it protects before choosing an outage mode. Authentication, password reset, expensive partner calls, money-moving commands, and abuse-sensitive operations usually need fail-closed or a small, explicit local emergency budget. Public browsing or a cacheable read may prefer fail-open for a short window, but record the exception and cap it with a local limiter so an outage does not become unlimited traffic. A local fallback is not equivalent to a global quota: each instance can overshoot, and a network partition can make the aggregate error large. Allocate per-cell budgets when the business can tolerate bounded overshoot. The decision belongs to the policy owner, not to a generic Redis client default. Emit metrics for limiter latency, errors, fallback decisions, denied requests, estimated overshoot and recovery. Reconcile billing or quota state from authoritative usage records; a rate limiter is an admission control, not a correctness ledger. Document the mode per route and failure reason, test failover and clock/region partitions, and alert before the emergency budget is exhausted. The shared Lua transition and Cluster key rules remain the mechanism; this item owns the failure policy. Crash-window and test matrix: Scenario Risk Required assertion Primary ACK then failover accepted tokens disappear overage ≤ policy or reconcile Clock skew/backward jump wrong refill/expiry bounded decision; no negative refill Cluster reshard (MOVED/ASK) routing or unknown result one-slot keys; safe retry classification Queue overflow unbounded memory or late work finite bound; explicit reject/drop; oldest age Cache invalidation loss stale read reaches a decision version/rebuild and authority revalidation Run these with representative load and fault injection. Record limiter decision errors, fallback decisions, estimated overage, queue age, and stale duration; the test passes only when the documented bounds and recovery path are observable.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/distributed-rate-limiter#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q6"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q1","surface":"track","en":"Given sustained overload, how would you sequence workload reduction, asynchronous work, caching, isolation, and autoscaling while preserving the critical path and its invariants?","bodyEn":"Stateless + scale horizontally: push state out to cache/DB/queue, add instances behind the LB Go async: heavy/non-real-time work goes onto a queue, processed in the background Multi-tier caching + read replicas reduce DB load Bulkhead + circuit breaker isolate failures auto-scaling by metric; sane connection pools & timeouts (1) stateless + scale horizontally (state to Redis/DB/queue, auto-scaling by metric) (2) make heavy work async (queue + worker) → reduces hot-path latency (3) multi-tier caching + read replicas; (4) bulkhead + circuit breaker + timeout to isolate failures; (5) correctly sized connection pools; (6) backpressure/load shedding under overload OTA: search/pricing (read-heavy) → cache + read model; booking/payment (writes, must be correct) → atomicity + idempotency, never cache balances; GDS/PSP → circuit breaker + bulkhead + timeout + idempotent retry. Core idea: separate the hot path from heavy work, 'fail in a controlled way' under overload.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/surviving-high-load#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q1"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q2","surface":"track","en":"When a dependency or worker queue saturates, how do you set bounded queues, admission or concurrency limits, priorities, and shedding rules—and which signals trigger and reverse them?","bodyEn":"Backpressure: signal upstream to slow down (bounded queue, reactive request(n)) instead of absorbing unboundedly and running OOM Load shedding: proactively reject/downgrade lower-priority requests under overload (429/503, a degraded response) to keep the system alive for what matters Prioritize requests by importance (OTP > marketing). Bottom line a good system 'fails in a controlled way' — better to reject part of the traffic than crash entirely. backpressure = signal upstream to slow down (a full bounded queue blocks, reactive request(n), TCP flow control) instead of absorbing unboundedly → OOM/cascading failure load shedding = reject/downgrade lower-priority requests + admission control (limit incoming concurrency) & a priority queue Set each bound from the protected resource and the useful deadline: concurrency limit: cap in-flight work per dependency, tenant, and priority class; derive it from measured service time and reserve a critical share. bounded queue: choose capacity from service_rate × useful_wait, not from available memory. Reject or cancel work whose deadline cannot be met, and expose oldest age. retry budget: reserve only a small share of traffic for retries, use exponential backoff with full jitter, and stop at the end-to-end deadline. A retry consumes admission like a new request. priority lanes: keep checkout/OTP capacity separate from bulk work; shed bulk before borrowing the reserved share. Trigger on sustained queue-oldest-age or deadline misses, in-flight saturation, downstream p99/error/timeout, retry ratio, and rejection rate. CPU is supporting evidence, not the only trigger. Use hysteresis and a gradual drain when reversing the policy (see surviving-high-load.q5). OTA: under overload → prioritize checkout/payment & OTP over search/marketing; temporarily disable secondary features (recommendations, images) to protect the booking flow. Measure via saturation metrics to trigger early. Retry and ambiguous outcome: If the limiter allowed POST /booking and the provider timed out, the outcome is unknown: the provider may have committed. A blind retry can double-book or double-charge. Use a stable idempotency key and a status/reconciliation path (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1). Count each retry against the end-to-end budget; a 429 is retryable only under the caller contract, not because the limiter made the operation safe.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/surviving-high-load#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q2"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q3","surface":"track","en":"Given measured read/write load and access patterns, which database-scaling step would you choose next, and what metric, failure mode, and exit criterion justify that choice?","bodyEn":"There is no universal database-scaling ladder. Start with the measured bottleneck and the invariant that must remain true; the cheapest change is useful only when it addresses both. Query and connection shape: inspect plans, indexes, lock time, pool waits, payload size, and transaction scope before adding infrastructure. Read path: replicas, caches, or a derived read model help only when the read can tolerate their freshness and authorization boundary. Write path: batching, asynchronous handoff, or a queue can shorten the request path, but they do not erase write volume and introduce ordering, retry, and reconciliation work. Partition or shard: choose a key that keeps the common invariant local, quantify skew, and provide a migration, rollback, and repair plan before distributing the source of truth. Bottom line For every proposed layer, state the measured constraint, the expected effect, the new failure mode, and the metric that would remove or reverse the change. a table with tens of millions of rows or a primary handling thousands of writes per second is neither automatically healthy nor automatically a reason to shard. Hardware, schema, indexes, durability settings, payloads, and p99 targets decide the useful threshold. replicas introduce lag and read-after-write choices (06-db-scaling.replication-consistency.q2); caches introduce invalidation and origin amplification (10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3); queues introduce delivery and ordering semantics. OTA search may use a rebuildable read model while booking keeps its inventory and money invariant local. Partition by time only when retention and query patterns support it; shard by a domain key only after proving the key spreads writes and preserves the common transaction. measure each change with a before-and-after workload, error budget, and rollback criterion. Architecture is not a checklist ordered by fashionable components.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/surviving-high-load#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q3"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q4","surface":"track","en":"An endpoint’s p99 has regressed: how do you use metrics, tracing, profiles, and queue, lock, connection, or GC signals to prove the constraint and select a safe experiment?","bodyEn":"Measure before optimizing. First define the user symptom, the time window, and a baseline; then prove which resource is saturated before changing the design. metrics: request rate, errors, latency percentiles, utilization, saturation, queue age, pool waits, and retry volume traces: compare the critical path with a healthy request and identify the dependency or stage that consumes the budget profiles and runtime evidence: CPU, allocation, lock contention, GC pauses, I/O, network retransmits, and database plans Little's law is L = lambda × W at steady state: average work in the system equals throughput times average time in the system. It is a consistency check for measured concurrency and latency, not a standalone sizing promise. Amdahl's law likewise says to improve the part that dominates observed time, not the component that is easiest to discuss. compare before and after a traffic change, deploy, configuration rollout, or dependency incident. A single high CPU graph cannot distinguish useful work from retry storms or lock waits. look for a saturation signal that matches the symptom: pending database connections, oldest queue age, lock wait, CPU run queue, disk latency, GC pause, or a slow remote span. turn the diagnosis into a bounded experiment with a success metric and rollback. if a PSP or GDS span dominates checkout, do not casually cache or parallelize the final money or booking decision; reduce optional work, set deadlines/concurrency limits, and preserve an explicit pending/reconciliation state. Common mistake: micro-optimizing local code while the bottleneck is a database, network dependency, or an unbounded retry loop. the first-60-seconds host process is in (21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q1); for a JVM suspicion, use a thread dump (21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q1) before production-safe profiling (21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q3).","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/surviving-high-load#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q4"},{"id":"10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q5","surface":"track","en":"What makes overload control reversible instead of a permanent emergency switch?","bodyEn":"Define overload as a controller with an activation rule, a recovery rule, and hysteresis. Observe queue depth and age, in-flight concurrency, admission rejections, downstream latency, error and timeout rate, retry amplification, CPU/memory, and the resource that is actually saturated. Activate bounded admission, priority scheduling, feature degradation, or load shedding when the signal crosses a sustained threshold; do not react to one noisy sample. Recovery should require a lower threshold held for a window, then ramp traffic gradually. Keep a separate emergency path for critical work, but make its capacity and exhaustion visible. When the queue is full, fail fast or return a documented pending/retry response; an unbounded queue only moves the outage into latency and memory. Test the controller with a dependency stall, cache cold start, retry storm, worker crash, and recovery. Record the state transition and reason so operators can tell whether the system is protecting itself or silently losing work. Bottom line Overload policy is part of the product contract: which work is rejected, degraded, delayed, or preserved must be explicit and measurable.","contextEn":"System design foundations, high load & rate limiting","href":"/system-design/surviving-high-load#question-10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q5"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q1","surface":"track","en":"Design a wallet-to-wallet payment system. Which ledger invariants must be atomic, and how do you handle idempotency, PSP timeouts or unknown outcomes, and reconciliation without coupling the design to an OTA booking flow?","bodyEn":"Scope and invariants: transfer money between wallets; no duplicate debit or credit, no money created or lost, an immutable audit trail, and an explicit state for every external outcome. Source of truth: the Ledger Service owns append-only, double-entry postings. A completed internal transfer writes balanced debit and credit entries in one local ACID transaction. Available balance and reserved/pending balance must be defined separately; a cache may serve display data only when its staleness contract is explicit, never the final authorization decision. Idempotent command: POST /transfers carries an Idempotency-Key. In one local transaction, persist the key and canonical request fingerprint, create a transfer intent, and write an outbox event. The same key with a different fingerprint is rejected; the same key with the same fingerprint returns the recorded transfer resource. External boundary: do not hold a database transaction open while calling a PSP. A worker calls the PSP with a stable provider idempotency key. Persist callbacks and status-query results idempotently. A timeout is UNKNOWN, not failure or success: query the provider, retry only with the same provider key where safe, then reconcile against settlement records. Pin the chosen provider/API version, account/region, key scope/retention, and status/webhook contract; this case deliberately does not assume another PSP behaves like Stripe. The canonical provider-contract checklist is in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1) and (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2). Lifecycle: PENDING → SUCCEEDED FAILED REVERSED, with UNKNOWN/RECONCILING for ambiguity. Compensation is a new reversal entry, never mutation or deletion of a posted ledger entry. Answer frame: invariant → authority → projection → failure recovery. State one of each before choosing components; keep the ledger and PSP boundary explicit. Key takeaway Keep the money invariant inside one ledger transaction; cross the PSP boundary through durable intent, idempotency, status queries and reconciliation—not a distributed rollback. Case metadata: Workload: transfer rate, amount and tenant mix, plus ledger latency Freshness: ledger state is synchronous while balance projections have an explicit bound Duplicate tolerance: caller key, provider key and replay deduplication Recovery owner: ledger and reconciliation service State transition guard: One possible model is PENDING -> SUCCEEDED FAILED; UNKNOWN -> RECONCILING -> SUCCEEDED FAILED RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED; and COMPENSATING -> REVERSED RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED. Every edge needs the ledger version and evidence that authorizes it. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The freshness budget each product actually promises for search, feed, typeahead and analytics — the number that decides whether a projection is acceptable. Which cases need exact global rate limiting, and the bounded overshoot acceptable during a partition. The upload provider and region: resumable-session lifetime, checksum and URL behaviour are provider-specific. The compliance and privacy baseline the payment, notification, upload and trace examples assume. What would falsify \"keep the state local\": a measured invariant needing cross-resource atomicity, or a provider contract that already supplies a stronger guarantee. What would falsify \"at-least-once plus idempotency\": a downstream contract that cannot tolerate replay and offers no durable deduplication.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/payment-ledger#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q1"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q2","surface":"track","en":"Design a notification platform for OTP, transactional, and marketing messages. Define acceptance and delivery semantics, preference enforcement, deduplication, retries and DLQ ownership, provider failover, and delivery-status trust.","bodyEn":"Define the contract before adding queues: accepted means the caller was authorized, policy-checked, deduplicated/idempotently recorded, and the notification request was durably stored or queued. It does not mean delivered. Track states such as accepted → queued → sent-to-provider → provider-accepted → provider-confirmed / failed / expired. A provider callback may prove provider or carrier handling, but usually cannot prove a user read the message. Flow: producer → idempotent ingestion → policy and preference router → priority channel queues → workers → provider adapters → status store and DLQ. OTP OTP should have a priority lane, a short deadline, and bounded retries. Retrying after its code expires is usually worse than failing fast and asking the user to request a new code. enforce consent, channel preference, quiet hours, unsubscribe, suppression, and per-user limits again at send time; an opt-out after enqueue must still win. distinguish ingestion idempotency, semantic deduplication window, and provider idempotency. If the first provider outcome is unknown, failover can create a duplicate; query status or wait for a callback when possible. APNs and FCM have provider-specific token lifecycle, TTL/expiry, and collapse/coalescing behavior. Provider acceptance or a callback is still provider-side evidence: persist the provider message ID, update invalid-token state, and do not retry a dead token forever. for critical state changes, keep a durable in-app inbox/read model and let clients sync; push is a wake-up hint that may be delayed, collapsed, reordered, or expire. Do not use collapse/TTL for OTP or a unique state transition without an explicit product contract. sign and verify callbacks, deduplicate them, and make state transitions tolerant of duplicate or out-of-order events. DLQ is a quarantine with an owner, alerting, remediation reason, expiry policy, and controlled replay; it is not an invisible infinite retry queue. expose delivery semantics honestly: transactional messages are high priority and monitored, while marketing is best-effort and may be shed under overload. Case metadata: Workload: OTP, transactional and marketing rates, payload size, channel/provider mix and per-tenant burst Freshness: acceptance is synchronous but provider delivery/read status is asynchronous and bounded by its contract Duplicate tolerance: ingestion key, semantic dedup window and provider message ID Recovery owner: notification router, provider adapter and DLQ owner Failure matrix: Window Incorrect shortcut Required state and owner --- --- --- Provider accepted but the response is lost Send through a second provider immediately Keep an uncertain send record, query/correlate when supported, and let the notification owner reconcile Opt-out races with a queued message Treat enqueue as final permission Re-check policy at send time; preference owner wins and records suppression Poison message or exhausted retry Retry forever or drop silently Quarantine in a bounded DLQ with reason, alert, expiry and controlled replay","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/notification-service#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q2"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q3","surface":"track","en":"Design a URL shortener with custom aliases and analytics. How do redirect semantics and cache policy support mutable versus permanent destinations, while privacy, abuse prevention, and click measurement remain explicit?","bodyEn":"Core mapping: a short code resolves to a destination URL plus ownership, status, expiry, abuse, and analytics metadata. Creation must normalize the destination, enforce uniqueness, and use a collision-safe code generator; use cryptographically random codes when non-enumerability is a security requirement. Read path: CDN or edge cache → redirect service → cache / key-value store → redirect response; emit redirect events asynchronously so analytics cannot slow the redirect. redirect status is not an analytics strategy. Immutable mappings can use 301 or 308 with an explicit long cache policy. Mutable mappings normally use 302 or 307 with explicit no-store or bounded cache lifetime. A 302 can also be cached when headers allow it. click counting is inherently approximate because browsers, proxies, bots, prefetchers, and cached redirects may bypass the origin. Record server or edge redirect events, filter abuse, and disclose the measurement definition. a public shortener may intentionally redirect to arbitrary HTTP/S destinations. Validate and normalize scheme and URL, scan or block abusive destinations, rate-limit creation, and make allowlists a product policy rather than a universal rule. custom aliases need ownership checks and reservation rules. Protect users with phishing and malware controls, report-and-takedown workflows, privacy-aware logging, and expiration or deletion policies. size the code space from expected creations, retention, collision retry, and whether codes may be reused; do not infer capacity from a single fixed traffic number. Case metadata: Workload: create/redirect QPS, alias mutations, region and bot skew Freshness: immutable redirects may be cached while mutable aliases need a stated revalidation bound Duplicate tolerance: create request key and click-event deduplication policy Recovery owner: redirect/abuse owner, with analytics treated as a projection Provider and security boundary: Apply the chosen RFC 9110/9111 semantics and name the CDN/cache version and headers. 301/308 versus 302/307 is a permanence and method-preservation decision, not a universal CDN rule. Do not fetch arbitrary destinations server-side unless the product has an isolated SSRF-safe scanner. Security and tests: Test alias ownership, reserved-code collision, scheme validation, takedown propagation, cache invalidation, redirect method behavior, bot/pre-fetch measurement and privacy-safe logs. Rate-limit creation and scanning; never treat click analytics as proof that a human visited.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/url-shortener#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q3"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q4","surface":"track","en":"Design a home feed for regular users and celebrities. Choose a fan-out strategy and specify ordering/ranking, pagination, deletion and privacy propagation, freshness, and the celebrity path.","bodyEn":"A feed is a ranked, bounded candidate list, not a single universally O(1) lookup. Separate post storage, social graph, feed candidates, and ranking policy. Concern Useful design Required contract Ordinary author fan-out on write into follower candidates idempotent delivery and repair Celebrity author fan-out on read or hybrid candidates bounded merge and load protection Read page bounded lookup and ranking cursor and deterministic tie-break Privacy/delete filter at read plus async removal no exposure before propagation finishes fan-out on write improves common reads, but the read is still O(page size) plus ranking and possible candidate merging. A single sorted set cannot be both a chronological source and an unconstrained ML ranking system without defining score and refresh semantics. use keyset cursors such as rank-or-time plus post ID, and carry a ranking or feed-generation version so pagination remains explainable as scores change. store references to posts rather than blindly duplicating content. At read time enforce deletion, block, audience, moderation, and privacy policy; asynchronous fan-out repair is not permission to leak removed or private posts. high-fan-out accounts need a deliberate hybrid plan, quotas, queue backpressure, and a freshness SLA instead of a universal threshold. Case metadata: Workload: follower/fan-out distribution, celebrity skew, post rate and page reads Freshness: ranking and fan-out lag are product budgets, while privacy/deletion checks are stronger Duplicate tolerance: post ID, fan-out task ID and replayed ranking input Recovery owner: feed projection and privacy owner","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/news-feed#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q4"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q5","surface":"track","en":"Design a shared rate-limiting service for stateless gateways across regions. Which limits require an exact global decision, which may be approximate, and how do allocation, Redis Cluster, latency, and outage behavior affect the answer?","bodyEn":"A shared limiter needs an explicit policy contract: trusted code chooses policy ID, authenticated subject, scope, route, and validated cost. The caller must not freely choose its own limit or identity key. for one subject bucket, a short Redis Lua script can atomically read state, refill, decide, consume, and set its idle expiry. A GET followed by SET is a race. Redis Cluster is slot-based: all keys used by one script must share a hash slot through a deliberate hash tag. Cluster replication and failover do not make it an exact global, multi-region authority. distinguish strict global quotas from approximate protection. a contractual supplier quota, paid quota, or security lockout may need a home region, strong coordinator, or non-overlapping expiring leases. regional token leases can improve availability, but must declare bounded overshoot or under-use; total divided by N plus periodic sync is not a correctness guarantee. choose time authority, endpoint-specific fallback, and no-result retry behavior. Do not blindly repeat an uncertain consume request, because the first execution may already have deducted a token. return a documented denial and retry delay only when they represent the actual policy; edge, gateway, service, and downstream concurrency limits protect different resources. Case metadata: Workload: requests per second, key cardinality, burst, cost per request and region skew Freshness: the limiter decision has a declared exactness/overshoot bound while telemetry may lag Duplicate tolerance: uncertain consume requests need a request key or safe retry contract Recovery owner: limiter service and the protected domain Provider and failure boundary: For the chosen Redis version and Redis Cluster topology, document hash tags, Lua/function atomicity, replication and failover behavior, clock/TTL assumptions and hot-key limits. Async replication or WAIT does not by itself create a strongly consistent global quota. A partition must select fail-open, fail-closed or bounded leases per policy. Failure matrix: Window Incorrect shortcut Required state and owner --- --- --- Primary fails after a token decision Repeat the consume blindly Return an explicit uncertain result or use a request key; reconcile against the policy owner Cross-region partition Pretend every region has one exact counter Enforce a declared lease/overshoot bound or route to the home authority Resharding or hot key Assume more shards remove the hotspot apply a key strategy, load-shed and measure decision latency; limiter owner repairs allocation","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/distributed-rate-limiter#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q5"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q6","surface":"track","en":"Design a real-time leaderboard. Define update volume, rank accuracy, tie-breaking, retention and recovery, and how regional or sharded scores produce an honest global top-N and user rank.","bodyEn":"Treat the leaderboard as a derived projection of durable score events, not as the only record of who earned a score. a Redis sorted set is useful for one leaderboard shard: rank and top-N are efficient locally. Define ties explicitly. Equal scores have lexicographic member ordering in Redis; use a stable member/tie key if that is the intended product rule. do not pack score and inverted timestamp into one floating-point score unless the full integer range and precision are proven below the safe limit. Otherwise ties and large scores can silently corrupt rank. make score-event ingestion idempotent, preserve a durable source or event log, and support replay/rebuild after cache loss. Snapshots help recovery speed but are not a sufficient source of truth alone. a global sharded top-N can merge bounded top candidates from shards. Exact global rank for every tail user needs an authoritative aggregate or scatter/gather work; otherwise state that the displayed global rank is approximate or eventually consistent. define season boundaries, resets, anti-cheat review, correction events, and rank freshness before optimizing the sorted-set commands. Case metadata: Workload: score updates per second, top-N reads, season retention and shard skew Freshness: top-N/rank freshness is explicit during ingest and rebuild Duplicate tolerance: score-event ID and versioned update Recovery owner: score authority and leaderboard rebuild job","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/realtime-leaderboard#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q6"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q7","surface":"track","en":"Design a distributed cache whose data remains disposable. How do source-of-truth ownership, tenancy, eviction, fill and invalidation, hot keys, cold start, and cache loss shape the design?","bodyEn":"A distributed cache is a bounded, disposable acceleration layer. The origin remains authoritative, and the system must still be correct during a cold start, eviction storm, or total cache loss. define source ownership and key schema: include tenant, authorization boundary, locale or schema version when they affect the result. Apply tenant quotas and eviction isolation so one tenant cannot evict another tenant's working set. use cache-aside for derived reads; after an origin commit, publish an invalidation or version event through a durable outbox path. TTL limits stale damage but is not a correctness proof. Redis Cluster uses hash slots and cluster-aware clients following redirects; it is not inherently a proxy-based cache. Multi-key operations need deliberate same-slot placement. replicate a hot key only with explicit replica routing and write/invalidation fan-out to every copy. Random key suffixes without that contract reduce hit rate and create inconsistent reads. protect the origin with TTL jitter, single-flight rebuilds, admission limits, warm critical keys, degraded responses, and bounded concurrency when cache capacity or availability disappears. Case metadata: Workload: hit/miss rate, working-set size, tenant skew and fill burst Freshness: TTL/invalidation and origin revalidation define the permitted staleness Duplicate tolerance: cache fill/single-flight key, not a business command key Recovery owner: origin data owner and cache platform","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/distributed-cache#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q7"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q8","surface":"track","en":"Design a chat service for one-to-one and group conversations. State the ordering and delivery guarantees, multi-device/offline behavior, connection routing, storage partition and time-bucket strategy, backpressure, and the effect of end-to-end encryption.","bodyEn":"Define ordering before choosing storage: most chat systems promise accepted order within one conversation, not one global order across all conversations. assign a durable per-conversation sequence through one partition owner or sequencer. A Kafka partition keyed by conversation can preserve append order, while idempotent client message IDs prevent duplicate submission. model Cassandra partitions with a bounded key such as conversation ID plus time bucket, and cluster by sequence or timeuuid. An unbounded partition keyed only by conversation eventually becomes hot and hard to repair. separate connection gateways from durable message handling. Keep a user/device-to-gateway registry, deliver through durable logs or queues, and retain per-device sync cursors for offline catch-up. define at-least-once delivery, duplicate handling, read/delivery receipt semantics, retention, and multi-device synchronization. Bound slow consumers with queues, deadlines, and disconnect or catch-up policy. large groups need hybrid fan-out and backpressure, not one broadcast loop. E2E encryption also removes server-side search, preview, and content moderation capabilities while leaving metadata and device/key management concerns. For connection lifecycle and delivery narration, see (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q4). Case metadata: Workload: conversation/member/device mix, offline duration, group fan-out and connection churn Freshness: accepted message order is durable while presence, unread and delivery receipts may lag Duplicate tolerance: durable message ID, receipt ID and replay-safe consumer Recovery owner: conversation partition and connection/offline delivery owner Canonical boundary: Keep broker ordering, redelivery, retention and backpressure in (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1/q6); this case keeps only conversation-specific ordering, device sync and E2E trade-offs.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/chat-messaging#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q8"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q9","surface":"track","en":"Design typeahead for a multilingual catalogue. Define latency and quality targets, prefix/fuzzy/personalized retrieval, index and serving freshness, ranking evaluation, and abuse or cost limits.","bodyEn":"Typeahead is a retrieval and ranking problem with a latency and quality budget. Define whether the product is suggesting queries, entities, products, or documents before choosing the index. normalize Unicode, case, token boundaries, locale, transliteration, and safe query length before lookup. Prefix lookup cost includes top-K retrieval and ranking, not only prefix length. use a versioned index pipeline: source change → normalized index event → build or update → validation → atomic alias/version switch. State freshness lag and rebuild behavior explicitly. choose a p95/p99 target from measured input-to-render latency and dependency budgets rather than promising a universal 100 ms. Measure zero-result rate, CTR, MRR or conversion, latency, cache hit rate, and index lag. fuzzy matching and typo tolerance need bounded expansion, a candidate cap, and a quality threshold. Personalized ranking is a separate candidate/reranking and privacy problem, not a free addition to the prefix trie. debounce improves UI load but does not stop abuse. Add server-side rate, cost, cache, and concurrency controls, and avoid logging sensitive typed text without a retention policy. Case metadata: Workload: query rate by locale, prefix/fuzzy mix, catalogue size and personalization skew Freshness: index freshness and ranking freshness are measured separately from API latency Duplicate tolerance: query replay is harmless, while index events need stable IDs Recovery owner: relevance/index owner and abuse-control owner Security: Normalize by locale without collapsing tenant boundaries or logging raw sensitive queries. Rate-limit expensive fuzzy/personalized paths and define abuse/cost budgets. Testing: Test Unicode normalization, locale changes, typo quality, stale/deleted catalogue entries, adversarial prefixes, index rebuild and p95/p99 latency; report relevance metrics separately from freshness.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/autocomplete-typeahead#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q9"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q10","surface":"track","en":"Design a resumable direct-to-object-store upload flow. How do authorization and tenant ownership, checksums, part finalization, quota, malware handling, abandoned uploads, and retention turn bytes into an available object safely?","bodyEn":"Treat uploads as a control-plane state machine, not merely a signed URL: UPLOADING → PENDING_SCAN → AVAILABLE or QUARANTINED, with FAILED and EXPIRED paths. create an upload session only after authorization and quota reservation. Bind each presigned URL to tenant, object or part, key prefix, method, expected content length/type/checksum, and short expiry; it is a narrowly scoped capability, not broad storage access. a presigned URL or resumable session URI is a bearer capability: anyone who obtains it can use it within scope until expiry. Keep it out of logs and analytics, give it one purpose and a short TTL, and assume the storage provider may not offer immediate revocation. complete multipart upload only after the server verifies expected parts, ownership, idempotency key, and checksum policy. A multipart or encrypted object ETag is not generally an MD5 integrity proof. persist the part manifest and final checksum so completion is idempotent and only one control-plane transition publishes the object. do not expose the object through CDN or read URLs until validation and malware scanning complete. Store metadata and access policy separately from opaque object-store keys. deduplicate only inside a tenant or an explicitly authorized boundary; cross-tenant hashes can leak that another customer uploaded the same file. abort abandoned multipart uploads, release or reconcile quota reservations, expire orphan metadata, and apply lifecycle, retention, legal-hold, audit, and deletion policies deliberately. Case metadata: Workload: bytes per object, multipart concurrency, tenant quota and upload burst Freshness: scan/finalization/publication status is durable while progress is approximate Duplicate tolerance: upload session, part number/checksum and finalize key Recovery owner: upload/security owner and object-store lifecycle job Provider/version boundary: S3 multipart uploads and GCS resumable sessions have different session, checksum, completion and cleanup contracts. An S3 multipart ETag is not generally a complete content hash; use the documented checksum algorithm and finalization response for the selected provider/version. A presigned URL or session URI is a time-limited capability, not a harmless API token. Security and failure tests: Bind capability to tenant/object/method/expiry and do not put it in ordinary logs. Test checksum mismatch, replayed part, wrong offset, URL leakage, scan timeout/quarantine, finalize replay, quota race, abandoned-part cleanup and publication only after policy approval. Failure matrix: Window Incorrect shortcut Required state and owner --- --- --- Capability leaks or expires mid-upload Widen permission or restart anonymously Re-issue a scoped capability after authorization; keep the session owner Checksum or malware scan fails Publish the object because bytes arrived Mark QUARANTINED, retain evidence and let security owner decide Client disappears after parts upload Keep parts forever Mark abandoned and clean by provider lifecycle/job policy","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/object-storage-large-upload#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q10"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q11","surface":"track","en":"Design an idempotent, asynchronous `POST /payments` contract. How are request fingerprinting, duplicate/replay behavior, durable state transitions, PSP execution, unknown outcomes, and reconciliation defined?","bodyEn":"POST /payments requires an Idempotency-Key scoped to the caller and endpoint. Canonicalize the semantic request and store its fingerprint. In one local DB transaction, insert the unique idempotency row with IN_PROGRESS, create the Payment resource/intent, and write an outbox command. Do not call the PSP inside that transaction. If the same key and fingerprint arrive again, return the recorded payment resource and its current state. If the same key carries a different fingerprint, reject it as a conflict; never silently reuse a key for a different amount or payee. Creation may return 201 Created with Location and status=PENDING; that creates a durable resource, not a claim that money has moved. The client reads the resource to observe the final state. A worker calls the PSP with a stable provider idempotency key. Webhooks, polling, and status queries update the same payment state idempotently. After a timeout, mark the outcome UNKNOWN/RECONCILING; do not issue a fresh charge. Keep idempotency records for the documented retry window and retain payment/ledger audit records according to reconciliation and compliance policy. Pin the PSP's contract with the canonical checklist in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2): API/version, account/region, key scope/retention, payload-mismatch and concurrent-reuse behavior, status inquiry, webhook authenticity/replay, settlement/reversal. 202 confirms resource acceptance, not settled money. Reconciliation resolves a payment that remains ambiguous. Key takeaway The atomic unit is local intent + idempotency state + outbox. The PSP is an at-least-once external boundary, so its result is confirmed by idempotent callbacks/status queries and reconciliation. Turn the design into a wire-level HTTP contract transcript: curl -i -X POST https://api.example.test/v1/payments \\ -H 'Idempotency-Key: pay-8f2' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"account\":\"A1\",\"amount\":\"10.00\",\"currency\":\"USD\"}' HTTP/1.1 202 Accepted Location: /v1/payment-operations/op-42 Retry-After: 2 {\"id\":\"op-42\",\"status\":\"PENDING\"} same key + same canonical fingerprint => same operation, no second PSP call same key + different amount/currency/account => 409 Problem Details curl -i https://api.example.test/v1/payment-operations/op-42 HTTP/1.1 200 OK {\"id\":\"op-42\",\"status\":\"SUCCEEDED\",\"paymentId\":\"p-9\"} Automate these three assertions in a contract test and count provider calls with a stub. Benefit: the API never claims synchronous success while the PSP outcome is unknown. Cost: clients must poll or receive a webhook, operation records need retention, and canonical fingerprint rules become part of the public contract. Case metadata: Workload: payment request rate, amount/currency/tenant mix and PSP quota Freshness: local acceptance is immediate, PSP status is asynchronous with an explicit unknown-age budget Duplicate tolerance: caller idempotency key, provider key and webhook/event ID Recovery owner: payment state machine and reconciliation service State transition guard: A durable graph can be: RECEIVED -> PROCESSING -> PENDING UNKNOWN -> SUCCEEDED FAILED RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED a confirmed charge: SUCCEEDED -> COMPENSATING -> COMPENSATED RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED 202 means durable acceptance of processing, never proof of success. Canonical boundary: Keep provider idempotency, unknown outcomes, Saga/Outbox and reconciliation mechanics in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) and use this prompt for the HTTP/domain adaptation.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/payment-ledger#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q11"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12","surface":"track","en":"Given a proposed system design, how would you review scope, workload, contracts, state ownership, critical paths, failure behavior, operations, and trade-offs—and turn each gap into a concrete follow-up question?","bodyEn":"A strong design is a chain of explicit decisions. Use the same scorecard for a payment system, feed, cache, chat, or URL shortener so visual complexity does not hide missing correctness. Dimension Evidence expected Scope and success actors, top journeys, out-of-scope features, SLI/SLO, compliance and business invariants Workload read/write/event rates with units, payload and retention ranges, peak/average ratio, growth and skew assumptions Contracts API/event examples, idempotency, ordering, pagination, compatibility, authentication and authorization boundaries State ownership data model, source of truth, indexes/partitions, consistency, transaction boundary, lifecycle and deletion Critical path one end-to-end write and read, sync/async boundaries, queues, caches and external dependencies Failure behavior timeouts, retry eligibility/budget, duplicates, partial failure, overload/backpressure, recovery and reconciliation Operations SLIs, alerts, tracing/audit, rollout/rollback, capacity headroom, disaster recovery and cost drivers Trade-off why this option fits the stated constraints, what was rejected, and which measurement would change the choice Interview flow Clarify and rank requirements, estimate only the quantities that affect architecture, draw a minimal baseline, walk one critical path, then deepen the highest-risk bottleneck or invariant. End by testing the design against a traffic spike, dependency outage, duplicate request, zone loss, and schema/version rollout. Adapt the time split to the interview instead of reciting a fixed minute-by-minute script. Use ranges and sensitivity analysis rather than precision theatre: peak writes/s × bytes/event × retention is useful when every term has a unit and source. Label assumptions and revisit them. A diagram earns credit only when ownership, protocol, and failure semantics are explainable. Correctness and availability must be separated. Some domains tolerate stale reads; a ledger cannot tolerate an unbalanced entry. Likewise, adding Kafka, Redis, or multiple regions is not “scale” by itself—it introduces state, failure modes, and an operating cost that must answer a stated requirement. Case metadata: Workload: read/write/event rates, payload, retention, peak ratio, tenant skew and dependency quota Freshness: each projection and measurement has a stated observation time Duplicate tolerance: repeated review evidence is harmless but business effects still need owners Recovery owner: design reviewer plus the owner of each invariant Invariant assertion: For every critical scenario, write a property such as “no two active holds for one version” or “one idempotency key maps to one effect.” The test must inject timeout, crash, replay and stale projection, then assert the property at the authority—not only that an HTTP response looks correct. Canonical links: Use these as owners, then record only the case-specific adaptation: delivery semantics: (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1) payment idempotency: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) fan-out at scale: (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q4) idempotent consumer and inbox: (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q2) crash walkthrough: (15-transactional-outbox-order-workflow)","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/design-review-framework#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q13","surface":"track","en":"A travel marketplace receives 1M requests/day, a 100:1 search-to-booking ratio, and 250 search requests/sec at peak. Design its availability and booking paths. What may search promise, which write is authoritative, and how do holds, Kafka fan-out, and replay keep one unit from being confirmed twice?","bodyEn":"Contract: Search reads a cache/index projection and returns its observed_at time or version. It may be stale and is never a promise of stock. Only a short transaction on the inventory unit may create an active hold and order; a conditional write or uniqueness constraint prevents double-selling, and confirmation revalidates there. Flow: Persist the idempotency key and canonical request fingerprint with the hold/order and an outbox record in that same transaction. Relay events at least once; payment, notification, and index consumers deduplicate using a stable event ID or aggregate version. Call the PSP outside the database transaction. Failure path: Index lag may advertise a unit that has gone, so the hold returns the truthful result. A PSP timeout is UNKNOWN; query and reconcile before finalising, then release or compensate through explicit state transitions. Search stays isolated from booking backlog. Case metadata: Workload: 1M requests/day, 100:1 search-to-booking ratio, peak search rate, hold concurrency and supplier quota Freshness: search exposes observed version/time while hold/booking authority is synchronous Duplicate tolerance: search replay is harmless, hold/order key and event version are not Recovery owner: inventory/booking authority and reconciliation owner Failure matrix: Window Incorrect shortcut Required state and owner --- --- --- Search projection is stale Present availability as a reservation Return observed time/version and re-check the hold authority Hold expires while payment/provider is unclear Release and forget the external attempt Keep UNKNOWN/PENDING, query the chosen supplier/PSP, then reconcile or compensate Kafka event is replayed or out of order Apply every event to the index/payment path Deduplicate by event ID and guard aggregate version; park impossible transitions Canonical boundary: Use (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q4) for the OTA framing and (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) for payment ambiguity; the prompt owns only search-versus-hold adaptation.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/high-traffic-booking-search#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q13"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q14","surface":"track","en":"A checkout returns `PENDING` after a PSP timeout, then a provider webhook arrives after the hold may have expired. Design the state machine and recovery path. How do expiry, event publishing, retries, and late success avoid confirming or charging one unit twice during queue lag and consumer replay?","bodyEn":"Durable state: Keep a versioned hold, order, payment attempt, provider idempotency key, and explicit PENDING, UNKNOWN, CONFIRMED, FAILED, and RELEASED states. Expiry is a conditional transition: it may release only the expected active version. If payment authorization has started, use a bounded, durable policy rather than letting a timer blindly reclaim stock. Local expiry does not prove that a supplier/GDS released inventory. Persist the supplier reference, quote/hold version, and correlation ID, then query the chosen supplier authority before confirming late success or issuing compensation; exact expiry and idempotency behavior belongs to that provider contract. External boundary: Commit the order transition and outbox locally, then let a worker call the PSP with the same provider key on every safe retry. Never retry an unknown charge with a fresh key. Webhooks and status queries are idempotently recorded before they advance state. Recovery: A late success reloads the authoritative hold and order. If stock can no longer be confirmed, do not allocate silently; reconcile, cancel or refund according to policy, and record the compensation. Outbox consumers use stable IDs, so replay cannot duplicate downstream effects. Case metadata: Workload: hold expiry rate, provider latency/lag, queue depth and late-callback volume Freshness: hold/order version is authoritative while provider status has an unknown-age budget Duplicate tolerance: hold version, provider idempotency key and webhook/event ID Recovery owner: expiry worker, payment owner and reconciliation/compensation owner State transition guard: A possible graph is: hold: ACTIVE_HOLD -> EXPIRED RELEASED CONFIRMED payment: PENDING UNKNOWN -> CONFIRMED FAILED RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED late success: COMPENSATING -> REFUNDED RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED Expiry and confirmation must compare the expected hold version, never just a wall-clock timestamp. Failure matrix: Window Incorrect shortcut Required state and owner --- --- --- Late PSP success after release Confirm the old hold blindly Reload authority and apply the policy: rebook only with stock, otherwise refund/compensate Webhook arrives twice or out of order Trust arrival order Verify authenticity, dedup by event ID and guard the state/version transition Queue lag crosses the hold deadline Let a timer race an in-flight worker Fence stale work, record the decision and send the case to reconciliation Canonical boundary: Mechanism details belong to (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) and the concrete crash sequence belongs to (15-transactional-outbox-order-workflow); this case states the late-success policy.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/high-traffic-booking-search#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q14"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q15","surface":"track","en":"A supplied e-commerce architecture claims 1M DAU and a 10x promotion, while the shared planning tiers are 1M and 10M requests/day. Normalize the workload, then evolve CDN, gateway, search, cart and a checkout Saga from 1M to 10M without premature service or database sharding. Which lane scales first, and how do local outboxes preserve order, inventory and payment correctness?","bodyEn":"Normalize the unit first: DAU is a population, not traffic. Write the assumptions explicitly: requests/day = DAU × journeys/user/day × API calls/journey; then split the result by browse, cart and checkout. Under the shared planning factor, 1M requests/day is about 250 rps peak and 10M/day about 2,500 rps peak. A 10x promotion is a burst with a duration and hot-SKU skew, not automatically a new daily tier. Physical shape at 1M: Keep the boxes as logical ownership boundaries. A modular monolith or a few coarse services may share one HA relational cluster while using separate schemas, transactions and connection pools. Put static assets on the CDN, let search read a rebuildable index/cache, keep Cart DB durable with Redis as acceleration, and isolate the checkout pool from browse traffic. Adoption order toward 10M: Fix query/index shape and pool waits; add CDN/cache plus request coalescing; scale gateway, search and cart horizontally; bound checkout admission by the measured inventory/PSP drain rate; then partition event consumers by order or SKU. Shard only when write/data volume, restore time or blast radius—not the headline rate—becomes binding. A service-local reservation plus outbox transaction: BEGIN; -- INSERT INTO command_inbox(command_id, request_hash, status) VALUES (:command_id, :request_hash, 'PROCESSING') ON CONFLICT (command_id) DO NOTHING; -- if row_count = 0: stop and return the stored outcome -- UPDATE inventory SET available = available - :qty, version = version + 1 WHERE sku_id = :sku_id AND available >= :qty AND version = :expected_version; -- require row_count = 1, otherwise roll back as SOLD_OUT/CONFLICT -- INSERT INTO reservation(id, sku_id, order_id, qty, state, expires_at) VALUES (:reservation_id, :sku_id, :order_id, :qty, 'HELD', :expires_at); -- INSERT INTO outbox_event (event_id, aggregate_id, aggregate_version, event_type, payload) VALUES (:event_id, :sku_id, :next_version, 'StockReserved', :payload); -- UPDATE command_inbox SET status = 'RESERVED' WHERE command_id = :command_id; COMMIT; The relay may publish that outbox row more than once after a crash. Consumers therefore keep an inbox or an effect-specific unique key and reject stale aggregate versions. This closes the database/broker dual-write gap; it does not make checkout exactly-once across the PSP. Impact and trade-off The 10M shape removes browse amplification and isolates slow effects without weakening stock correctness. It adds staleness, pending states, broker lag, deduplication tables and DLQ/reconciliation ownership. Kafka fits replayable domain events with many consumer groups; RabbitMQ fits routed commands/tasks—the slash in the diagram is a choice, not a guarantee that they behave the same. Case metadata: Workload: DAU, journeys and API calls per journey, promotion burst, hot-SKU skew and downstream drain rate Freshness: CDN/search/cart projections have separate budgets from checkout authority Duplicate tolerance: command ID, outbox event ID and consumer effect key Recovery owner: each service-local authority plus checkout reconciliation Provider/version scope: Kafka EOS, RabbitMQ confirms/ACKs, Redis cache behavior and database durability are scoped to the named deployed versions/configuration. Kafka transactions can cover a configured Kafka producer/consumer boundary; they do not atomically commit an arbitrary database or PSP effect. Benchmark the chosen topology before turning the planning arithmetic into a capacity claim. Canonical boundary: Reuse (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1), (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) and (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q1); keep this prompt focused on scale-lane sequencing.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/scaling-1m-to-10m-requests#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q15"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q16","surface":"track","en":"The same e-commerce system approaches 100M requests/day, or needs cells because restore time and regional blast radius already exceed target. How do you choose routing and authority boundaries for checkout, order, inventory and payment, migrate without independent dual writes, and keep event replay from applying a payment or compensation twice?","bodyEn":"Justification and boundaries: Shard or introduce cells only when measured write/data volume, restore time, coordination cost or blast radius is binding. Route checkout/order/payment by a stable customer or order cell; inventory remains authoritative by SKU/warehouse and is reached through idempotent commands. Do not pretend all service databases can share one cross-shard transaction—the Saga exists because each participant owns a local invariant. Migration: Use a versioned routing map. Copy historical rows, stream ordered changes through CDC or each service's outbox, and verify counts, checksums, aged pending states, captured totals and stock/reservation invariants before each range cutover. For one routing epoch every write has one authority; fence old writers instead of issuing independent writes to old and new stores. An idempotent, version-guarded payment event consumer: BEGIN; -- INSERT INTO event_inbox(consumer, event_id, aggregate_id, sequence_no) VALUES ('checkout-saga', :event_id, :order_id, :sequence_no) ON CONFLICT (consumer, event_id) DO NOTHING; -- if row_count = 0: duplicate; commit no business effect -- UPDATE checkout_saga SET state = 'PAYMENT_SUCCEEDED', version = version + 1 WHERE saga_id = :saga_id AND state IN ('PAYMENT_PENDING', 'PAYMENT_UNKNOWN') AND version = :expected_version; -- require row_count = 1; otherwise ROLLBACK and park/reconcile the event -- COMMIT; The inbox and Saga transition share one local transaction, so a crash cannot record only one of them. Aggregate sequence/version detects stale or out-of-order delivery; it does not repair it automatically, so the consumer parks the record rather than silently acknowledging an impossible transition. Operations and proof: A cell outage should create scoped PENDING or stale behavior, never hidden global inconsistency. Expose per-cell routing epoch, outbox age, consumer lag, PSP unknown age and recovery point. Reconcile Saga state against Order, Inventory, Payment and the PSP; replay committed outboxes; redrive DLQ records after the defect is fixed; and prove recovery with zero unmatched captures/reservations before removing the old route. Case metadata: Workload: request/data volume, restore time, cell traffic and blast-radius target Freshness: routing epoch and cross-cell projections are versioned Duplicate tolerance: CDC/outbox event ID, migration epoch and replayed payment/compensation command Recovery owner: cell migration owner and each domain authority Canonical boundary: cell/routing interview narrative: (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q4) payment reconciliation: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) replay/consumer ownership: (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6)","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/scaling-1m-to-10m-requests#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q16"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q17","surface":"track","en":"An API has worsening p99 latency for both reads and writes. A proposal says to add read replicas, Redis, and distributed locks immediately. How do you diagnose the actual bottleneck, choose the cheapest effective technique, and distinguish a performance mechanism from a correctness mechanism?","bodyEn":"Diagnose before naming tools: Break p99 down by endpoint into application time, connection wait, query time, lock wait, cache hit rate, and dependency timeouts. Read replicas cannot cure primary write latency, and a Redis cache cannot repair an unindexed or lock-contended transaction. Use the narrowest fix: Improve the query, index, transaction scope, and pool first. Use replicas for read capacity with a stated replication-lag contract; use caching only when the response may be stale, with single-flight fills and jittered TTLs. Move genuinely non-request work behind an outbox and queue, returning a durable pending state. Keep correctness local: A distributed lock may reduce duplicate work, but losing it must not decide whether data is valid. Protect money, inventory, and uniqueness with database constraints or conditional writes. Alert on the measured bottleneck and on the new failure mode each added tier creates. Case metadata: Workload: read/write mix, payload, dependency fan-out, resource contention and tenant skew Freshness: telemetry and replica/cache observations carry timestamps or lag Duplicate tolerance: diagnostic replay is harmless, business remediation still needs an idempotent command Recovery owner: endpoint owner and SRE/on-call Canonical boundary: Put pool, retry, cache, overload and observability mechanics in (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q1) and the relevant Topic 25 owner; this case chooses the cheapest measured fix and states what would falsify it.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/scaling-technique-catalogue#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q17"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q18","surface":"track","en":"Checkout needs one fulfilment job, while payment, notification, search indexing, and analytics all need to observe state changes; catalogue reads also need a freshness contract. Choose between RabbitMQ and Kafka, and between cache and replicas. What durability, ordering, replay, and failure behavior must the design state explicitly?","bodyEn":"Shape the messages first: A fulfilment command has one logical worker, so a work queue such as RabbitMQ can fit; it still needs idempotent handling because redelivery is normal. State changes that several consumers must replay suit Kafka, partitioned by the aggregate key when per-entity order matters. Neither choice makes a database commit and publish atomic: write an outbox with the transaction. Choose the read contract: Replicas are predictable when reads require near-fresh data, but lag must be visible. Cache removes origin load when bounded staleness is acceptable; it needs invalidation/TTL rules, request coalescing, and cold-start protection. It is never the source of truth. Operate the boundary: Record stable event IDs, schema versions, consumer offsets, retry policy, DLQ owner, and oldest-message age. A fast pending response is only honest if users and operators can see progress and recover ambiguity. Case metadata: Workload: command/event rate, consumer count, replay window, catalogue read ratio and payload/retention Freshness: catalogue projection and replica lag are explicit read contracts Duplicate tolerance: command ID, event ID, consumer effect key and offset replay Recovery owner: fulfilment consumer plus each projection owner Provider/version boundary: Kafka EOS is a scoped transaction/consumer guarantee for the configured Kafka client and broker boundary; RabbitMQ publisher confirms and consumer ACKs cover different boundaries and do not mean customer delivery. Name the selected versions, retention, retry/DLQ and database inbox/outbox contract before comparing them. Canonical boundary: See (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1/q6) for broker semantics, (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q1) for service operations and (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) when payment effects enter the flow.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/scaling-technique-catalogue#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q18"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q19","surface":"track","en":"A flash sale has 50,000 visitors refreshing five times in 60 seconds. One hot SKU has a 12 ms atomic-hold benchmark. Design a bounded admission and reservation path: calculate the useful per-SKU limit, decide what the client sees, and preserve inventory when demand is far above capacity.","bodyEn":"Size the hot key: 50,000 × 5 / 60 ≈ 4,167 edge requests/sec. A 12 ms serialized hold has a theoretical ceiling near 83/sec, so admit about 50/sec for failover and variance headroom—not thousands because more app replicas exist. A ten-second waiting room therefore holds at most about 500 admitted attempts; beyond that, return an honest queued range, Retry-After, or sold-out result. Protect the authority: Scope signed admission to campaign, SKU, account, and device/API key. Browse reads a versioned availability projection, while a short database conditional write enforces reserved + sold ≤ sellable and creates an idempotent, expiring hold. A Redis TTL lock is not the stock authority. Keep payment outside: The durable saga calls the PSP after the hold transaction and exposes PENDING. Watch queue age, p99 lock wait, hold success, DB-pool use, and negative inventory; reject when the queue cannot clear inside the sale window. Case metadata: Workload: refresh rate, hot-SKU demand, admission throughput, hold latency and payment drain rate Freshness: availability projection may lag but hold authority is current Duplicate tolerance: admission token, request key, hold/order key and provider key Recovery owner: admission, inventory and payment/reconciliation owners Failure matrix: Window Incorrect shortcut Required state and owner --- --- --- Admission burst exceeds the measured hold lane Admit all visitors because app replicas scale Queue or reject with an honest bound; admission owner protects the inventory budget Hold worker times out Retry with a new hold ID Reuse the request/hold key and return the recorded PENDING/conflict state Database/pool saturation Add a Redis lock and continue accepting Apply backpressure/load shedding and watch the authoritative negative-inventory invariant","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/flash-sale-booking-inventory-bottleneck#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q19"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q20","surface":"track","en":"During a flash sale, Redis is lost, an expired hold races with delayed PSP success, and the gateway, client, and payment webhook all retry. Define the authoritative state, idempotency boundary, expiry rule, and reconciliation path so neither oversell nor a duplicate charge can occur.","bodyEn":"Authority survives cache loss: The inventory row and hold/order state machine are authoritative. A short conditional transaction enforces confirmed + active-held ≤ sellable; every transition carries an expected version or fencing value. Redis may rebuild waiting-room counters and availability projections, but its loss cannot alter stock. One intent, one provider attempt: Persist the client idempotency key and request fingerprint with the hold/order, then create an outbox event. The PSP worker uses one stable provider idempotency key; a timeout is UNKNOWN, never permission to issue a second independent charge. Webhooks, polls, and queue deliveries are recorded idempotently. Resolve races explicitly: An expiry worker releases only the matching still-active version. A late PSP success may not silently confirm an expired hold: reload authority, query the provider, then confirm only if policy and stock allow it; otherwise cancel/refund or compensate through a new recorded transition. Monitor unknown-payment age, outbox lag, duplicate rates, and reconciliation debt. Case metadata: Workload: Redis-loss rate, hold expiry, PSP latency, retry/webhook volume and hot-SKU skew Freshness: inventory/order authority is current while waiting-room/cache/PSP views have explicit lag budgets Duplicate tolerance: client key, hold version, provider key and webhook/event ID Recovery owner: inventory, payment and reconciliation service State transition guard: A bounded model is ACTIVE_HOLD -> EXPIRED RELEASED CONFIRMED; PENDING UNKNOWN -> CONFIRMED FAILED RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED; and late success -> COMPENSATING -> REFUNDED RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED. Redis loss never authorizes a stock transition. Failure matrix: Window Incorrect shortcut Required state and owner --- --- --- Redis disappears during admission/hold Recreate a hold from cache state Re-read the inventory authority and fence stale workers PSP success races expiry Confirm on callback arrival Compare hold version and policy; refund/compensate when stock is no longer confirmable Gateway, client and webhook all retry Charge once per transport attempt Correlate one intent to one provider key and reconcile unknown age Canonical boundary: Use (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2), (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q2) and (15-transactional-outbox-order-workflow) for mechanism details; keep this prompt focused on the flash-sale authority and race policy.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/flash-sale-booking-inventory-bottleneck#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q20"},{"id":"11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21","surface":"track","en":"How do you review a system-design answer so repeated mechanisms stay canonical while each domain still gets a real design?","bodyEn":"Review the answer from the invariant outward. A named technology is not a design until it states authority, workload, freshness, failure cost and proof. Review field Question Good evidence Invariant What must never be false: no double charge, no oversell, no unauthorized read, or only best-effort freshness? State transition, constraint/version, owner and conflict response Authority Which store or provider decides when cache, replica, queue or webhook disagrees? One write owner, read-staleness contract, reconciliation and recovery owner Workload What are peak rate, size, tenants, hot keys, fan-out, burst and downstream quotas? Arithmetic with units, p99/budget, capacity headroom and overload behavior Failure What happens on timeout, duplicate, retry, partition, replay, crash, late success and dependency loss? Unknown/PENDING/compensating states, idempotency boundary, runbook and metrics Canonical ownership Is Saga, Outbox, broker delivery, lock/fencing, API contract or cache policy being re-taught? Link to the owner topic and keep only domain-specific adaptation here Bank/fintech payment may require ledger authority, exact idempotency, reconciliation and strong local consistency. OTA search can tolerate stale projections, while the booking/hold write still needs an authoritative state machine. A notification system may accept at-least-once delivery with deduplication; an OTP may need a tighter expiry and abuse budget. Do not reward a design for saying Kafka, Redis, Saga or distributed lock. Ask which invariant the mechanism protects, what it cannot protect, and what evidence would change the choice. Keep the mechanism explanation in its canonical topic: Topic 08 for broker semantics, Topic 09 for workflow correctness, Topic 17 for API contracts, Topic 25 for service/cache behavior, Topic 28 for leases/fencing. Cases should show their different authority and failure model. A complete answer includes a negative path and an operational proof: what is measured, who is paged, how to replay/reconcile, and how rollback works when the schema or provider version changes. Case metadata: Workload: review time, evidence volume, peak/average assumptions, tenant/data sensitivity and dependency limits Freshness: claims and measurements carry a reviewed-at date and scope Duplicate tolerance: repeated mechanisms are linked to one owner instead of copied Recovery owner: design reviewer and the canonical mechanism owner Canonical map: broker delivery → (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1) payment/workflow correctness → (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2) concurrency interview framing → (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q4) service/cache/operations → (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q1) concrete DB/outbox crash walkthrough → (15-transactional-outbox-order-workflow) For browser/API authorization, name the selected IdP and version and use the RFC 9700 profile rather than generalizing OAuth provider behavior.","contextEn":"System design — real interview prompts","href":"/system-design/design-review-framework#question-11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.architecture-styles.q1","surface":"track","en":"Layered (n-tier) architecture: pros/cons?","bodyEn":"Layers: controller → service → repository → DB, dependencies point downward. Pros: familiar, simple, easy to onboard Cons: business logic easily leaks into a thin service (anemic domain), framework/DB seeps back upward, hard to test logic in isolation, prone to becoming a 'big ball of mud' as it grows dependencies point downward to the DB → framework/JPA/DB seeps back up into the domain (JPA entity = domain model → tight coupling to infrastructure) business logic leaks into a thin 'service' → anemic domain (entity is just getters/setters); prone to becoming a 'big ball of mud' (god-class service) Layered is NOT wrong — it's a reasonable default for most apps; it only becomes a problem when the domain is complex & long-lived (fintech core). That's when you move to Hexagonal/Clean. Don't over-engineer Clean for simple CRUD. The clearest sign layered has run out of room: when a small business change forces edits across all four layers, or when testing one business rule requires standing up the whole Spring context plus a database. The real-world build of the replacement architecture is in 12.15, and the gradual migration path is in 12.21.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.architecture-styles.q1"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.architecture-styles.q2","surface":"track","en":"Hexagonal (Ports & Adapters): what's the core idea?","bodyEn":"Put the domain at the center, communicating outward through a port (interface). The outside world (web, DB, queue) is an adapter plugged into the port. The domain knows NOTHING about the framework/DB. driving adapter (UI/API): calls into the domain driven adapter (DB/broker): is called out to by the domain through a port Benefit: swapping infrastructure doesn't touch the domain, the domain can be tested with mock adapters, boundaries are clear. the domain defines the port (interface); the adapter implements the port (dependency inversion: the domain defines PaymentGateway, infrastructure implements a Stripe adapter) measurable benefit: test the use case with an in-memory adapter (no DB/Docker), swap infrastructure without touching the domain OTA: the Booking domain doesn't know about Postgres/Kafka/GDS — it has ports SeatInventory, PaymentGateway, GdsClient; adapters plug into them. The cost: mapping between domain entities and JPA + a lot of interfaces → only worth it when the domain is complex. The real-world build in code: port/in, port/out package structure, adapters for a Spring Boot service (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q1), and which layer transaction/validation/mapping belong in (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q5).","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.architecture-styles.q2"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.architecture-styles.q3","surface":"track","en":"Clean Architecture and the Dependency Rule?","bodyEn":"Concentric rings: Entities → Use Cases → Interface Adapters → Frameworks/Drivers. Dependency Rule: dependencies point inward only; an inner ring never knows about an outer ring. Uses dependency inversion (interface on the inside, implementation on the outside) → DB/web become swappable details. Bottom line Hexagonal, Onion, and Clean are the same idea: domain at the core, infrastructure at the edge, dependencies pointing inward. They differ mostly in vocabulary and level of detail. Clean/Hexagonal is NOT 'split into 4 folders' — it's a dependency rule — the domain imports nothing from Spring/JPA/Kafka enforced through dependency inversion: the domain defines PaymentGateway (a port), the infrastructure module implements it with a Stripe/PSP adapter benefit: test the use case with an in-memory adapter, swap the PSP without touching the domain, the domain reads as pure 'business' with no 'JPA' in sight The cost: lots of interfaces/mapping — only worth it when the domain is complex. Plain CRUD → layered is enough. Bottom line: 'architecture serves the business's rate of change, not diagram aesthetics'. Carry the rule into the real build: Package a Spring Boot service around the architecture: (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q1) Enforce the Dependency Rule with ArchUnit or Spring Modulith: (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q4) Without automated enforcement, the dependency rule drifts over time.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.architecture-styles.q3"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.architecture-styles.q4","surface":"track","en":"Layered vs Hexagonal/Clean — when to pick which?","bodyEn":"Layered Hexagonal/Clean Good fit for simple CRUD, small team, thin domain complex domain, long lifespan, many integrations Testability tied to DB/framework test the domain with a mock adapter Changing infrastructure seeps everywhere only touch the adapter Cost low more layers/abstraction Warning don't apply Clean to trivial CRUD — the boilerplate cost isn't worth it. Choose based on domain complexity. not a whole-system binary choice — apply it per service/context: complex Booking/Payment → Clean; simple lookup/reporting → layered signs you need Clean: logic is hard to test because it's tied to DB/framework, or swapping a PSP means editing everywhere signs of over-engineering: lots of interfaces/mapping for trivial CRUD 'Architecture serves the business's rate of change, not diagram aesthetics' — choose based on domain complexity & how often it changes. Measure the cost and the benefit with the test pyramid mapped onto each layer (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q6); migrating a running layered codebase gradually follows the six steps in 12.21.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.architecture-styles.q4"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q1","surface":"track","en":"Strategic DDD: bounded context, ubiquitous language, context mapping?","bodyEn":"Bounded Context: a boundary within which one model/language has consistent meaning ('Account' in Billing differs from Auth) → a natural choice for micro-service boundaries Ubiquitous Language: developers and domain experts share the same vocabulary, reflected in the code Context Mapping: describes the relationships between contexts (Customer-Supplier, Anti-Corruption Layer, Shared Kernel...) Bottom line Strategic DDD is essentially the tool for drawing micro-service boundaries along business lines — matching 'service boundary = consistency boundary'. a bounded context is usually = one service + one dedicated DB; the boundary sits where language/meaning changes (OTA: 'Booking', 'Inventory', 'Pricing', 'Payment', 'Loyalty' — 'fare' in Pricing differs from 'amount' in Payment) context map: Payment = Supplier, Booking = Customer; insert an Anti-Corruption Layer when integrating a legacy GDS an aggregate holds invariants within a transaction boundary (Booking guarantees 'total seats = number of passengers') Mistake: splitting services by technical layer (controller-service-dao) instead of by business capability → distributed monolith. Topic 24 is the deep dive on this section: getting the ubiquitous language right (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q1) finding boundaries with event storming (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q4) the relationship types in a context map (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3).","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q1"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q2","surface":"track","en":"Tactical DDD: entity, value object, aggregate, repository, domain event?","bodyEn":"Entity: has identity, a lifecycle (Account, Order) Value Object: immutable, defined by its value (Money, Address) Aggregate: a cluster of objects with an aggregate root as the entry point, protecting invariants; a transaction should be scoped to one aggregate Repository: an abstraction for retrieving aggregates (hides the DB) Domain Event: something that already happened (OrderPlaced) — the foundation for event-driven/outbox Money is the classic value object in fintech (immutable, carries a currency). an Aggregate is a transaction & consistency boundary — only changed through the aggregate root, protecting invariants; one transaction should be scoped to one aggregate (cross-aggregate work uses eventual consistency + domain events) keep aggregates small (don't gather the whole world → heavy contention/locking) Value Objects are immutable (Money, SeatNumber) — compared by value, thread-safe OTA: the Booking aggregate holds the seat/price invariant; LedgerEntry is append-only; a PaymentSucceeded domain event is published. An aggregate boundary that's too large = lock contention & hard to scale. Deep dive into each block: Entity vs. Value Object (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q1), how to size aggregate boundaries (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2), where Repository/Factory/Domain Service each belong (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q4).","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q2"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q3","surface":"track","en":"When SHOULD and SHOULDN'T you use DDD?","bodyEn":"Use it when the domain is business-complex (many rules, changing over time) — fintech, insurance, logistics Don't when it's just CRUD/forms-over-data: the conceptual cost (aggregate, context) > the benefit DDD is about modelling the business, not a mandatory code pattern. USE it when the domain is complex (many rules, needs a shared language with domain experts) — fintech, OTA (fare rules, overbooking, refund policy) DON'T when it's CRUD/forms-over-data (the cost of aggregates, bounded contexts, mapping > the benefit) you can use strategic DDD (bounded contexts splitting services) without heavy tactical DDD, or vice versa Apply DDD selectively: core domain (booking/pricing/ledger: full tactical DDD) vs. supporting/generic (notifications/reporting: layered/CRUD or buy off-the-shelf). Mistake: DDD-ifying everything, or skipping it on a genuinely complex core (anemic domain + logic leaking through every service). 'What problem DDD actually solves' is covered in more depth in 24.1 — and it also opens with exactly this question of when not to use it.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q3"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q4","surface":"track","en":"CQRS and Event Sourcing — when do they make sense?","bodyEn":"CQRS: separates the write model (command) from the read model (query) → each optimized independently, reads use a read model/projection Event Sourcing: state = a sequence of events A powerful pairing for audit trails & rebuilding views (a great fit for a ledger). Trade-off: complexity, eventual consistency between the write and read model, event versioning. Don't use it by default — only when audit needs/read scaling/model transformation justify it. CQRS separates the command (normalized, protects invariants) from the query (read model optimized for the screen) → scale & optimize independently; the read model can be denormalized/live in a different store (Elasticsearch/Redis) Event Sourcing stores the event sequence as the source of truth → perfect audit trail, time-travel, rebuildable projections trade-offs: eventual consistency between write↔read model, event versioning/upcasting, snapshots for fast replay OTA: an event-sourced ledger (easy reconciliation/balance rebuilding); a separate 'booking history' read model projection. But lightweight CQRS (just splitting the read model, not full ES) is usually enough — don't default to full ES because it 'sounds impressive'. Before even considering event sourcing, distinguish a domain event from an integration event first (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3): using domain events doesn't mean storing state as events.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q4"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q1","surface":"track","en":"Which design patterns should a senior engineer know cold (and why)?","bodyEn":"Strategy: pick an algorithm at runtime (PSP routing) — avoids a giant if-else chain Factory/Abstract Factory: create complex/multi-variant objects Builder: construct an immutable object with many fields Decorator: add behavior (logging, caching) without modifying the core Adapter: bridge incompatible interfaces (anti-corruption layer) Observer: internal event-driven behavior distributed: Circuit Breaker, Saga, Outbox, CQRS Describe a pattern through the problem + trade-off, not just the name. Strategy (routing PSP/GDS by condition, replacing if-else — underpins OCP), Factory (multi-variant objects) Builder (immutable object with many fields — Itinerary), Decorator (logging/caching/retry without touching the core), Adapter (ACL with a legacy GDS), Observer (internal event-driven behavior) A pattern is shared vocabulary + a proven solution — but don't cram patterns in (over-engineering); pick one when the problem genuinely shows up. Knowing when NOT to use a pattern matters just as much for a senior engineer.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q1"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q2","surface":"track","en":"SOLID — a pragmatic reading for backend engineers?","bodyEn":"SRP: a class has one reason to change OCP: open for extension, closed for modification (Strategy/polymorphism) LSP: a subtype can replace its supertype without breaking behavior ISP: small, need-specific interfaces, avoid a 'fat interface' DIP: depend on abstractions — the foundation of Hexagonal/Clean DIP + ISP are exactly what let the domain stay independent of infrastructure. SRP: a service/class shouldn't carry multiple responsibilities (PaymentService shouldn't also do notifications) OCP: adding a new PSP shouldn't require editing existing code (Strategy/polymorphism) LSP: don't override in a way that narrows behavior; ISP: small need-specific interfaces (avoid a fat interface); DIP: the domain defines the port, infrastructure implements it SOLID is guidance, not hard law — apply it with judgment (don't create an abstraction for something with only one implementation 'just in case' → YAGNI). The goal: code that's safe to change, not dogma compliance.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q2"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3","surface":"track","en":"Common anti-patterns: distributed monolith, anemic domain, god object?","bodyEn":"Distributed monolith: services are split but tightly coupled (shared DB, long synchronous call chains) → you get all of micro-services' downsides with none of the benefits. Sign: changing 1 service forces deploying several others Anemic domain model: entities are just getters/setters, logic piles up in the service → loses the power of OOP/DDD God object/service: one class does far too much Golden rule 'a service does not share a database'. Two services writing to the same table is a distributed-monolith red flag. Distributed monolith: tight coupling (shared DB, long synchronous call chains, synchronized deploys). Sign: changing 1 service forces deploying 5 others; two services writing to the same table → fix: database-per-service, clear async communication/contracts, boundaries drawn along bounded contexts Anemic domain → fix: move behavior/invariants into the aggregate; God object → fix: split along SRP 'Micro-services are a trade-off, not an upgrade'; without a clear organizational/scaling reason → stick with a modular monolith. Anemic domain from the DDD angle: how to spot it (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q1) and how to avoid it — behavior belongs to the aggregate, and only what belongs to no aggregate becomes a Domain Service (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q4).","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q4","surface":"track","en":"Coupling & cohesion — the design compass?","bodyEn":"Goal: low coupling, high cohesion — independent modules, each module gathering related things. low coupling → local changes don't spread high cohesion → code is easy to understand/locate This is the implicit criterion behind every module/service boundary decision. Coupling type Symptom Danger level Shared database two services write to the same table highest — nobody can change the schema anymore Long synchronous call chains A calls B calls C calls D high — errors and latency compound Shared model library one shared DTO jar medium — bumping the version touches everyone Via events with a contract publish/subscribe low — but still needs versioning low coupling = changing A doesn't force changing B (via interface/event, not sharing internals/DB) high cohesion = everything in a module serves one purpose measure by: how far do changes spread? (high coupling = spreads a lot); does a module have 'many reasons to change'? (low cohesion) A bounded context = a cohesion boundary; coupling between contexts is kept minimal via context map/ACL/events. Connascence (how strongly two parts must change together) is a more refined way to measure coupling. Bottom line: two things that must change together often → same module; loosely related → split apart.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q4"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q5","surface":"track","en":"Modular monolith vs micro-services — when do you split?","bodyEn":"Modular monolith Micro-services Deploy single each service independently Transaction local ACID distributed (Saga) Operations simple network, consistency, observability Split when — need independent scale/deploy/team 8 years of experience, bottom line start with a modular monolith with clean boundaries; only split into micro-services when there's a clear organizational/scaling reason. 'Microservices are a trade-off, not an upgrade.' Modular monolith: single deploy, clearly bounded modules (each module has an internal API, no reaching across internals) → 1 deploy, schema-per-module, local ACID transactions, boundaries are easy to refactor (same codebase) Micro-services when you need: independent scaling, independent deploys per team (Conway's law), isolating failures/technology — traded for distributed complexity Start with a modular monolith with clean boundaries (easy to split later); split when there's a clear organizational/scaling reason. Splitting too early + wrong boundaries = distributed monolith. Enforcing module boundaries right inside the monolith with Spring Modulith, and package-by-feature vs. package-by-layer (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8).","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q5"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q6","surface":"track","en":"What does an Anti-Corruption Layer (ACL) do when integrating with a legacy system?","bodyEn":"ACL (Anti-Corruption Layer) = a translation layer between your domain and an external/legacy system with a different model → protects the domain from being 'contaminated' by bad concepts/structures. usually implemented as an Adapter + mapping useful when integrating with old core banking systems or a PSP with a non-standard model Interview trap An ACL is easily mistaken for 'just a mapper'. The distinguishing point: a mapper translates structure (this field to that field), while an ACL also translates concepts — the other side calls it PAX with five states while your domain only has three, and the ACL must decide which mapping applies and reject what's invalid. If your ACL never throws an error anywhere, it's almost certainly just a mapper. ACL = Adapter + mapping between the domain and a messy external/legacy system → changes on their side only affect the ACL, never spreading into the domain implementation: an interface (port) on the domain side + an adapter that translates the external system's DTO ↔ the domain model, also handling differences in semantics/units/errors OTA: integrating a legacy GDS/airline system (Amadeus/Sabre with their own schema/format, odd error codes) through an ACL → the Booking domain only ever sees a clean model; switching GDS providers only touches the ACL. The ACL is also where you gather retry/idempotency/circuit-breaker logic for external integrations. ACL is one relationship type in a context map, alongside Customer/Supplier, Conformist, and Open Host Service — pick the wrong relationship type and an ACL can't save you (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3).","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q6"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q7","surface":"track","en":"The 23 GoF patterns — which does modern Java already give you, which do you still write, and which has the language replaced?","bodyEn":"The useful way to hold the catalogue is not 23 definitions but three groups, then a second cut by who writes the pattern today. Creational (5) — how an object is made: Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, Prototype, Singleton. Structural (7) — how objects are composed: Adapter, Bridge, Composite, Decorator, Facade, Flyweight, Proxy. Behavioral (11) — how objects divide work: Chain of Responsibility, Command, Interpreter, Iterator, Mediator, Memento, Observer, State, Strategy, Template Method, Visitor. Where it lives now Patterns Example The platform ships it — use it, don't write it Iterator · Proxy · Observer Chain of Responsibility · Facade · Flyweight Iterable · Spring AOP and @Transactional proxies ApplicationEventPublisher · servlet and Security filter chains SLF4J (the F is facade) · Integer.valueOf caching −128..127 You still write it in service code Strategy · Factory · Builder · Adapter Decorator · State · Template Method · Command PSP routing per provider · an adapter as anti-corruption layer a decorator for retry or metrics · an order state machine A language feature replaced it Visitor · Prototype · Singleton one-method Strategy or Command classes sealed types + pattern-matching switch (Java 21) copy constructor · container scope · a lambda Rare in backend code Interpreter · Memento · Mediator · Bridge · Composite expression languages · undo · UI coordination driver/API splits · trees How to talk about one Name the problem first, then the pattern, then its cost: \"the rules for choosing a PSP changed weekly, so routing became a Strategy per provider; the cost is one more indirection to trace.\" Visitor → switch. Visitor exists to add operations to a closed hierarchy without editing it. With a sealed interface and record patterns, a switch over the subtypes is exhaustive and compiler-checked, so a missing case is a compile error instead of a missing visit method. Prototype → copy constructors. Object.clone() is a shallow copy with an awkward contract (Effective Java, Item 13); a copy constructor or static copy factory says exactly what gets copied. Strategy and Command → lambdas. A strategy with one method and no state is a Function, Comparator or Runnable. A class per strategy earns its keep once it has configuration, a name in the logs, or tests of its own. Template Method vs callbacks. Spring's JdbcTemplate and TransactionTemplate keep the fixed steps (acquire, run, release) and take the varying part as a callback instead of a subclass — the same idea, with composition instead of inheritance. Decorator vs Proxy. Both wrap the same interface; a decorator adds behaviour the caller wants, a proxy controls access to the target — and a Spring proxy is why self-invocation skips @Transactional (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q2). State vs Strategy differ in who switches: a strategy is chosen from outside, a state replaces itself on each transition (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q2). Singleton in practice is (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q7). the short list to know cold is (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q1). The grouping above is this site's judgement for Java backend services, not a measurement; the catalogue and its intents come from Gamma, Helm, Johnson and Vlissides (1994).","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q7"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q1","surface":"track","en":"What does a real-world package structure for a Spring Boot service look like under Clean/Hexagonal?","bodyEn":"12.3 covers the rule; this one covers the folders. The most common structure, split by feature first, layer second: com.acme.booking ├── domain/ // does NOT import Spring, JPA, Jackson │ ├── Booking.java // business entity + invariants │ ├── Money.java // value object │ ├── BookingStatus.java │ └── DomainException.java ├── application/ │ ├── port/in/ // use case interface (entry port) │ │ ├── HoldSeatUseCase.java │ │ └── CancelBookingUseCase.java │ ├── port/out/ // exit port — domain DEFINES it, infrastructure implements it │ │ ├── BookingRepository.java │ │ ├── PaymentGateway.java │ │ └── SeatLockPort.java │ └── service/ │ └── HoldSeatService.java // orchestration, opens the transaction ├── adapter/ │ ├── in/web/ // Controller, request/response DTOs │ ├── in/messaging/ // Kafka consumer │ ├── out/persistence/ // JpaBookingRepository + BookingJpaEntity │ └── out/psp/ // StripePaymentGateway └── config/ // @Configuration, wiring, the only place that knows everything Dependency direction: adapter → application → domain. Never the other way around. An easily missed point: port/out lives inside application, not inside adapter. The interface is defined by the inside according to its own needs, and the outside is what implements it — that's exactly dependency inversion, and it's also what separates someone who understands it from someone who just copied the folder layout. Name ports after the business language, not the technology: PaymentGateway, not StripeClient; SeatLockPort, not RedisLock. A name that leaks the technology is a sign infrastructure is seeping inward. one use case = one small interface in port/in (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q3). A 20-method interface is the old Service wearing a new outfit. config is the only place allowed to know about every layer — it wires adapters into ports. Keep it thin and free of logic. package-private is your friend: keeping BookingJpaEntity package-private inside out/persistence means no other layer can accidentally use it (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8). Don't create a separate Maven module right from the start — use packages first, split into modules only when you genuinely need to enforce the boundary (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q4). For a thin domain (CRUD), this structure is over-engineering — 12.4 already warned about this. Use it when the domain has real invariants worth protecting.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q1"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2","surface":"track","en":"Domain model and JPA entity: two separate classes, or share one class?","bodyEn":"The most contentious real-world question when applying Clean to Spring. Three options: Share 1 class Split into 2 classes + mapper Shared but hides JPA Boilerplate none a lot (two-way mapper) little Clean domain no — tied to JPA completely clean mostly clean Invariants hard — JPA needs a no-arg ctor + setters protected partially protected Lazy loading leaks outside the domain doesn't leak still leaks Good fit when CRUD, thin domain complex, long-lived domain middle ground Why sharing hurts: JPA requires a no-arg constructor and setters so it can reconstruct the object → the domain can no longer enforce its invariants. On top of that, a lazy @OneToMany turns a plain property read in the domain into an actual SQL query, and LazyInitializationException blows up in a layer that has nothing to do with the DB. // domain/Booking.java — no annotations at all public final class Booking { private final BookingId id; private BookingStatus status; private final List<Passenger> passengers; public void cancel () { // invariant lives in the domain if (status == ISSUED) throw new DomainException( \"Ticket already issued, must be refunded\" ); status = CANCELLED; } } // adapter/out/persistence/BookingJpaEntity.java — pure storage @Entity @Table (name = \"bookings\" ) class BookingJpaEntity { @Id private String id; @Enumerated (STRING) private BookingStatus status; protected BookingJpaEntity () {} // JPA needs it — but never exposed to the domain } Hand-written mappers are error-prone and tedious → use MapStruct (generates code at compile time, no reflection at runtime). This is a case where a library is worth it. The real cost of splitting is not the mapper — it's when the domain and the table diverge significantly (an aggregate spanning 4 tables). That's when the repository adapter has to assemble/split them — and that's exactly what it exists to do. Half-hearted trap: splitting into 2 classes but letting the domain's getId() return the DB's auto-increment Long. The domain should use its own id (UUID/business code), generated in the domain — see 17.1. If you choose to share: at minimum keep setters package-private, add @Access(FIELD), and forbid EAGER — reduces leakage without paying for a mapper. Decide per bounded context, not for the whole repo: Booking/Payment get split; a simple airport-code lookup table can share a class, or even skip a domain model entirely. Common trick question: \"does the repository return the domain object or the entity?\" — always the domain object. If it returns the entity, that port isn't really a port.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q3","surface":"track","en":"How should a use case (application service) be designed? One class per use case, or grouped into a service?","bodyEn":"One class per use case — this is the biggest difference from an 800-line BookingService in the layered style. // port/in — the contract, reading it tells you what the system can do public interface HoldSeatUseCase { HoldResult hold (HoldSeatCommand cmd); } // application/service — orchestration, contains NO business rules @Service class HoldSeatService implements HoldSeatUseCase { private final BookingRepository bookings; // port/out private final SeatLockPort locks; @Override @Transactional // the transaction boundary is HERE public HoldResult hold (HoldSeatCommand cmd) { if (!locks. tryLock (cmd. flightId (), cmd. seatNo ())) return HoldResult. taken (); Booking b = bookings. findById (cmd. bookingId ()) . orElseThrow (NotFound:: new ); b. holdSeat (cmd. seatNo ()); // the rule lives in the DOMAIN bookings. save (b); return HoldResult. ok (b. holdExpiresAt ()); } } Clear boundary: the use case orchestrates (fetch → call the domain → save → publish event). The business decision of \"can this seat be held\" lives inside Booking, not here. If a use case is full of business if statements, the domain is anemic (12.11 anemic domain). Command/Result are the application layer's DTOs, separate from the web layer's DTOs (adapter/in/web). This sounds redundant, but it lets you change the REST contract (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4) without touching the use case, and lets the same use case run from a Kafka consumer. A Command should self-validate its syntax right in the constructor (a record compact constructor) — it should be impossible to create a meaningless HoldSeatCommand. This is the \"make invalid state unrepresentable\" style. A use case calling another use case is a smell: either extract the shared part down into a domain service, or use a domain event. Nested calls muddy the transaction and make errors hard to trace. Don't let a use case return a raw JPA entity or domain object to the controller — return a Result so the controller doesn't depend on the domain's shape. A read-only (query) use case usually doesn't need to go through the domain: read directly via a projection for speed. That's the natural gateway into CQRS (12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q4) without doing full event sourcing. Class names should be business verbs: HoldSeat, IssueTicket, RefundBooking. Reading the file list in port/in tells you what the system does — 'screaming architecture' (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8). Quick quality check: application/service imports should only include domain, port, and transaction annotations. Seeing javax.persistence or HttpServletRequest here means it's already broken.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q3"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you enforce the Dependency Rule? Without tooling, the architecture drifts within 3 months.","bodyEn":"A rule that lives only in people's heads will drift — a new dev adds import javax.persistence to the domain and nobody catches it in review. Three levels of enforcement, increasing in strength: 1. ArchUnit — a test like any other, cheapest, do this first: @AnalyzeClasses (packages = \"com.acme.booking\" ) class ArchitectureTest { @ArchTest static final ArchRule domain_does_not_depend_on_infrastructure = noClasses().that().resideInAPackage( \"..domain..\" ) .should().dependOnClassesThat() .resideInAnyPackage( \"..adapter..\" , \"org.springframework..\" , \"jakarta.persistence..\" , \"com.fasterxml..\" ); @ArchTest static final ArchRule layers_respect_direction = layeredArchitecture().consideringOnlyDependenciesInLayers() .layer( \"Domain\" ).definedBy( \"..domain..\" ) .layer( \"Application\" ).definedBy( \"..application..\" ) .layer( \"Adapter\" ).definedBy( \"..adapter..\" ) .whereLayer( \"Adapter\" ).mayNotBeAccessedByAnyLayer() .whereLayer( \"Application\" ).mayOnlyBeAccessedByLayers( \"Adapter\" ); @ArchTest static final ArchRule no_field_injection_allowed = noFields().should().beAnnotatedWith(Autowired. class ); } 2. Maven/Gradle module — domain is a module that declares no dependency on Spring/JPA at all. The compiler blocks it instead of a test; in exchange the build gets more complex and cross-module refactors get slower. 3. Spring Modulith — ApplicationModules.of(App.class).verify() checks module boundaries inside a monolith, and also generates documentation and diagrams (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8). Practical recommendation: ArchUnit from day one (10 lines, runs in CI), split into modules only once the team is large and the rule keeps getting violated. Put ArchUnit in mandatory CI, not an optional test. A rule that doesn't run in the pipeline is a rule that doesn't exist. Start with a single rule (domain doesn't depend on infrastructure) and tighten gradually. Applying 15 rules to an old codebase at once makes the team disable the whole test suite. For a codebase with existing violations: ArchUnit has freeze() (FreezingArchRule) — it locks in current violations as a baseline and only blocks new ones. This is the only way to apply it to a project already in flight, and it's a very \"actually done this\" answer. Rules worth adding early: forbid System.out, forbid java.util.Date, require class names in port/in to end with UseCase, forbid cycles between packages (slices().should().beFreeOfCycles()). ArchUnit reads bytecode, so it cannot catch dependencies through reflection or class-name strings — rare, but worth knowing the limitation. Tooling still needs to run alongside review: dependencies pointing the right way while business logic sits in the wrong place is something no tool catches.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q4"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q5","surface":"track","en":"Which layer should transactions, validation, and mapping live in?","bodyEn":"Three things that cut across layers, and getting them wrong is a common reason an architecture with \"the right folders\" is still hard to maintain. Transaction — in application/service, exactly one place. Use case = one transaction. Opens on entry, closes on exit. Don't put @Transactional on the controller (holds the transaction open through JSON serialization) and don't put it on the repository adapter (each call becomes its own transaction → the use case loses atomicity). The domain must not know what a transaction even is — it's an infrastructure detail. Validation — three kinds, three different places: Kind Example Lives in Returns error Syntactic well-formed email, positive number, required field adapter/in ( @Valid on the web DTO) 400 Domain invariant can't cancel an already-issued ticket domain 409/422 Rule needing external data is the seat still free, is the balance sufficient application (read via port, then ask the domain) 409 A domain invariant must live in the domain, even if the adapter already checked it — the adapter can be bypassed (Kafka consumer, batch job, test), the domain cannot. Mapping — each layer maps into the layer inward from it, never skipping layers: WebRequest ──(adapter/in)──> Command ──> Domain Domain ──> Result ──(adapter/in)──> WebResponse Domain <──(adapter/out)──> JpaEntity @Transactional on a private method or called internally via this doesn't run — Spring's proxying (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q4). This is a classic bug, and in this architecture it tends to show up when a use case gets split into small methods within the same class. Calling an external API (PSP, GDS) must not happen inside a DB transaction (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4): it holds the connection open for the entire network wait. Correct pattern: a short transaction writes PENDING status → call external system → a short transaction writes the result, or use an outbox (topic 9). Any invariant that can't be protected in memory (preventing double-booking across multiple instances) has to fall back to a DB constraint or an atomic update this is the real boundary of Clean Architecture, and being able to explain it scores well (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2). @Valid only checks shape. Don't stuff business rules into a custom validator — they'll end up outside the domain and unreusable. Mapping across many layers looks excessive for CRUD, so for a simple context deliberately allow a shortcut and record the decision (an ADR) instead of rigidly applying it everywhere.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q5"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q6","surface":"track","en":"How does the test pyramid map onto the layers? Why does Clean make testing cheaper?","bodyEn":"The most pragmatic reason to choose Clean is not \"cleanliness\" but fast, stable tests — answering with this hits the real point. Layer Test kind Tooling Speed Proportion Domain pure unit, no Spring JUnit + AssertJ ~1ms the most Application (use case) unit with fake ports JUnit + fake/mock ~5ms a lot Adapter out (DB) narrow integration @DataJpaTest + Testcontainers ~1s moderate Adapter in (web) narrow integration @WebMvcTest ~1s moderate Full system main happy paths @SpringBootTest ~10s very few Architecture rules ArchUnit ~2s 1 suite Why it's cheaper: the domain doesn't import Spring, so testing it needs no context to start up. A 2000-test domain suite runs in a few seconds. In a layered codebase where logic sits in @Service calling Repository directly, every test has to spin up a context or mock JPA — slow and brittle. // Testing the use case: the port is an interface so it can be faked, no DB needed @Test void cannot_hold_a_seat_someone_else_already_holds () { var locks = new InMemorySeatLocks(); // hand-written fake, not Mockito locks. tryLock ( \"VN231\" , \"12A\" ); // someone else holds it first var service = new HoldSeatService( new InMemoryBookings(booking), locks); var r = service. hold ( new HoldSeatCommand(id, \"VN231\" , \"12A\" )); assertThat(r. status ()). isEqualTo (TAKEN); } Hand-written fakes beat mocks for a port used a lot: InMemoryBookingRepository written once, used in 50 tests, is easier to read than 5 lines of when(...).thenReturn(...). Mocks are fine for a single call. Anti-pattern number one: @SpringBootTest for everything. The suite takes 8 minutes to run, developers stop running tests locally, and tests fail for reasons that have nothing to do with what they're testing. Testcontainers beats H2: H2 has no next-key locking, different syntax, a different query plan — tests pass while prod fails. This is a place worth being strict about. Domain tests should be named after the business rule (cannot_cancel_an_already_issued_ticket) rather than the method (testCancel). Reading the test list reads like a spec. This architecture also makes consumer-driven contract testing (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4) natural: the web adapter is thin, so contract tests only need to touch the adapter. Measure with mutation testing (PIT) if you want to know whether tests actually catch bugs — high coverage with a low mutation score means tests exercise the code without actually checking anything.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q6"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q7","surface":"track","en":"How do you migrate a running layered codebase to Clean, step by step?","bodyEn":"This question separates people who read about it from people who've actually done it: nobody gets to rewrite from scratch. Do it incrementally, staying deployable at every step. A deliberate order — 6 steps: Pick the single most painful bounded context (usually Booking or Payment), not the whole repo. Win in one place before spreading out. Build a safety net first: characterization tests for current behavior. Without tests, every refactor is a gamble. Extract port/out first — this is the highest-payoff step. Create a BookingRepository interface in application/port/out and have the existing JpaBookingRepository implement it. No need to change the model or add a mapper yet. Pull business rules out of Service and down into the domain, one rule at a time. Each pull adds a pure domain test. Split use cases out of the BookingService god-class: each public method becomes its own use case class. Split the model (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2) — do this last, since it's the most effort for the least payoff. Many teams stop at step 5, and that's good enough. Guarding against drifting back: turn on ArchUnit with freeze() starting at step 3 — lock in old violations as a baseline, block new ones (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q4). Without it, new code keeps getting written the old way while you're cleaning up the old code. Common mistake Starting by creating all 4 folders and dumping the old classes into them as-is. The result is layered wearing a Clean costume: folders are right, dependencies are still wrong, and the team believes it's done. The dependency direction is the architecture, not the folder tree. Apply the strangler fig pattern: write new features in the new structure right away, migrate old features when there's a reason to touch them anyway. No sprint ever gets approved for \"pure refactoring\" — so it has to ride along with real work. Step 3 has the highest payoff because it unlocks testing: having an interface means you can fake it, which means you can write tests fast, and every later step depends on that. Measure progress with numbers: how many classes in domain don't import Spring, how many tests need no context, how long the suite takes to run. Saying \"it's cleaner now\" doesn't get you approval to keep going. Write an ADR (Architecture Decision Record) for big decisions — six months later even you will have forgotten why you chose to split the model. For legacy systems integrated externally, insert an Anti-Corruption Layer (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q6) at the boundary so their messy model doesn't seep into the new domain. Know when to stop: a purely CRUD context only needs steps 1–3. Forcing Clean onto a lookup module is exactly the over-engineering 12.4 warns about. A migration slice needs an executable equivalence gate, not only a package move: @Test void extractedQuoteUseCasePreservesObservedLegacyBehaviour() { QuoteRequest fixture = Fixtures.quoteWithTaxAndDiscount(); Quote legacyResult = legacyPricing.quote(fixture); Quote extractedResult = new QuoteUseCase( new LegacyPricingAdapter(legacyPricing), fixedClock).quote(fixture); assertThat(extractedResult) .usingRecursiveComparison() .isEqualTo(legacyResult); } ./mvnw -Dtest=PricingMigrationCharacterizationTest test This characterization test creates a seam before callers move. Benefit: extraction can be incremental and rollback is a routing change. Cost: it freezes observed behaviour, including bugs. Mark known defects, then replace temporary equivalence with domain/contract tests once the new owner is established.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q7"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8","surface":"track","en":"Package-by-layer vs package-by-feature, and Spring Modulith for a modular monolith?","bodyEn":"Package-by-layer (the default for most Spring projects): com.acme ├── controller/ BookingController, FlightController, UserController ├── service/ BookingService, FlightService, UserService └── repository/ BookingRepository, FlightRepository, UserRepository Opening the repo only tells you \"this is a Spring app\". Changing one feature touches 3 folders, and nothing stops UserService from calling BookingRepository directly. Package-by-feature (also called screaming architecture): com.acme ├── booking/ web/ · application/ · domain/ · persistence/ ├── flight/ web/ · application/ · domain/ · persistence/ └── user/ web/ · application/ · domain/ · persistence/ Opening the repo tells you right away what this system does. More importantly: booking can keep everything except its public API package-private — the compiler blocks cross-access, no tooling needed. Spring Modulith — turns that boundary into something checkable: @Test void modules_respect_boundaries () { ApplicationModules. of (Application. class ). verify (); // fails on a violation } // Modules communicate via events, not by calling each other's beans directly @ApplicationModuleListener // = async + transactional void on (BookingConfirmed e) { notifications. send (e. bookingId ()); } It also generates module diagrams and documentation straight from the code, and has an event publication registry so events aren't lost when a consumer fails. The two axes are independent: layer vs. feature is how you split packages; Clean/Hexagonal is dependency direction. The best combination is feature on the outside, layer on the inside (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q1). A modular monolith is the right stopping point for most teams (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q5): clear boundaries without paying the distributed-systems cost. Split into micro-services when there's an operational reason — uneven scaling, different release cadences, separate teams — not just for \"cleanliness\". Modules communicating via domain events instead of calling beans directly is the best preparation for splitting services later: switching from in-process events to Kafka barely touches business code. Spring Modulith by default treats each direct subpackage of the root package as a module, and only what's at the module's top level counts as its public API — this convention is what keeps it lightweight. Don't split modules by technical layer (web, db) — split by business capability. A shared persistence module is a distributed monolith in miniature (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3). Getting the split wrong is still fixable while it's a monolith; getting it wrong after splitting into micro-services is very expensive. That's why modular monolith first, split later is almost always the right advice.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8"},{"id":"12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q9","surface":"track","en":"How do architecture fitness functions and ADRs keep a boundary honest after the initial design?","bodyEn":"A diagram is a hypothesis. An architecture fitness function is an executable check that protects a boundary while the code changes; an ADR records why the boundary exists, what trade-off was accepted, and when to revisit it. Neither proves that the original boundary is still correct. Boundary claim Executable signal Review question Domain does not depend on infrastructure ArchUnit/package rule and compile-time module dependency Can the domain test run without Spring/JPA/Jackson? Module owns its data No foreign repository/table access; migration owner is explicit Which use case needs this data, and who approves the schema change? Integration is asynchronous Versioned event contract, outbox/relay or durable publication, consumer idempotency What happens after commit, before publish, on replay, and during version skew? Service can deploy independently Compatibility test, bounded synchronous dependencies, rollback path Can old and new versions run together without a coordinated deploy? A fitness rule should fail on a new violation, not merely produce a report nobody reads. Keep exceptions small, named, reviewed and time-bounded. An ADR should state context, decision, alternatives, consequences, owner and trigger for reassessment. It is not a substitute for a test or a ticket. Measure the boundary with change-impact evidence: number of modules touched per feature, cross-module calls, deployment coordination, projection lag, contract failures and incident blast radius. A passing package rule can still protect the wrong boundary. Revisit it when language, invariant, ownership, contention, latency or recovery requirements change. Do not turn every class dependency into a forbidden edge. A rule that blocks legitimate adapters creates workarounds and loses trust; enforce the smallest rule that protects a real invariant.","contextEn":"Software architecture: DDD, Clean, Hexagonal & design patterns","href":"/topics/12-architecture-patterns#question-12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q9"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q1","surface":"track","en":"OAuth 2.x: what are the roles, modern flows, and flows to avoid?","bodyEn":"Roles: the resource owner grants access; the client requests it; the authorization server authenticates/authorizes and issues tokens; the resource server enforces the token for its API. Deployments can combine these roles, but the protocol roles remain distinct. OAuth governs delegated authorization—it does not authenticate a user or define a login UI. OIDC is the authentication protocol layered on that OAuth authorization. Choose a flow from the client and user context: Authorization Code + PKCE for browser, native, and other user-facing clients. A server-side BFF can keep tokens away from browser JavaScript; a pure SPA remains a public client. Client Credentials when a confidential workload acts as itself, not as a user. Prefer workload identity, private-key authentication, or federation over a long-lived shared secret. Device Authorization Grant for input-constrained devices when supported. Refresh Token Grant renews an existing authorization; issuance is optional and requires replay protection. Avoid Implicit and Resource Owner Password Credentials. RFC 9700 recommends authorization-code responses and removes password handling from clients. OAuth 2.1 is still an Internet-Draft (draft-16, September 2026), not an RFC. Use RFC 9700, the OAuth 2.0 Security BCP, as normative deployed guidance. For browser public clients, RFC 10017 (published August 2026) is the browser-specific BCP and requires Authorization Code + PKCE. Provider support, metadata, redirect registration, and exact version/configuration remain deployment questions. Scopes and audiences must be least-privilege and resource-specific. Client Credentials must not manufacture end-user identity; when service A calls B on behalf of a user, use an explicit delegation/token-exchange design with downscoped audience rather than blindly forwarding the original token. Passkeys/WebAuthn and MFA are authentication methods at the authorization server/identity provider, not OAuth grants. They strengthen how the user signs in while OAuth/OIDC governs tokens and delegation. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The browser architecture — direct SPA, BFF, server-rendered, native, or hybrid — because it decides token storage and PKCE guidance. The IdP/Keycloak and Spring Security versions, signing algorithms, issuer topology and regions in scope. The maximum revocation window for access and refresh tokens, and how emergency revocation works during an IdP or JWKS outage. Whether service-to-service callers are workload identities, OAuth clients, user-delegated calls, or all three — and which domain actions need resource-level authorization beyond scopes. What falsifies a JWT: key rotation that cannot meet the outage bound, revocation latency beyond the risk window, or a payload that must stay confidential. What falsifies a policy cache: a stale policy permitting a prohibited write, a policy version that cannot be observed, or unproven recovery after a policy-store outage.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q1"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q2","surface":"track","en":"How does Authorization Code + PKCE work, and why is PKCE needed?","bodyEn":"The client creates a high-entropy code_verifier, derives an S256 code_challenge, stores verifier plus transaction state, and redirects the browser to the authorization endpoint. After authentication/consent, the authorization server sends a short-lived, one-time code to an exactly registered redirect URI. The client redeems that code over the token endpoint and proves possession of the verifier. Blue arrows travel through the browser, where a code can leak; green ones are the client's back channel. Only the client holds the verifier, so only it can redeem the code. authorization request (line breaks only for readability) GET /authorize?response_type=code&client_id=web& code_challenge=BASE64URL(SHA256(verifier))&code_challenge_method=S256& state=BOUND_TO_BROWSER_SESSION&redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example%2Fcallback back-channel token request: the stolen code is useless without verifier POST /token grant_type=authorization_code&code=ONE_TIME_CODE&code_verifier=ORIGINAL_VERIFIER PKCE prevents authorization-code interception and injection. RFC 9700 requires PKCE protection for public clients; RFC 10017 (published August 2026) makes Authorization Code + PKCE the browser public-client baseline. Confidential clients should also use it as defense in depth. The current OAuth 2.1 draft also makes PKCE part of the default authorization-code flow, but OAuth 2.1 is not yet an RFC. Check the authorization server's metadata and provider/version compatibility before assuming every deployment exposes the same options. PKCE is one control in a transaction-bound flow: use S256, high verifier entropy, one-time codes, short code lifetime, and exact redirect-URI matching; bind state to the user-agent session for request/response correlation and CSRF defense; in OIDC also validate nonce in the ID token and the authorization-response issuer where applicable; at token validation, check issuer and audience so a code/token from another authorization server or resource cannot be substituted; do not put access tokens in redirect URLs. What draft-16 (September 2026) tightened: the plain challenge method is gone, leaving S256 or a secure extension method, and the authorization server must send iss in the authorization response (RFC 9207). The client still picks its mix-up defence, but iss is now always there to check. For browser apps, prefer a BFF when the deployment can support one: the BFF is the confidential OAuth client, stores tokens server-side, and exposes a Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite session cookie with CSRF protection. A pure SPA uses Authorization Code + PKCE, minimizes token lifetime/storage, enforces a strong CSP, and assumes XSS can act with any token JavaScript can access.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q2"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q3","surface":"track","en":"What's the difference between OAuth2 and OIDC?","bodyEn":"OAuth2 OIDC Answers 'what is the app allowed to do' 'who is the user' Token Access Token + ID Token (JWT, sub, claims) Purpose authorization authentication (+ authorization) OIDC is built on top of OAuth2, adding the ID Token + the /userinfo endpoint. Modern SSO uses OIDC. OAuth2 returns an access token (what the app is allowed to do) — it does NOT standardize saying who the user is OIDC adds the ID Token (JWT: sub, iss, aud, email, auth_time), /userinfo, discovery via /.well-known/openid-configuration, and JWKS Common mistake: using the access token to determine user identity (wrong — the access token is for the resource server); using the ID token to call an API (wrong — the ID token is for the client to know who the user is). OTA: OIDC for B2C login (via Google/social IdP), access tokens for calling internal services with the appropriate scope.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q3"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q1","surface":"track","en":"What is the JWT structure, and how is it signed and verified?","bodyEn":"A commonly used signed JWT is the compact JWS form header.payload.signature, with each part base64url-encoded. The payload is not encrypted unless a separate JWE design is used. Header: a pinned alg, a rotation key ID kid, and an explicit typ such as the access-token profile expected by the API. Claims: iss, sub, aud, exp, iat, optionally nbf/jti, and authorization data such as scopes. Signature: integrity/authenticity over the encoded header and payload using a trusted key. // Validation order; use a maintained JOSE/OAuth library, not home-grown crypto requireExpectedTypAndAllowedAlgorithm (header); Key key = trustedJwks. resolve (header.kid()); Claims c = jws. verifyThenDecode (key); requireIssuer (c, EXPECTED_ISSUER); requireAudience (c, THIS_API); requireTimeWindow (c.nbf(), c.exp(), ALLOWED_CLOCK_SKEW); requireScopesAndSubjectPolicy (c); Parsing is not validation Do not authorize from unverified claims. Reject alg: none, algorithm/key-type confusion, unexpected typ, untrusted jku/x5u, missing issuer/audience/time claims, and tokens intended for another API or token class. An ID token is not an API access token. JWKS handling is production logic: fetch only from issuer configuration you trust; cache keys; rate-limit refresh on an unknown kid; retain previous keys through the maximum token lifetime; and overlap old/new signing keys during rotation. A naïve “refetch on every unknown kid” path is a denial-of-service vector, while a cache that never refreshes breaks rotation. typ, issuer, audience, key set, and validation rules together prevent cross-JWT and token-substitution attacks. Signature validity says who signed bytes; authorization still has to check tenant, subject, scope/role, resource ownership, and current policy. Keep claims minimal, never put secrets in a readable payload, never log the token, and choose access-token lifetime from replay risk and revocation capability rather than a universal number of minutes. Validation checklist: Check Required decision Issuer and key source trust one configured issuer; derive JWKS only from its trusted metadata Algorithm and signature allow-list the algorithm/key family, resolve a trusted kid , then verify the signature Token profile require the expected typ and token class; an ID token is not an API access token Issuer and audience match iss exactly and require this API's expected aud or resource Time window require exp , apply nbf when present, and bound clock skew explicitly Permission require the scope/role for this operation; a valid signature is not permission Resource ownership load tenant, owner, object state, and action policy from trusted server state Cryptographic checks can be delegated to a maintained library, but resource ownership and business authorization remain the service's responsibility. Bound JWKS recovery with a single-flight refresh per issuer. An unknown kid may trigger one bounded metadata refresh; concurrent requests share it and repeated misses use backoff/negative caching. Reject the token until the new key is fetched and verified. Never fetch an attacker-selected URL.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q1"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q2","surface":"track","en":"JWT (stateless) vs opaque token / session — what are the trade-offs?","bodyEn":"Choice Good fit Main cost or failure Short-lived JWT local validation with a bounded revocation window stale claims, key/audience errors, readable payload Opaque + introspection central revocation or active-state checks IdP latency/availability, cache staleness, load BFF + server session sensitive browser session with a backend session store, CSRF, cookie policy, BFF availability DPoP-bound token (RFC 9449) public/browser/native client needs sender-constraining and provider support key/nonce/replay handling; XSS can still act while running mTLS-bound token (RFC 8705) workload/partner owns PKI and certificate-bound tokens CA/cert rotation, proxy termination, device support Bottom line JWT trades revocability for scale. Fintech typically uses: short-lived JWT access token + revocable refresh token + blacklist/rotation. need immediate revocation (locking a fraudulent account) → short-lived access token + introspection for sensitive operations, or an opaque token common hybrid pattern: JWT access token for 5–15 minutes (validated locally) + server-side refresh token rotation (revocable, reuse detection → revokes the whole family) + jti denylist for emergencies 'JWT trades revocability for statelessness/scale; we compensate with a short TTL + revocation at the refresh layer' — avoid saying 'JWT is always better than sessions'.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q2"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q3","surface":"track","en":"Access token vs refresh token, and what's the revocation strategy?","bodyEn":"An access token is presented to a resource server and should be audience-restricted, least-privilege, and short enough for the replay/revocation risk. A refresh token is a higher-value credential used only at the authorization server. Issuance and lifetime are policy decisions; inactivity, consent, credential, device, or risk changes can terminate the grant early. For public clients, RFC 9700 requires refresh tokens to be sender-constrained (for example DPoP) or rotated with reuse detection. Rotation issues a new token on every refresh, invalidates the predecessor, and revokes the active family when reuse reveals likely theft. Confidential clients must authenticate and the server must bind the token to that client. Storage follows the platform threat model: a BFF keeps OAuth tokens server-side and uses a Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite cookie plus CSRF defense; native apps use OS-protected storage; browser-only apps minimize token lifetime and commonly keep access tokens in memory. HttpOnly prevents JavaScript reads, not requests made by an XSS payload, so CSP and output safety still matter. The two credentials side by side, because almost every mistake here is treating them alike: Access token Refresh token Presented to every resource server the authorization server only, never an API Typical lifetime 5–15 minutes hours to months, bounded by inactivity as well as absolute age Blast radius if stolen bounded by the expiry you chose a standing ability to mint access tokens Validation local signature check, or introspection when central revocation matters more than latency always a server-side lookup — it has state Revocation usually just expiry; that is the trade a short lifetime pays for immediate, and it takes the whole family with it Storage memory in a browser app, OS keystore on native never in a browser app without a BFF holding it server-side Rotation with reuse detection, in the order the checks must happen: // 1. the refresh arrives; look it up by its family, not just by its value Family family = store. byToken (presented); if (family == null ) throw new InvalidGrant (); // // 2. THE detection: this token was already spent. Only two things explain that — // a stolen copy being replayed, or a client that retried. Both are handled the // same way, because you cannot tell them apart and only one is safe to assume. if (presented. isSuperseded ()) { store. revokeFamily (family. id ()); // kill every descendant, not just this one audit. refreshReuse (family. subject (), family. id ()); throw new InvalidGrant (); } // // 3. only now issue the pair, and mark the predecessor spent in the same write return store. rotate (family, presented); The retry that looks exactly like theft A client that loses the response to a refresh will retry with the old token and trip reuse detection — the user is logged out for a network blip. Absorb it at the client (one in-flight refresh per app, shared by every waiting request), not by weakening the check on the server. Revocation is layered: revoke the authorization/refresh-token family on logout, consent withdrawal, account compromise, credential reset, or reuse detection; use opaque access tokens with introspection when immediate central revocation outweighs the availability/latency cost, or a short-lived signed token when local validation is the better trade-off; use a jti denylist only for bounded emergency cases with an explicit retention/availability design; require reauthentication or step-up with WebAuthn/MFA for high-risk operations, even when a token is valid; consider DPoP or mTLS sender-constrained access tokens where bearer-token replay is a material risk. Never log tokens or URLs/headers containing them. Redact at ingestion, restrict security-log access, and alert on refresh reuse without recording the credential itself (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4).","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q3"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q4","surface":"track","en":"Where should a browser store JWTs: cookies, sessionStorage, or localStorage?","bodyEn":"The default answer is none of those places for the JWT itself. JWT is only a token format. Storage depends on whether it is an access, refresh or ID token and whether browser JavaScript must ever possess it. For a first-party web application with a backend, prefer a Backend for Frontend (BFF). The BFF keeps OAuth access and refresh tokens server-side. The browser receives an opaque, random session identifier in a hardened cookie—not the JWT. The BFF is the OAuth client. It adds state and availability work in exchange for keeping bearer tokens out of the page. Place XSS consequence CSRF consequence Lifetime Verdict BFF + HttpOnly cookie script cannot read the cookie but can make authenticated same-origin calls browser sends cookie automatically needs CSRF defense cookie + server session policy preferred when a backend is available Memory running malicious script can request or use a token persistent theft is harder Authorization header is not automatic lost on reload/tab close best direct-SPA baseline sessionStorage same-origin script can read and exfiltrate it not sent automatically one tab; survives reload shorter exposure, not XSS-safe localStorage same-origin script can read every persisted token not sent automatically persists across restarts and tabs avoid for credentials JS-readable cookie readable like Web Storage also sent automatically to matching requests cookie expiry worst properties combined; do not use A hardened session cookie looks like this: Set-Cookie: __Host-session=RANDOM_OPAQUE_ID; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax Cache-Control: no-store HttpOnly prevents JavaScript from reading the cookie; it does not stop XSS from operating the application through the victim's browser. Secure restricts transport to HTTPS. The __Host- prefix also requires Path=/ and no Domain , preventing broad subdomain scope. Choose SameSite=Strict or Lax from the navigation flow. SameSite is defense in depth, not a universal replacement for a CSRF token, Origin/Fetch-Metadata checks and correct non-mutating GET semantics. Rotate the session identifier on login and privilege change; enforce idle and absolute expiry server-side. Logout deletes server session state, clears the cookie and revokes the refresh-token family where supported. What if the product is a pure SPA that calls an API directly? Use Authorization Code + PKCE. Keep a short-lived, narrowly scoped access token in memory or an isolated service worker. Send it in Authorization: Bearer . Accept that reload loses it and re-establish authorization through the IdP session. A service worker can reduce direct token exfiltration only if it never returns tokens to the page or persists them in storage shared with the window. An XSS payload can still ask that trusted component to make allowed calls while it is running; isolation is not an XSS cure. If the authorization server issues refresh tokens to a public browser client, use rotation or sender-constrained tokens when supported, detect reuse, cap absolute lifetime and avoid persistent JavaScript-readable storage. A stolen bearer token remains usable by whoever holds it until expiry or revocation policy intervenes (13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q3). Token type matters: Access token: API credential. Keep it short-lived and out of URLs, logs, analytics and crash reports. Refresh token: long-lived authority to mint access tokens. Keep it server-side whenever possible; it deserves stronger protection than the access token. ID token: a client authentication result, not an API credential. Validate it during the OIDC flow, derive the local session and do not persist it merely to display profile fields. Encryption in localStorage does not close the boundary If JavaScript can decrypt the token, injected JavaScript can call the same decrypt path or use the token through your code. Moving the encryption key into the same origin only rearranges the secret. Decision rule Have a backend? Use BFF plus an opaque HttpOnly cookie. Pure SPA? Prefer memory/service-worker isolation and short-lived access tokens. sessionStorage is a bounded compromise; localStorage is for non-sensitive UI state, not bearer credentials. Cookie size and transport matter: a JWT in a cookie may approach browser limits and is attached to every matching request. An opaque session id is smaller and gives logout/revocation one server-side control point. Cookie authentication changes the API threat model from header-bearer handling to ambient authority. CORS does not replace CSRF protection; CORS governs response access, while a forged request can still have effects. CSP, Trusted Types, dependency control and output encoding reduce XSS likelihood. None converts JavaScript-readable storage into a protected credential vault. Do not copy this browser decision to native mobile. Native apps use the operating system's protected credential storage and an external user-agent flow (RFC 8252). The normative browser-specific source for this decision is RFC 10017, OAuth 2.0 for Browser-Based Applications, published in August 2026. RFC 9700 remains the general OAuth 2.0 Security BCP. Re-check provider support and the target client architecture before applying either document as a deployment recipe.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q4"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q1","surface":"track","en":"How would you design the authentication/authorization system for microservices (SSO + gateway)?","bodyEn":"Design three identities separately: the human/session, the OAuth client, and the calling workload. A central authorization server/IdP handles OIDC login, WebAuthn/passkeys/MFA, consent, token issuance, keys, and session risk. A gateway or BFF validates the external protocol at the edge, strips any client-supplied identity headers, and creates trusted internal context. A BFF keeps browser tokens server-side. Each resource-owning service enforces scopes plus domain authorization such as tenant, object ownership, amount, and state transition. Edge validation alone cannot decide those invariants. Workloads authenticate with short-lived federated identity, mTLS/SPIFFE, or an OAuth confidential-client method; avoid one shared static credential across services. Choose one internal token contract Either services validate an externally issued token with the exact issuer/audience profile, or a trusted edge exchanges it for a short-lived, downscoped internal token. Do not forward arbitrary identity headers or every original bearer token through all hops. Preserve the actor/delegation chain explicitly when a service acts for a user. Validation and authorization belong at different boundaries. The gateway can reject malformed/expired tokens, enforce coarse scopes, rate-limit, and translate protocols. The ledger still verifies that this subject may move money from this account and requires step-up for a high-risk action. For local JWT validation, distribute trust through OIDC discovery/JWKS with bounded caches and rotation overlap. For opaque tokens, design introspection availability, caching, and revocation semantics. Internal calls need workload identity even when user context is present; one credential answers “which service?”, the delegated token answers “for which actor and authority?” Propagate an absolute deadline, trace context, and only the minimum identity attributes. Log authorization decisions and policy version without logging credentials or sensitive claims.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q1"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q2","surface":"track","en":"What is mTLS, and how is it used for service-to-service authentication?","bodyEn":"mTLS authenticates both TLS peers with certificates while encrypting the channel. It proves possession of a private key bound to an accepted certificate/trust domain; it does not by itself decide whether that workload may perform a business action. Common uses: workload-to-workload identity with certificates/SVIDs rotated by a mesh, workload-identity platform, or PKI automation; OAuth client authentication and certificate-bound access tokens under RFC 8705, reducing replay from a different TLS client; B2B partner APIs where both organizations can operate certificate issuance, renewal, revocation, and incident procedures. DPoP (RFC 9449) is an application-layer proof-of-possession option that can sender-constrain tokens without requiring the same TLS-client-certificate topology. Neither mechanism protects a fully compromised client that loses both token and key. A service mesh can automate issuance, rotation, and transport policy, but it is not “security with no code or operations.” Define trust domains, namespace/service authorization, certificate lifetime, clock-skew behavior, CA/root rotation, revocation/compromise response, telemetry, and fail-open/fail-closed behavior. Test that plaintext bypass paths are closed. Use mTLS where the threat model and platform justify it; “every request must use a mesh” is not a universal architecture rule. It adds proxies or library integration, control-plane dependencies, latency/resource cost, and another policy layer. Cloud workload federation or signed workload tokens may fit some boundaries better. Keep service identity and end-user delegation separate. An mTLS peer may be booking-service, while a scoped token says it is acting for user U on booking B. Authorization should evaluate both when both matter.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q2"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q3","surface":"track","en":"How should OWASP Top 10:2025 and API Security Top 10:2023 shape backend controls?","bodyEn":"Treat both lists as awareness and threat-modelling inputs, not a compliance checklist. OWASP Top 10:2025 emphasizes broken access control, security misconfiguration, software supply-chain failures, cryptographic/injection/insecure-design/authentication failures, integrity, logging/alerting, and mishandled exceptional conditions. API Security Top 10:2023 adds API-specific focus such as object/function/property authorization, resource consumption, sensitive business flows, SSRF, inventory, and unsafe consumption of upstream APIs. Risk Engineering controls injection / XSS / CSRF parameter binding; context-aware output encoding and CSP; SameSite plus CSRF tokens for cookie-authenticated writes BOLA/IDOR and function/property authorization server-side tenant, object, field, and action checks on every path; deny by default; test negative cases resource and sensitive-flow abuse payload/page limits, deadlines, quotas, bot/fraud controls, idempotency, and cost-aware admission SSRF / unsafe upstream APIs destination allowlists, safe URL parsing, egress controls, response validation, timeouts, and no blind redirect/credential forwarding supply chain and integrity locked dependencies, SCA, SBOM, provenance, signed artifacts, isolated builds, and signature/policy verification at deploy Mass assignment is prevented by explicit input DTOs/allowlists, not by hiding fields in the UI. Error paths need the same authorization and data-minimization rules as success paths: fail closed where correctness/security requires it, return stable problem details, and avoid stack traces or secrets in responses. Build a security test matrix from assets and abuse cases: cross-tenant IDs, role downgrade, hidden fields, large/deep payloads, race/replay, webhook SSRF, partner schema surprises, dependency compromise, key rotation, and unavailable policy/identity services. Run SAST/SCA/secret scanning early, DAST and authorization tests against a deployed system, then verify audit/alert signals through incident exercises. WAF and rate limiting are defense in depth, not substitutes for resource-level authorization, safe code, or business-flow anti-automation. Negative test corpus: JWT: algorithm confusion/alg: none, wrong issuer or audience, invalid signature, unexpected typ , expired/early nbf , and unknown kid during rotation; flow and session: replayed authorization code, state , OIDC nonce , or PKCE verifier; refresh-token reuse; and replayed DPoP proof; boundary: mass assignment of role / tenantId , SSRF redirect/DNS-rebinding/metadata access, unsafe polymorphic deserialization/XXE/deep payload, and cross-tenant object or function access. Each test should assert deny, no protected side effect, no secret in response/log, and a privacy-safe audit reason.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q3"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you manage secrets properly?","bodyEn":"Prefer no long-lived secret when workload identity or short-lived federation can provide the credential. When a secret is necessary, keep it out of source, build logs, images, CI variables with broad scope, and client-visible configuration. store and issue it through a managed secret/KMS/HSM system with least-privilege identity and audited access; inject at runtime using the platform channel whose leakage risks you understand (agent/file/volume/API; environment variables can appear in dumps or diagnostics); separate environments and tenants, limit blast radius, and use short-lived dynamic credentials where supported; rotate on compromise, personnel/ownership change, cryptoperiod, or policy trigger; automate renewal and test overlap/rollback rather than rotating blindly and causing an outage; scan current code, Git history, artifacts, logs, tickets, and images; a discovered credential is revoked/reissued, not merely deleted from the latest commit. A Kubernetes Secret is base64-encoded data, not encryption by itself. Configure etcd encryption at rest, tight RBAC, namespace/service-account isolation, audit, and preferably an external secret/KMS integration for high-value material. JWT signing-key rotation requires publishing old and new public keys under distinct kid values, switching the signer, retaining the old verification key through maximum token lifetime plus skew, then retiring it. Root/CA, database, webhook, PSP/GDS, and encryption-key rotations need similarly explicit dependency order and rollback. Use envelope encryption for stored data: a data-encryption key encrypts data and a KMS/HSM-managed key encrypts the DEK. Maintain an inventory of secret owner, consumers, scope, creation/expiry, rotation method, and emergency revocation. Never log the material; log the secret identifier/version and access decision.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q4"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you protect auth endpoints from brute-force/credential stuffing?","bodyEn":"rate limit by IP + by account exponential backoff/temporary lockout; CAPTCHA after N failed attempts detect anomalies (login from an unusual location) store passwords with a slow, salted hash function (bcrypt/argon2/scrypt), never plain MD5/SHA MFA for sensitive operations don't reveal 'whether the user exists' through error messages rate limit + lockout by IP and by account (defends against credential stuffing across many IPs against one account, & brute-force against one account from one IP); exponential backoff; CAPTCHA past a threshold; anomaly detection (unfamiliar location/device → step-up MFA) argon2id (recommended) / bcrypt / scrypt — NEVER plain MD5/SHA (fast, easy to brute-force on GPUs); don't leak 'user exists' via errors/timing (timing-safe) Fintech/OTA: require phishing-resistant or step-up MFA where transaction risk, regulation and recovery policy demand it; prefer passkeys/WebAuthn where supported, and treat device/location signals as risk inputs rather than identity proof. Pitfall: lockout too aggressive → DoS against the real user (an attacker locks out the victim's account) → use CAPTCHA instead of a hard lockout, or lock by IP+account combined.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q5"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q6","surface":"track","en":"RBAC vs ABAC — which authorization model should you choose?","bodyEn":"RBAC ABAC Basis roles (admin, teller) attributes (user, resource, context, time) Pros simple, easy to manage/audit flexible for complex rules Cons role explosion as rules get complex harder to debug/audit Many systems combine both: RBAC as the base + ABAC for fine-grained rules. ABAC example: 'an agent may only approve bookings below their branch's limit during working hours' engines: OPA/Rego, Cedar for policy-as-code decoupled from business logic OTA: RBAC for customer/agent/admin; ABAC for 'agent from airline X only sees airline X's bookings', 'refunds above a threshold need higher-level approval'. Check permissions server-side (gateway + service), never trust the client; log every sensitive authorization decision (audit).","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q6"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q7","surface":"track","en":"API key vs OAuth token — when do you use which?","bodyEn":"API key OAuth token Identifies an application/partner a user/scope Granularity low high (scope) Lifetime static, long-lived short-lived, revocable Good for partner server-to-server acting on behalf of a user API key: a secret string identifying an application/partner (not a user), used alongside an IP allowlist + rate limit/quota. Risk: static, long-lived, once leaked it stays usable indefinitely → needs rotation + a restricted scope OAuth token: authorization scoped to a user/scope, short-lived, revocable OTA/fintech: B2C → OAuth/OIDC; internal services → mTLS + client-credentials; B2B partners → API key + mTLS + quota, or client-credentials with scope. Managing API keys: hash them at rest (like a password), use a prefix for identification, rotate without downtime (two keys active simultaneously during rotation), audit. Never embed an API key in a client/mobile app (too easy to extract).","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q7"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q1","surface":"track","en":"What is Keycloak, and what are realms, clients and its core endpoints?","bodyEn":"Keycloak is an open-source identity provider (CNCF incubating since 2023) implementing OAuth 2.x, OIDC and SAML 2.0. It plays the authorization server role from (13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q1) so you do not build login, MFA, federation and token issuance yourself. The object model, from the outside in: Concept What it is Gotcha realm An isolated tenant: its own users, roles, clients and signing keys Cross-realm token trust does not exist. master is for administering Keycloak — never put application users there client One application asking for tokens public (SPA/mobile, no secret, PKCE required) vs confidential (backend, has a secret) client scope Reusable bundle of claims/roles added to a token Default scopes apply silently; optional ones only when requested realm role / client role Realm-wide vs per-client authority They land in different token claims — see (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q3) identity provider Upstream federation (Google, Azure AD, LDAP) Keycloak still mints its own token; the upstream one is not what your API sees Every endpoint is advertised by discovery — never hardcode paths: // one document tells you every other URL GET /realms/{realm}/.well-known/openid-configuration // authorization_endpoint /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/auth token_endpoint /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/token jwks_uri /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/certs end_session_endpoint /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/logout introspection_endpoint /realms/{r}/protocol/openid-connect/token/introspect Interview framing Say what Keycloak replaces: it is the authorization server, so your services keep only token validation — the resource-server half. That split is the whole reason to adopt it. issuer must match exactly, character for character, including scheme, port and any trailing path. A Keycloak behind a reverse proxy that rewrites the host mints tokens whose iss your service then rejects — fix it with KC_HOSTNAME plus --proxy-headers=xforwarded, not by loosening validation. Keycloak is stateful: it needs a real database (not the dev-mode H2) and, for a cluster, an Infinispan cache for sessions. Run start and not start-dev in production — the latter disables HTTPS requirements and uses an in-memory store. The legacy keycloak-spring-boot-starter and its adapters are end-of-life. New services must use plain Spring Security OAuth2 resource server (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2); there is nothing Keycloak-specific left on the resource-server side. Realm config belongs in version control. Export it and import declaratively (kc.sh import, or the Keycloak Operator's KeycloakRealmImport) so the IdP is reproducible rather than a hand-clicked snowflake. Provider/version boundary. The endpoint paths, realm behavior, and KC_ settings above are Keycloak examples, not OAuth/OIDC requirements. Discovery is the portable contract; verify the exact Keycloak distribution/version and reverse-proxy mode before copying an endpoint or configuration.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q1"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2","surface":"track","en":"How does a Spring Boot service validate a Keycloak token, and what must you verify?","bodyEn":"Your service is a resource server: it never talks to Keycloak per request. It fetches the JWKS once, caches the public keys, and verifies signatures locally — the mechanism from (13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q1). The whole configuration is one property: issuer-uri alone is enough — Spring reads discovery and finds the JWKS spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri: \\ https://kc.example.com/realms/fintech it validates: signature, exp, nbf, and iss — but NOT audience aud is not checked by default, and that is the trap. Any token from the same realm — including one minted for a completely different client — verifies fine. Add the check explicitly: @Bean JwtDecoder jwtDecoder (OAuth2ResourceServerProperties p) { String issuer = p.getJwt().getIssuerUri(); NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = JwtDecoders. fromIssuerLocation (issuer); decoder. setJwtValidator ( new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>( JwtValidators. createDefaultWithIssuer (issuer), // sig, exp, iss new JwtClaimValidator<List<String>>( // the missing one \"aud\" , aud -> aud != null && aud. contains ( \"orders-api\" )))); return decoder; } Getting aud into the token at all takes a Keycloak-side audience mapper on the client — by default Keycloak puts the client only in azp, so aud may be absent entirely. Never trust these Do not authorize on preferred_username or email — both are mutable and email need not be verified. Bind rows to the immutable sub, exactly as the local app does with the Google token (see the security model in CLAUDE.md). JWKS caching and key rotation. Spring refreshes the key set when it sees an unknown kid, with a rate limit (default 5 minutes) so a bad token cannot turn into a JWKS stampede. Rotate keys in Keycloak before retiring the old one — it keeps serving both so in-flight tokens stay valid. Local verification means revocation is not immediate — the same trade-off as (13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q2). Usual answer: short access-token lifetimes (1–5 min) plus refresh-token rotation. introspect gives instant revocation, at the cost of a network hop and a hard Keycloak dependency on every request. realm_access is not a Spring authority. Spring reads scope/scp by default, so hasRole('ADMIN') fails silently until you supply a converter (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q3). Keycloak must be reachable at startup only if you build the decoder eagerly; JwtDecoders.fromIssuerLocation fetches discovery immediately, which makes a cold Keycloak a boot failure. Use NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(...) for lazy fetching when boot ordering matters. The Spring snippets describe one Spring Security configuration, not a timeless default. Defaults and extension APIs are version-sensitive. Make issuer discovery/JWKS and the audience validator explicit, pin the target Spring Security line, and test the exact dependency instead of assuming issuer-uri validates every claim.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q3","surface":"track","en":"Where do Keycloak roles land in the token, and how do you map them to Spring authorities?","bodyEn":"Keycloak writes roles into nested claims that Spring Security does not read, so a fresh integration authenticates correctly and then fails every authorization check. What is actually in the token: { \"sub\" : \"8f14e45f-…\" , // the only stable user id \"azp\" : \"orders-web\" , // which client asked \"realm_access\" : { \"roles\" : [ \"admin\" , \"teller\" ] }, \"resource_access\" : { // per-client roles \"orders-api\" : { \"roles\" : [ \"orders:write\" ] } }, \"scope\" : \"openid profile email\" // <-- Spring reads ONLY this } The converter that bridges the gap: static Collection<GrantedAuthority> authorities (Jwt jwt) { Map<String,Object> realm = jwt. getClaim ( \"realm_access\" ); if (realm == null ) return List. of (); var roles = (Collection<String>) realm. getOrDefault ( \"roles\" , List. of ()); return roles. stream () // hasRole('ADMIN') looks for the ROLE_ prefix; omit it and // only hasAuthority('admin') works — a classic silent 403 .<GrantedAuthority> map (r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority( \"ROLE_\" + r. toUpperCase ())) . toList (); } Decision Use Authority spanning every service realm_access — a realm role Permission meaningful to one API only resource_access — a client role, so orders:write cannot leak authority into the payments service What the client app may ask for, not what the user is scope — delegation, not identity Composite roles expand at token-mint time, so manager containing teller arrives as both. Convenient, and the reason tokens quietly grow — a user in many groups can push the JWT past an 8 KB proxy header limit. Keep coarse roles in the token and resolve fine-grained permissions service-side. Group membership is not in the token by default. Add a group-membership mapper if you need it, but prefer mapping groups to roles: groups are an organisational structure, roles are the authorization contract. scope vs role is a real interview distinction: scope is what the client may request, role is what the user is. scope=openid with realm_access.roles=[admin] = an admin using an app granted only identity access. Both limits apply, and the effective permission is the intersection. Prefer @PreAuthorize on the method over URL-pattern matching for anything non-trivial; the model is RBAC/ABAC from (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q6), and Keycloak only supplies the attributes. Realm roles, client roles, claim names, role mappers, and Spring authority conversion here are Keycloak/provider configuration examples, not portable OAuth/OIDC claims. Pin the provider/version and test token fixtures after every mapper or authority-prefix change.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q3"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do services call each other with Keycloak, and what is token exchange for?","bodyEn":"Two different problems, two different grants — conflating them is a common design error. Service acting as itself → client_credentials. There is no user; the token's sub is the service account: POST /realms/fintech/protocol/openid-connect/token Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded // grant_type=client_credentials &client_id=batch-poster&client_secret=… Service acting on behalf of a user → token exchange (RFC 8693). The gateway holds the user's token; the downstream service needs one scoped to itself but still carrying the user's identity: grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange &subject_token=<the caller's access token> &audience=ledger-api // mint for this service // result: a NEW token, aud=ledger-api, sub still the original user Approach When it is right Cost Forward the same token Trusted internal mesh, one audience Any hop can replay it anywhere — audience is meaningless Token exchange Crossing a trust boundary while keeping user identity A Keycloak round-trip per hop (cache it for the token's lifetime) client_credentials Batch jobs, cron, no user involved Loses user identity — audit logs show only the service Do not forward a user token indefinitely A token minted for orders-api and forwarded through four services can be replayed by any of them against any service in the realm. Set a real audience and validate it (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2) — that is what makes the boundary mean something. Token exchange is opt-in per client, gated by fine-grained admin permissions: the exchanging client needs explicit authority over the target audience. It has moved between \"preview\" and supported across versions — check the feature flags for the version you actually run rather than assuming. For pure machine-to-machine inside a cluster, mTLS (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q2) may replace tokens entirely: the identity is the certificate and there is nothing to steal or replay. Use tokens when you need user identity to travel; use mTLS when you need service identity. Bind the token to the client that holds it where the stakes justify it: mTLS-bound tokens (RFC 8705) or DPoP make a stolen bearer token useless elsewhere. Cache service tokens and refresh before exp, honouring expires_in. A service that requests a fresh token per outbound call turns Keycloak into the bottleneck for the entire system. RFC 8693 defines a token-exchange framework, but this endpoint, grant enablement, permission model, and resulting claims are Keycloak/provider-specific. The example is not a portable guarantee: check the exact Keycloak version, feature flag, client policy, and target audience before depending on it.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q4"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q5","surface":"track","en":"What breaks when you run Keycloak in production?","bodyEn":"Keycloak is not a stateless sidecar — it is a database-backed, clustered, single point of failure for every login. The failure modes are operational, not conceptual. Failure Cause Fix invalid_token , issuer mismatch Proxy rewrites the host; iss no longer matches what services expect KC_HOSTNAME + --proxy-headers=xforwarded ; never \"fix\" it by skipping validation Random logouts in a cluster Sessions live in Infinispan; a node dies with the only copy Distributed cache with owners > 1, sticky sessions at the LB Slow logins under load Every login writes to the DB; connection pool exhausted Size the pool, watch DB latency — Keycloak is as fast as its database Token verification stalls everywhere Keycloak down and services fetch JWKS per request Cache the JWKS (default in Spring); verification must survive a Keycloak outage Upgrade breaks the realm DB schema migrates in place, and it is one-way Back up the DB, test the upgrade on a copy, pin the image tag The thing to say out loud: if Keycloak is down, existing tokens keep working (local verification) but nobody can log in or refresh. That asymmetry decides your token lifetimes — long enough to ride out a restart, short enough that revocation means something. Capacity reality Login is expensive (password hashing is deliberately slow — Argon2 by default in recent versions), token verification is free because it happens in your service. Size Keycloak for the login and refresh rate, not for request throughput. Run it HA, and treat the DB as the tier that matters: at least two replicas, a real Postgres/MySQL with backups, PodDisruptionBudget, and readiness on /health/ready (management port 9000 in recent versions). The Keycloak Operator handles most of this on Kubernetes (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q6). Blast radius. One realm for every application means one bad realm export takes down every login. Separate tenants into separate realms; keep master for administration only. Observability — alert on login failure rate (credential stuffing, see (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q5)), token endpoint p99, DB pool saturation, and Infinispan cluster size. Keycloak exposes Micrometer metrics; feed them into the same stack as (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q1). Do not fork Keycloak to add a feature. Use its extension points (SPIs, custom authenticators, event listeners) — a forked IdP is unupgradable, and upgrades are how you get security fixes. Health paths, management ports, Argon2 defaults, Infinispan behavior, and Operator workflow are distribution/version/configuration examples. Pin the target Keycloak release and deployment mode, then verify each operational default in its current guide.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q5"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q6","surface":"track","en":"Keycloak vs a managed IdP vs rolling your own — how do you argue the choice?","bodyEn":"Interviewers ask this to hear whether you can weigh operational cost against control instead of naming a favourite. Keycloak (self-hosted) Managed (Auth0/Cognito/Entra) Build it yourself Cost shape Infra + your ops time; flat as users grow Per-MAU — cheap early, painful at scale Cheapest to start, most expensive to keep Data residency You hold the user data — often the deciding factor in fintech Third party holds it; check the jurisdiction You hold it, including the breach You now operate A clustered stateful service + DB (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q5) Nothing Password hashing, MFA, token revocation, federation, and every CVE Customisation Deep — SPIs, custom flows, own login theme Bounded by the vendor Unlimited, and entirely your problem Lock-in Low: standard OIDC, exportable realm Real: proprietary rules and user store None The defensible position: never build your own for authentication — it is the least differentiating, highest-blast-radius code you could write. Choose managed when the team is small and data residency permits. Choose Keycloak when residency, per-user cost, or protocol control decide it. Both keep you on standard OIDC, so the resource-server code in (13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q2) is unchanged either way — which is exactly why the decision is reversible. The migration path is the point worth making: because both speak OIDC, switching IdP changes an issuer-uri and a role-claim converter, not your services. What does not migrate cleanly is password hashes (formats differ — usually a rehash-on-next-login shim) and the vendor-specific rules engine. Keycloak is Apache 2.0 with no open-core split, so there is no feature paywall; the cost is the operations. That matters when comparing against products whose SSO or audit-log tier is priced separately. If you take Keycloak for its features but not its operations, hosted Keycloak (Red Hat build of Keycloak, or a managed provider) is the middle path and a good answer to \"what if we lack an ops team?\". Whatever you choose, the security model stays yours: aud validation, immutable sub as the user key, short token lifetimes, and authorization decided in your service. An IdP outsources authentication, never authorization. The managed-provider names and cost/features in this table are examples, not a universal price or capability contract. Compare the chosen tenant, plan, region, version, residency terms, outage guarantees, and export/migration path before making the build-versus-buy decision.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.keycloak-in-practice.q6"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q1","surface":"track","en":"Injection is more than SQL. Where does untrusted input still reach an interpreter in a modern Spring service?","bodyEn":"Parameter binding solved the textbook case and moved the problem elsewhere. Injection survives everywhere a string is assembled rather than bound. Surface The unsafe shape The fix JPQL / native SQL string concatenation into the query bind parameters; never concatenate values Dynamic ORDER BY / table name identifiers cannot be bound, so people concatenate allowlist of column names, map input to constants MongoDB filters a raw map from the request body becomes the query bind typed fields; reject $ operators from clients Shell / process Runtime.exec(\"sh -c \" + arg) pass an argument array, no shell Logs user text written straight into the log line structured fields, encode newlines Templates / expressions user string evaluated as SpEL or a template never evaluate user input as an expression // unsafe: sort is a request parameter concatenated into JPQL var q = em.createQuery( \"select o from Order o order by o.\" + sort); // // safe: identifiers come from a closed set the code owns private static final Map<String, String> SORTS = Map.of( \"created\" , \"createdAt\" , \"total\" , \"totalAmount\" ); var column = SORTS.getOrDefault(sort, \"createdAt\" ); // unknown input degrades, never injects The rule that generalises Data is bound, structure is chosen from a set you control. If input decides structure — a column, a table, an operator, a class name — it must be mapped through an allowlist. log injection is underrated: a newline in a username lets an attacker forge log lines and poison the incident timeline you will trust later (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4). second-order injection stores the payload safely and interpolates it later in a report or admin query. The dangerous moment is the read, not the write. ORMs are not immunity: @Query with concatenation, native queries, Specification built from raw strings and JPQL LIKE patterns are all reachable. validation is not the control here, it is defence in depth. Reject obviously wrong input early (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q8), then still bind properly. for MongoDB, an untyped Map from the body can smuggle {\"$ne\": null} into a filter and turn an equality check into \"match anything\" (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q4).","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q1"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q2","surface":"track","en":"A user sets their own role by adding one field to the request body. How does mass assignment happen and how is it closed?","bodyEn":"Mass assignment is what happens when the request body is bound straight onto a persistent object. The client controls every field the class exposes, including the ones no form ever shows. // the entity has fields the client must never set @PutMapping ( \"/users/{id}\" ) User update ( @PathVariable Long id, @RequestBody User body) { // entity as the API type body.setId(id); return users.save(body); // role, status, credit, verified — all writable } // // the DTO is the contract: it can only carry what the caller is allowed to change record UpdateProfile ( @NotBlank String displayName, @Email String email) { } // @PutMapping ( \"/users/{id}\" ) UserView update ( @PathVariable Long id, @RequestBody @Valid UpdateProfile in) { var user = users.findOwned(id, currentUser()); // authorisation, not just authentication user.rename(in.displayName()); return UserView.of(user); } Three defences, in order of strength: a dedicated DTO per operation — the type system makes the extra field impossible, not merely rejected explicit mapping into the domain object, so adding an entity field never silently widens the API reject unknown properties (FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES) so a renamed or dropped field fails loudly instead of being ignored Response side has the same bug Serialising the entity back leaks passwordHash, internal flags and the whole object graph. A view model is not ceremony; it is the outbound half of the same control. this is broken object property level authorisation in the API Security Top 10 — the same family as IDOR, one level down: not \"which object\" but \"which field of it\" (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q3). the field does not have to exist today. Adding isAdmin to an entity six months later silently turns a safe endpoint into a privilege escalation. separating the domain model from the JPA entity removes the temptation entirely, and pays for itself in other ways too (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2). @JsonIgnore and setDisallowedFields work but are denylists: they protect the fields somebody remembered. The DTO is an allowlist by construction. test it as a security case: post a body containing role and assert the stored role is unchanged. That test outlives every reviewer's memory (26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q1).","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q2"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q3","surface":"track","en":"Which parsing and deserialization features turn \"just reading input\" into remote code execution or a denial of service?","bodyEn":"Every parser is an interpreter with a large feature set. The dangerous ones let the document decide which code runs or how much memory is allocated. Feature What an attacker gets Control Java native Serializable gadget chains & 8594; code execution on readObject never accept it from outside; use JSON with a schema Polymorphic typing by class name instantiates classes you never intended no default typing; bind a sealed set of subtypes XML external entities (XXE) reads local files, reaches internal hosts disable DTDs and external entities on every factory Deeply nested or huge JSON stack overflow, heap exhaustion size limits, depth limits, streaming parse Compressed uploads zip bomb: 1 MB expands to 10 GB cap the decompressed size while expanding Catastrophic regex one request pins a CPU core bounded patterns, no user-supplied regex // polymorphic JSON, done safely: the set of types is closed and named by the code @JsonTypeInfo (use = Id.NAME, property = \"kind\" ) @JsonSubTypes ({ @Type (value = CardPayment.class, name = \"card\" ), @Type (value = BankTransfer.class, name = \"bank\" ) }) sealed interface PaymentInstruction permits CardPayment, BankTransfer { } For uploads, validate before you trust the name: decide the type from content, not from the extension or the client's Content-Type store outside the web root with a generated name; never execute or serve it back inline cap size at the edge and again while decompressing, and scan when the file will be shared with others A file upload is untrusted input that persists It is stored, retrieved, and often rendered later by a different component with different assumptions. That later component is where the exploit lands. the root cause is trusting the document to choose behaviour. Ask of every parser: can the input decide which class is created, which file is read, or how much memory is allocated? responses matter too: serve user files from a separate origin with Content-Disposition: attachment and a restrictive Content-Security-Policy, so a stored HTML file cannot execute in your domain. limits belong at the edge as well as in code — the proxy rejects a 2 GB body long before the JVM tries to buffer it (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q1). log and alert on parse failures at volume: a sudden burst of malformed payloads is usually somebody probing, not a broken client. fuzz the parsing layer of anything that accepts third-party data; it is one of the few places where random input finds real bugs cheaply (26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q4).","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q3"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q4","surface":"track","en":"Your service fetches a URL the user supplied. What is the full SSRF blast radius and what actually stops it?","bodyEn":"SSRF turns your service into the attacker's proxy: it already sits inside the network, already holds credentials, and is already trusted by everything around it. Typical entry points: avatar-from-URL, webhook registration, PDF or link preview generation, XML with external entities, an import job that takes a file URL, and any \"callback URL\" field. attacker & 8594; POST /import {\"url\": \"http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/…\"} & 8594; your pod fetches it with its own identity & 8594; response body returned to the attacker Control Strength Why it fails alone Blocking localhost and 127.0.0.1 weak 0.0.0.0, IPv6, decimal and octal forms, DNS names that resolve inward Allowlist of hosts strong only workable when destinations are known Resolve then validate the IP strong must re-check after redirects and pin the resolved address Egress firewall / proxy strongest the one control that survives a code mistake Disable redirects, cap size and time supporting limits the damage rather than preventing it What makes SSRF different from other input bugs Validating the string is not enough, because DNS can answer differently the second time. Validate the resolved address and then connect to that address, not to the name again. the cloud metadata endpoint is the classic prize; it hands out instance credentials. Enforce the hardened metadata service and give the workload the narrowest role it can do its job with (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q5). internal services usually have no authentication because \"they are internal\". SSRF is exactly the assumption breaker, which is why service-to-service auth belongs everywhere (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q2). blind SSRF still works without a response body: timing and DNS lookups leak whether a host exists, which is enough to map a network. treat outbound fetches as a privileged operation: run them from a component with its own restricted egress, not from the service holding your database credentials. webhooks make you the caller by design, so the same controls apply to every delivery attempt, including retries (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1).","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q4"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q1","surface":"track","en":"Storage is encrypted, so the data is safe. Which attacks does that actually stop, and which does it not?","bodyEn":"Disk-level encryption protects the disk. Almost every incident that matters reaches the data through the application, which sees plaintext by design. Layer Stops Does not stop Full-disk / volume a stolen or discarded disk any authenticated query, SQL injection, a leaked dump Database TDE reading data files or backups directly a DBA, a compromised app, SELECT Field-level (app encrypts) DBA access, dumps, most injections the service itself once it is compromised Tokenisation / vault the value never enters your system abuse of the token where it is accepted Hashing (passwords) reversal after a breach weak passwords, credential stuffing Start from a data classification, not from a technology: public, internal, personal, and regulated (payment, health, government id) for each class: who may read it, how long it is kept, and where it may be copied the strongest control is not storing it — a field nobody stores cannot leak \"Encrypted at rest\" is a compliance sentence, not a threat model Say which attacker it stops. If the answer is \"somebody who steals the physical disk\", it is true and rarely the attacker you have. field-level encryption has to answer the query problem first: an encrypted column cannot be searched or ranged over. A blind index (a keyed hash of the normalised value) buys equality lookups only, and leaks equality. key management is the real work: a key in the same database is decoration. Use a KMS, keep keys per environment, and rotate with a key id stored next to the ciphertext so old rows stay readable. passwords are not encrypted, they are hashed with a memory-hard function — that distinction still separates engineers in interviews (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q5). backups and non-production copies inherit the classification. A staging database restored from production is a production data breach waiting for a weaker password. pick the layer by threat: TDE for lost media and auditors, field-level for insiders and dumps, tokenisation to remove the data from scope entirely. Independent controls, independent owners: Control Answers Does not replace Encryption at rest limits direct media, file, and backup reads application authorization or safe handling of plaintext Key management KMS/HSM custody, separation, rotation, and emergency revocation permission to read a domain object Application authorization principal, tenant, object, field, action, and current-state decisions encryption or key custody Retention/deletion legal basis, expiry, copies, backups, and deletion/crypto-shredding workflow confidentiality or tamper evidence Immutable audit append-only, tamper-evident access and change evidence data secrecy or permission to retain personal data Treat a pass in one row as no evidence that the other rows are implemented. Name a control owner, test, retention window, and failure response for each.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q1"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q2","surface":"track","en":"Where does sensitive data actually leak in a well-built system, and how do you close those paths?","bodyEn":"Breaches through the front door make the news; the routine leaks are internal copies nobody classified. Path What escapes Control Logs request bodies, tokens, card numbers, full stack traces structured fields with an allowlist, never log whole payloads Traces and metrics identifiers as span attributes or labels allowlisted baggage, no PII in labels Error responses internal messages, SQL fragments, hostnames a stable error contract, detail behind a trace id Support and admin screens full records to anyone with the role mask by default, reveal on justified action, log the reveal Exports and reports everything, downloaded to a laptop scoped columns, expiring links, audit each export Non-production copies the whole database, weakly guarded anonymise or synthesise on the way out of production Third-party tools whatever the SDK collects by default review defaults, scrub before send // masking belongs in the type, not in each log statement record Pan (String value) { public String toString () { return \"\" + value.substring(value.length() - 4 ); } } The design move that scales Wrap sensitive values in their own type whose toString is already masked. Then a careless log line, an exception message and a debugger all show the masked form by default. the most common leak is an exception: a message containing the offending value, serialised into an error response or shipped to an aggregator. Log the trace id, keep detail server-side (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2). \"temporary\" debug logging is the second: it is added during an incident, survives the fix, and quietly writes card numbers for months. anonymisation is harder than masking. A dataset with a birth date, a postcode and a gender is usually re-identifiable; sampling and generalising beats replacing names. treat the log pipeline as a data store with its own retention, access control and audit. It frequently holds more sensitive data than the database does (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4). add a CI check for the obvious patterns — card-like digit runs and Authorization: in log statements — because review misses them at 2am.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q2"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do retention, deletion requests and an immutable audit trail coexist when the two requirements contradict each other?","bodyEn":"\"Delete everything about me\" and \"keep an immutable financial record\" are both real obligations. The resolution is to separate the identity from the transaction. Data Obligation Design Identity and contact details erasable on request one owning service, referenced by id elsewhere Financial and tax records retain for years keep the amounts and the id, drop the personal fields Security audit trail tamper-evident, retained append-only, actor as a stable id, not a name Behavioural and analytics data minimise and expire aggregate early, keep no raw identifiers What makes erasure achievable at all: personal data lives in one place and everything else stores a reference, so deletion is one operation instead of a manhunt crypto-shredding for stores you cannot rewrite: encrypt per subject and destroy that subject's key, which renders backups and archives unreadable without rewriting them an inventory of copies: search index, cache, data warehouse, exports, partner systems, backups — a deletion that misses these is not a deletion Backups are the requirement everyone forgets You cannot rewrite last month's backup. Either commit to a retention window after which backups age out, or make the data unreadable by destroying its key. an audit trail answers \"who did what to which record, and when\" — including reads of sensitive data, which is what regulators ask for first and what most systems cannot answer at all. append-only plus a hash chain gives tamper evidence cheaply: each entry carries a hash of the previous one, so silent edits become detectable. separate the audit trail from application logs. Logs are operational, rotate quickly and can be edited; audit records are evidence with their own retention (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4). a ledger keeps corrections as new entries rather than updates, which is the same discipline that makes financial records reconstructable (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3). write the retention rule as code — a scheduled job with its own tests — rather than a paragraph in a policy document nobody executes.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q3"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q4","surface":"track","en":"Most of your running code came from other people. How do you keep dependencies and images from becoming the breach?","bodyEn":"A typical service ships a few thousand lines of your code on top of hundreds of megabytes of other people's. Supply chain work is about knowing what you ship and being able to replace it quickly. The practices that matter, in order of payoff: know what is in there — generate an SBOM at build time and keep it with the artifact, so answering \"are we affected by this CVE?\" takes minutes rather than a week scan continuously, not at release — a dependency that was clean at build becomes vulnerable the day an advisory is published upgrade on a cadence — small, frequent updates are how you stay able to patch fast; a two-year-old framework turns an urgent fix into a migration project pin and verify — exact versions and digests, lockfiles committed, images pinned by digest so a moved tag cannot swap your base layer Surface What to check Application dependencies direct and transitive CVEs, licence, abandoned projects Base image OS package CVEs, size, non-root user, distroless where possible Build pipeline who can trigger it, what secrets it can read, provenance of artifacts Plugins and actions third-party CI steps run with your credentials The question that reframes the work Not \"do we have vulnerabilities?\" — you do. It is \"how long does it take us to ship a patched build?\" Everything else follows from that number. triage by reachability, not by CVSS alone: a critical rating in a code path you never call is lower priority than a medium one on your request path. Blind severity queues cause alert fatigue and then get ignored. the build pipeline is production. It holds credentials for every environment, so a compromised CI step is a compromised deployment (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q4). pin third-party CI actions to a commit digest; tags are mutable and have been repointed to malicious code in real incidents. keep one artifact promoted through environments rather than rebuilding per stage, so what you scanned is exactly what you run (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q1). transitive dependencies are the majority of the risk and the hardest to reason about; dependency management that reports the resolved graph is worth more than a scanner nobody reads (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q3).","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q4"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q5","surface":"track","en":"A request carries a valid access token. Why can it still be a cross-tenant data breach, and where should the authorization decision live?","bodyEn":"A valid signature proves that a trusted issuer minted the token; it does not prove that this principal may access this object, tenant, field, or workflow transition. Treat authentication and authorization as two separate gates. Build the effective authorization context from trusted state: principal: sub, trusted issuer, intended audience/resource server, client, authentication strength, and token scopes/roles resource: tenant, owner, object state, and relationship loaded from the server-side record—not copied from tenantId, ownerId, role, or isAdmin in the request body operation: action, fields being changed, amount, and workflow transition; a GET permission is not automatically a permission to export or approve policy: the service’s versioned RBAC/ABAC/relationship rule and any explicit deny or separation-of-duty rule Put the invariant at the resource boundary. The gateway can reject missing tokens and coarse scopes, but the owning service must scope the database query and re-check the object policy before returning or mutating data. derive the tenant from the authenticated context and include it in every read/write predicate allowlist writable fields instead of binding JSON directly to a domain entity A central policy decision point can reduce duplicated rules, but it adds an availability, cache-freshness, and policy-version boundary; it does not remove the service’s responsibility to bind the decision to the resource. The same check must survive caches, search indexes, exports, batch APIs, and asynchronous workers. A worker should carry a bounded, auditable authorization context or re-evaluate policy at execution time; it must not silently replace the user with an unbounded system identity. Strip client-supplied identity headers and re-bind trusted service identity at every hop. Test the access paths: horizontal access (user A swaps user B’s object) and vertical access (ordinary user invokes an admin action) tenant changes, deleted memberships, and stale policy caches bulk/mass-assignment fields and object enumeration a retried async job Record subject, tenant, action, resource, decision, policy version, and request/job id in an access audit event—never the bearer token or other secrets. OWASP calls the object-id failure mode broken object-level authorization; the durable fix is server-side ownership and action enforcement, not a more convincing JWT.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q5"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q6","surface":"track","en":"How do you design a webhook or signed/capability URL so a valid signature cannot be replayed or broadened into access?","bodyEn":"Treat a webhook and a capability URL as related cryptographic boundaries, not as the same kind of identity. For a webhook: verify the provider’s signature over the exact raw request bytes before parsing or re-serializing; select the expected key and algorithm, use constant-time comparison, and enforce a bounded timestamp window where the provider supports one. A valid signature still does not prove that the event is current, unique, for the expected account, or safe to apply. So: bind the event to the integration/account, expected object and state, amount or version where relevant, and a provider event id record that id with an atomic uniqueness constraint before the side effect, then make the handler idempotent prefer durable acceptance followed by asynchronous processing over holding the provider connection open; retries must converge to one state give key rotation an explicit old/new overlap and a bounded unknown-key refresh path For a capability or presigned URL: it is a bearer right. Sign the least privilege possible—specific resource, method, content constraints, audience or tenant where supported, and a short expiry. Assume the URL can leak through browser history, referrers, proxy logs, support tickets, or copy/paste. Use HTTPS, prevent sensitive query logging, restrict upload size/type, and add a server-side one-time or revocation record for high-risk operations. Do not rely on expiry to make a URL confidential or immediately revocable, and do not treat possession of it as a user session or proof of ownership. The final authorization decision still belongs to the resource owner: account binding, current workflow state, idempotency, and allowed transition must be checked before applying the event or serving the object. Test altered bytes, old timestamps, duplicate event ids, wrong accounts, key rotation, URL method/resource changes, expired and revoked capabilities, and a retry after the side effect. A signature proves integrity under a key; it does not by itself prove freshness, uniqueness, business authorization, or safe replay.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q6"},{"id":"13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q7","surface":"track","en":"What has to be operationally true for key rotation, session revocation, and audit to remain safe during an IdP or policy outage?","bodyEn":"Key rotation and revocation are operational workflows, not a checkbox in the token library. Write down the accepted window and rehearse the failure path. Key lifecycle: inventory every issuer, audience, algorithm, kid, JWKS source, signing key, and verifier cache. publish the new verification key before signing with it, keep the old key only for the maximum still-valid token window, and remove it deliberately unknown-kid refreshes must be bounded and deduplicated; otherwise one bad token can create a refresh storm an emergency compromise path needs a way to disable a key and invalidate dependent caches without waiting for a normal deployment Never accept an algorithm, issuer, audience, or JWKS URL selected by the request. Session lifecycle: define whether logout means local session removal, refresh-token-family revocation, device revocation, or a bounded access-token expiry. Rotate refresh tokens and detect reuse where the risk justifies it. Keep session-family and device state so a stolen refresh token cannot mint forever. Do not promise instant revocation if resource servers intentionally cache decisions for hours. Outage policy: high-risk writes—money movement, privilege changes, ownership changes, exports—should fail closed when the required identity or policy proof is unavailable. A low-risk read may use a tightly bounded stale decision only when the risk owner accepts it, with an expiry, visible telemetry, and an audit reason. Avoid one global fail-open rule. Circuit breakers and cache TTLs must prevent an IdP/PDP outage from becoming either a security bypass or a retry storm. Audit and proof: send access, key, policy, revocation, and break-glass events to an independently protected sink. Capture subject, issuer, key id, policy version, action, resource, result, reason, request id, and time; omit tokens, refresh secrets, private keys, and sensitive payloads. Test overlapping rotation, clock skew, JWKS failure, revoked-key replay, logout during an in-flight request, refresh reuse, a second operator session, and recovery/rollback. The system is not operationally safe until those tests show both the intended denial and a recoverable path. Bound the degraded mode: record a maximum stale-key age, maximum stale-policy age, access-token/revocation window, retry budget, and recovery owner. If JWKS or the IdP is unavailable, continue only with cached trusted keys inside that policy; do not mint or refresh credentials, and fail closed for high-risk writes. Unknown-kid recovery must be single-flight with bounded backoff/negative caching. A key compromise bypasses normal TTL: disable the key, invalidate dependent caches, revoke sessions, and preserve a separate audit event.","contextEn":"Security: OAuth2, OIDC, JWT & designing an auth system","href":"/topics/13-security-oauth2#question-13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q7"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q1","surface":"track","en":"Docker: image layers, multi-stage builds, and common commands?","bodyEn":"An image is made up of stacked layers (each Dockerfile instruction creates one cacheable layer). Multi-stage build: a build stage (Maven/JDK) produces the artifact; the runtime stage copies only that artifact into a minimal base image (JRE/distroless) → a smaller image with fewer vulnerabilities. FROM maven AS build COPY . . && mvn package FROM eclipse-temurin: 21 -jre COPY --from=build /app/target/app.jar /app.jar ENTRYPOINT [ \"java\" , \"-jar\" , \"/app.jar\" ] Common commands: docker build -t, run -d -p, ps, logs -f, exec -it, image prune. Cache optimization: copy pom.xml and download dependencies before copying the source. each instruction creates an immutable layer cached by content → put the least frequently changed parts first (copy pom.xml/lockfile and download dependencies), then copy the source → a code change does not force dependencies to be downloaded again multi-stage: use a heavyweight build stage (JDK+Maven) → copy only the JAR into the runtime stage (JRE slim/distroless) → a smaller image, fewer CVEs, and no build tools (a smaller attack surface) a smaller image pulls faster (so rollouts are faster) and has fewer vulnerabilities; distroless images can omit the shell, while Alpine keeps only a minimal userland (safer and smaller, but harder to debug) Pitfall: a secret placed in a layer remains in the image history even if a later layer deletes it → use build secrets or a multi-stage build. Pin the base image by digest for reproducible builds. For Java, use Spring Boot layered JARs to separate the dependency layer. Build-secret and image-identity boundary: syntax=docker/dockerfile:1 RUN --mount=type=secret,id=maven_settings,target=/root/.m2/settings.xml \\ mvn -q package The Dockerfile above uses readable tags as teaching shorthand. For a real build, pin every FROM reference to an approved digest, record its provenance, and let admission reject mutable or unsigned images. A digest, signature, SBOM, or vulnerability scan is evidence for a policy; none alone proves that the image or its runtime configuration is safe. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The Kubernetes distribution and version, ingress or Gateway controller, runtime, mesh, CI provider, registry and database versions in scope — most \"best practice\" wording is version-bound. Whether the application is stateless, stateful-but-replicated, or a controller, and who owns backup, restore and data failover. The real rollout capacity, startup time, request-tail, connection-drain, collector-volume and migration-lock budgets. Which controls are mandatory by regulation or region: KMS, audit, data residency, signing, separation of duties. What falsifies progressive rollout: no representative traffic, no trustworthy user SLI, no spare capacity, or a rollback that is not itself rehearsed. What falsifies mesh mTLS: unsupported protocols, incomplete workload coverage, a CA-rotation outage, or policy latency. What falsifies expand–contract: an old binary that cannot tolerate the expanded schema, a backfill that saturates the primary, or lock and replication lag beyond budget.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q1"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q2","surface":"track","en":"Kubernetes architecture: control plane, nodes, and pods?","bodyEn":"Control plane: API server (entry point), etcd (state store), scheduler (places pods), controller manager (reconciles desired vs. actual state) Node: kubelet (runs pods), kube-proxy (networking), container runtime Pod: the smallest deployable unit (1+ containers sharing networking and volumes) Kubernetes operates through declarative configuration + reconciliation: you declare the desired state, and controllers continuously drive the system toward it. A Deployment written through the API server is stored in etcd; the controller sees fewer Pods than desired and creates them, the scheduler binds each to a node, and that node's kubelet starts it. Delete a Pod and the same loop puts it back. Kubernetes version/distribution preface (reviewed 2026-08-23): The platform semantics here are anchored to the Kubernetes v1.36 documentation selected in the source ledger; this is a documentation reference, not a target cluster. Pin the chosen distribution and minor version in manifests and runbooks. Check every alpha/beta feature gate and controller extension against that exact release before using it; feature maturity and defaults are not portable. control plane: api-server (the single entry point, authentication/admission), etcd (a Raft KV store—the source of truth), scheduler (assigns pods according to resources/affinity), controller-manager (the 'desired vs actual' control loop) node: kubelet (runs pods), kube-proxy (Service networking), containerd; a pod is 1+ containers sharing networking and volumes (for example, a sidecar) reconciliation loop: provides self-healing (a pod dies → another is created); this explains why 'delete the pod and it comes back → update the Deployment instead' etcd is mission-critical (backups plus an odd-numbered HA quorum). Run stateless applications in Deployments; for any stateful workload, use StatefulSet only for identity/storage semantics, then use an operator or managed service and verify replication, backup/restore, fencing, and failover.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q2"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q3","surface":"track","en":"Deployment vs. StatefulSet—what differs, and when should each be used?","bodyEn":"Deployment StatefulSet Workload replaceable replicas workloads needing stable identity/storage ordering; not data safety Pod identity arbitrary (cattle) stable: pod-0, pod-1 (pets) Storage not permanently bound a dedicated PVC per pod Scaling/startup flexible ordered Rule of thumb Use Deployment for replaceable replicas; use StatefulSet when stable identity/storage ordering is required. For a database, Kafka, or Zookeeper, pair the controller with an operator or managed service and verify replication, backups, restore, fencing, and failover—the controller alone does not make data safe. StatefulSet: stable names name-0..N, dedicated PVCs through volumeClaimTemplates (data survives rescheduling), a headless Service that provides DNS such as pod-0.svc, and ordered scaling/rolling updates Pitfall: putting a stateless application in a StatefulSet 'just to be safe' sacrifices scaling flexibility; putting a database in a Deployment loses the pod-to-volume association The concise distinction is 'cattle vs pets': Deployment pods are cattle; StatefulSet pods are named pets.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q3"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q4","surface":"track","en":"What are the most useful kubectl commands, and how do you 'deploy a new pod' or perform a rolling update?","bodyEn":"Common commands include kubectl get/describe, logs -f, exec -it, apply -f, rollout status/undo, scale, and port-forward. Deploy a new version declaratively: update the image in the manifest → run kubectl apply -f (or set image) → the Deployment performs a rolling update by creating a new ReplicaSet and gradually scaling new pods up and old pods down according to maxSurge/maxUnavailable. On failure, run kubectl rollout undo. Avoid manually running kubectl edit in production because it creates drift from Git. DO NOT use kubectl run to create a bare pod; it cannot self-heal. Update the Deployment's image tag instead → Kubernetes performs the rolling update automatically use set image or apply -f (GitOps favors apply); use rollout status/undo to monitor the rollout or roll back to the previous ReplicaSet; maxSurge/maxUnavailable plus a readiness probe govern a zero-downtime rolling update Pitfall: without a readiness probe, traffic reaches pods that are not ready (5xx); without preStop and graceful shutdown, active connections are cut. A PodDisruptionBudget protects the minimum available pod count while draining a node. In production, let a GitOps controller such as ArgoCD apply changes instead of running kubectl manually. When a pod dies for no obvious reason, trace OOMKilled and exit code 137 as described in (21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q4).","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q4"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q5","surface":"track","en":"What roles do Service, Ingress, ConfigMap, and Secret play?","bodyEn":"Object What it gives The default to know Service a stable address that load-balances across a set of pods (ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer) pod IPs change; the Service's DNS name does not Ingress external HTTP(S) routing to services by host/path, TLS terminated by an ingress controller such as Nginx stable but frozen: new routing features land in Gateway API ConfigMap non-sensitive configuration changing it does not reload the application Secret sensitive data, which should be encrypted at rest with KMS only base64-encoded until encryption at rest is enabled Both ConfigMaps and Secrets can be injected through environment variables or volumes. a Service targets pods through a label selector; its forms include ClusterIP (internal), NodePort, LoadBalancer (cloud LB), and Headless (per-pod DNS for a StatefulSet) Ingress provides L7 routing and TLS termination through Nginx/Traefik; Kubernetes documents Ingress as stable but frozen, while Gateway API is a separate API recommended for new features. A Secret is only base64-encoded; it is NOT encrypted by default → enable encryption at rest for etcd, enforce RBAC, and keep secrets out of logs a Service decouples the stable 'address' from ephemeral pods: pod IPs change, while Service DNS remains stable Pitfall: treating a Secret as inherently secure. Production systems need KMS, Sealed Secrets, or External Secrets backed by Vault; ConfigMap changes do not reload applications automatically, so use a restart or reloader. For an OTA platform, source PSP/GDS API keys from Vault through External Secrets. Ingress/Gateway selection boundary: choose Ingress when the selected controller's existing routes and extensions are sufficient; record the controller version and its conformance limits choose Gateway API for role-oriented or richer routing only when the selected implementation supports the required Gateway and HTTPRoute features; conformance does not erase provider extensions test TLS, redirects, timeouts, retries, auth filters, and failure behavior on the pinned controller; neither API is a universal provider-neutral deployment","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q5"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q6","surface":"track","en":"Why are liveness, readiness, and startup probes separate?","bodyEn":"Probe Question Failure effect Liveness can this container recover only by restart? kubelet restarts the container after the configured threshold Readiness should this pod receive Service traffic now? pod is removed from ready endpoints; it is not restarted Startup has slow initialization completed? liveness/readiness remain disabled until startup succeeds; repeated failure restarts Dependency checks are contextual Liveness should not fail because a shared database is slow: restarting every pod amplifies the outage. Readiness may check a dependency only when this particular pod truly cannot serve and removing it helps. If every replica fails readiness for the same shared dependency, the Service can lose all endpoints and turn a degraded dependency into a full outage. Prefer local readiness plus explicit dependency metrics/degraded behavior when possible. Probe an outcome with a cheap, bounded check: Liveness: detect unrecoverable event-loop/thread deadlock or internal corruption; do not treat transient latency as death. Readiness: include local initialization, drain state, required local cache/config, and only dependency state needed for this pod's traffic contract. Startup: give cold JVM/cache/migration initialization its own failure budget instead of inflating liveness forever. Set timeoutSeconds, periodSeconds, and failure/success thresholds from measured startup and transient behavior. Keep probe handlers independent from saturated request pools where possible, but do not make them lie. Expensive probes and synchronized probe storms can overload the very dependency they test. Inspect pod status, events, termination reason, previous container logs, and exit code before changing probes; OOMKilled, crash, image/config failure, and a probe restart require different fixes (21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q4). Probe-to-termination state machine: Starting: the startup probe owns initialization; readiness stays false until user-serving prerequisites are met Serving: readiness is true; liveness checks only local recoverability Draining: mark unready, observe EndpointSlice/traffic removal, stop new work, and let bounded requests and consumers finish Terminating: verify preStop, application drain, SIGTERM, the grace deadline, and connection/worker shutdown in the selected runtime and controller; timing can overlap Forced: SIGKILL or node loss is expected; durable work, idempotency, and reconciliation handle what the hook could not finish Measure each transition and test it under keep-alive, HTTP/2, eviction, node failure, and slow dependencies; no preStop sleep is a correctness guarantee.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q6"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q1","surface":"track","en":"How does Nginx work as a reverse proxy and load balancer?","bodyEn":"Nginx uses an event-driven, non-blocking architecture and sits in front of the application: reverse proxy: hides backends, terminates TLS, manages headers, and applies gzip compression load balancing: supports round-robin, least_conn, and ip_hash across an upstream caches static content, rate-limits requests with limit_req, and buffers traffic It is well suited to the edge/ingress role and serves static content or proxies traffic very efficiently with few resources. important settings include proxy_buffering off for SSE/streaming; upstream keepalive to reuse connections and reduce handshakes; proxy_read_timeout for slow backends such as GDS; and X-Forwarded-For/proto so the backend knows the original client and protocol Nginx can serve HTTP/2 on the client-facing side while using HTTP/1.1 to upstreams In an OTA platform, Nginx/Envoy at the edge handles TLS, coarse-grained rate limiting, and routing; an application gateway such as Spring Cloud Gateway handles authentication and business-aware routing. Envoy underpins service meshes: it understands HTTP/2/gRPC and provides configuration-driven retries and circuit breakers, making it an increasingly common replacement for Nginx inside the mesh.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q1"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do you manage high-volume logs with structured logging and ELK/Loki?","bodyEn":"At multi-service scale, do not SSH into hosts to read log files. Instead: emit structured logs as JSON with standard fields such as service, level, trace_id, request_id, and an anonymized user_id collect logs centrally in ELK (Elasticsearch + Logstash/Fluentd + Kibana) or Loki (lower cost, label-based indexing, and a natural fit with Grafana) define retention/rotation policies and sample noisy logs DO NOT log sensitive data such as PII, PCI data, or tokens Key point Attach trace_id/request_id to every log so you can reconstruct one request's path across services—the foundation of distributed debugging. emit structured JSON with required fields (timestamp, level, service, trace_id, span_id, request_id); ship it through an agent such as Fluent Bit/Vector → buffer it in Kafka → ingest it into ELK/Loki control cost by sampling noisy logs, separating business logs from debug logs, and tiering retention (for example, 7 days hot and a cold archive in S3) Never log PII/PCI data such as card numbers, tokens, or passwords; this is a common compliance violation, so apply masking For an OTA platform, log every GDS/PSP interaction with a correlation ID so teams can reconcile records and investigate stuck transactions. Collector failure boundary: bound agent/collector memory, queue length, batch size, and export retry time; define what drops first and preserve error and latency signals monitor collector self-telemetry such as queue age, dropped records, export failures, CPU/memory, and backend availability; alert before buffers threaten application nodes normalize route templates and bound dimensions; never use raw URL paths, user IDs, tokens, or arbitrary error text as metric labels. Keep detail in redacted logs/traces and sample it","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q2"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q3","surface":"track","en":"How does distributed tracing work in Kubernetes with OpenTelemetry and Jaeger?","bodyEn":"every request carries a trace ID; each hop is a span with a parent, except for the root span; context is propagated through HTTP/gRPC headers and message headers OpenTelemetry provides vendor-neutral instrumentation and exports data to Jaeger, Tempo, or Datadog a trace shows which services handled a request and where time was spent—for example, 'checkout is slow because a synchronous fraud-check call adds 600 ms' in Kubernetes, run the OTel Collector as a DaemonSet or sidecar OTel standardizes creation & propagation of trace context using the W3C traceparent header → each hop creates a span that is a child of its parent and shares the same trace_id; use auto-instrumentation for HTTP/gRPC/JDBC/Kafka plus manual spans, then export OTLP → collector → backend one request becomes a span tree that exposes the duration of each hop; sampling at the head or tail controls cost, with tail sampling retaining failed or slow traces the real value is correlation: add trace_id to logs and metric exemplars, and propagate it in Kafka headers to trace asynchronous flows In an OTA platform, a 'book ticket' trace across booking→payment→gds→ticketing can reveal that GDS contributes 600 ms of latency; without traces, finding that bottleneck is like looking for a needle in a haystack.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q3"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q4","surface":"track","en":"What are the three pillars of observability and the RED/USE metrics?","bodyEn":"The three pillars are: Logs (structured and correlated with a trace ID) Metrics (Prometheus + Grafana) Traces (OpenTelemetry→Jaeger) Measurement frameworks: RED for services: Rate, Errors, Duration USE for resources: Utilization, Saturation, Errors Alert against SLOs/SLIs rather than alerting on every spike. a metric says 'something is wrong' → a trace locates 'where' → logs explain 'why' alert on SLOs and user-visible symptoms such as p99 latency or user-visible error rate, not on causes such as CPU reaching 80%, which may not represent a problem → this reduces alert fatigue saturation, such as pending work in a queue or pool, is a leading indicator; business metrics such as booking_failed_total and payment_pending are as important as infrastructure metrics For an OTA platform, combine a RED dashboard for each service with the business funnel (search→hold→pay→issue) to identify the exact step where the workflow breaks. Topic 20 owns the detailed treatment: Metrics for services and resources: (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q3) Cardinality and labels that can overload the metrics system: (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q4) Decisions driven by SLOs and error budgets: (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q1) Searchable logs for investigations: (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4)","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.web-server-networking-observability.q4"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q1","surface":"track","en":"What does a typical CI/CD pipeline look like, and how do blue-green and canary deployments compare?","bodyEn":"Stages: source → build → unit test → SAST/SCA scan → immutable, versioned image → deploy to staging → integration test → gated production deployment → smoke test → rollback readiness. Blue-green Canary Method maintain two environments and switch all traffic shift a growing percentage of traffic gradually Rollback immediate (switch back) stop or reduce the percentage Cost roughly double the resources requires strong observability Blast radius all users if smoke tests miss the defect small Fintech Prefer canary releases, feature flags, and backward-compatible database migrations so rollback remains safe. pipeline: commit → build + unit test → SAST + SCA (CVE) + secret scan → build an image pinned by digest + sign it with cosign → push to the registry → staging + integration/contract tests → approval → production Database migration is the hardest part—use expand-contract: add a backward-compatible column first, deploy the code, then remove the old column later, so rollback remains possible For OTA/fintech systems, use canaries for high-risk payment-flow changes, and use feature flags to separate 'deployment' from 'release' (Argo Rollouts plus a mesh for traffic splitting). Promotion and admission boundary: before production promotion, verify the immutable image digest, signature, SBOM/provenance policy, vulnerability gate, and runtime configuration/RBAC. These controls provide supply-chain evidence; they do not prove source correctness, business safety, or least-privilege runtime behavior. Rollout sizing worksheet (fill from the measured workload): replicas, maxSurge, maxUnavailable, terminationGracePeriodSeconds, and startup/drain budget CPU/memory requests and limits, node/zone topology, and PodDisruptionBudget autoscaling metric/window and spare capacity for canary or blue-green promotion per-pod and downstream connection-pool limits, queue age, request deadline, and retry budget promotion and rollback guardrails with an owner; do not copy a number across distributions or providers","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q1"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q2","surface":"track","en":"What problems does a service mesh such as Istio or Linkerd solve, and what are the 2026 best practices?","bodyEn":"A service mesh moves networking cross-cutting concerns—retries, circuit breakers, mTLS, traffic splitting/canaries, and observability—into the infrastructure layer through a sidecar proxy instead of application code. Istio and Linkerd are product choices, not a universal “2026 baseline”: supported Kubernetes versions, sidecar/ambient modes, maturity, extensions, resource cost, and failure behavior must be checked against the selected release. start with one capability, such as mTLS or canary delivery, and monitor sidecar and control-plane resource usage extract service communication logic (mTLS, retries, timeouts, circuit breakers, traffic splitting, observability) into an Envoy sidecar → applications do not implement network resilience/security themselves; declarative policies configure it transport mTLS and certificate rotation authenticate the workload or channel; they do not grant application authorization. Traffic management can support canary/A-B, and telemetry can be standardized, but each implementation's metrics and policy behavior must be verified. costs: added sidecar latency and resources plus operational complexity; Istio ambient mesh is a version-scoped feature whose availability and behavior must be verified for the selected distribution and release. Do not introduce a mesh too early: for a handful of services, resilience4j plus the OTel SDK is enough. A mesh becomes valuable when many polyglot services need consistent mTLS and traffic policies. Separate mesh controls: mTLS: authenticates a workload or transport channel and protects it in the covered path application authorization: evaluates the caller, action, resource, and ownership at the service boundary policy availability: defines cache staleness, failure mode, rotation, and recovery when the CA, policy engine, or control plane is unavailable non-meshed traffic: is outside automatic mesh guarantees and needs an explicit ingress, egress, workload-identity, or application control","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q2"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q3","surface":"track","en":"Which backend architecture practices remain sound in 2026, and when do they become cargo cult?","bodyEn":"Durable practices are decision principles, not a required technology stack: Explicit ownership and boundaries: one service/team owns writes to a data model. A physically separate database can enforce that boundary, but “database per service” is not a command to create a cluster for every small service; shared infrastructure with isolated schemas/roles can be a deliberate stage. Evolutionary delivery: modular monolith first when boundaries are uncertain; extract only where independent scaling, reliability, security, or team ownership pays for distribution. Platform as a product: paved roads, self-service, policy, and observable templates when many teams repeat the same work; measure adoption and developer outcomes. Open standards and operability: OpenTelemetry, SLOs, structured events, reproducible builds, SBOM/provenance, signed artifacts, and automated rollback. Fit-for-purpose integration: synchronous calls for immediate results; messaging/outbox for decoupling and durable asynchronous workflows; neither everywhere. Complexity and cost budgets: every gateway, mesh, broker, database, language, and control plane must have an owner, failure model, and measured benefit. Version scope for the examples: “current” means principles reviewed on 2026-08-23. Pinned by the source ledger: Kubernetes documentation v1.36, PostgreSQL 18, the Java 21 example, and the Spring Boot 4 fragment where named. Product/release-specific examples: Docker BuildKit, NGINX/Envoy, ELK/Loki, OpenTelemetry/Jaeger, Istio/Linkerd, ArgoCD/Flux/Rollouts, Terraform/Pulumi, Flyway/Liquibase, and gh-ost. Pin the selected release and provider before production and recheck feature gates, extensions, and migration behavior; this card does not choose a target deployment. Trend filter Ask which concrete constraint a technology solves, what simpler option was rejected, how it fails, and how the team will operate/exit it. “AIOps,” “serverless,” “mesh,” and “polyglot” are capabilities or product categories—not architecture outcomes. Scale-to-zero comes from the compute platform and application startup/readiness behavior; ZGC is a low-pause collector, not a scale-to-zero mechanism. Native images may improve startup/footprint but trade dynamic behavior, build time, profiling, and ecosystem compatibility. AI-assisted incident analysis or remediation should start read-only, show evidence, preserve human approval for material changes, use narrowly scoped credentials, and be evaluated against false-positive/rollback risk. Moving complexity into a platform does not remove it; it concentrates responsibility, so platform SLOs and on-call ownership become critical. Do not prescribe “Java for core, Go for edge.” Choose a language from domain/library fit, measured runtime economics, team expertise, security patching, and the operational cost of another stack (02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.java-vs-go-2025-2026.q3).","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q3"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q4","surface":"track","en":"Why are GitOps and Infrastructure as Code important?","bodyEn":"IaC (Terraform): declares infrastructure as versioned, reproducible code instead of configuring it manually through a UI GitOps (ArgoCD/Flux): makes Git the source of truth for cluster state; an agent reconciles the cluster to Git → auditable changes, rollback through git revert, and no drift This model suits compliance-heavy environments such as fintech because every change leaves a trace. IaC (Terraform/Pulumi) makes infrastructure reproducible, versioned, and reviewable while avoiding 'snowflake servers'; plan/apply exposes changes before execution GitOps makes Git the single source of truth; a controller reconciles the cluster to Git, every change goes through a PR (audit + review + rollback = git revert), and drift is detected the result is declarative + auditable + self-healing delivery: deployment means merging a PR; keep secrets out of Git with Sealed Secrets or External Secrets For fintech/OTA systems, an audit trail for every infrastructure and deployment change is a compliance requirement. Pitfall: Terraform state can contain sensitive data, so store it securely and lock it.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q4"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q5","surface":"track","en":"What does zero-trust security mean in microservices?","bodyEn":"Zero trust removes location-based implicit trust. Every access crosses a policy boundary where the caller's identity, requested action/resource, context, and current policy are evaluated with least privilege. It is a strategy, not a product and not necessarily “mTLS plus JWT on every hop.” authenticate workload identity with mTLS/SPIFFE, cloud federation, or another short-lived credential appropriate to the platform; carry end-user/delegation context only when the downstream needs it, with narrow audience and scope; authorize at the service that owns the resource and invariant, not only at the gateway; segment network paths, minimize Kubernetes RBAC/service-account permissions, protect secrets/keys, and record decisions; continuously patch, inventory, and verify workloads and artifacts—identity does not make compromised code trustworthy. A mesh can automate transport mTLS and workload certificates, but application authorization still needs domain context. NetworkPolicy constrains paths but does not authenticate a business actor. A signed token proves claims only after issuer/audience/time/key validation and does not replace object ownership checks. Design degraded behavior: what happens if the CA, policy engine, introspection endpoint, JWKS endpoint, or mesh control plane is unavailable? Cache only what is safe, bound staleness, make fail-open/fail-closed choices per operation risk, and rehearse root/key rotation. For PII/payment systems, map controls to actual data flows and compliance scope. A mandatory technology checklist can increase blast radius; a small system may achieve the same principles with cloud workload identity, TLS, service-local authorization, and tight IAM without operating a mesh.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q5"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q6","surface":"track","en":"How do graceful shutdown and connection draining keep Kubernetes rolling updates from dropping in-flight requests?","bodyEn":"When a pod is deleted, Kubernetes starts the termination grace period. Endpoint state begins changing while the kubelet runs a configured preStop hook (whose time counts against that grace period), then sends SIGTERM; SIGKILL follows if processes remain after the deadline. Endpoint/Ingress/load-balancer propagation and application shutdown therefore overlap. The application should enter a drain state, stop admitting new work, finish or hand off bounded in-flight work, stop polling consumers, flush durable acknowledgements, and close resources. Spring Boot can use server.shutdown=graceful plus a bounded spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase. Make the budget explicit Set terminationGracePeriodSeconds to cover any preStop action, the maximum supported request/job drain time, cleanup, and margin. Every request and consumer operation still needs its own deadline; an unbounded request cannot be made safe by an enormous pod grace period. Do not cargo-cult preStop: sleep 15. A sleep can bridge known endpoint/LB propagation delay, but it consumes rollout time and grace budget and provides no acknowledgement that draining completed. Prefer application drain hooks, load-balancer/native deregistration, readiness/drain state, and measured propagation; add a bounded sleep only when evidence shows that specific race remains. Clients may reuse established keep-alive or HTTP/2 connections, so the server/load balancer must stop new streams/requests while allowing existing bounded work to finish. Consumers should stop fetching first, complete or safely abandon current records, commit/ack only durable work, then leave the group. Graceful shutdown is availability optimization, not a correctness guarantee: nodes crash and SIGKILL still happens. Money/workflow correctness must survive abrupt loss through idempotency, transactions, outbox/inbox, durable queues, and resumable saga state (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2). Illustrative Spring Boot 4 + Kubernetes fragment (pin exact patch/runtime versions; not a target deployment): application.yml — graceful shutdown is the Boot 4 default; budget its phase spring: lifecycle: timeout-per-shutdown-phase: 25s --- apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment spec: template: spec: terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 35 preStop + app drain + margin containers: name: api image: example/booking-api:1.7.0 lifecycle: preStop: exec: command: [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"sleep 5\"] endpoint propagation window readinessProbe: httpGet: {path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 8080} periodSeconds: 2 failureThreshold: 1 The fragment's numbers on one clock: preStop and the drain phase share the 35-second grace period, and endpoint removal runs alongside preStop rather than before it. Verify under load: start a 10-second request, run kubectl rollout restart deployment/booking-api, and assert it completes while new traffic moves elsewhere. Benefit: fewer reset in-flight requests. Cost: slower rollouts and overlapping capacity; preStop uses the same grace period, so 35s must exceed 5s + 25s + margin. Boundary: persist critical jobs instead of hoping shutdown hooks finish them.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q6"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q7","surface":"track","en":"How do versioned migrations with Flyway/Liquibase and expand-contract change a live database with zero downtime?","bodyEn":"Use versioned migrations with Flyway/Liquibase: each change is an immutable, checksummed file that runs automatically & in the correct order during deployment. Apply expand-contract as a parallel change across several releases for zero downtime: expand: add a new nullable column/table, backfill it, and make the code write both old and new structures migrate: switch reads to the new structure contract: remove the old structure in a later release Every step remains backward-compatible so old and new pods can run together during a rolling update. A zero-downtime outcome is a compatibility and database-engine contract, not a property guaranteed by Flyway or Liquibase. Before choosing the sequence, pin the engine/provider/version and measure lock, replication-lag, backfill, and rollback budgets. Why not 'ALTER, then deploy': a rolling update temporarily runs both old and new pods & 8594; the schema must support both versions; abruptly renaming/dropping a column or adding NOT NULL breaks old pods. The safe way to add NOT NULL is: add nullable & 8594; backfill in batches & 8594; set NOT NULL afterward. Large-table pitfall: a MySQL ALTER can lock the table, make DDL expensive & cause replication lag → use online DDL such as ALGORITHM=INPLACE or gh-ost/pt-online-schema-change, and backfill in small batches. Run the backward-compatible migration before deploying code that reads the new structure. In fintech, never drop a monetary/ledger column until you are certain no readers remain; every migration needs a rollback plan. Expand-contract discipline enables several deployments per day without a maintenance window. Engine-specific failure cases (verify on the pinned version): PostgreSQL: ALTER TABLE lock level can block traffic; CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY has different transaction and monitoring trade-offs and is not a universal drop-in. Measure lock waits and invalid-index recovery. MySQL: ALTER TABLE algorithm and lock behavior depend on the engine and version; gh-ost or pt-online-schema-change is a MySQL/binlog workflow with its own capacity, trigger, replication, and cutover constraints. Any engine: pause or throttle backfill when lock wait, replication lag, error rate, or pool saturation crosses budget; test old/new binaries and restore before contract. Failure contract: if expand fails, the old schema remains usable if dual writes diverge, stop promotion and reconcile if migrate exceeds budget, leave old readers and writers in place Only after reader inventory, retention window, backup/restore proof, and a rollback decision should the later contract/drop phase run. A destructive drop is a separate release, never a prerequisite for the first compatible deploy.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q7"},{"id":"14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q8","surface":"track","en":"What rollout and termination contract keeps Kubernetes traffic safe during deploy, drain, eviction, and rollback?","bodyEn":"Treat rollout as a state machine, not as a successful kubectl apply. A serving instance moves through starting, ready, draining, terminated and rollback states, and each transition needs a signal and a budget. Phase Required contract Failure to catch Start Startup probe gates liveness/readiness while dependencies and migrations reach a safe state Slow startup is killed repeatedly or a half-ready pod receives traffic Serve Readiness represents ability to serve the user path, not merely an open socket Requests/limits and disruption budget are sized Healthy-looking pods overload a dependency or leave no capacity during drain Drain Mark unready, stop new work, honor preStop/termination grace, close listeners and finish/cancel owned work Requests arrive after readiness change, keep-alive streams are cut, or background jobs duplicate Promote New version passes functional, compatibility, latency/error and business guardrails before increasing traffic Probe green but schema, auth, queue, dependency or user outcome is broken Rollback Pause promotion and route to a version compatible with the expanded schema and persisted state Binary rollback is attempted after a destructive migration or irreversible side effect Kubernetes probe success is not business success. Keep liveness cheap and local; use readiness for traffic eligibility and startup for slow initialization. A readiness failure should normally remove traffic, not restart a process. A termination timeline must include EndpointSlice removal, application drain, preStop, SIGTERM, grace period, forced termination, connection pools and worker shutdown. Verify the ordering for the chosen runtime/controller rather than relying on folklore. Resource requests affect scheduling and limits affect enforcement/pressure; neither is a capacity plan by itself. Pair them with queue age, downstream connection budgets, PDB/topology, autoscaling signals and realistic load tests. Progressive delivery needs representative traffic, trustworthy SLIs, pause criteria and a rollback that is schema-compatible. Blue-green spends capacity for simpler rollback; canary spends observability/analysis effort for smaller blast radius. Image digest, signature, SBOM and provenance improve supply-chain evidence but do not prove runtime authorization or application correctness. Admission policy and least privilege remain separate controls.","contextEn":"DevOps & infrastructure: Docker, K8s, Nginx, logging + 2026 best practices","href":"/topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices#question-14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q8"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q1","surface":"track","en":"How do HTTP and HTTPS differ? How does a TLS 1.3 handshake work?","bodyEn":"HTTPS = HTTP over TLS → encryption (confidentiality), integrity, and server authentication (via a certificate). With plain HTTP, packets can be read or modified in transit — unacceptable for fintech/OTA systems. TLS 1.3 and round trips (path-dependent): A full TLS 1.3 handshake can complete in one TLS handshake round trip after transport setup; TLS 1.2 commonly needs an additional handshake flight. TCP versus QUIC, resumption, proxies, and packet loss change the end-to-end count. ClientHello (key share, cipher list) → ServerHello + certificate + key share both sides derive session keys (ECDHE, forward secrecy) → Finished → encrypted data Resumed sessions may use fewer flights; QUIC/TLS 0-RTT sends early data before handshake completion. It is replayable, so only explicitly replay-safe operations may use it; it does not make a mutation idempotent. Key takeaway The certificate authenticates the server (PKI/CA); ECDHE provides forward secrecy. mTLS adds a client certificate for mutual authentication (service-to-service). forward secrecy (ECDHE): even if the long-term private key is exposed, previously captured sessions still cannot be decrypted — critical for payment data certificate chain: server certificate → intermediate CA → root CA; a common failure is omitting the intermediate certificate (Java clients may fail). OCSP stapling checks revocation; HSTS enforces HTTPS where TLS terminates (LB/gateway/mesh) determines where certificates live & how internal mTLS is handled; handshakes consume CPU → reuse sessions/keep-alive connections. any PCI DSS/TLS-version statement must name the applicable PCI DSS version, environment scope, and security baseline; do not present it as timeless law. OTA: card data/PII requires TLS 1.2+ end-to-end; consider mTLS with PSPs/banks.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q1"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3 differ, and why was HTTP/3 introduced?","bodyEn":"HTTP/1.1: an ordinary persistent connection usually has one in-flight response; clients may open several connections, while pipelining is a separate legacy behavior. HOL blocking at the application layer HTTP/2: multiplexes many streams over one TCP connection and compresses headers (HPACK). It still has TCP-layer HOL (one lost packet can stall every stream) HTTP/2 server push is standardized but deprecated or unsupported in many major browsers/clients, so do not rely on it without target-client evidence HTTP/3: runs over QUIC (over UDP). Loss on one stream does not impose TCP-style cross-stream blocking, but congestion is still controlled at the connection and stream flow-control limits still apply; setup and connection migration depend on full/resumed handshake, path, and implementation Key takeaway HTTP/2 removes application-layer HOL but retains TCP-layer HOL; HTTP/3/QUIC reduces cross-stream transport HOL without removing connection congestion, stream limits, or application ordering. gRPC commonly relies on HTTP/2 for multiplexing + streaming. HTTP/2 multiplexing provides clear benefits for pages with many small resources & for gRPC; it offers less benefit for APIs dominated by a single large request. Server push is deprecated or unsupported in many major browsers/clients (Chrome removed it), so its use requires target-client evidence HTTP/3 (QUIC) can help on lossy/mobile networks and high-latency links, but connection-level congestion, per-stream/connection flow control, CPU, path support, and firewall policy still bound it; provide HTTP/2 fallback (Alt-Svc) where the target path needs it operations: enable HTTP/2 at the LB/Ingress for client-facing traffic (the upstream hop may remain HTTP/1.1); HTTP/3 requires edge/CDN support gRPC + L4 LB pitfall: HTTP/2 pins all RPCs to one backend → use an L7/client-side LB or service mesh. OTA: internal gRPC benefits from HTTP/2; HTTP/3 at the CDN improves latency for mobile users on weak networks.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q3","surface":"track","en":"What makes a well-designed RESTful API: Richardson maturity, idempotency, status codes, and versioning?","bodyEn":"Richardson Maturity Model: L0 (RPC over HTTP), L1 (resources), L2 (HTTP verbs + correct status codes — where most production APIs land), L3 (HATEOAS, rarely used) Principles: model resources as nouns, let verbs carry semantics, and remain stateless. Idempotency: GET/PUT/DELETE are idempotent; POST is not → creation requests (booking, payment) need an Idempotency-Key Status codes: 201 (created + Location), 409 (conflict), 422 (validation), 429 (rate limit), 503 + Retry-After (degraded service) Versioning: bump the version only for breaking changes; adding an optional field is backward-compatible Production pitfall Returning 200 for everything with an 'error' field in the body makes client behavior, retries, and monitoring harder to implement correctly. The HTTP status code is part of the contract. most good APIs stop at Level 2; HATEOAS (L3) is rarely worth the cost more important is consistency (naming, error format, and pagination conventions across the platform) — what partners and frontends actually need; use ETag/If-Match for optimistic concurrency actions that do not map cleanly to CRUD ('cancel', 'refund') → use a sub-resource (POST /bookings/{id}/cancellation) or an RPC-style endpoint (do not force everything into doctrinaire REST) OTA: issue/void/refund/reissue do not map cleanly to CRUD → design them explicitly, make them idempotent, return business status in the body, and use the correct HTTP status so partners can automate reliably.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q3"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you handle a large HTTP request end to end without turning it into a memory incident?","bodyEn":"Treat the body as a resource budget at every hop: client, CDN, gateway, service, proxy, parser, temporary store, and downstream call. Reject before expensive parsing when the declared or observed size exceeds the policy, and use 413 Content Too Large with a useful limit or upload contract. Do not trust Content-Length alone: chunked or HTTP/2 bodies still need byte and time limits, and decompression can expand a small compressed input into a dangerous output. Stream bytes to a bounded sink when incremental processing is safe; otherwise use a direct multipart/resumable object-storage session. Along the way: avoid copying the whole body through every layer, and bound in-memory buffers enforce upload rate/deadline validate authorization and checksum clean up abandoned temporary data A proxy retry is safe only when the body is replayable and the business operation has a stable idempotency identity. The API contract owns upload-session state, completion, and asynchronous processing; see 17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q8-q9. This item owns the transport and memory boundary.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q4"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q1","surface":"track","en":"How do polling, SSE, and WebSocket compare, and how does event-driven architecture differ from realtime delivery to clients?","bodyEn":"Polling/long polling: the client asks repeatedly — simple, but adds latency and request overhead SSE: one-way server→client over HTTP with automatic reconnection — suitable for feeds, notifications, and price updates WebSocket: bidirectional full-duplex over a persistent connection — suitable for chat, trading, and realtime booking tracking Important distinction: event-driven architecture (Kafka/RabbitMQ) is how services communicate INSIDE the system (asynchronously and loosely coupled); WebSocket/SSE is how the system pushes updates TO clients in realtime. Realtime systems commonly combine both: an internal event updates state → a WebSocket gateway fans it out to clients. Key takeaway Do not confuse event-driven architecture (internal architecture) with realtime client delivery (transport). \"WebSocket vs event-driven\" compares two different, complementary layers. prefer the simplest option that meets the requirement — push notifications or SSE cost less to operate than WebSocket WebSocket and SSE both maintain stateful connections. Scaling depends on browser/client, negotiated HTTP version, proxy/LB/provider connection and idle limits; HTTP/1.1 browser limits and HTTP/2 multiplexing are different, so do not assume a universal six-connection cap prefer a shared connection registry + pub/sub or replayable event log over stickiness when failover matters; if affinity is used, test reconnect and drain behavior the LB must support long timeouts and disable buffering use heartbeat/ping-pong to detect dead connections; support reconnect + resume (Last-Event-ID for SSE) OTA: push \"price/seat changed\" while the user is on the selection screen (SSE is sufficient), stream booking status in realtime, and use WebSocket for customer-support chat. Backpressure must prevent a slow client from taking down the gateway.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q1"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q2","surface":"track","en":"How does event-driven programming differ from conventional sequential programming in mindset and code?","bodyEn":"Sequential/imperative: call A, wait for its result, then call B — the flow is linear and easy to read, but a thread remains occupied while I/O is pending Event-driven: react to events/callbacks; instead of \"waiting,\" register what should happen on completion → a small number of threads can handle many operations, but the flow is split across handlers, making ordering and failures harder to reason about (callback hell, lost stack traces) Core tradeoff: event-driven programming trades straightforward sequential readability for resource efficiency under I/O-heavy workloads. With Java 21 virtual threads, teams can regain sequential code while scaling I/O. That changes the cost of waiting; it does not replace event-driven delivery semantics, replay, or decoupling. Synchronous and blocking are separate axes. A synchronous HTTP endpoint may wait for the final result while its I/O chain stays non-blocking; an asynchronous consumer may still block on JDBC. Event-driven describes coordination around events, not a guarantee that every handler is non-blocking. An event records a fact (OrderCreated); a command asks an owner to act (ReserveInventory). Kafka and RabbitMQ can carry either. A broker can add a durable/asynchronous boundary when configured for it, but delivery is not business completion. HTTP request → validate + local transaction/outbox → broker broker → consumer group/queue → side effect → offset/ack 202 Accepted means the command was accepted, not that every consumer has finished. Expose a status/read model for eventual completion, and commit the Kafka offset or acknowledge the RabbitMQ message only after the idempotent side effect succeeds. Key takeaway Event-driven programming excels when you need backpressure, streaming, or decoupling. If the only goal is \"more concurrent I/O with fewer threads,\" virtual threads provide a simpler alternative, but they do not provide broker durability, replay, or a business workflow by themselves. losing linear flow makes \"what happens after what\" harder to reason about (logic is distributed across consumers); debugging requires distributed tracing with trace context propagated in events, plus acceptance of eventual consistency and idempotent design in return: decoupling (add a consumer without changing the producer), resilience (the broker buffers work), independent scaling, and auditability (event log) for Kafka/RabbitMQ consumers, threading is a separate decision: a Spring listener can use platform or virtual threads, while a Vert.x handler normally arrives on an event loop. An async delivery callback can still run blocking JDBC; isolate it or use a blocking-friendly executor OTA: use events so that after PAID, ticketing + loyalty + notification + analytics react independently (real decoupling); keep the synchronous flow inside one request (validate→hold→charge) sequential, using virtual threads where appropriate. Do not turn everything into events (over-engineering).","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q2"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do the I/O models of Spring MVC, WebFlux, Vert.x, and Go compare?","bodyEn":"Spring MVC / Spring Boot (Servlet): a request normally occupies a platform thread while blocking I/O waits. It is simple and easy to debug, but threads are exhausted when I/O is slow or highly concurrent WebFlux (Reactor): a small Netty event-loop set; non-blocking I/O + backpressure. It delivers high throughput with little RAM, but reactive code is harder and one accidental blocking call stalls the entire loop Vert.x: also event-loop based (verticles), with the golden rule \"do not block the event loop\"; a worker pool handles blocking work; Java 21+ also offers virtual-thread verticles. It is lightweight and low-latency Go: M:N goroutines let you write natural blocking-style code while the runtime yields on I/O; channels coordinate work. Goroutines use little RAM and start quickly Spring Boot is packaging and auto-configuration, not a fourth I/O model. With a target such as Java 21+ and Spring Boot 3.2+, spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true can make Tomcat/Jetty request handling use virtual threads; the property and container support are version/configuration-specific, so verify effective settings. This is not a migration to WebFlux. Comparison card before claiming scale: record runtime/framework version, OS/container CPU quota, active connections/streams, payload and serialization size, blocking-call share, downstream pool limits, p50/p99/p999, RSS/GC, and the benchmark/fault method. Key takeaway There is no universal winner: MVC + virtual threads for imperative request-response code and blocking JDBC/JPA when dependency limits are understood WebFlux for end-to-end reactive streams and backpressure Vert.x for explicit event-loop, multi-reactor, or polyglot toolkit control Every model still needs bounds at the database, HTTP client, broker, and partner API. the Servlet platform-thread pool is configurable, not a universal 200-thread contract; its practical limit is how many requests may occupy workers while waiting. A slow downstream consumes that capacity until timeout or rejection an event loop uses few threads, but one accidental blocking call is catastrophic (it stalls the loop → total throughput collapses) — the most common WebFlux/Node failure mode virtual threads store and restore continuations with growable heap-backed stacks. They reduce the cost of I/O waiting, but do not make CPU-bound work faster or create more database connections; watch for pinning/native calls and heavy ThreadLocal use for messaging, Kafka consumer parallelism is bounded by partitions and group assignments; RabbitMQ delivery is bounded by consumers, queue topology, prefetch, and acknowledgements. Switching to virtual threads or an event loop changes waiting cost, not broker or downstream capacity Choose by workload and dependency rather than a calendar trend: MVC/virtual threads for imperative blocking I/O, reactive for a true stream/backpressure contract, and Vert.x when explicit event-loop control is part of the architecture. protect downstream systems (DB/PSP) with pools/semaphores — the concurrency model does NOT limit downstream load by itself.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q3"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q1","surface":"track","en":"How does multithreading work in Spring Boot, from an incoming request to the thread pools?","bodyEn":"In the conventional Spring MVC model, the embedded servlet container accepts a connection, parses HTTP, and dispatches a request to a worker. One platform worker normally stays associated with the synchronous filter → controller → service path until the response completes. Exact connector, worker, accept-count, and queue defaults depend on the Spring Boot and container version, so inspect the effective configuration rather than memorizing “200 threads.” Other concurrency domains are independent: @Async uses a TaskExecutor; its bounds and rejection policy come from the actual Boot auto-configuration or application bean. @Scheduled needs an explicit overlap/concurrency policy for long jobs. CompletableFuture uses the executor supplied to each stage, otherwise often the common pool. HikariCP bounds database connections, which are usually scarcer than request threads. Spring Boot 3.2+ can enable virtual threads with spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true. This changes the cost of a waiting request, not the capacity of the database or a partner API. Protect resource hold time Increasing request concurrency while keeping a small DB pool moves waiting to getConnection(). A worse failure mode is calling a slow remote service inside a transaction: the request holds a connection while waiting on unrelated I/O. Keep transactions short, set deadlines at every remote boundary, and reject or shed excess work before queues exceed the SLO. Build a capacity map instead of tuning one pool in isolation: request admission → servlet/virtual thread → executor handoff → partner semaphore → Hikari connection → database worker. For each boundary record active, queued, rejected/timed-out, service time, and configured maximum. The smallest effective capacity plus its hold time controls throughput. Thread handoffs can lose MDC, tracing, security, and cancellation unless context is propagated deliberately. TaskDecorator can help for Spring executors; OpenTelemetry instrumentation and framework-specific security context strategies should be tested end to end. On JDK 21–23, blocking under synchronized could pin a virtual-thread carrier. JDK 24 removed monitor-related pinning; native/foreign calls can still hold carriers. Regardless of JDK, bound downstream concurrency and observe pending connections plus request latency. For within-request fan-out, compare CompletableFuture in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q1) with virtual threads and structured concurrency in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4). Runnable JDK 21+ proof that virtual threads still need a downstream bulkhead: import java.util.concurrent.; import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger; import java.util.stream.IntStream; public class BoundedIo { static final Semaphore SLOTS = new Semaphore(32); static final AtomicInteger ACTIVE = new AtomicInteger(), PEAK = new AtomicInteger(); static void call() { boolean acquired = false; try { acquired = SLOTS.tryAcquire(50, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS); if (!acquired) return; // map to controlled overload int n = ACTIVE.incrementAndGet(); PEAK.accumulateAndGet(n, Math::max); Thread.sleep(20); // stand-in for JDBC/HTTP } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); } finally { if (acquired) { ACTIVE.decrementAndGet(); SLOTS.release(); } } } public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception { try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) { var jobs = IntStream.range(0, 1000).mapToObj(i -> executor.submit(BoundedIo::call)).toList(); for (var job : jobs) job.get(); } System.out.println(\"peak downstream concurrency=\" + PEAK.get()); // never above 32 } } Impact: a thousand cheap request threads cannot turn into a thousand simultaneous database calls. Trade-off: the bulkhead rejects or delays work; 32 is illustrative, not a default. Sizing rule: derive it from measured downstream capacity, expose wait/rejection metrics and bound acquisition by the request deadline.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q1"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q2","surface":"track","en":"In network programming, how do TCP and UDP, blocking and non-blocking I/O, and the C10K problem relate?","bodyEn":"TCP UDP Reliability ordered, reliable delivery no delivery guarantee Connection three-way handshake connectionless Used for HTTP/DB DNS, streaming, QUIC Blocking I/O (one thread per connection): simple, but idle connections consume waiting threads → the historical C10K problem. C10K is a resource-model explanation, not a capacity target. Non-blocking I/O / I/O multiplexing: one thread monitors many sockets through epoll (Linux)/kqueue; Java NIO exposes Selector. This underpins Netty/event loops, but actual connection capacity still depends on payload, event rate, TLS, CPU, memory, FD and downstream work That is the technical reason event loops are efficient: epoll can \"wait on many sockets at once\" without assigning one thread to each socket. The same server implemented with two I/O models: // Blocking: one waiting thread per connection — N idle connections mean N waiting threads in this model ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket( 8080 ); while ( true ) { Socket s = server. accept (); // blocks until a client connects pool. submit (() -> handle (s)); // thread stays occupied even while the client is idle } // Non-blocking: one event-loop thread can monitor many sockets via epoll/kqueue; measure the actual limit Selector sel = Selector. open (); channel. configureBlocking ( false ); channel. register (sel, SelectionKey.OP_READ); while ( true ) { sel. select (); // blocks ONCE for all sockets for (SelectionKey k : sel. selectedKeys ()) { if (k. isReadable ()) read ((SocketChannel) k. channel ()); } } epoll (Linux) uses a readiness model (reports which sockets are ready) — one thread in epoll_wait can monitor many sockets → the foundation of Netty/Nginx/event loops C10K names the historical thread-per-idle-connection pressure; edge-triggered versus level-triggered behavior and the measured workload still matter io_uring (newer) provides true asynchronous I/O and reduces syscalls. Java NIO Selector wraps epoll/kqueue TCP: three-way handshake + slow start + Nagle (disable with TCP_NODELAY for low latency) + congestion control; UDP applications must implement reliability themselves when required (QUIC does so in user space) Understanding epoll explains how event-loop servers can keep many idle sockets off a thread per connection. It does not predict capacity: measure active events, payload, TLS, CPU, memory, FD limits and downstream work. Zero-copy (sendfile) avoids copies between kernel↔user space (Kafka). Ephemeral port exhaustion from missing connection pooling is a common failure at scale.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q2"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q3","surface":"track","en":"Why are keep-alive and connection pooling critical in microservices?","bodyEn":"Opening a new TCP+TLS connection for every request is expensive (handshake, slow start). Keep-alive reuses a connection across multiple requests clients calling other services should use a connection pool (HTTP client pool, gRPC channel) → avoids ephemeral-port exhaustion and reduces latency Configure maximum connections per route and idle timeout; keep [[o:client idle Layer Failure signal Validation owner Client pool acquisition wait, stale reuse, ephemeral-port/FD pressure client team LB/proxy idle close, 502/504, protocol downgrade platform/edge team Server keep-alive or request-timeout mismatch service team NAT/OS conntrack expiry, retransmits, port exhaustion network/platform team DB/provider pool wait, server timeout, quota/throttle dependency owner OTA: services make frequent GDS/PSP calls, so correct pooling + keep-alive + timeouts determine p99 latency; validate the complete client → LB → server → NAT → DB/provider contract rather than tuning one idle value.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q3"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q4","surface":"track","en":"Why is gRPC over HTTP/2 efficient for internal service communication?","bodyEn":"gRPC leverages HTTP/2: multiplexes many RPCs over one connection (no new connection per call) compressed headers and four streaming modes compact binary Protobuf payloads + generated stubs from .proto files (contract-first, versioned through field numbers) It is a candidate for low-latency, high-throughput service-to-service communication when a matched benchmark supports the choice. Measure payload size, serialization CPU, batching, connection reuse, proxy/TLS cost, stream concurrency, flow control and failure behavior; Protobuf and HTTP/2 do not guarantee faster results. Limitations: browsers cannot call it directly without gRPC-Web/a proxy, and binary payloads are harder to inspect manually → a common pattern is REST at the edge and gRPC internally. gRPC uses HTTP/2 flow control for streaming backpressure and supports deadline propagation (the client sets a deadline → it propagates across every hop → downstream services can stop unnecessary work once the deadline expires) Protobuf remains forward/backward-compatible through field numbers (adding optional fields is safe; never reuse a deleted field number); interceptors handle authentication, logging, metrics, and retries Load-balancing pitfall: a long-lived HTTP/2 connection is pinned to one backend → use an L7/lookaside LB/service mesh, or client-side round-robin across multiple subchannels; debug with grpcurl/reflection OTA: use internal gRPC between search/pricing/inventory/booking services (low latency, strict contracts, and deadline propagation for a time-budgeted flight search), with REST/GraphQL at the edge. Do not expose gRPC publicly to partners unfamiliar with it (REST is easier to integrate).","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q4"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q5","surface":"track","en":"How should you combine idempotency, retries, timeouts, backoff, and circuit breakers at the network boundary?","bodyEn":"Networks fail, so design for failure: set reasonable timeouts for both connect and read retry only idempotent operations, with exponential backoff + jitter (to avoid retry storms) use a circuit breaker to stop calling a failing service (CLOSED→OPEN→HALF_OPEN) use a bulkhead (separate pools per dependency) so one slow downstream cannot consume every thread Important: retrying a non-idempotent operation = duplicate effects (double charge) → every outbound command (PSP/GDS) must carry an idempotency key. Deadline waterfall: caller total budget > edge/LB budget > service budget > dependency-acquisition/connect/read budget > DB/provider operation budget. Each inner budget must leave margin for cleanup and response propagation; no layer may silently reset the absolute deadline. Outcome Safe action Unsafe shortcut Before bytes are sent Retry only a classified transient failure within the remaining budget Retry every network exception Request may have reached the server Reuse the idempotency key and query status; preserve UNKNOWN Create a new mutation key after timeout Non-replayable body One attempt or a resumable part/session protocol Blindly replay the stream Safe read-only/replay-safe effect Bounded retry with jitter and an attempt budget Unbounded retries or multiple retry owners Use Topic 17 for the HTTP idempotency-key/status-resource contract; use Topic 09 for PSP/GDS unknown and reconciliation ownership. timeouts (connect + read) should be shorter than the upstream timeout so failures surface quickly retry only idempotent work, with backoff + jitter being essential (without jitter, every client retries together and creates a thundering herd) cap attempts and enforce a retry budget (do not retry while the downstream is collapsing) circuit breaker (CLOSED→OPEN when failures exceed a threshold→HALF_OPEN) can protect one call boundary if its state, probe budget, fallback and recovery metrics are scoped; it cannot decide whether an already-sent mutation committed pair it with a bulkhead (isolated pools/semaphores per dependency) Fintech/OTA: retrying NON-idempotent work = double charge/double issue → every outbound PSP/GDS command must carry an idempotency key. Combine all four patterns with resilience4j. Bottom line: \"networks fail — design so failures do not propagate & retries do not duplicate side effects.\"","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q5"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q6","surface":"track","en":"What is the end-to-end lifecycle of an HTTP request, from DNS resolution until the client renders the response?","bodyEn":"Use this map to answer \"where is it slow?\" — each layer has its own bottlenecks and metrics. 1 Client — DNS (browser cache → OS cache → recursive resolver) → TCP three-way handshake → TLS handshake → send request. Keep-alive lets subsequent requests skip all three setup stages 2 Edge (optional) — CDN → WAF → Load Balancer → reverse proxy/API gateway: SSL termination, path/host routing, health checks 3 Kernel — NIC (hardware interrupt/NAPI) → ring buffer & SoftIRQ → TCP stack (SYN queue, accept queue) → socket receive buffer 4 Tomcat — Acceptor accept() → Poller (Selector/epoll) → worker thread pool → Coyote HTTP11 parses the request into an HttpServletRequest 5 Spring — filter chain (doFilter) → DispatcherServlet → controller → service (@Transactional) 6 Data — HikariCP → JDBC driver → DB; Redis cache access may run in parallel 7 Response — unwind through the filter chain → socket write → TCP ACK → TLS encryption → client parse/render Only ONE worker thread is involved from step 4 through step 7 — it returns to the pool only after the response has been flushed. Any delay between those points increases thread hold time. Layer Bottleneck Metric / signal 1 Client DNS latency, TLS handshake first request is unusually slow; high CPU without keep-alive 2 Edge LB saturation 502/504 at the LB with no corresponding application log 3 Kernel accept queue full connection refused/timeout, ss -ltn Recv-Q reaches max 4 Tomcat thread pool exhausted tomcat_threads_busy = max 5 Spring large-payload serialization, GC pause high Jackson CPU; p99 spikes in step with the GC cycle 6 Data HikariCP exhausted, slow query/lock, slow Redis hikaricp_connections_pending > 0 Pitfall often misattributed to the application A full accept queue (server.tomcat.accept-count + net.core.somaxconn) → the kernel silently drops SYNs: the client sees a timeout/connection refused, but application logs are completely clean and no Spring metric reflects the failure. Inspect ss -ltn (Recv-Q/Send-Q) and netstat -s grep -i listen. NIO decouples connections from threads: configured maxConnections may be much larger than configured maxThreads → the server can retain many idle connections and consumes a worker thread only while a socket actually has data. The effective limits are container, OS, proxy and workload-specific. the practical exhaustion order follows the configured HikariCP, Tomcat-worker and accept-queue capacities plus their hold times; identify the first saturated boundary from metrics → increasing maxThreads when HikariCP is the bottleneck only lengthens the queue and worsens p99 timeouts must decrease at each layer: client > LB > Tomcat connection-timeout > HTTP client timeout > query timeout. Reversing that order stacks retries and multiplies load during an incident the client-side pool/keep-alive idle-timeout must be shorter than the LB/DB idle timeout; otherwise the client reuses a connection the peer has already closed → intermittent 502/'connection reset' errors @Transactional opens at step 5, but JSON serialization occurs at the end of step 5 (after commit) → lazy loading there throws LazyInitializationException; moreover, calling an external API while holding a DB connection turns network latency into connection hold time Java 21 virtual threads (spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true) remove the maxThreads ceiling at step 4 but do not remove the HikariCP ceiling at step 6 — concurrency to the DB/downstream must still be bounded","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q6"},{"id":"15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q7","surface":"track","en":"How do deadlines and retries behave when a request body or mutation may have partially completed?","bodyEn":"Use one absolute deadline for the caller's intent, then allocate smaller budgets for connection acquisition, connect, request headers, upload or response body, and downstream work. A timeout is not proof that the server did nothing: the peer may have accepted all bytes, committed the mutation, or failed before execution. Retry only when all three hold: the bytes can be replayed the error is classified as transient the operation has a stable idempotency key or equivalent deduplication identity For a non-replayable stream, use one attempt or a resumable protocol with a part/session identity. After an ambiguous timeout, query the operation or provider status before creating a new intent. A circuit breaker can stop additional calls; it cannot decide whether an already-sent mutation committed. Keep retry ownership at one layer, budget attempts and bytes, propagate cancellation, and record an UNKNOWN outcome for reconciliation rather than silently converting it to failure. Boundary Use q5 for the general retry composition. This item adds the large-body and partial-completion rule that prevents a retry from duplicating a side effect.","contextEn":"Networking, protocols & I/O models","href":"/topics/15-network-i-o-models#question-15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q7"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1","surface":"track","en":"Given an OTA flight-booking brief, sketch the system for search, hold, payment, and ticketing. Which assumptions, ownership boundaries, and browse-versus-commit consistency rules would you state before drawing?","bodyEn":"Present it concisely, layer by layer: Clients (web/mobile/B2B agents) → API Gateway (authentication, rate limiting, routing) bounded-context services: Search/Pricing, Inventory/Seat-Hold, Booking Orchestrator, Payment (+ ledger), Ticketing/PNR, Notification, Loyalty edge integrations: GDS/Airline (Amadeus/Sabre/NDC), PSP/bank (3DS, webhooks) data infrastructure: database per service (Postgres/MySQL), Redis (cache + seat holds), Kafka (event bus + Outbox), reconciliation jobs When drawing, emphasize consistency boundaries: the seat and money path requires strong consistency (ACID + holds + idempotency) the search/availability path accepts caching + eventual consistency Primary flow: search → hold a seat (TTL) → payment → issue the ticket (PNR) → on timeout, release the seat and reconcile. Presentation tip Start with one use case—booking a single ticket—and trace it end to end before expanding into scale and edge cases. Do not present a disconnected collection of boxes; tell the story of one request. be prepared to explain why the services are split this way (boundaries follow bounded contexts and consistency boundaries, not technical layers), plus each component's bottlenecks and scaling strategy be ready for follow-ups: 'What if the GDS is slow or unavailable?' (circuit breaker + cache + graceful degradation), 'How do you prevent double-selling a seat?' (atomic conditional update), and 'What about data residency/compliance?' (regional partitioning, PII encryption) Draw one end-to-end use case first, then expand into scale. Knowing what the system DOES NOT have & why—for example, 'we have not sharded because a single primary is still sufficient'—demonstrates depth & pragmatism. Name the authorities before naming technologies: search is a projection the quote is a time-bounded commercial contract the local hold store is an admission authority the GDS/airline is the external inventory authority the PSP owns the payment outcome State the assumptions: the key as a segment/flight/cabin/seat (or SKU) identity, the quote and hold TTL, the peak search-to-book ratio, supplier p99, and whether controlled overbooking is allowed. For one booking, draw QUOTED -> HELD -> BOOKING -> PAYMENT_PENDING/UNKNOWN -> TICKETED, with EXPIRED, RELEASED, and MANUAL_REVIEW exits. A stale browse result may end in REPRICE or SOLD_OUT; it must never silently allocate inventory. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: Which supplier or GDS contract the example assumes for hold expiry, booking idempotency and late ticketing success — the external authority decides most of the state machine. Whether controlled overbooking is a permitted product policy, and which compensation or re-accommodation authority owns that risk. The target search-to-book ratio, supplier p99, hold duration, connection count and WebSocket offline retention — the numbers behind every pool and queue size. The acceptable limiter failure mode: fail closed, bounded regional overshoot, or a degraded local budget. And the Java target, because it changes the wording on virtual threads, structured concurrency and pinning. What falsifies conditional writes over a long-held lock: a supplier API that requires a lease protocol, or a measured transaction pattern that needs multi-row atomicity. What falsifies \"WebSocket plus status cursor\": a product contract with no offline or replay requirement and a verified loss-tolerant notification path.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/system-design/ota-flight-booking#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2","surface":"track","en":"Two users race to reserve the same flight seat. Design the hold state machine, database invariant, expiry behavior, idempotency, payment-after-expiry path, and supplier reconciliation that prevent double booking.","bodyEn":"A seat hold is a lease on authoritative inventory, not a promise that a background cleanup job can safely reopen at any time. Acquire: atomically change one authoritative seat or inventory record from FREE to HELD only when it is still free or its prior lease is expired according to the database clock. Store a generated hold ID, lease version or fencing token, expiry, actor, and idempotency key. Confirm: require the same hold ID and version, and require the lease to be unexpired. First move HELD to a non-expirable CONFIRMING state with a conditional database update; then durably record the supplier/payment intent and dispatch it outside the database transaction. an expiry worker may release only a HELD lease whose hold ID, version, and expiry still match. It must never reopen CONFIRMING, BOOKED, or CANCELLED inventory. supplier and airline inventory remain authoritative outside the OTA database. Persist an intent/outbox record, call the supplier idempotently, then query/reconcile unknown results rather than assuming a timeout failed. one authoritative seat row is often simplest. If holds are separate, use a database-specific partial unique constraint for active allocation states; do not describe a vague active_hold column as a complete concurrency solution. a payment result arriving after expiry must enter reconciliation: confirm inventory if the supplier can still prove it, otherwise void/refund according to the payment contract. Do not silently re-hold another seat. bucketizing does not reduce contention for the same seat. Use admission control, per-seat serialization, or a product-level random-seat pool when the product permits it. One atomic PostgreSQL hold claim — run the same statement from two sessions: CREATE TABLE seat_inventory( flight_id bigint, seat_no text, hold_id uuid, hold_until timestamptz, version bigint NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, PRIMARY KEY(flight_id, seat_no) ); INSERT INTO seat_inventory(flight_id, seat_no) VALUES (7, '12A'); UPDATE seat_inventory SET hold_id='11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111', hold_until=clock_timestamp()+interval '5 minutes', version=version+1 WHERE flight_id=7 AND seat_no='12A' AND (hold_id IS NULL OR hold_until < clock_timestamp()) RETURNING hold_id, hold_until, version; -- first contender returns one row; the second waits, rechecks, then returns zero The row and predicate—not a prior SELECT—decide the winner. Benefit: the database invariant survives two application instances. Cost: a hot seat serializes on one row and expiry needs a durable state/reconciliation rule. Late success rule: transition only the matching unexpired hold; never resurrect whichever hold currently occupies the seat. The state machine must make expiry and stale workers safe: AVAILABLE -> HELD -> CONFIRMING -> BOOKED, with explicit EXPIRED, RELEASE_PENDING, and UNKNOWN states. Three rules keep it safe: Accept a transition only when the authoritative record still matches (resource_key, version, hold_id), and increment an integer version on every transition. A TTL lease is a liveness mechanism, not fencing: a paused worker can wake after expiry, so the protected authority must reject its old version/token. Reuse the same command and provider idempotency key after a timeout; keep the result UNKNOWN until inquiry or reconciliation rather than issuing a second hold or charge.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/system-design/ota-flight-booking#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3","surface":"track","en":"If an airline permits controlled overbooking, how would you model sellable inventory, policy ownership and versioning, concurrent sales, carrier reconciliation, audit, and legal or customer-experience guardrails?","bodyEn":"An OTA usually does not own the airline's overbooking policy. It should consume a carrier or supplier's versioned sellable allocation, hold it idempotently, and reconcile against the supplier's authoritative outcome. model sellable limit separately from physical seats. It can reflect cabin, fare class, channel allotment, blocked seats, holds, cancellations, and a supplier-approved overbooking allowance; it is not universally physical seats multiplied by one factor. use an atomic predicate that matches the counter: for example, increment sold only where sold is below sellable_limit, or decrement remaining only where remaining is above zero. Include a version and correlation ID for replay/reconciliation. policy has effective dates, version, channel and legal constraints. Never lower sellable capacity below already sold or protected holds without an explicit exception workflow. if more passengers arrive than physical seats, customer handling is governed by carrier policy and applicable law. The OTA should surface the policy, preserve audit evidence, and avoid inventing a universal bumping rule. treat supplier updates, cancellation releases, and OTA retries as idempotent events; stale availability must not overwrite a newer supplier version. Treat controlled overbooking as an explicit risk budget, not as permission to read a stale counter. The policy must have an owner, effective time, version, channel/fare scope, protected inventory, and a measured reaccommodation or compensation path. The admission write is still conditional and idempotent; a policy update cannot silently reduce capacity below sold or protected holds. Reconciliation must compare supplier sequence/version and distinguish SOLD, CANCELLED, PROTECTED, and UNKNOWN rather than overwriting the newest state with an older event.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/system-design/ota-flight-booking#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4","surface":"track","en":"Design a browse-to-book flow when price and availability sources are stale or slow. How do cache and freshness contracts, quote binding, final revalidation, customer messaging, and supplier failure handling preserve both speed and correctness?","bodyEn":"Browsing data is advisory; the supplier-bound quote and final inventory hold decide whether a booking can proceed. Browse path: supplier adapters may ingest feeds, webhooks, or polls and normalize them into internal versioned events. Cache and stale-while-revalidate can improve browsing only when the UI labels the result as non-binding. bind a quote to quote ID, itinerary, fare, tax, currency, rules, supplier version, expiry, and user/session where required. At checkout, reprice and request a supplier hold or confirmation using an idempotency key. only promise a price or seat guarantee when the supplier actually supports that hold/guarantee. WebSocket or SSE updates are UX signals, not a correctness mechanism. protect caches from older supplier updates overwriting newer state with versions or monotonic sequence rules. Cache invalidation is an optimization, not a substitute for final revalidation. a supplier timeout after a mutation creates PENDING or UNKNOWN, not an automatic failure. Query status, reconcile using correlation IDs, and show the user an honest pending state instead of blindly retrying a booking. decide the payment authorization/capture order together with supplier rules, hold expiry, and the recovery plan for partial outcomes. Make freshness a contract, not an implementation detail: expose availability age, quote expiry, supplier version, and the maximum time a result may be shown as non-binding. Propagate one absolute deadline and a retry budget across search, reprice, hold, and supplier calls. If a mutation times out, return PENDING/UNKNOWN with a status resource and correlation ID; do not retry with a new key. Supplier events need monotonic sequence/version checks, while the final hold remains the admission decision.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/system-design/ota-flight-booking#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5","surface":"track","en":"Design the long-running OTA booking saga from hold through payment and ticket issue. Specify durable states and timers, transaction boundaries, idempotency, retries, unknown PSP/GDS outcomes, compensation, and reconciliation.","bodyEn":"A booking saga is a durable state machine around inventory, payment, and supplier issuance; it is not a database transaction spanning a PSP or GDS. Example states: CREATED → HOLDING → HELD → PAYMENT_PENDING → AUTHORIZED → SUPPLIER_PENDING → ISSUED, with FAILED, CANCELLED, EXPIRED, UNKNOWN, and RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED paths. The exact authorization/capture sequence depends on the supplier and payment contracts. persist the business state and an outbox record atomically in the local database. A relay or CDC process publishes the outbox at least once; consumers and external calls must be idempotent. This is not an atomic database-to-Kafka handoff. do not call a PSP or GDS inside an open database transaction. Persist intent, dispatch with an idempotency/correlation key, record the outcome, and query/reconcile when the outcome is unknown. a supplier PNR, ticket issuance, and payment capture can be different pivots. define timers and compensation for each: release hold before expiry, void authorization where possible, refund after capture when issuance cannot be recovered, and preserve the customer-visible pending state while reconciling. every transition needs an owner, retry deadline, terminal condition, audit trail, and duplicate-event behavior. “Compensation” is a new business action, not a true rollback of external systems. Draw the recovery table beside the Saga: PAYMENT_UNKNOWN: inquire/reconcile with the same provider reference TICKET_UNKNOWN: query the GDS/airline before releasing or refunding late success: apply it only through an allowed versioned transition Each timer needs an owner, deadline, retry budget, terminal state, and repair queue. Compensation is a new business action that can fail or be partial, so the customer-visible state must remain pending/manual-review until the authority confirms it.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/system-design/ota-flight-booking#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q1","surface":"track","en":"When asked, 'Tell me about your most challenging project,' how do you demonstrate technical depth?","bodyEn":"Use the STAR framework, then close with metrics and lessons learned. An OTA example: Situation: under heavy load during a hot-ticket sale, the system suffered double-booking and transactions stuck when the GDS/PSP was unreliable Task: prevent selling the same seat twice, prevent double charges, and keep bookings from becoming stuck in an unknown state Action: implemented atomic seat holds (conditional update + TTL), idempotency keys for every command sent to the GDS/PSP, Outbox + Saga for hold→pay→issue, and reconciliation jobs against settlement records Result: 'double-booking fell to zero; stuck transactions dropped from X% to nearly zero through status queries + reconciliation; p99 seat-hold latency < Y ms' Bottom line Choose a project with real engineering trade-offs—consistency versus latency, build versus buy—and explain why you chose the approach and what it cost. Finish with measurable outcomes and what you learned. Do not narrate features—narrate decisions. choose a project with real engineering trade-offs, not merely 'I finished feature X'; explain why you selected one option over another and the cost you accepted, using before-and-after metrics after STAR, expect follow-ups such as 'What alternatives did you consider?', 'What happens at 10× scale?', 'What can fail?', and 'What would you do differently?' Prepare those branches include a failure or lesson learned—for example, 'we initially used a distributed lock for correctness, then realized the database had to be the arbiter.' Honesty and the ability to learn distinguish genuine seniority","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q1"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q2","surface":"track","en":"When asked, 'What is your current scope or task?' and then probed deeply, how should you prepare?","bodyEn":"Interviewers use your own project as a launchpad for testing your knowledge. Prepare a 'knowledge tree' for each area: task → technical decision → trade-off → what you would change if you rebuilt it. 'seat hold' → concurrency (conditional update, TTL, hot row), isolation levels, deadlocks 'PSP integration' → idempotency, unknown states, reconciliation, webhook security 'search optimization' → caching, stale-while-revalidate, indexes, EXPLAIN, GDS rate limits 'messaging' → RabbitMQ confirms/Kafka semantics, Outbox, idempotent consumers Note Only discuss mechanisms you genuinely understand; exaggeration will collapse under deeper probing. It is better to say, 'Another team owned that part, but I understand the underlying principle as follows...' For each task, build a three-level 'drill-down tree': mechanism (how it works under the hood) trade-offs (why you chose it and what you gave up) edge cases/failures (what can go wrong and how you handle it) For example, 'seat hold' → mechanism (conditional update + TTL) → trade-offs (versus a Redis lock or pessimistic locking) → failures (payment completes after the hold expires, a hot seat, duplicate submission). The questions will become increasingly specific and deep until they reach the boundary of your understanding; that is the point, because the interviewer is calibrating depth. Only claim understanding at the level where you truly know the mechanism. If another team owned a component, say so plainly—exaggeration will break under scrutiny and cost credibility. Honesty about the limits of your knowledge is a sign of mature seniority.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q2"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q3","surface":"track","en":"Which concurrency problems are commonly asked beyond seat holds, and how should you approach them?","bodyEn":"Start by naming the invariant and the shared mutable state; only then choose a primitive. Producer–consumer: use a bounded BlockingQueue so overload becomes visible backpressure. Consider a ring buffer such as Disruptor only when measurements justify its operational complexity. Hot counters: LongAdder often scales better under heavy write contention, while AtomicLong is preferable when callers need one exact value for compare-and-update decisions. Benchmark the actual access pattern. Read-mostly state: immutable snapshots or copy-on-write work well for small data with rare writes; StampedLock can help when optimistic reads are cheap to retry. Neither is a default. Admission control: a Semaphore caps concurrent work; it is not a requests-per-second rate limiter. Pair it with a deadline and a bounded wait/queue policy. Memoization: ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent makes the per-key insertion atomic, but the mapping function should be short, side-effect-free, and must not recursively update the same map. Task coordination: CompletableFuture remains useful for pipelines. Structured Concurrency makes task lifetime, cancellation, and failure policy explicit; in JDK 25 it is still a preview API built around StructuredTaskScope.open(...) and a Joiner, so compile and run with --enable-preview. Reasoning sequence State the correctness invariant, ownership, contention pattern, overload behavior, cancellation/deadline policy, and how the design will be tested. Then discuss throughput or latency. A fast design that violates the invariant under one interleaving is still wrong. Typical traps are check-then-act races, lost updates, unsafe publication, inconsistent lock order, and unbounded queues. Prefer an atomic database condition, CAS, or a single owner when that removes shared-memory coordination. With locks, define ordering and a timeout/recovery policy; with lock-free code, explain progress guarantees and retry cost. For an OTA fan-out, “query several providers and stop after a satisfactory successful result” needs a first-success policy, deadline propagation, cooperative cancellation, bounded concurrency, and a definition of partial success—not merely an old ShutdownOnSuccess class name. The current Java baseline and a complete structured-concurrency example live in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4). A useful follow-up is to name the exact guarantee: AtomicLong can support an exact compare-and-update for one in-process value LongAdder is appropriate for an approximate aggregate metric Semaphore limits concurrent permits rather than a time-window budget Keep blocking I/O outside computeIfAbsent, bound every executor queue, and propagate cancellation/deadline to downstream work. Version-label the answer: virtual threads are delivered in JDK 21 for high-concurrency blocking I/O, JEP 491 changes synchronized pinning behavior in JDK 24, and StructuredTaskScope is preview in JDK 25.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q3"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q4","surface":"track","en":"At scale, how do you fan out real-time updates to thousands of clients?","bodyEn":"The challenge is maintaining many long-lived WebSocket connections while pushing updates quickly. Architecture: separate a WebSocket gateway from the business service; with Netty/NIO, each thread can manage many connections publish updates through pub/sub (Redis Pub/Sub or Kafka), so every gateway node receives them and fans them out to its own clients keep a presence registry mapping user→node in Redis implement backpressure so slow clients cannot exhaust the system, rate-limit each connection, and use stickiness/affinity or the registry for routing for offline clients, fall back to push notifications Bottom line Separate the responsibility of maintaining real-time connections—the gateway, scaled by connection count—from event production in business services, and connect the two with pub/sub. This is the standard fan-out pattern for live price and seat notifications in an OTA. separate the connection layer (WebSocket gateways maintaining connections with Netty NIO; one node can hold tens to hundreds of thousands of connections) from the business layer that produces events connect them with pub/sub: Redis Pub/Sub is simple and low-latency but not durable; Kafka is durable and replayable but heavier. Maintain a user→node registry in Redis to route a message to a specific user scaling: connection state is stateful, unlike stateless HTTP, so it requires affinity or a registry apply backpressure by bounding buffers and then dropping messages or disconnecting slow clients rate-limit each connection mitigate reconnect storms after a gateway restart with jitter + tokens detect zombie connections with heartbeats In an OTA, push live price, seat, and booking-status changes. When thousands of users watch a hot flight, fan out through pub/sub; use push notifications for offline users. Measure connections per node, p99 message latency, and drop rate. A WebSocket is a live transport, not durable delivery. Persist booking-status or domain events with a cursor; on reconnect, resume from last_seen_event_id, deduplicate by event ID, and preserve ordering only within the aggregate that requires it. Bound each client queue, disconnect or downgrade slow consumers, and expose replay/polling through a status resource. Authenticate the handshake and refresh path, validate origin where applicable, avoid putting PII in trace attributes, and never use a successful send as proof that the client applied the business event.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q4"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q5","surface":"track","en":"Fundamentals for a rapid review: NoSQL vs. SQL, Redis, indexes (partitioning/sharding/composite), GC, networking, and security?","bodyEn":"These are common areas interviewers sweep through quickly. Each has a deeper question in an earlier day; the key takeaways are: SQL vs. NoSQL (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.the-big-picture.q1): choose based on relationships and consistency; use polyglot persistence where appropriate Redis (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q6): in-memory and single-threaded execution, sorted sets/TTL/Lua; Sentinel/Cluster for HA; beware blocking O(N) operations Indexes (05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q4), (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q1): partitioning (within a node) ≠ sharding (across nodes) ≠ a composite index (multiple columns); B+Tree, leftmost prefix, covering indexes GC (01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q3): generational heap, G1 versus ZGC, and signs of a leak Networking (15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q2): HTTP/1·2·3, TLS, TCP/UDP, non-blocking I/O Security (13-security-oauth2.oauth2-oidc.q3): OAuth2/OIDC, JWT, mTLS, OWASP Turn each area into one takeaway plus one trade-off: SQL vs. NoSQL: 'choose by access pattern and consistency needs; use polyglot persistence—SQL/ACID for the ledger, Redis for caching, Elasticsearch for search' Redis: 'single-threaded in-memory execution, sorted sets/TTL/atomic Lua; Cluster for HA; beware blocking O(N) commands' Indexes: 'partitioning ≠ sharding ≠ composite indexes; B+Tree's high fan-out keeps the tree shallow; respect the leftmost prefix; covering indexes avoid table lookups' GC: 'generational collection; G1 by default versus ZGC pauses < 1 ms; suspect a leak when old-generation usage climbs and does not fall after a Full GC' Networking: 'HTTP/3 over QUIC removes TCP-layer head-of-line blocking; epoll solves C10K; pooling and keep-alive protect p99 latency' Security: 'OAuth2 is authorization and OIDC is authentication; use short-lived JWTs + refresh-token rotation; prevent IDOR and apply least privilege; use zero trust + mTLS' Prepare one concrete example from the OTA project for each area, plus one trap or edge case, rather than reciting theory. For a rapid review, force every technology into the same five-column check: source of truth, consistency needed, partition/index key, capacity limit, and recovery test. SQL is usually the admission authority for a multi-field invariant; Redis can provide a fast cache or atomic coordination primitive but is not automatically the money or inventory ledger. Ask which replica may be stale, which region owns a write, what happens during a partition, and which metric proves the chosen trade-off.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q5"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q6","surface":"track","en":"How do you write a design doc or ADR that actually gets a decision made — and how does that become an interview answer?","bodyEn":"Senior work is judged on decisions, and a decision nobody wrote down is one the team relitigates every quarter. The document is the deliverable, not the meeting. Section What it must contain Failure mode Context the forces: load, deadline, team, existing systems starting from the solution Goals / non-goals what this explicitly does not solve scope creep in review Options 2-3 real candidates, each with a cost one option plus two strawmen Decision the choice and the reason it wins here \"best practice\" as the reason Consequences what becomes harder, what you accept only upsides listed Migration & rollback how to get there and back a design that only works greenfield The consequences section is what separates a senior document from a proposal. Anything can look good with its costs omitted; naming the cost you accept is the argument (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q5). Make it reviewable. State the decision in the first paragraph, keep the doc to what a reviewer needs, and mark the open questions explicitly so people know where their input is wanted. A reader who cannot find the decision will comment on formatting instead. An ADR is small on purpose One decision, one page, immutable once accepted. Superseding it means writing a new one that references the old — the history of why is more valuable than a tidy current-state document (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q7). write it before the work when the decision is expensive to reverse — schema, contract, boundary — and after the spike when the point was to learn something. the interview version is the same structure compressed: situation and constraint, the options you considered, why you picked one, what it cost you, and what you measured afterwards (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q1). interviewers listen for the rejected option. \"We chose Kafka\" says nothing; \"we chose Kafka over a database queue because we needed replay for three consumers, and accepted running a cluster\" says you made a decision. record the decision even when you take the shortcut deliberately. An undocumented shortcut is indistinguishable from ignorance six months later. a doc that no one disagrees with was probably written after the code was merged; circulate it while the answer can still change. A decision record is complete only when a future reader can falsify it: write the invariant, workload envelope, alternatives rejected, failure matrix, owner, rollout/rollback trigger, and the metric that would reopen the decision. For an OTA example, “use conditional hold plus a durable workflow” is incomplete until it names the hold conflict rate, unknown supplier age, expiry lag, and the test that pauses a worker after lease expiry.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q6"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q7","surface":"track","en":"How do you review other people's code and argue for engineering investment without becoming the person everyone routes around?","bodyEn":"A review has a budget of attention. Spend it in order of what actually costs the company money, and say clearly which comments are blocking. 1 correctness does it do the thing, including the failure paths? 2 security authorisation per object, injection, what gets logged 3 operability timeouts, retries, idempotency, what an alert would say 4 clarity will the next reader understand it at 2am? 5 style formatter's job, not yours Label the intent of every comment. must: blocks the merge, should: is a real suggestion, nit: is optional and the author may ignore it. Unlabelled reviews make a formatting preference look like a defect, which is how reviews turn adversarial. Ask instead of assert when you are unsure: \"what happens if this retries?\" invites a fix; \"this is wrong\" invites a defence. When you and the author disagree twice, stop typing and talk — the third comment never resolves it. Arguing for investment. Nobody funds \"the code is ugly\". The same work funds easily when it is framed as risk and cost: \"we should refactor the payment module\" & 8594; \"three of the last five incidents came from this module; a week of work removes the class of bug\" \"we need more tests\" & 8594; \"we cannot release on Fridays because nothing verifies the settlement path\" \"the library is old\" & 8594; \"we are two majors behind; the next security advisory becomes a migration, not a patch\" What review is really for Catching defects is the smaller half. The larger half is that two people now understand the change, and the author learns something they will apply without being asked next time. review the diff against the design, not just the lines. A perfectly written change to the wrong boundary is more expensive than a typo, and only a reviewer who read the design will see it. big reviews get rubber-stamped. If a pull request is a thousand lines, the useful comment is \"this needs splitting\", and that comment is worth more than fifty inline notes. some rules should not be human work at all: formatting, dependency direction, coverage floors and forbidden APIs belong in CI (26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q5). carry incident findings back into the review checklist. A postmortem action item that never becomes an automated check or a review habit will be repeated (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q3). as the senior in the room, be explicit when you are overruled and move on. Documenting the disagreement in the ADR and supporting the decision keeps you someone people bring problems to. A practical review order: data loss or invariant violation security/authorization timeout/retry/idempotency resource exhaustion observability style Each blocking comment should include a concrete failure interleaving and a small fix or test. If the risk is uncertain, label it as a question, add a metric or experiment, and avoid turning personal taste into a blocker.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q7"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1","surface":"track","en":"Whiteboard exercise: a gateway must enforce per-tenant and per-endpoint limits across 50 stateless instances. How would you clarify requirements, choose an algorithm, model atomic distributed state, handle failures, and explain the trade-offs?","bodyEn":"Answer framework: Clarify: what is the limit keyed by (user/IP/API key)? Per second or per minute? Are bursts allowed? Is this single-node or distributed? Choose an algorithm: token bucket is common and allows bursts; a sliding-window counter is more precise and smooths window boundaries Single node: store (tokens, last_refill) in a map and refill lazily Distributed: keep state in Redis and use Lua to atomically calculate the refill and consume a token, avoiding a racy GET-then-SET sequence API: return 429 + Retry-After + X-RateLimit-Remaining Edge cases/SPOF: if Redis fails, choose fail-open or fail-closed based on risk; enforce limits at multiple layers (CDN/gateway/service) token bucket (lazy refill) now=time(); tokens=min(B, tokens+(now-last)r); last=now if tokens>=1: tokens-=1; return ALLOW return DENY Bottom line The scoring hinges on articulating the trade-offs—bursty versus smooth behavior, precision versus cost, fail-open versus fail-closed—and guaranteeing atomicity in the distributed design, not on producing runnable code. spend the first minute clarifying requirements: keyed by what, which window, whether bursts are allowed, single-node or distributed, required precision, and fail-open or fail-closed. This distinguishes senior reasoning; do not jump straight into code present and compare algorithms—token bucket for bursts versus the more precise and inexpensive sliding-window counter—then choose one for a stated reason for the distributed design, emphasize atomicity: a Redis Lua script calculates the refill and consumes a token in one operation, because GET-then-SET races cover HA/SPOF behavior (if Redis is down, authentication fails closed while search can fail open) latency (a local bucket plus synchronization) multiple enforcement layers Interviewers often ask, 'How is it exact across N instances?', 'What about clock skew?', 'Sliding-window log versus counter?', or 'Can limits be weighted by cost?' The scoring is based on reasoning, trade-offs, and correctness under concurrency—NOT runnable code. The same exercise appears in an LLD round but is scored very differently: implement token bucket and sliding window, decide which class owns state, and explain synchronization (22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q3). With 50 instances, first decide which guarantee is required: an exact global budget needs one authoritative atomic decision point, and accepts its latency/failure coupling a regional or cell budget trades exactness for availability and must state its bounded overshoot a local bucket is only per-instance unless requests are partitioned consistently Define the clock/refill rule, Redis fail-open or fail-closed behavior, Retry-After, weighted cost, hot-key handling, and reconciliation before presenting Lua.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/system-design/distributed-rate-limiter#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q2","surface":"track","en":"Whiteboard DSA: how does a senior engineer approach a problem beyond merely producing the answer?","bodyEn":"A process that scores well on any problem: Clarify the input, output, and constraints: how large is n, are duplicates possible, is the data sorted, and what are the memory limits? Give an example and edge cases before coding: empty input, one element, duplicates, negative values, overflow, sorted or reverse-sorted input State the brute-force approach and its complexity briefly, then optimize by changing the data structure or traversal Analyze Big-O time and space Write clean code with clear names Self-test using the example and edge cases already identified Pattern catalog: HashMap (O(1) counting/lookup) two pointers (sorted arrays or symmetry) sliding window (substrings/subarrays and sums) binary search (sorted search space or a monotonic answer) stack (parentheses or monotonic structures) BFS/DFS (graphs, trees, grids) heap (top-K) DP (overlapping subproblems) prefix sum Bottom line Senior candidates are evaluated on how they think—clarification, edge cases, complexity trade-offs—how they communicate while solving, and how they test, not only on whether they reach the correct answer. Process skills: communicate continuously by thinking aloud, clarify before coding, raise edge cases proactively, move from brute force to an optimized approach with Big-O analysis, write clean code, and self-test. Pattern-to-signal mapping: 'pair/sum in a sorted array' → two pointers; 'optimal substring/subarray window' → sliding window; 'counting, fast lookup, or duplicates' → HashMap/Set 'sorted search space or monotonic answer' → binary search, including binary search on the answer; 'parentheses, undo, or monotonic order' → stack 'paths, propagation, or connectivity' → BFS/DFS + visited; 'top-K' → heap; 'overlapping subproblems or counting ways' → DP with state, transition, and base case A senior engineer is expected to recognize patterns quickly, analyze trade-offs such as time versus space, and recover when stuck by returning to brute force, testing a small example, or asking for a hint. Calm, systematic progress matters more than immediately producing the optimal solution. The six-step framework for verbalizing this process, including phrasing for each step, appears in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q1). After the algorithm, state the proof obligation: what invariant is preserved, what state is owned by whom, and why every mutation is atomic or version-checked. Then give complexity, memory, and a deterministic test for empty, maximum, duplicate, overflow, concurrent, and cancellation cases. In a distributed answer also test delayed responses, retries with the same command ID, partition behavior, and recovery after the process owning the work dies.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q2"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q3","surface":"track","en":"Which edge cases should you proactively raise when solving an algorithm problem?","bodyEn":"Empty / null: an empty array or string, or null input One or two elements: boundary conditions for a two-pointer or window algorithm Duplicates: identical elements or duplicate keys Already sorted / reverse-sorted / all equal: including quicksort's worst case Negative numbers / zero / overflow: addition or multiplication can overflow, so use long, or compute mid=lo+(hi-lo)/2 Very large n: is O(n²) acceptable, and what is the memory budget? Not found / multiple answers: what should be returned—an index or a value? Unicode/whitespace for string problems Raising edge cases before coding demonstrates defensive reasoning, which matters in fintech and booking systems where a defect can lose money. identify edge cases before coding to demonstrate defensive thinking: one missed case can lose money or double-sell a seat, and early analysis helps produce a correct design from the start Extend the checklist by problem type: Arrays: empty, one element, duplicates, sorted, or reverse-sorted Numbers: negative, zero, overflow with lo+(hi-lo)/2, or division by zero Search: not found or multiple valid answers Graphs: cycles, disconnected components, or self-loops Concurrency: races, ordering, or idempotency Strings: Unicode, whitespace, or case rules distinguish an edge case—a rare but valid input that must be handled—from invalid input, which should be validated or rejected. Asking 'Does the problem guarantee valid input?' is a valuable clarification Apply the same checklist to APIs and business logic: nulls, duplicates, races, monetary overflow, and timeouts. This is a habit of strong backend engineers, not a whiteboard-only skill. The six-step coding framework shows exactly where to raise edge cases (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q1). For concurrency problems, add the edges that ordinary examples hide: two first claims at the same instant a retry after an ambiguous timeout a delayed old event clock skew around expiry a worker pause after lease loss a duplicate callback a hot partition, and a full queue Ask what is monotonic, what may be UNKNOWN, and which repair path makes the outcome observable rather than silently dropping it.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q3"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q4","surface":"track","en":"Which questions can you ask the interviewer to demonstrate depth, particularly in an OTA or fintech domain?","bodyEn":"How does the system handle double-booking and overbooking? How does it reconcile with airlines and PSPs? What path does a booking take through the system, and how do you preserve consistency between money and seats? In the GDS/NDC integration, where do you handle unknown states and idempotency? How is the system observed through tracing, logging, and alerts? What are the on-call and postmortem processes, and is the culture focused on learning or blame? How does the team balance feature delivery against technical debt and refactoring? How do compliance constraints affect the design? Bottom line Strong questions both help you evaluate the team and system and show that you think like someone who will operate them over the long term—the perspective expected of a senior engineer. The purpose is twofold: evaluate the team and system while demonstrating depth and long-term operational thinking. Group the questions: technical/domain: double-booking and overbooking, money-seat consistency, ambiguous GDS states, and where idempotency is enforced—evidence that you understand the real problem operations: observability, on-call, blameless postmortems, SLOs, and alert fatigue—evidence that you care about production engineering/organization: feature delivery versus technical debt, who makes architectural decisions, how compliance affects design, and where service boundaries lie Avoid superficial questions such as 'Is there overtime?' during the technical round. A strong question is: 'When a transaction becomes stuck in an unknown state at 2 a.m., how does the team detect and resolve it?' It connects the domain to operations and shows that you understand the real pain of money-moving systems. Strong follow-ups include: Who is authoritative for seat, price, payment, and ticket? Is the browse result binding? Which command/provider keys survive retries? What happens after a lease expires? Can a callback arrive after cancellation? Which side owns compensation? What exact consistency is needed for bank/fintech versus OTA? The best answer names the customer-visible REPRICE, PENDING, UNKNOWN, SOLD_OUT, and MANUAL_REVIEW outcomes instead of hiding every conflict behind a 500.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q4"},{"id":"16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q5","surface":"track","en":"Your boss asks you to count the number of trees on Earth. How would you do it?","bodyEn":"This is a Fermi estimation problem, not a test of whether you remember one magic number. The evaluation is about how you clarify an underspecified problem, decompose it into measurable parts, state assumptions, and communicate uncertainty honestly. Opening line in the interview Before solving it, I want to clarify what count means and what accuracy the business actually needs. 1. Clarify the requirement first I would ask the interviewer: What counts as a tree: mature trees, saplings, planted trees, or every stem above an operational threshold? Are we counting only land, or urban and agricultural trees as well? Do we need an exact count, or is an estimate within roughly ±10–20% sufficient? Is this a five-minute interview estimation, or a real project for which I can build a system? May I use satellite imagery, forestry datasets, and public datasets? The key principle is do not solve an undefined problem. A false-precision number is worse than a range with explicit assumptions. 2. If this is an estimation exercise The first model is: Trees ≈ Area × TreeDensity I would not use one density for the whole planet: the Sahara, the Amazon, cities, and boreal forests are fundamentally different. I would partition Earth into biome or land-use classes and sum the parts: Earth ├── Ocean → 0 trees └── Land ├── Tropical forest ├── Temperate forest ├── Boreal forest ├── Grassland / savanna ├── Agricultural land ├── Urban └── Desert / ice TotalTrees = Σ (Area_i × Density_i) A deliberately rough assumption table could be: Region Area Density Trees Tropical forests 18M km² 70k/km² 1.26T Boreal forests 12M km² 60k/km² 0.72T Temperate forests 10M km² 60k/km² 0.60T Savanna / farm / urban / desert — — ~0.3–0.5T Under these assumptions, the result is around 2.9–3.1 trillion. I would answer with an order of magnitude of about 3 trillion trees, not pretend that 3,000,000,000,000 is exact. A 2015 Nature study combined more than 400,000 ground-based tree-density measurements with spatial data and modeling, and estimated about 3.04 trillion trees. That is a useful benchmark for checking the order of magnitude; its operational definition of a tree and its method do not automatically become the interview requirement. 3. A possible interview answer First, I wouldn't try to count every tree individually. I would clarify the required accuracy and the definition of a tree. If an approximation is acceptable, I would divide the Earth's land area into biomes such as tropical forests, temperate forests, boreal forests, grasslands, deserts and urban areas. For each biome, I would estimate the total area and average tree density from representative samples. The total would be the sum of area multiplied by density for each biome. I'd calculate an uncertainty range because tree density varies significantly by location. Finally, I'd validate the estimate against independent sources such as satellite imagery and forestry datasets. This is a strong Senior answer because it includes clarification → decomposition → sampling → estimation → uncertainty → validation. It naturally extends the senior whiteboard process in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q2) and the edge-case checklist in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q3). 4. If the boss really means a project For a real system, I would not try to detect every tree on the entire planet individually. I would use sampling + spatial modeling: Satellite images + forestry datasets │ ▼ Image processing / ML detection │ ▼ GIS grid: 1 km² cells │ ▼ biome + density + confidence │ ▼ GlobalTreeCount = Σ area × estimated density Partition the land surface into grid cells, for example 1 km² each. Each cell can carry data like this: record GridCell( double latitude, double longitude, Biome biome, double areaKm2, double estimatedTreeDensity, double confidence ) {} long treeCount(GridCell cell) { return Math.round(cell.areaKm2() cell.estimatedTreeDensity()); } A practical pipeline would be: Satellite imagery and land-cover data identify areas likely to contain trees. Ground plots or forestry datasets provide representative density samples. A model classifies biome and predicts density from climate, topography, and land use. Each cell returns an estimate together with a confidence or prediction interval. The global result sums the cells; uncertainty should be propagated rather than hidden behind point estimates. Validate against independent sources, especially in bias-prone areas such as cities, savanna, sparse forests, and regions with cloud cover. This is really spatial estimation, not an object-by-object counting problem. A 1 km² resolution is an engineering choice: refine it where density changes sharply and use coarser cells where the landscape is homogeneous to balance cost and accuracy. 5. What is the interviewer actually evaluating? Level Typical response Junior Google a number and answer immediately. Mid Land area × average tree density. Senior Clarify, segment, sample, estimate, quantify uncertainty, and validate. Instead of saying There are 3 trillion trees, say: Based on these assumptions, I'd estimate the order of magnitude to be around 10¹², probably roughly 2–4 trillion trees. I'd refine it with better biome-specific data. A Senior candidate should also ask about cost versus accuracy, sampling bias, confidence intervals, existing datasets, and what decision the result will support. The question is a small System Design exercise: requirement → decomposition → model → failure or bias → validation → trade-off. The strongest opening is: Before solving it, I want to clarify what count means and what accuracy the business actually needs. Then state the assumptions and do the arithmetic. Do not turn a rough estimate into false precision. Give a range such as 2–4 trillion or an order of magnitude of 10¹², and use 3.04 trillion only as a benchmark. If the interviewer changes the accuracy, time limit, or available data, your decomposition should still be reusable. This demonstrates requirement engineering before implementation: define scope, choose the right abstraction, expose uncertainty, and design a refinement path for when better data becomes available.","contextEn":"The OTA/booking project, concurrency–realtime problems & whiteboard","href":"/topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard#question-16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q5"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q1","surface":"track","en":"How should resources and URIs be modelled when moving from a use case to an API?","bodyEn":"Start with business nouns, not database tables and certainly not service methods. Naming rules: use plural nouns, lowercase letters, and hyphens: /flight-bookings use hierarchy to express ownership: /bookings/{id}/passengers do not put verbs in URIs: /getBooking and /createBooking are RPC disguised as REST limit nesting to two levels; beyond that, expose an independent resource and use query parameters A resource is not a table. /bookings may be assembled from four tables; conversely, one user table may be exposed as /users and /users/{id}/preferences, two distinct resources. A one-to-one mapping between resources and tables leaks the storage model into the public contract; a later schema change then breaks clients. resources should align with the bounded context (DDD), not the ERD identifiers in URIs should be external IDs (a UUID or booking reference such as VN8H2K), not internal auto-increment values: sequential IDs expose order volume and enable enumeration attacks (IDOR) for a many-to-many relationship, model the relationship itself as a resource (/bookings/{id}/seat-assignments) when it has its own attributes always wrap collection responses in an object ({\"data\":[...], \"next\":\"...\"}); do not return a bare array because it leaves nowhere to add metadata without breaking clients In an OTA, /flights (search-oriented, read-heavy, cacheable) should be separate from /bookings (transactional); they have different consistency requirements and rate limits.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q1"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should non-CRUD actions such as cancellation, refund, and ticket issuance be mapped to REST?","bodyEn":"Interviewers often use this question to distinguish someone who has only read tutorials from someone who has designed real APIs. There are three approaches, in order of preference: 1. Model the action as a resource — best when the action has its own lifecycle: POST /bookings/{id}/refunds → creates a Refund with an ID, status, and history. It can be queried later with GET /refunds/{rid}. 2. Change state through a sub-resource — when the operation is only a state transition: PUT /bookings/{id}/status with body {\"value\":\"CANCELLED\"}. 3. Use a controller action — the final escape hatch, using a verb deliberately: POST /bookings/{id}:cancel (Google AIP style) or /bookings/{id}/cancel. Decision rule: if an action needs an audit trail, retries, or its own state, use option 1. If it only flips a flag, use option 2. Otherwise use option 3 without apology. Option 1 wins because it answers questions that option 3 cannot: How far has the refund progressed? Who initiated it? Which retry attempt is this? money-moving actions (refund, charge, payout) should always be resources; you will inevitably need their history a POST to an action is not idempotent by default, so pair it with an Idempotency-Key (see 17.5) avoid multiplexing many actions through one endpoint with an action field in the body: doing so removes per-action authorization, rate limiting, and observability an invalid state transition should return 409 Conflict with the current state, not 400 In an OTA, POST /bookings/{id}/cancellations supports partial cancellation, such as canceling one of three passengers—something PUT .../status cannot express. This is a contract style, not a universal REST law Resource-shaped actions are preferable when the result has its own lifecycle, history, authorization, or retry identity. A deliberate controller action is still a valid compatibility choice when the domain operation is atomic and short-lived; document its status, idempotency, authorization, and error contract instead of pretending the URI alone supplies those guarantees.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q2"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q3","surface":"track","en":"PUT vs PATCH: how do JSON Merge Patch and JSON Patch differ, and when should each be used?","bodyEn":"PUT replaces the entire representation. An omitted field is deleted or reset to its default. It is idempotent. PATCH performs a partial update. PATCH itself is not guaranteed to be idempotent; that depends on the patch format. JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396) JSON Patch (RFC 6902) Content-Type application/merge-patch+json application/json-patch+json Syntax same shape as the original object, containing only fields to change array of operations: add/remove/replace/move/test Delete a field null {\"op\":\"remove\",\"path\":\"/x\"} Set a field to null cannot be expressed supported Update one array element not supported; the entire array must be sent supported, by index Idempotent yes not always ( add to an array) Choose Merge Patch for ordinary object updates when its null/delete semantics fit; choose JSON Patch when updating individual array elements, using test for optimistic concurrency, or building collaborative editing. The classic Merge Patch trap is that it cannot distinguish 'field omitted' from 'set field to null'. In Java, both {\"note\": null} and {} deserialize to note == null. Handle this by wrapping fields in an Optional-like type (JsonNullable from openapi-generator), or by reading the raw JsonNode and checking has(\"note\"). for PATCH with optimistic concurrency, require If-Match: \"<etag>\" and return 412 Precondition Failed on a mismatch; this prevents lost updates when two clients edit concurrently PUT to a resource that does not exist may create it (upsert) and return 201, but that behavior must be documented explicitly failing to validate the entire entity after applying a PATCH is a common vulnerability: each field may be valid independently while the resulting combination is invalid Patch format is a contract, not a percentage rule The “95%” choice is a teaching heuristic, not an HTTP or RFC requirement. Choose Merge Patch when object replacement and null-delete semantics fit; choose JSON Patch when operation-level array edits or test preconditions are needed. Validate the complete post-patch representation and bind If-Match to the resource version; neither patch format provides optimistic concurrency by itself.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q3"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q4","surface":"track","en":"How can bulk APIs and field selection reduce chattiness without switching to GraphQL?","bodyEn":"Use three techniques, and only after clients have demonstrated real pain rather than as speculative design: 1. Field selection — GET /bookings?fields=id,status,total reduces the payload while preserving the opportunity to use a covering index. 2. Expand / embed — GET /bookings/{id}?expand=passengers,payments collapses N+1 client round trips into one. Enforce an allowlist and a maximum depth. 3. Bulk endpoint — POST /bookings/bulk accepts an array and returns a per-item result array. A bulk endpoint should return 207 Multi-Status, or 200 with a result array containing a status for every item. Do not let one failed item fail the entire batch unless the consumer explicitly requires all-or-nothing semantics, in which case document that it is transactional. with bulk requests and Idempotency-Key, the key must be at the individual-item level, not the request level: if three items in a batch of 100 succeeded before a retry, only the remaining 97 should be processed enforce a hard batch-size limit, such as 100, and return 413 when it is exceeded; move larger batches to an asynchronous job (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q3) arbitrarily deep expand support means building a DoS vector against your own service because every expansion level multiplies the query count field selection also helps the database: fields=id,status can enable an index-only scan (see 18.5) consider GraphQL only when clients genuinely need arbitrary response shapes (04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q6), and accept its costs as well: query-depth limits, persisted queries, and N+1 resolvers Do not build all three on day one. Measure which client calls dominate, then add the appropriate capability. Bulk is a contract boundary, not an unbounded array 207 Multi-Status versus 200 with per-item results is a client contract choice; neither implies all-or-nothing behavior. Define per-item authorization, validation, retry identity, ordering, maximum size, and whether partial effects are allowed. When the work cannot fit the request deadline, accept it as a durable operation with 202 and a status resource instead of increasing the synchronous batch limit without bound.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.modelling-resources-uris.q4"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1","surface":"track","en":"How should an Idempotency-Key be designed end to end for POST requests that create bookings or payments?","bodyEn":"The problem: a client calls POST /payments and the network times out. Did the server debit the money or not? If the client retries, it may charge the customer twice. Contract: the client generates a key (UUID) for each business intent and sends it in the Idempotency-Key header. Every retry must reuse exactly the same key. On the server: INSERT the key into an idempotency_keys table protected by a unique constraint; the winner continues and a loser jumps to step 4 execute the business operation store the status, response body, and request hash in that same row for a later call with the same key, return the stored response verbatim without executing the operation again INSERT INTO idempotency_keys(key, user_id, req_hash, state) VALUES (?,?,?, 'IN_PROGRESS' ) ON CONFLICT (key) DO NOTHING ; -- 0 rows => another request is already processing it A different key with the same body is a new request, so process it normally. The same key with a different body should return 422 because it is a client bug. Three details distinguish a textbook answer from a system that has been operated in production: req_hash: without it, a client that reuses a key for a different payload receives an unrelated old result. Hash a canonicalised body and compare it. IN_PROGRESS state: if a retry arrives before the first request completes, return 409 with Retry-After; do not run it concurrently and do not block indefinitely. Retention/expiry: choose it from the business retry and late-arrival window, provider contract, storage cost, and replay policy. Keeping keys forever bloats the table; deleting them too early allows a late retry through. Persisting the key and executing the business operation must happen in the same transaction—or through an outbox—otherwise there is a window in which the money has been debited but the key has not been recorded. GET, PUT, and DELETE are already idempotent by semantics and do not need a key. POST and controller actions are the operations that need one. Stripe and Adyen document idempotency behavior in their own APIs; do not generalize that contract to VNPay or another provider without current first-party evidence. Naming the header and persistence table demonstrates operational understanding, but the provider-specific scope still belongs in the contract. Scope and expiry are part of the contract Idempotency-Key deduplicates one declared operation intent only within a scope such as tenant + endpoint + provider account. Persist a canonical request fingerprint and reject same-key/different-body reuse. COMPLETED replays the stored response. IN_PROGRESS needs a bounded lease/recovery path. Expiry must cover the documented late-arrival window; it is not a universal 24-hour or 7-day rule. A new key for the same business intent remains a domain duplicate, so pair this API control with a business identifier/unique constraint and provider status or reconciliation when the external result is unknown.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should errors be standardized with RFC 9457 Problem Details, and why should it be used?","bodyEn":"Letting every service invent its own error format is the fastest way to force clients to maintain five different parsers. RFC 9457 (which supersedes RFC 7807) standardises the contract. Content-Type: application/problem+json { \"type\" : \"https://api.acme.vn/errors/seat-unavailable\" , \"title\" : \"Seat is already held\" , \"status\" : 409 , \"detail\" : \"Seat 12A on flight VN231 was held at 10:04.\" , \"instance\" : \"/bookings/8H2K\" , \"traceId\" : \"4bf92f3577b34da6\" , // custom extension \"errors\" : [{ \"field\" : \"seatNo\" , \"code\" : \"TAKEN\" }] } type — a stable URI identifying the category of error; clients branch on it title — a short human-readable description that does not vary by occurrence status — the repeated HTTP status, convenient for logging detail — a description specific to this occurrence instance — the resource associated with the error Do not make clients parse title to choose a branch; localisation will break them. Branch on type or on a stable code field. Key interview point Say: 'The status code serves the infrastructure layer—proxies, retries, and circuit breakers—while type/code serves the business layer.' Two layers, two purposes. Never put a stack trace, table name, SQL statement, or original exception message in detail; that leaks information. Log the complete error on the server and return a traceId that the customer can provide to support. set traceId to the OpenTelemetry trace ID so a reported failure can be located in seconds for validation failures across multiple fields, use an errors[] extension instead of concatenating strings 401 vs 403: 401 = the server does not yet know who you are (missing or expired token), while 403 = your identity is known but lacks permission. Confusing them makes clients retry incorrectly. transient errors (429, 503) must include Retry-After; otherwise every client invents its own backoff, usually badly Spring Boot 3 provides ProblemDetail and @ExceptionHandler out of the box; enable spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q3","surface":"track","en":"For a long-running REST operation, when should you use 202 with polling, a webhook, or streaming?","bodyEn":"Ticket issuance, reconciliation, and report exports can take 30 seconds to several minutes. Keeping the HTTP connection open and waiting is the wrong design because of load-balancer timeouts (often 60 seconds), client retries, and occupied threads. Standard pattern — 202 with polling: POST /reports → 202 Accepted, a Location: /operations/{opId} header, and a body containing opId the client calls GET /operations/{opId} → {\"state\":\"RUNNING\",\"progress\":42} on completion → {\"state\":\"SUCCEEDED\",\"resultUrl\":\"/reports/{id}\"} or {\"state\":\"FAILED\",\"error\":{...}} Include Retry-After in polling responses so the client does not send ten requests per second. Approach Use when Trade-off 202 + polling the default; the client is a browser or mobile app latency equals the polling interval; additional requests Webhook / callback the client is a server, such as a B2B partner the client needs a public endpoint, signature verification, and idempotent handling SSE / WebSocket the UI needs real-time progress stateful connections are harder to scale through a load balancer operations is a real resource: it has an ID and state, can be retrieved with GET, and remains available after the job finishes. That is what distinguishes it from an internal job queue. POST /reports must be idempotent through Idempotency-Key (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1), otherwise every retry creates another expensive job support cancellation with DELETE /operations/{opId} or POST /operations/{opId}:cancel resultUrl should be an expiring pre-signed URL, not a permanently public link for webhooks, sign the payload with HMAC plus a timestamp to prevent replay, retry with backoff, and remember that the client must handle duplicates because at-least-once delivery is the default retain the operation record for several days after completion so a client that loses connectivity can still query it In an OTA, ticket issuance through a GDS may take 5–40 seconds: use 202 plus polling for the consumer app and a webhook for B2B partners. One job, two notification channels. 202 means durable admission, not completion Persist the operation intent and status before returning 202; after a process restart the client must still be able to query it. Define retention/expiry, terminal SUCCEEDED and FAILED states, provider UNKNOWN or reconciliation states where relevant, authorization on the status and result resources, and Retry-After or Link guidance. A response body saying “accepted” without durable status is only an optimistic acknowledgment.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q3"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you roll out deprecation and safely retire an old API version in production?","bodyEn":"(04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q4) covers choosing a versioning scheme. This is the harder part: retiring an old version without breaking consumers. Five-step process: Measure first — log api_version and client_id for every request. Without usage data, nothing may be switched off. Signal through headers — on responses from the old version itself: Deprecation: @1735689600 (RFC 9745, Unix timestamp) Sunset: Sat, 01 Nov 2026 00:00:00 GMT (RFC 8594) Link: <https://docs.acme.vn/migrate/v2>; rel=\"deprecation\" Contact the highest-volume clients directly — headers do not read anyone's email for them. Brownout — deliberately return 503 for a few minutes each day, then increase the duration. Any live consumer will surface errors and its owner will call you before the deadline rather than after it. Retire the version — return 410 Gone with Problem Details linking to the migration guide, not 404. The golden rule is that additive changes do not require a new version. Adding an optional field, a documented enum value, or an endpoint is safe when correctly written clients ignore unknown fields. A breaking change always requires a version: removing or renaming a field, changing its type, tightening validation, changing the semantics of an existing field, or changing a returned status code. Enum trap: adding a new enum value is breaking for a client whose switch has no default. Document from v1 that clients must tolerate unknown enum values. consumer-driven contract tests such as Pact catch breaking changes in CI before they reach production run no more than two versions concurrently; the appearance of a third indicates that the deprecation process has failed v2 should be a thin adapter in front of the same core domain, not a code fork; a fork duplicates every bug fix reasonable deprecation windows are 6–12 months for public APIs and 1–3 months for internal APIs Mentioning step 4, the brownout, is a strong differentiator because very few candidates do. Wire-visible lifecycle contract (RFC 9745 + RFC 8594): HTTP/1.1 200 OK Deprecation: @1798761600 Sunset: Wed, 30 Jun 2027 23:59:59 GMT Link: </docs/migrate-v2>; rel=\"deprecation\"; type=\"text/html\" after the announced sunset HTTP/1.1 410 Gone Content-Type: application/problem+json Link: </docs/migrate-v2>; rel=\"deprecation\" {\"type\":\"https://api.example.test/problems/version-retired\", \"title\":\"API version retired\",\"status\":410} Deprecation uses an epoch-seconds structured date; Sunset uses an HTTP date and cannot precede it. Benefit: clients and inventory scanners detect lifecycle state without scraping release notes. Limit: headers do not migrate anyone. Measure traffic by client, notify owners, run a reversible brownout and keep rollback until residual use is understood. Retirement is an observed migration, not a date in a document Measure traffic by client, version, tenant and operation before choosing a retirement date. Publish owners, compatibility rules, warning/deprecation signals, migration examples, a brownout or canary plan, and a rollback path; keep telemetry until the last supported client has moved. Deprecation headers and sunset dates communicate intent, but they do not replace enforcement, authorization, or a tested migration.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q5","surface":"track","en":"How should cursor pagination, HTTP caching, conditional requests, and rate-limit fields work together?","bodyEn":"Design them as one observable contract; each mechanism has a different scope. Cursor pagination: use a stable, unique total order such as (created_at, id) and keyset predicates rather than a deep offset. Make the cursor opaque and versioned; encode or server-side store the last sort tuple plus the filters/order and, when the product requires repeatable traversal, a snapshot or high-water mark. Sign or authenticate client-visible cursors, set an expiry policy, clamp page size, and return a link or next_cursor. Define what inserts, deletes, and updates between pages mean—an opaque cursor does not automatically create snapshot consistency. GET /bookings?status=CONFIRMED&limit=50&cursor=eyJ2IjoyLC4uLn0 200 OK ETag: \"page-7f42\" Cache-Control: private, max-age=30 {\"items\":[...],\"next_cursor\":\"eyJ2IjoyLC4uLn0\"} Caching and preconditions: the ETag identifies that response representation, including authorization-sensitive variants and query parameters. If-None-Match can yield 304 Not Modified; configure Cache-Control and Vary so shared caches do not cross users or representations. For a write, If-Match can protect a versioned resource and returns 412 Precondition Failed when stale. It does not replace a domain idempotency key. Rate limits: use 429 Too Many Requests and, when useful, Retry-After. As of 2026-08-01, the IETF RateLimit-Policy/RateLimit field work is still Internet-Draft draft-ietf-httpapi-ratelimit-headers-11, not an RFC. If adopted, pin the draft/version in the API contract and do not mix its structured-field semantics with legacy X-RateLimit- fields accidentally. Quota values are hints, not authorization or a service guarantee. Return invalid/expired cursor and precondition failures as stable RFC 9457 problem types (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2). Do not expose database keys, tenant information, capacity, or signed state that can be tampered with. A cached page may contain stale quota fields; clients should prioritize Retry-After and the documented contract, while servers must enforce limits independently. Test duplicate/omitted records under concurrent mutation, cursor replay across users and filter changes, 304 behavior through the real CDN/proxy, ETag changes on representation changes, 429 caching, clock skew, and client backoff.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q5"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q6","surface":"track","en":"You have to deliver webhooks to hundreds of partner endpoints you do not control. What does that system actually need?","bodyEn":"Publishing webhooks makes you an HTTP client to servers that are slow, wrong, or gone — while still owing every one of them delivery. Treat it as a queue with retries, never as a call inside the request that produced the event. business transaction commits & 8594; outbox row & 8594; dispatcher picks it up & 8594; per-subscriber delivery attempt & 8594; 2xx: done 4xx: stop 5xx or timeout: schedule retry & 8594; retries exhausted & 8594; dead-letter + subscriber marked unhealthy What each part is protecting you from: signing — HMAC over the raw body plus a timestamp header, so the receiver can prove the payload is yours and reject a replay bounded retries with backoff and jitter — a published schedule (say 1m, 5m, 30m, 2h, 6h) that partners can reason about, not an infinite loop isolation per subscriber — one partner timing out at 30 s must not consume the workers every other partner is waiting on (25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q4) a delivery log — attempt, status, response snippet, next retry — because the first support question is always \"we never received it\" Guarantee What you can promise Delivery at-least-once — so include a stable event_id Order none; include occurred_at and a per-resource sequence Timing best effort; a partner outage delays everything behind it Payload a versioned contract you cannot silently change The subscriber URL is attacker-controlled input Whoever registers the endpoint chooses where your servers send traffic. Validate the resolved address and send through a restricted egress path, or webhook registration becomes SSRF (13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q4). generate the event from the same transaction that changed the data, via an outbox. Firing an HTTP call inside the transaction couples your commit to a partner's uptime (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7). give each subscriber two active secrets during rotation so they can switch without a coordinated deploy; sign with the new one and accept both for a window (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q4). auto-disable endpoints that have failed for days, and require a manual re-enable. Retrying a dead URL forever is how a delivery queue turns into a backlog nobody can drain. offer replay as a product feature — \"resend events from this window\" — otherwise every partner incident becomes a manual database job for you. alert on delivery lag and failure rate per subscriber, not globally; one broken partner is invisible in an aggregate success rate (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q2).","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q6"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q7","surface":"track","en":"A payment provider posts a webhook to you. What must the handler do before it trusts a single field?","bodyEn":"An inbound webhook is an unauthenticated public endpoint carrying instructions about money. The order of operations is the control. @PostMapping ( \"/webhooks/psp\" ) ResponseEntity<Void> receive ( @RequestBody byte [] raw, @RequestHeader ( \"X-Signature\" ) String sig, @RequestHeader ( \"X-Timestamp\" ) String ts) { if (!signatures.valid(raw, ts, sig)) return ResponseEntity.status( 401 ).build(); if (clock.instant().minus(TOLERANCE).isAfter(Instant.parse(ts))) return ResponseEntity.status( 400 ).build(); // outside the replay window inbox.recordIfNew(eventIdOf(raw)); // unique index = dedup return ResponseEntity.accepted().build(); // process asynchronously } The order matters: verify the signature over the raw bytes, before parsing — a re-serialized body no longer matches, and parsing untrusted input is itself an attack surface (13-security-oauth2.secure-coding-at-the-boundary.q3) compare in constant time, and reject anything outside the timestamp tolerance so a captured request cannot be replayed tomorrow acknowledge fast, process later — providers time out in seconds and retry, so heavy work inside the handler manufactures duplicates deduplicate on the provider's event id with a unique constraint, since at-least-once is the only guarantee on offer (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1) Situation Wrong response Right response Signature invalid ignore quietly 401 and alert — someone is probing Event already processed reprocess it 2xx, do nothing Event arrives out of order apply blindly compare state or sequence, ignore the older one Your handler is broken 200 so retries stop 5xx, let the provider retry while you fix it Unknown event type fail 2xx, record it, ignore Never let the payload be the last word on money Amounts and statuses in a webhook body are a notification, not a source of truth. For anything financial, confirm against the provider's API before crediting an account (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1). webhook plus polling is the reliable pairing: the webhook makes the common case fast, a reconciliation job catches what was never delivered. Neither alone is enough. store the raw payload and headers before processing. When a partner disputes an outcome, the received bytes are the only evidence you have. keep the endpoint out of your standard auth chain but inside rate limiting; it is public by necessity and a free amplification target otherwise. a 409 or 422 teaches a provider nothing — most implement \"retry on 5xx, give up on 4xx\", so choose the class deliberately (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2). test the ugly paths: duplicate delivery, out-of-order arrival, a signature from a rotated secret, and a payload for an entity you have never seen (26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q3).","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q7"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q8","surface":"track","en":"How should a large upload be modelled as an API resource?","bodyEn":"Model the upload session as a durable resource rather than keeping one HTTP request open. A typical contract is: POST /uploads authenticates the owner and returns 201 Created with an upload ID, object constraints, part size, expiry, checksum algorithm, and either an upload URL or direct-storage instructions. The client uploads parts with the upload ID, part number or byte range, size, and checksum. A part retry addresses the same session and part; it does not create a second business object. GET /uploads/{id} returns accepted parts, progress, expiry, and an explicit state: INITIATED , PARTIAL , VERIFYING , COMPLETE , ABORTED , or EXPIRED . POST /uploads/{id}/complete supplies or confirms the manifest. The server verifies ownership, part list, size and integrity before publishing the immutable object. DELETE /uploads/{id} or an expiry job aborts incomplete parts and releases temporary storage. Only after completion is durable should the API enqueue downstream processing and return 202 Accepted for a long-running job. Completion must be conditional and idempotent; a repeated request returns the same result or a documented conflict. Enforce limits at every proxy, protect the session from cross-tenant access, and clean up abandoned parts. Direct multipart/resumable object storage reduces application bandwidth, but it does not remove authorization, expiry, checksum, reconciliation, or lifecycle responsibility. The upload resource is not the source-of-truth business record until completion passes.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q8"},{"id":"17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q9","surface":"track","en":"When is 202 Accepted a truthful contract for a long-running job?","bodyEn":"202 Accepted means the request was accepted for processing; it does not mean the work succeeded, will finish exactly once, or can be cancelled for free. Before returning 202, create a durable job identity and persist enough intent to recover it: owner and authorization scope, input or object version, idempotency key, state, timestamps, attempt policy, and a status URL. A retry with the same client intent must return or resume the same job rather than enqueueing a duplicate. Define states and ownership explicitly: ACCEPTED , RUNNING , SUCCEEDED , FAILED , CANCELLED , EXPIRED , and UNKNOWN when the worker or provider outcome cannot yet be determined. A worker claim needs a lease or visibility deadline, bounded retry, checkpoint semantics, and a rule for side effects already committed. Cancellation is cooperative unless the system can prove no side effect escaped. Return progress only when it is meaningful and monotonic; expose a result resource or error problem type, and offer reconciliation for ambiguous timeouts. A queue, Kubernetes Job, or workflow engine supplies execution primitives, not automatically the API's idempotency, authorization, cancellation, or exactly-once guarantee. Ownership Topic 17 owns the wire contract. Topic 08 owns broker delivery semantics; Topic 25 owns consumer retry and deduplication; domain topics decide which side effects need compensation or strong consistency.","contextEn":"REST API design — resources, contracts and lifecycle","href":"/topics/17-rest-api-design#question-17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q9"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q1","surface":"track","en":"What is the standard process for investigating a slow query, and where should you start?","bodyEn":"The number-one mistake is jumping straight to ADD INDEX. Redundant indexes slow every write and consume the buffer pool. Six steps: Identify the actual culprit — use pg_stat_statements, the slow query log, or APM. Sort by total_time = mean × calls, not by mean alone. A 20 ms query called three million times hurts more than a 2 s query called ten times. Reproduce it — run against data whose distribution resembles production. A local table with 1,000 rows is always fast and proves nothing. Read the plan — use EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) on Postgres or EXPLAIN ANALYZE on MySQL 8. Find the node consuming the most time, not the first node displayed. Form a hypothesis — is an index missing? Is the predicate non-sargable? Is the join order poor? Are statistics inaccurate? Are unnecessary columns being read? Change one thing and measure again — changing two things at once makes it impossible to know which one helped. Capture a baseline — retain the good plan so it can be compared when a regression is suspected later. The question to ask first 'Does this query need to run at all?' The fastest query is one that never runs: serve from cache, read from a read model, or eliminate a redundant call in the layer above. Before optimizing, define the target: what p99 latency is required, and at what concurrency? Without a number, optimization has no stopping condition. measure p95/p99, not the average; averages hide exactly the tail latency users experience EXPLAIN without ANALYZE only estimates and does not execute the statement; EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes it, so be careful with UPDATE/DELETE and wrap the statement in a transaction followed by ROLLBACK the database cache makes a second run inherently faster; comparisons need the same cache state, and BUFFERS reveals whether pages came from disk or memory on MySQL, use SET profiling=1 or performance_schema; on Postgres, use auto_explain to log slow-query plans directly in production 80% of production cases end with one of four causes: a missing appropriate index, SELECT , an ORM N+1, or a large OFFSET A safe PostgreSQL investigation ladder: -- 1. No execution: capture a machine-readable candidate plan first EXPLAIN (COSTS, VERBOSE, FORMAT JSON) SELECT FROM booking WHERE user_id=42 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50; -- 2. Execute only when the load and statement are safe BEGIN; SET LOCAL statement_timeout='3s'; EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, WAL, FORMAT JSON) SELECT FROM booking WHERE user_id=42 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 50; ROLLBACK; -- compare plan node, estimated/actual rows, loops, shared hit/read and total time ANALYZE really executes the query; ROLLBACK does not make dangerous production work cheap, and DML may still lock rows or run triggers. Prefer production-shaped staging or a carefully bounded read. Benefit: the before/after JSON plan turns tuning into evidence. Cost: warm cache can hide I/O and one parameter can hide skew; preserve representative binds and repeat under controlled cache/load conditions.","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q1"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do you read EXPLAIN ANALYZE in depth, and what do estimated versus actual rows, loops, and buffers reveal?","bodyEn":"An execution plan is a tree. Read it from the leaf nodes toward the root, remembering that each node includes the cumulative cost of its subtree. Nested Loop (cost=0.86..8534 rows= 1 width=64) (actual time=0.05.. 2841 rows= 48210 loops= 1 ) -> Seq Scan on bookings b (actual rows= 48210 loops= 1 ) Filter: (created_at > now() - '7 days' ) Buffers: shared read= 31204 -> Index Scan on passengers p (actual rows= 1 loops= 48210 ) Index Cond: (p.booking_id = b.id) Three numbers demand attention: estimated rows versus actual rows — an estimate of 1 row versus 48,210 actual rows is a red flag. The optimiser chose a Nested Loop because it expected one row, but actually repeated the inner operation 48,210 times. The cause is almost always stale statistics or correlated columns. loops — a node's real cost is actual time × loops. A node showing 0.05 ms with loops=48210 consumes about 2.4 s. This is easy to overlook. Buffers — shared hit means a page came from memory and is cheap; shared read means it came from disk and is expensive. Reading 31,204 blocks is approximately 244 MB of I/O for one query. Fix: run ANALYZE bookings; to refresh statistics so the optimiser estimates cardinality correctly and can switch to a Hash Join. in Postgres, rows is the row count per loop; MySQL EXPLAIN ANALYZE uses the same convention. The real total is rows × loops. a large Rows Removed by Filter means many rows were read and discarded, so the predicate should be pushed into an index high Heap Fetches during an Index Only Scan indicates a stale visibility map; run VACUUM to recover a true index-only scan cardinality estimates can be wrong because of column correlation (city='Hà Nội' AND district='Cầu Giấy'—the optimiser multiplies probabilities as if they were independent): create CREATE STATISTICS (Postgres 10+) for the column pair Sort Method: external merge Disk: 84MB means work_mem is insufficient and sorting has spilled to disk; increase work_mem for the session or eliminate unnecessary sorting on MySQL, Using filesort and Using temporary together in a query whose GROUP BY and ORDER BY use different columns is a classic slow pattern compare old and new plans with explain.dalibo.com or pev2; a visual tree is faster to inspect than raw text","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q3","surface":"track","en":"How does the optimiser choose join algorithms and join order, and when does it choose incorrectly?","bodyEn":"Algorithm How it runs Cost Best when Nested Loop for each outer-table row → probe an index on the inner table O(n × log m) the outer table is very small and the inner table is indexed Hash Join build a hash table from the smaller input and probe it with the larger input O(n + m) large equality joins with enough work_mem Merge Join sort both inputs and merge them O(n log n + m log m) both inputs are already ordered by the join key The optimiser is cost-based: it estimates each node's cardinality from statistics such as histograms and n_distinct, assigns I/O and CPU costs, and chooses the cheapest tree. Everything rests on one assumption—that the cardinality estimates are accurate. It chooses incorrectly when: statistics are stale after a bulk load (the most common case) → run ANALYZE columns are correlated → the optimiser multiplies probabilities as if the columns were independent it cannot estimate a predicate, such as a user-defined function, LIKE '%x%', or a bind parameter whose value is unknown too many tables are joined (in Postgres, beyond join_collapse_limit=8 it stops searching for the optimal order) The signature symptom is a Nested Loop with a very large loops count, indicating that the optimiser believed the outer input was small. Join order matters more than join algorithm: joining A⋈B and then ⋈C may produce 10 million intermediate rows, whereas joining A⋈C and then ⋈B may produce only 200. The optimiser explores orders with dynamic programming, but the search space grows factorially and must be pruned. fix the root cause first: run ANALYZE, increase default_statistics_target for skewed columns, and use CREATE STATISTICS for correlated columns forcing a plan is the last resort: MySQL STRAIGHT_JOIN or optimiser hints, Oracle hints, and Postgres pg_hint_plan or a session-level SET enable_nestloop=off. A hint freezes a plan and becomes wrong when the data changes MySQL before 8.0.18 had no Hash Join, only Block Nested Loop, so a large join without the right index was extremely slow. Knowing this boundary explains why older code often replaced joins with subqueries in Postgres, insufficient work_mem makes a Hash Join partition into batches on disk; remember that work_mem applies to each sort/hash node, not to the query as a whole a semi-join (EXISTS) is usually cheaper than an IN (subquery) returning many rows and always cheaper than applying DISTINCT after a join","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q3"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you trace a plan regression when a previously fast query suddenly becomes slow?","bodyEn":"The code and indexes are unchanged, yet p99 jumps from 20 ms to 4 s. There are four common causes, in descending frequency: 1. Statistics changed — the table just received 10 million new rows and autovacuum has not run ANALYZE yet, so cardinality estimates drift and the plan changes. Fix it with ANALYZE, then tune autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor for large tables. 2. Parameter sniffing — the database caches a plan using the first parameter value. WHERE status = 'PENDING' (0.1% of rows) produces an Index Scan; a later execution with 'COMPLETED' (95% of rows) reuses that plan and performs an index scan over 9.5 million rows. This is especially painful in SQL Server and Oracle; Postgres has custom versus generic plans (plan_cache_mode). 3. Data crossed a threshold — the table grew until its index no longer fits in the buffer pool, making every lookup perform real I/O. Nothing visibly \"changed\"; the workload simply crossed a capacity boundary. 4. Heap or index bloat — frequent UPDATE/DELETE activity outpaces autovacuum, filling the heap and indexes with dead tuples so more pages must be read for the same rows. A disciplined investigation looks like this: use pg_stat_statements to determine when the query's mean_exec_time changed and narrow the time window compare the current plan with the stored baseline (18.1 step 6) inspect last_analyze, n_dead_tup, and n_mod_since_analyze in pg_stat_user_tables examine the buffer hit ratio and table size over time Prevention: run ANALYZE immediately after every bulk load or migration; one line in a script prevents many sleepless nights monitor n_dead_tup and tune autovacuum for write-heavy tables (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor defaults to 0.2, which is far too high for a 100-million-row table) on Postgres 12+, use plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan for queries with heavily skewed parameter distributions keep plan baselines in the repository for critical queries and compare them in CI or a scheduled job Oracle provides SQL Plan Management, where only approved baselines may be used; Postgres and MySQL have no direct equivalent, so monitoring must be built around them Mentioning parameter sniffing and autovacuum not having reached ANALYZE yet signals real operational experience rather than documentation knowledge. The two queries that answer \"what changed?\": -- 1. Which statement regressed, and by how much SELECT queryid, calls, mean_exec_time, stddev_exec_time FROM pg_stat_statements WHERE query LIKE '%FROM bookings%' ORDER BY mean_exec_time DESC LIMIT 5 ; -- -- 2. Is it stale stats or bloat? (the usual answer) SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, n_mod_since_analyze, last_analyze, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname = 'bookings' ; -- n_mod_since_analyze = 9.4M on a 12M-row table -- last_analyze = 6 days ago -> the estimates are fiction A high stddev_exec_time with a normal mean is the fingerprint of parameter sniffing: the same statement is fast for one bind value and slow for another. A uniformly high mean points to stats or bloat instead.","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q4"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q5","surface":"track","en":"What is a parameter-sensitive plan, and how do you diagnose a cached plan that is fast for one value but slow for another?","bodyEn":"Parameterized SQL improves safety and can reduce parse/plan work, but one reusable plan may be poor when tenant sizes or predicate values are highly skewed. Typical signatures are “fast after one restart, slow after another,” a seek suitable for a rare value being reused for a common value, or an optional WHERE (:p IS NULL OR col = :p) predicate forcing a compromise. Diagnose with evidence: capture the normalized statement and representative parameter buckets (rare/common, small/large tenant, null/non-null). For each bucket compare actual rows, estimates, loops, buffers/I/O, memory spill, duration, and plan identity. Check histogram/statistics freshness and the engine's plan cache or Query Store. Do not clear a shared production plan cache as an experiment—it destroys evidence and perturbs unrelated workloads. -- PostgreSQL: inspect the plan selected for actual values PREPARE find_orders(bigint, text) AS SELECT FROM orders WHERE tenant_id = $1 AND status = $2; EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) EXECUTE find_orders(42, 'OPEN'); PostgreSQL can choose custom plans based on values or a reusable generic plan; EXPLAIN EXECUTE, EXPLAIN (GENERIC_PLAN), and plan_cache_mode help diagnose that choice. Overrides such as force_custom_plan are diagnostic or workload-specific controls, not global tuning defaults. SQL Server 2022+ at compatibility level 160 can use Parameter Sensitive Plan optimization to keep variants for eligible skewed equality predicates; inspect Query Store and dispatcher/query variants before adding hints. Fix the cause in this order: validate statistics and data distribution make predicates sargable and types consistent improve indexes/data model split materially different query shapes consider engine-specific adaptive-plan features, selective recompilation/custom planning, or carefully tested hints Each remedy trades execution quality against planning CPU, plan-cache memory, operational complexity, or code paths. Build a regression workload with several selectivity buckets and production-like concurrency. A single “representative parameter” benchmark merely selects which users will see the bad plan. This extends the plan-regression workflow in (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q4).","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q5"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q6","surface":"track","en":"Where do EXPLAIN's shared hit and read numbers come from — PostgreSQL's buffer cache","bodyEn":"PostgreSQL never reads a table file directly. Every page a query touches is first copied into a fixed array of 8 KB buffers in shared memory — shared_buffers — and EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) is a report about exactly that array. The four counters, and what each one really tells you: shared hit=N — the page was already in the array, so nothing left the process. shared read=N — it was not, so the page came from the OS page cache or from the disk. A read is not proof of disk I/O: PostgreSQL cannot tell the two apart, which is the double-buffering blind spot. track_io_timing is what turns that guess into a number. shared dirtied=N — this query was the first to modify pages that were clean. shared written=N — this backend had to write dirty pages out itself to make room. That is the checkpointer and the bgwriter falling behind, not a property of the query (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q7). Eviction is a clock sweep, not an LRU list. Every buffer carries a usage counter that rises when the buffer is pinned and stops at 5. The sweep hand moves around the array: a buffer with a nonzero count loses one and survives, an unpinned buffer at zero is taken. A page therefore has to be ignored for several full passes before it leaves, which is why frequency beats recency here — one scan touching a page once does not promote it over a page read all day. A large sequential scan cannot flush the cache. Once a table is bigger than a quarter of shared_buffers, the scan runs inside a 256 KB ring of buffers and recycles them instead of sweeping the whole array, so a nightly report does not evict the working set. Bulk writes — COPY, CREATE TABLE AS — use a 16 MB ring, capped at an eighth of shared_buffers. VACUUM and ANALYZE use vacuum_buffer_usage_limit, 2 MB by default. Read what is in the cache, not a hit ratio. pg_buffercache shows which relation owns each buffer and its usage count, and pg_statio_user_tables gives hits and reads per table. A 99% global ratio hides the one table that misses, exactly as a global cache hit rate does (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q7). BUFFERS is included automatically with EXPLAIN ANALYZE in PostgreSQL 18, so a plan read today carries the counters whether or not they were asked for; on older servers you still spell out EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS). reads are asynchronous in 18: io_method defaults to worker with three io_workers — io_uring is available on Linux builds — and effective_io_concurrency now defaults to 16, so a bitmap heap scan really does issue concurrent reads. temp and local blocks are different memory. A temporary table lives in temp_buffers (8 MB per session) while sorts and hashes spill through work_mem (4 MB), so temp read/written in a plan means spilling, not cache pressure (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2). heap pages and index pages compete for the same buffers, which is part of the price of an index nobody queries: every insert still dirties its pages and those pages still take space in the array.","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q6"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q7","surface":"track","en":"Sizing shared_buffers, and the write side: checkpoints, bgwriter and full-page writes","bodyEn":"The default shared_buffers is 128 MB — a value picked so the server starts anywhere, not a recommendation. The usual first move is about a quarter of RAM, and that is a starting point rather than a target, because two forces push back. Double buffering: whatever is not in shared_buffers is usually still in the OS page cache, so enlarging the array can simply buy a second copy of the same pages. Checkpoint size: every dirty buffer has to be written eventually, so a bigger array means a bigger burst of writes per checkpoint. The number is settled by measurement: do shared read, backend writes in pg_stat_io and checkpoint write time actually fall when you raise it? effective_cache_size allocates nothing. It defaults to 4 GB and exists only for the planner: it states how much cache a query may assume, which moves the choice between an index scan and a sequential scan. Setting it to the memory the machine really leaves for PostgreSQL costs nothing and changes plans (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q3). The write path is where a buffer cache becomes an incident. A commit does not write data pages. It writes WAL and fsyncs that (synchronous_commit = on), while the page itself stays dirty in the array. The checkpointer writes those pages later: every checkpoint_timeout (5 min) or when max_wal_size (1 GB) fills, spread across checkpoint_completion_target (0.9) of the interval. The bgwriter trickles out victims ahead of the sweep — bgwriter_delay 200 ms, at most bgwriter_lru_maxpages (100) buffers per round — so that backends do not have to write their own. pg_stat_checkpointer answers the first question: num_requested rising against num_timed means checkpoints are driven by WAL volume, not by the clock, and max_wal_size is what to raise before touching anything else. Frequent checkpoints multiply WAL, not just I/O. With full_page_writes = on, the first write to a page after each checkpoint copies the entire 8 KB page into WAL — the same mechanism that makes random-UUID inserts degrade (05-db-core-index-lock.indexes-what-they-really-are.q7). -- which relations own the cache right now, and how much of it is hot -- (approximate: relfilenode does not match for mapped catalog relations) SELECT c.relname, count() 8 / 1024 AS mb, round( 100.0 count() FILTER ( WHERE b.usagecount >= 3 ) / count(), 1 ) AS pct_hot FROM pg_buffercache b JOIN pg_class c ON c.relfilenode = b.relfilenode GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10 ; -- -- clock-driven checkpoints, or WAL-driven ones? SELECT num_timed, num_requested FROM pg_stat_checkpointer; huge_pages defaults to try, which means it silently does nothing when the kernel has none reserved. With a large shared_buffers the per-backend page tables are expensive, so read postgres -C shared_memory_size_in_huge_pages and set vm.nr_hugepages to that. after a restart or a failover the array is empty, so every read misses and p99 stays bad until it refills. pg_prewarm with the autoprewarm worker dumps the buffer list and reloads it at startup — the same event as a cold cache after a deploy (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q7). pg_stat_io separates who did the writing: backend_type together with context (normal, vacuum, bulkread, bulkwrite) tells you whether pages left through the checkpointer, the bgwriter, or backends stalling to make room. by tier: through T1–T3 (up to 1M req/day, ~250 rps peak) tuning shared_buffers, effective_cache_size and the checkpoint settings is worth it; at T4 (~25,000 rps peak) the buffer cache needs its own scaling story — replicas, partitioning and a cache in front of it.","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q7"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q1","surface":"track","en":"How should WHERE predicates be written so that indexes can actually be used?","bodyEn":"Sargable describes a predicate the optimizer can map to an efficient search argument for an available access method. For an ordinary B-tree column index, comparing the indexed column directly with compatible constants/ranges is the most portable shape—but “the left side must always be a bare column” is not a law. Expression/generated-column indexes, partial indexes, bitmap/index-merge plans and other access methods change what is indexable. -- Usually blocks a plain index on created_at because every row's value is transformed. WHERE DATE (created_at) = '2026-07-31' -- Portable range form for a half-open day. WHERE created_at >= '2026-07-31' AND created_at < '2026-08-01' -- Expression index can make the matching expression searchable. CREATE INDEX users_lower_email_idx ON users (( lower (email))); WHERE lower (email) = 'a@b.vn' Common obstacles are a transformation without a matching expression/generated-column index, an implicit conversion applied to the indexed side, an incompatible collation/operator, and a leading-wildcard search unsupported by a plain B-tree. Rewrite date/time predicates with correct timezone and half-open boundaries; match bind-parameter types; use full-text/trigram/search-specific indexes when substring semantics require them. !=, NOT IN, OR, and low-cardinality columns are not automatically “non-indexable.” Their selectivity and cost often make a scan cheaper, while some engines can use multiple ranges, bitmap/index-merge operations, partial/composite indexes or histograms. OR → UNION ALL is safe only when duplicate semantics and branch overlap are handled; measure the actual plan. NOT IN with a nullable subquery has a separate three-valued-logic trap; NOT EXISTS often expresses the intended anti-join, but plan quality remains workload-dependent. Expression indexes add write/storage cost and require the query expression to match engine rules. MySQL 8.4 can use indexed generated expressions; PostgreSQL supports indexes on expressions. Sargability only creates an access-path option. The cost-based optimizer may correctly choose a scan when many rows qualify or the table is small. Compare estimates, actual rows, buffers/I/O and timing on representative data (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2).","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q1"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should COUNT(*) and pagination be handled on a table with tens of millions of rows?","bodyEn":"These are the two most expensive features on a list screen, and neither usually needs to be exact. Total COUNT() — both MySQL/InnoDB and Postgres must scan because under MVCC every transaction may see a different row count, so the result cannot be cached globally. use an estimate for display-only counts: SELECT reltuples::bigint FROM pg_class WHERE relname='bookings' is immediate and usually within a few percent use a capped count: SELECT count() FROM (SELECT 1 FROM t WHERE ... LIMIT 1001) x lets the UI display \"1000+\" maintain a counter table through a trigger or CDC when exactness is required remove the total page count entirely; Google, Slack, and Stripe expose only next/previous navigation Pagination — OFFSET 500000 LIMIT 20 must read and discard 500,000 rows. -- Keyset: fast and stable, using the (created_at, id) index SELECT id, code, created_at FROM bookings WHERE user_id = ? AND (created_at, id) < (?, ?) -- cursor from the previous page ORDER BY created_at DESC , id DESC LIMIT 20 ; The id tie-breaker is mandatory. Without it, rows sharing the same created_at value will be skipped or repeated between pages. the limitation of keyset pagination is that it cannot jump directly to \"page 500\". If the business genuinely requires that, retain OFFSET but enforce a hard maximum, or use Elasticsearch search_after cursors returned to clients should be opaque (a base64-encoded tuple plus a signature); exposing raw created_at leaks information and lets clients forge cursors ORDER BY must match the index order, including ASC/DESC direction, to eliminate sorting. Postgres can scan an index backwards, but a multicolumn index with mixed directions (a ASC, b DESC) must be declared correctly when created a covering index (INCLUDE in Postgres, or extra indexed columns in MySQL) enables an index-only scan that avoids the heap and is often many times faster COUNT() and COUNT(1) have identical performance; the difference is an old myth. COUNT(col) differs because it excludes NULL values for deep pagination during export, use a database cursor or an asynchronous job (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q3) rather than making the client iterate with OFFSET An OTA customer's booking history is naturally ordered by descending time, making keyset pagination the natural choice and eliminating the need for a total page count.","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q2"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q3","surface":"track","en":"For heavy aggregation, should you pre-aggregate, use a materialized view, or move the workload to OLAP?","bodyEn":"A \"12-month revenue by airline\" dashboard scans 200 million rows every time it opens. No index can rescue that workload; you must change how the result is computed, not how it is looked up. The options, from least to most expensive, are: 1. Cache the result — use Redis with a TTL of 5–60 minutes. It is the cheapest option and should be tried first. It is enough for most dashboards. 2. Materialized view — use Postgres CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW and refresh it periodically: CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW mv_revenue_daily AS SELECT airline_id, created_at::date AS d, count () AS bookings, sum (total) AS revenue FROM bookings GROUP BY 1 , 2 ; -- CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ON mv_revenue_daily(airline_id, d); REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY mv_revenue_daily; CONCURRENTLY requires a unique index but does not block readers. Without it, the entire view is locked during refresh. 3. Incremental rollup table — update a revenue_daily table through a job or CDC only for days that changed. This is cheaper than a full refresh and can retain years of data. 4. Move to OLAP — feed ClickHouse, BigQuery, or Redshift through CDC such as Debezium when arbitrary ad-hoc queries over billions of rows are required. The decision boundary is: is the query known in advance? known queries with few dimensions → use a rollup table or materialized view ad-hoc queries with many user-selected dimensions → use a columnar OLAP system. Forcing ad-hoc analytics into OLTP is one of the surest ways to take production down materialized views do not update themselves in Postgres (Oracle supports fast refresh on commit); they need a scheduled job, and that job needs monitoring incremental rollups must handle late-arriving data, such as a booking amended three days later; refresh a rolling window of the most recent N days, not only today a read replica is a useful intermediate step: move all reporting to the replica and accept a few seconds of lag. It is often sufficient and costs little engineering effort columnar OLAP is fast because it reads only required columns, compresses by column, and vectorises execution; the trade-off is poor point-lookup and individual-update performance use approximation when allowed: HyperLogLog (APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT) estimates unique visitors with roughly 2% error and can be hundreds of times faster a window function such as SUM() OVER often replaces a self-join or correlated subquery, staying within OLTP while running much faster","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q3"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you optimize the application layer through connection pools, statement timeouts, batching, and fetch size?","bodyEn":"A well-optimized query can still be slow because much of the elapsed time may not be inside the database. Connection pool (HikariCP) a reasonable pool size is cores × 2 + number of spindles, usually 10–20. A pool of 200 is not faster; it merely moves the queue from the application into the database and makes every query uniformly slow. connectionTimeout, the time spent waiting for a connection, should be short (2–5 s) so the service fails fast instead of building a backlog an exhausted pool is usually a symptom, not the cause: a slow query may hold a connection too long, or a transaction may remain open around an outbound HTTP call Timeouts — three layers, ordered from shortest to longest database statement_timeout (for example, 5 s) is the final safety net and should always be configured application @Transactional(timeout=...) client socket/HTTP timeout Without the database layer, a broken query can run for 40 minutes while holding locks for the entire duration. Batching and fetching writes: rewriteBatchedStatements=true on MySQL plus hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50 turns 1,000 individual INSERTs into roughly 20 round trips large reads: configure fetchSize (Postgres also requires autoCommit=false) to stream results rather than loading the entire result set into the heap, preventing OOM Derive the pool size instead of guessing it. Little's Law gives the number directly: concurrency = throughput × service time. // a service at 250 rps peak, average 8 ms inside the database // 250 x 0.008 = 2 connections busy on average // x3 for variance and slow outliers -> a pool of 6-10 is right // // a pool of 200 for the same load does not add capacity: the database still // executes ~2 at a time, and the other 198 requests wait inside the database // where no application metric can see them. One configuration, all three timeout layers, ordered shortest to longest. Order is the whole point: an inner layer that outlives an outer one can never fire. application.yml spring: datasource: hikari: maximum-pool-size: 10 from the arithmetic above, not from a habit minimum-idle: 10 same as max: a fixed pool has no warm-up cliff connection-timeout: 3000 wait for a connection, then fail fast (3s) validation-timeout: 1000 max-lifetime: 1740000 29m — must be under the DB/proxy idle cut keepalive-time: 120000 leak-detection-threshold: 20000 names the code path that held it jpa: open-in-view: false or a connection is held for the whole render properties: hibernate.jdbc.batch_size: 50 hibernate.order_inserts: true batching only groups adjacent same-table statements hibernate.order_updates: true hibernate.jdbc.fetch_size: 200 jakarta.persistence.query.timeout: 4000 2. per statement (4s) 1. innermost — the database's own net, and the only one a runaway query obeys Postgres: statement_timeout = 5s · MySQL: max_execution_time = 5000 3. outermost — the HTTP client calling this service, longer than both Two things silently switch batching back off, and neither reports anything: an IDENTITY generator disables insert batching entirely, because Hibernate has to read each generated key back before it can continue. Use a sequence or a table generator wherever insert throughput matters. a flush in the middle of the batch — a query against the same table, or saveAndFlush in a loop — closes the batch early and you get the round trips back. Batching that reports success and does nothing On MySQL, hibernate.jdbc.batch_size alone changes almost nothing: without rewriteBatchedStatements=true on the JDBC URL, the driver still sends one statement per round trip. Nothing warns you — the only signal is the round-trip count. long transactions are the primary enemy: open a transaction, call a partner API for 3 s, then commit. The connection is held uselessly for 3 s, MVCC retains an old snapshot, and autovacuum cannot reclaim dead tuples. Move external calls outside the transaction. Spring Boot's open-in-view is enabled by default; it holds a connection while rendering the view and conceals N+1 queries. Disable it with spring.jpa.open-in-view=false, then repair the failures it reveals because those paths were already incorrect. detect N+1 (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q6) with hibernate.generate_statistics or datasource-proxy; fix it with JOIN FETCH, @EntityGraph, or @BatchSize, not EAGER use separate pools by workload: a small reporting pool and a primary customer-request pool prevent a heavy job from exhausting connections needed by the money path PreparedStatement improves both security and performance by avoiding repeated parsing, but an oversized prepared-statement cache consumes database memory measure pool acquisition time separately from query execution time; Micrometer already exposes hikaricp.connections.acquire. Without that metric, diagnosis is guesswork","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q5","surface":"track","en":"A job must insert two million rows and process a work queue. What breaks if you treat it like ordinary application writes?","bodyEn":"Bulk work fails differently from request-scoped work. One transaction around two million rows holds locks and an MVCC snapshot for its entire runtime, blocks vacuum, and loses everything if it fails at 98%. Structure the job so failure is cheap: process in chunks with a commit per chunk, plus a checkpoint recording the last id done — a restart resumes rather than repeats iterate with keyset pagination; OFFSET 1900000 re-reads everything before it clear the persistence context each chunk, or Hibernate keeps every entity of the run in memory for the initial load, a bulk path (COPY, LOAD DATA) beats even batched inserts by an order of magnitude -- queue table: many workers, no contention, no double processing SELECT id, payload FROM jobs WHERE status = 'PENDING' AND run_after <= now() ORDER BY run_after LIMIT 100 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED ; -- locked rows are skipped, not waited on -- -- chunked scan: resume from the last id, never OFFSET SELECT FROM orders WHERE id > :lastId ORDER BY id LIMIT 1000 ; FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED is what makes a database usable as a work queue: each worker takes rows nobody else holds, so N workers scale linearly without a broker (25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q2). Operation Cost people miss Bulk UPDATE/DELETE via JPQL bypasses the persistence context — stale entities remain in memory Mass DELETE leaves dead tuples and bloat; partition-drop is often the right tool Insert into an indexed table every index is written too; dropping and rebuilding can be faster for a big load Long-running read holds a snapshot and blocks cleanup for the whole run A batch job competes with live traffic It shares the pool, the CPU and the buffer cache. Give it its own small pool, run it off-peak, and throttle it — a \"quick backfill\" is a classic cause of a production latency spike (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4). make the job idempotent per chunk: it will be re-run. A unique constraint or an upsert keyed on the natural identifier turns a rerun into a no-op (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1). keep chunk size tunable and measure it. Too small pays per-transaction overhead, too large brings back the long-transaction problems you were avoiding (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q2). for the queue table, index on (status, run_after) and keep the table narrow; a queue that grows forever slows down every claim, so archive completed rows. add attempt counting and a dead-letter status to the queue rows, otherwise one poison job is retried until someone notices. report progress: rows done, rate, estimated finish. A silent job that has run for six hours cannot be judged as stuck or merely slow.","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q5"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q6","surface":"track","en":"When should a slow query become a cache, materialized view, CDC projection, or OLAP model?","bodyEn":"Choose the read shape from measured frequency, latency target, result size, freshness budget, write rate, and rebuild cost. The options are different contracts: a cache is disposable and fast, but invalidation, eviction, stampede control, and stale reads are part of the design a materialized view is a database-managed or scheduled snapshot; define refresh blocking, versioning, and failure behavior a CDC projection is a separately stored read model with a watermark, replay, schema/delete handling, and repair path an OLAP model is for scans and aggregates over governed history; it needs late-data, correction, lineage, and access-control rules Do not use any of these to hide an unmeasured query. First capture the plan, cardinality, selectivity, rows examined, CPU/I/O, concurrency, and write amplification. Then compare the new path against the authoritative query using a representative data distribution. Refresh/rebuild gate: record the source snapshot and change position, build a new version, catch it up, compare counts/checksums and semantic invariants, observe freshness and error rate, and only then swap or route traffic. If a correction or delete cannot be replayed, the “read model” is not safely rebuildable. A dual-write from request code is usually the least defensible option because it couples two commits without a shared recovery point. Prefer a transactionally coupled outbox or a database-log/CDC path, then make the projection idempotent (06-db-scaling.sharding-partitioning.q7), (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2). A read model is justified when its freshness and operational cost are explicit. If a detail page needs the newest authoritative value, read the authority; let the projection serve browse, search, reports, or aggregates whose stale window is acceptable.","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q6"},{"id":"18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q7","surface":"track","en":"INNER vs LEFT JOIN — what does each return, and why does a filter in `WHERE` quietly turn a LEFT JOIN into an INNER one?","bodyEn":"INNER JOIN keeps only the pairs that match the ON condition. LEFT JOIN keeps every left row; where nothing matches, the right side's columns come back NULL. The trap is where the filter goes: -- every customer, with their PAID orders if any SELECT c.id, o.id FROM customer c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id AND o.status = 'PAID' ; ──── the same filter in WHERE: customers with no paid order vanish SELECT c.id, o.id FROM customer c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE o.status = 'PAID' ; -- NULL = 'PAID' is unknown, so the row is dropped ON decides which right rows match; WHERE decides which result rows survive. A WHERE condition on the right table that a NULL cannot pass removes exactly the rows the LEFT was there to keep — and the optimizer simply plans it as an inner join. \"Rows with no match\" — three spellings, one of them wrong: WHERE NOT EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id) -- anti-join, clearest LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE o.id IS NULL -- same result: test the key WHERE c.id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders) -- one NULL → zero rows The rule Conditions about which rows match go in ON; conditions about which results you want go in WHERE. For \"has none\", write NOT EXISTS. NOT IN fails because x NOT IN (1, NULL) means x <> 1 AND x <> NULL, and the second half is unknown for every x. One nullable customer_id column is all it takes. a LEFT JOIN to a one-to-many table multiplies the left rows: COUNT() then counts orders, and a customer with none counts as 1. Count the right key (COUNT(o.id)) or aggregate in a subquery first. WHERE filters rows before grouping, HAVING filters groups after it. Put a condition in WHERE whenever it needs no aggregate, so fewer rows reach the GROUP BY. RIGHT JOIN is a LEFT JOIN with the tables swapped, and many teams ban it for readability. FULL OUTER JOIN is for reconciliation — rows missing on either side; MySQL has none, so emulate it with UNION. inner joins can be reordered freely and the optimizer does; outer joins constrain that freedom, which is one way an unnecessary LEFT JOIN costs a plan (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q3). PostgreSQL and MySQL 8 both plan NOT EXISTS as an anti-join, so choosing it over LEFT JOIN … IS NULL is readability, not speed — check with EXPLAIN before believing otherwise.","contextEn":"Query optimisation — read the plan, rewrite it, keep it fast","href":"/topics/18-query-optimization#question-18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q7"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q1","surface":"track","en":"What is the six-step framework for solving a coding interview problem while thinking out loud?","bodyEn":"Use this six-step framework as a practical checklist, not a law about every interviewer. It makes assumptions, trade-offs, and evidence inspectable; the interviewer or domain may weight communication, correctness, or implementation differently. Restate the problem in your own words (30s) — surface misunderstandings immediately. Ask about constraints — how large can n be? Can values be negative? Can there be duplicates? Can the input be empty? Unicode or ASCII? Constraints determine the algorithm: n ≤ 1000 may allow O(n²); n ≤ 10⁵ does not. Work an example by hand — write out one small example and one edge case yourself. This often exposes a misunderstanding. Start with brute force and state its complexity — \"the naive approach is O(n²) because...; now I will look for a way to eliminate repeated work\". Do not jump straight to the optimal solution — if you get stuck, you otherwise have nothing to submit. Optimize, and explain the idea before typing — \"I will use a hash map to turn O(n) lookup into O(1), making the overall solution O(n)\". Check that the approach and its assumptions are understood before coding; no universal approval ritual is required. Dry-run the code and cover edge cases — walk through the code with the original example, then proactively call out the boundary cases (19.x, and 16.13). A lifeline when you are stuck \"This looks like a sliding-window problem because the window only expands or contracts; the pointers never move backward. May I try that direction?\" — when you can explain how you recognized the pattern, the interviewer will often offer the next hint. At senior level, two things are weighted more heavily than coding speed: Naming and structure: extract small functions, use meaningful variable names (left/right rather than i/j), and do not cram everything into a 60-line function Discussing trade-offs: \"this approach takes O(n) time and O(k) space; if memory is the binding constraint, I can return to the O(n·k) approach that uses no extra space\" always state both time and space complexity, for both the brute-force approach and the final solution get working code first and micro-optimize later — but make it clear that you know what can be optimized when the interviewer is unusually quiet, ask directly: \"Does this approach look reasonable to you?\" — they are often waiting for you to check in if time runs out before you finish, summarize what remains and how you would implement it — that still earns credit Proof checkpoint before coding: write the invariant in one sentence, its initialization, the update that preserves it, and the termination condition. Then state time, auxiliary space, and the input precondition that makes the proof apply. A dry run is evidence for the example, not a substitute for this proof. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The Java baseline the examples target — 17, 21, 25, or language-neutral — because a JDK-specific claim is only as good as that choice. Whether string problems are ASCII and lowercase on purpose, or should handle code points and user-perceived characters. The maximum n, graph depth, output size and memory budget an example assumes; recursion is falsified as a default once input exceeds the call-stack budget. Whether ties in top-K, intervals, BST duplicates and output order must be deterministic. What falsifies a sliding-window solution: a negative-value or non-monotone counterexample that breaks its stated invariant. What falsifies answer-space binary search: a feasibility predicate with several feasible regions or an unprovable boundary. What falsifies a benchmark: a changed JDK, CPU or input distribution, too little warm-up, allocation and GC confounding, or an unreported difference in output cost.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q1"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q2","surface":"track","en":"Sliding window: what is the general template, and how do fixed-size and variable-size windows differ?","bodyEn":"Recognition: a fixed-size window needs only a contiguous range and an updateable aggregate; it does not require monotonicity. A variable-size window needs a monotone validity predicate: after expansion, moving the left boundary must be able to restore validity without revisiting earlier positions. Under that precondition the pointers never move backward, giving O(n) state transitions. Variable-size (dynamic) — the standard template: int left = 0 , best = 0 ; for ( int right = 0 ; right < n; right++) { add(a[right]); // expand on the right while (!valid()) { // invalid => shrink from the left remove(a[left]); left++; } best = Math. max (best, right - left + 1 ); } Fixed-size (constant k) — no while is needed; just slide: for ( int right = 0 ; right < n; right++) { add(a[right]); if (right >= k) remove(a[right - k]); // remove the element that fell out if (right >= k - 1 ) record(left = right - k + 1 ); } Each element enters the window exactly once and leaves exactly once → O(n), even with a while nested inside the for. Interviewers often probe this point: \"Why is a while loop nested inside a for loop still O(n)?\" The necessary condition for sliding window to work is that the property be monotonic with respect to window length. An array with negative numbers breaks that condition for \"subarray sum ≥ k\" → use prefix sums plus a deque or binary search, not sliding window. Shrinking with while versus if: use while for a \"longest valid window\" problem; use if for a fixed-size window. Window state is typically a frequency-counting HashMap, a running sum, or a monotonic Deque for window maximum/minimum problems. The \"exactly k distinct characters\" variant = atMost(k) - atMost(k-1) — a useful and frequently tested trick. Classic progression by difficulty: LC 3 (no repeated characters) → LC 76 (minimum window substring) → LC 30 (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q3) → LC 239 (sliding window maximum, which requires a deque). Precondition check: for a variable window, write the predicate and its monotonicity before the loop. With non-negative values, once sum >= target, removing from the left cannot increase sum; with arbitrary negatives that reasoning is unavailable. Fixed-size windows need boundary checks and an updateable aggregate, not this monotonicity proof.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q2"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q3","surface":"track","en":"LC 30 · Substring with Concatenation of All Words — Rakuten has asked this problem; how do you solve it?","bodyEn":"Solve on LeetCode ↗ Problem: given s and an array words (every word has the same length w, and duplicates are allowed), find every starting index of a substring formed by concatenating all the words in words, using each word exactly once with no intervening characters. s = \"barfoothefoobarman\", words = [\"foo\",\"bar\"] → [0, 9] Key insight: because every word has the same length w, the string can be viewed as a sequence of tokens — but there are w different ways to align those tokens (starting at offsets 0, 1, ... w−1). Run an independent sliding window for each offset, advancing w characters at every step. Brute force — try every position i, slice out k words, and compare frequency counters: O(n × k × w). State this approach first. Optimized — a word-level sliding window: public List<Integer> findSubstring (String s, String[] words) { int k = words.length, w = words[ 0 ]. length (); List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>(); if (s. length () < ( long ) k w) return res; Map<String,Integer> need = new HashMap<>(); for (String x : words) need. merge (x, 1 , Integer::sum); for ( int off = 0 ; off < w; off++) { // w token alignments Map<String,Integer> win = new HashMap<>(); int left = off, formed = 0 ; for ( int r = off; r + w <= s. length (); r += w) { String word = s. substring (r, r + w); if (!need. containsKey (word)) { // unknown word => reset the entire window win. clear (); formed = 0 ; left = r + w; continue ; } win. merge (word, 1 , Integer::sum); formed++; while (win. get (word) > need. get (word)) { // excess => shrink from the left String out = s. substring (left, left + w); win. merge (out, - 1 , Integer::sum); formed--; left += w; } if (formed == k) { // complete => record it res. add (left); String out = s. substring (left, left + w); win. merge (out, - 1 , Integer::sum); formed--; left += w; // slide by exactly 1 word } } } return res; } Parameters: let n = s , m = words , and w = words[i] ; the required window has mw characters. With Java substring and hashing that process w characters per token, the scan is O(nw) time. Map storage is O(m) entries (plus O(mw) characters for copied word keys) and output storage is O(z), where z is the number of matches. If token IDs or a rolling hash avoid per-token character work, the scan can be O(n) expected under the hash assumption. The O(n) label is conditional on the input/runtime model, not just on w ≤ 30. Three easy mistakes words may contain duplicates ([\"word\",\"good\",\"best\",\"word\"]) → count frequencies; using a Set is incorrect. On an unknown word, reset the entire window instead of shrinking it gradually. And once the window contains k words, still slide its left edge by exactly one word and continue — do not break, because the next position may also be valid (\"aaaaaa\", [\"aa\",\"aa\"] → [0,1,2]). Why the off loop must run from 0 through w−1: the window advances only in multiples of w, so a window starting at 0 can never reach positions 1, 2, and so on. Each offset is an independent \"lane,\" and the w lanes cover every position. formed (the number of valid words currently in the window) avoids comparing the two maps at every step — this is what reduces O(n·k) to O(n). Test cases to volunteer proactively: s=\"barfoothefoobarman\", [\"foo\",\"bar\"] → [0,9] s=\"wordgoodgoodgoodbestword\", [\"word\",\"good\",\"best\",\"word\"] → [] (duplicate word) s=\"aaaaaa\", [\"aa\",\"aa\"] → [0,1,2] (overlapping results) s=\"a\", [\"a\"] → [0] (boundary case) s shorter than kw → [] (early exit) Further optimization if asked: replace each String key with a numeric ID (map every word to an int through a prebuilt HashMap) → comparison and hashing become much cheaper than String.substring + hashCode. Alternatively, use a rolling hash to avoid allocating substrings. Mentioning both directions earns credit. General recognition pattern: \"all words, each exactly once, contiguous\" + \"every word has the same length\" = a sliding window over tokens. This belongs to the same family as LC 76 (minimum window substring) and LC 438 (find all anagrams); only the unit of movement changes from a character to a word. Input contract: the stated LC problem uses non-empty, equal-length words made of lowercase English letters. A reusable API should handle an empty words array before reading words[0]; arbitrary Unicode or unequal word lengths require a different tokenization contract. Duplicate words stay in need as counts, and the w offset lanes cover starts not aligned to zero.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q3"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q4","surface":"track","en":"Two pointers: how do you recognize the pattern, and what common variants should you know?","bodyEn":"There are three variants, each with a different recognition signal: 1. Converging from both ends (left=0, right=n-1) — requires a sorted array or a symmetric property. Two Sum on a sorted array: sum too small → left++; sum too large → right-- Container With Most Water, Valid Palindrome, 3Sum (sort, then fix one value + two pointers) 2. Same direction, different speeds (fast/slow) — typically separates the \"processed region\" from the \"region being scanned.\" Remove Duplicates: slow is the next write position; fast performs the scan Move Zeroes, Remove Element 3. Fast/slow on a linked list (Floyd) — slow advances by 1 and fast by 2. Detect a cycle: if they meet, there is a cycle Find the middle: when fast reaches the end, slow is at the middle Find the cycle entry: after the pointers meet, move one pointer back to the head, then advance both one step at a time The commonality with sliding window is that the pointers never move backward → O(n). The difference is that two pointers does not necessarily maintain a stateful \"window.\" A problem that requires O(1) space but appears to need a hash map is often signaling two pointers, after sorting if sorting is permitted. 3Sum: sort in O(n log n), fix i, then apply two pointers to the remainder → O(n²); handling duplicates by skipping repeated values at i, left, and right is where most solutions fail. Floyd's algorithm works because the gap between the pointers changes by exactly 1 per step → they must eventually meet within the cycle. Deriving the cycle-entry rule algebraically earns extra credit. To merge two sorted arrays in place, scan from the end toward the beginning so you do not overwrite unprocessed values (LC 88) — an easy trick to forget. Always use the boundary left < right (not <=) when converging from both ends, and fast != null && fast.next != null for a linked list. Precondition map: converging pointers need sorted order (or another monotone relation) so a discarded region contains no answer. Same-direction compaction needs a write-position invariant. Floyd's cycle claim applies to a functional graph where each node has at most one successor; it does not solve arbitrary graph cycle detection. State sorting cost and whether recursion depth is bounded.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q4"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you prove an algorithm and its performance claim before trusting one visual trace?","bodyEn":"Use three separate proofs: correctness, complexity, and measurement. A trace is a teaching aid; it covers one input and cannot establish a loop invariant, a worst-case boundary, or a runtime comparison. Proof Write down Typical falsifier Correctness State invariant, initialization, preservation and termination/postcondition Empty/duplicate/negative/cyclic/overflow input breaks an unstated precondition Complexity Parameterize input and output sizes; count visits, pushes/pops, comparisons and allocations Hidden sort, repeated scan, hash worst case or output size is omitted Measurement Pin JDK/hardware, warm-up, repetitions, distribution, allocation/GC, input generation and output cost Cold start, cache locality, JIT or data shape changes the ranking Regression Brute-force oracle for small cases plus property/differential tests and saved seeds Only happy-path examples pass; a generated counterexample is lost State the precondition before choosing the pattern: sorted order, non-negative weights, monotone feasibility, equal word length, bounded alphabet, or no negative edge. If the precondition is not guaranteed, validate/reject it or choose another algorithm. For amortized claims, count total work over the sequence: a monotonic stack is O(n) because each index is pushed and popped at most once, not because one iteration is constant time in isolation. For Java, distinguish asymptotic complexity from implementation behavior: HashMap expected operations, PriorityQueue heap-head contract, integer overflow, UTF-16 code units and JIT/GC are separate claims. A benchmark can compare implementations under a named workload; it cannot prove correctness or establish a universal constant factor. Use a proper harness and keep a simple oracle for small inputs. Put the invariant next to the code and make the trace show boundary transitions. A reader should be able to construct a counterexample when the invariant is false.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q5"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q1","surface":"track","en":"Hash map + prefix sum: how do subarray-sum problems work, and why can sliding window fail?","bodyEn":"Canonical problem: count the subarrays whose sum is exactly k, where the array may contain negative numbers. Sliding window fails here: with negative numbers, expanding the window no longer increases the sum monotonically → there is no reliable signal for when to shrink it. Idea: let P[i] be the prefix sum through i. The subarray (j, i] has sum k ⟺ P[i] − P[j] = k ⟺ P[j] = P[i] − k. We therefore only need to count how many times we have already seen the value P[i] − k. long subarraySum ( int [] a, int k) { Map<Long,Integer> seen = new HashMap<>(); seen. put ( 0L , 1 ); // empty prefix: important! long sum = 0L , count = 0L ; for ( int x : a) { sum += x; count += seen. getOrDefault (sum - k, 0 ); seen. merge (sum, 1 , Integer::sum); } return count; } O(n) time and O(n) space. The seen.put(0, 1) line handles the case where the prefix from the beginning already sums to k — forget it and the first test case fails immediately. The same template produces several other solutions by changing what you store or count: Sum divisible by k → store sum % k (remember to normalize negative values: ((sum % k) + k) % k) Longest subarray with sum = k → map each prefix sum to the first index where it appears, and insert only when it is absent Equal numbers of 0s and 1s → convert every 0 to −1, reducing the problem to sum = 0 Parity and XOR problems → use prefix XOR with the analogous equation P[i] ^ P[j] = k In 2D, use two-dimensional prefix sums + inclusion–exclusion for O(1) rectangle-sum queries. If dynamic updates are required, replace the prefix array with a Fenwick tree / BIT so both queries and updates are O(log n). Integer overflow: summing 10⁵ elements as large as 10⁹ exceeds int → use long. Interviewers frequently probe this exact issue. State the boundary clearly: sliding window for non-negative arrays, prefix sum + hash map for arrays that may contain negatives. Articulating this distinction is a strong signal. The sentinel is P[0] = 0, the prefix before index 0; it counts a match whose left boundary is 0. Use a numeric type that cannot overflow for every allowed prefix, and a count type that can hold up to n(n+1)/2 matches. Hash-map O(n) is expected under normal hashing, not a worst-case guarantee.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q1"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q2","surface":"track","en":"Heaps and top-K: when should you use a heap, quickselect, or sorting?","bodyEn":"For the problem \"find the k largest elements among n elements\": Approach Time Space Use when Sort, then take k O(n log n) O(1)–O(n) k is close to n, or the complete ordering is needed Size-k min-heap O(n log k) O(k) k ≪ n , or the input is a stream Quickselect O(n) average, O(n²) worst case O(1) one-off query with the entire array in memory Easy detail to reverse: to find the k largest elements, use a min-heap (its root is the smallest member of the retained k, so it is easy to evict). Conversely, use a max-heap to find the k smallest. PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(); // min-heap for ( int x : a) { pq. offer (x); if (pq. size () > k) pq. poll (); // discard the smallest element } // exactly the k largest elements remain The senior answer selects the approach from the constraints: \"if n arrives as a stream that cannot be retained in full, an O(n log k) heap is required; if the array is already in memory and this is a one-off query, quickselect gives O(n) expected time.\" Two heaps (a max-heap for the lower half + a min-heap for the upper half) maintain the median of a stream — LC 295, a common fintech interview problem because it connects to real-time statistics. Merge k sorted lists: keep the k current head pointers in a heap → O(N log k), where N is the total number of elements. Top-K by frequency: count with a HashMap, then heapify by count; if counts are bounded by n, bucket sort achieves true O(n). Quickselect degrades to O(n²) with consistently bad pivots → choose pivots randomly, or use median-of-medians for guaranteed O(n) (theoretical, and rarely implemented in an interview). Java's PriorityQueue is not a sorted collection — iterating over it does not produce ascending order; only repeated poll() calls do. Building a heap from an existing array takes O(n) (heapify), not O(n log n) — a detail interviewers like to challenge. Distributed top-K, freshness, shard skew, and recovery belong in (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q3); the leaderboard example is (11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q1). This topic only establishes the bounded local selection contract. Tie and output contract: define whether equal values are separate elements and how equal keys break ties. A heap gives a bounded set, not sorted output; sort the final k if ordered output is required. Streaming favors the heap; quickselect needs a mutable full input; sorting is simplest when deterministic order matters. Quickselect's O(n) is expected, with O(n²) worst case unless a stronger pivot strategy is chosen.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q2"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q3","surface":"track","en":"Binary search on the answer: how do you recognize a 'minimize the maximum' problem?","bodyEn":"There may be no sorted input array: first write the ordered answer domain, the feasibility predicate, and the boundary invariant. Signal: the problem asks for \"the smallest value of X for which the task remains possible\" or says \"minimize the maximum\" / \"maximize the minimum,\" and there is a monotonic predicate feasible(x): if x is feasible, every x' > x is also feasible. Binary-search the answer space, not an array: int lo = maxElement, hi = totalSum; // valid bounds while (lo < hi) { int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2 ; // avoid int overflow if ( feasible (mid)) hi = mid; // a smaller value may still work else lo = mid + 1 ; } return lo; // lo == hi == smallest answer Example: \"split an array into m contiguous segments while minimizing the largest segment sum\" (LC 410). feasible(x) = greedily scan once and count how many segments are required if each segment's sum must be ≤ x; it is feasible when the count is ≤ m. Complexity: O(n log(sum)). The same family includes Koko Eating Bananas (LC 875), Ship Packages in D Days (LC 1011), and Minimum Time to Complete Trips. The hardest part is not the binary search; it is implementing feasible(), usually as one O(n) greedy pass. Saying \"I will binary-search the answer and use a greedy feasibility check\" demonstrates the correct solution structure. Choose the bounds correctly: lo = the smallest value that could be feasible (often max(a), because every element must fit in some segment), while hi = a value guaranteed to be feasible (sum(a)). Incorrect bounds are the most common source of bugs. The while (lo < hi) + hi = mid / lo = mid + 1 template returns the smallest satisfying value — memorize one template and reuse it instead of reinventing it each time. The \"largest satisfying value\" case is symmetric: lo = mid, hi = mid - 1, with mid rounded upward. mid = (lo + hi) / 2 can overflow int when both bounds are near Integer.MAX_VALUE. Use lo + (hi - lo) / 2; the exact bound still depends on the chosen numeric type. For a real-valued answer, such as a minimum radius, run a fixed number of roughly 100 iterations or continue until hi - lo < 1e-9; do not compare floating-point values for equality. Practical analogues include choosing a rate-limit threshold or the minimum number of workers needed to drain a queue on time — the same reasoning template applies. Before the loop, state the domain [lo, hi], the predicate feasible(x), and the invariant: the smallest feasible answer, if it exists, remains inside [lo, hi]. For LC 410, feasible(x) means a left-to-right greedy scan needs at most m segments, with max(a) ≤ x ≤ sum(a); the predicate is false then true.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q3"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q4","surface":"track","en":"Graphs: when should you choose BFS, DFS, topological sort, or Dijkstra?","bodyEn":"Algorithm Answers Complexity BFS shortest path by number of edges (unweighted); level-order traversal O(V + E) DFS reachability, components, cycle detection, or enumeration O(V + E) with adjacency lists Topological sort execution order when dependencies form a DAG O(V + E) Dijkstra shortest path with non-negative edge weights depends on representation; heap + adjacency list is often O((V + E) log V) Union-Find are two vertices in the same component? count components; Kruskal ~O(α(n)) per operation Most common trap: using DFS for a \"shortest path\" problem. DFS finds a path, not necessarily the shortest path. Unweighted → BFS. Weighted → Dijkstra. Topological sort (Kahn's algorithm) — also a direct way to detect a cycle in a directed graph: Queue<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>(); for ( int v = 0 ; v < n; v++) if (indeg[v] == 0 ) q. offer (v); int done = 0 ; while (!q. isEmpty ()) { int v = q. poll (); order. add (v); done++; for ( int nx : adj[v]) if (--indeg[nx] == 0 ) q. offer (nx); } return done == n ? order : List. of (); // done < n => there is a cycle Many problems never use the word \"graph\": course scheduling (LC 207 — topological sort), word ladder (LC 127 — BFS), number of islands (LC 200 — DFS/Union-Find), package dependencies, and deadlocks between transactions (5.10 — cycle detection). Recognizing \"this is a graph\" is the difficult part. BFS on a grid uses a queue + a visited array; mark each cell when enqueuing it, not when dequeuing it — otherwise the same cell may enter the queue multiple times. Multi-source BFS: enqueue every source at the beginning (problems such as \"Rotting Oranges\" and \"distance to the nearest zero\") — many candidates run BFS n times and make the solution n times slower. 0-1 BFS: when every edge weight is either 0 or 1, use a Deque (push weight-0 edges to the front and weight-1 edges to the back) for O(V+E); Dijkstra is unnecessary. Dijkstra is incorrect with negative edges → use Bellman-Ford in O(VE); Bellman-Ford can also detect negative cycles, as in currency-exchange arbitrage — a strong fintech example. Recursive DFS on a graph with 10⁵ vertices can cause a Java StackOverflow → switch to an explicit stack. Cycle detection in a directed graph requires three colors (white/gray/black); in an undirected graph, visited plus skipping the edge back to the parent is sufficient — confusing the two is a common mistake. Complexity needs a representation: adjacency lists visit O(V + E) for reached vertices and edges, while an adjacency matrix may spend O(V²) scanning neighbors. DFS is not inherently cheaper than BFS. Dijkstra requires non-negative weights; qualify heap choice and stale entries, and use iterative DFS when external input can exceed the call-stack budget.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q4"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you distinguish dynamic programming from backtracking, and derive either solution instead of memorizing templates?","bodyEn":"Both explore a state space; the difference is what the problem asks you to reuse or enumerate. Aspect Dynamic programming Backtracking Signal overlapping subproblems plus an optimal/count/feasible result enumerate or find assignments under constraints Core cache one result per sufficient state choose → recurse → undo, pruning invalid branches Complexity reachable states × transition cost size of the explored search tree, often output-sensitive For DP, write five things before code: state meaning, transition, base cases, evaluation order, and where the answer lives. Start top-down when it exposes the recurrence; convert to bottom-up when stack depth, iteration order, or memory locality matters. Compress a dimension only after proving future transitions no longer need overwritten states. Store a predecessor/choice when the problem asks for the actual path, not just its score. // Top-down: state must contain every fact that affects future choices. long solve (State s) { if (s. done ()) return s. baseValue (); if (memo. containsKey (s)) return memo. get (s); long best = IDENTITY; for (Choice c : s. choices ()) best = combine (best, solve (s. take (c))); memo. put (s, best); return best; } For backtracking, define the partial assignment and invariant, generate only legal choices, apply one choice, recurse, then undo exactly that mutation. Sort when it enables duplicate skipping or monotone pruning. If two paths reach the same sufficient state and only the best/count result matters, memoization often turns the search into DP. Common mistakes are memoizing an incomplete state, using a mutable collection as a map key, pruning without a proof, sharing a result list that later mutations corrupt, and claiming O(2^n) when pruning or output size dominates. Validate the recurrence on a tiny brute-force oracle, include empty/minimal cases, and test reconstruction separately from the optimal value. Memoization removes repeated work only when the key contains every fact that affects continuation. DP cost is reachable states times transition cost; backtracking cost includes every emitted solution and the cost of copying it. A pruning rule needs a proof that no discarded branch can produce a required result.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q5"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q6","surface":"track","en":"When should you use a monotonic stack or deque, and how do interval and sweep-line problems relate?","bodyEn":"Use a monotonic structure when an element that is worse than a newer element can never become useful again. A decreasing stack answers next/previous greater, stock span, and histogram-boundary questions. State whether it stores values or indices and whether equal values are popped; that equality choice defines boundaries and duplicate behavior. When an item is popped, the current index often finalizes its answer. A monotonic deque handles a moving window. Remove expired indices from the front, remove dominated candidates from the back, then add the new index. The front is the best live candidate. Every index enters and leaves at most once, giving amortized O(n). Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>(); for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) { while (!dq. isEmpty () && dq. peekFirst () <= i - k) dq. removeFirst (); while (!dq. isEmpty () && a[dq. peekLast ()] <= a[i]) dq. removeLast (); dq. addLast (i); } For intervals, first define endpoint semantics. Half-open [start,end) intervals make adjacent ranges non-overlapping and match many time APIs. Sorting by start supports merge/insert/meeting-room scans. A sweep line converts ranges into ordered start/end events or deltas; maintain the active count/state between events. For equal coordinates, event ordering must follow the endpoint definition—do not rely on an accidental comparator tie. Recognize the shared invariant: discard state only after proving it cannot affect a future answer. For stacks/deques that proof is dominance; for merged intervals it is coverage; for sweep lines it is that all events before the cursor are finalized. Use long for coordinates/deltas when subtraction can overflow. With a huge sparse coordinate space, sort events or coordinate-compress; with a small bounded space, a difference array may be simpler. Test equal values, zero-length/adjacent intervals, duplicate endpoints, k=1, k=n, and empty input. Make duplicate policy executable: <= in this deque code removes an older equal candidate, so the newest equal index wins; using < keeps older equal candidates. For a stack, strict versus non-strict comparison changes which equal endpoint is assigned. State whether intervals are closed or half-open; zero-length and touching ranges follow that choice.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q6"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q7","surface":"track","en":"How should you reason about tree traversal, BST invariants, and tries?","bodyEn":"Start with what must flow down from ancestors, what must flow up from children, and whether processing is preorder, inorder, postorder, or level order. DFS suits subtree aggregation and path state; BFS suits levels and unweighted distance. Recursive DFS uses the call stack—on a degenerate tree, use an explicit stack or another strategy if depth can exceed safe limits. For a BST, the invariant is global: every key in the left subtree belongs in the permitted lower range and every key in the right subtree in the upper range. Checking only node.left < node < node.right misses a violation deeper in a subtree. Carry exclusive/inclusive bounds according to an explicit duplicate policy, and use a wide type or nullable bounds instead of computing value ± 1 near numeric limits. Plain BST operations are O(h), which becomes O(n) when unbalanced; O(log n) requires a balance guarantee. boolean valid (Node n, Long low, Long high) { if (n == null) return true; if (low != null && n.key <= low) return false; if (high != null && n.key >= high) return false; return valid (n.left, low, (long) n.key) && valid (n.right, (long) n.key, high); } A trie trades memory for prefix operations. Each edge represents a symbol; nodes need an end-of-key marker because a key can prefix another. Choose child storage from the alphabet and workload: fixed arrays are fast but sparse, maps save sparse space, compressed/radix tries collapse one-child chains. Define normalization, case, and Unicode code-point/grapheme semantics before coding. Many “tree problems” are tree DP: return a small summary such as height, validity, best downward path, and best subtree answer from each child instead of rescanning subtrees. If the input can contain shared nodes or cycles, it is a graph, so ownership and a visited policy matter. Test empty/single/skewed trees, duplicate policy, numeric boundaries, prefix-as-key, and non-ASCII input. BST validation is a global-bound invariant, not a child comparison. Choose strict or inclusive bounds for duplicates and carry them without value ± 1; use iterative traversal when depth is external or unbounded. A trie is prefix-correct only after alphabet, normalization, and symbol unit (ASCII, code point, or grapheme) are fixed.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q7"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q8","surface":"track","en":"Which bit-manipulation techniques are worth knowing, and what correctness traps matter in Java?","bodyEn":"Use bit operations when the domain is a fixed-width set of flags, parity/XOR relation, compact subset state, or power-of-two structure—not merely to make code shorter. long bit = 1L << k; // use long before shifting mask = bit; // set mask &= ~bit; // clear boolean has = (mask & bit) != 0; long low = mask & -mask; // lowest set bit, two's complement mask &= mask - 1; // remove lowest set bit Key patterns are XOR cancellation (x ^ x = 0) when the input contract really gives pairs, bitmasks for subset/state DP, Integer.bitCount/Long.bitCount, and submask enumeration: for (int sub = mask; sub != 0; sub = (sub - 1) & mask) { // every non-empty submask exactly once } In Java, shifts use only the low 5 bits of an int distance or low 6 bits for long; 1 << 40 therefore does not mean what it appears to mean. >> preserves the sign bit, while >>> shifts in zeroes. Parenthesize mixed comparisons and bit operations, distinguish signed from unsigned intent, and prove that the chosen width covers every state. Avoid clever arithmetic swaps and magic masks when library operations are clearer. Use BitSet or BigInteger when the state exceeds 64 bits, and document serialization/endianness when bits cross a protocol boundary. Check zero, the sign bit, maximum shift, overflow, duplicates outside the promised pairing rule, and whether the empty subset belongs in the answer. Complexity must include the state space: enumerating all masks is O(2^n) and all submasks across all masks is O(3^n). Compact storage does not make exponential work polynomial. JLS/API boundary: Java int and long arithmetic and shifts are fixed-width; ordinary arithmetic does not report overflow. The shift distance is masked, so a bit index must be within the chosen width (0..31 or 0..63) before using 1 << k or 1L << k. Use BitSet or BigInteger for a larger domain; their cost and serialization semantics differ.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q8"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q9","surface":"track","en":"Ten million fixed keys, a flood of \"does this key exist?\" requests, and 10 MB of RAM. How do you answer, and what does the memory actually buy?","bodyEn":"Size the problem first. Ten million 64-bit keys in a sorted array are 80 MB; a HashSet<Long> is several times that. Exact membership does not fit, so memory answers \"definitely not / probably yes\", and only a \"probably\" pays for an exact check. A Bloom filter, sized by arithmetic rather than habit: m = 10 MiB × 8 = 83,886,080 bits m / n ≈ 8.4 bits per key best k = (m/n) · ln 2 ≈ 5.8 → use 6 positions FP rate ≈ (1 − e^(−k·n/m))^k ≈ 1.8 % k = 3 (\"hash it three times\") ≈ 2.7 % Six positions do not need six hash functions: two 64-bit hashes combined as h1 + i·h2 give the same false-positive rate (Kirsch–Mitzenmacher). The whole filter is one long[] and one hash mix: final class Bloom { private static final int K = 6; private final long[] words = new long[1_310_720]; // 10 MiB = 83,886,080 bits private final long m = 64L words.length; void add(long key) { long h1 = mix(key), h2 = mix(h1) 1; // odd step: the K probes never coincide for (int i = 0; i < K; i++) { long bit = Long.remainderUnsigned(h1 + i h2, m); words[(int) (bit >>> 6)] = 1L << bit; // a long shift uses the low 6 bits } } boolean mightContain(long key) { long h1 = mix(key), h2 = mix(h1) 1; for (int i = 0; i < K; i++) { long bit = Long.remainderUnsigned(h1 + i h2, m); if ((words[(int) (bit >>> 6)] & (1L << bit)) == 0) return false; // one 0 bit is proof } return true; // only \"probably\": confirm on disk } private static long mix(long z) { // splitmix64 finalizer, a stand-in for xxHash z = (z ^ (z >>> 30)) 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9L; z = (z ^ (z >>> 27)) 0x94D049BB133111EBL; return z ^ (z >>> 31); } } Because the keys never change, a static filter does better. A binary fuse filter stores one fingerprint per key in about 1.13 × its width; 8.4 bits per key fits a 7-bit fingerprint — about 0.8% false positives, and three or four memory reads per lookup instead of six. The exact check for a \"probably\": sort the keys once on disk (external sort — sorted runs, then a k-way merge), keep every 4,096th key in memory as a sparse index (about 2,450 entries), and one page read confirms or rejects the key. What the interviewer is testing That you size before you choose, that \"no false negatives\" is the property doing the work, and that you can say what the false-positive rate costs downstream. a Bloom filter cannot delete and degrades as keys are added past its design n — harmless here, which is why the question says the keys never change. if the keys are dense integers in a known range (IDs 0..80M, say), a plain bitset is exact: one bit per possible value, 80M bits = 10 MB. Ask what the keys look like before reaching for hashing. the false-positive rate is a load number: at 1.8%, a million lookups per second for absent keys send about 18,000 per second to the exact check. That sizes the disk tier, not the filter. this is the mechanism inside LSM stores — RocksDB and Cassandra keep a filter per SSTable so a point read skips files that cannot hold the key (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q7) — and caches use one against penetration (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q3). hash quality matters more than count: use a well-mixed 64-bit hash (xxHash, Murmur3). Long.hashCode() folds the value to 32 bits with an XOR and is far too regular for this. in a scaled-out service the filter is built once offline and shipped as a file; every replica memory-maps the same bytes instead of rebuilding them.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q9"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q1","surface":"track","en":"Two pointers: trace the converging scan that finds a pair summing to a target.","bodyEn":"The array is sorted, so the sum at (L,R) tells you which pointer to move — and each move permanently discards a whole set of pairs. That is why one pass suffices. int l = 0 , r = a.length - 1 ; while (l < r) { int sum = a[l] + a[r]; if (sum == target) return new int []{l, r}; if (sum < target) l++; // need bigger & 8594; only L can help else r--; // need smaller & 8594; only R can help } The three shapes this pattern takes: converging (above), fast/slow (cycle detection, middle of a list), and same-direction read/write (in-place dedupe, where the write pointer lags the read pointer). Recognition Sorted array plus \"find a pair/triplet\", or \"remove/partition in place\", or \"is there a cycle\". The deeper explanation of variants is in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q4). This trace is valid only under the sorted-order (or equivalent monotone-feasibility) precondition. Without it, moving L or R can discard a pair that is still a solution. If values may overflow int, use a wider sum type before applying the same invariant.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q1"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q2","surface":"track","en":"Sliding window: trace a fixed window of size 3 as it slides, and show what changes per step.","bodyEn":"A window slides by one add and one remove — never by recomputing the whole window. That is the entire trick: O(1) work per step instead of O(k). long sum = 0 , best = Long.MIN_VALUE; for ( int r = 0 ; r < n; r++) { sum += a[r]; // the element sliding in if (r >= k) sum -= a[r - k]; // the one falling out if (r >= k - 1 ) best = Math. max (best, sum); } For a variable-size window the shape changes: expand right always, and while the window is invalid, shrink left. The full template plus the monotonicity requirement is in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-solving-framework-core-patterns.q2). The condition people forget Sliding window needs the property to be monotonic in window length. With negative numbers, \"subarray sum ≥ k\" is not — shrinking can increase the sum, so the window is unsound. Use prefix sums instead (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q7).","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q2"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q3","surface":"track","en":"Binary search: trace the halving, and state the invariant that makes it correct.","bodyEn":"Every comparison eliminates half the remaining candidates. Correctness comes from one invariant: the answer, if it exists, is always inside [lo, hi]. int lo = 0 , hi = n - 1 ; // inclusive bounds while (lo <= hi) { // <= : the 1-element case still runs int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2 ; // overflow-safe if (a[mid] == target) return mid; else if (a[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1 ; else hi = mid - 1 ; } return - 1 ; The version worth memorising is not this one — it is the boundary search (lowerBound): find the first index where a predicate turns true. It answers \"insert position\", \"first bad version\" and \"count of x\" without any of the off-by-one debates. // smallest i in [0,n] with ok(i) true; assumes ok is false...false,true...true int lo = 0 , hi = n; // half-open: hi is a valid answer while (lo < hi) { int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2 ; if ( ok (mid)) hi = mid; // keep mid as a candidate else lo = mid + 1 ; } return lo; // == n means \"no such index\" Termination, the thing that actually causes infinite loops: every branch must shrink the range. lo = mid without +1 loops forever when hi == lo+1. Pick one style — inclusive [lo,hi] with lo<=hi, or half-open [lo,hi) with lo<hi — and never mix them. The array is not the only thing you can search. Binary searching the answer space turns \"minimise the maximum\" into a feasibility check; see (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q3). Rotated arrays: compare a[mid] against a[lo]/a[hi] to decide which half is sorted, then binary search that half. Duplicates degrade it to O(n) worst case. In Java, Arrays.binarySearch returns -(insertionPoint) - 1 when absent — the negative encoding carries the boundary, so -result - 1 is the insert position. For answer-space search, replace “the array is sorted” with a written contract: ok(x) is false then true over an ordered domain, and [lo, hi] brackets the first true value. If that predicate is not monotone, this loop can return a plausible but wrong value.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q3"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q4","surface":"track","en":"Frequency counting: trace building a count map, and say when an array beats a HashMap.","bodyEn":"Counting turns \"compare everything with everything\" (O(n²)) into one pass plus O(1) lookups. The map is the algorithm; the rest is reading it. // idiomatic Java: merge is the one-liner people forget Map<Character,Integer> freq = new HashMap<>(); for ( char c : s. toCharArray ()) freq. merge (c, 1 , Integer::sum); // // fixed small alphabet: array avoids boxing and hashing; measure if the factor matters int [] cnt = new int [ 26 ]; for ( char c : s. toCharArray ()) cnt[c - 'a' ]++; Use When Why int[26] / int[128] Known small key domain (lowercase, ASCII, digits) No boxing, no hashing, cache-friendly — say this out loud in an interview HashMap Unbounded or non-integer keys O(1) average, but boxed Integer and pointer chasing int[] of size 2 Only \"seen once vs twice\" matters Or skip counting entirely — XOR does it in O(1) space (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q8) Counting is the state inside other patterns. The window in (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q2) is usually a frequency map, and \"window contains all of t\" compares two counts. Track a matched counter rather than comparing maps each step, or you pay O(k) per slide and lose the O(n). Two strings are anagrams iff their counts match: +1 for the first, -1 for the second, then assert every bucket is zero. One array, one pass, no sorting. Unicode breaks c - 'a'. A char in Java is a UTF-16 code unit, so anything outside the BMP arrives as a surrogate pair — iterate codePoints() when the input is real user text rather than LeetCode input. getOrDefault(k,0)+1 and merge(k,1,Integer::sum) are equivalent; computeIfAbsent is for building collections as values, not counters. The array version assumes lowercase ASCII a–z; Java char is a UTF-16 code unit, and HashMap has expected rather than guaranteed constant-time operations. There is no portable speed factor without a named workload, JDK, hardware, warm-up, and benchmark harness.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q4"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q5","surface":"track","en":"Matrix traversal: trace a spiral walk and show how the four shrinking boundaries stay correct.","bodyEn":"Spiral order is not a clever formula — it is four boundaries that close inward. Keep top/bottom/left/right and walk one edge at a time, shrinking after each. int top = 0 , bot = m - 1 , left = 0 , right = n - 1 ; List<Integer> out = new ArrayList<>(); while (top <= bot && left <= right) { for ( int c = left; c <= right; c++) out. add (g[top][c]); top++; for ( int r = top; r <= bot; r++) out. add (g[r][right]); right--; if (top <= bot) { // guard: row not consumed for ( int c = right; c >= left; c--) out. add (g[bot][c]); bot--; } if (left <= right) { // guard: col not consumed for ( int r = bot; r >= top; r--) out. add (g[r][left]); left++; } } The two guards are the whole interview. Without them, a 1×n or n×1 remainder gets visited twice — and it only shows up on non-square input, which is exactly the test case people skip. Related matrix moves, all boundary arithmetic rather than tricks: transpose then reverse each row = rotate 90° clockwise, in place. rn + c flattens 2-D to 1-D, so a row-sorted matrix binary searches as one array. mark the first row/column as dirty flags for set-matrix-zeroes in O(1) extra space. A grid is also a graph: neighbours are {{1,0},{-1,0},{0,1},{0,-1}}, and island/flood-fill problems are plain BFS/DFS (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q13). Reach for the traversal, not a bespoke loop. Always bound-check before dereferencing (0 <= r < m && 0 <= c < n) — in Java the reward for forgetting is ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException rather than the silent corruption you would get in C. The four boundaries are inclusive: after each move, the unvisited rectangle is the only remaining region. The guards prevent a single row or column from being emitted twice; for an empty matrix, return before initializing bottom and right from its dimensions.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q5"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q6","surface":"track","en":"Monotonic stack: trace \"next greater element\" and explain the amortised O(n).","bodyEn":"The stack holds indices whose answer is still unknown, kept in decreasing value order. A new element that is larger resolves — and pops — every smaller one waiting below it. int [] ans = new int [n]; Arrays. fill (ans, - 1 ); // \"no greater element\" default Deque<Integer> st = new ArrayDeque<>(); // holds INDICES, not values for ( int i = 0 ; i < n; i++) { // while the pending top is smaller, a[i] is its answer while (!st. isEmpty () && a[st. peek ()] < a[i]) ans[st. pop ()] = a[i]; st. push (i); } Why the nested while is still linear: the loop body is bounded by the total number of pops, and each index can be popped only once. n pushes ⇒ at most n pops across the entire run — this is the amortised argument interviewers want stated explicitly. Want Stack order Pop condition Next greater Decreasing a[top] < a[i] Next smaller Increasing a[top] > a[i] Previous greater Decreasing Same, but read the top before pushing Store indices, not values. You almost always need the distance (i - st.peek()) for problems like daily temperatures or largest rectangle in a histogram, and you cannot recover an index from a value. Circular variants (\"next greater in a circular array\") iterate 2n times using i % n, pushing only during the first pass. The stack does not need a second copy of the data. The sliding-window maximum is the same idea with a deque instead: pop from the back to keep it decreasing, and pop from the front when the index leaves the window. That is LC 239, the hardest of the window problems (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q6). In Java use ArrayDeque, not java.util.Stack — Stack is synchronised legacy built on Vector. Note ArrayDeque.push adds at the head, so peek sees the most recent element, which is what you want here. The O(n) claim is amortized: charge one push and one pop to each index, so total stack operations are at most 2n. The result still depends on equality policy: < leaves equal values pending, while <= pops them.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q6"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q7","surface":"track","en":"Prefix sum: trace the build, and show why it answers any range query in O(1).","bodyEn":"Precompute once so every later range query is one subtraction. sum(i..j) = P[j+1] - P[i] — the shared beginning cancels out. long [] p = new long [n + 1 ]; // n+1 entries; long avoids overflow for ( int i = 0 ; i < n; i++) p[i + 1 ] = p[i] + a[i]; // inclusive range [i..j], no edge cases thanks to the sentinel long range ( int i, int j) { return p[j + 1 ] - p[i]; } Paired with a hash map it counts subarrays. \"How many subarrays sum to k\" becomes: for each running sum s, how many earlier prefixes equalled s - k? Map<Long,Integer> seen = new HashMap<>(); seen. put ( 0L , 1 ); // the empty prefix — required long s = 0L ; long count = 0L ; for ( int x : a) { s += x; count += seen. getOrDefault (s - k, 0 ); // every match is one subarray seen. merge (s, 1 , Integer::sum); } Forgetting the sentinel Omit seen.put(0L, 1) and you miss every subarray starting at index 0 — a wrong answer that passes small hand-made tests and fails the real suite. Same for P[0] = 0. Overflow is a genuine failure here, not pedantry: 10⁵ values of 10⁴ each exceed int. Use long for the prefix array unless you have proved the bound. Extensions: a 2-D prefix sum answers submatrix sums with four lookups (inclusion–exclusion); a difference array is the inverse — add to a range in O(1), then one prefix pass materialises the result, which is how interval-booking problems are done. The deeper treatment of subarray-sum counting, including why sliding window is invalid with negatives, is in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q1). P[0] = 0 is the empty prefix before index 0, so a range beginning at zero uses the same subtraction as every other range. The O(1) query assumes the input stays unchanged after the O(n) build; updates need another structure. long is safe only after checking the input bound, and the frequency count needs room for all matches.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q7"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q8","surface":"track","en":"Overlapping intervals: trace the merge, and justify why sorting by start is the right move.","bodyEn":"Sort by start, then walk once holding a current interval. Each next interval either extends it or begins a new one — there is no third case. Arrays. sort (iv, Comparator. comparingInt (x -> x[ 0 ])); // by start List< int []> out = new ArrayList<>(); for ( int [] cur : iv) { int [] last = out. isEmpty () ? null : out. get (out. size () - 1 ); if (last != null && cur[ 0 ] <= last[ 1 ]) // touching counts as overlap last[ 1 ] = Math. max (last[ 1 ], cur[ 1 ]); // extend in place else out. add ( new int []{cur[ 0 ], cur[ 1 ]}); } max is not optional. [1,10] followed by [2,3] must stay [1,10]; writing last[1] = cur[1] silently truncates it. This is the single most common bug in interval merging. Problem Sort by Then Merge intervals Start Extend or emit, as above Max concurrent (meeting rooms) Split into events Sweep line: +1 at start, −1 at end, track the running peak Max non-overlapping (erase fewest) End Greedy: always keep the earliest-ending — see (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q9) Sorting by start vs end is a real decision, not a coin flip. By start you can merge; by end you can pack the most intervals. If you sort by the wrong key the greedy choice stops being optimal, so say which you chose and why. Decide up front whether the interval is closed or half-open. With half-open [s,e), cur[0] < last[1] is the overlap test and touching endpoints do not merge — for calendars and time slots this is usually what you want. The sweep line generalises past intervals: sort all boundary events, process in order, keep a running count. That is how you answer \"maximum rooms needed\" without ever materialising a timeline (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q6). This merge assumes closed intervals because touching endpoints satisfy start <= lastEnd. For half-open intervals use start < lastEnd; state whether zero-length ranges are valid. Sorting by start makes the scanned prefix fully resolved, while end-sorting serves a different greedy objective.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q8"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q9","surface":"track","en":"Greedy: trace coin change with canonical denominations, and state when greedy is provably wrong.","bodyEn":"Greedy takes the locally best choice and never reconsiders. That is only correct when a local optimum cannot destroy the global one — a property you must argue, not assume. // correct ONLY for canonical systems such as {1,5,10,25} int count = 0 ; for ( int c : coins) { // descending count += remaining / c; remaining %= c; } The two ways to justify greedy in an interview: An exchange argument — any optimal solution can be transformed into the greedy one without getting worse. Citing a known-safe structure. If neither is available, say \"greedy is a heuristic here; the exact solution is DP\". That scores better than a confident wrong claim. Problem Greedy rule Safe? Activity selection Earliest end time first Yes — classic exchange argument Jump game (reachability) Track the furthest reachable index Yes Fractional knapsack Best value/weight ratio Yes — items are divisible 0/1 knapsack Same ratio rule No — indivisible items, use DP Coin change, arbitrary coins Largest first No — as shown above The interview signal Volunteering where your greedy fails demonstrates more depth than the working solution does. Interviewers routinely follow up with \"are you sure that's optimal?\" — have the counterexample ready.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q9"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q10","surface":"track","en":"Top-K with a heap: trace the min-heap of size K and explain why it beats sorting.","bodyEn":"To keep the K largest, hold a min-heap of size K. Its root is the weakest survivor, so it is exactly what a better candidate should displace. PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(); // MIN-heap for top-K for ( int x : stream) { if (heap. size () < k) heap. offer (x); else if (x > heap. peek ()) { // beats the weakest heap. poll (); heap. offer (x); } } // heap now holds the K largest; heap.peek() is the Kth largest The counter-intuitive part, and the usual interview question: use a min-heap for the K largest. A max-heap would put the biggest element at the root, which tells you nothing about who should be evicted. Approach Time Space Use when Sort, take K O(n log n) O(n) K ≈ n, or you need the order anyway Min-heap size K O(n log K) O(K) K ≪ n, or a stream that does not fit in memory Quickselect O(n) average O(1) Array in memory, unordered K is fine, O(n²) worst case acceptable Streaming is the heap's real argument. Quickselect needs the whole array in memory and reorders it; the heap needs only K items and never sees the input twice. For \"top K of a feed\" the heap is the only one of the three that works. Java's PriorityQueue is a min-heap by default. For max behaviour pass Comparator.reverseOrder(). Its iterator() is not in sorted order — only poll() yields sorted output, which is a common silent bug. Building from a known collection is O(n) via new PriorityQueue (collection) (heapify), not n offers. Rarely the bottleneck, but worth knowing. Full comparison of heap vs quickselect vs sorting, including the k-closest and median-of-stream variants, is in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q2). This trace proves only the heap invariant: after each input, the root is the weakest retained candidate. It does not prove a universal speedup. Define a tie-breaker for equal values; if ordered output is required, poll or sort the k survivors at O(k log k).","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q10"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q11","surface":"track","en":"Backtracking: trace the explore-then-undo loop and show where pruning cuts the tree.","bodyEn":"Backtracking is DFS over a decision tree: choose, recurse, then undo the choice. The undo is what makes one mutable buffer serve the whole search. void backtrack ( int start, List<Integer> path) { out. add ( new ArrayList<>(path)); // COPY — path keeps mutating for ( int i = start; i < n; i++) { // if (!promising(i)) continue; <- pruning goes here path. add (a[i]); // choose backtrack (i + 1 , path); // explore (i+1 = no reuse) path. remove (path. size () - 1 ); // UNDO — the backtrack step } } Two bugs account for most failures here. Adding path instead of new ArrayList (path) stores a reference that later mutations empty — every result ends up identical or blank. Forgetting the remove leaks state into sibling branches, so results grow monotonically and nothing is reproducible. Want Recurse with Subsets / combinations (no reuse) i + 1 Combinations with repetition allowed i Permutations 0 plus a used[] flag array Skip duplicate results Sort first, then if (i > start && a[i] == a[i-1]) continue; Complexity is inherently exponential — O(2ⁿ) subsets, O(n!) permutations — so pruning is not an optimisation, it is the difference between finishing and not. State the bound, then say what prunes it: an infeasibility check, a bound on the partial cost (branch and bound), or a sorted order that lets you break instead of continue. Backtracking vs DP: backtracking enumerates solutions; DP counts or optimises over overlapping subproblems. \"How many ways\" or \"the minimum cost\" with repeating subproblems ⇒ DP (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q15). Enumerating and counting is the same algorithm with an exponentially worse runtime. N-Queens is the canonical pruning exercise: track occupied columns and both diagonals (r+c, r-c) in sets, so validity is O(1) instead of an O(n) scan per placement. The derivation-not-memorisation framing for both families is in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q5). For enumeration, report output-sensitive cost: copying each path costs its length, so total work is at least the sum of emitted solution sizes, not only O(2^n). Pruning improves the explored tree only when its condition is sound; it does not turn the worst case polynomial.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q11"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q12","surface":"track","en":"Binary tree traversal: trace inorder and say which traversal each problem actually needs.","bodyEn":"The three depth-first orders differ only in when you visit the node relative to its subtrees. Everything else is identical. void inorder (Node t) { if (t == null ) return ; inorder (t.left); // everything smaller, in order visit (t); // move this line to change the traversal inorder (t.right); // everything larger, in order } Validating a BST is the classic trap. Comparing each node only with its children passes invalid trees — a node deep in the left subtree can still exceed the root. Carry bounds down instead: boolean valid (Node t, long lo, long hi) { if (t == null ) return true ; if (t.val <= lo t.val >= hi) return false ; // range, not parent return valid (t.left, lo, t.val) && valid (t.right, t.val, hi); } // long bounds so Integer.MIN_VALUE/MAX_VALUE as node values still work Recursion depth is a real limit. A degenerate tree is a linked list: 10⁵ nodes overflow the default JVM stack. The iterative form uses an explicit Deque and is what you write when the input size is unbounded — or you rebalance. BFS versus DFS on trees follows the question: \"level order\", \"minimum depth\", \"right side view\" and \"zigzag\" are all BFS with a queue.size() snapshot per level. Depth, diameter and subtree sums are DFS, because they need the children's results before the parent's. Postorder is the one to reach for when a node's answer depends on its subtrees — height, diameter, \"is this subtree balanced\", and pruning a subtree all compute bottom-up. Morris traversal reaches O(1) space by temporarily rewiring right pointers. Worth naming as a follow-up, not worth writing under time pressure. Tries and BST invariants get fuller treatment in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q7). The long bounds shown implement a strict-duplicate policy for an int key; if duplicates are allowed, encode that policy in one side's inclusive bound instead of silently changing comparisons. The recursive version uses O(h) call-stack space, so use an explicit stack when h is not bounded.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q12"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q13","surface":"track","en":"DFS vs BFS on a graph: trace both from the same node and show why BFS alone finds shortest paths.","bodyEn":"Same traversal, different container: DFS uses a stack (or recursion) and goes deep; BFS uses a queue and expands in rings of equal distance. // BFS: shortest path in an UNWEIGHTED graph Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>(); int [] dist = new int [n]; Arrays. fill (dist, - 1 ); q. add (src); dist[src] = 0 ; while (!q. isEmpty ()) { int u = q. poll (); for ( int v : adj. get (u)) if (dist[v] < 0 ) { // mark ON ENQUEUE, not on dequeue dist[v] = dist[u] + 1 ; q. add (v); } } Marking on enqueue is not a style choice. Mark on dequeue and a node reachable from several frontier nodes gets queued repeatedly — the queue blows up and the complexity degrades badly on dense graphs. Question Algorithm Why Fewest edges / minimum moves BFS Rings of equal distance Connectivity, cycle detection, components DFS Same O(V + E) asymptotic; stack/frontier, ordering, and early exit differ Order respecting dependencies Topological sort Kahn's BFS on in-degrees, or DFS postorder reversed Shortest path with non-negative weights Dijkstra BFS is wrong once edges have differing costs Weights of only 0 and 1 0-1 BFS Deque: push-front for 0, push-back for 1 A grid is a graph with implicit edges, so islands, flood fill and shortest-path-through-a-maze are these same two loops with {{1,0},{-1,0},{0,1},{0,-1}} as the adjacency (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q5). Multi-source BFS seeds the queue with every source at distance 0 — \"nearest exit\", \"rotting oranges\", \"distance to the closest 0\" all collapse to one pass instead of one BFS per source. Cycle detection differs by graph type: undirected needs a parent check; directed needs three colours (white/grey/black), because seeing a visited node is fine but seeing a node still on the stack is a back edge. The choice matrix including Dijkstra and topological sort is expanded in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q4). BFS's shortest-path claim assumes an unweighted graph (or equal edge costs), a consistent adjacency representation, and visited marking on enqueue. DFS can find a path but has no shortest-path guarantee; recursive depth is a resource limit, not a complexity improvement.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q13"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q14","surface":"track","en":"Level-order BFS: trace the per-level loop and explain the size snapshot.","bodyEn":"When a problem asks for something per level — not just per node — you need to know where each level ends. One line does it: snapshot queue.size() before draining. Deque<Node> q = new ArrayDeque<>(); q. add (root); while (!q. isEmpty ()) { int sz = q. size (); // SNAPSHOT — this level's width List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>(); for ( int i = 0 ; i < sz; i++) { // drain exactly sz, not q.size() Node t = q. poll (); level. add (t.val); if (t.left != null ) q. add (t.left); if (t.right != null ) q. add (t.right); } out. add (level); // one entry per level } Everything \"per level\" is this loop with a different line inside it: the last element of level is the right-side view; max(level) is the largest value per row; reversing on alternate passes is zigzag order; the pass index when you first meet a leaf is the minimum depth. Two traps A null root must be handled before the loop, or q.add(null) produces a NullPointerException on the first poll. And on a graph rather than a tree, you still need a visited set marked on enqueue (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q13) — a tree cannot revisit a node, a graph can. The queue-size snapshot is a tree-level invariant, not a promise about graph traversal. Java's ArrayDeque rejects null elements, so handle a null root before add; for a graph, mark node identity visited on enqueue.","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q14"},{"id":"19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q15","surface":"track","en":"Dynamic programming: fill a DP table by hand and derive the recurrence from the table.","bodyEn":"DP is recursion plus memory. The table is not the algorithm — the recurrence is, and the table is just where you put its results so each subproblem is solved once. // bottom-up, one rolling row int [] dp = new int [n]; Arrays. fill (dp, 1 ); // first row: one path along the edge for ( int i = 1 ; i < m; i++) for ( int j = 1 ; j < n; j++) dp[j] += dp[j - 1 ]; // dp[j]=from above, dp[j-1]=from left return dp[n - 1 ]; How to derive a DP instead of recalling one — the four questions, in order: State: what minimal set of variables identifies a subproblem? (dp[i][j] = ways to reach cell (i,j)) Recurrence: how does a state follow from smaller ones? (arrive from above or from the left) Base case: which states are known outright? (the first row and column: one path each) Order: what fill order guarantees dependencies are ready? (top-to-bottom, left-to-right) Answer those four out loud and the code writes itself. Skip them and you are pattern-matching against remembered solutions, which fails the moment the problem is twisted slightly. Family State Recurrence shape 0/1 knapsack dp[i][w] take vs skip: max(dp[i-1][w], val + dp[i-1][w - wt]) Longest common subsequence dp[i][j] match ⇒ 1+dp[i-1][j-1] ; else max of dropping either Coin change (min coins) dp[amount] min(dp[a], 1+dp[a-coin]) — the case greedy got wrong (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q9) Longest increasing subsequence dp[i] O(n²) pairwise, or O(n log n) with patience sorting + binary search Top-down vs bottom-up. Memoised recursion mirrors the recurrence and only visits reachable states — better when the state space is sparse or the order is awkward. Tabulation avoids stack depth and enables the rolling-array space trick. Both are the same recurrence; say which you chose and why. Space reduction is the standard follow-up. If dp[i] reads only dp[i-1], two rows suffice; if it reads only dp[i][j-1] and dp[i-1][j], one rolling row does. Iterate j backwards for 0/1 knapsack, or you reuse an item twice and silently solve the unbounded variant instead. Reconstructing the choices, not just the optimal value, means either storing parent pointers or walking the table backwards, re-deriving which branch produced each cell. The DP-versus-backtracking decision is in (19-dsa-leetcode.the-patterns-that-keep-coming-up.q5), and the derivation framing continues (19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q11). The table is correct because each cell depends on already-filled neighbors. Rolling storage is valid only after proving no overwritten state is needed; reconstructing a path needs parent choices or a reverse walk. DP still costs reachable states times transitions, and requesting every solution may be output-dominated. Handle an empty grid before dp[n - 1].","contextEn":"DSA & LeetCode — the patterns and how to present them","href":"/topics/19-dsa-leetcode#question-19-dsa-leetcode.fifteen-patterns-visualized.q15"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q1","surface":"track","en":"How do metrics, logs, and traces differ, and when should each be used?","bodyEn":"There is no fixed count of “pillars.” Choose signals by the question, decision, and risk: metrics, logs, and traces are common, while profiles, events, continuous verification, and business-correctness signals may be the better fit. Name the signal's owner, cost, retention, and failure behavior. Question Signal Useful for Limit Is a journey healthy? metrics rate, error ratio, latency, queue age aggregate; cannot identify one request What happened? logs and events discrete context, audit, state transitions volume, privacy, and search cost Where did this request go? traces causal path and dependency timing sampling, propagation gaps, and storage cost Why is CPU, lock, or allocation high? profiles hot code, lock, and allocation evidence capture overhead and sensitive-data handling Did the business outcome become correct and fresh? business signals correctness, freshness, durability, and reconciliation domain-specific contract and denominator The standard investigation flow is: a metric triggers an alert (p99 spikes) → a trace identifies the slow span → that span's logs explain why. a common mistake is deriving metrics from logs, such as counting error lines to alert. It is expensive, slow, and breaks when logs are rate-limited. The code should emit counters and histograms directly. a metric is a lossy aggregation: it cannot identify one specific request. That is the trace's job. an exemplar in Prometheus and OTel attaches a trace ID to a histogram observation, allowing an operator to jump directly from a p99 spike on a dashboard to the trace that caused it. Mentioning this is a strong interview signal. continuous profiling with tools such as Pyroscope or Parca answers 'which line of code consumes CPU?', something a trace cannot reveal (21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q3) events and business-correctness signals answer state-transition, freshness, reconciliation, or audit questions that aggregate telemetry cannot answer signals should share bounded navigation dimensions such as service, env, and version; do not force user IDs, order IDs, or trace IDs into high-volume metric labels Signal budget worksheet (illustrative fields, not provider prices): Input Record before rollout Why it matters Environment / region / provider prod / ap-southeast-1 / OTel Collector + chosen backend residency, ingestion pricing, limits, and ownership differ Volume and shape peak and p95 requests/events per second; spans per request turn traffic into bytes, series, and collector queue estimates Cardinality active series and bounded values per label check backend limits and the effect of route, status, tenant, and version Retention and sampling metrics/logs/traces/profiles days; head/tail rates price and investigation coverage are policy choices Failure budget maximum telemetry loss, queue memory, export delay keep telemetry from blocking or exhausting the application Replace every example with measured values for the target region and provider. Cost and retention are not properties of the signal type alone. In an OTA, flight-search p99 spikes → a trace shows that the GDS call consumes 90% of the request → the logs for that span reveal three retries after timeouts. Without a trace, the backend and integration teams can spend hours blaming each other. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The actual user journeys and correctness or freshness invariants that should become SLIs — beyond HTTP status and latency. The OTel semantic-convention version, SDK and collector distribution, backend, region, retention and sampling policy in scope; every threshold, provider cost and collector setting on this page is illustrative until audited against a real deployment. The cardinality, event volume, queue and memory, and telemetry-loss budgets acceptable per service and environment. How the alerting path is monitored when the primary metrics or log backend is down, and which data is personal, financial or security-sensitive — and who may read raw telemetry. What falsifies an SLO-based page: too little traffic, a manipulated denominator, a missing critical flow, or a known correctness failure the SLI cannot see. What falsifies tail sampling: collector decision latency or memory beyond budget, errors dropped before the decision point, or backend cost forcing an unsafe rate.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q1"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2","surface":"track","en":"How does OpenTelemetry propagate trace context across services and through message queues?","bodyEn":"A trace is a causally related set of spans. Over synchronous HTTP/RPC, W3C Trace Context commonly carries the active context and the server span becomes a child of the client span. traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01 ^ ^ ^ ^ trace-id parent span-id flags version Pin the OpenTelemetry SDK, Collector distribution, propagator, and semantic-convention version used by the deployment. The source review used semantic-conventions 1.44.0 as a dated snapshot; it is not a universal target, because conventions have mixed stability and migration guidance. Record the schema URL/version and an upgrade policy before relying on attribute names. At the producer boundary, inject context once into message properties/headers with the configured propagator; at each consumer boundary, extract it once and model send, receive, process, and settle according to the pinned OpenTelemetry messaging conventions. A synchronous hop makes the server span a child. A queue hop carries the same context in record headers, and the consumer links to it — the shape that stays honest for batches and fan-out. Which relation to record: one message: a parent–child span can be valid when the instrumentation contract allows it a batch: link every message-creation context fan-out: each consumer span links to the creation context, rather than inventing one consumer as the parent of its siblings redelivery: retain a stable message identity and record the attempt, without pretending that every attempt is a new causal root Exact span names and attributes remain SDK/instrumentation/version-specific. propagator. inject (Context. current (), record. headers (), (headers, key, value) -> headers. add (key, value. getBytes (UTF_8))); Links versus parent–child A span link records asynchronous causality without forcing a batch or fan-out into one misleading parent tree. Parent–child is a choice for a single-message process only when the pinned convention and instrumentation conditions allow it. For a batch, link every message context; selecting one message as the parent misrepresents the rest. For fan-out, create independent consumer spans that link to the producer/message-creation context. Test redelivery and retries separately so correlation remains useful without double-counting business work. Propagation is a contract: standardize propagators, header size, trust boundaries, retries/redelivery, and what happens when headers are absent or malformed. Do not allow an untrusted external trace header to overwrite security identity; tracing context is correlation, not authentication. Head sampling decides early and is operationally cheap; tail sampling decides after collectors see enough spans and can retain policy-selected slow/error traces, but costs buffering and requires all relevant spans to reach the decision point. Tail sampling cannot recover spans already dropped by an upstream head sampler. Choose rates from traffic, investigation value, privacy, and cost—“keep 100% of every slow/error trace” is not universally affordable or even well-defined. Context can be lost at executors, callbacks, reactive boundaries, custom clients, and message libraries; virtual threads do not remove that need. Test one trace across each real boundary. The boundary test matrix should cover HTTP ingress/egress, one message, batch, fan-out, redelivery, missing/malformed headers, executor/callback/reactive hops, and exporter loss. Assert that an untrusted trace header cannot change the authenticated principal or authorization decision. Put trace_id in structured logs, keep baggage small and allowlisted, and never propagate PII/secrets merely for convenience (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4).","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do RED and USE guide metric selection for services and resources?","bodyEn":"RED and USE are measurement lenses, not a fixed SLI set or a law. Start with the user journey and the decision the signal must support; then apply each lens where it fits instead of measuring everything without knowing what to inspect. RED is for services that handle requests; USE is for resources such as CPU, disks, pools, and queues: Lens Signal Measures Java-service example RED R ate requests per second http_server_requests_seconds_count RED E rrors failed requests per second and error ratio 5xx ratio per route template RED D uration the distribution of processing time, using a histogram rather than an average p99 from _bucket USE U tilization percentage of time busy hikaricp_connections_active over _max USE S aturation queued demand, such as run queue, pool wait, or lag hikaricp_connections_pending , consumer lag USE E rrors failures in the resource itself hikaricp_connections_timeout_total A minimum service dashboard has RED for critical endpoints plus USE for the connection pool, thread pool, and consumer lag. The SLO may measure availability or latency, but it may instead need freshness, correctness, queue age, durability, or a business outcome; RED/USE should explain that contract, not replace it. A useful closing line 'High utilization is not necessarily painful; saturation is what the customer experiences.' A CPU at 90% with an empty run queue is healthy—it is using the capacity you paid for. do not measure averages. An average of 200 ms may combine 95% of requests at 50 ms with 5% at 3 s. Use histograms and inspect p50/p95/p99. do not average percentiles across instances. Adding the p99 of ten pods and dividing by ten produces a meaningless number. Aggregate histogram buckets first, then calculate the percentile (histogram_quantile over rate(..._bucket[5m]) performs that aggregation). measure latency separately for successes and errors: failed requests often fail fast and pull p99 down, hiding the real problem coordinated omission occurs when server-side measurement misses time spent waiting outside its queue; add client-side or load-balancer measurements Count a logical user request and a retry attempt according to the SLI contract; otherwise retries can inflate rate and error numbers or hide an unknown outcome. useful Java-service saturation signals include hikaricp.connections.pending, thread-pool queue length, GC pause time, and Kafka consumer lag. These four catch most incidents. Google's four golden signals are RED plus saturation. Being able to connect both frameworks is sufficient. Concrete PromQL for one HTTP service (adapt metric names to the instrumentation): RED — request rate by bounded route template sum by (route) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m])) RED — 5xx ratio sum by (route) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{status=~\"5..\"}[5m])) / clamp_min(sum by (route) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m])), 1e-9) RED — p99 from a classic histogram; keep le during aggregation histogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (route, le) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket[5m]))) USE — utilization of the JDBC pool (busy share, not yet pain) max by (pool) (hikaricp_connections_active / hikaricp_connections_max) USE — saturation: threads waiting for a connection max by (pool) (hikaricp_connections_pending) Impact: Benefit: the queries make “watch latency” testable and alertable. Cost: p99 is estimated from bucket boundaries; raw URL, user or trace-id labels create unbounded series. Boundary: metrics reveal symptoms and pressure; traces/logs explain the request and dependency that caused them.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q3"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q4","surface":"track","en":"Why can one bad label cause a cardinality explosion that takes down the entire metrics system?","bodyEn":"Cardinality is the number of distinct time series. In a Prometheus-style backend, each label combination creates a series and active-series memory is a real limit; exact storage, ingestion, and billing behavior is provider-specific. 1 series — safe http_requests_total{service=\"booking\"} 5 endpoints x 4 statuses x 3 methods = 60 series — still safe http_requests_total{service=\"booking\", endpoint=\"/bookings\", status=\"200\", method=\"POST\"} + user_id (1 million users) = 60,000,000 series — failure http_requests_total{service=\"booking\", ..., user_id=\"u_8412773\"} Labels with unbounded values are almost always the cause: user_id, booking_id, email, trace_id, full URLs containing path IDs, or exception messages. Prevention: label values must come from a finite, known set normalise paths to /bookings/{id}, not /bookings/8H2K; this is the most common mistake high-dimensional analysis by user or booking belongs in logs or traces, not metrics configure provider-specific scrape/ingestion/series limits; sample_limit bounds one scrape but does not replace backend cardinality budgets or query protection Cardinality, cost, and retention worksheet (illustrative fields): Dimension Record by environment/region/provider Decision or test Metric series active series, label value counts, histogram buckets, churn from deploys product of bounded dimensions stays under the backend budget Logs events/sec, bytes/event, sampled rate, indexed fields, retention days estimate ingest/index cost and keep high-cardinality detail out of labels Traces/profiles spans/request, fan-out, head/tail rate, profile frequency, retention bound collector memory and decide what evidence must survive Region/provider residency, pricing unit, quota, deletion/legal-hold rule do not copy a cost or retention number between providers a quick estimate: a metric's cardinality is the product of the value counts for all labels. Adding a label with ten values multiplies cardinality by ten; it does not add ten. symptoms include steadily growing Prometheus RAM until OOM, progressively slower queries, and increasing scrape_duration. Diagnose with topk(10, count by (__name__)({__name__=~\".+\"})) and the /api/v1/status/tsdb endpoint. using an exception message as a label is a subtle trap: error=\"connection timeout to 10.0.3.41:5432\" makes each IP and port combination a new series. Use a classified error type such as error=\"timeout\". histogram le buckets also multiply cardinality: a 12-bucket histogram creates 12 series plus two. Do not add a histogram to an already highly labeled metric. continuous deployments plus a version label create churn because old series consume RAM until retention expires. Consider removing version from high-frequency metrics. in systems billed by custom metric, such as Datadog or CloudWatch, a cardinality explosion may not crash the platform; it explodes the bill, sometimes discovered a month later Pre-rollout tests: reject user/order/trace IDs, raw URLs, exception messages, and secrets as metric labels generate representative traffic and assert series growth stays under the budget assert route normalization emit synthetic PII/token/payment fields and verify source redaction verify retention/deletion and legal-hold behavior Keep trace IDs in controlled detail or exemplars, not primary labels, and test that forged trace context cannot authorize a request.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q4"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q1","surface":"track","en":"What decisions should SLI, SLO, SLA, and an error budget drive?","bodyEn":"SLI is a measured user-relevant outcome. It may be availability/latency, freshness, correctness, queue age, durability, or a business success measure; for example, the ratio of valid booking requests completed successfully within 300 ms. SLO is the target and measurement window for an SLI, plus its inclusion/exclusion rules. SLA is an external agreement; consequences may include service credits, termination rights, reporting, or other remedies—not necessarily a financial penalty in every contract. An error budget is the allowed bad portion. The 99.9%/43m12s arithmetic is an example for a time-based objective over exactly 30 days; a request-based objective allows 0.1% of eligible requests, not 43 minutes. Use budget and burn-rate data to govern risk: release pace, experiment exposure, maintenance, reliability work, and escalation. The response to exhausted budget is a policy agreed with product and engineering; it can include slowing risky changes, narrowing rollout, or prioritizing reliability, but an automatic company-wide “feature freeze” is not universal. What the objective buys in this illustrative 30-day calculation (43,200 minutes; not a universal target): Objective Time-based budget / 30d Request-based, at 1M req/day What that buys 99% 7h 12m 300,000 bad requests a comfortable internal tool 99.5% 3h 36m 150,000 a normal internal service 99.9% 43m 12s 30,000 one possible public-facing policy; validate with journey data 99.95% 21m 36s 15,000 needs redundancy, not just care 99.99% 4m 19s 3,000 shorter than most human responses; it has to be automatic Request-based counterexample: a 10-minute outage at 1,000 eligible requests/second creates about 600,000 bad requests. a time-based 30-day SLO may still pass, because 10 minutes is below 43m12s a 99.9% request SLO with 1M requests/day has only 30,000 bad requests of budget for the month This is why the journey, denominator, and window must be stated together. Read the last row before promising it. At 99.99% a single failed deploy that takes five minutes to roll back has spent the entire month, so the objective is really a statement about automation, not about effort. Burn rate is the number that makes an SLO alertable. It is how fast the budget is being spent relative to spending it exactly evenly. The windows and thresholds below are examples derived from the stated 30-day objective, not universal alert settings: burn rate = observed error ratio / (1 - SLO) for a 99.9% objective the budget is a 0.1% error ratio, so: observed 0.1% -> burn 1x the budget lasts exactly 30 days observed 1.44% -> burn 14.4x the budget is gone in ~2 days observed 10% -> burn 100x the budget is gone in ~7 hours Alert on burn rate with two windows, never on a raw threshold. The long window decides that something is really wrong; the short window makes the alert stop when the incident does: Long window Short window Burn rate Budget spent before it fires Action 1 hour 5 min 14.4× 2% page — fast burn, someone is losing money now 6 hours 30 min 6× 5% page — sustained, will exhaust the month 1 day 2 hours 3× 10% ticket — real, not urgent 3 days 6 hours 1× 10% ticket — the slow leak nobody notices both windows must be burning, or a five-minute blip pages someone at 3am alert: HighErrorBudgetBurn expr: ( job:slo_errors:ratio_rate1h{job=\"checkout\"} > (14.4 0.001) and job:slo_errors:ratio_rate5m{job=\"checkout\"} > (14.4 0.001) ) for : 2m labels: { severity: page } annotations: summary: \"checkout is burning its error budget 14.4x — 2% spent in an hour\" Why two windows and not one A single long window is slow to fire and, worse, slow to clear — it keeps paging for hours after the incident is fixed, which is how a team learns to ignore the alert. A single short window fires on every blip. The short window is the reset switch. Separate availability from correctness A 100% availability target is usually unrealistic and produces no learning budget. Some correctness invariants—no unbalanced ledger entry, no unauthorized transfer—are intentionally absolute and need transactional controls, reconciliation, and audit rather than being diluted into an availability SLO. Measure at multiple meaningful points: client/edge for user experience and service boundaries for diagnosis. Define eligible events, “good,” planned maintenance, retries, partial responses, low traffic, and data latency so teams cannot reinterpret the SLO during an incident. Request-based SLIs suit event volume; time-window SLIs can be useful for sparse traffic but hide burst size. Segment journeys with materially different expectations instead of averaging critical checkout with history browsing. Dependency SLO multiplication is only a rough model under serial, independent, no-mitigation assumptions. Redundancy, caching, hedging, fallbacks, correlated failures, and traffic split change end-to-end availability. Measure the composed user journey and use dependency models for design, not as a mathematical ceiling.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q1"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should alerts be designed to avoid fatigue: should they fire on symptoms or causes?","bodyEn":"Page on a condition that is urgent, actionable, and important. User-visible symptoms and fast error-budget burn are strong defaults because they aggregate many causes. Cause-based signals can still justify a page when they predict imminent impact and require immediate action—for example exhausted queue capacity, a certificate about to expire, data-loss risk, or a failed redundancy layer before the last copy fails. Route Contract Example Page a named responder must act now to limit material impact fast multi-window SLO burn, ledger invariant breach Ticket/work queue action has an owner and due window but can wait for business hours slow budget burn, capacity forecast Dashboard/event context for diagnosis; no guaranteed action CPU, pod restart, GC cycle Multi-window, multi-burn-rate alerts pair a short window for fast incidents with a longer confirmation window for sustained burn. Values such as 14.4× or 6× are examples derived from an SLO window and desired exhaustion time, not universal thresholds; calculate them for the actual objective and traffic. Operational multi-window table (example policy to tune): Condition Example windows Traffic floor Owner / runbook / action If monitoring is down Fast user-impact burn 1h + 5m at 14.4× Enough eligible requests in both windows; example floor: 100 in the short window service on-call; rollback/degrade runbook; mitigate now independent heartbeat pages monitoring owner; use manual escalation Sustained burn 6h + 30m at 6× confirm denominator is non-zero and representative service owner; capacity/release runbook; act before budget exhaustion black-box journey and last-known SLI; do not interpret stale data as zero Slow burn 1d + 2h at 3× route to ticket when volume supports a meaningful estimate service owner; capacity/reliability ticket; fix in business window record missing-data ticket and inspect independent trend/heartbeat Missing evaluator or notification heartbeat/evaluation age beyond contract not a request-volume alert; synthetic check is the floor monitoring owner; monitoring-path runbook; restore alert delivery page through an independent channel and retain manual escalation The traffic floor is an anti-noise guard, not permission to ignore low-volume correctness, security, or freshness signals; use a synthetic journey or an absolute invariant for those cases. Every routed alert needs an owner, severity, tested notification path, evidence, and a first safe action. A runbook is especially valuable for operational pages, but a security or correctness alert may instead require a restricted incident procedure. Tune for duration to the failure mode. Five minutes may suppress noise but can be unacceptable for rapid data loss; use multi-window logic, hysteresis, grouping, inhibition, and deduplication based on evidence. “Two non-actionable alerts” is a review trigger, not an automatic delete if the signal reveals a missing capability. Measure pages, acknowledgment, actionable rate, duplicate volume, escalation, and missed incidents per rotation. There is no universal sustainable pages-per-week number: staffing, duration, severity, follow-up load, and recovery opportunity all matter. Also detect abnormal silence with expected-traffic or heartbeat signals, especially for low-error systems.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q2"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q3","surface":"track","en":"What is the incident-response process from the initial page through the postmortem?","bodyEn":"Incident response optimizes safe impact reduction while preserving the evidence and control needed for the incident type. Declare and scope: record impact, affected journeys/tenants/data, severity, incident lead, and communication cadence. Stabilize: choose the lowest-risk mitigation supported by evidence—rollback, disable a flag, shed/degrade load, fail over, revoke credentials, or isolate a component. A recent deployment is a strong clue, not proof that immediate rollback is always safe. Coordinate: separate incident command, technical operations, communications, and subject expertise when incident size permits. Small teams may combine roles, but one person owns decisions and the timeline. Verify recovery: check user SLIs, queues/backlogs, data integrity, security containment, and downstream health; “graphs turned green” may not mean work or data is correct. Transition: preserve artifacts, define monitoring/reconciliation, communicate status, and hand off follow-up risk. Closure gate: close only when every condition below holds. If any remains unknown, keep the incident in monitoring, recovery, or reconciliation status with an owner and next check. Closure conditions: the user SLI has recovered and held for the agreed verification window data correctness and reconciliation are checked security/privacy containment is verified queues/backlogs and customer-visible state are accounted for telemetry/evaluator/notification paths are healthy Availability and security can differ For a routine availability incident, restore service before deep root-cause analysis. For suspected compromise, fraud, or data corruption, blindly restoring or destroying evidence can increase harm. Follow the security/data incident procedure, contain access, and involve legal/privacy/compliance roles where required. The learning review should be blameless and evidence-based: timeline, contributing technical/organizational conditions, detection and decision quality, impact, what helped, what made recovery harder, and actions with owners and verification dates. “Five whys” is one prompt, not a mandatory proof of one root cause; complex incidents usually have interacting causes and failed defenses. Track metrics only when they drive improvement. Time to detect/acknowledge/mitigate/recover, customer harm, data reconciliation, and recurrence can be useful, but a single MTTR average hides severity and encourages premature closure. Review distributions and qualitative decision points. Corrective actions should reduce recurrence probability, blast radius, detection time, or recovery risk. Exercise rollback/failover, credential rotation, backups/restores, degraded modes, and communication through game days. Fintech/OTA recovery always includes reconciliation for stuck bookings, duplicate attempts, and ambiguous payment outcomes.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q3"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4","surface":"track","en":"How should structured logging and correlation be designed so logs are actually searchable?","bodyEn":"Structured logging starts with a versioned event schema, not with “turn every string into JSON.” A useful event normally includes a timestamp, severity, service/environment/version, a stable event name, outcome, and the fields needed to investigate that event. Add trace_id and span_id when a valid span is active; background jobs and bootstrap logs may legitimately have neither. {\"ts\":\"2026-07-31T10:04:12Z\",\"severity\":\"WARN\",\"service\":\"booking\", \"version\":\"2026.07.31.2\",\"event\":\"seat_hold_failed\",\"outcome\":\"rejected\", \"trace_id\":\"4bf92f3577b34da6\",\"seat\":\"12A\",\"flight\":\"VN231\", \"reason\":\"already_held\",\"duration_ms\":42} Use stable field names and bounded classifications such as reason=\"already_held\"; keep free-form detail in a message field. Correlation IDs, tenant IDs, and business IDs should be allowlisted according to privacy and cardinality policy rather than copied into every event. Context lifecycle Install trace context at the ingress or job boundary and remove or restore only the keys that boundary owns. A scoped MDC API can make cleanup explicit: try (MDC.MDCCloseable ignored = MDC. putCloseable ( \"trace_id\" , traceId)) { chain. doFilter (req, res); } Test propagation across the actual executor, virtual-thread, callback, reactive, and messaging boundaries. A ThreadLocal-backed MDC does not by itself establish cross-boundary propagation. Define severities as an operational contract. ERROR should represent a failed operation or condition that is actionable under the team's policy; it does not universally imply that a human must inspect every occurrence. Sample or rate-limit noisy events using a risk- and cost-based policy. Security/audit events may require separate immutable retention; “100% failures and 1% successes” is not a universal safe ratio. Metrics should record aggregate rates even when individual logs are sampled. Redact or avoid credentials, session/token material, payment data, and unnecessary personal data at the source. Appender-side masking is defense in depth, not permission to emit secrets first. Log an exception where ownership and outcome are known. Multiple layers may emit different useful events, but duplicating the same stack trace at every rethrow adds cost without evidence. Containerized applications commonly write to stdout for platform collection, while direct OTLP export, sidecars, or managed file agents can also be valid. Choose one delivery contract and specify buffering, backpressure, loss behavior, and retention. Telemetry contract tests: emit synthetic token, payment, address, and personal fields and assert redaction at the source reject unexpected high-cardinality fields from metric labels verify route normalization, required correlation fields, sampling behavior, and bounded buffering exercise deletion and legal-hold retention paths A forged traceparent, tracestate, or baggage value must remain correlation data and must not change authentication or authorization decisions. Trace/log correlation and sampling mechanics are covered in (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2). Never treat trace context as authentication identity.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q5","surface":"track","en":"An API is healthy, but the queue, outbox, or reconciliation backlog is growing. What should the SLI and alert measure?","bodyEn":"Separate request acceptance from work completion. A 200 response can truthfully mean “the command was durably accepted” while the business outcome remains pending; it must not make a growing backlog invisible. Version the SLI contract with the journey, owner, eligible population, good/bad/excluded events, clock/source, region, retry treatment, and alert query. The alert and SLO must use the same denominator; distinguish logical work from delivery attempts and keep unknown outcomes visible. For asynchronous work, define the population and clock explicitly: accepted commands or events as the denominator age of the oldest item and p95/p99 completion age, not only average queue depth accepted-to-completed success, retry/DLQ rate, and items stuck in an unknown state outbox/CDC watermark, consumer lag, reconciliation delta, and the value/age of unresolved breaks user-visible freshness or promise, such as “search reflects a catalog change within the stated window” Page when the user/business SLO is burning or a bounded capacity threshold predicts imminent loss, and route a ticket when the condition is actionable but not urgent. A queue with 10,000 old items can be worse than one with 100,000 young items; the age and invariant decide. During recovery, drain with an explicit rate budget, preserve idempotency and ordering scope, and verify counts, duplicates, missing work, domain invariants, and customer-visible status. “Queue depth returned to zero” is not proof if the consumer discarded or misapplied work. Keep the API SLI, worker SLI, freshness SLI, and reconciliation SLI separate, then publish a journey-level view that explains how they combine. Do not average them into one flattering number.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q5"},{"id":"20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q6","surface":"track","en":"How should observability fail without becoming a second outage, and how do you verify the monitoring path?","bodyEn":"Treat telemetry as a bounded dependency with its own health signals. An exporter outage, collector queue, cardinality spike, sampling rule, or backend limit must not exhaust application memory or block the critical request path. Design the failure boundary: bound collector memory, batch size, queue depth, retry duration, and disk/WAL usage where persistence is justified monitor accepted, dropped, failed, queued, and exported telemetry, plus collector CPU/memory and backend rejection keep low-cardinality SLO metrics independent from high-cardinality traces and logs; prioritize error/latency evidence under pressure redact credentials, tokens, payment data, addresses, and user content before export; sampling is not a privacy control define a degraded mode: drop debug detail first, retain aggregate/business failure signals, and recover without replaying an unbounded storm Crash-window checks: Window Failure Independent evidence or recovery Before instrumentation initializes startup crash has no application telemetry platform logs/exit code, startup diagnostic, and black-box SLI Context or sampling boundary trace splits or an error is dropped before tail sampling propagation tests; independent error metrics/logs; tail sampling only when capacity allows Collector queue/cardinality pressure telemetry drops, backend rejects, or memory grows bounded queue/WAL, accepted-dropped-failed-exported counters, collector CPU/memory, and a pressure drill Evaluator/notification outage no page while service or monitoring is failing independent heartbeat, evaluator-age/missing-data alert, alternate notification channel, manual escalation Retention/deletion boundary sensitive data remains or required evidence disappears classification, access audit, deletion test, and separate legal-hold evidence path Verify the monitoring path with a synthetic heartbeat, a deliberately generated known error, a collector/backend outage drill, and an alert-delivery test. Record detection delay, dropped evidence, memory ceiling, recovery time, evaluator freshness, notification success, and which user-facing SLO remained observable. Self-observe collectors, evaluators, and notification paths through signals that do not rely solely on the component or backend being tested. A green application dashboard during a telemetry outage is not proof of health; it may only prove that the dashboard has stopped receiving data. Alert on missing data and stale evaluation separately from zero-valued service metrics. The monitoring system is not the source of truth for its own availability Use an independent or lower-dependency check for alert delivery and retain a manual escalation path. The system that tells you monitoring is down must not depend on the same failing collector/backend.","contextEn":"Observability & SRE — measuring, alerting and handling incidents","href":"/topics/20-observability-sre#question-20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q6"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q1","surface":"track","en":"A server suddenly becomes slow: which commands do you run in the first 60 seconds?","bodyEn":"This repository's first-minute checklist is deliberately ordered — each command rules out a category of causes. The order follows Brendan Gregg's \"Linux Performance Analysis in 60,000 Milliseconds\" (Netflix Technology Blog, 2015); the commands are guidance, not a universal runbook: uptime 1/5/15-minute load averages — is the trend rising or falling? dmesg tail OOM killer? hardware errors? packet drops? vmstat 1 r (run queue), si/so (swap), us/sy/id/wa mpstat -P ALL 1 one stuck core or the entire machine? (single-thread bottleneck) pidstat 1 which process is consuming CPU, steadily or in bursts? iostat -xz 1 %util, await — is disk the bottleneck? free -m how much RAM remains, and how much is in buff/cache? sar -n DEV 1 has network bandwidth reached its ceiling? sar -n TCP,ETCP 1 retransmits and connection errors top one final overall view The idea: within one minute, you know whether the bottleneck is in CPU, memory, disk, or the network — only then do you drill down in that direction. Which reading sends you where: First signal Points to Drill down with vmstat r stays above the core count CPU saturation mpstat -P ALL for one hot core, pidstat 1 for the process, then a profile high load with idle CPU; b or wa up tasks blocked in D state, usually disk or NFS iostat -xz 1 : await and aqu-sz , not %util on SSD/NVMe si / so nonzero, or OOM lines in dmesg memory pressure free -m , /proc/pressure/memory , the cgroup's memory.events st climbing on a cloud VM the hypervisor is taking CPU the instance's CPU credits or host; nothing inside the VM fixes it nr_throttled rising in cpu.stat the container's CPU quota the cgroup's cpu.max , not the host's idle CPU retransmits in sar -n TCP,ETCP the network or the peer ss -ti on the connection, then a capture Reading those counters correctly is (21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q2). Descriptor and port exhaustion has its own signature (21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q3), and the packet-level drill-down is (21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q4). Interview answer You do not need to memorize all ten commands. Articulate the diagnostic framework — \"I first narrow it down to CPU / RAM / disk / network, then move into the JVM\" — and add 3–4 concrete commands; that is convincing enough. The biggest trap: jumping straight into the application when the cause lies in a lower layer (full disk, slow DNS, NTP drift, or a CPU-throttled container). inside a container, top/free often report host metrics rather than cgroup metrics → read /sys/fs/cgroup/... (memory.max, cpu.stat). A rising nr_throttled means CPU throttling — a symptom commonly misdiagnosed as 'the app is slow'. df -h and df -i: a full disk and inode exhaustion both make applications fail in confusing ways; df -i is easy to forget check time synchronization with chronyc tracking — clock drift breaks tokens, signatures, and every time-based calculation modern tools such as bpftop, execsnoop, and biolatency (bcc/BPF) expose syscalls and per-process I/O latency without stopping anything always compare against a baseline: 'load 8' is meaningless unless you know the normal value and the machine's core count Make the scope explicit before interpreting a number. A host view, a container/cgroup view, and a process view can disagree. In a container, capture the process identity and the cgroup files visible from that PID namespace before comparing the result with a host dashboard: pid=4127 ps -o pid,ppid,user,stat,etime,rss,vsz,cmd -p \"$pid\" readlink /proc/\"$pid\"/ns/pid cat /proc/\"$pid\"/cgroup cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat cat /proc/pressure/{cpu,io,memory} The exact cgroup path and fields depend on cgroup v1/v2 and the runtime. Treat memory.max, memory.current, memory.events, cpu.max, cpu.stat, and PSI as runtime evidence; do not copy a host path into a container recipe. jcmd/jstack also require the target PID to be visible in the same PID namespace and normally require the same host and effective user. Record those boundaries with the command output. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The Linux distribution, kernel and cgroup mode, container runtime, Kubernetes version, JDK vendor and version, and cloud instance family — the commands and their defaults differ across them. Whether the service is CPU-, I/O-, network- or dependency-bound under the incident workload, and which baseline or SLI proves it rather than assumes it. The allowed diagnostic pause, artifact size, retention, access group and cleanup deadline for heap dumps, profiles and packet captures. Which sysctl, file-descriptor and port settings are managed by systemd, the image, the node or the platform — and which you can actually change. What falsifies the -Xmx and native-memory budget: a cgroup OOM with a stable heap, direct-buffer, thread or metaspace growth, or a runtime detection mismatch. What falsifies a network diagnosis: packet evidence contradicting application errors, failures that are zone-specific, or retries hiding the original reset or timeout.","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q1"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should you interpret load average, iowait, and CPU steal?","bodyEn":"Load average — on Linux, it is not merely the \"number of processes waiting for CPU.\" Linux also counts processes in the D state (uninterruptible sleep — usually waiting on disk or NFS). The consequence: high load with idle CPU means an I/O bottleneck, not a CPU shortage. This is a very common misreading. It only becomes meaningful relative to the core count: a load of 8 on a 16-core machine is comfortable; on a 2-core machine, it is overloaded. Compare the 1/5/15-minute values to see whether the trend is rising or falling. Reading vmstat 1: procs -----------memory---------- ---swap-- -----io---- --system-- ------cpu----- r b swpd free buff cache si so bi bo in cs us sy id wa st 9 2 0 128360 22140 918224 0 0 12 840 4210 8830 71 12 5 10 2 r — tasks waiting for CPU. If it remains above the core count, the machine genuinely lacks CPU capacity. b — tasks blocked on I/O si/so — swap in/out. Any nonzero value is a red flag for a latency-sensitive service. wa (iowait) — the CPU is idle while I/O remains pending st (steal) — the hypervisor has taken CPU time to run another VM High iowait is not necessarily a problem wa says only, \"the CPU is idle and some I/O is waiting.\" High wa is normal on a machine running disk-heavy batch jobs. Conversely, wa = 0 does not mean the disk is healthy — if the CPU is 100% busy, time spent waiting for I/O is not counted in wa. steal time (st) is a cloud-specific gotcha: when a shared instance (T-series burstable) exhausts its credits, st spikes and the application slows down while every internal metric looks 'normal'. On EC2 T2/T3, also inspect CPUCreditBalance. Calling this out is a strong signal of production experience. abnormally high context switches (cs) indicate too many threads or severe lock contention → correlate with a thread dump (21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q1) %util from iostat is misleading for SSDs/NVMe: they process many I/Os concurrently, so 100% %util does not necessarily mean saturation. Inspect await and aqu-sz instead of %util. for Java services, swap policy is a runtime and platform decision: inspect node/container policy, cgroup limits, swappiness, and the JDK/runtime before changing it. Paging a latency-sensitive heap can amplify pauses, but do not turn one environment's policy into a universal Kubernetes claim. pressure stall information (/proc/pressure/{cpu,io,memory}) is a modern signal that is far more interpretable than load average — mentioning it shows current operational knowledge","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q2"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q3","surface":"track","en":"Running out of file descriptors or ephemeral ports: what are the symptoms and fixes?","bodyEn":"These are two different failures with the same symptom: a service runs normally for hours, then suddenly cannot accept or open any connections. File descriptor exhaustion java.net.SocketException: Too many open files Every socket, file, and pipe consumes an fd. ulimit -n soft limit for the current shell cat /proc/<pid>/limits ACTUAL limit for the running process ls /proc/<pid>/fd wc -l number currently in use lsof -p <pid> awk '{print $5}' sort uniq -c sort -rn The cause is almost always a leak: an HttpClient/InputStream/Connection that is never closed. Raising ulimit merely postpones the crash — you still have to find the leak. Ephemeral port exhaustion java.net.BindException: Cannot assign requested address The client opens too many outbound connections. Do not assume a universal ephemeral range. Read /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_local_port_range (or the platform's equivalent); usable ports are constrained by local address, destination tuple, namespace, and kernel/runtime policy. ss -s overview, including the TIME-WAIT count cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_local_port_range ss -tan state time-wait wc -l The usual culprit is creating a new client for every request instead of reusing a connection pool. TIME_WAIT duration and reuse behavior are kernel/version/configuration-sensitive; measure the states with ss, inspect the effective network policy, and use the target kernel documentation instead of assuming 60 seconds. systemd ignores /etc/security/limits.conf; set LimitNOFILE= in the unit file instead. This explains the classic 'I already raised ulimit, but it still fails' incident. in K8s, the fd limit comes from the image/runtime, not your shell — inspect /proc/1/limits inside the container The root fix for ephemeral port exhaustion is connection pooling plus keep-alive. Share one WebClient/HttpClient across the application, not one per call. TIME_WAIT is intentional: it lets delayed packets from an old connection expire. tcp_tw_reuse, port-range changes, and any reuse/tuning knob are kernel- and topology-sensitive; inspect the effective value and test the client path before changing them. tcp_tw_recycle was removed because it breaks NAT — proposing it as a universal solution is a red flag. distinguish them quickly: Too many open files = the local process exhausted its fds; Cannot assign requested address = source ports are exhausted; Connection refused = nothing is listening remotely; Connection reset = the peer closed abruptly monitor proactively: export process.files.open / process.files.max to Prometheus and alert at 80% — this class of incident is entirely predictable","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q3"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q4","surface":"track","en":"The OOM killer terminated a service: how do you trace the cause and prevent it?","bodyEn":"OOM has several failure boundaries; do not confuse them: Java OutOfMemoryError Linux OOM killer Who terminates it the JVM throws an exception the kernel sends SIGKILL Evidence a stack trace in the application log nothing in the application log Where to look application logs dmesg , /var/log/syslog Cause insufficient heap / a leak total RSS exceeds the machine or cgroup limit dmesg -T grep -i -E \"oom killed process\" Out of memory: Killed process 4127 (java) total-vm:9863244kB, anon-rss:6291456kB In K8s, an OOM-terminated container is reported as OOMKilled with exit code 137 (128 + 9). Why OOM can still occur when -Xmx is lower than RAM: the heap is not the JVM's entire memory footprint. There is also metaspace, the code cache, thread stacks (~1MB/thread), direct ByteBuffers, and native-library memory. RSS ≈ Heap + Metaspace + CodeCache + (Threads × Stack) + Direct + Native Start with a budget, not a universal percentage: container_limit = heap + metaspace + code_cache + thread_stacks + direct/native + agents + safety_headroom Xmx = container_limit - measured_nonheap - safety_headroom A 50–70% Xmx value is only a labeled starting example for a measured workload, not a guarantee; validate it under peak traffic and the target JDK/container. Java 10+ can detect the cgroup limit (UseContainerSupport is enabled by default in the documented JDK line), but that does not choose a safe application budget. -XX:MaxRAMPercentage is one configurable starting point; derive the value from measured non-heap usage and headroom, then verify the effective flags on the target JDK. older Java 8 releases do not recognize cgroups: the JVM sees all host RAM and sets its default heap to one quarter of that value → immediate OOMKilled. This is a classic failure when containerizing an old Java application. use Native Memory Tracking to investigate memory outside the heap: -XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary, then jcmd <pid> VM.native_memory summary common off-heap leaks include direct ByteBuffers that are not released (Netty), thread pools that create unbounded numbers of threads, and ZipInputStream/native libraries that are not closed always enable -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/dump — without a dump, there is nothing to investigate (21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q2). Remember to mount a volume; a dump written to the container's ephemeral filesystem disappears with the container. in K8s, set requests = limits for critical services (QoS Guaranteed) so they are not killed first when a node runs short of memory; oom_score_adj determines which process dies first OOMKilled recurring at regular intervals indicates a leak; occurring specifically at peak load indicates underprovisioning. These are different conclusions with different fixes. Evidence matrix for the failure boundary: Failure Scope Evidence Next check Java heap OOME JVM heap exception, GC/live-set trend, optional heap dump heap histogram, retained paths, allocation/GC history Metaspace/code-cache exhaustion JVM non-heap specific OOME text, class-loading/code-cache metrics class-loader churn, generated code, target JDK flags Direct/native/thread exhaustion JVM or native process RSS NMT where covered, RSS/cgroup counters, thread count, exit evidence direct-buffer limits, native libraries, thread creation, PID limits cgroup OOMKilled / exit 137 container cgroup memory.events , orchestrator reason, exit code 137, node/runtime event peak RSS plus cgroup limit and sibling/container pressure kernel global OOM host/node kernel log, killed PID, global memory/swap/PSI evidence node pressure, oom score, other workloads, reclaim policy Exit code 137 is evidence of SIGKILL, not proof of one specific memory source. Correlate the process, cgroup, node, timestamp, and runtime event before choosing a fix.","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q4"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q1","surface":"track","en":"How do you read a thread dump to find deadlocks and blocked threads?","bodyEn":"jcmd <pid> Thread.print > td1.txt recommended approach jstack -l <pid> > td1.txt equivalent; -l adds lock information Take three dumps 5–10 seconds apart — a single snapshot cannot distinguish \"running\" from \"stuck.\" A thread whose stack remains unchanged across all three is the likely culprit. States to recognize: State Meaning Concerning when RUNNABLE running (or waiting on network I/O) many threads are stopped at the same line of compute code BLOCKED waiting to enter synchronized many threads are waiting on the same monitor WAITING wait() or park() with no timeout a thread waits indefinitely because nothing wakes it TIMED_WAITING has a timeout usually normal Deadlock — the JVM detects it automatically and prints it explicitly at the end of the dump: Found one Java-level deadlock: ============================= \"thread-A\": waiting to lock monitor 0x00007f8 (object 0x000000076ab, a java.lang.Object), which is held by \"thread-B\" \"thread-B\": waiting to lock monitor 0x00007f9 (object 0x000000076ac, a java.lang.Object), which is held by \"thread-A\" Common misreading A RUNNABLE thread at socketRead0 is not consuming CPU — it is waiting on the network. The JVM cannot distinguish blocking I/O from computation here. Many RUNNABLE threads at socketRead0 mean an external dependency is slow, not that the application is CPU-bound. The most important pattern to recognize is dozens of BLOCKED threads on the same monitor → a contention bottleneck. Inspect the - locked line to identify the owning thread, then read its stack — that is the code path you need to fix. pool exhaustion is immediately visible in a dump: many TIMED_WAITING threads at HikariPool.getConnection → this is not a database failure; connections are being held too long elsewhere (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4) jcmd is safer than jstack (no -F required and less risk of hanging the JVM). Forced jstack -F can pause the JVM for a long time — use it sparingly in production. meaningful thread names (booking-worker-3) make a dump far easier to read than pool-2-thread-7. Name every custom thread pool. deadlocks involving ReentrantLock are not always detected by the JVM as synchronized deadlocks are — use jstack -l to see Locked ownable synchronizers analysis tools include fastThread.io, or use jstack grep -A3 \"java.lang.Thread.State\" sort uniq -c for a quick count by state virtual threads (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4) change the workflow: use jcmd <pid> Thread.dump_to_file -format=json to capture them all JDK 21 attach boundary and impact: jcmd/jstack commands are version-scoped to the JDK 21 tooling documented here. They normally require the same host and effective user, a PID visible in the same namespace, attach permissions, and enough output destination. Thread.print is a bounded snapshot but can still add work during a stressed incident; take spaced samples and record the target PID, user, namespace, and timestamp. Do not assume PID 1 in a container is the host JVM.","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q1"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do you use a heap dump to find the source of a memory leak?","bodyEn":"Recognize the leak before taking a dump: graph heap usage after each Full GC. Normal behavior is a sawtooth around a stable baseline; a leak produces a steadily rising sawtooth floor that eventually ends in OOM. jcmd <pid> GC.heap_info quick overview jcmd <pid> GC.class_histogram head -30 low overhead and often enough for an initial diagnosis jcmd <pid> GC.heap_dump /dump/heap.hprof full dump Heap dumps can pause the JVM GC.heap_dump causes a stop-the-world pause proportional to heap size — dumping an 8GB heap can stall the process for tens of seconds. In production, drain the instance from the load balancer first, or dump an instance that is leaking but is not serving traffic. Analyze it with Eclipse MAT — use these three views in order: Leak Suspects Report — MAT identifies likely suspects automatically and is correct in most cases Dominator Tree — sort by retained heap (memory that would be released if this object died), not shallow heap Path to GC Roots (exclude weak/soft references) — answers the most important question: what is retaining it Classic Java leak patterns: a static Map/List used as a cache with no bound and no TTL a registered listener/callback that is never unregistered a ThreadLocal without remove() — pool threads live forever, so their values do too a ClassLoader leak during hot redeployment (a classic Tomcat problem) a HashMap key that does not implement equals/hashCode → prior entries are never found again and only accumulate Distinguish shallow size (the object itself) from retained size (the entire object graph kept alive exclusively by it). Sorting by shallow size almost always leads to char[]/byte[] — correct but useless. seeing char[]/byte[] at the top of the histogram is normal; the real questions are which strings and what retains them — only Path to GC Roots can answer those compare two dumps taken a few hours apart (MAT can compare histograms) — growing objects are the suspects; objects that were already large are often innocent with an off-heap leak, the heap dump looks completely clean while RSS keeps growing → switch to Native Memory Tracking (21-linux-production-debug.diagnosing-the-system.q4) continuous JFR recording lets you investigate after an incident without reproducing it: -XX:StartFlightRecording=disk=true,maxsize=1g,dumponexit=true a dump contains all live data held in memory — passwords, tokens, and customer information. Treat the .hprof file as sensitive data: encrypt it, restrict access, and delete it after analysis. JDK 21 impact labels: GC.class_histogram is a lighter first probe but still walks heap state and can require a safepoint. GC.heap_dump is a high-impact operation whose pause, dump size, free disk, and encryption/access path must be approved. Both require attach/user/PID-namespace checks. Confirm the target JDK 21 command set and free space before running them; a failed dump can still consume disk or disturb the evidence window.","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q2"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q3","surface":"track","en":"How safe are async-profiler and JFR for profiling in production?","bodyEn":"Both tools can be production-safe when the recording is bounded, the chosen events/frequency are understood, and you can stop or roll back quickly. “Low overhead” is a design goal, not a universal 1–3% guarantee—measure on the actual JDK, hardware, container limits, event set, and workload. async-profiler 4.x uses the asprof binary launcher (the old profiler.sh examples are obsolete): asprof -d 30 -e cpu -f /tmp/cpu.html <pid> on-CPU samples asprof -d 30 -e wall -f /tmp/wall.html <pid> elapsed/waiting stacks asprof -d 30 -e alloc -f /tmp/alloc.html <pid> allocation samples asprof -d 30 -e lock -f /tmp/lock.html <pid> lock contention Modern async-profiler releases avoid classic safepoint-only sampling and can show Java, native, kernel, GC, and JIT frames. The implementation has evolved: newer 3.x/4.x releases introduced a binary launcher and VMStructs-based stack walking rather than relying on the old “AsyncGetCallTrace plus -XX:+DebugNonSafepoints” recipe as a timeless explanation. Read the matching release documentation. JFR is built into the JDK and records a broad event model: jcmd <pid> JFR.start name=diag settings=profile duration=60s filename=/tmp/r.jfr jcmd <pid> JFR.check name=diag jcmd <pid> JFR.dump name=diag filename=/tmp/r.jfr Start narrow during an incident Establish disk/memory limits, duration, sampling interval, output path, data sensitivity, and attach permissions before profiling. Start with a short CPU or standard JFR recording, inspect service SLO/resource impact, then add allocation, wall-clock, lock, native-memory, or method-tracing events only for a hypothesis. Some modes can be materially more expensive than CPU sampling. Choose the event from the symptom: high CPU → CPU samples; slow with idle CPU → wall-clock plus traces/queue/thread states; GC pressure → allocation profile plus GC/JFR events and live-set evidence; contention → lock/monitor events and thread dumps; native/RSS growth → native-memory tooling, not only Java heap allocation. Flame-graph width is the number/weight of samples for the selected event, not automatically elapsed time. Compare the same settings with a healthy baseline, and inspect time range/heatmaps before assuming one aggregate plateau explains the incident. Container profiling may require matching PID namespace, JVM attach permission, perf settings/capabilities, seccomp allowances, and writable output. Grant the minimum needed; SYS_ADMIN is broad and should not be a default recommendation. JFR collection still needs authorization to attach/control the target process even though JFR itself ships with the JDK. Always-on JFR can provide pre-incident history, but set a low-overhead configuration, bounded disk/age, access controls, and redaction policy. Virtual-thread events differ by JDK: monitor pinning existed in JDK 21–23 and was removed in JDK 24, so interpret recordings against the target runtime (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4). JDK 21 profiling/NMT labels: JFR default/profile, event set, duration, disk limit, and output path determine impact; “low overhead” is not a fixed number. Native Memory Tracking must be enabled with its documented startup option and has overhead; summary/detail coverage is JVM-specific and does not account for every third-party/native allocation. For both JFR and NMT, record JDK 21 version, PID namespace, effective user/attach permission, capability/seccomp requirements, disk budget, and stop condition.","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q3"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do tcpdump, ss, and netstat help debug networking between two services?","bodyEn":"When two services \"cannot talk to each other,\" you need to determine where the packets stop. Check the connection first: ss -tanp grep :5432 connections to Postgres, including the process ss -tan state syn-sent SYN sent with no reply => blocked by firewall/SG ss -ti dst 10.0.3.41 RTT, cwnd, and retransmits for each connection ss replaces netstat and is much faster on hosts with many connections — using netstat today signals outdated operational knowledge. Capture packets when you need definitive evidence: tcpdump -i any -nn 'host 10.0.3.41 and port 5432' -c 200 -w /tmp/c.pcap tcpdump -nn -r /tmp/c.pcap head -40 -nn, which disables DNS and port-name resolution, is important: resolution can hang the command when DNS itself is the problem. Reading the result: repeated SYN packets with no SYN-ACK → traffic is being blocked (security group, NACL, or iptables), or the destination does not exist SYN → immediate RST → nothing is listening on that port the handshake completes, followed by a long silence → the peer received the request but is processing it slowly (an application problem, not a network problem) [R] partway through → the peer closed abruptly, often because of an LB timeout or idle timeout symptoms can distinguish these cases faster than a packet capture: hanging until the timeout means packets are being swallowed (firewall DROP); an immediate failure means an RST or ICMP-unreachable response arrived ss -s shows total connections and TIME-WAIT (see 21.3); ss -ti shows retrans — high retransmissions mean there is a genuine network problem The MTU trap: the connection opens and small requests succeed, but large requests hang → packet fragmentation or PMTU discovery is broken because ICMP is blocked. This is common with VPNs and K8s overlay networks, and very hard to recognize until you have seen it once. in K8s, use kubectl debug/an ephemeral container to run tcpdump in the same network namespace without adding diagnostic tools to the production image DNS: run dig +short svc.ns.svc.cluster.local and inspect ndots:5 in /etc/resolv.conf — this setting can make each external-name lookup try 4–5 suffixes and is a common cause of 'mysterious slowness' on K8s TLS payloads are encrypted, so tcpdump shows only the handshake — diagnose certificate/SNI issues with openssl s_client -connect host:443 -servername host A pcap file contains real traffic data. Limit the capture with -c, use a narrow filter, and delete it after analysis.","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q4"},{"id":"21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you investigate a production latency incident without making the system worse?","bodyEn":"Start with the user-facing symptom and a time window, then collect the cheapest bounded evidence before changing anything: request/error/latency rate, saturation, queue age, database connections, GC pauses, file descriptors, socket states, retransmits, and dependency health. A safe sequence: Compare a healthy instance, an affected instance, and the dependency path. Establish whether the problem is local, shared, or traffic-shaped. Use low-overhead snapshots first: process/container metrics, three thread dumps separated by a few seconds, socket/connection state, and a bounded JVM/GC view. If profiling is justified, set duration, event set, output size, permission, and stop conditions. A short JFR or async-profiler capture is evidence, not a universal low-overhead guarantee. Take a heap dump only when the memory hypothesis warrants the pause, disk, privacy, and post-processing cost; capture metadata and protect the file. Use packet capture narrowly by interface, peer, port, and duration; redact or restrict payloads, and do not assume “no packets” means the application is healthy. Change one reversible variable at a time, record the expected signal, and keep the incident commander informed. Do not raise limits, restart every replica, enable verbose logging, or flush caches just to make a graph move; each can erase evidence or amplify the outage. After mitigation, compare the same SLI and resource window before/after, preserve command output and timestamps, and turn the confirmed failure mode into a regression or runbook test. A diagnosis that cannot be reproduced or bounded remains a hypothesis. Artifact policy: treat heap dumps, JFR recordings, profiler output, and pcaps as sensitive operational artifacts. Before capture, name the owner, purpose, path/volume, maximum size, encryption/access group, retention deadline, and cleanup command. Keep only the minimum evidence, store it outside an ephemeral container filesystem when it must survive restart, log the artifact hash/creation time without logging secrets, and verify deletion after the incident. Symptom Evidence first Reversible action Rollback/cleanup CPU or run queue spike PSI, per-core/process CPU, three thread dumps bounded CPU/JFR recording or traffic shift stop recording, restore traffic, compare same SLI window Heap floor rising GC trend, histogram, live-set and RSS drain one instance before heap dump protect/delete dump per retention policy; restore instance only after evidence is safe Network timeout/reset ss , retransmits, narrow tcpdump, dependency SLI capture bounded peer/port window or route one canary stop capture, restrict/delete pcap, undo route change Telemetry or diagnostic pressure collector queue, disk, memory, dropped evidence reduce event set/rate and preserve critical signals remove temporary config, reclaim disk, verify monitoring health","contextEn":"Linux & production troubleshooting — diagnosing while it burns","href":"/topics/21-linux-production-debug#question-21-linux-production-debug.jvm-network-deep-dive-on-prod.q5"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q1","surface":"track","en":"How does a low-level design interview differ from system design, and what five-step framework should you use?","bodyEn":"System design (HLD) Low-level design (LLD) Unit service, database, queue, cache class, interface, method Focus scale, consistency, partitioning responsibilities, coupling, extensibility Deliverable diagram + trade-offs working code Follow-up \"What happens at 10× traffic?\" \"How many places change when type X is added?\" Five-step framework (a communication and invariant workflow; timeboxes are adjustable): Clarify the scope — LLD prompts are deliberately ambiguous: \"Elevator: how many cars? Priority floors? Scheduling only, or a full simulation?\" Agree on the smallest scope, actors, and success invariants that fit the interview; three to five minutes is a useful example, not a ritual. List use cases — identify what each actor can do. These become the methods later. Identify entities and responsibilities — nouns become classes and verbs become methods. State explicitly: \"This class has the single responsibility of…\" Sketch the API before implementing it — write method signatures and interfaces, confirm them, then fill in method bodies. Avoid writing 40 lines before discovering that the prompt was misunderstood. Code the core — implement the most interesting part as working code; describe repetitive parts as \"analogous\" and move on. What the interviewer actually evaluates The extensibility of the design. A common follow-up is \"What if we add another vehicle, fee, or strategy type?\" If the answer is \"add one class implementing the existing interface\", the design is easy to extend. If it is \"edit the switch in three places\", the design has a change hotspot. do not draw an ERD. LLD asks for the in-memory object model, not a schema. Jumping to tables and foreign keys immediately leaves the problem's scope. begin with interfaces and enums, and defer storage; an in-memory Map is more than enough unless the prompt says otherwise state the system invariants, such as \"a seat cannot be both FREE and HELD\", then show where the code enforces them. This distinguishes a senior from a mid-level answer. prefer composition over inheritance: a deep inheritance tree signals immature design. A four-level Car extends Vehicle hierarchy for a \"vehicle types\" problem almost always has a better composition-based alternative. manage time with a heuristic: reserve enough time for a working core, then adjust clarification, design, coding, and extension discussion to the prompt. Running out of time without working code is the worst outcome, so implement the core early. always ask \"Does this need to be thread-safe?\" If yes, explain the strategy (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3); if no, mention that concurrency was considered.","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q1"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do you design an LRU cache with O(1) get and put, and why do you choose those data structures?","bodyEn":"A common LLD exercise (LeetCode 146) asks for O(1) get and put under expected hash-map performance. Assumptions matter: this example is process-local, uses non-null keys/values, and requires capacity > 0. A concurrent variant needs an explicit synchronization policy; the complexity claim excludes lock contention and resize spikes. Why two structures are required: a HashMap provides O(1) lookup but no order; a list provides order but O(n) lookup. Combine them so the map points directly to a list node, making lookup, removal, and insertion all O(1). It must be a doubly linked list because removing a node requires its predecessor. Two sentinel nodes (head, tail) eliminate every special null case. class LRUCache { private final int cap; private final Map<Integer, Node> map = new HashMap<>(); private final Node head = new Node( 0 , 0 ), tail = new Node( 0 , 0 ); LRUCache ( int cap) { this .cap = cap; head.next = tail; tail.prev = head; // two sentinel nodes } public int get ( int key) { Node n = map. get (key); if (n == null ) return - 1 ; moveToFront (n); // just used => most recent return n.val; } public void put ( int key, int val) { Node n = map. get (key); if (n != null ) { n.val = val; moveToFront (n); return ; } if (map. size () == cap) { // full => evict the last node Node lru = tail.prev; remove (lru); map. remove (lru.key); } Node fresh = new Node(key, val); map. put (key, fresh); addFirst (fresh); } private void moveToFront (Node n) { remove (n); addFirst (n); } private void remove (Node n) { n.prev.next = n.next; n.next.prev = n.prev; } private void addFirst (Node n) { n.next = head.next; n.prev = head; head.next.prev = n; head.next = n; } private static class Node { int key, val; Node prev, next; Node ( int k, int v) { key = k; val = v; } } } Each node must also store its key: during eviction you have the node but need the key to remove its map entry. LinkedHashMap can implement this compactly with accessOrder=true and an overridden removeEldestEntry, but access-order reads mutate the list and the class is not thread-safe. ConcurrentHashMap alone does not preserve LRU order; a concurrent design needs an explicit lock or a sharded policy whose contract is understood. for thread safety, one global lock is simple but blocks all operations. Sharding can reduce contention, but library internals and eviction guarantees are provider/version-specific; state the contract and measure it instead of assuming every implementation has multiple segments. LRU is not always the best policy: one full-table scan can evict all hot data. LFU or W-TinyLFU can resist scans at the cost of frequency-tracking state; see the admission-versus-eviction design in (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q10). adding TTL requires a third structure, such as an expiry min-heap or timing wheel; see 22.8 in practice, Redis uses approximate LRU by sampling several random keys and evicting the oldest, avoiding a linked list for millions of keys. Mentioning this earns points. useful test cases include capacity = 1, overwriting an existing key, getting a missing key, and verifying eviction order after interleaved get/put operations","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q2"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do you design and code token-bucket and sliding-window rate limiters?","bodyEn":"Topic 10 discusses the algorithms at system level; here you must write the code. Token bucket — it permits bounded bursts, which is useful when the workload allows bursts; no single limiter algorithm is universally best. The examples below assume a process-local bucket, capacity > 0, refill rate >= 0, and 0 < n <= capacity. Inject a monotonic elapsed-time source; wall-clock time is not appropriate for measuring a duration. class TokenBucket { private final long capacity; private final double refillPerMs; private final LongSupplier nowMs; private double tokens; private long lastRefill; TokenBucket ( long capacity, double refillPerSec, LongSupplier nowMs) { this .capacity = capacity; this .nowMs = nowMs; this .refillPerMs = refillPerSec / 1000.0 ; this .tokens = capacity; // full at initialization this .lastRefill = nowMs. getAsLong (); } synchronized boolean tryAcquire ( int n) { refill (); if (tokens < n) return false ; tokens -= n; return true ; } private void refill () { long now = nowMs. getAsLong (); tokens = Math. min (capacity, tokens + (now - lastRefill) refillPerMs); lastRefill = now; // clamp at capacity: idle time cannot accumulate without bound } } Use lazy refill, calculated when called, rather than a periodic refill thread. It requires no dedicated refill thread and remains accurate to the millisecond. Sliding window log — exact, at the cost of O(requests in the window) memory: boolean allow (String key, int limit, long windowMs, LongSupplier nowMs) { long now = nowMs. getAsLong (); Deque<Long> q = log. computeIfAbsent (key, k -> new ArrayDeque<>()); synchronized (q) { while (!q. isEmpty () && q. peekFirst () <= now - windowMs) q. pollFirst (); if (q. size () >= limit) return false ; q. addLast (now); return true ; } } Algorithm Memory Allows bursts Accuracy Token bucket O(1) yes, up to capacity high Fixed window O(1) yes, up to 2× at the boundary low — boundary error Sliding window log O(n) no exact Sliding window counter O(1) limited good approximation fixed-window boundary error: with a limit of 100 per minute, 100 requests at 10:00:59 and another 100 at 10:01:00 produce 200 requests in one second while remaining \"within the rules\". This is a useful boundary test. in a distributed system, state must live in Redis, and the read-modify-write operation must be atomic through a Lua script or INCR plus EXPIRE. Per-instance in-memory limits become N times the intended limit across N instances. a Redis token bucket stores tokens and lastRefill in a hash and wraps refill plus deduction in one Lua script return the correct 429 status with Retry-After and X-RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset headers (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2) choose the key by layer: API key for partners, user for authenticated consumers, and IP for anonymous users. IP-only limiting is wrong when customers behind NAT or CGNAT share an address. distinguish rate limiting, which prevents abuse, from load shedding, which protects against overload using current load, and from concurrency limits. Real systems commonly need all three. the hard testing problem is freezing time. Inject Clock or Supplier<Long> instead of calling System.currentTimeMillis() directly; this makes the code testable and demonstrates good design.","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q3"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q4","surface":"track","en":"How should classes be divided when designing a parking lot or elevator system?","bodyEn":"These are two classic problems. Evaluation is not about one \"correct answer\" but about responsibility boundaries and the ability to add new types. Parking lot — a compact model: enum SpotType { MOTORBIKE, COMPACT, LARGE } // interface Vehicle { SpotType requiredSpot (); String plate (); } // interface PricingStrategy { Money price (Ticket t, Instant out); } interface SpotAllocator { Optional<Spot> allocate (Vehicle v); } // class ParkingLot { private final SpotAllocator allocator; // change the allocation policy private final PricingStrategy pricing; // change the pricing policy public Ticket park (Vehicle v) { ... } public Money unpark (String ticketId) { ... } } Those two interface declarations answer the extension question \"What if pricing changes during peak hours?\": add another PricingStrategy and do not modify ParkingLot. Elevator — the key distinction is separating commands from scheduling: enum Direction { UP, DOWN, IDLE } // class Request { int floor; Direction dir; } // hall call class Elevator { // only knows how to move ITSELF int current; Direction dir; TreeSet<Integer> stops; void step () { ... } // move one floor } interface Scheduler { // choose which car serves the request Elevator assign (Request r, List<Elevator> cabins); } An Elevator must not know about other cars. If it does, it violates single responsibility and cannot be tested independently. the number-one mistake is putting everything in a 300-line ParkingLot class with if (type == ...) scattered throughout. An extension question exposes it immediately. state the invariants aloud: one vehicle occupies one spot, one spot holds one vehicle, and a paid ticket cannot be paid again. Then show where the code enforces them. allocate efficiently with Map<SpotType, PriorityQueue<Spot>> in O(log n) instead of scanning every spot. Complexity analysis earns points even in an LLD round. for elevators, use the SCAN/elevator algorithm, traveling fully in one direction before reversing. Disk I/O scheduling uses the same idea, and connecting it to operating systems is valuable. a TreeSet for stops turns \"the next stop in the current direction\" into O(log n) ceiling()/floor() calls, cleaner than manually sorting concurrency matters when many people press buttons simultaneously (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3). At minimum say, \"I will use a request queue and one dispatcher thread.\" the same template solves vending machines (State pattern), cinema reservations (seat locking), chat, and libraries. Mastering the template matters more than memorizing each problem.","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q4"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q5","surface":"track","en":"Take-home brief: a Q&A REST service where users ask questions, answer open ones, and up- or down-vote both. How do you keep the score right under concurrent votes, and how do you scope three days?","bodyEn":"Read the brief as a contract. Create questions, list open ones, answer, vote ±1 on questions and answers; tags and sign-up are optional. CRUD is not the hard part — a vote is one per user per post, and the score must agree with the votes. API: POST /questions {title, body, tags[]} → 201 + Location GET /questions?status=open&tag=java&cursor=… → one page POST /questions/{id}/answers {body} → 201 PUT /posts/{id}/vote {\"value\": 1 -1} → 200 {score, myVote} DELETE /posts/{id}/vote → 200 {score, myVote: 0} PUT because a vote is the state of my vote, not an event: sending it twice is harmless, so a retry is safe without an idempotency key (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1). Questions and answers share a post table with a kind, so a vote has one foreign key. The constraint is the rule, and a changed vote moves the score by the difference (+1 to −1 is −2): CREATE TABLE vote ( post_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES post, user_id bigint NOT NULL , value smallint NOT NULL CHECK (value IN (- 1 , 1 )), PRIMARY KEY (post_id, user_id) -- one vote per user per post ); ──── one transaction; PostgreSQL 18 returns the old value from the upsert INSERT INTO vote VALUES ($1, $2, $3) ON CONFLICT (post_id, user_id) DO UPDATE SET value = EXCLUDED .value RETURNING COALESCE (old.value, 0 ) AS before; -- old. is NULL on a fresh insert UPDATE post SET score = score + ($3 - :before) WHERE id = $1; -- relative: no lost update On an older PostgreSQL or on MySQL, start the transaction with SELECT … FROM post WHERE id = ? FOR UPDATE: votes on one post then serialize, and reading the old vote is safe. Scope three days by risk, not by feature count: day one: the vote path end to end, with a test that fires 50 concurrent votes and asserts score = SUM(value). day two: questions, answers and the open list with cursor pagination. day three: docker compose up that starts the app and the database with migrations, plus a README listing every assumption. Tags and auth come last. What reviewers read first The vote invariant and its test, the README's assumptions, and whether docker compose up works on a clean machine. Feature count comes after all three. stored score or computed? SUM(value) over an index on vote(post_id) is exact and needs no update path; a stored score makes \"sort by score\" an index scan. Store it only when listing by score is required, and keep a reconciliation query that recomputes it. the relative score = score + delta is the point: read-modify-write in Java (post.setScore(post.getScore() + 1)) loses votes under concurrency unless the row is locked or versioned (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3). per tier: at T1–T3 serializing the votes of one post on its row costs nothing anyone notices; at T4 a viral post is a hot row that needs its own scaling story — votes appended as events, the score folded asynchronously and shown as approximate (25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q1). rules to state as assumptions: no voting on your own post (enforce in the service and answer with a problem-details error), whether closed questions accept votes, and whether a deleted post keeps its votes. \"open\" needs a definition — no accepted answer, or not closed by a moderator. Write it down, index (status, created_at) for the list, and page with a cursor (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q5). RFC 9457 error bodies, validation at the controller boundary and Flyway owning the schema make a take-home read like production code (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2). the same shape — a uniqueness constraint as the rule, a relative counter as the projection — answers likes, follows and reactions in a system-design round.","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q5"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q1","surface":"track","en":"How do you reference SOLID naturally in an LLD interview without sounding formulaic?","bodyEn":"Reciting the five letters from memory is counterproductive. Use them correctly by reaching a design decision, naming the relevant principle, and immediately moving on. S — Single Responsibility. \"I separated PricingStrategy because its reason to change differs from ParkingLot.\" The rule is \"one reason to change\", not \"one method\". O — Open/Closed. \"Adding a new vehicle type means adding an implementation, not editing existing classes.\" This is often useful to demonstrate in an extension question. L — Liskov. A subtype must replace its base type without violating expectations. The classic Square extends Rectangle fails because setWidth also changes the height. I — Interface Segregation. Interfaces should be small and consumer-specific. A 20-method Repository forces every implementation to provide all 20 methods. D — Dependency Inversion. ParkingLot depends on the PricingStrategy abstraction, not the concrete WeekendPricing. A strong interview line \"I apply Open/Closed where I expect change—the pricing calculation. I keep unlikely-to-change areas simple because premature abstraction is also debt.\" Explaining a principle's limits is what sounds senior. the over-engineering trap is creating an interface for every class \"to follow SOLID\". An interface with one implementation that will never gain a second is noise, not good design. YAGNI wins until a real axis of change appears. Dependency Inversion is not Dependency Injection. DI is a common mechanism for achieving DIP; confusing the concepts is a frequent interview mistake. SRP correctly means \"group things that change for the same reason and separate things that change for different reasons\". It is based on who or what requests the change, not the number of tasks a class performs. a common real-world Liskov violation is a subclass throwing UnsupportedOperationException, as with Collections.unmodifiableList. Recognizing that even standard libraries violate the rule shows understanding rather than memorisation. SOLID arose around inheritance-heavy OOP; modern design leans toward composition, pure functions, and immutable data. Mentioning that perspective demonstrates maturity. combine other principles naturally: DRY, while avoiding merging coincidentally similar code, KISS, YAGNI, and the Law of Demeter","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q1"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q2","surface":"track","en":"When should you choose Strategy, State, or Chain of Responsibility?","bodyEn":"The three patterns look similar because all of them plug in behavior, but they answer three different questions. Change axis Prefer Signal Algorithm/rule changes Strategy same lifecycle, different way to do the work Lifecycle/state changes State legal behavior follows the current state Request routing Chain handlers may accept or forward No real change axis plain class/function an extra pattern would be over-engineering Pattern Answers Who chooses the next behavior Strategy how should it be done? — interchangeable algorithms the client chooses; it does not change mid-operation State where are we now? — behavior changes with state the current state transitions to the next state Chain who handles it? — try handlers in sequence until one accepts each handler decides whether to handle or forward Strategy — pricing, compression choice, or scheduling algorithm: lot = new ParkingLot( new WeekendPricing()); // injected at construction State — booking lifecycle, vending machine, or order: interface BookingState { BookingState pay (Booking b); // return the next state BookingState cancel (Booking b); } // class Held implements BookingState { public BookingState pay (Booking b) { b. charge (); return new Paid(); } public BookingState cancel (Booking b) { b. releaseSeat (); return new Cancelled(); } } The benefit is that invalid transitions become impossible: Cancelled.pay() can simply throw an error, with no scattered if statements. Chain of Responsibility — middleware, filters, or validation pipelines: auth. setNext (rateLimit). setNext (validate). setNext (handler); the signal for State is the same if (status == ...) chain repeated across several methods. That is a state machine hidden inside if statements. State holds context and transitions itself; Strategy is stateless and selected externally. It is a useful distinction when the lifecycle, not just the algorithm, changes. in modern Java, a Strategy is often just a lambda (Function/Predicate) rather than a class hierarchy. Saying so shows understanding rather than pattern worship. an enum can implement State compactly in Java by overriding methods per constant, especially when states hold no private data a long Chain is difficult to debug; add logs or traces at every link and define whether an unhandled input is an error or is silently ignored patterns commonly paired in LLD include Factory (construct the correct type from input), Observer (notify changes), Builder (objects with many optional arguments), and Command (undo/redo or queued tasks) do not force patterns into a design to show them off. A misplaced pattern is worse than none; the interviewer will ask \"Why is it needed here?\", and the answer must be real.","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q2"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3","surface":"track","en":"What thread-safety questions arise in an LLD interview, and how should you answer them?","bodyEn":"Almost every LLD problem ends with \"What if multiple threads call it concurrently?\" A prepared framework makes the answer quick. Four levels, in preferred order: Immutability — no shared mutable state means no concurrency problem. Mention this first. Single-thread confinement — funnel work through a queue to one coordinator thread, as in an elevator or actor model. Concurrent data structures — ConcurrentHashMap, AtomicLong, and BlockingQueue. These are sufficient for most problems. Explicit locking — synchronized or ReentrantLock. This is the most powerful and the easiest to get wrong. The common interviewer trap is a compound operation on ConcurrentHashMap: // WRONG: each call is atomic, but the PAIR is not if (!map. containsKey (k)) map. put (k, v); // // CORRECT: one atomic operation map. putIfAbsent (k, v); map. computeIfAbsent (k, key -> new Spot(key)); \"Using ConcurrentHashMap makes it safe\" is incorrect. It guarantees individual operations, not sequences of operations. A correct parking-space allocation example: public Optional<Spot> allocate (Vehicle v) { Queue<Spot> free = freeByType. get (v. requiredSpot ()); Spot s = free. poll (); // ConcurrentLinkedQueue: atomic poll return Optional. ofNullable (s); // one vehicle = one spot, no lock required } minimize lock scope: lock an individual spot or key rather than the entire lot. Using synchronized on every method of a singleton serializes the entire system. consistent lock ordering is the cheapest deadlock prevention: when two locks are needed, always acquire them by increasing ID (see 5.10 and 23.3) ReentrantLock versus synchronized: the former provides tryLock(timeout), interruptible acquisition, and multiple Conditions (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q1). Without those requirements, synchronized is simpler and well optimized by the JIT. do not make outbound HTTP or database calls while holding a lock; doing so lengthens the critical section and increases deadlock risk for read-heavy, write-light workloads, use ReadWriteLock, or copy-on-write when writes are extremely rare (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4) a useful knowledge check: volatile guarantees visibility, not atomicity. volatile int i; i++ is still broken. This is a classic follow-up question. if the prompt does not require concurrency, say \"I will design the single-threaded version first for clarity, then identify the synchronisation points.\" Raising it proactively is better than waiting to be challenged.","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you design an in-memory key-value store with TTL?","bodyEn":"This natural extension of LRU is a miniature Redis and a common extension exercise. Requirements: put(k, v, ttl), get(k), automatic expiry, and no memory leaks. Validity and cleanup are different contracts: get must treat an expired entry as a miss, while cleanup only reclaims space. Lazy expiry therefore needs a bounded sweep, sampling job, or expiry index; a delayed cleanup must not make stale data valid. Three expiry approaches — production systems use all three: Approach Mechanism Trade-off Lazy check expiry during get cheap, but an unread key can remain forever Active sampling a periodic job samples random keys and deletes expired ones steady low CPU cost; cleans cold keys Expiry min-heap a job reads exactly the next key due to expire precise, but the heap needs cleanup when keys are overwritten class TtlStore { private final Map<String, Entry> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(); private final LongSupplier nowMs; TtlStore (LongSupplier nowMs) { this .nowMs = nowMs; } private record Entry(String val, long expireAt) { boolean alive ( long now) { return expireAt == 0 now < expireAt; } } public void put (String k, String v, long ttlMs) { long exp = ttlMs <= 0 ? 0 : nowMs. getAsLong () + ttlMs; map. put (k, new Entry(v, exp)); } public Optional<String> get (String k) { long now = nowMs. getAsLong (); Entry e = map. get (k); if (e == null ) return Optional. empty (); if (!e. alive (now)) { map. remove (k, e); // remove(k,v): do not delete a newer entry return Optional. empty (); } return Optional. of (e. val ()); } } The two-argument map.remove(k, e) is important: another thread may put a new value between the read and delete. Removing by key alone would delete newly written data. Redis uses exactly lazy expiry plus active sampling: every 100 ms it samples 20 keys with TTLs, deletes expired keys, and repeats if more than 25% were expired. Mentioning this is a major plus. a timing wheel, such as Netty's HashedWheelTimer or Kafka's implementation, outperforms a min-heap for millions of expirations with O(1) insertion/removal instead of O(log n) combining TTL with LRU requires both a capacity limit and expiry. Caffeine already provides expireAfterWrite plus maximumSize; mention it to demonstrate library awareness. The injected nowMs source must be monotonic for elapsed TTL checks; a wall clock can jump when NTP adjusts it. Persisted/distributed deadlines need a separate absolute-time and clock-skew policy. persistence requires a WAL or snapshots, such as Redis AOF and RDB. It is usually outside a 45-minute scope but worth identifying. a common extension is getOrLoad using cache-aside plus cache-stampede protection through computeIfAbsent or single-flight, directly connecting to the Microservices caching chapter","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q4"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you design an object model for testability, invariants, and deterministic concurrency tests?","bodyEn":"Testability is an architectural property. Keep domain decisions in small objects or pure functions; put clocks, randomness, ID generation, persistence, messaging, and scheduling behind narrow injected ports. This lets tests control inputs without exposing private fields or adding production-only switches. interface Clock { Instant now (); } interface IdSource { String next (); } final class HoldService { private final Clock clock; private final HoldRepository holds; // constructor injection; no Instant.now() hidden in domain logic } Express each aggregate invariant and legal transition close to the state it protects: a hold cannot expire before creation, a released hold cannot be confirmed, balance-changing commands must preserve the ledger equation. Return explicit outcomes instead of using exceptions for every expected rejection. Make side effects occur after a decision through ports, and define idempotency where commands can be retried. Use a layered test portfolio: Example/unit tests cover boundaries and state transitions with a fake clock and deterministic IDs. Property/model-based tests generate command sequences and assert invariants after every step; a simple reference model can catch optimized-implementation mistakes. Contract/integration tests verify adapters against the real database, broker, serializer, or HTTP contract. Concurrency tests first identify the linearization point, then coordinate targeted interleavings with barriers/latches and bounded timeouts. Add sustained stress or OpenJDK jcstress as exploratory stress evidence for Java Memory Model questions; a passing run is evidence, not proof. Do not use Thread.sleep as synchronization. Inject virtual time for TTL and refill logic; use explicit signals to know when a worker reached the intended point. Record seeds and shrink failing property cases. Assert final state and externally visible history, not implementation timing. Thread safety is part of the public contract: document which methods are safe concurrently, compound-operation semantics, callback/reentrancy rules, fairness, blocking, cancellation, and ownership of returned mutable data. Prefer designs with a single owner or immutable snapshots when they make illegal interleavings unrepresentable. The concurrency reasoning framework is covered in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q3).","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q5"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q6","surface":"track","en":"What must a correct in-memory TTL cache specify beyond O(1) get and put?","bodyEn":"O(1) lookup is only the data-structure part. A correct TTL cache needs an explicit contract for time, visibility, cleanup, replacement, capacity, restart and concurrency. Decision Safe default to discuss Failure if omitted Expiration time Store an absolute deadline; use an injected monotonic elapsed-time source for in-process expiry Wall-clock jumps make entries live too long or expire early Read semantics get checks the deadline and treats expired data as a miss Expired data is returned until a background thread happens to clean it Cleanup Lazy cleanup plus bounded scheduled/eviction work; cleanup is not validity Expired keys leak memory or a cleanup scan creates latency spikes Replacement race Version/generation or compare-and-remove when deleting an expired entry Old cleanup deletes a newer value written for the same key Restart/distribution State whether data is disposable and process-local; use a shared authority only when the contract needs it A local cache is mistaken for a lock, source of truth, or cross-instance rate limit java.time.Clock is injectable but represents wall-clock time. For elapsed TTL in one process, inject a monotonic ticker backed by System.nanoTime; for persisted/distributed deadlines, define clock-skew and absolute-time behavior explicitly. ConcurrentHashMap makes individual map operations safe, not the whole get-or-refresh, put-if-newer, or stale-cleanup workflow. Use compute, a lock/actor, or a versioned compare-and-remove around the invariant. A timer is an optimization. Correctness must remain true if cleanup is delayed, duplicated, reordered, or stopped. In Redis or another shared cache, TTL/eviction/replication are provider contracts; they do not automatically provide fencing, durable truth or business idempotency. Link distributed leases to Topic 28 and API semantics to Topic 17. Tests should advance a fake ticker, race expiry against replacement, restart the process, bound memory, and verify metrics for hit/miss/expired/evicted/stale-cleanup outcomes.","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q6"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q7","surface":"track","en":"How do you write a thread-safe singleton in Java, why is double-checked locking broken without `volatile`, and when should you not write one at all?","bodyEn":"Three correct versions, starting with the one to reach for first: // 1. enum — one line, safe against serialization and reflection enum Registry { INSTANCE; void register (String key) { … } } ──── 2. holder class — lazy and lock-free: the JVM runs Holder's static init once, on first use final class Pricing { private Pricing() {} private static class Holder { static final Pricing I = new Pricing(); } static Pricing get () { return Holder.I; } } ──── 3. double-checked locking — for a lazy INSTANCE field that cannot be static private volatile Config cfg; // volatile is the whole fix Config config () { Config c = cfg; if (c == null ) { synchronized ( this ) { c = cfg; if (c == null ) cfg = c = load (); } } return c; } Why DCL without volatile breaks: cfg = new Config() is not one step. With no happens-before edge, another thread can see the reference before the constructor's writes, skip the lock because the field is non-null, and read a half-built object (01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q1). volatile supplies that edge: writing the reference happens-before every later read of it, and the constructor's writes come with it. That guarantee dates from the Java 5 memory model; before it, DCL was broken even with volatile. In a Spring service, don't write one A singleton-scoped bean is one instance per container, created thread-safely and replaceable in a test. A static GoF singleton is global state a test cannot swap out. eager private static final X I = new X(); is correct and usually enough — laziness only matters when construction is expensive and the class loads long before first use. synchronized on the whole getter is correct too. The objection is not speed (an uncontended lock is cheap) but that the holder idiom makes it unnecessary. the enum form alone resists serialization (otherwise readResolve is needed) and reflective constructor calls (Effective Java, Item 3). Its limit: it cannot extend a class. a singleton is per class loader, not per JVM: two web apps, or a hot-reload loader, give you two (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q8). the local variable c in DCL is not decoration: the fast path reads the volatile field once instead of twice. the strongest reason to avoid the pattern is testability — hidden global state ties every caller to one implementation (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q5).","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q7"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q8","surface":"track","en":"What should a team's coding conventions actually enforce — and which rules belong in a tool rather than a review comment?","bodyEn":"Conventions exist so a reviewer spends attention on design, not on spacing. That splits them in two. Mechanical — a tool fails the build and nobody argues in review: formatting: one formatter (google-java-format through Spotless, or a checked-in IDE profile), run on commit and in CI. style a parser can see: Checkstyle with a shared config, Google's or Sun's as the base. bug patterns: Error Prone; nullness through JSpecify annotations checked by NullAway — the annotations Spring Framework 7 and Boot 4 adopted. Judgement — written down once, applied in review: Rule Good Why names say purpose, not form FILE_PATH_SEPARATOR = \"/\" not SLASH the value may change; the meaning should not booleans read as a question isActive · hasOrders() · canCheckout() if (order.canCheckout()) reads as a sentence one verb per act always findBy… , never also get… or select… for the same lookup a synonym makes the reader hunt for a difference domain words in code the term the business uses the ubiquitous language (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q1) Null is a design decision, not a check sprinkled everywhere. Decide which values may be absent, mark them, and validate at the boundary. Optional belongs on return types — the JDK's own note calls it \"primarily intended for use as a method return type\" — not on fields, parameters or collections. Optional<Customer> findByEmail (String email); // absence is a real answer List<Order> ordersOf (CustomerId id); // empty list, never null void notify(Optional<String> channel) // use an overload or a default if (!ObjectUtils.isEmpty(x)) x.run(); // hides why x could be null The test for a convention If a tool can check it, automate it; if it needs judgement, write the reason beside the rule. A rule with no reason gets broken by the next person who has one. \"never a for loop, always streams\" is a dogma to drop: streams win for transform-filter-collect pipelines; a loop is clearer with an early exit, checked exceptions or index arithmetic. Choose per case, and keep side effects out of map and filter. a first-class collection — a class wrapping one collection with the behaviour on it (Orders.totalPaid()) — gives that behaviour one home; worth it once the same filtering appears in three places. getters only where a caller needs them, and no setters on domain objects: behaviour lives on the object, rather than data pulled out and decided elsewhere — the anemic model (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3). \"a method does one thing\" is about one level of abstraction, not a line count; a long if/else over a type is a strategy, or a switch over a sealed type, waiting to happen (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q7). a constant lives next to its only user (private static final); a project-wide Constants class becomes a junk drawer that every module depends on. comments explain why — a decision, a constraint, a workaround and its ticket. A comment that repeats the code goes stale the first time the code changes. a quality gate in CI (Sonar, Checkstyle) works only if a red gate blocks the merge; a gate people merge past teaches them to ignore it.","contextEn":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","href":"/topics/22-low-level-design-ood#question-22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q8"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q1","surface":"track","en":"Bounded blocking queue: how would you implement it with wait/notify and with Lock/Condition?","bodyEn":"The \"hello world\" of concurrency. The way you implement it immediately reveals your level of fluency. Proof labels — safety · liveness · visibility. The safety invariant is 0 ≤ q.size() ≤ cap. Liveness requires every signal or notifyAll to wake a waiter whose predicate may now hold. Lock/monitor release and reacquisition provide the visibility edge; interruption is part of the contract. ReentrantLock + two Condition instances (this is the version to present): class BoundedQueue<T> { private final Queue<T> q = new ArrayDeque<>(); private final int cap; private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock(); private final Condition notFull = lock. newCondition (); private final Condition notEmpty = lock. newCondition (); BoundedQueue ( int cap) { this .cap = cap; } public void put (T x) throws InterruptedException { lock. lockInterruptibly (); try { while (q. size () == cap) notFull. await (); // WHILE, not IF q. add (x); notEmpty. signal (); // wake only a consumer } finally { lock. unlock (); } } public T take () throws InterruptedException { lock. lockInterruptibly (); try { while (q. isEmpty ()) notEmpty. await (); T x = q. poll (); notFull. signal (); return x; } finally { lock. unlock (); } } } The wait/notify version — the same logic, but there is only one wait set, so notifyAll() is mandatory: public synchronized void put (T x) throws InterruptedException { while (q. size () == cap) wait (); q. add (x); notifyAll (); // notify() may wake another producer => possible liveness failure } Three classic mistakes if instead of while — spurious wakeups and contention after a wakeup can make the condition false again, so it must be rechecked. notify() with one shared wait set — it can wake a thread of the wrong type, one that is waiting for the wrong condition, and the entire system can hang. unlock() outside finally — an exception partway through means the lock is never released. The decisive advantage of Condition is two separate wait sets → signal() (wake one) instead of notifyAll() (wake everyone and let them contend). On a heavily used queue, the performance difference is substantial. await() releases the lock while waiting and reacquires it before returning. Without understanding that, it is impossible to explain why both sides can wait under the same lock without deadlocking. In production, use the built-in ArrayBlockingQueue/LinkedBlockingQueue. Mention that, then still implement it by hand, because that is what the interviewer wants to see. ArrayBlockingQueue uses one lock for both ends. The current JDK implementation of LinkedBlockingQueue uses separate put/take locks, which may permit more overlap; that layout is an implementation detail, not part of the BlockingQueue contract. Common follow-ups: offer(x, timeout), where notFull.await(t, unit) returns false on timeout; and graceful queue shutdown using a closed flag plus signalAll. For a completely lock-free queue, use ConcurrentLinkedQueue, but it is neither blocking nor bounded, so it does not solve this problem. Shutdown contract (abridged): BlockingQueue has no built-in close operation. Define closed under the same monitor, reject new puts, wake every waiter, and let consumers drain existing elements before returning an end-of-stream result. private boolean closed; // same monitor and queue as the wait/notify example public synchronized void close () { closed = true ; notifyAll (); } public synchronized T take () throws InterruptedException { while (q. isEmpty () && !closed) wait (); if (q. isEmpty ()) return null ; // closed and drained T x = q. remove (); notifyAll (); return x; } Because null elements are forbidden by BlockingQueue, null can be the reserved end-of-stream result in this abridged API. A put must wait while !closed and q.size() == cap, then reject after close. wait, await, put, and take may throw InterruptedException: propagate it, or restore the interrupt flag after cleanup; never swallow it. Use ArrayBlockingQueue or LinkedBlockingQueue for the queue contract: put/take are interruptible, offer/poll can be timed, and capacity is explicit. Add closure outside that contract with wrapper state or a sentinel; do not depend on a current implementation layout. Test full and empty states with multiple producers/consumers; close while put/take waits; interrupt each wait; assert bounded size, prompt wake-up, drained items, and no task remains blocked.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q1"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q2","surface":"track","en":"Printing in strict alternating order (print FooBar / print in order): what are the approaches and trade-offs?","bodyEn":"LC 1114/1115. The problem: two (or three) threads must produce output in the required order regardless of scheduling order. Proof labels — safety · ordering · liveness · visibility. The safety property is the exact output sequence; fairness is a scheduling policy, not the proof of ordering. Cancellation is separate: if a participant exits after taking its permit, its partner can wait forever unless the protocol publishes a terminal state and wakes both sides. Approach 1 — Semaphore (the most concise and the best one to present): class FooBar { private final int n; private final Semaphore fooReady = new Semaphore( 1 ); // foo goes first private final Semaphore barReady = new Semaphore( 0 ); public void foo (Runnable printFoo) throws InterruptedException { for ( int i = 0 ; i < n; i++) { fooReady. acquire (); printFoo. run (); barReady. release (); // hand the turn to bar } } public void bar (Runnable printBar) throws InterruptedException { for ( int i = 0 ; i < n; i++) { barReady. acquire (); printBar. run (); fooReady. release (); } } } The idea: each thread has its own permit. After printing, it hands the permit to the next thread. The initial permit counts determine who goes first. Approach 2 — synchronized + wait/notifyAll + a turn variable: private int turn = 0 ; public synchronized void foo (Runnable p) throws InterruptedException { while (turn != 0 ) wait (); p. run (); turn = 1 ; notifyAll (); } This approach scales easily to three or more threads (turn cycles through them), but notifyAll wakes threads unnecessarily. Approach Advantages Drawbacks Semaphore concise, no explicit lock, clear intent more threads require more semaphores wait/notifyAll + turn scales easily to n threads unnecessary wakeups; must remember while Lock + n Condition precise wakeups the most verbose CyclicBarrier suited to round-based synchronization does not impose order within a round by itself Do not use volatile plus busy-waiting (while (turn != 0) {}). It produces the right output but burns CPU. The interviewer will immediately ask, \"What if the other thread is slow?\" Semaphore generalizes to n threads: use a Semaphore[n] array; thread i waits on sem[i] and releases sem[(i+1)%n]. Explaining the general solution earns points. The printNumber variant (LC 1116, zero-even-odd) follows the same pattern: one semaphore each for zero, even, and odd. Semaphore(1) differs from ReentrantLock: a semaphore has no owner, so thread A may acquire it and thread B may release it. That is precisely why it fits this problem while a lock does not. Exchanger is the right tool when two threads need to exchange paired data, not merely hand off turns. Always cover the edge cases: when n is zero, nothing is printed; and every thread must be able to exit, with none left blocked in acquire after the final round. A fair Semaphore(1, true) can reduce starvation among queued acquirers, but it does not establish output order; the permit handoff does. If foo/bar is interrupted or printing fails after acquire, publish cancellation and release or wake the peer according to a terminal-state policy; otherwise the other loop can block forever. Test ordering with a barrier that starts both methods repeatedly. Inject interruption and failure at each handoff, then assert the peer exits instead of waiting on a permit that will never return.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q2"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q3","surface":"track","en":"Dining philosophers: which approaches prevent deadlock?","bodyEn":"Five philosophers, five forks, and each philosopher needs the two adjacent forks. If everyone picks up the left fork first, the result is a perfect deadlock. This problem is really about the four Coffman conditions and four ways to break them. Proof labels — safety · liveness · ordering · visibility. For resource ordering, define one total order for every fork and acquire strictly ascending. A philosopher may hold a lower-ranked fork while waiting for a higher-ranked one. A circular wait would require an edge to go down the order; that is impossible. This proves deadlock freedom, not starvation freedom. Approach 1 — Resource ordering (the most common approach; breaks \"circular wait\"): public void wantsToEat ( int p, Runnable pickLeft, Runnable pickRight, Runnable eat, Runnable putLeft, Runnable putRight) { int left = p, right = (p + 1 ) % 5 ; int first = Math. min (left, right), second = Math. max (left, right); forks[first]. lock (); // ALWAYS acquire the lower-numbered fork first forks[second]. lock (); try { pickLeft. run (); pickRight. run (); eat. run (); putLeft. run (); putRight. run (); } finally { forks[second]. unlock (); forks[first]. unlock (); } } Approach 2 — Limit how many philosophers may sit at the table (breaks \"hold and wait\"): use Semaphore(4) for five philosophers, ensuring that at least one can always acquire both forks. This breaks deadlock, but the semaphore's default unfairness does not prove starvation freedom; choose fair permits or an explicit queue when every philosopher needs a turn. Approach 3 — Asymmetry: even-numbered philosophers pick up the left fork first; odd-numbered philosophers pick up the right one first. Approach 4 — tryLock with a timeout (breaks \"no preemption\"): if a philosopher cannot acquire both forks, release everything and retry. Watch for livelock; randomized backoff is required. The four Coffman conditions (all four are necessary for deadlock): mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, and circular wait. Each approach above breaks one condition. Framing the answer this way makes the reasoning rigorous. Resource ordering is the most broadly applicable technique in production: when transferring money between two accounts, always lock them in ascending ID order. Connecting this to 5.10 (database deadlocks) is a strong bonus. tryLock without backoff can produce livelock: everyone repeatedly acquires and releases in lockstep, so nobody gets to eat. The CPUs remain busy, yet the system makes no progress; recognizing that distinction demonstrates depth. Fairness: new ReentrantLock(true) can reduce starvation by favoring queued waiters, but it adds scheduling overhead and is not a substitute for bounded work inside the critical section. State the trade-off instead of choosing one policy by default. A fifth concern that is often overlooked is starvation. It is not deadlock: the system continues to run, but one philosopher never gets a turn. Approach 2 and fair locks address it. Production mapping: forks can represent database row locks, pooled connections, or file locks. It is the same problem with different names. Test a bounded run with every approach under contention; inject interruption and timeout while a philosopher holds one resource. Assert every acquired fork is released, then record both deadlock freedom and wait-time/starvation rather than only the final output.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q3"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4","surface":"track","en":"Read-heavy, write-light workloads: ReadWriteLock, StampedLock, or copy-on-write?","bodyEn":"Proof labels — safety · ordering · visibility. Tool Mechanism Best fit synchronized one thread at a time frequent writes or a very short critical section ReentrantReadWriteLock multiple readers OR one writer reads ≫ writes and long critical sections StampedLock adds lock-free optimistic reads extremely frequent reads and very rare writes CopyOnWriteArrayList copies the entire array on each write extremely rare writes (listener lists) StampedLock — the standard optimistic-read pattern: private final StampedLock sl = new StampedLock(); // public double distanceFromOrigin () { long stamp = sl. tryOptimisticRead (); // acquire NO lock double cx = x, cy = y; // copy values into local variables if (!sl. validate (stamp)) { // did a writer intervene? stamp = sl. readLock (); // yes => fall back to a real read lock try { cx = x; cy = y; } finally { sl. unlockRead (stamp); } } return Math. sqrt (cx cx + cy cy); } Optimistic read is not a read lock. Copy every field in the snapshot before validate; if validation fails, discard the entire local snapshot and reread under readLock. This is safe only for state protected by this StampedLock, not a transaction across unrelated state. The fast path performs no memory writes → it can be much faster than ReadWriteLock when writes are rare. The trade-off is that you must follow the pattern exactly: copy into local variables, call validate, and provide a fallback path. StampedLock is not reentrant Re-entering it can deadlock immediately, unlike synchronized and ReentrantLock. It also does not support Condition. This is the main reason it should not be the default choice. ReadWriteLock is not always faster than synchronized. Its bookkeeping costs more, so synchronized wins for short critical sections of just a few lines. Measure it; do not guess. Writer starvation: the default non-fair ReentrantReadWriteLock can admit readers continuously while a writer waits indefinitely. new ReentrantReadWriteLock(true) fixes that at the cost of throughput. You can downgrade (write → read), but you cannot upgrade (read → write). Upgrading is a classic deadlock when two readers both attempt it. A write to CopyOnWriteArrayList is O(n) and creates garbage. It is ideal for listener lists that are populated at startup and read forever, but a disaster for lists that change continuously. A copy-on-write iterator captures a snapshot at creation time: iteration never throws ConcurrentModificationException, but it also does not observe later changes. State that semantic explicitly. Often, the best lock is no lock: use ConcurrentHashMap, AtomicReference + updateAndGet, or an immutable snapshot swapped with a single volatile assignment. Copy-on-write allocates a new backing array O(n) for each mutation. Old snapshots may remain live while iterators or readers use them, so memory and GC pressure depend on element count, write bursts, and retained iterators. Measure allocation and write latency; read-heavy alone is not enough. Test optimistic reads while writers mutate, validate before using composite values, and verify the fallback path. Test that a copy-on-write iterator sees its creation-time snapshot, then measure allocation under a write burst.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q1","surface":"track","en":"CompletableFuture: how do you correctly make parallel calls, handle failures, and enforce timeouts?","bodyEn":"A common interview prompt: \"A booking page must call three services (pricing, seat availability, and promotions). How do you run them in parallel so total latency is determined by the slowest service?\" Proof labels — safety · liveness · visibility. The safety boundary is one result contract per request; liveness is bounded completion and cancellation; visibility follows the future and executor handoff. Failure policy must be explicit at the fan-in point. CompletableFuture<Price> price = CompletableFuture. supplyAsync (() -> priceApi. get (id), pool) . orTimeout ( 800 , MILLISECONDS) // Java 9+ . exceptionally (e -> Price. unavailable ()); // degrade gracefully; do not fail the entire page // CompletableFuture<Seats> seats = CompletableFuture. supplyAsync (() -> seatApi. get (id), pool) . orTimeout ( 800 , MILLISECONDS) . exceptionally (e -> Seats. unknown ()); // // allOf waits for every stage; the subsequent join() calls no longer block in practice CompletableFuture<Page> page = CompletableFuture . allOf (price, seats) . thenApply (v -> new Page(price. join (), seats. join ())); Methods you must distinguish: thenApply (synchronous transformation) vs thenCompose (chain another future — effectively flatMap) thenCombine (combine two futures) vs allOf (wait for many) vs anyOf (take the first to complete) exceptionally (runs only on failure) vs handle (runs on success or failure) vs whenComplete (observes the outcome without transforming it) Three traps you must know Using ForkJoinPool.commonPool() for I/O can saturate a resource shared across the JVM. Its default parallelism is often available processors minus one, but runtime configuration and container limits can differ. Inspect it; use an owned I/O pool. Calling get()/join() too early in the chain turns asynchronous work into sequential work and eliminates the benefit. Swallowing exceptions: without exceptionally/handle, the failure remains in the future until join() surfaces it, wrapped in CompletionException. thenApply vs thenApplyAsync: the non-Async variant may run on the thread that completes the preceding stage. If that is Netty's I/O thread, you have just occupied the event loop. Heavy work belongs in the Async variant with a dedicated pool. orTimeout (Java 9+) completes the future exceptionally with TimeoutException; completeOnTimeout supplies a default value. The latter is often the better degradation policy. A CompletableFuture timeout does not cancel the underlying HTTP call; its thread can continue running to completion. You must also configure a timeout at the client layer (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q4). allOf returns CompletableFuture<Void>. To obtain the results, join() each constituent future afterward; that is safe because they have completed. Failures are wrapped in CompletionException. Use ex.getCause() when classifying them; this is easy to forget. Context such as MDC, trace IDs, and SecurityContext does not propagate automatically to another thread. Wrap the executor (for example, with Spring's TaskDecorator), or logs will lose their trace ID (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q4). Java 21+: for I/O, virtual threads plus sequential code are often far clearer than a CompletableFuture chain (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4). Mentioning this shows that your concurrency model is current. Exception policy belongs at fan-in. If degraded values are not acceptable, keep raw futures, use handle to collect outcome records, and make the aggregate preserve the root causes. allOf does not cancel siblings. orTimeout only completes the stage. Keep a handle to the HTTP client's request or Future, configure a client-level deadline, and close or cancel that resource on timeout. Future.cancel(true) is only an interrupt request; test that the I/O stops and releases sockets. The executor is an owned resource: use a bounded executor for the blocking workload, define rejection or degradation when full, and shut it down after its lifecycle. Never shut down an executor shared by other callers. Test one fast failure, one slow child, timeout, and caller cancellation; assert the slow client is closed and no late side effect remains after the page future completes.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q1"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q2","surface":"track","en":"CountDownLatch, CyclicBarrier, Semaphore, and Phaser: how do they differ?","bodyEn":"These four synchronization tools are often asked about back-to-back because they are easy to confuse. Proof labels — safety · liveness · visibility. The safety property is the intended phase or permit state; liveness requires every participant to reach a terminal phase or be cancelled; synchronizer memory effects provide visibility across the handoff. Tool Purpose Reusable Who waits for whom CountDownLatch wait for N tasks to finish before proceeding no — one-shot one or more waiters await N events CyclicBarrier rendezvous N threads at one point yes — resets automatically the threads wait for one another Semaphore limit concurrent entrants yes threads wait for permits Phaser a barrier whose party count can change yes, across multiple phases flexible CountDownLatch — accept traffic only after startup completes: CountDownLatch ready = new CountDownLatch( 3 ); // pool. submit (() -> { loadCache(); ready. countDown (); }); pool. submit (() -> { warmPool(); ready. countDown (); }); pool. submit (() -> { loadConfig(); ready. countDown (); }); // if (!ready. await ( 30 , SECONDS)) throw new IllegalStateException( \"startup timed out\" ); CyclicBarrier — round-based simulation, with every participant meeting after each round: CyclicBarrier barrier = new CyclicBarrier( 4 , () -> mergeRoundResults ()); // each worker has finished computing one round barrier. await (); // the last worker to arrive runs mergeRoundResults() The key distinction: CountDownLatch cannot be reset. For repeated coordination, create a new latch or use CyclicBarrier/Phaser. This is the most common comparison question. countDown() must be in a finally block; otherwise, one task throwing an exception can leave everyone waiting forever. await() should always have a timeout. Waiting indefinitely in a production service is a recipe for hanging the entire system. CyclicBarrier throws BrokenBarrierException for every participant when one thread is interrupted or times out. This is deliberate: without the full group, the round is meaningless. Semaphore limits concurrency (for example, at most 10 simultaneous calls to a partner). This is a manual bulkhead, not rate limiting, which limits work over time (22.3). Phaser is powerful but less common: the number of registered parties can change between phases (register/arriveAndDeregister), which suits multi-stage computations whose workers join and leave. Java 21+: StructuredTaskScope can replace most \"run several tasks, then wait\" use cases more safely by canceling siblings automatically when one branch fails (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4). Exercise the failure lifecycle: make one worker throw before countDown, interrupt a barrier waiter, exhaust semaphore permits, and add or remove Phaser parties. Every test needs a timeout and must verify that no worker remains blocked.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q2"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q3","surface":"track","en":"Atomics and CAS: how do you implement a lock-free counter/stack, and what is the ABA problem?","bodyEn":"Proof labels — safety · linearizability · liveness · visibility. The counter invariant is exact only when the operation's atomicity matches the business rule; a stack additionally needs linearizability and a progress argument. CAS visibility does not prove either one. CAS (compare-and-swap) is an atomic CPU instruction: \"If the memory location still equals expected, replace it with newValue.\" The entire java.util.concurrent.atomic package is built on it. // The essence of incrementAndGet: a retry loop public int increment () { int cur, next; do { cur = value. get (); next = cur + 1 ; } while (!value. compareAndSet (cur, next)); // on failure, reread and retry return next; } No locking and no blocking: a losing thread retries. This is lock-free as a system-level progress claim—some operation completes despite contention—not a guarantee that every thread finishes within a bound. Lock-free stack (Treiber stack): public void push (T item) { Node<T> newHead = new Node<>(item); Node<T> oldHead; do { oldHead = head. get (); newHead.next = oldHead; } while (!head. compareAndSet (oldHead, newHead)); } The ABA problem: thread 1 reads head = A. Before it performs CAS, thread 2 pops A, pops B, and then pushes A back. Thread 1's CAS still succeeds because the head is again A, but A.next has changed and the stack is corrupted. The solution is to attach a version number. AtomicStampedReference compares both the reference and the stamp. It detects a class of ABA histories; it does not by itself prove the algorithm's linearizability, prevent stamp overflow, or define safe node-reclamation policy. AtomicStampedReference<Node> head = new AtomicStampedReference<>( null , 0 ); head. compareAndSet (oldHead, newHead, stamp, stamp + 1 ); CAS excels under low contention. Under high contention, retry loops burn CPU and can be slower than locks; a lock can at least park the thread. Use LongAdder for high-contention statistics, not for an exact balance, sequence, or ID. It spreads updates across cells and combines them on read; sum() is not an atomic snapshot while updates continue. Prefer AtomicLong, a lock, or ownership when the invariant needs an exact value. GC makes ABA less dangerous than C++ for reclamation: a reachable object is not reclaimed and reused as an unrelated object. Logical A→B→A is still possible, and deliberately reused nodes still need a version or stamp plus an algorithm proof. AtomicReference.updateAndGet(fn) packages the CAS loop for you. The function must be pure and free of side effects because it may be invoked multiple times. The atomic API gives operations, not a proof that your stack is linearizable or lock-free. Distinguish the progress guarantees: lock-free means system-wide progress, stronger than obstruction-free and weaker than wait-free, where every thread completes within a finite number of steps. volatile provides visibility only; CAS provides both visibility and atomicity. This is one of the most common conceptual follow-ups (see 1.1, JMM). Java 9+ provides VarHandle instead of Unsafe for low-level atomic operations; knowing the name is sufficient. Use jcstress for JMM and atomic interleavings, and a linearizability or history checker for the stack. Include CAS-failure and progress observations, ABA or version-wrap cases, and memory-reclamation assumptions; a final-size assertion alone misses stale-state histories.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q3"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4","surface":"track","en":"Virtual threads (Java 21+): how do they change the way you write concurrency code?","bodyEn":"Proof labels — safety · liveness · visibility. The lifetime safety rule is that child work stays inside its owner scope; liveness is bounded by downstream admission and cancellation; virtual-thread scheduling does not remove either resource contract. A virtual thread is a Java Thread scheduled by the JVM over carrier threads. It is cheap enough to create per task; supported blocking operations park and unmount it so the carrier can run other work. // One virtual thread per task; this executor is not a reusable thread pool try ( var executor = Executors. newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor ()) { for ( var id : bookingIds) { executor. submit (() -> { var price = priceApi. get (id); var seats = seatApi. get (id); return new Page(price, seats); }); } } // close() waits for submitted tasks The main benefit is structured-looking blocking code with ordinary stack traces, exceptions, and debugger behavior at high I/O concurrency. It is not automatically faster than reactive code, and it does not add backpressure. JDK 25 preview API — manage related task lifetimes together: // Compile and run with --enable-preview on JDK 25 try ( var scope = StructuredTaskScope. open ()) { var price = scope. fork (() -> priceApi. get (id)); var seats = scope. fork (() -> seatApi. get (id)); scope. join (); // failure cancels the sibling and propagates return new Page(price. get (), seats. get ()); } JDK 25 preview boundary: The no-argument StructuredTaskScope.open() is a fail-fast preview policy: a failed subtask cancels siblings and join propagates the failure. Pinning guidance changed in JDK 24 On JDK 21–23, blocking while holding a synchronized monitor could pin the carrier, so moving I/O out of the monitor or using ReentrantLock was a valid targeted fix. JEP 491 removed monitor-related pinning in JDK 24. Native/foreign calls and class-initialization edge cases can still hold a carrier, but replacing every monitor is no longer sound advice. Do not pool virtual threads merely to bound a scarce resource. Create one per task, then use a semaphore or the downstream's real pool as the capacity control; pooling tasks and limiting a resource are different concerns. ThreadLocal remains compatible, but per-thread mutable state can be costly at large concurrency. ScopedValue is final in JDK 25 for immutable bounded context; it is not a replacement for all mutable thread-local state. CPU-bound work remains limited by cores. Use bounded parallelism and measure context-switch/queue overhead. A 20-connection database pool still admits only 20 concurrent queries. Cheap waiters can improve utilization, but excessive waiters consume memory and exhaust deadlines. For JDK 21–23, jdk.VirtualThreadPinned JFR data and -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads can locate monitor pinning. The system property was removed in JDK 24; on newer JDKs use JFR, JSON thread dumps, carrier utilization, and latency evidence. StructuredTaskScope is preview in JDK 25 and its constructors changed to open(...); do not publish the older new ShutdownOnFailure() form as JDK 25 code. Use reactive/event-loop I/O where explicit streaming and backpressure fit the whole stack; use virtual threads where blocking APIs and sequential control flow improve maintainability. Compare the broader models in (15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q3). JEP 491 changed monitor pinning in JDK 24; it did not promise that virtual threads never pin. Test native or foreign calls, class initialization, and blocking resource clients on the target JDK with JFR instead of inferring safety from thread count. Test cancellation and scope close: interrupt a child during I/O, verify resource cleanup, and run the same pinning probe on JDK 21–23 versus JDK 24+.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q5","surface":"track","en":"Interview drill: 20 threads each add one million to a `volatile int counter`. Is the total right, and how do you prove your answer?","bodyEn":"Proof labels — safety · visibility. The safety question is whether each increment is an atomic compound action; volatile supplies visibility and ordering only. The proof must cover every interleaving, not one benchmark result. No guarantee. The maximum is 20,000,000, and that result is possible when increments happen without overlapping. With concurrent read-modify-write interleavings, lost updates can make the result smaller; volatile provides visibility and ordering, not atomicity. counter++ is three bytecodes: read the field, add one, write it back. Two threads that read the same value both write back v + 1, so one increment disappears. volatile guarantees each read sees a value from the synchronization order; it never fuses the three steps into one. static volatile int counter = 0 ; // 20 threads x 1_000_000 increments try ( var pool = Executors. newFixedThreadPool ( 20 )) { for ( int t = 0 ; t < 20 ; t++) pool. submit (() -> { for ( int i = 0 ; i < 1_000_000 ; i++) counter++; }); } System.out. println (counter); // e.g. 3_147_882 — lower is possible; 20_000_000 is possible too, so one run proves nothing The fix ladder, cheapest correct option first: Tool Use when What it costs AtomicInteger one counter, moderate contention CAS retries, all on one cache line LongAdder write-heavy counter, read rarely striped cells; sum() is not a snapshot while writes continue synchronized / lock two or more fields must move together blocking; keeping the critical section small is on you no sharing at all per-thread totals combined at the end nothing — this is why reduce and collect scale What volatile is actually for Publication, not accumulation: a stop flag, a swapped configuration reference — state whose every invariant fits inside one atomic read or one atomic write (01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q1). \"Run it and look\" is a weak proof. A lost update is timing-dependent, and a JIT-optimised loop on an idle laptop can print the right number while proving nothing. Two stronger arguments: by construction — name the three steps and the interleaving that drops one; no measurement makes that safe by tooling — jcstress, the OpenJDK concurrency harness, drives the interleavings and reports the outcomes actually observed, including ones a plain test never reaches Atomics do not make compound actions atomic either. if (counter.get() < limit) counter.incrementAndGet() is the same bug one level up: use compareAndSet in a loop, updateAndGet, or a lock (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q3). Watch the read side too. Incrementing under a lock is correct, but reading the field from another thread without that lock is a fresh visibility problem — the fix has to cover both sides of the field, which is exactly what AtomicInteger gives you in one object. Test hooks. A jcstress test with Actor methods and an Arbiter can record allowed outcomes for the race; a stress test can expose loss but cannot prove its absence. For an exact total, use AtomicInteger, a lock, or per-thread ownership followed by a final reduction.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q5"},{"id":"23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q6","surface":"track","en":"How do you design cancellation and backpressure for async fan-out without orphan work?","bodyEn":"Start with the resource contract, not with CompletableFuture.allOf. Fan-out must have a parent deadline, a bounded admission policy, an explicit executor owner, and a rule for what happens to children when the caller leaves. Proof labels — safety · liveness · ordering · visibility. The safety property is no work or side effect after scope closure; liveness is bounded completion and cancellation; ordering covers deadline and admission propagation; visibility follows the owned cancellation state. Concern Required decision Failure to test Admission Bound concurrency with a semaphore, bounded executor/queue, or structured scope; reject or degrade explicitly Unbounded fan-out exhausts CPU, sockets, DB connections or provider quota Deadline Carry one monotonic deadline into each child and subtract elapsed time Each child gets a fresh timeout and total latency exceeds the request budget Failure policy Choose fail-fast, best-effort aggregation, or partial result; preserve the first/root cause One failure is hidden behind an aggregate exception or partial side effects are mistaken for success Cancellation Define whether cancellation interrupts the task, closes the I/O, releases permits, and prevents retries orTimeout completes a future while the underlying supplier keeps running Lifecycle Own and shut down executors; do not leak work into the common pool or a request-scoped executor Orphan tasks survive deploy/shutdown and continue writing after the response timed out CompletableFuture.orTimeout changes completion of the future; it is not a proof that arbitrary blocking I/O stopped. The adapter must own cancellation/close semantics, and tests must observe the resource after timeout. allOf does not decide whether sibling tasks should be cancelled, whether their exceptions are collected, or whether side effects are safe to retry. Write that policy in code and documentation. A semaphore limits admission but does not cancel work already admitted; a bounded queue creates a different latency/rejection contract. Measure queue age, permit wait, child p99 and cancellation lag. StructuredTaskScope in the researched JDK 25 API is preview. It provides a useful lifetime model, but the selected compiler/runtime must opt in and the application still needs downstream bulkheads. For mutations, cancellation may leave an unknown external outcome. Use an idempotency key and reconciliation boundary rather than blindly retrying a timed-out child. Tests should cover deadline propagation, sibling cancellation, executor shutdown, interrupted/late completion, retry suppression, permit release and no work after the request scope closes.","contextEn":"Java concurrency coding — synchronisation problems, written out","href":"/topics/23-java-concurrency-coding#question-23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q6"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q1","surface":"track","en":"What problem does DDD actually solve? What does ubiquitous language really mean?","bodyEn":"DDD is not a catalog of patterns to copy. It solves a communication problem: the business uses one vocabulary, the code uses another, and every translation between them is another opportunity for bugs. Ubiquitous language = one shared vocabulary used by developers, BAs, QA, and domain experts — and represented verbatim in the code. // The business says: \"hold a seat\", \"issue a ticket\", \"refund a ticket\" // // WRONG — technical vocabulary that must be translated at every meeting bookingService. updateStatus (id, 2 ); bookingRepository. setFlag (id, true ); // // RIGHT — reads exactly like a sentence the business uses booking. holdSeat (seatNo); booking. issueTicket (gdsRef); booking. refund (reason); A simple test: read a method aloud to a domain expert. If they understand it immediately → the language is shared. If you have to explain that \"status 2 means the seat is held\" → it is not shared yet. But the language is consistent only within a bounded context — not across the entire company. That is the subject of 24.2. The biggest trap DDD is not \"having a domain directory\" or \"using @Entity\". Many projects claim to practice DDD but are really layered architecture with renamed directories: entities have only getters and setters, while every rule lives in a Service (12.11 anemic domain). Directory structure is not DDD; modeling is. DDD has a cost: it requires a domain expert working closely with the team. Without one, everything else is ceremony — this is a prerequisite that books are often skimmed past. Separate its two parts clearly: strategic design (dividing contexts and drawing maps — the highest-value part, which everyone should know) and tactical design (aggregates, VOs, and repositories — worth the cost only in the core domain). Classify subdomains to decide where to invest: core (competitive advantage — engineer it carefully and apply DDD here), supporting (necessary but not differentiating — keep it simple), and generic (sending email, generating PDFs — buy it or use a library). Being able to articulate all three is a sign of genuine understanding. The shared language must live in the code: when a business term changes, rename the class as well. A glossary in Confluence while the code still says status = 2 is a dead language. When NOT to use it (12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q3): plain CRUD, a thin domain, a short-lived project, or a team with no DDD experience. Explaining when it is inappropriate matters as much as explaining when it is appropriate.","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q1"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q2","surface":"track","en":"Bounded contexts: how do you identify the right boundaries?","bodyEn":"A bounded context is the scope within which one model and one vocabulary have consistent meaning. Outside that boundary, the same term may mean something different. How to find boundaries — in descending order of reliability: The language changes meaning — the strongest signal. If you must ask \"which Ticket do you mean?\", you are standing at a boundary. Different rates of change — a part that changes weekly should not be coupled to one that changes once a year. Ownership — under Conway's law, system boundaries tend to align with team boundaries. Going against that means fighting the organization. Invariants that must be maintained together — do not separate things that require immediate consistency (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2). A boundary is not: a database table, a technical module (web, service), or a JPA entity. Splitting by table is a reliable way to create a distributed monolith (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3). A bounded context may contain several aggregates; an aggregate must not span two contexts. In source code, a context should be a top-level module/package (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q8). Spring Modulith can enforce that boundary inside a monolith without extracting a service. Boundaries are not fixed: redraw them as understanding grows. Redrawing one in a monolith is a refactor; redrawing one after extracting microservices is a project. That is precisely the case for \"modular monolith first\" (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q5). A common mistake is creating a Common/Shared context for shared entities. It becomes a shared dependency that tightly couples the contexts; a change by either team can break the other; prefer controlled duplication within each context. For a legacy system that cannot be separated, place an Anti-Corruption Layer (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q6) at the boundary so the old model does not leak into the new context. A probing question: \"Are a context and a microservice the same thing?\" — no. A context is a design concept; a service is a deployment unit. A service should contain exactly one context, but a context can live perfectly well inside a monolith.","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q2"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3","surface":"track","en":"Context mapping: what relationships can exist between two bounded contexts?","bodyEn":"After contexts are separated, they still have to communicate. A context map describes the power relationships between them — a subject that few people mention but that often appears in senior interviews. Type Meaning Use when Partnership two teams succeed or fail together and collaborate closely the two contexts must release together Shared Kernel share part of the model/code rare — both sides must agree on every change Customer / Supplier the upstream serves the downstream, with negotiation both are in one company and the downstream has influence Conformist the downstream accepts the upstream model there is no negotiating leverage (a third-party API) Anti-Corruption Layer the downstream translates into its own model the upstream model is polluted or legacy Open Host Service the upstream exposes a common API to many consumers several downstreams use it Published Language a shared, versioned contract OpenAPI/Protobuf/event schema Separate Ways do not integrate; accept duplication integration cost > benefit In the OTA example, Booking ↔ Payment is Customer/Supplier. Ticketing ↔ GDS (Amadeus/Sabre) uses an ACL: the GDS model is very old and cannot be changed, so translation belongs at the boundary. A public partner API is Open Host + Published Language (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q4). The real value of a context map is not its boxes and arrows, but how it exposes organizational relationships: \"the other team will never change it for us\" → Conformist or ACL, not a fantasy Partnership. An ACL pays off most when integrating with a legacy system or third party: a thin translation layer prevents their polluted model from spreading into your domain. The cost is mapping; the benefits are a clean domain and a single place to change when replacing the provider. A Shared Kernel is debt: it sounds economical, but forces two teams to coordinate every change. Use it only when one team genuinely owns both contexts. Integration through events (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3) is often healthier than synchronous calls: a failing downstream cannot take down the upstream, and the relationship naturally becomes Open Host + Published Language. Separate Ways is underrated: letting two contexts calculate tax independently can be far cheaper than building a shared TaxService that makes everyone wait. Draw the context map before extracting microservices. Without one, extraction merely splits the system by database table — the recipe for a distributed monolith.","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q4","surface":"track","en":"Event storming: how do you run a workshop that reveals the boundaries?","bodyEn":"Event storming is the most practical way to move from \"nobody knows what the system does\" to a context map in a single workshop. Developers, BAs, and domain experts stand together at one wall. Sticky-note color conventions (standardized; do not invent your own): Color Represents Example Orange Domain event — something that happened, in the past tense SeatHeld , PaymentCaptured Blue Command — an intention, in the imperative mood HoldSeat , IssueTicket Yellow Actor — who initiates it Customer, customer-service agent Pink External system GDS, PSP Purple Policy — \"whenever X, then Y\" seat hold expires → release seat Pale yellow Aggregate — where the command lands Booking, Payment Red Hot spot — a disputed or unclear point \"how is a partial cancellation charged?\" Four steps: Put all domain events on the wall without ordering them yet — whenever someone names one, add it. Arrange them on a timeline from left → right. Contradictions surface immediately; mark them with red notes. Add the command, actor, and external system before each event; add policies between events. Cluster events that tend to occur together → each cluster is an aggregate; where the language changes meaning → a context boundary. Red notes are the most valuable output of the workshop. They pinpoint where the business has not reached agreement — exactly where bugs appear when developers guess. The golden rule: do not discuss technical solutions during the workshop. Someone saying \"we'll put this on Kafka\" pulls the entire room off course. Events must be in the past tense (SeatHeld, not HoldSeat). That sounds minor, but it forces everyone to discuss what happened rather than how to implement it. There are three scopes: big picture (the whole company, to find contexts), process modeling (one process), and design level (deriving aggregates and commands directly). Choose the scope that fits the question you need to answer. Before everyone leaves, turn the result into a context map (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q3) plus an aggregate list (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2). Otherwise, the next day all that remains is a photograph nobody reads. Remote sessions work in Miro or FigJam, but a physical wall is still better because people can stand up, point, and argue more naturally.","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q4"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q1","surface":"track","en":"Entity vs. Value Object: what distinguishes them, and why are Value Objects more useful than they seem?","bodyEn":"Entity Value Object Identity has an id ; distinguished by identity has no id; equal when all fields are equal Mutable? yes; its state changes over time immutable — replace it to make a change Example Booking , Passenger Money , SeatNumber , DateRange Equality equals by id equals by value Value Objects are among the most underused and highest-leverage building blocks. They turn runtime mistakes into compile-time errors: // Before: everything is a primitive — \"primitive obsession\" void refund (String bookingId, long amount, String currency) { ... } refund (seatNo, price, code); // compiles successfully; completely wrong // // After: types express intent public record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) { public Money { if (amount. scale () > currency. getDefaultFractionDigits ()) throw new IllegalArgumentException( \"invalid decimal scale for \" + currency); } public Money plus (Money o) { if (!currency. equals (o.currency)) throw new DomainException( \"currency mismatch\" ); return new Money(amount. add (o.amount), currency); } } void refund (BookingId id, Money amount) { ... } // an invalid call does not compile Adding amounts in two different currencies is now impossible, rather than something developers must remember not to do. Java 16+ record types are an almost perfect fit for VOs: they are immutable, implement equals/hashCode with value semantics, and provide a compact constructor for validation. Before records, all of that required hand-written code — one reason VOs are less common in older Java codebases. VOs are the most natural place for small rules: Money.plus rejects different currencies, while DateRange rejects an end date before its start date. Put a rule here and callers cannot forget to apply it. Immutability gives thread safety for free (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4): a VO can be shared between threads without locking. Persist VOs in JPA with @Embeddable/@Embedded, or an AttributeConverter for a single-field VO. If the domain model is separate from persistence entities (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2), this presents no difficulty at all. Do not turn every String into a VO. Use one when it carries rules or when parameter confusion creates real risk (money, identifiers, seat numbers). A Name that merely wraps a String without enforcing anything is noise. Money is always a VO and never a double (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q4) — the canonical example, and one that is universally accepted.","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q1"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2","surface":"track","en":"Aggregates: where should the boundary lie, and how large should an aggregate be?","bodyEn":"An aggregate is a cluster of objects that are loaded, saved, and kept consistent together. The aggregate root is the sole entry point into that cluster. Vaughn Vernon's four rules — know these and you can answer most aggregate questions: Protect invariants within one boundary. Anything that must be true immediately belongs in the same aggregate. Keep aggregates small. Start small; enlarge one only when an invariant requires it. Reference other aggregates by id, not by object. Use booking.flightId, not booking.flight. One transaction = one aggregate. If several aggregates must change, use eventual consistency through domain events (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3). public class Booking { // AGGREGATE ROOT private final BookingId id; private final FlightId flightId; // rule 3: id only private final List<Passenger> passengers; // inside the boundary private BookingStatus status; public void addPassenger (Passenger p) { // every change goes through the ROOT if (status != DRAFT) throw new DomainException( \"booking is finalized; passengers cannot be added\" ); if (passengers. size () >= 9 ) throw new DomainException( \"maximum 9 passengers\" ); passengers. add (p); // the invariant is enforced HERE } public List<Passenger> passengers () { return List. copyOf (passengers); // prevents callers from mutating state by bypassing the root } } The sizing trap A large aggregate locks the whole cluster: two users modifying different parts still collide, and loading one booking may fetch 500 rows. An aggregate that is too small leaves invariants spanning several aggregates, so events and compensation must maintain them. The deciding question is always: which invariant absolutely must hold immediately? \"A seat must not be booked twice\" should not live in a Flight aggregate containing 300 seats. That would be a hot aggregate in which everyone booking the same flight contends. A database constraint / atomic update (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2) is a better fit. Recognizing this demonstrates where the theory's boundary lies. References by id have secondary benefits: aggregates serialize compactly, do not pull in half the database, and later service extraction requires almost no domain-model changes. For concurrency, an aggregate is the natural unit of optimistic locking: keep one version column on the root; when two users edit concurrently, the later writer retries (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3). Provide one repository per aggregate root, not one per table. A PassengerRepository is a sign that Passenger has been pulled outside its boundary. Aggregate boundaries do not correspond one-to-one with tables — one aggregate can span several tables, and the persistence adapter reassembles it (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2). Start with small aggregates and merge them when you see an invariant being violated. The reverse direction — splitting a large aggregate — is much harder.","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do domain events differ from integration events, and where should each be published?","bodyEn":"The distinction is semantic and contractual, not “in-process versus Kafka” or “inside versus outside a transaction.” Domain event Integration event Meaning a business fact meaningful inside a bounded context a fact/notification intentionally exposed to another context Contract owner the domain model/team; may evolve with the model producer and consumers; compatibility and lifecycle are explicit Delivery in-memory, persisted, or both broker, webhook, stream, file, or another inter-context channel Payload stable values needed by local handlers; avoid mutable aggregate references consumer-oriented schema: notification-by-ID or event-carried state are deliberate trade-offs Reliability depends on the local dispatch/storage design outbox/relay, retries, idempotency, ordering, schema and observability contract An aggregate may record immutable domain events without knowing infrastructure. The application layer can handle local reactions and translate selected committed facts into versioned integration events. Not every domain event should leave the bounded context, and an external contract should not leak an internal aggregate object. @Transactional public HoldResult hold (HoldSeatCommand cmd) { Booking booking = bookings. get (cmd.bookingId()); booking. hold (cmd.seat()); // records SeatHeld domain fact bookings. save (booking); for (DomainEvent event : booking. pullEvents ()) { localHandlers. handle (event); // local policy; semantics must be explicit integrationMapper. map (event) . ifPresent (outbox:: append ); // same DB transaction as aggregate } return HoldResult. ok (); } // a separate relay publishes committed outbox rows and retries safely Transaction timing is an implementation choice. A local domain-event handler may run before commit because it contributes to the same invariant, after commit for a best-effort side effect, or asynchronously from durable storage. Document the failure/rollback semantics. @TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT) avoids publishing before rollback but is not durable across a process crash. For reliable external publication, write the integration event/outbox row atomically with the state change, then publish after commit through a relay (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3). Consumers still need idempotency because commit/publish acknowledgement races create duplicates (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6). Payload strategy is not “always IDs only.” Notification events minimize coupling and exposure but make consumers call back and depend on current producer state. Event-carried state transfer improves consumer autonomy and historical meaning but duplicates data, increases privacy/schema obligations, and can grow messages. Include the minimum stable facts required by the declared consumer contract, classify sensitive fields, and version compatibility deliberately. Name facts in past tense (SeatHeld), include event ID, occurrence time, producer/schema version and domain identifiers where needed, and distinguish occurrence time from publish time. Domain events also do not imply event sourcing; that is a separate persistence decision (12-architecture-patterns.domain-driven-design.q4).","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q4","surface":"track","en":"Repository, Factory, and Domain Service: where does each belong, and when is it needed?","bodyEn":"These three building blocks are often put in the wrong place. Repository — a collection of aggregates, not a DAO. // RIGHT: interface in domain/application, expressed in domain language public interface BookingRepository { Optional<Booking> findById (BookingId id); List<Booking> findExpiredHolds (Instant now); void save (Booking booking); // saves the ENTIRE aggregate } // WRONG: a DAO masquerading as a repository interface BookingRepository { void updateStatusById (Long id, int status); // bypasses the domain List<Object[]> findRawJoin (); // leaks persistence concerns } Provide one repository for each aggregate root (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q2), returning domain objects rather than JPA entities (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q2). Factory — when object creation is complex or must protect invariants at construction time. If a constructor is sufficient, do not create a factory. Use one when construction draws from several sources, rules select a subtype, or a named operation better expresses intent (Booking.draftFor(flightId, passengers)). Domain Service — only when behavior does not belong to any entity or VO. Good signals are logic that needs several aggregates, or a pure stateless calculation. // Appropriate: pricing needs a fare table + fare class + promotions — it belongs to none alone public class FareCalculator { public Money fareFor (Flight f, FareClass c, List<Promotion> promos) { ... } } Domain Service is the most abused If behavior uses only one aggregate's data, it belongs on that aggregate. Pushing everything into XxxDomainService recreates an anemic domain (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3) under a different name. Distinguish the three kinds of service: An Application service represents a use case, coordinates work, and opens the transaction: (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q3) A Domain service contains pure business rules and knows nothing about transactions. An Infrastructure service is an adapter that calls an external system. They belong to different layers; do not merge them. Repository methods should be driven by real use-case needs. findAll() over a table with tens of millions of rows is a trap; when paginating, use keyset pagination (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q2). Complex reporting queries should not be forced into an aggregate repository. They belong to a separate read model (12.8 CQRS), which may query a projection directly. The Specification pattern wraps a business predicate in a reusable object (new ExpiredHold(now)), preventing a repository from accumulating dozens of findByXxxAndYyy methods. Factory and Repository complement each other: a factory creates a new aggregate; a repository reconstructs an existing one. Both return aggregates in valid states. At interview depth, saying \"a repository is a domain concept; a JPA repository is an implementation detail\" is enough to distinguish the model from habitual Spring Data usage.","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q4"},{"id":"24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q5","surface":"track","en":"How do you turn a DDD model into an executable boundary and review checklist?","bodyEn":"A DDD model becomes useful when each important term is tied to an owner, invariant, command, persistence boundary and failure response. A context map or EventStorming board alone is not evidence that the boundary works. Worksheet field Example question Executable proof Language and owner Does hold mean the same thing for Booking, Inventory and Payment? Who decides? Glossary, named team owner, scenario review and unresolved-term log Invariant What must be true immediately, and which aggregate protects it? Domain/property/concurrency test plus DB constraint or version check where needed Transaction and authority Which state is authoritative, and what is allowed to be eventually consistent? Transaction boundary, outbox/relay or reconciliation design, freshness SLO Context relationship Who supplies, consumes, translates or conforms to whose model? Versioned contract, ACL mapping tests, compatibility window and ownership Failure and evolution What if a command, event, projection, consumer or model migration is retried or delayed? Idempotency/replay test, recovery runbook, schema migration and observability signals Draw a boundary around a semantic and consistency problem, not around nouns or tables. If two teams use the same word differently, that is evidence for a context boundary; if they share one invariant, it may be evidence for one aggregate or an explicit coordination workflow. Separate domain events from integration events. A local event may evolve with the model; an external event needs a consumer contract, compatibility and privacy policy. Publish durable external facts after the source transaction through the canonical Outbox/Saga owner in Topic 09. Add a negative case to every model: stale command, duplicate event, unknown legacy code, projection lag, delete/redaction request, and a boundary-crossing transaction. A model that only describes the happy path is not ready. Review the model with production signals: conflict rate, aggregate load/lock duration, event lag, reconciliation age, translation failures, change-impact distance and number of coordinated deploys. Keep the model proportional. A low-risk CRUD context may need a small vocabulary and explicit API contract; a payment or booking context needs stronger invariants, audit/recovery and domain-expert sign-off.","contextEn":"Domain-Driven Design — splitting the domain and modelling it","href":"/topics/24-domain-driven-design#question-24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q5"},{"id":"25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q1","surface":"track","en":"Why can one slow downstream service take down every upstream service even when CPU utilization is still low?","bodyEn":"The root cause is that threads/connections are held while waiting for the downstream service. Service A has a Tomcat pool of 200 threads, and every request calls B B normally responds in 20ms → threads are released quickly (~10,000 req/s) B slows to 5s → each thread is held for 5s → A's throughput collapses to ~40 req/s A fails not because of CPU exhaustion, but because of thread starvation — it has no concurrent execution units left. A retry storm compounds the problem: A times out calling B and retries; B is lagging rather than dead → retries double B's load → B fails completely → A retries aggressively → B cannot recover even after a restart. This is called a metastable failure: the system becomes trapped in a bad state and cannot escape even after the original trigger is gone. Two reinforcing loops are at work: retry amplification — retries multiply load at every layer; across three layers, the amplification is x8 load-induced slowdown — higher load makes the system slower (GC, lock contention, context switching) → more timeouts → more retries Recovery requires actively shedding load, not merely waiting for the system to heal itself. Amplification is topology-dependent The x8 illustration assumes a particular three-layer retry topology; it is not a universal per-service multiplier. Real amplification depends on retry placement, concurrency, timeout, partial success, jitter, cache behavior, and whether downstream work continues after caller cancellation. Choose one retry owner per call path, propagate a deadline and retry budget, and measure original calls, attempts, queueing, and downstream load before claiming a safe margin. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The runtime and provider versions the samples target — JDK, Kafka, RabbitMQ, Redis, database, Kubernetes, region — and the real SLOs, payload sizes, concurrency, tenant skew, replication factor and retention behind every numeric example. Which service is allowed to own retries in each call chain, and the absolute deadline budget across the fan-out. Which cache use cases carry a freshness or version contract and which are explicitly disposable. The operational owner and maximum age for DLQ, outbox, reconciliation, job and stale-projection debt. What falsifies \"at-least-once plus idempotency\": a downstream effect with no stable identity or durable dedup. What falsifies modular-monolith-first: a measured team or deployment boundary, an independent scaling or isolation need, or a local invariant that cannot remain local. What falsifies cache-as-derived-projection: a read contract that needs stronger availability or freshness than the origin can give.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should timeouts be configured?","bodyEn":"Timeouts should consume one end-to-end deadline budget, not form a fixed hierarchy in which every outer timeout is greater than the sum of every inner timeout. Sequential calls and retries consume time cumulatively; parallel branches consume the longest branch plus coordination overhead. At each hop, derive a local timeout from the remaining deadline while preserving time to cancel work, release resources, and return a useful response. Boundary Failure it bounds pool acquisition waiting for a connection/thread/permit before any I/O starts DNS/connect/TLS establishing the connection request/write and response/read a peer that accepts a connection but stalls mid-protocol database statement/lock server-side work that can outlive an abandoned HTTP request overall deadline the maximum time the caller is willing to wait for the whole operation edge deadline: T+3000ms parallel fraud and limits checks: each receives remaining budget, capped locally sequential PSP call: starts only if budget includes call + cleanup margin retry: allowed only when eligibility, backoff, and another attempt fit the deadline Propagate an absolute deadline when protocols support it (gRPC does); relative “3 seconds from now” values at every hop accidentally recreate the budget. Cancellation must reach the HTTP client, executor and database driver where possible—timing out the caller while the query continues still consumes the pool. Choose values from latency distributions by operation and failure mode, including cold paths and dependency degradation. Define an acceptable false-timeout rate and test it; “p99.9 plus a margin” is a starting method, not a universal formula. Observe timeout reason, remaining budget, canceled work and late completions without turning raw URLs or IDs into high-cardinality metrics. Retry policy is covered in (25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q5).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q3","surface":"track","en":"Circuit breakers: the three states and key Resilience4j parameters","bodyEn":"A circuit breaker is a local admission policy around one dependency/operation: State Admits Leaves when Parameters that decide it CLOSED every call, recording classified outcomes over its window the failure or slow-call rate reaches its threshold, once the window holds enough calls slidingWindowSize , minimumNumberOfCalls , failureRateThreshold , slowCallRateThreshold , slowCallDurationThreshold OPEN nothing: calls are rejected, and a fallback is optional and must be semantically safe the open-state wait policy permits another evaluation waitDurationInOpenState HALF_OPEN a bounded set of probes the probes' classified results close it or reopen it permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState // Illustrative values only—derive them from this operation's traffic and SLO. CircuitBreakerConfig. custom () . slidingWindowSize (100) . minimumNumberOfCalls (20) . failureRateThreshold (50) // open at or above 50% . slowCallRateThreshold (80) // open at or above 80% . slowCallDurationThreshold (Duration. ofMillis (800)) . waitDurationInOpenState (Duration. ofSeconds (5)) . permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState (10) . build (); Classify which exceptions/statuses count as failures, which are ignored, and what “slow” means for this endpoint. A validation rejection should not normally trip a dependency breaker. minimumNumberOfCalls protects low-volume windows from computing a rate on too little data, but it also delays reaction; choose count- or time-based windows from traffic shape. Breakers are commonly per process/pod, so instances can disagree. Centralizing breaker state adds coordination and another dependency; use it only for a demonstrated requirement. Protect recovery from synchronized probes with bounded HALF_OPEN traffic, jittered/open durations where supported, dependency capacity limits and gradual ramp-up. A fallback is not automatically resilience: stale data, an empty success, or a second dependency can violate the contract or create another cascade. Expose breaker state, rejected/slow/classified calls and fallback outcome at bounded cardinality. The documented Resilience4j defaults are not a substitute for a load/degradation test.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q4","surface":"track","en":"Bulkheads: semaphore vs thread pool, and what changes with virtual threads?","bodyEn":"A bulkhead partitions concurrency or execution capacity by downstream dependency so one slow dependency cannot consume every shared resource. Resilience4j provides two distinct mechanisms: SemaphoreBulkhead FixedThreadPoolBulkhead Runs on the caller's thread a dedicated fixed pool Limits simultaneous calls threads plus a bounded queue, isolating both threads and queued work When full waits up to maxWaitDuration , then rejects rejects once the queue is full A thread-pool bulkhead does not impose a hard execution timeout. Compose a separate TimeLimiter/client deadline and make cancellation effective in the underlying call. With virtual threads, a semaphore is often the simpler way to cap downstream concurrency, but a separate executor can still be useful for ownership, queueing, or legacy isolation—choose from failure behavior, not fashion. A circuit breaker reacts after enough outcomes are recorded; a bulkhead limits concurrency immediately. A rate limiter controls starts over time; a timeout bounds waiting/execution time. They solve different dimensions and should be configured from one end-to-end deadline and overload policy. Little's Law, L = λW, gives a starting estimate only when the system is stable and units align. Use observed arrival rate and service-time distribution, leave headroom for variance, then load-test rejection and recovery. For a downstream with long-tail latency, sizing from the mean alone creates a queue precisely during incidents. Record permitted, rejected, queued, timed-out, cancelled, and completed calls per dependency. A limit is useful only if rejection maps to a deliberate response such as fallback, 429/503, or bounded retry—not an unobserved exception loop.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q4"},{"id":"25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q5","surface":"track","en":"Safe retries: jitter and retry budgets","bodyEn":"A safe retry policy classifies the operation, failure, deadline, and shared load before choosing attempts. IntervalFunction backoff = IntervalFunction. ofExponentialRandomBackoff ( Duration. ofMillis ( 100 ), 2.0 , 0.5 ); // example values; derive from the service contract Retry only failures explicitly classified as transient and only when repeating the operation is safe. An idempotency key/unique business key is required for retrying a money-creating POST. Give every attempt a timeout inside one absolute end-to-end deadline. Stop when remaining time cannot support another useful attempt. Use exponential backoff and usually jitter under distributed load so clients do not synchronize; also honor a valid Retry-After. Bound attempts and total retry traffic. A retry budget can cap retries relative to successful/original traffic and close when the dependency is unhealthy. Do not treat all 4xx or 5xx alike: policy may retry 408, 429, selected conflicts, or some 5xx, while many validation/authentication failures are permanent. Define it per API. There is no universally correct decorator order. If the circuit breaker wraps the whole retry, it records one final outcome per logical operation; if retry wraps the breaker, the breaker observes each failed attempt and may open sooner. Per-attempt versus whole-operation timeout placement changes cancellation and metrics similarly. Write the desired semantics first—what counts as a breaker failure, which layer owns retries, how many attempts can occur across the call chain, and whether a fallback is safe—then compose and test the actual library decorators. Avoid retries at multiple layers unless a propagated budget prevents multiplicative amplification. Record original calls, attempts, successes-after-retry, exhausted/rejected retries, added latency, and downstream load. If retries rarely recover but materially amplify incidents, remove or narrow them.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q5"},{"id":"25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q6","surface":"track","en":"Load shedding — the last line of defense","bodyEn":"Load shedding rejects lower-value or excess work before it consumes the scarce resource, preserving useful throughput and bounded latency for admitted work. Place admission at the earliest boundary that knows both cost and priority: gateway, per-endpoint limiter, worker queue, or dependency bulkhead. Use direct saturation signals such as in-flight work, queue age/depth, available permits/connections and deadline-miss probability. CPU and p99 are valuable confirmation but may react after queues have already grown. Define overload classes and fairness: reserve capacity for critical operations, cap noisy tenants, and ensure a large expensive request does not count like a cheap lookup merely because both are “one request.” Rejection contract Use 429 for a client/quota policy and 503 for temporary service capacity when those meanings fit the API. Return Retry-After only with a retry policy the server can support; clients still need jitter, a deadline and a retry budget. For non-idempotent work, never invite an automatic retry without an idempotency contract. Fixed concurrency/queue limits are simple and auditable. Adaptive limiters can adjust from latency or gradient signals as workload cost changes, but they are control loops—not a way to eliminate maximum bounds, minimum capacity, priority rules or tuning. Test stability, cold start, low traffic, dependency slowdown and correlated bursts; expose the chosen limit and rejection reason. Shedding after work enters an unbounded queue is too late. Couple admission with bounded queues, cancellation and backpressure so rejected/expired work releases resources. Compare this incident card with the canonical overload workflow in (10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q2).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q6"},{"id":"25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q1","surface":"track","en":"Connection-pool sizing: why is a larger pool not always better?","bodyEn":"A database connection pool is an admission-control boundary as well as a reuse mechanism. Too many active queries can increase CPU scheduling, lock/latch contention, memory, I/O queueing, and cache churn, so adding connections can reduce throughput and worsen tail latency. Do not use (cores × 2) + spindles or “10–20 connections” as a universal answer. Start from: the database's measured concurrent-query capacity for this workload and its CPU/I/O/lock profile; an aggregate connection budget after reserving administration, migrations, replicas/HA, background jobs, and other applications; per-instance traffic, query service-time distribution, transaction hold time, autoscaling maximum, and acceptable pool wait; separate budgets for interactive and batch traffic when one can starve the other. Load-test increasing concurrency while plotting throughput, p95/p99 query latency, DB CPU/I/O/saturation, locks, pool active/pending/timeouts, and application SLO. Select the smallest pool that meets peak throughput with recovery headroom; validate failure and autoscaling scenarios, not only steady state. Little's Law can estimate needed in-flight work from arrival rate and observed hold time in a stable system, but transactions, burstiness, and long tails require headroom and measurement. A pool queue absorbs only a bounded burst; it must not become an unbounded latency buffer. Connections also consume server and client memory/state, but the cost is engine/version/configuration-specific. Measure the selected PostgreSQL/MySQL/cloud product instead of quoting a fixed megabytes-per-connection value.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q2","surface":"track","en":"Aggregate connections across instances exceed max_connections","bodyEn":"The budget is fleet-wide: max replicas × pool size, plus jobs, migrations, admin tools, and other services, must fit below the database limit with failover/recovery reserve. Autoscaling can therefore exhaust connections even while each pod's pool looks healthy. First control demand: cap replica count and per-pod pools coherently, shorten transactions, separate batch traffic, shed excess work, and raise database capacity only after measuring. A connection proxy/pooler such as RDS Proxy or PgBouncer is one option, not an automatic requirement; it helps when many client connections are mostly idle or churn, but it cannot create database query capacity. Proxy semantics matter. Session pooling preserves a backend connection for a client session and multiplexes less. Transaction pooling reassigns a backend after each transaction, so arbitrary session state—temporary tables, session-level SET, some advisory-lock/listen behavior, and driver assumptions—may not survive. “Prepared statements must always be disabled” is outdated and too broad. PgBouncer version/configuration and protocol matter: modern releases can track protocol-level named prepared statements when configured, while SQL-level PREPARE/DEALLOCATE, session state, DDL changes, and particular drivers need explicit compatibility tests. RDS Proxy can pin sessions for operations that prevent multiplexing, reducing its benefit. Test transactions, prepared statements, failover, credential rotation, cancellation, idle/maximum lifetime, and observability through the exact proxy mode before rollout. Monitor client connections, backend connections, pinning/multiplex ratio, wait, transaction latency, and rejected connections.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q3","surface":"track","en":"HikariCP: critical parameters","bodyEn":"maximumPoolSize: 20 example only; must fit the fleet-wide DB budget connectionTimeout: 3000 maximum wait to borrow from the pool validationTimeout: 1000 must be less than connectionTimeout maxLifetime: 1740000 maximum connection age; below infrastructure max-age limit keepaliveTime: 120000 ping an idle connection; must be less than maxLifetime leakDetectionThreshold: 60000 logs a possible long checkout; zero disables Parameter What it bounds Set it from maximumPoolSize total connections, idle and in use the fleet-wide database budget, divided across every replica connectionTimeout the wait to borrow from the pool — not JDBC connect, socket, statement or transaction time a deliberate rejection budget; those other deadlines are set in their own layers maxLifetime a connection's age; it is retired after it returns to the pool a little below a known database/proxy/network maximum lifetime; Hikari adds the jitter keepaliveTime infrastructure idle expiry, by testing an idle connection the shortest idle timeout on the path; must stay below maxLifetime leakDetectionThreshold how long one checkout may last before Hikari logs it above the longest legitimate transaction, which would trigger it too Do not confuse idle timeout with maximum age: keepaliveTime answers the first, maxLifetime the second. A leak-detection log signals a possible leak, not proof that code never returns the connection. Hikari recommends considering a fixed-size pool (minimumIdle left unset so it follows the maximum) for responsiveness, but that is not a universal mandate. An elastic pool can reduce idle connections at the cost of creating them during demand; choose from database budget, connection-establishment cost, workload burst, and platform behavior. idleTimeout applies only when minimumIdle is below maximumPoolSize. Keepalive occurs only on idle connections, while an in-use connection is never retired at maxLifetime until returned. This distinction prevents the common mistake of setting maxLifetime from an infrastructure idle timeout while omitting keepalive. Monitor active, idle, pending, acquire duration/timeouts, usage duration, creation failures, and DB-side sessions. A short connectionTimeout is useful only when rejection/degradation is deliberate and clients do not immediately retry into another storm.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q4","surface":"track","en":"Thread pools: the queueCapacity gotcha","bodyEn":"The actual ThreadPoolExecutor admission order is core → queue → max → reject, not the intuitive core → max → queue. running < core? → create a new thread else queue not full? → PUT IN QUEUE (do not create a thread!) else thread < max? → create a new thread else → REJECT The trap: with a large queueCapacity—and the default LinkedBlockingQueue capacity is Integer.MAX_VALUE—the queue never fills → the pool never grows beyond corePoolSize → maxPoolSize becomes meaningless. → use a bounded queue with CallerRunsPolicy. It provides natural backpressure: the task runs on the calling thread, which automatically slows task submission. To \"scale threads first and queue later,\" use SynchronousQueue or a custom queue that overrides offer(), as Tomcat does with TaskQueue. CallerRunsPolicy has a downside on a web server: it consumes a Tomcat request thread → fewer threads remain to accept new requests → backpressure propagates to the client. That can be intentional, but a latency-critical path should consider failing fast with 503 instead.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q4"},{"id":"25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q5","surface":"track","en":"Virtual threads remove the I/O-bound bottleneck—and introduce new traps","bodyEn":"Virtual threads remove the need to use a small platform-thread pool merely because blocked OS threads are expensive. They do not remove any downstream limit. A supported blocking operation parks and unmounts the virtual thread, allowing its carrier to run another task. On JDK 21–23, blocking under a synchronized monitor could pin the carrier. JDK 24 removed monitor-related pinning; native/foreign calls and class-initialization edge cases can still hold it. CPU-bound work remains limited by cores. Cheap threads remove an accidental admission limit: thousands of requests can now wait on a 20-connection pool or flood a partner unless concurrency is bounded explicitly. Do not quote a universal “6× improvement” or a fixed stack cost. Measure throughput, RSS, allocation, carrier utilization, pending connections, queue depth, and p99 under the same container limits and workload. Use one virtual thread per task, then place a semaphore or the resource's own pool at each scarce dependency. Give waiting an absolute deadline and shed work before the queue consumes the latency budget. On JDK 21–23, the old pinned-thread diagnostic and JFR event can help; -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads was removed in JDK 24, so use current JFR/thread-dump evidence instead. The runtime details are covered in (01-java-core-jvm.concurrency.q1) and the JDK 25 coding API in (23-java-concurrency-coding.async-the-modern-toolkit.q4).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q5"},{"id":"25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q1","surface":"track","en":"The dual-write problem — why is this code subtly wrong?","bodyEn":"@Transactional public void placeOrder(Order order) { orderRepository.save(order); // write to the DB transaction messageBroker.publish(orderEvent); // publish to the broker } The database and broker are not part of the same transaction. @Transactional covers only the database. if the database commits and the process crashes before publish → the event is lost if publish succeeds but the database transaction then rolls back → downstream consumers process an order that does not exist Do not solve this with 2PC/XA: it is slow and fragile, and holds blocking locks across systems. 2PC is generally avoided because the prepare phase holds locks on every participant until commit. If the coordinator dies mid-protocol, participants can remain \"in-doubt\" and retain locks indefinitely → destroying throughput and introducing a single point of failure. Reversing the order and publishing first is still wrong: the problem is not ordering, but the lack of atomicity across two media. The reliable approach is to place both writes in one transactional medium—the database—and relay from there. That is the Outbox pattern.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q2","surface":"track","en":"Transactional Outbox: polling vs CDC","bodyEn":"Write the event to the same database in the same transaction as the business data, then use a separate relay to publish it to the broker. @Transactional void placeOrder(Order order) { orderRepository.save(order); outboxRepository.save(new OutboxEvent(\"OrderCreated\", order.getId(), toJson(order), PENDING)); // commit together or roll back together — atomic because both writes use the same DB } Polling relay CDC (Debezium) Finds events by SELECT ... WHERE status='PENDING' FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED reading the transaction log (binlog/WAL) Latency up to one poll interval low: changes stream as they commit Database load a repeated query plus a status update per event no polling query; the log is read instead Ordering parallel relays can publish out of order unless work is split by key commit order of the log You operate the relay and an index on status Debezium, Kafka Connect and log retention on the database Choose it when it is simple and often sufficient volume or latency justifies more infrastructure to operate FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED lets N relays scan the same table while skipping rows another relay has locked → horizontal scaling without double-publishing through concurrent selection. The Outbox pattern guarantees at-least-once, not exactly-once delivery. A crash before marking an event SENT causes it to be sent again, so the consumer must be idempotent (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q3","surface":"track","en":"Sagas: orchestration vs choreography and compensation traps","bodyEn":"A saga is a sequence of local transactions; when a step fails, completed work is reversed with a compensating transaction. Choreography is event-driven and loosely coupled, but it can turn into a \"pinball\" flow that is difficult to debug. Beyond four or five steps, prefer orchestration with an explicit state machine such as Temporal or Camunda. Compensation traps in fintech: Compensation ≠ rollback: after debiting money and sending an SMS, you cannot unsend the SMS → design a semantic refund by crediting the amount back, not by \"un-debiting\" it Pivot transaction: after crossing the pivot point, such as disbursing funds externally, compensation is impossible → after the pivot, only retry forward Compensation can also fail → it must be retried and idempotent A semantic lock reduces user-visible inconsistency between a completed step and unfinished compensation. Keep an explicit intermediate state such as PENDING_CONFIRM instead of immediately changing the effective balance, and finalize it only when the entire saga succeeds.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q4","surface":"track","en":"Where does eventual consistency hurt?","bodyEn":"Read-after-write is not guaranteed: a user deposits money and refreshes immediately, but the balance has not updated because the replica or projection has not caught up. Mitigations include: read-your-writes consistency by routing reads to the primary for N seconds an optimistic UI that shows \"processing\" distinguishing data that must be strongly consistent, such as balances and limits, from data that can be eventually consistent, such as history and statistics Consistency is a spectrum that includes read-your-writes, monotonic reads—never observing data move backward to an older value—and causal consistency. Many fintech UX bugs violate monotonic reads: the user sees a new balance, refreshes, and sees the old value after being routed to a different replica. Fix this with a version token: after a write, return an LSN/version to the client; the next read sends that token, and the server routes it to a replica that has caught up to at least that version.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q4"},{"id":"25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q5","surface":"track","en":"What does “exactly once” guarantee, and where does that guarantee end?","bodyEn":"“Exactly once” is meaningful only after defining the scope, observer, failure model, and side effects. It is wrong both to promise universal exactly-once delivery and to dismiss every exactly-once guarantee as a myth. Kafka can provide scope-bound exactly-once processing: idempotent producers and transactions atomically commit consumed offsets with produced records, and consumers using read_committed avoid aborted writes. Kafka Streams' exactly-once processing mode applies within Kafka-managed input/state/output boundaries. It does not automatically include a PostgreSQL commit, email, payment-provider call, or arbitrary REST effect. For an effect outside that transaction boundary, crashes around “effect succeeded / acknowledgement failed” create ambiguity. Use a local transaction with inbox/outbox, stable operation/event IDs, unique constraints, idempotent receiver semantics, and reconciliation so repeated delivery produces one business outcome. Separate terms: delivery: how often transport presents a record; processing attempt: how often handler code executes; business effect: what a declared observer can see in durable state. At-least-once delivery can produce effectively-once business effects when the effect is idempotent and deduplication is atomic with it. A transactional system can give exactly-once visibility within its own boundary even though networks still retry messages. Ask vendors which resources share the transaction, how failover/fencing works, what consumers must configure, and what happens when an external effect is introduced. The practical design question is: “If this operation runs twice or its outcome is unknown, how do we detect, converge, and reconcile?”","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q5"},{"id":"25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q1","surface":"track","en":"Backpressure and queue buildup: RabbitMQ vs Kafka","bodyEn":"When producers outpace consumers, the queue grows → end-to-end latency rises and broker memory pressure increases. RabbitMQ Kafka Where the backlog sits the queue: quorum queues never keep message bodies in memory; classic queues hold at most 2,048 a disk-backed log, so accumulating messages alone does not run it out of memory Signal to watch queue depth and the age of the oldest message consumer lag : rising lag means increasingly stale data Consumer-side brake prefetch (QoS): unlimited lets one consumer take all work while others starve max.poll.records , and pausing partitions the consumer cannot keep up with Starting value roughly 10–50 for lightweight tasks, 1 for heavy tasks sized from processing time against max.poll.interval.ms RabbitMQ 3.12 retired the old \"lazy\" queue mode: classic queues now page to disk on their own, so \"make it lazy\" is no longer a fix for broker memory pressure. Optimal prefetch is approximately throughput × round-trip time, an inverse application of Little's Law. Too low and the consumer waits for acknowledgments; too high and work becomes imbalanced, with many unacknowledged messages stranded when a consumer dies. For Kafka, alert on the derivative of lag—whether it is growing or shrinking—not only its absolute value. Partition count is the consumer group's scaling ceiling: a group cannot have more active consumers than partitions. Name the boundary before saying reliable A producer confirm means broker acceptance under that broker contract; it does not mean retention through expiry, consumer acknowledgement/offset commit, or business-effect completion. Define all four separately and expose backlog age/lag and business completion state. A queue or log can provide durable delivery within its scope, but it cannot make an external side effect end-to-end exactly once.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q2","surface":"track","en":"Message ordering under horizontal scale","bodyEn":"Ordering is a scoped guarantee. Kafka orders records in one partition; RabbitMQ preserves queue delivery order under its documented conditions, but multiple consumers, redelivery and application concurrency can change the order in which side effects complete. Define the business scope first—usually one account/order/aggregate—not “all events globally.” Choose a stable key and keep every event that shares an ordering invariant on the same partition/consumer lane. A hot key limits parallelism, and changing partition count or key logic needs a migration plan. With Kafka, producer idempotence preserves order for supported configurations; retries with idempotence disabled and more than one in-flight request can reorder batches. Do not copy the old blanket rule “always set max in-flight to 1”—verify the client/version contract. A rebalance or ack/offset failure can reprocess records. Consumer idempotency handles duplicates but does not by itself reject a late older event; use an aggregate version/sequence and an atomic conditional update when stale events would corrupt state. Parallel handlers may finish out of order even when delivery was ordered. Commit/ack only after the intended side effect and define how poison messages or slow keys affect progress. RabbitMQ single-active-consumer can serialize consumption with failover; consistent-hash routing can shard by key, but redelivery still requires idempotency. Kafka partition order is broker append order, not necessarily event-time order from distributed producers. If producers can race, allocate the sequence at the state owner or make the consumer compare versions. Global total order usually destroys throughput and still does not make external side effects atomic. State the narrow invariant and test producer retry, rebalance, duplicate, delayed event, key migration and DLQ replay. Broker-specific details live in (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q4). Ordering is a producer key plus a consumer-side aggregate sequence: // every event for one order resolves to the same partition producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(\"order-events\", orderId, eventJson)); -- consumer transaction: accept exactly the next event for this aggregate UPDATE order_projection SET status=:status, last_seq=:seq WHERE order_id=:orderId AND last_seq=:seq-1; -- update count 1: apply side effects and commit offset with the transaction -- update count 0 + seq <= last_seq: duplicate/replay, acknowledge safely -- update count 0 + seq > last_seq+1: gap, pause/retry/reconcile; do not skip it Keep producer idempotence enabled with acks=all, but do not confuse broker retry ordering with end-to-end processing. Benefit: unrelated aggregates scale across partitions while each order stays serial. Cost: a hot key is bounded by one partition, re-keying can split history, and DLQ replay still needs the sequence guard.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q3","surface":"track","en":"Poison messages and DLQs","bodyEn":"A poison message repeatedly fails for a reason retries cannot heal: unreadable bytes/schema, invalid data, an unsupported version, or a deterministic handler bug. A transient dependency timeout is different; classify failures before choosing retry or quarantine. Use bounded attempts, exponential backoff with jitter and an overall age/deadline. Delayed retry topics/queues or broker features keep the main consumer lane from sleeping, but they change ordering and increase operational state. After the policy is exhausted, quarantine the original payload plus source topic/queue, partition/offset or message ID, headers/schema version, first/last failure time, attempt count, producer identity and a sanitized error classification. A DLQ is a workflow, not storage Alert on rate and oldest age; assign ownership and retention. Provide an audited replay tool that can inspect, patch/transform when authorized, choose a target version, throttle traffic and record who replayed what. Replay must pass through an idempotent consumer and may need the original key/order lane. For batch handlers, isolate or bisect the failing record when the broker/framework supports partial acknowledgement; blindly retrying a large batch reprocesses good work and can block a partition. Never deserialize untrusted DLQ content in an operator UI without the same security controls as the main path. Test malformed bytes, incompatible schema, permanent domain rejection, dependency outage, handler bug, batch partial failure and replay after the original state has changed. The full broker guidance is (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q5), with deduplication in (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q4","surface":"track","en":"RabbitMQ Publisher Confirms: three modes and ConcurrentSkipListMap","bodyEn":"Publisher confirms acknowledge publisher-to-broker acceptance according to the queue type and durability configuration. Three common application patterns are synchronous per message, synchronous batches, and asynchronous callbacks; they are patterns with different throughput/failure bookkeeping, not three different broker guarantees. channel. confirmSelect (); ConcurrentNavigableMap<Long, Message> outstanding = new ConcurrentSkipListMap<>(); channel. addConfirmListener ( (seqNo, multiple) -> { if (multiple) outstanding. headMap (seqNo, true ). clear (); else outstanding. remove (seqNo); }, (seqNo, multiple) -> retryQueue. addAll ( removeNacked (outstanding, seqNo, multiple))); // register before publish; remove/resolve the entry if basicPublish itself throws long seq = channel. getNextPublishSeqNo (); outstanding. put (seq, message); channel. basicPublish (exchange, key, properties, body); multiple=true acknowledges or rejects the prefix through seqNo. A skip-list map makes ordered concurrent prefix lookup practical, but clearing k entries is not O(log n): locating the range is logarithmic while visiting/removing the acknowledged entries costs at least O(k) work (and implementation overhead per removal). A confirm is not an end-to-end delivery or processing acknowledgement. Durability depends on queue type, replication policy, durable declarations, persistent-message properties, and broker failure semantics; consumer acknowledgements remain separate. Test node loss, channel close, returned unroutable messages, and connection recovery. Asynchronous mode must handle single and cumulative ack/nack, channel shutdown with unresolved entries, bounded outstanding memory, mandatory-return routing failures, retry backoff, and duplicate publication. Keep callback work small; hand retries to a controlled executor/queue and do not assume channel operations are freely thread-safe. Confirms also do not solve the database→broker dual write. Use a transactional outbox for that boundary (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7), then treat confirm state as relay bookkeeping.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q4"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q1","surface":"track","en":"Cache stampede (thundering herd)","bodyEn":"A hot key expires → thousands of requests miss simultaneously → all hit the database together → a lethal load spike. Single-flight / mutex: allow only one request to rebuild the entry using a setnx lock with a TTL; all other requests wait and read again XFetch (probabilistic early expiration): each request has a small probability of rebuilding before expiration → there is no single instant when every request misses Logical expiration: never let the key physically expire; after its logical expiry, one request rebuilds it in the background while all others receive the stale value → nobody waits, at the cost of accepting staleness Logical expiration suits data that tolerates staleness, such as a displayed exchange rate; single-flight suits data that must be fresh but can tolerate waiting. Fintech balances should not use simple cache-aside. A lock needs a TTL so the cache does not deadlock if the lock holder dies; also beware of the lock itself becoming a hot key → add a local L1 cache.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q2","surface":"track","en":"Hot keys","bodyEn":"One extremely hot key concentrates traffic on a single Redis node, causing a bottleneck while the rest of the cluster is idle because sharding assigns that key to one slot. L1 (Caffeine) + L2 (Redis): a short L1 TTL keeps most reads away from Redis Synthetic key replication: write key 1..key N and choose a replica randomly on reads → spread load across multiple slots, at the cost of more complex invalidation L1+L2+DB is a standard production hierarchy: The L1 hit ratio is workload-specific; measure it by key class and include invalidation traffic, memory pressure, cold starts, and stale-read cost before sizing Redis. The cost is distributed invalidation. When data changes, the L1 entry must be removed from every instance using Redis pub/sub, or the system must accept a short TTL. Detect hot keys with Redis --hotkeys and client-side sampling. Because hot keys can appear suddenly during events such as flash sales, mature systems need automatic detection and dynamic promotion into L1.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q3","surface":"track","en":"Cache penetration","bodyEn":"Cache penetration is repeated lookup of keys that do not exist or are not visible to the caller, so every request bypasses the positive cache and reaches the source of truth. It may come from normal misses, stale links, enumeration attacks, or a client bug. Validate key shape and authorization before expensive lookup; apply per-principal/tenant/IP risk-based rate limits rather than trusting cache defenses alone. Negative-cache “not found” with a bounded, jittered TTL and a key that includes every authorization/tenant dimension. Invalidate or version it when creation can make the key valid. Bound cache size so attacker-generated junk cannot evict useful data. A Bloom filter can reject definite non-members with compact memory. Standard Bloom filters allow false positives but not false negatives only while the filter correctly represents all inserted valid keys; stale construction or missed updates can violate the application's assumption. Size it from expected cardinality and target false-positive rate. For high-churn/deletion workloads, rebuilding, partitioning by generation, a counting filter, or a Cuckoo filter may fit better; each adds update and consistency complexity. A filter also reveals nothing about authorization and should not become a side channel for whether another tenant's object exists. Observe negative-cache insert/hit/eviction, filter saturation/false-positive estimates, source lookup rate and top bounded rejection classes. Test create-after-negative-cache, tenant crossover, random-key flood, filter lag and source outage. The broader cache strategy is (10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q4","surface":"track","en":"Cache avalanches and invalidation","bodyEn":"Avalanche: many keys with the same TTL expire together → a load spike. Fix it with TTL jitter such as base + random(0, base0.2) Invalidation: after writing the database, delete the key rather than updating it → avoid a race in which an older value overwrites the new cached value A rare delete race remains: a read misses → loads an old value → a concurrent write updates the database and deletes the key → the read writes the old value back into the cache. Mitigate it with delayed double-delete—delete, write the database, wait a few hundred milliseconds, then delete again—or with a short TTL. The golden rule for fintech is not to cache balances or limits with plain cache-aside. If caching is unavoidable, use versioning or push updates through CDC.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q4"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q5","surface":"track","en":"The read-path patterns: cache-aside, read-through, refresh-ahead","bodyEn":"All six cache patterns answer one question: who writes the entry, and when. On the read path that narrows to who handles a miss — the application, the cache itself, or a background worker. 1 · Cache-aside (lazy loading) — the application asks the cache; on a miss it reads the database and writes the value back itself. Why it is the default: the cache is optional. Lose it and every read still answers, only slower, and nothing in the write path changes. What it costs: a miss is three hops instead of one, the first read of a key is always slow, and the get/set logic ends up copied to every call site. Fits reads that dominate writes and data that may be stale for the length of the TTL. 2 · Read-through — the application only ever talks to the cache; a loader inside the cache reads the database on a miss and stores the result. What you gain: one place owns loading, so no call site can forget the set or invent its own key format. What it costs: you need a cache that supports loaders, lookups are by key only — no joins, no dynamic filters — and the cache is now on the critical path of every read. 3 · Refresh-ahead — a worker reloads a key before its TTL runs out, so no reader ever pays for the reload. What you gain: miss latency disappears for the keys it covers, and a hot key stops being a stampede trigger (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q1). What it costs: it is a guess. Refreshing a key nobody reads spends a database query on nothing, so it pays only where the hot set is known — a campaign, a flash sale, a home page. A hit costs the same in all three They differ in who owns the miss, not in speed. What changes is where the loading code lives and what the system does when the cache is gone. Spring's @Cacheable looks read-through but is cache-aside: the proxy does the get and set around your method, so a cache outage surfaces inside your code, and sync = true is its single-flight switch (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q6). Caffeine's LoadingCache is real read-through in-process, and it collapses concurrent misses on one key into a single load while the rest wait — the stampede fix arrives with the pattern (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q1). refreshAfterWrite is refresh-ahead in one line: the first read after the refresh point starts a reload in the background and still returns the old value. read-through against a remote cache makes that cache a hard dependency. Decide fail-open or fail-closed per use, and decide it together with where L1 and L2 live (10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q6).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q5"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q6","surface":"track","en":"The write-path patterns: write-through, write-behind, write-around","bodyEn":"On a write the question is whether the cache sits on the path at all, and whether the database write happens inside the request. 4 · Write-through — the write goes to the cache and the database in one synchronous flow, and the client waits for both. What you gain: a read straight after a write never sees the old value, and there is no separate invalidation step to forget. What it costs: every write pays both latencies, and the cache fills up with values nobody reads. 5 · Write-behind (write-back) — the cache acknowledges the write immediately and a worker flushes to the database later, in batches. What you gain: the highest write throughput of the six; a thousand increments of one counter collapse into one row update. What it costs: a real loss window. This is the only pattern of the six where an acknowledged write can disappear. 6 · Write-around — the write goes straight to the database and the key is deleted from the cache; the next read reloads it. What you gain: write-once-read-never data never occupies cache memory, which keeps the hot set resident. What it costs: the read right after a write is guaranteed to miss, and a forgotten delete leaves a stale entry until the TTL expires. What people actually run: Ordinary read-heavy service: cache-aside + write-around — read lazily, write the database, delete the key. Cheapest to operate, and correct as long as the delete happens after the commit (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q4). Large read volume over a small hot set, such as a flash sale or a home page: add refresh-ahead or logical expiry so the hot key never expires under load. Counters, view counts, metrics, logs: write-behind with a bounded buffer and a loss window someone has agreed to. Write-behind is the one pattern that can lose an acknowledged write The client is told 200 before the row exists, so the loss window is the flush interval plus the buffer depth. Keep it away from balances, orders and ledger entries; use it where a lost increment is a rounding error. write-through is not a transaction: two stores, two writes. Decide what the code does when the second one fails — this is the dual-write problem in another costume (25-microservice.03-distributed-data-consistency.q1). on invalidation, delete the key instead of writing the new value into it; two concurrent writers can otherwise leave the older value sitting in the cache (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q4). write-behind's durability is the buffer's durability. An in-process buffer loses everything on a crash, Redis AOF with everysec can still lose about a second of writes, and the durable version is a log the worker consumes. write-around plus a short TTL is the honest version of \"the delete can fail\": the delete is best-effort and the TTL is the backstop.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q6"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q7","surface":"track","en":"Two sizing mistakes that turn the cache into the incident","bodyEn":"A cache does not make a system faster; it moves the load elsewhere and adds a second copy of the truth. Two decisions settle whether that trade pays: how long an entry lives, and which entries are let in at all. Mistake 1 — a short TTL on a hot key. TTL is a staleness budget, not a performance knob. A key read 5,000 times a second with a 30-second TTL schedules a stampede every 30 seconds: at expiry every in-flight request misses together and the database takes the full read rate. Changing the number is not the fix. Five seconds makes it worse and five minutes only moves it; what has to change is that expiry stops being a cliff — single-flight, logical expiry, or refresh-ahead (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q1). Give every TTL jitter so keys loaded together do not expire together (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q4). Mistake 2 — writing every key you happen to read into the cache. An entry earns its memory by being read again before it changes or expires. Cache everything and the working set outgrows the memory: eviction starts, entries that were about to be reused get dropped, and the hit rate collapses while memory reads 100%. Decide per key class, not per endpoint. A product page is read thousands of times; an audit record is read once and belongs in the database only — which is exactly what write-around is for. Size it from the real thing: MEMORY USAGE <key> on a representative key, times the number you intend to keep resident, then leave headroom for fragmentation and replication buffers. maxmemory-policy is a product decision, not a default. noeviction turns a full cache into write errors, allkeys-lru keeps it behaving as a cache, and allkeys-lfu protects a small hot set from a scan. Admission and the real engine algorithms are compared in (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q10). Read the hit rate per key class, never globally. One class at 99% and one at 20% average out to a number that looks healthy, while the second class is the one arriving at the database. Size it with arithmetic, not with a round number. The working set is what you intend to keep resident — not the key space, and not yesterday's peak: Input Where the number comes from Worked example Resident keys Keys read more than once inside one TTL window. Measure it, do not assume it. 2,000,000 product keys Bytes per entry MEMORY USAGE key on a representative key — it counts the key, the value and Redis' own overhead. 1,400 B Payload subtotal keys × bytes 2.8 GB Fragmentation The allocator hands back size classes, not exact bytes. Read mem_fragmentation_ratio ; 1.2–1.5 is normal. × 1.3 → 3.6 GB Replication buffer A replica that falls behind buffers on the primary. It is not free and it is not small under load. + 0.5 GB Headroom Room to rebalance and to survive a burst without evicting. Below ~25% free, eviction starts making the decision for you. → ~5.5 GB of maxmemory The same table read backwards is the more useful direction: given the memory you already have, it tells you how many keys you may admit — which is the admission decision, made with a number instead of a hope. The four commands that answer \"is this cache healthy?\": 1. is it evicting, and is that eviction or expiry? redis-cli INFO stats grep -E 'evicted_keys expired_keys keyspace_hits keyspace_misses' evicted_keys climbing = the working set no longer fits. expired_keys is normal. 2. how much of maxmemory is actually payload? redis-cli INFO memory grep -E 'used_memory_human maxmemory_human mem_fragmentation_ratio' ratio > 1.5 means the allocator is holding memory you cannot use 3. what does one entry really cost, including overhead? redis-cli MEMORY USAGE product:v7:1042 4. which keys are hot enough to deserve an L1 in front of them? redis-cli --hotkeys needs maxmemory-policy allkeys-lfu never KEYS on a production instance — it is O(n) and single-threaded. SCAN with a COUNT, or read the keyspace from INFO. Hit rate is a per-class metric or it is nothing. keyspace_hits is instance-wide, so tag the metric at the call site instead: public Product byId ( long id) { Product hit = redis. get (key(id)); meter. counter ( \"cache.lookup\" , \"class\" , \"product\" , // the key class, not the endpoint \"result\" , hit != null ? \"hit\" : \"miss\" ) . increment (); return hit != null ? hit : loadAndCache (id); } eviction is not expiry: an entry can vanish long before its TTL, so no correctness rule may assume it is still there. Idempotency keys kept only in a cache are the classic version of this bug (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1). by tier, for a read-heavy service: at T1–T2 (10k–100k req/day, ~2.5–25 rps peak) a cache is not needed — the missing index is usually the real fix; at T3 (1M req/day, ~250 rps peak) it is worth it. at T4 (100M req/day, ~25,000 rps peak) the cache is mandatory, and the hot set needs its own scaling story: an L1 in front of exactly those keys, not a larger Redis (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q2). measure the cost of a miss, not the hit rate alone: 95% hits with a two-second miss is worse at p99 than 80% hits with a 40 ms miss. a cold start after a deploy or a failover is the same event as a mass expiry. Warm the keys you know are hot, or admit misses gradually with a bounded rebuild concurrency (10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q7"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q8","surface":"track","en":"Redis beyond the cache: which structure answers which problem","bodyEn":"Redis is a server of data structures with expiry, and its data path executes one command at a time — which is why every single command is already atomic. That one sentence is the reason it ends up doing a dozen jobs; the real question is which of them it is allowed to own. Job Structure and commands What it costs you Session / token store HSET + HEXPIRE (Redis 7.4+), or a JSON string + EXPIRE eviction is not expiry — on a shared cache instance a live session can be evicted give it its own instance with noeviction Distributed lock SET key token NX PX ttl , released by a Lua compare-and-delete the TTL can expire mid-work, so the resource needs a fencing token (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q6) Rate limit / quota INCR + EXPIRE , a token bucket in Lua, or a sliding window with ZADD + ZREMRANGEBYSCORE refill and consumption must be one atomic step, and the clock source has to be decided (10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q4) Idempotency / dedup SET key NX tells the caller whether it was first; PEXPIRE bounds the record an evicted key means a duplicate is accepted the database constraint stays the real guarantee (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1) Work queue List: LPUSH + BLMOVE into a processing list (6.2+) Stream: XADD · XREADGROUP · XACK · XAUTOCLAIM List: no ack — a consumer that dies mid-job loses it unless the processing list is used Stream: the pending list needs reclaiming, and trimming is your job Fan-out / invalidation PUBLISH + SUBSCRIBE at-most-once with no storage: a subscriber that is disconnected never sees it right for cache invalidation, wrong for work Leaderboard / ranking sorted set: ZADD · ZREVRANGE · ZRANK , O(log N) memory grows with members, ties need a composite score, and the whole board is one key (11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q6) Delayed / scheduled jobs sorted set scored by due time, then ZRANGEBYSCORE and ZREM inside one script the pop must be atomic or two workers run the same job, and the poll interval is your latency floor Counters and metrics INCRBY / HINCRBY on a per-minute key that carries a TTL this is write-behind by another name: whatever has not been flushed dies with the instance (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q6) Unique-visitor counts HyperLogLog: PFADD · PFCOUNT · PFMERGE , about 12 KB per key approximate — 0.81% standard error — and the members cannot be listed back Daily-active flags bitmap: SETBIT · BITCOUNT · BITOP , one key per day needs dense integer ids; sparse ids allocate the whole range in memory Geo proximity GEOADD · GEOSEARCH , a sorted set underneath radius and box only, no polygons, and the index is a single key by construction Anything that has to be read-modify-write goes into a script, because two round trips from the client are two chances to interleave: -- one worker wins one due job: read and remove in the same atomic step local due = redis. call ( 'ZRANGEBYSCORE' , KEYS[ 1 ], '-inf' , ARGV[ 1 ], 'LIMIT' , 0 , 1 ) if due[ 1 ] then redis. call ( 'ZREM' , KEYS[ 1 ], due[ 1 ]) end return due[ 1 ] -- -- ZRANGEBYSCORE from the client, then ZREM: two workers can take the same job The rule that decides all twelve rows Redis may own state that is derived, ephemeral or rebuildable. State whose loss changes what the business owes somebody lives in the database first and in Redis second. choose a stream over a list the moment a message must survive a consumer crash: XREADGROUP moves the entry into the group's pending list and only XACK removes it, while XAUTOCLAIM (6.2+) hands entries idle beyond a threshold to another consumer. a stream still needs trimming — XADD stream MAXLEN ~ 1000000 ... — and it is one key on one shard, so ordering and throughput are bounded by that shard. When partitions and replay are the requirement, that is a broker's job (08-message-queue.the-two-models.q3). keyspace notifications (notify-keyspace-events) ride the same Pub/Sub delivery, so they are at-most-once too. A scheduler built on expiry events drops jobs silently whenever a subscriber reconnects; the sorted-set poll above is the version that survives. keep cache and state on different instances. maxmemory-policy is per instance, so one shared server cannot both evict cold product pages and be trusted to hold locks and sessions (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q7).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q8"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q9","surface":"track","en":"What Redis actually guarantees before you keep state in it","bodyEn":"Every job in the previous item rests on four properties. Read them once and the list of what Redis may own writes itself. 1 · One command at a time. The data path executes commands sequentially, which is where per-command atomicity comes from. io-threads parallelizes socket reads and writes only — execution stays serial. The cost is that one slow command delays every client: KEYS, HGETALL or ZRANGE key 0 -1 on a huge key, DEL on a multi-million-element key (use UNLINK). Find them before production does, with SLOWLOG GET, redis-cli --bigkeys and LATENCY DOCTOR. 2 · Multi-command atomicity is opt-in. MULTI/EXEC runs the queued commands without interleaving, but there is no rollback — a command that fails at execution time leaves the earlier ones applied, and WATCH is the compare-and-set that gets you out of it. Lua via EVAL, or a Function via FCALL (7.0+), executes atomically and is the right home for read-modify-write. The script blocks the server while it runs, so keep it small and loop-free; the same single thread is what makes it atomic. 3 · Cluster splits the keyspace into 16384 hash slots. A multi-key command, a transaction or a script must have every key in one slot or the server answers CROSSSLOT. -- release the lock only if this holder still owns it if redis. call ( 'GET' , KEYS[ 1 ]) == ARGV[ 1 ] then return redis. call ( 'DEL' , KEYS[ 1 ]) end return 0 -- -- GET then DEL from the client can delete a lock somebody else already re-took -- the hash tag {order:42} forces both keys into one slot, so the script is legal EVAL script 2 '{order:42}:lock' '{order:42}:state' 4 · Replication is asynchronous, so a failover can lose acknowledged writes. WAIT blocks until N replicas have received the write and WAITAOF numlocal numreplicas timeout (7.2+) until it is fsynced, but the documentation is explicit that neither makes Redis strongly consistent. That is the whole argument against a lock, an idempotency record or a balance living only in Redis: after a failover the key can simply be absent (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q6). Durability on one node has the same shape: RDB loses everything since the snapshot, AOF everysec about a second, always a fsync per write (07-sql-nosql-db-engines.engine-by-engine.q6). Read this row as the verdict Cache values and derived reads: Redis alone is fine. Sessions, locks, queues and idempotency: Redis plus a database rule that catches the loss. Money, inventory and anything audited: the database decides and Redis only accelerates. expiry is lazy plus sampled, so memory is released when a key is touched or when the sampler reaches it, not at the TTL instant — a mass expiry shows up as a slow decline in used_memory, not a cliff (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q4). a hash tag is a co-location decision, and co-location is exactly how a hot slot is built. Tag by the entity a transaction needs, never by tenant or by day. INFO memory gives mem_fragmentation_ratio: well above 1 means the allocator is holding memory the dataset no longer uses, and below 1 means the process is swapping — the second one is an incident, not a tuning note. a slow SUBSCRIBE client is charged to the server through its output buffer, and client-output-buffer-limit pubsub is what disconnects it before it becomes the server's memory problem. sizing, eviction policy and the hit-rate metric belong to the same decision (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q7).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q9"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q10","surface":"track","en":"Cache eviction: how do the working set, admission, and real algorithms fit together?","bodyEn":"Eviction is capacity pressure, not TTL cleanup. Expiration decides when an entry is no longer usable; invalidation removes or supersedes a value because the source changed. Eviction chooses what must leave a bounded cache so another entry can occupy the space. An expired entry may still consume memory until the engine discovers it, while an unexpired entry may be evicted at any time (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q4). Start with the working set, not an algorithm name. The dataset is all authoritative data. The working set is the distinct data referenced during a chosen observation window and likely to be reused while resident. Both can grow or shrink; traffic shape, key cardinality, object size and reuse distance matter more than the raw user count. Capacity must cover entry bytes plus keys, metadata and headroom—not just serialized values. If usable capacity comfortably exceeds the active working set, steady-state capacity eviction is rare and policy differences narrow. If it is smaller, candidates continually displace residents; hit ratio becomes workload- and policy-dependent. A low hit ratio with few evictions points elsewhere: little reuse, fragmented keys, short TTLs, invalidation churn or a cache key that encodes too much context. There are two separate gates. Admission asks whether a new candidate deserves residency. Eviction asks which current resident is the victim. Rejecting admission still returns the fetched value to this request; it only declines to retain a copy. A batch scan should ideally bypass the user cache; a short TTL limits residence time but does not prevent immediate displacement. Admission choice Decision Cost and trap Always admit Every write or loaded miss enters. Zero policy state; vulnerable to one-hit pollution. Bypass / namespace Known reports, crawlers or large objects never enter this cache. Cheapest defense, but classification must be trustworthy. Two-hit doorkeeper Record the first miss; admit after evidence of reuse. A Bloom filter is approximate. General N-hit needs counters or a sketch. Frequency duel Admit only when candidate frequency beats the chosen victim. A sketch saves memory but has collisions and must age. Value / size aware Compare expected saved miss cost with bytes consumed. Needs item size, miss cost and reuse estimates—not merely one division. Replacement families make different predictions: Family What it remembers Blind spot LRU Recency; adapts quickly when traffic shifts. A sequential scan makes one-hit entries look hottest. Aging LFU Recent frequency; protects a stable hot set. Without decay, yesterday's winner can become immortal. SLRU / 2Q-style Probation separates first touch from repeated use. Segment sizing is workload-dependent. ARC Resident recency/frequency lists plus non-resident ghost history. More metadata and implementation complexity. CLOCK / second chance A small reference or usage count scanned by a clock hand. Approximate ordering; a sweep may inspect several buffers. W-TinyLFU Short recency window plus aged approximate frequency. More moving parts than LRU; estimates can collide. SLRU and ARC are easier to distinguish as state machines. SLRU is segmented replacement, not an admission gate: every new entry still occupies probation and can displace its LRU victim. The protected segment merely prevents a one-hit scan from directly replacing proven entries. ARC stores identifiers and recency metadata in B1/B2, not cached payloads. That history is bounded but not free; a B1 hit grows the recency target p, while a B2 hit shrinks it in favor of frequency. Keep the long tail of policies in perspective: Random, FIFO and MRU trade prediction quality for cheap metadata or a specialized access pattern. They are alternatives to benchmark, not a quality ladder below LRU; Random can be competitive when accesses are close to uniform. CLOCK and Second-Chance are a family of approximations, not always one bit per entry. PostgreSQL's clock sweep uses a bounded usage_count, while current Linux reclaim must be described separately. LIRS estimates inter-reference recency and keeps deeper history; it buys scan resistance with more bookkeeping. Belady's MIN evicts the item whose next use is farthest away, so it is an offline lower bound for trace simulation—not a deployable policy. W-TinyLFU combines a chance for new keys with a frequency admission duel. A new entry first enters a small Window LRU, so a fresh burst is not rejected before it can prove itself. When the window evicts, that candidate competes with a victim from the main cache. TinyLFU estimates both frequencies from an aged 4-bit Count-Min Sketch; the winner stays. The window supplies recency; the main SLRU supplies protection; TinyLFU decides admission between them. Implementation reality matters more than the family name: Redis uses sampled approximations of LRU and LFU. Its LFU is a probabilistic Morris counter with decay. Redis selects an eviction candidate but has no TinyLFU candidate-versus-victim gate. noeviction rejects writes under pressure; that is a capacity failure, not a quality filter. Caffeine 3.2.4 uses adaptive W-TinyLFU. maximumWeight bounds weighted capacity, but its documentation explicitly says relative weights do not choose eviction victims; do not call that benefit-per-byte admission. Memcached's maintained LRU is split per slab class into HOT, WARM and COLD, with an optional TEMP area. Repeated hits promote entries and one-hit items flow toward COLD, improving scan resistance. MySQL 8.4 InnoDB inserts new pages at the midpoint, by default 3/8 from the LRU tail, and delays promotion with innodb_old_blocks_time=1000 ms. Those are defaults to benchmark, not universal tuning constants. PostgreSQL uses a clock-sweep policy over usage_count. Linux should not be summarized as “also CLOCK”: current kernels document active/inactive LRU machinery and optional Multi-Gen LRU. ZFS ARC balances recent and frequent resident lists using ghost history. Its L2ARC is a separate secondary cache, not one of ARC's ghost lists. Operate the decision, not the label. Track eviction rate, hit ratio by key class, origin QPS, miss p99, bytes per entry and reuse distance. Replay a production trace at several capacities; a policy win at one cache size may reverse at another. Diagnose before adding RAM High evictions plus recently reused victims and origin load that falls when capacity rises indicate a cache that is too small. High misses with almost no evictions usually indicate low reuse, key design or freshness churn; another eviction algorithm cannot manufacture reuse. The interview answer is: size the usable cache against the measured working set, bypass known one-hit traffic, choose admission before debating victims, and name the actual engine behavior. “Use LRU” is an implementation detail. The design is which data earns residency and how the decision adapts when the workload changes.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q10"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q11","surface":"track","en":"Cache stack and AMAT: where is latency actually paid before Redis?","bodyEn":"A request does not meet one cache; it crosses a stack of reuse boundaries. The stack is a debugging model, not eight interchangeable key-value stores. Browser and CDN cache HTTP representations, an application L1 caches objects, Redis caches shared derived state, while database and OS caches operate on pages or blocks. Each layer has a different key, owner, freshness rule, observability surface and failure mode. AMAT turns “high hit ratio” into a break-even calculation. For one cache-aside layer, let L_c be lookup latency, L_o origin latency, L_w fill latency and h the hit probability: E[T] = L_c + (1 - h) × (L_o + L_w) cache wins when E[T] < L_o h > (L_c + L_w) / (L_o + L_w) Example: lookup 1 ms · origin 5 ms · fill 1 ms break-even h > 2 / 6 = 33.3% at h = 80%: E[T] = 1 + 0.2 × 6 = 2.2 ms This expectation is only the first gate. A design can improve the mean and still lose at p99 when misses queue at the origin. Calculate per key class and include cache timeouts, serialization, network hops, fill failures and request coalescing. Layer Inspect Typical hidden failure Browser / CDN Age , Cache-Control , Vary , edge status wrong cache key or stale content in an outer tier App L1 hit rate, load penalty, eviction, entry weight per-instance divergence and cold deploys Redis hit/miss by key class, RTT, eviction, bytes per key an extra network hop on every miss Database logical reads versus physical reads Redis hides a missing index; cold failover exposes it OS / storage major faults, read IOPS, queue latency double-counting the DB buffer and page cache as one ratio Freshness belongs to each layer. HTTP max-age, shared s-maxage, an L1 expiry and a Redis TTL are independent clocks. A purge that reaches Redis but not the CDN can still serve stale data; a short inner TTL cannot correct a fresh outer response. Version cache keys or carry a data version so an older fill cannot resurrect state after invalidation. Measure conditional hit rate at each stop: P(hit at layer i all earlier layers missed). Adding global hit rates together is mathematically wrong. Read the series internally as patterns (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q5), write paths (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q6), hit economics here, expiry/invalidation (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q4), then eviction (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q10). A cache can lower origin QPS and still make user latency worse If L_c is slow, miss probability is high, or the origin call still runs after a cache timeout, the new layer adds work without removing old work. Compare end-to-end latency and origin saturation before and after—not hit ratio alone.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q11"},{"id":"25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q12","surface":"track","en":"Cache expiration and TTL policies: which clock should end an entry's life?","bodyEn":"TTL is a freshness and retention contract, not a cleanup scheduler. Three events are easy to blur: Expiration is covered here; invalidation and avalanche are in q4, capacity eviction in q10. Logical expiry and physical removal are separate moments. A cache must stop returning an expired entry at its deadline; it does not have to reclaim those bytes at that exact millisecond. Implementations make different trade-offs. “Expired” is a read-semantic guarantee; prompt physical cleanup is engine-specific. Policy Clock starts / ends Use it for Hidden failure Expire after write fixed TTL last create or replace + T catalog, exchange-rate snapshot, derived reads bounds cache age, not source divergence; a failed refresh can still leave a miss storm Expire after access idle timeout last accepted access + I session retention and expensive idle objects a hot stale value can live forever; each Redis GETEX is a write-like operation Expire at absolute instant business deadline as Unix time campaign close, daily epoch, signed authorization shared deadlines synchronize misses; clock skew matters Jittered a bounded random offset around a base policy keys filled in one batch spreads different keys only; one hot key still needs single-flight Contextual a bounded function of value, tenant or load source-provided expiry, payload cost, freshness class unbounded load-aware TTL hides stale data and creates feedback loops An inactivity window is never permission to extend an authorization beyond its absolute expiry. Jitter needs a freshness boundary. Choose the distribution from the contract, not by habit: // base is the normal reload interval; hardDeadline is the latest legal expiry candidate = base + random(0, spread) ttl = max(0, min(candidate, hardDeadline - now)) // if base itself is the maximum stale age, subtract jitter instead of adding it Jitter prevents many different keys from falling off a cliff together. It does not coalesce concurrent misses for one hot key, and an exact campaign cutoff must never be jittered past the cutoff. Prewarm the next version, use a versioned namespace, stale-while-revalidate where legal, and cap rebuild concurrency (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q4). Engine semantics are part of the design: Redis uses access-time expiry plus an adaptive active cycle. Expiry timestamps are absolute internally; the primary synthesizes deletion for AOF and replicas. GETEX (6.2+) can implement sliding TTL, but Redis classifies it as a write because it changes metadata. SET normally discards the old TTL unless KEEPTTL is requested; a non-positive EXPIRE deletes immediately. Caffeine offers expireAfterWrite, expireAfterAccess and per-entry Expiry. Cleanup runs during maintenance on writes and occasional reads; configure a Scheduler when prompt removal matters. Memcached treats expiry 0 as no time expiry, but the item may still be evicted. A relative value over 30 days is interpreted as an absolute Unix timestamp; gat or touch moves the deadline. The same number therefore does not mean the same thing in every product. Persist the selected policy beside the key class, test boundary behavior with a controllable clock, and never use an expiry notification as a durable business event. Pick policy from the invariant Ask four questions: what is the maximum legal staleness; is there a hard business deadline; may activity extend retention; and how many keys can expire in one recovery window? The answers choose the policy, jitter bound and rebuild concurrency together. Observe expiry-driven misses separately from cold, eviction and invalidation misses. Correlate them with origin QPS, coalesced waiters, refresh failures, expired-key backlog and memory/RSS. Dynamic TTL must stay inside explicit minimum and maximum bounds, expose the reason it chose a value, and use hysteresis. Extending TTL merely because the database is overloaded trades availability for staleness; make that degraded mode an explicit product decision. TTL is a backstop, not distributed consistency. If a source mutation must become invisible immediately, use versioning or invalidation and keep TTL as the bounded failure mode (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q4). Across the stack, the outermost still-valid copy wins. Align this policy with HTTP and L1 clocks described in (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q11).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q12"},{"id":"25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q1","surface":"track","en":"Hot partitions: why does skew remain after sharding?","bodyEn":"Sharding can still leave the workload skewed because: sharding by time sends all new writes to the current shard sharding by entity creates a whale problem when, for example, one merchant owns 40% of transactions → that merchant's shard becomes hot a monotonic key keeps the first or last partition permanently hot Choose a shard key that is evenly distributed and aligned with query patterns; hashing userId is often better than using a timestamp. The tension is that a shard key should both distribute load evenly and colocate data commonly queried together, but those goals conflict. Hashing breaks range queries, while range partitioning is prone to hot spots → use a composite such as hash(tenant)+time. Give a whale entity its own shard or sub-partition it internally. Real distributions are often Zipfian, so plan for whales rather than assuming uniform load.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q2","surface":"track","en":"Read-replica lag","bodyEn":"Asynchronous replicas expose a position behind the primary. A write acknowledged at the primary followed by an unconstrained replica read can return older state; routing two reads to replicas at different positions can also violate monotonic reads. Choose semantics per operation: Operation Read from What must also hold Authoritative decision, such as available funds the same primary transaction, or a data source whose consistency guarantee satisfies the invariant no replica read feeds the decision Read-your-writes the primary for a bounded session, or a replica that has replayed at least the returned commit/version token a defined fallback when the replica cannot catch up before the deadline History/search projection any replica, with bounded staleness the UI/API communicates it, and no correctness decision consumes it Measure transport, receive/write, flush and replay/apply positions where the engine exposes them; a single “lag seconds” metric can hide clock skew or which stage is stuck. Causes include long-running transactions, apply conflicts, insufficient replica I/O/CPU, WAL/binlog retention pressure, large DDL and network loss. Adding replicas can increase primary shipping work and does not repair an apply bottleneck. Sticky routing alone provides monotonicity only while the chosen replica does not fall behind or fail over. Test replica loss, region latency, token expiry, failover, clock skew and a lag spike during peak reads. Fintech consistency boundaries are detailed in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q3","surface":"track","en":"N+1 and unindexed queries","bodyEn":"An N+1 access pattern loads N parent rows and then issues one query per parent. Under concurrency it amplifies round trips, pool hold time and database scheduling even when each query is individually fast. Detect it from query counts per request/trace, ORM statistics and normalized-query telemetry—not only from one slow-query threshold. Choose the fix from the shape: projection or join for one bounded result, batch/IN loading, ORM batch fetch/entity graph, or a separately queried collection assembled in memory. Joining multiple to-many collections can multiply rows, distort pagination and use more memory than batched reads; measure result cardinality and deduplicate deliberately. An unindexed predicate is not automatically a bug, and a full scan can be correct for a small table or a query returning much of the data. For hot OLTP paths, compare estimated versus actual rows, buffers/I/O, loops, lock time and returned columns with production-like statistics. Build the smallest index that serves the workload while accounting for write amplification and cache footprint. Use statement deadlines, bounded pools and query attribution so one bad shape cannot consume every connection. Slow-query logs, traces and plan-regression tests complement each other; EXPLAIN in CI is useful only with controlled schema/statistics and should not be treated as proof of a production plan. Test realistic parent counts, empty and maximum pages, multiple collections, skewed tenants and concurrency. The canonical investigation process is (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q1), with plan reading in (18-query-optimization.measure-first-reading-the-execution-plan.q2).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.07-observability-debugging.q1","surface":"track","en":"Distributed tracing","bodyEn":"Generate the trace ID at the edge, propagate it end to end through a W3C traceparent header, and attach it to every log record and outbound call. OpenTelemetry can auto-instrument Spring Boot through the Java agent the trace must cross the message broker by propagating context in message headers; otherwise, it breaks at the asynchronous boundary The three pillars are not interchangeable: traces / metrics / logs. Connect them with an exemplar, which attaches a trace ID to a metric so an engineer can jump from a p99 spike to a representative trace, and include the trace ID in every log. Context propagation breaks most easily across thread pools and asynchronous boundaries because ThreadLocal state does not transfer automatically, and across virtual-thread or reactive execution. This is the part teams most often miss. Trace context crosses trust boundaries carefully Trace IDs and trace state should be opaque correlation data, not a container for user data, credentials, or authorization decisions. Control which baggage is accepted and propagated across tenants, brokers, and external calls; bound cardinality and retention, and redact payloads before logging or exporting spans. A trace helps locate evidence but does not prove a business effect committed.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.07-observability-debugging.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.07-observability-debugging.q2","surface":"track","en":"Metrics: RED, USE, and p99","bodyEn":"Use RED to observe a request/event-serving boundary—Rate, Errors and Duration—and USE to observe a resource—Utilization, Saturation and Errors. They are prompts, not dashboards to copy: a queue has arrival/completion rate and age; a database pool has active/waiting connections and acquisition time; a business flow needs success/correctness indicators beyond HTTP status. Report a latency distribution with a correctly configured histogram or equivalent, plus count and error classification. Averages remain useful for capacity/cost and data-quality checks, while percentiles describe tail experience; neither replaces the other. Do not average precomputed p99 values across instances or time windows. Aggregate histogram buckets or use a backend data type whose merge semantics are understood. Fan-out amplifies tails, but 1−(1−p)^n assumes independent, identical branches and a precise threshold. Shared dependencies create correlation, retries change the number of attempts, and some branches run in parallel or are optional. Measure the composed user journey rather than presenting the formula as a prediction. Segment only by bounded dimensions that correspond to a decision—operation, outcome, region, dependency class—not raw user/booking/URL values. Align SLIs with the user-visible boundary and attach exemplars where supported so an operator can move from a histogram observation to a trace. The canonical RED/USE treatment is (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q3); cardinality controls are in (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q4).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.07-observability-debugging.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.07-observability-debugging.q3","surface":"track","en":"Sampling and health checks","bodyEn":"Sampling and health checks solve different failure modes, and both require an explicit policy. Trace sampling: head sampling decides early and cheaply; tail sampling can retain policy-selected slow, failed, or high-value traces after observing enough spans, but requires buffering and consistent routing. It cannot recover spans already dropped upstream, and retaining every error/slow trace is not automatically affordable or well-defined. Startup probe protects slow initialization from premature liveness failure. Liveness asks whether restarting this process can help. Readiness asks whether this instance should receive new traffic now. Dependency checks are a traffic decision Putting a database or remote dependency in readiness can be correct when the instance truly cannot serve any useful request, but it can also remove every pod during a shared dependency slowdown and turn degradation into an outage. Model critical and optional capabilities, use bounded probe timeouts, and prefer graceful degradation when the product contract permits it. Set sampling from traffic volume, diagnostic value, privacy, collector capacity, and cost; monitor dropped spans and decision latency. Specify probe thresholds and failure behavior from measured startup/recovery characteristics rather than copying defaults. The canonical tracing discussion is (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2); the canonical Kubernetes probe semantics are (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q6).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.07-observability-debugging.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q1","surface":"track","en":"API versioning and backward compatibility","bodyEn":"Independent deployments guarantee periods when A-v2 communicates with B-v1. add only optional fields so older consumers can ignore them for a breaking change, introduce an explicit version and run both versions in parallel evolve the database schema with expand-contract: add a column → write both old and new forms → backfill → switch reads → remove the old form. Never make a breaking schema change in one step Events are harder than REST because they live long enough to be replayed. Use a Schema Registry with Avro or Protobuf and enforce BACKWARD or FORWARD compatibility according to which side deploys first. Expand-contract is mandatory during rolling deployments because two application versions share the same database. Renaming a column in one step immediately breaks old pods, so spread the change across multiple releases.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q2","surface":"track","en":"Safe deployments","bodyEn":"A safe deployment limits blast radius, detects regressions against a valid baseline, and preserves compatibility long enough to stop or reverse the change. Rolling update manages capacity with maxSurge/maxUnavailable, readiness and draining; it is not automatically a canary because traffic may spread across every new pod. Canary/progressive delivery exposes a chosen cohort, region, tenant or traffic share and advances on technical plus business guardrails. The starting percentage and observation time come from risk, traffic volume and statistical power—low traffic may need synthetic checks or a longer window. Blue-green gives a fast traffic switch but duplicates capacity and still needs database/message compatibility. Feature flags separate code deployment from exposure. Give temporary flags an owner and removal date; secure long-lived kill switches, audit their use, and test meaningful combinations rather than assuming all 2^n combinations are reachable. Use expand-migrate-contract for databases and tolerant readers/writers for APIs/events so old and new versions overlap safely. A rollback is safe only if the new version has not made an incompatible state transition; otherwise pause exposure, disable the feature, or roll forward with a repair. Define this before deployment. Compare canary and control concurrently under similar traffic, but account for cohort/region bias. Gate on SLO burn, error/latency/saturation, data invariants and business outcomes; one green CPU chart is insufficient. High-risk money flows often merit a narrowly scoped, authorized kill switch plus reconciliation—not a universal switch for every method. Kubernetes rollout mechanics are covered in (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q1), and live schema changes in (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.ci-cd-2026-best-practices.q7).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q3","surface":"track","en":"Autoscaling lag and API gateways","bodyEn":"Autoscaling is a delayed control loop. Detection interval, metric lag, scheduling, image pull, startup probes, dependency registration and application warmup all contribute to time-to-useful-capacity; there is no universal “30–60 second JVM warmup.” Measure the distribution for the real image, node pool, traffic mix and runtime. Scale on the earliest reliable signal for the bottleneck. CPU can be useful for compute-bound work, but queue age/depth, in-flight concurrency, request/event arrival rate, pool saturation or a predictive schedule may lead it for I/O and bursty workloads. Use multiple signals only with a documented control policy, and configure scale-up/down rate plus stabilization to prevent oscillation. Capacity arrives before traffic only by design Keep headroom for latency-critical paths, pre-scale known events, reduce image/startup work, and ensure readiness represents useful service—not merely an open port. Kubernetes HPA deliberately handles not-yet-ready pod metrics conservatively; align startup/readiness probes and HPA initialization behavior with measured warmup. CRaC checkpoints, CDS and native images can reduce some startup costs but introduce build/runtime, resource-rebinding, profile-guidance or compatibility constraints. Benchmark steady-state throughput, memory and restore safety; they are options, not default remedies. Scale-to-zero may be unsuitable when cold-start latency violates the product SLO. A stateless gateway is easy to replicate, but a gateway cannot manufacture downstream database/broker capacity. Couple autoscaling with bounded admission, rate limits, backpressure and dependency budgets. Test a step spike, ramp, slow dependency, cold node, image-cache miss, rollout plus scale event and scale-down under long requests. Probe semantics are canonical in (14-devops-k8s-best-practices.docker-kubernetes.q6); overload handling in (10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q2).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q4","surface":"track","en":"Scheduled and background jobs across N replicas","bodyEn":"A scheduler is state pretending to be code. Three replicas mean three schedulers, and every failure mode below comes from that. Duplicate runs. @Scheduled fires on every instance, so a nightly settlement job runs three times. Election is the fix — a database lock row, ShedLock, or a Kubernetes Lease. Which one matters less than the lock's lease outlasting the job's worst case (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q6). Lease expiry mid-run. A job that outlives its lease loses ownership while still writing, a second instance acquires the lock, and both are active. That is the split-brain a distributed lock has by nature, which is why the work must be idempotent or carry a fencing token (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q6). Overlap. fixedRate schedules from the previous start, so a run that overruns queues another behind it. Long jobs need an explicit policy: skip if still running, or refuse to start and alert. Missed runs. After an outage, does the 02:00 job run late, run twice for two days, or not at all? That is a business answer, not a framework default — and it has to be written down. lease TTL = 15 min job p99 = 12 min // fine job worst case = 40 min // lease expires, a second runner starts & 8594; extend the lease while running, or checkpoint and make reruns free Make the job restartable rather than perfect Chunk the work, commit progress, record the last processed id. A job you can kill and restart at any moment needs neither a perfect lock nor a perfect schedule (18-query-optimization.rewriting-the-query-reshaping-the-model.q5). the silent failure is a job that stopped running at all. Alert on the absence of a completion heartbeat, not on job errors — nobody notices missing output for weeks (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q2). cron expressions carry a time zone, and DST makes 02:30 run twice or never. Schedule in UTC and convert only for display (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q7). graceful shutdown must let an in-flight job finish or checkpoint; a rolling deploy that kills the runner mid-batch is a deploy that corrupts a report. keep batch jobs off the request-serving pool: their own connection pool, their own instances if the load is heavy, so a backfill cannot take latency with it. for anything critical, prefer a queue with visible state over a timer: rows in a table with SKIP LOCKED scale to N workers and leave an audit trail a cron log never will.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q4"},{"id":"25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1","surface":"track","en":"How does an idempotency key work?","bodyEn":"The client sends an idempotency key, typically a UUID representing one unique operation intent: @PostMapping(\"/transfers\") Response transfer(@RequestHeader(\"Idempotency-Key\") String key, @RequestBody TransferRequest req) { boolean firstTime = idempotencyStore.tryInsert(key, IN_PROGRESS); // atomic if (!firstTime) { var rec = idempotencyStore.get(key); if (rec.status == COMPLETED) return rec.savedResponse; // return the original result if (rec.status == IN_PROGRESS) return Response.status(409); // still processing } Response resp = doTransfer(req); idempotencyStore.update(key, COMPLETED, resp); // store the ENTIRE response return resp; } Store the entire response because a retry needs the original transaction ID, not merely an empty 200 OK use an atomic CAS such as INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING backed by a unique constraint, never SELECT-then-INSERT, which races handle IN_PROGRESS by returning 409 so the client waits. Keep the TTL long enough to cover the legitimate retry window The trap: doTransfer completes, but the process crashes before update → the key remains IN_PROGRESS forever. Put a timeout on IN_PROGRESS and make doTransfer idempotent at the next layer; ideally, include both operations in the same database transaction. Also use a natural key such as transferId as a database unique constraint → two independent safety nets. Recovery needs a lease, fingerprint, and effect boundary An IN_PROGRESS record needs an owner/lease, timeout, safe reclaim rule, canonical request fingerprint, and retention policy. If the side effect is external, a local key row cannot prove its outcome after a timeout; use the provider operation identity, status lookup, webhook, and reconciliation. When the effect and marker cannot share a transaction, make the effect converge through a natural business key and document the duplicate window.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q2","surface":"track","en":"Idempotent consumers and the inbox pattern","bodyEn":"At-least-once delivery plus the Outbox pattern can produce duplicates, so the consumer deduplicates by eventId: void handle(Event e) { if (!processedEvents.tryInsert(e.getEventId())) return; // already processed; ignore process(e); } Ideally, tryInsert and process run in the same transaction, which is the inbox pattern. Otherwise, a gap remains in which the consumer records the event as processed, crashes, and never processes it. Operation order determines the failure mode: mark first → risk losing the message's effect process first → risk repeating the effect, which is harmless if processing is idempotent If the two operations cannot share a transaction, choose \"process first\" and make processing idempotent. The deduplication store's TTL must cover the retention and replay window. If a message remains in a DLQ for several days and is replayed after its dedup record expires, it will be processed again.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q3","surface":"track","en":"Why is idempotency the central link?","bodyEn":"Idempotency connects recovery mechanisms because it makes a repeated command converge on the same defined outcome within a stated scope: a timed-out caller can retry an eligible operation without intentionally creating a second payment (25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q5) an at-least-once consumer can reprocess a delivery after ack/offset ambiguity (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6) an Outbox relay can publish again after a publish-before-mark crash (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3) The contract includes identity, scope/tenant, canonical request fingerprint, concurrency behavior, stored status/result, retention and what happens after expiry. The same key with different material input must be rejected; two concurrent first attempts need one atomic winner; an IN_PROGRESS record needs a lease/recovery policy. Necessary, not sufficient Idempotency does not replace authorization, an atomic ledger invariant, uniqueness constraints, ordering/version checks, reconciliation or protection against a caller using a new key for the same business intent. A duplicate “success” response is safe only when it represents the same committed outcome. Place deduplication in the same transaction as the side effect it protects when possible. Define the boundary: HTTP request, aggregate command, consumer plus event ID, or external provider operation. For money movement, combine idempotency with double-entry accounting and an immutable audit/reconciliation trail (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q1","surface":"track","en":"A system-design framework and back-of-the-envelope estimates","bodyEn":"Use the shared review rubric in (11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12): scope and invariants → workload ranges → contracts/data ownership → critical paths → failure/operations → explicit trade-offs. Back-of-the-envelope estimates are inputs to a decision, not evidence that a named database will handle the workload. For an illustrative e-wallet workload only, suppose the product gives these assumptions: active users: 100M money commands/day: 10M → average ≈ 116/s assumed peak factor: 5–10× → design range ≈ 580–1,160 commands/s stored event payload: 1 KiB → raw payload ≈ 3.65 TiB/year Every line must retain its unit and source. The storage result excludes indexes, row/page overhead, WAL/binlog, replicas, backups, compaction, audit copies and growth. “Read:write = 100:1” should be another labeled assumption split by journey—authoritative balance decisions, cached display reads, history search and batch reporting have different stores and consistency needs. Turn estimates into tests and triggers: benchmark the real schema, transaction mix, durability/HA configuration, hardware and skew; load-test failure modes; measure headroom. A PostgreSQL deployment may handle the example or may not—TPS alone says nothing about lock contention, query cost, connection count, I/O latency, failover or operational limits. Shard only when a measured constraint and viable shard key justify its distributed-transaction cost. Use ranges and sensitivity: ask which conclusion changes if peak factor, payload, retention or one hot tenant is 10×. Record the threshold, owner and evolution path for cache, replica, archive or partitioning. Capacity-estimation technique is canonical in (10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2). Opening decision flow Start with invariant and authority, then state workload and queue shape, deadline and retry owner, resource protection, recovery/reconciliation, and the evidence or trigger that would change the design. This order prevents a named technology from becoming the first decision.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q1"},{"id":"25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q2","surface":"track","en":"Service boundaries: synchronous or asynchronous?","bodyEn":"Start with bounded contexts—language, invariants, data ownership, change cadence and team responsibility—not with a requirement to deploy many services. A well-modularized monolith can preserve those boundaries until independent deployment or scaling is worth the distributed cost (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q2). Choose synchronous vs asynchronous per interaction: Use synchronous request/response when the caller needs a decision now and the callee can participate within the latency/availability budget. Keep fan-out bounded and propagate deadlines/cancellation. Use messaging when temporal decoupling, buffering, independent recovery or multiple subscribers are requirements. Define delivery, ordering, schema, idempotency, backlog and completion visibility; asynchronous work is not free or instantly consistent. Keep invariants that require one atomic decision inside one state owner/transaction where possible. A remote “check balance” followed by a later debit creates a race even though both calls were synchronous. Map one user-critical path and count remote dependencies. Tail risk is workload- and correlation-dependent; do not infer end-to-end latency from a universal “1% per service” formula. Decide what can degrade, what must fail closed and what result the user sees while async work continues. For a transfer, commit ledger state atomically, publish integration events through an Outbox, and send notifications asynchronously. If a PSP is external, model its ambiguous timeout and reconciliation rather than pretending the call joins the local transaction. Integration-event boundaries are detailed in (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3) and Outbox semantics in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q2"},{"id":"25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q3","surface":"track","en":"Data models and partitioning","bodyEn":"Default to the simplest authoritative store that can enforce the invariants. Add a cache, stream, search index, replica or warehouse only for a named access pattern, isolation or retention need; each derived store needs ownership, freshness semantics, rebuild/reconciliation and failure behavior. Ledger command path: append balanced debit/credit entries in one ACID transaction with currency/amount and uniqueness constraints. A current balance may be maintained transactionally as a derived value, but the ledger remains auditable; never authorize spend from an eventually consistent cache. Read models: history, search, notifications and analytics may consume Outbox events and tolerate stated lag. Expose freshness/version where users or operators need it. Partitioning: choose from dominant access, invariant and failure domain. Hashing accountId can distribute account-local reads, but a transfer touches two accounts, hot institutions/merchants remain skewed, and regulatory/tenant placement may constrain routing. Prototype cross-partition behavior before committing to the key. Consistency is chosen per operation/read model and invariant, not merely “per field.” A cached balance string can be stale for display while the debit decision still requires authoritative transactional state. Define monotonic/read-your-writes behavior explicitly. Sharding introduces routing, resharding, unique-key scope, cross-shard transactions, backups and incident tooling. First exhaust query/index/schema work, bounded connections, partitioning/archival and vertical/replica options as appropriate; then shard on measured limits. Double-entry design is canonical in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3) concurrency correctness in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q5) allowed eventual consistency in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6)","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q3"},{"id":"25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q4","surface":"track","en":"Worked example: design an end-to-end money-transfer flow","bodyEn":"For an internal transfer between two accounts owned by the same ledger, keep the money invariant inside one ACID double-entry transaction. Do not model the debit and credit as separate saga steps: a crash between them would temporarily create or destroy money. Validate POST /transfers and bind Idempotency-Key to tenant + normalized request hash. In one ledger DB transaction: atomically claim the idempotency/business transfer ID; lock/version the affected accounts in a deterministic order; append balanced immutable debit and credit entries; write transfer status and an outbox event; store the replayable API result, then commit. Outbox relay publishes TransferPosted; consumers deduplicate by eventId. Notification/search/analytics update asynchronously and may lag without changing ledger truth. Apply deadlines, concurrency limits, retry, breaker, or fallback only where each downstream's failure semantics require them; do not wrap every call with every resilience decorator. Trace the flow without putting credentials/PII in trace baggage, and continuously reconcile ledger balance, transfer state, outbox, and external statements. If the destination is an external bank/PSP, the local transaction records a balanced internal obligation/pending state plus outbox. A durable orchestrator then performs the external step with a stable provider idempotency key, handles timeout as an unknown outcome, polls/webhooks/reconciles, and records a compensating or forward recovery entry. It never edits immutable ledger history to pretend the attempt did not occur. The correctness chain is explicit: payment idempotency and ambiguous outcomes: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1) and (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2); balanced ledger model: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q3); outbox atomicity: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3); idempotent consumer: (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6); network deadline/retry controls: (15-network-i-o-models.threading-network-programming.q5); trace and messaging causality: (20-observability-sre.the-three-pillars-how-to-measure.q2). Crash-test every boundary: before/after DB commit, before/after broker confirmation, duplicate/out-of-order delivery, provider success followed by local timeout, process kill during recovery, and stale read. For each, state the durable source of truth and the mechanism that converges without double effect.","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q4"},{"id":"25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q5","surface":"track","en":"A trade-off framework — there is no universally correct architecture","bodyEn":"There is no architecture score without a stated objective, workload and failure model. Frame each decision as: constraint → options → evidence → choice → consequence → trigger to revisit. Consistency/availability under partition: CAP applies when communication is partitioned; choose which operations reject/queue and which may serve stale or tentative state. Outside a partition, latency, coordination and freshness still trade off, but “the whole service chooses C or A” is too coarse. Cost/risk/performance: quantify the availability gain, latency, recovery objective, operational skill and failure modes of another region, broker, cache or database—not a universal cost multiplier. Simplicity/evolution: prefer the smallest design meeting current invariants and SLOs, with observed thresholds for adding an index, replica, partition, shard or region. A round TPS number cannot prove one PostgreSQL instance is sufficient. A mature answer names rejected alternatives and the evidence that would reverse the choice. For example: “single-region primary plus tested restore/failover meets the current RTO/RPO; reconsider active-active when jurisdiction or measured outage cost requires it.” Then identify migration compatibility and ownership. Design for failure without pre-installing every failure mechanism. Measure user SLIs, saturation, correctness/reconciliation and cost; run representative failure tests. The reusable decision rubric is (11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12), while SLO/error-budget decisions are in (20-observability-sre.slos-alerting-incidents.q1).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q5"},{"id":"25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q6","surface":"track","en":"Priority order for fintech money flows","bodyEn":"There is no universal product list or fixed order, but a money flow should make correctness and recovery dependencies explicit: Contract and threat model: identity, authorization, limits, currency/amount rules, idempotency scope, ambiguous outcomes and user-visible status. Atomic state transition: balanced double-entry ledger, uniqueness/version constraints and an immutable audit trail in one local transaction where possible. External ambiguity and reconciliation: provider idempotency/status lookup, durable workflow state, timeout handling, repair queues and operator controls. Reliable publication: Outbox in the business transaction, idempotent/version-aware consumers and explicit ordering/schema policy. Resource protection: end-to-end deadlines, bounded pools/queues, admission, retry budget, breaker/bulkhead and graceful degradation chosen per dependency. Detection and evidence: business invariants, reconciliation deltas, audit/security events, SLO burn, traces/log correlation and capacity signals. Scale and recovery: benchmark, failover/restore drills and evolve replicas/partitioning/proxies only when measured constraints require them. Managed components are options RDS Proxy, PgBouncer, Kafka, Redis or a service mesh can solve specific constraints; none is a mandatory rung in every fintech design. A proxy cannot fix an oversized aggregate connection budget or a slow query, and observability must exist from the first production slice rather than being postponed until after scaling. The dependencies matter more than the numbering: safe retries depend on idempotency; Outbox consumers depend on deduplication; reconciliation depends on durable identifiers and audit evidence; load shedding must preserve critical invariants. Validate the complete flow with duplicate/concurrent requests, PSP timeout-after-commit, broker redelivery, database failover and replay. Payment idempotency is canonical in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2), ambiguous PSP outcomes in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1).","contextEn":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track","href":"/topics/25-microservice#question-25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q6"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q1","surface":"track","en":"Why do heavily mocked unit tests break on every refactor even though behaviour never changed?","bodyEn":"A test should pin observable behaviour, not the path the code walks to produce it. Mock every collaborator and the test becomes a copy of the implementation: change the path and it fails while the system still works. Two failure modes tell you which mistake you made: test red, behaviour unchanged & 8594; test coupled to implementation — it verifies calls, not outcomes behaviour broken, tests green & 8594; the test asserts nothing — the mock returned the answer being checked Implementation test Behaviour test Asserts a call happened the resulting state Survives refactor no yes Catches real bugs rarely usually Reads like the code the requirement // implementation test — dies the day the service batches its writes @Test void places_order () { service.place(cmd); verify(repo).save(any(Order.class)); // a call, not an outcome } // // behaviour test — same requirement, survives the refactor @Test void placed_order_is_pending_and_holds_stock () { var id = service.place(cmd); assertThat(orders.find(id).status()).isEqualTo(PENDING); assertThat(stock.available(SKU)).isEqualTo( 9 ); } Where verify() still earns its place When the call itself is the requirement: an email sent, a payment captured, an event published. Everywhere else, assert on state. the twelve-mock smell: if arranging one unit test needs a dozen stubs, the class under test is orchestrating too much. The fix is in the design, not in the test. mocking a type you do not own (a driver, an SDK client) pins your assumption about that library. When the assumption is wrong, the test agrees with you and production does not. a test that never fails is not protection. Break the production line on purpose and confirm the test goes red — that is the cheap version of mutation testing (26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q5). naming carries the requirement: placed_order_is_pending survives review; testPlace1 tells the next reader nothing when it fails at 2am. Use risk and feedback cost, not a universal test shape: Choose the portfolio from the failure you are buying down and the time you can afford for feedback. Pure domain rules usually favour fast behaviour tests; SQL, locking, serialization, and commit claims need a real-engine boundary. Independently released messages need a contract; a revenue-critical deployed journey may need a small E2E or fault test. There is no honest unit/integration/E2E percentage that applies across providers, versions, or workloads. The \"twelve mocks\" rule is a smell detector, not a threshold. If a test needs twelve collaborators, ask whether the boundary is too broad or the fixture is too indirect. Keep the test when those interactions are the behaviour being protected. Test contract template: Field Record before writing the test Purpose Which risk or user-visible behaviour is protected? Boundary Which real or fake dependency boundary is crossed? Invariant What must be true, including the negative or duplicate case? Fixture Which isolated, minimal data and provider version are used? Time/randomness Which clock, timezone, seed, order, and deadline make it replayable? Fault Which timeout, reset, duplicate, or provider failure is injected? Cleanup How are rows, ports, files, messages, and containers released and verified? Evidence Which assertion, request count, trace, metric, or artifact proves the result? Owner Who maintains the test and decides when its scope or quarantine expires? What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The production database, broker, IdP and provider versions and the deployment topology the real integration suite must cover. The feedback-time budget and risk tiers that decide which tests run per change, pre-merge, post-merge, nightly and before production. Which domain invariants need real commit, unique-constraint, lock, fencing or provider-idempotency tests — and the permitted CI parallelism, container resources, test-data retention and secret-redaction rules. What falsifies the H2-or-fake shortcut: a production-only dialect, locking or schema bug, a contract mismatch, or a false-green migration test. What falsifies the chosen load method: an arrival model unlike production, throughput below target, a percentile method that hides queueing, or a saturated load generator. What falsifies flake quarantine: a quarantine that becomes permanent, a test hiding a product race, or a retry rate over the agreed CI budget.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q1"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q2","surface":"track","en":"Dummy, stub, spy, mock, fake — which double do you reach for, and why do seniors prefer fakes?","bodyEn":"The five are not synonyms. Picking the wrong one is how a suite becomes brittle. Double What it does Use it when Dummy fills a parameter, never used a constructor demands an argument you do not exercise Stub returns canned answers you need the collaborator to produce input Spy records what happened, real or partial you must assert an outbound side effect Mock pre-programmed with expectations the interaction itself is the requirement Fake a working, simplified implementation the port has real behaviour: storage, clock, queue A fake is the one that scales. An in-memory implementation of your repository port gives real semantics — write then read, unique keys collide, ordering holds — with none of the setup: class InMemoryOrders implements Orders { private final Map<OrderId, Order> db = new ConcurrentHashMap<>(); public void save (Order o) { if (db.putIfAbsent(o.id(), o) != null ) throw new DuplicateOrder(o.id()); } public Optional<Order> find (OrderId id) { return Optional.ofNullable(db.get(id)); } } Write it once per port and every use-case test gets faster and more honest than a pile of when(...).thenReturn(...). The fake must be tested too A fake that silently accepts duplicates while the real repository rejects them turns green tests into a production incident. Run the same contract test suite against both. classicist vs mockist: the classicist tests through real collaborators and mocks only the edges of the system; the mockist mocks every dependency. In backend code the classicist style ages better, because the edges are few and the internals move constantly. mock only what you own and control — your own ports. For a driver or SDK, prefer an integration test against the real thing in a container (26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q1). partial mocks (spy() on the class under test, stubbing one of its own methods) mean the class has two responsibilities. Split it instead. a fake for the clock is Clock.fixed(...); for the outbox, a list you can assert on; for the payment gateway, a scripted one that can be told to time out — that is the seam every failure test needs.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q2"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q3","surface":"track","en":"Which bugs never show up in unit tests, and what does that imply for where you spend test budget?","bodyEn":"Unit tests cover the code you wrote. Most production incidents come from the places where your code meets something else. The recurring blind spots: SQL and the mapping layer: a wrong join, a lazy association, a missing index, a dialect difference serialization at the boundary: an unknown field, a date format, a BigDecimal losing scale time and time zones: DST, a fixed Instant.now() in the code path, expiry compared across skewed clocks concurrency: two requests on the same row, a lost update, a race the single-threaded test cannot express configuration: the value that differs only in production, the profile nobody loads in CI failure paths: the timeout, the retry, the compensation that has never once been executed Layer What it can prove What it cannot Unit domain rules, branching, calculations that the query is correct or the schema matches Integration SQL, transactions, serialization, migrations that two services agree on a contract Contract producer and consumer still fit that the whole flow meets its latency budget End-to-end the critical path really works anything cheaply, or anything about rare branches That is why the pyramid is a cost argument, not a moral one: put the volume where feedback is fast, and buy a small number of expensive tests exactly where the money is — checkout, payment, auth (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q6). The question that reallocates budget Take the last five incidents. For each, ask which test would have caught it. The answer is rarely \"one more unit test\". an untested failure path is not a gap in coverage, it is dead code you are relying on. Compensation logic and DLQ handlers are the classic case (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2). coverage reports flatter you here: the happy path through a repository is covered, while the constraint violation it must translate never runs. keep one end-to-end test per revenue path and treat the rest as diagnostics. E2E suites that grow unbounded become the thing everyone reruns until it passes. if a bug escaped to production, the fix ships with the test that reproduces it — at the cheapest layer that can express it, not always end-to-end. Match each test tier to a risk and retain the evidence that makes its claim falsifiable: Test surface Can prove Blind spot Required evidence Behaviour unit domain rule and error policy database, wire, and runtime semantics observable invariant and focused inputs Sociable unit / fake orchestration and owned-port assumptions fake drift from the real provider the same contract run against fake and real implementation Real-engine integration SQL, locking, schema, serialization, and commit managed topology and deployment wiring pinned image/config, schema version, logs, and committed read-back Consumer/provider contract agreed request or event interaction workflow, auth, data, and third-party behaviour contract artifact plus provider verification result Component/API E2E a critical deployed path rare branches and broad failure coverage environment/config identity, business assertion, and trace Spend deeper-test budget where the incident history and blast radius justify it, then keep the faster tier for feedback. The table is a portfolio decision aid, not a prescribed count or ratio.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q3"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you build test data so the suite stays readable, and where does property-based testing pay off?","bodyEn":"Shared fixtures are the usual reason a suite becomes unreadable and flaky: every test silently depends on a row someone else inserted, and reordering tests changes results. Prefer local, explicit, minimal data: a builder per aggregate, with sane defaults, overriding only the field the test is about each test creates what it needs and owns it — no shared \"seed\" file the whole suite reads values that carry meaning: orderWith(total(\"120.00\"), currency(\"VND\")), never new Order(1, 2, \"x\", true, null) // the builder keeps the requirement visible: this test is about expiry only var hold = aSeatHold() .expiringAt(now.plus( 5 , MINUTES)) .build(); // // property-based: the rule must hold for every split, not for the one you imagined @Property void split_never_loses_a_cent ( @ForAll @IntRange (min= 1 , max= 50 ) int parts, @ForAll @BigRange (min= \"0.01\" , max= \"1000000\" ) BigDecimal total) { var shares = Money.of(total).splitInto(parts); assertThat(shares.stream().reduce(ZERO, BigDecimal::add)).isEqualByComparingTo(total); } Property-based testing pays off exactly where examples run out: rounding and money splits, serialization round-trips, parsers, pagination boundaries, idempotency keys, and any invariant of the form whatever the input, this must still hold. Random data without a seed is a flaky test Frameworks report the failing seed — capture it in the failure message and pin it as a regular example test once it finds a bug, otherwise the next run may not reproduce it. round-trip properties are the cheapest to write and catch the most: parse(render(x)) == x finds date-format, encoding and scale bugs that no hand-written example thought of. shrinking is the real value — the framework reduces a 40-field failing case to the minimal one, which is usually the bug report itself. for database tests, prefer per-test transactional cleanup or a fresh schema over a shared seed; ordering dependence is the second most common source of flakiness after time (26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q5). an object mother (Orders.pending(), Orders.paid()) reads better than a builder when the test needs a state, not a field. Use both: mother for the state, builder for the one deviation.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q4"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q1","surface":"track","en":"Why does an H2-backed test suite pass while production fails, and how do you run the real engine without wrecking build time?","bodyEn":"H2 is a different database wearing a compatibility mode. It agrees with you on SELECT, and diverges exactly where the interesting bugs live. Behaviour H2 in compatibility mode The real engine Locking no gap or next-key locks InnoDB gap locks change what deadlocks (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3) Query plan a different optimiser entirely the plan you tuned for Types looser: dates, numerics, JSON rejects what H2 accepted DDL migrations often need an H2 variant the migration you actually ship Upsert / window / CTE partial dialect coverage full Testcontainers removes the whole class of problem by starting the engine you deploy. The cost people fear is startup, and it is fixable: // one container for the whole JVM, reused by every test class abstract class DbTest { static final PostgreSQLContainer DB = new PostgreSQLContainer( \"postgres:18-alpine\" ); static { DB.start(); } // no @Container: do not stop per class @DynamicPropertySource static void props (DynamicPropertyRegistry r) { r.add( \"spring.datasource.url\" , DB::getJdbcUrl); r.add( \"spring.datasource.username\" , DB::getUsername); r.add( \"spring.datasource.password\" , DB::getPassword); } } Testcontainers 2.x Since 2.0 (2.0.5 as of 2026-10-26) the module artifacts carry a testcontainers- prefix (testcontainers-postgresql), and each container lives in its module's own package: org.testcontainers.postgresql.PostgreSQLContainer, no longer generic. The 1.x org.testcontainers.containers.PostgreSQLContainer<?> still compiles but is deprecated, so code copied from an older guide warns rather than fails. What makes it fast enough in CI: one shared container per JVM plus reuse locally, instead of one per test class run the real Flyway/Liquibase migrations once, then clean data between tests, not schema keep the DB tests in their own task so the unit suite still returns in seconds What an integration test is for Prove the things only the engine can prove: the SQL, the constraint, the migration, the transaction boundary. Business branching still belongs in fast unit tests. migrating with the same migration tool as production is half the value: a broken changelog fails in CI rather than during a deploy window (26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q2). clean between tests by truncating tables or restoring a template database; @Transactional rollback is faster but hides flush-time failures. containers are not only for SQL: Kafka, Redis, LocalStack and a mock OIDC provider all remove a class of \"works on my laptop\" surprise (03-spring-boot-deep-build.auto-configuration-build.q10). pin image tags. postgres:latest turns an unrelated upstream release into a red build on a Monday morning. the one thing H2 still buys you is a smoke test that the context loads. That is not worth the false confidence it grants elsewhere. Record the provider boundary instead of calling a container production-identical: Pin the selected database image by version or digest, migration tool/version, extensions, locale/timezone, schema volume, network mode, and resource limits. reuse can shorten local feedback, but CI must still guarantee ownership and cleanup. For parallel classes, use unique database/schema names, isolated ports or a resource lock; a shared container is not shared mutable state for free. Re-run the real-engine suite when the image, migration, schema, or managed-provider configuration changes.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q1"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q2","surface":"track","en":"Why does @Transactional on a test hide real bugs, and how do you test transaction boundaries honestly?","bodyEn":"A transactional test runs your code inside a transaction that never commits. That is convenient for cleanup and dishonest about everything that happens at commit time. What the rollback hides: constraint violations that only fire on flush or on COMMIT (deferred constraints, unique indexes) the persistence context papering over your query: an entity is returned from the first-level cache, so a broken SELECT still passes REQUIRES_NEW, @TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT) and outbox publishing, none of which behave the same way when the outer transaction is never committed (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q3) lock behaviour and deadlocks, since the test holds one long transaction instead of the short ones production runs // dishonest: passes even if the entity was never really written @Test @Transactional void saves_order () { orders.save(order); assertThat(orders.findById(id)).isPresent(); // served from the 1st-level cache } // // honest: force the write out, then read with fresh eyes @Test void saves_order () { txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(s -> orders.save(order)); // commits em.clear(); assertThat(orders.findById(id)).isPresent(); } The rule Use @Transactional on tests for read-only setup convenience. The moment the assertion is about persistence, commit for real and clear the persistence context first. to test propagation you need two real transactions: call the outer service through the proxy, make the inner one REQUIRES_NEW, throw after it, then assert the inner row survived and the outer one did not (03-spring-boot-deep-build.ioc-container-transactions.q2). self-invocation bugs are invisible to a mock-based test and obvious in an integration test — the annotation on the inner method simply does nothing. TestEntityManager flush then clear is the cheapest way to make an ORM test tell the truth: flush pushes the SQL, clear forces the next read to hit the database. assert on what the next request would see, not on the object you just handed to save(). Those two are different things, and every N+1 or stale-read bug lives in the gap. rollback-based cleanup also hides trigger and ON CONFLICT behaviour, which is exactly what idempotency relies on (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1). Make the boundary explicit in the test: For a read-back assertion, call flush() and clear() before reading through the repository, so the test checks SQL and a fresh persistence context. For a commit claim, execute the write in txTemplate.executeWithoutResult(...) or through the application boundary. Then observe the after-commit effect from a separate context or request. Spring TestContext transaction handling, JUnit timeout execution, and provider flush/constraint timing are version- and runner-sensitive; pin the selected versions and verify the actual runner rather than copying a default.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q2"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q3","surface":"track","en":"Your Spring test suite takes 14 minutes, mostly starting contexts. What is actually happening and how do you fix it?","bodyEn":"Spring caches an application context and reuses it across test classes — but the cache key is the whole configuration. Any difference mints a new context and pays the startup cost again. Everything below is part of that key: the set of @ContextConfiguration classes and locations active profiles and @TestPropertySource values @MockBean / @SpyBean declarations, including which beans they replace context initializers, @DirtiesContext on any test in the class Pattern Effect on the cache Do instead A different @MockBean combo per class one context per combination a small number of shared test configurations @DirtiesContext \"to be safe\" evicts the context, everything after pays again clean the state you dirtied Per-class properties new key per class one base class per profile @SpringBootTest for pure logic starts everything for a calculation plain JUnit, no container // one base per shape of test: two contexts total, not forty @SpringBootTest (webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT) @ActiveProfiles ( \"test\" ) abstract class AppTest extends DbTest { } // @WebMvcTest (OrderController.class) // slice: no DB, no Kafka, ~1s class OrderControllerTest { } The measurement that settles the argument Turn on context-loading logs and count how many times the banner prints. If it is more than the number of distinct configurations you intended, that number is your build-time bill. slice tests exist to keep the key small: @WebMvcTest builds MVC only, @DataJpaTest the persistence layer only. They start in about a second and cover serialization, validation and mapping — the parts most likely to break at the boundary. @MockBean is not free: besides forking the context, it re-introduces the implementation coupling that unit tests were supposed to avoid. Prefer a test configuration that supplies a fake bean. run test classes in parallel once contexts are shared and state is per-test; JUnit 5 parallelism turns a shared container from a bottleneck into a multiplier. keep the unit suite as a separate, always-run task and the container suite as a second stage. Fast feedback is the entire point of the pyramid (12-architecture-patterns.clean-layered-building-it-for-real.q6). one banner per configuration is the target. Everything else is cache misses you are paying for on every commit. Treat context caching and parallelism as versioned resource contracts: For the selected Spring and JUnit versions, record the context-cache key inputs, cache size/eviction setting, forked-JVM boundary, and the parallel execution mode. A test may share a cached context only when its beans are immutable or its state is reset; databases, ports, files, clocks, and message topics need per-test ownership or an explicit lock. Measure context-load count and resource contention before and after changing @DirtiesContext, @MockBean, profiles, or parallel settings; do not assume a setting from another framework version has the same semantics.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q3"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q4","surface":"track","en":"Two teams, two services, one broken deploy. How do contract tests catch it when neither side runs the other in CI?","bodyEn":"Spinning up the other team's service in your pipeline does not scale: you inherit their database, their fixtures and their outages. A contract test replaces the service with an agreement about the messages that cross the boundary. Consumer test Contract (pact / schema) Provider verification expects {id, total} ───────────────────────────────▶ replayed against the real provider The two shapes you will meet: consumer-driven contracts for request/response APIs: the consumer's test generates the contract; the provider's CI replays every consumer's expectations against the real implementation and fails before merge schema compatibility for events: the registry rejects a producer change that would break existing consumers — the same idea, enforced at publish time Change Compatible? Why Add an optional field yes old readers ignore it Remove a field a consumer reads no the reader loses data it depends on Rename a field no a removal and an addition in one deploy Narrow a type or add a required field no old writers produce invalid messages Add an enum value careful fine only if readers handle the unknown case What a contract test does not prove It proves the shape and the semantics you agreed on, not that the provider is fast enough, authorised correctly, or correct in its business logic. Those need their own tests. the contract lives with the consumer's expectations, which is what makes it useful: a provider learns which of the forty fields anyone actually reads, and can drop the rest safely. rolling deploys mean both versions run at once, so every change must be compatible in both directions for at least one release — the same expand/contract discipline as a schema migration (25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q1). for events, keep the registry check in CI rather than at runtime. Discovering incompatibility when the producer is already publishing is discovering it too late. versioning the whole API is the expensive escape hatch. Additive change plus a deprecation window handles the vast majority of cases without doubling the surface you support. record the contract for external partners too — a webhook payload is an API you can never take back once someone has parsed it (26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q3).","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q4"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q1","surface":"track","en":"How do you test logic that depends on time — expiry, schedules, time zones — without sleeping or waiting for midnight?","bodyEn":"Every Instant.now() scattered through the code is a hidden, untestable dependency. Inject a Clock and time becomes an argument you control. // the clock is a bean; production gets systemUTC, tests get whatever they need class SeatHolds { private final Clock clock; boolean isExpired (SeatHold h) { return h.expiresAt().isBefore(clock.instant()); } } // // no sleeping: move the clock instead of waiting for it var clock = Clock.fixed(Instant.parse( \"2026-03-29T00:59:00Z\" ), ZoneOffset.UTC); var later = Clock.offset(clock, Duration.ofMinutes( 6 )); assertThat( new SeatHolds(later).isExpired(hold)).isTrue(); Three time bugs worth a dedicated test each: expiry across a DST boundary — pick a zone that shifts and assert the duration, not the wall-clock difference a schedule expressed in local time running on a UTC server, especially the 00:30 job that runs twice or not at all comparison against a token or lock lease issued on another machine, where a few seconds of clock skew flips the result For anything genuinely asynchronous, waiting is still needed — but poll for the condition instead of guessing a duration: // flaky: passes on a fast laptop, fails on a loaded CI runner Thread.sleep( 500 ); assertThat(repo.findById(id)).isPresent(); // // deterministic in intent: bounded wait, fast exit, clear failure message await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds( 5 )) .pollInterval(Duration.ofMillis( 50 )) .untilAsserted(() -> assertThat(repo.findById(id)).isPresent()); The smell to grep for Thread.sleep in a test is either a race you have not understood or a wait you have not bounded. Both are future CI failures. store and compare in UTC, convert only at the edges. A test that passes only when the developer's machine is in one zone is a bug report about the code, not about the test. Clock.tick is useful when the code assumes monotonically increasing timestamps: a fixed clock can make two events share an instant and hide an ordering bug. for durations use a monotonic source (System.nanoTime) and never wall-clock subtraction, which can go backwards on an NTP correction. schedules deserve a test that runs the cron expression through the same parser production uses; \"every 5 minutes\" and \"at second 0 of every 5th minute\" differ on the day the job takes six minutes (26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q2). when the assertion is about something that must not happen, a bounded wait is the only honest tool — assert the absence after the window, and keep the window short. Use a time-test matrix, because a virtual clock does not model every clock: Inject a virtual or fixed Clock for expiry and advance it without sleeping. Add cases with explicit ZoneId and instants on both sides of a DST transition, and test local-time schedules through the same parser used by production. Use System.nanoTime or another monotonic source for elapsed-duration assertions. Clock.fixed makes one instant deterministic; it does not prove scheduler delivery, timezone conversion, monotonicity, persistence serialization, or clock-skew behaviour. Record the zone, instant, expected local result, and deadline in the test evidence.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q1"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do you write a test that actually reproduces a race, and why does a green concurrency test prove so little?","bodyEn":"A race needs two threads to collide in a narrow window. Starting them one after another almost never hits it — you have to release them together and repeat. // 50 threads, one seat: the invariant is that exactly one succeeds var start = new CountDownLatch( 1 ); var ok = new AtomicInteger(); try ( var pool = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) { IntStream.range( 0 , 50 ).forEach(i -> pool.submit(() -> { start.await(); // all threads wait on the same gate try { booking.hold(seatId); ok.incrementAndGet(); } catch (SeatTaken OptimisticLockException e) { / expected loser / } return null ; })); start.countDown(); // released together } assertThat(ok.get()).isEqualTo( 1 ); assertThat(seats.find(seatId).version()).isEqualTo( 1 ); What makes such a test worth having: assert an invariant, not a schedule: exactly one winner, stock never negative, no duplicate charge — not \"thread A ran first\" run it against the real database, because the guarantee comes from the unique index or the row lock, not from Java (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2) repeat it; a single pass on a quiet machine means nothing Green means \"not observed\", never \"impossible\" An interleaving that needs a 20-microsecond window may appear once in ten thousand runs, and only on a loaded CI machine. Concurrency tests find bugs; they do not prove their absence. reproduce first, then fix. A failing test that fails only sometimes is still evidence; add a repeat count and run it under load until the failure rate is stable enough to work with. correctness must come from a mechanism you can point at: a unique constraint, SELECT … FOR UPDATE, an optimistic @Version, or a single-writer partition. If the answer is \"we tested it a lot\", there is no mechanism. for low-level memory-model questions (visibility, reordering) hand-rolled tests are close to useless — JCStress exists precisely because the JVM is allowed to do things your test will never observe. instrument the loser path too: silently swallowing OptimisticLockException in a test is how a retry storm ships to production unnoticed (25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q5). deadlock tests belong here as well: two transactions taking the same rows in opposite orders, asserting that one is aborted rather than both hanging (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q4). Control the race schedule, then retain enough evidence to replay the miss: Use a barrier or latch to release contenders together, assert the domain invariant and the loser outcome, and run the scenario repeatedly with a bounded deadline. Record the repetition count, random seed or scheduling parameters, database/provider version, and the first failing interleaving. Stress tools such as jcstress target JVM memory-model questions; a database race still needs the real constraint, lock, or fencing authority. A passing repetition is evidence for that schedule and environment, not proof that every schedule is safe.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q2"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q3","surface":"track","en":"Your retry, timeout, circuit breaker and compensation logic has never run in a test. How do you inject failure on purpose?","bodyEn":"Resilience code is the least-executed code in the repository and the code you most depend on during an incident. It needs a seam where failure can be dialled in. Failure to inject Tool What it must prove HTTP 500 / 429 WireMock stub the error is translated, not leaked to the caller Slow response fixed delay above the read timeout the client gives up in time and the thread is released Connection reset / packet loss Toxiproxy in front of the container retry only fires on transient, idempotent calls Repeated failure a stub that fails N times the breaker opens, then half-opens and recovers Broker or DB down pause or stop the container backpressure and DLQ, not silent data loss // the timeout is the contract: prove the client honours it before the pool drains stubFor(post( \"/charges\" ).willReturn(aResponse().withFixedDelay( 5000 ))); // assertThatThrownBy(() -> payments.charge(cmd)) .isInstanceOf(GatewayTimeout.class); assertThat(hikari.getHikariPoolMXBean().getActiveConnections()).isZero(); verify(exactly( 1 ), postRequestedFor(urlEqualTo( \"/charges\" ))); // no blind retry on a charge The three assertions people forget: the resource is released — pool connections, threads, the HTTP connection itself the retry did not duplicate a side effect — a charge attempted twice must carry the same idempotency key (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1) the compensation actually ran and left the aggregate in a state the business accepts The order to write them in Happy path, then timeout, then duplicate delivery, then compensation. The last two are where money is lost, and they are the two nobody writes. test the breaker's state machine, not its configuration: N failures open it, calls fail fast while open, one probe succeeds after the wait, traffic resumes (25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q3). a compensation test must include the ugly case — compensation itself failing. What the system does then is a business decision that belongs in a test, not in a runbook nobody reads (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2). retry on a non-idempotent POST is the classic double-charge. The test that catches it counts requests reaching the stub, not just the final status code. chaos experiments in staging are the next step up, but they replace neither of these: an automated test fails a pull request, a chaos experiment fails a Wednesday afternoon. keep the fake gateway scriptable — succeed, time out, return a duplicate, return a late success — so the same harness covers webhook and callback paths too. Give every injected fault an effect, deadline, and recovery assertion: Compose a single deadline and retry budget across client, library, and worker layers; record remaining deadline, attempt count, and the idempotency key on every attempt. For each timeout, reset, 500/429, broker stop, or duplicate response, assert resource return, no duplicate business effect, and the required compensation or escalation. A WireMock/Toxiproxy stub proves the selected seam; run a real dependency or transport scenario when connection reuse, transaction state, or broker recovery is part of the claim.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q3"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q4","surface":"track","en":"A load test reports p99 = 80 ms while users complain. What is the test lying about?","bodyEn":"Most load tests measure a system that is politely waiting for them. Three things usually explain the gap between a green report and an angry user. 1. A closed model hides the queue. A closed-loop tool with 50 virtual users sends the next request only after the previous one returns. The service slows down, the tool slows down with it, and arrival rate drops exactly when you needed it held constant. Real users do not behave that way. Use an open model with a fixed arrival rate and watch the queue grow. 2. Coordinated omission. If a request stalls for 3 seconds, the requests that would have been sent during that window are never sent, and never counted. The worst latencies are simply missing from the histogram. Tools that keep a fixed schedule (constant-arrival-rate, corrected recording) close that hole. 3. Everything else is warm and empty. A JIT that has not compiled, a cache that starts empty and a database holding 1,000 rows instead of 50 million benchmark a system you do not operate (04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q1). Test Question it answers Run it when Load does the target rate hold at the target latency? before every capacity change Stress where does it break, and how? to find the real bottleneck Spike does it survive a sudden 10x? flash sale, campaign, retry storm Soak does it leak or degrade over hours? before a long weekend release Averages are marketing Report p95, p99 and the error rate together with the achieved throughput. A p99 that looks good because half the requests failed fast is the most common false green. always record achieved rate vs target rate. If the tool wanted 500 rps and delivered 300, latency numbers describe a system serving 300 — the test found saturation and then measured the wrong thing. watch the resource that saturates first, not the one you suspect: DB pool waiting time, thread pool queue depth, GC pause, Kafka lag (25-microservice.02-pool-exhaustion-connection-thread.q1). warm up before measuring and keep the JIT profile realistic — a benchmark that never triggers C2 compilation reports startup performance forever. a load test with one hot key and a load test with a realistic key distribution answer different questions; production usually has the hot key (25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q1). end every run with an answer to \"what is the bottleneck now?\". A number without that sentence changes nothing. A load result is incomplete until its workload and histogram are named: Report the target and achieved arrival rate, open or closed model, arrival process, warm-up and steady-state window, payload/cache/key distribution, error and timeout rate, and resource saturation. Record load-generator capacity and the percentile/histogram method for p50/p95/p99 (and p999 when useful). Compare open and closed runs when queueing matters. If achieved throughput is below target, label the run as a saturation or generator result; do not present its p99 as proof that the target workload met the latency objective.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q4"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q5","surface":"track","en":"A test fails once a week and passes on rerun. What is the policy, and why is high coverage no defence?","bodyEn":"A flaky test is worse than a missing one: it trains the team to rerun the pipeline instead of reading it, and the first real regression gets rerun away with it. Where flakiness comes from, in the order you should check: time and waiting: Thread.sleep, a real clock, a timeout tuned to a fast laptop shared state: a database row, a static field, a container reused without cleanup, test ordering concurrency in the test itself, or an assertion racing an async handler the environment: a fixed port, DNS, an external endpoint, an image tag that moved Signal Reality 90% line coverage lines executed; nothing about assertions Branch coverage closer, still blind to missing assertions Mutation score mutants introduced in the code that tests failed to kill Escaped defects the only metric the business feels A suite can execute every line while asserting nothing: coverage measures reach, not verification. Mutation testing flips the question. Change >= to >, delete a line, and see whether any test notices. High coverage with a low mutation score is the signature of tests that run the code and check nothing. A policy that works Quarantine on the second failure: the test moves out of the blocking suite the same day, with an owner and a deadline. Nothing gets muted quietly, and the main pipeline stays trustworthy. track flakiness as data — rerun history per test, sorted by failure rate. Without the list, everyone remembers a different culprit. reproduce with the same seed, order and parallelism. Randomised execution order is worth enabling exactly because it surfaces hidden coupling early. do not add a retry annotation to the whole suite. Retrying at the framework level converts a design problem into permanent noise. run mutation testing on the domain package only, where it is cheap and the results are actionable; running it over the whole codebase is where teams try it once and give up. CI hygiene closes the loop: fixed test ordering off, parallelism on, containers pinned, one artifact promoted through environments, and every production bug arriving with the test that reproduces it (26-testing-strategy.what-a-test-is-for.q3). Make flaky handling observable and non-silent: After the second independent failure, quarantine the test from the blocking suite with an owner, an expiry date, a tracking issue, and a remediation path. Preserve the first and latest failure, rerun count, seed, test order, parallelism, environment/image/provider versions, and diagnostic artifacts. A bounded diagnostic retry may collect evidence, but it must not turn the result green silently. Report a separate flake rate over a stated window and distinguish it from product-failure rate. Remove quarantine only after a fix, reproducible explanation, or an explicit reviewed decision.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q5"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q6","surface":"track","en":"How do you test a distributed failure state with timeout-after-commit, retries, and an unknown outcome?","bodyEn":"Test the state machine and the crash window, not only the final HTTP status. Build a matrix for failure before the authority commit, after commit but before the response, during provider processing, during consumer apply, and during checkpoint/acknowledgement. For each cell, assert: the caller receives success, pending, failure, or unknown according to the API contract the same idempotency key and request fingerprint cannot create two business effects retries stop at the composed deadline/attempt budget and do not multiply across layers a reconciliation/status query can discover an UNKNOWN provider or worker result duplicate/out-of-order events are harmless or explicitly quarantined compensation/manual escalation is visible when forward recovery is impossible Use the parts that can actually fail: a real database for transaction/constraint behavior a provider stub that can delay or lose responses a consumer that can crash between side effect and checkpoint Repeat the test concurrently and after restart. A unit test that asserts a mock was called once does not prove any of these boundaries. The oracle is the domain invariant and the durable evidence: one booking/charge/ledger effect, no lost accepted work, a traceable status, and a repair path. Record attempt count, deadline remaining, event identity, checkpoint, and reconciliation result.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q6"},{"id":"26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q7","surface":"track","en":"What must a restore, replay, or cutover test prove before you call a system recoverable?","bodyEn":"A backup file or green job is only an input. A recovery test must exercise the path a real operator would use and measure it against the stated RPO/RTO. Minimum evidence: identify the exact backup/snapshot and log/WAL/CDC position, retention, encryption, and access path restore into an isolated environment with the application schema/version, extensions, indexes, configuration, secrets, roles, certificates, and dependent services verify row/control totals, checksums where meaningful, reference constraints, idempotency state, deletes/tombstones, and business invariants replay or catch up queues/events with duplicate/out-of-order behavior, then run a user-path smoke test measure restore, replay, traffic return, backlog drain, and observed data-loss windows; compare with RPO/RTO test rollback/abort and cleanup, preserve an evidence report, and assign the repair of every failed step For a migration or reindex, keep the old version serving until the new target passes parity and freshness checks, then stage traffic and retain rollback. For a disaster drill, include the configuration and identity plane; restoring data without the ability to authenticate or route traffic is not service recovery. Schedule the drill and repeat it after schema, provider, topology, backup, or deployment changes. “We restored it once” is evidence for that version and scope, not a permanent guarantee.","contextEn":"Testing — what proves the system works, and what only looks like proof","href":"/topics/26-testing-strategy#question-26-testing-strategy.testing-what-usually-breaks.q7"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.the-edge-in-four-sizes.q1","surface":"track","en":"How do you turn 10k, 100k, 1M and 100M requests a day into the peak rate the edge is sized for, and what does one extra hop cost at each tier?","bodyEn":"Start with average traffic: requests per day divided by 86,400. For a consumer or campaign workload, use roughly a 20× peak heuristic and state that it is an assumption, not a capacity guarantee. T1: 10k req/day is 0.12 average rps, so plan around 2.5 peak rps. One gateway hop is mostly a routing and connection-management choice; a second proxy is usually more operational cost than capacity benefit. T2: 100k req/day is 1.2 average rps, so plan around 25 peak rps. A shared gateway can add authentication, rate limiting and metrics, but measure p99 latency with the real payload and TLS path. T3: 1M req/day is 11.6 average rps, so plan around 250 peak rps. Replicas, connection pools, distributed limits and dependency budgets matter; a hop also consumes sockets and retry budget. T4: 100M req/day is 1,157 average rps, so plan around 25,000 peak rps. Treat it as cells or regional slices, not one giant gateway cluster; each cell should be a T3-to-waypoint system plus routing. The 10M/day waypoint is about 116 average rps and 2,500 peak rps. It is often where a single regional fleet becomes a cell design. The same 1M/day anchor appears in (11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q13); keep the arithmetic consistent. An extra hop costs more than its median processing time: it adds queueing, a connection pool, health state, tracing spans, timeout accounting and another failure boundary. Set a per-hop latency budget, then load-test p99 and saturation. For internal or B2B traffic with a measured 3–5× concentration, do not blindly apply 20×. Recompute from the arrival trace and keep the peak assumption beside the decision. Sizing card — declare these inputs before choosing a tier: Arrival: measured peak rps, burst duration, open-loop model, and tenant/route skew. Request shape: payload size, compression, streaming ratio, and HTTP version. Connections: new TLS rate, keep-alive reuse, upstream pool and connection-acquisition p99. Identity/dependencies: JWT or opaque-token mix, JWKS/introspection hit rate, PDP calls, and downstream concurrency. Contract: end-to-end p99/p999 deadline, accepted overage, retry budget, and failure mode. The T1–T4 labels are scenario names, not hard capacity breakpoints. Keep the measured inputs and the 20× multiplier beside the sizing decision. Edge contract For each route, record the owner, trusted ingress, identity source, authorization owner, per-hop deadline, retry budget, error contract, and rollback owner. What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The gateway implementation and release train in scope — Spring Cloud Gateway WebFlux, Envoy, a managed edge, a Kubernetes Gateway API implementation, or a combination. Whether it is public, private, mesh-local or all three, with the paths that can bypass it and the owner of each trust boundary. The p99 and p999 end-to-end deadline, connection reuse, payload, token mix and downstream concurrency targets at each tier. Which routes carry mutations where a 504 can mean \"committed but unknown\", and the idempotency-key or status-lookup contract behind them. The maximum JWT, JWKS, PDP or policy-cache staleness after a revocation or emergency deny. Whether IdP, introspection or PDP failure fails closed, serves a bounded cached read, or enters an explicit degraded mode per route class; the policy runtime, bundle delivery, decision cache and version, and who can roll it back. What falsifies the edge boundary: gateway p99 eating more than its hop budget, a shared pool failure taking down unrelated routes, or an internal identity header accepted from direct service ingress, batch workers or cross-region calls that were never tested.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.the-edge-in-four-sizes.q1"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.the-edge-in-four-sizes.q2","surface":"track","en":"At each traffic tier, which edge capability is not needed, worth it, mandatory, or needs its own scaling story?","bodyEn":"Use the same four verdicts for every control: not needed means the operating cost dominates, worth it is cheap insurance, mandatory prevents a predictable incident, and needs its own scaling story means a config line is no longer enough. T1 — 10k/day: CDN for public static assets is worth it; a managed gateway can route and authenticate. A distributed Redis limiter, PDP and multi-region identity plane are usually not needed. T2 — 100k/day: rate limiting, timeout budgets, circuit breaking and route-level RED metrics are worth it. Keep identity verification local to the gateway and services; a separate policy service is not mandatory unless policy churn or audit requires it. T3 — 1M/day: gateway replicas across zones, bounded connection pools, a shared limiter, JWKS cache, trace propagation and dependency isolation are mandatory. IdP failover, policy decision caching and asynchronous audit may need their own scaling story. T4 — 100M/day: cells, regional routing, independent gateway fleets, regional identity/key distribution, policy bundles and blast-radius controls need their own scaling story. A single global Redis limiter or one global PDP is a hidden critical path. The verdict is about the failure model as well as rps. A low-volume payment API may require stronger isolation than a high-volume anonymous read. State the protected resource, its abuse cost and the owner before selecting the control.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.the-edge-in-four-sizes.q2"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.the-edge-in-four-sizes.q3","surface":"track","en":"Which signals say a tier boundary has been crossed before the incident does?","bodyEn":"A tier boundary is crossed when the current shape cannot meet its SLO with ordinary headroom, not when a dashboard has a round number. Look for several signals moving together: Queueing: gateway event-loop delay, upstream connection acquisition, pending requests and p99 latency rise while average CPU still looks healthy. Dependency pressure: usable connection pools, Redis operations, JWKS refreshes, PDP latency and downstream concurrency approach a hard budget. Failure feedback: 429, 5xx, 504, retry/original ratio, circuit-open time and client cancellation increase; adding replicas no longer lowers the tail. Blast radius: one tenant, route, region or identity provider incident consumes shared pools or makes unrelated routes fail. At the 10M/day waypoint, ask whether a single fleet can still be upgraded and recovered as one unit. Near T4, ask whether a cell can fail without taking the region with it. Define the next action before the threshold: increase capacity, split a pool, introduce admission control, move to cells or isolate a dependency. Benchmark with production-shaped payloads, token mix, cache hit rate and retry behavior. A synthetic open-loop rps number that omits connection churn can postpone the boundary until the incident.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.the-edge-in-four-sizes.q3"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.gateway-configuration-that-matters.q1","surface":"track","en":"In Spring Cloud Gateway, what does a request pass through in route, predicate and filter order, and where should authentication sit?","bodyEn":"A route is a destination plus an ordered filter chain. Predicates decide whether the route matches; filters transform the request or response, enforce policy, and forward or short-circuit it. The exact ordering is part of the contract, so inspect the effective route rather than relying on the order in a YAML file. Authentication belongs before the request reaches a protected service, but it does not have to be the first operation. Reject malformed or oversized input early, establish correlation and trace context, then authenticate before route-specific authorization and forwarding. Do not let a rewrite or a client-controlled header change the identity that is verified. Spring Cloud Gateway 5.0.4 documents distinct Server WebFlux and Server Web MVC starters: spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux and spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webmvc. The WebFlux server is built on WebFlux, Reactor and Netty; choose the server flavor deliberately instead of copying the older spring-cloud-starter-gateway artifact into a new build. Record the chosen release train in the build and test the actual filter order. Use a supported Java LTS runtime; at this review JDK 25 is the current LTS reference. Verify vendor patch support against the selected Spring Boot/Cloud train before rollout. The gateway may make a coarse decision such as scope=orders:read and route ownership. The service still authenticates the caller at its own trust boundary and performs object-level authorization. This is the edge-specific application of (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q1). Effective-chain regression: pin the gateway server flavor and release, then render or inspect the effective chain. Assert the expected order for a normal request, an authentication rejection, an oversized body, and a rewrite. The YAML order is configuration input, not a portable proof of runtime order.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.gateway-configuration-that-matters.q1"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.gateway-configuration-that-matters.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should gateway, downstream and client timeouts relate, and why is the edge the fastest place to build a retry storm?","bodyEn":"Give the client an end-to-end deadline, then spend it from the outside in. The gateway deadline must leave time for response handling, while each downstream deadline is shorter than the gateway budget and includes connection acquisition. A client retry deadline must not cause a fresh request after the server has already committed an effect. A retry budget limits retries as a fraction of original traffic over a window. Retry only errors that are classified transient, only when the operation is safe or idempotent, and add exponential backoff with jitter. A gateway retry multiplies every downstream hop, so a 2× retry at two layers can approach 4× work before the client is counted. Keep retries at one deliberate owner for a hop. The gateway should normally retry a new connection failure or a bounded 502 before bytes reach the service; it should not blindly replay a timed-out POST. Carry an idempotency key when replay is part of the contract. The mechanism and storm failure modes belong to (25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q5); this edge question is about the budget and filter placement. Return a stable timeout/overload contract. 504 means the gateway did not receive a usable upstream response inside its deadline; it does not prove that the service did nothing. Spring Cloud Gateway 5.0.4 timeout shape (values are illustrative): spring: cloud: gateway: server: webflux: httpclient: connect-timeout: 500 milliseconds response-timeout: 2s global Duration routes: id: orders uri: http://orders.internal:8080 predicates: Path=/api/orders/ metadata: connect-timeout: 300 per-route milliseconds response-timeout: 1500 Load-test a slow upstream and verify the gateway stops near its 1.5-second route budget while the external client deadline leaves response margin. Benefit: bounded pool occupancy and a predictable tail. Cost: a tight value creates false timeouts and retries; cancellation does not undo a committed downstream effect. Replay rule: do not retry this POST route unless its idempotency contract supports replay. Timeout crash-window table: Window Observed result Required action Accepted, upstream not reached Retryable rejection or no durable effect Return retryable error or persist a command; do not claim success. Upstream reached, response lost Client sees timeout or 504 ; effect may have committed Query by idempotency key or status resource; do not blindly replay. Retry after partial work Attempts overlap Reuse the operation identity, deduplicate downstream, and stay inside the retry budget.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.gateway-configuration-that-matters.q2"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.gateway-configuration-that-matters.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do CircuitBreaker and RequestRateLimiter filters change gateway behaviour, and what is the per-tier verdict?","bodyEn":"A CircuitBreaker filter must be scoped to a route or a deliberately grouped dependency. Its failure predicate, sliding window, minimum calls, failure/slow-call thresholds, open wait and half-open probe count decide when traffic stops; a shared breaker for unrelated routes turns one bad backend into a larger outage. Open should return a documented 503 or a safe fallback, with a metric that names the route and cause. RequestRateLimiter needs a stable KeyResolver, a cost model and a burst policy. With the Redis token-bucket implementation, replenishRate is sustained tokens per second, burstCapacity is the bucket, and requestedTokens lets an expensive operation spend more than one token. The Redis check is a distributed dependency, not magic capacity. Return 429 and Retry-After only when the delay is meaningful and derived from policy; make the client contract explicit. T1: local or managed rate limiting is worth it; a shared Redis limiter is not needed unless abuse risk demands it. T2: route-level limits and a breaker are worth it; keep policies few and inspectable. T3: per-tenant or weighted limits, Redis HA, breaker isolation and retry budgets are mandatory. T4: limiter state, policy distribution and breaker ownership need their own scaling story, usually per cell or region. Resilience4j 2.4.0 exposes the same conceptual controls in-process, but a gateway filter still has to choose route scope and distributed state. 2.4.0 is also the release that adds Spring Boot 4 support, through a separate resilience4j-spring-boot4 starter. Confirm the BOM actually resolves that artifact for the Spring Cloud train you pinned rather than assuming the umbrella dependency covers it. The breaker state machine and its parameter set are owned by (25-microservice.01-cascading-failure-retry-storm.q3); what the edge adds is route scope and a distributed limiter. Do not paste service-level defaults into the edge; compare the edge decision with (10-system-design-rate-limit.rate-limiting-in-depth.q5). Rate-limit a verified tenant claim, never a public header: @Bean KeyResolver tenantKeyResolver() { return exchange -> exchange.getPrincipal() .filter(JwtAuthenticationToken.class::isInstance) .cast(JwtAuthenticationToken.class) .flatMap(a -> Mono.justOrEmpty(a.getToken().getClaimAsString(\"tenant_id\"))) .filter(tenant -> !tenant.isBlank()); // empty keys are denied by default } application.yml (under spring.cloud.gateway.server.webflux.routes) id: checkout uri: http://checkout.internal:8080 predicates: [Path=/api/checkout/] filters: name: RequestRateLimiter args: key-resolver: \" {@tenantKeyResolver}\" redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 10 redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 20 redis-rate-limiter.requestedTokens: 1 burst test: some requests beyond capacity should return 429 seq 1 25 xargs -P25 -I{} curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' https://edge/api/checkout/x Impact: Benefit: all gateway replicas spend one shared tenant budget; anonymous requests cannot choose another tenant's bucket. Cost: Redis joins the request path, hot tenants create hot keys, and policy needs an explicit fail-open/closed mode. Scale boundary: scope state per region/cell when a global round trip or blast radius is unacceptable.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.gateway-configuration-that-matters.q3"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.gateway-configuration-that-matters.q4","surface":"track","en":"What connection-pool and request-hygiene settings matter at the edge, and what must the gateway strip or never trust?","bodyEn":"Netty connection pools trade sockets and memory for reuse. Size pending acquisition, max connections, keep-alive and idle eviction from measured upstream concurrency and service limits, not from gateway replica count. A pool that is too small queues at the edge; one that is too large moves the queue into the database or partner. Set an explicit maximum body and header size, reject malformed framing, and choose buffering only when a filter actually needs the body. A large upload or response held in memory can turn one request into a heap incident. CORS is a browser contract, not an authorization control; allow only the origins and methods the product owns. Strip inbound identity and routing headers before adding trusted values. X-Forwarded-, user, tenant, role, internal route and correlation headers are untrusted when supplied by the client. Rebuild them from the verified connection, token and gateway trace context. Preserve only the headers the downstream contract needs. Keep-alive and idle eviction must match load balancers and services. After scale-out, a fleet of cold instances can create a connection storm; warm pools gradually and watch connect rate, pending acquisition, event-loop delay and upstream resets. Configuration defaults change across Spring Cloud Gateway and Netty versions, so verify the selected 5.0.4 line and benchmark the deployment. Bound the pool and erase spoofable identity before routing (illustrative limits): spring: cloud: gateway: server: webflux: trusted-proxies: '10\\.20\\.[0-9]{1,3}\\.[0-9]{1,3}' httpclient: connect-timeout: 500 response-timeout: 2s pool: type: fixed max-connections: 200 acquire-timeout: 200 max-idle-time: 30000 metrics: true default-filters: RemoveRequestHeader=X-User RemoveRequestHeader=X-Tenant RemoveRequestHeader=X-Roles RequestHeaderSize=16KB RequestSize=2MB Only a verified token or signed internal assertion may recreate identity after these removals. Benefit: bounded sockets, bounded acquisition wait and a smaller smuggling/spoofing surface. Cost: low limits reject healthy bursts; high limits move the queue downstream. Sizing rule: derive 200 from measured upstream concurrency and correlate pool active/pending with route p99 and capacity.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.gateway-configuration-that-matters.q4"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.identity-at-the-edge.q1","surface":"track","en":"Who verifies the token: gateway only, service only, or both? What does each choice cost and leave open?","bodyEn":"Gateway-only verification is cheap for the service path and centralizes rejection, but it makes the gateway a trusted security oracle. A bypass, misroute or internal caller that reaches the service can skip the check unless the service has another boundary. Service-only verification keeps the resource owner authoritative and supports calls that do not traverse the edge, but every service pays verification cost and must implement consistent issuer, audience, key and time validation. It also lets bad traffic consume more downstream work before rejection. The practical default is both, with different jobs. The gateway verifies the bearer token and applies coarse route policy; the service verifies the original token or a signed internal assertion, then performs tenant and object authorization. Cache public keys, not an unbounded authorization decision. If a service trusts a gateway-issued identity, bind it to audience, expiry, route and a key the service validates; never trust a conventionally forwarded header. This is defense in depth, not permission to configure two independent policy engines that disagree. Define one owner for each decision and measure the added p99 verification cost. The identity provider and token contract are covered in (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q1).","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.identity-at-the-edge.q1"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.identity-at-the-edge.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should the edge verify a JWT, including JWKS fetch, kid rotation, clock skew, iss, aud and exp?","bodyEn":"Treat a JWT as untrusted bytes until all relevant checks pass. Decode only to select a candidate key; verify the signature with an allowed algorithm, then validate iss, aud, exp, nbf and the token type/scope required by the route. kid selects a key inside the issuer's JWKS; it is not an authorization decision. Cache the issuer metadata and JWKS with bounded freshness. On an unknown kid, refresh once with backoff and protect the refresh path from a key-id spray. Keep old and new signing keys available during a rotation overlap. If the JWKS endpoint is unavailable, continue only with a still-valid cached key under a documented risk policy; do not accept an unverified token because the IdP is down. Allow only measured clock skew. A generous skew extends token lifetime and weakens revocation, while zero skew rejects legitimate requests near a boundary. Reject issuer confusion and algorithm substitution; RFC 8725 is the normative JWT guidance. OpenID Connect Discovery defines the issuer and jwks_uri relationship, and Spring Security's resource-server reference describes the validation sequence. Signature verification is not a fixed number of microseconds. RS256 versus ES256, token size, JIT state, key cache hit, CPU generation and concurrency change it. Benchmark accepted and rejected tokens at the target p99, then budget the result per tier. Minimal reactive resource-server boundary with issuer, audience and route scope: application.yml spring: security: oauth2: resourceserver: jwt: issuer-uri: https://idp.example.test/realms/prod audiences: api://edge @Bean SecurityWebFilterChain security(ServerHttpSecurity http) { return http.authorizeExchange(auth -> auth .pathMatchers(\"/actuator/health\").permitAll() .pathMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, \"/api/orders/\") .hasAuthority(\"SCOPE_orders.read\") .anyExchange().authenticated()) .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults())) .build(); } Test three paths: no token → 401; valid signature but wrong issuer/audience → 401; valid token without orders.read → 403. Benefit: signature and cached-JWKS verification stay local while key rotation remains discoverable. Cost: local JWT acceptance lasts until expiry; logout/revocation needs short TTL or another mechanism. Boundary: the service still owns object-level authorization.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.identity-at-the-edge.q2"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.identity-at-the-edge.q3","surface":"track","en":"When does opaque-token introspection make sense, and how should identity be passed inward without trusting a client header?","bodyEn":"Opaque tokens give the authorization server a live revocation point, but every cache miss creates an IdP dependency and a latency tail. Cache introspection only with a TTL shorter than the token's accepted lifetime and an explicit fail-open or fail-closed rule. JWT verification pays local crypto cost but can remain valid after logout until expiry or another revocation mechanism acts. Token relay is simple: the service receives the original bearer token and verifies it. It preserves the original issuer/audience semantics but spreads token handling and sends a user credential across more hops. A signed internal claim is narrower: the gateway signs sub, tenant, scopes, route audience, issue time and expiry for the service. The service verifies that assertion and rejects a token made for another service. Never accept X-User, X-Tenant or X-Roles from the public request. Strip them at the edge, add trusted identity only after verification, and use mTLS or a workload identity between gateway and service so an arbitrary internal process cannot forge the channel. A header is a transport representation; the signature and trust path are the boundary. Choose per route. High-risk money actions may prefer live introspection or service-local verification; high-volume reads may use short-lived JWTs and cached keys. The trade-off is the subject of (13-security-oauth2.jwt-tokens.q2), not a reason to duplicate its token taxonomy here. Layer Good fit Still required JWT Local verification after a warm key cache Expiry, issuer/audience checks, and service-side resource authorization Opaque introspection Central freshness or revocation decision Introspection deadline, outage mode, and cache staleness bound mTLS/SPIFFE Workload-to-workload identity Credential rotation and end-user/application authorization Application authorization Tenant, object, and state-transition decision Trusted identity input and an authoritative resource check Accept forwarded identity only from a configured trusted proxy chain. Test public, private, direct-service, batch-worker and cross-region ingress separately. Send forged X-User / X-Tenant / X-Roles headers and expect strip-or-reject behaviour. Rotate keys and verify that tokens, authorization headers and sensitive policy input are absent from logs and metric labels.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.identity-at-the-edge.q3"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.identity-at-the-edge.q4","surface":"track","en":"How should revocation, logout and IdP failure work at the edge, and what changes at each tier?","bodyEn":"Short access-token TTL is the baseline revocation bound, not a logout guarantee. A deny-list gives immediate invalidation but adds a read and an eviction/retention problem. A BFF or edge session can hide tokens from a browser and centralize logout, but it creates session state and a high-availability edge store. Choose from the risk of a stolen token, not from a generic security checklist. When the IdP is unavailable, already cached JWKS can still verify an unexpired JWT; an introspection-only path cannot safely invent a positive answer. Fail closed for money movement, privilege changes and sensitive reads. A low-risk public or previously authorized read may serve a bounded stale session if the product explicitly accepts that risk. Never fail open merely because the gateway wants availability. Rotate keys with overlap: publish the new key before signing with it, accept both during the maximum token lifetime plus propagation margin, then retire the old key after observing zero legitimate use. Refresh unknown kid values once, rate-limit misses and alert on a sudden key-id distribution. T1/T2: short JWT TTL plus cached JWKS is often worth it; a global deny-list is usually not needed for low-risk routes. T3: regional IdP/JWKS redundancy, refresh protection and route-specific fail-closed policy are mandatory. T4: identity, key distribution, revocation and session state need their own scaling story per region or cell. RFC 9700 recommends modern OAuth security practices, but it does not choose the product's availability mode. That decision remains a route risk and business contract.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.identity-at-the-edge.q4"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.authorization-without-a-bottleneck.q1","surface":"track","en":"What may the gateway authorize coarsely, what must stay in the service, and where does tenant resolution belong?","bodyEn":"The gateway can authorize decisions that are visible from the request contract: token is valid, route is in the audience, scope allows orders:read, method is permitted, and the caller is inside a coarse tenant or product boundary. These checks are useful before spending downstream capacity. The service owns domain decisions: whether this user may read this order, whether the tenant owns the object, whether the account is suspended, and whether the requested state transition is legal. The gateway should not fetch domain rows just to decide a policy; that turns it into a second application with a wider blast radius. Resolve tenant from a verified claim, mTLS/workload context or an explicitly mapped host, then let the service confirm membership and object ownership. A client-supplied tenant_id is only a request hint until it matches the authenticated subject's allowed tenancy. Never use a URL path alone as proof of tenant identity. Keep the same rule for gateway bypasses and east-west calls. The owner service must be able to enforce its invariant without assuming that a particular proxy was in the path. This is the gateway-specific application of (13-security-oauth2.designing-the-auth-system.q6).","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.authorization-without-a-bottleneck.q1"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.authorization-without-a-bottleneck.q2","surface":"track","en":"Should roles live in the token or in a policy decision point such as OPA or Cedar, and what can decision caching safely accept?","bodyEn":"Roles in a token are fast and available during an IdP outage, but they become stale until the token expires and are a poor fit for resource attributes or rapidly changing entitlements. A PDP call can evaluate tenant, resource, action and context centrally, but it adds latency, availability coupling and a policy deployment path. Use the token for stable coarse facts such as subject, tenant, audience and broad scopes. Use a service-local check or PDP for dynamic roles, ownership, time, geography and resource state. OPA/Rego or Cedar is a policy choice, not a replacement for a service's invariant; the service still supplies trustworthy input and owns the final state transition. Cache a decision only with a key containing subject or principal, tenant, action, resource class, policy version and relevant context. Set a TTL from the harm of stale allow, not from cache convenience. Invalidate on role removal for high-risk actions, or choose a short fail-closed cache. A stale deny usually costs availability; a stale allow can be a security incident. Audit the decision without logging bearer tokens or sensitive payloads: principal pseudonym, tenant, action, resource class, policy version, decision, reason code, trace id and timestamp are usually enough. Record policy changes separately so an auditor can explain which rule produced an allow. Placement Useful when Failure contract Local bundle Predictable p99 and operation during PDP outage Version the bundle and roll out/roll back it as code. Remote PDP Dynamic policy and centralized audit Bound decision latency; choose fail closed or an explicitly limited degraded mode. Decision cache Repeated decisions absorb dependency load Key by relevant context, record policy version, and bound stale allow TTL. Roll out policy with a version/hash, shadow or compare decisions where safe, and alert when the active version differs across gateway replicas. A stale deny is normally an availability problem; a stale allow is a security problem, so the route owner must choose the failure mode per action.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.authorization-without-a-bottleneck.q2"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.introducing-a-gateway-into-a-running-system.q1","surface":"track","en":"What belongs at the CDN, L4 load balancer, gateway, mesh and service, and what happens when the same control is configured twice?","bodyEn":"Give each layer a narrow owner: CDN: cacheable public bytes, edge TLS and coarse abuse protection. It should not decide a private order's ownership. L4 load balancer: anycast/health-based transport distribution and connection termination where appropriate. Gateway: L7 route matching, external authentication, coarse authorization, public rate limits, request normalization and edge observability. Mesh: east-west mTLS, service identity, traffic policy and transport-level telemetry when the organization can operate the control plane. Service: domain authorization, invariants, idempotency and state transitions. Duplicate controls do not automatically add safety. Two retries multiply work, two timeouts create ambiguous ownership, two rate limiters make capacity unpredictable, and two identity transforms can disagree about the principal. Put one owner and one contract in the design, then make other layers enforce only a compatible guardrail. A CDN can reject obvious abuse before the gateway, and a service can reverify a token at its trust boundary. Those are complementary because they protect different resources. Cross-ref the general layered trade-off in (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q5).","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.introducing-a-gateway-into-a-running-system.q1"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.introducing-a-gateway-into-a-running-system.q2","surface":"track","en":"How do you keep the gateway from becoming a single point of failure while migrating a running system?","bodyEn":"Run at least N+1 healthy gateway capacity across failure domains, with no route depending on one instance, zone, configuration store or identity endpoint. Health checks must test the gateway's ability to accept traffic, not synchronously fail every pod because a shared dependency is slow. Keep a last-known-good route snapshot so a control-plane outage does not erase the data plane. Treat routes, filters, policies and limits as code. Validate them in CI, render the effective configuration, canary it on a small route/tenant/cell, compare RED metrics and keep a rollback that does not require the failing control plane. Never roll out a gateway change and a backend contract change as one irreversible switch. Migrate route by route: shadow or mirror safe reads, then send a small percentage of real traffic, expand by zone, and only remove the old path after logs and authorization decisions agree. Preserve the original correlation id and error contract during the transition. At T1/T2 a pair across zones may be enough; at T3 multi-zone fleet and config validation are mandatory; at T4 each cell needs independent gateway capacity, policy distribution and rollback. The exact N comes from failure tests and warm-up time, not a universal replica count. Shadow/cutover contract: every route has a named owner and configuration hash. Shadow traffic is read-only or side-effect isolated; it never sends a second mutation to a real provider. During canary, compare route, authorization, status, latency, retry and error outcomes by route/tenant/cell. Roll back to the last-known-good hash before removing the old path, then keep a measured rollback window.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.introducing-a-gateway-into-a-running-system.q2"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.introducing-a-gateway-into-a-running-system.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do you operate the edge: trace propagation, correlation ids, RED metrics, 504 interpretation, warm-up and connection storms?","bodyEn":"Propagate W3C trace context across HTTP and gRPC, create one gateway span per route, and preserve a bounded correlation id for logs. Do not use raw user, tenant or URL values as metric labels. Emit RED metrics—request rate, error rate and duration—by route, status class, outcome and region; keep high-cardinality detail in sampled logs and traces. A gateway 504 says the gateway's deadline expired without a usable upstream response. The service may still be running, may have committed an effect, or may have been rejected before execution. Pair the metric with upstream connect time, response headers received, cancellation, retry count and an idempotency/reconciliation signal. Treat it as a symptom, not proof of rollback. After scale-out, cold JVMs, empty DNS/connection pools, JWKS refreshes and synchronized health checks can create a warm-up storm. Pre-warm instances, gradually open connection limits, stagger refreshes, and gate readiness on measured local warm-up without making liveness depend on the entire dependency graph. The operational owner must see route-level saturation before aggregate CPU. Use the autoscaling and gateway signals described in (25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q3), then rehearse a 504, IdP outage and config rollback.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.introducing-a-gateway-into-a-running-system.q3"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.internal-calls-sync-async-or-neither.q1","surface":"track","en":"What decision rule chooses synchronous or asynchronous work per hop, and when is REST or gRPC worth the change?","bodyEn":"Ask one question for every hop: does the caller need this answer to produce its own response or commit its next local decision? If yes, keep the hop synchronous, bound it with a deadline and make the failure visible. If no, persist a command or event and return a durable pending state; the consumer now owes delivery, deduplication and observability. REST is usually the cheaper boundary for public APIs, browser clients, heterogeneous teams and independently evolved contracts. gRPC is useful for controlled east-west calls where Protobuf contracts, generated clients, HTTP/2 multiplexing or streaming solve a measured problem. It does not remove timeout, authorization, compatibility or load-shedding work. Do not put an internal call through the external gateway merely to reuse a route. That adds public auth, edge limits, extra hops and a larger blast radius. Use service discovery or a mesh for east-west transport, and keep the service's authorization boundary intact. The protocol comparison is owned by (04-rest-grpc-webflux.protocols.q2). This item applies that choice to a gateway topology and preserves the one-decision-per-hop rule.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.internal-calls-sync-async-or-neither.q1"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.internal-calls-sync-async-or-neither.q2","surface":"track","en":"What do you gain and owe when moving a hop onto a queue, and why must east-west traffic bypass the edge gateway?","bodyEn":"A queue removes the producer from the consumer's immediate latency and failure path, smooths a burst and lets each side scale from its own signal. It also changes the user contract from a response to accepted/pending, and creates obligations: durable command identity and idempotent consumers; ordering only within the entity that needs it; bounded retries, expiry and a visible DLQ; backpressure, oldest-message age and consumer capacity; schema compatibility, audit and reconciliation for external effects. For a three-hop synchronous chain, approximate p99 as gateway overhead plus each hop's queueing, service and network tail. If each service has 40 ms p99 and there are 2 ms of gateway/transport overhead, the optimistic serial budget is about 122 ms before retries; independent tail correlation and connection waits can make it worse. Use a measured budget, not the sum of averages. East-west calls should use mesh or service discovery because the external gateway is optimized for north-south policy: public auth, tenant quotas, route exposure and edge protection. Sending service A through it to reach service B duplicates controls, consumes public capacity and lets an edge outage take down internal recovery. The sync/async failure semantics are expanded in (25-microservice.10-system-design-putting-it-all-together.q2); here the decisive boundary is the caller's need for an immediate answer.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.internal-calls-sync-async-or-neither.q2"},{"id":"27-api-gateway-identity-edge.internal-calls-sync-async-or-neither.q3","surface":"track","en":"How should an edge route large uploads and asynchronous jobs without hiding failure?","bodyEn":"The edge should enforce cheap, uniform controls: authenticated ownership, size and decompression limits, deadlines, rate/concurrency budgets, and clear 413 or 429 responses. For a large body, stream or redirect to an authorized upload session. Do not buffer and replay it through every gateway replica. For an asynchronous command, the edge must not return 202 merely because a downstream call was started. Before 202, the service must durably record the command or upload completion, return a stable operation identity, and expose a status or inquiry path. A gateway timeout after forwarding bytes is UNKNOWN until that path is checked. Do not add broad automatic retries around a non-replayable body or a mutation. The API resource owns completion, idempotency and reconciliation. The gateway owns hop budgets, observability, request hygiene and traffic protection. Internal workers should use service discovery or a private mesh, not route back through the public edge. This is the edge application of 15-network-i-o-models.web-protocols.q4 and 17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q8-q9; it does not duplicate their upload or job state machine. Durable async acceptance contract: Stage Required evidence Accept Command and idempotency identity are durable before 202 . Status A stable operation resource exposes pending, success and terminal failure. Delivery Consumer deduplicates duplicates and rejects or reconciles out-of-order messages. Recovery Retry/DLQ age, operator owner and reconciliation path are observable.","contextEn":"API Gateway & Identity Edge — control traffic before it enters the system","href":"/system-design/api-gateway-identity-edge#question-27-api-gateway-identity-edge.internal-calls-sync-async-or-neither.q3"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.the-protocol-before-the-product.q1","surface":"track","en":"What problem does a distributed lock solve, and what does it leave to the rest of the system?","bodyEn":"A distributed lock is a coordination primitive: for a named resource, it lets competing processes agree that one claimant may start a critical section. Before choosing a product, name four separate roles: Authority: grants ownership and, when needed, a new generation/fencing token. Lease: the authority's time-bounded recovery rule; expiry makes takeover possible. Worker: carries the current owner/term and must stop issuing new work after ownership loss. Resource: owns the business invariant and must enforce the conditional write or fencing check. A lease expiry changes who may acquire; it does not cancel an in-flight request or revoke a paused worker's already-issued write. It is useful for reducing duplicate schedulers, serialising a repair, or protecting a non-transactional resource for a bounded interval. It does not make a remote side effect atomic, does not roll back work after a process dies, and does not stop an old holder that keeps running after its lease expires. That last distinction is why a lock alone is not a correctness boundary. Question What a lock can answer What it cannot promise alone Coordination Who may enter now? That the winner will finish Failure When may another claimant try? That the old claimant has stopped Business invariant How to reduce concurrent attempts That money, inventory or a remote write is correct The rule that survives production Lock for coordination. Put correctness in the authority that owns the invariant: a database constraint, a conditional write, a version check, or a downstream system that enforces a fencing token. A lock is often a good admission control: it prevents ten replicas from starting the same expensive rebuild. A unique constraint or conditional update is a better correctness control: it rejects the eleventh write even if the network split the lock holders. If the protected resource cannot observe ownership, expiry and fencing, the lock service is only making a best effort. This is the same boundary as the distributed-transaction answer: keep the strong invariant local where possible, and make the asynchronous edge idempotent (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q5). Database lock/isolation mechanics belong in (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q1); local thread ordering and starvation belong in (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q3). What would change this answer — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it: The exact invariant protected: one row, one object, one scheduler role, one external API operation, or duplicate suppression only — and which component is the source of truth and can reject a stale owner generation. The p99 and maximum critical-section time, GC and pause tail, network delay, authority failover time and maximum acceptable takeover time. Whether fairness is required or throughput and availability matter more, and the maximum queue age or starvation window. Whether the deployment is one region or several, and which quorum, replication and partition model the chosen provider and version actually support. Whether every writer — scripts, admin tools, batch jobs, disaster-recovery paths — can use the same condition or lock; the policy for an unknown outcome after a timed-out acquire or protected write; and who may force-release or fence an owner, how that is authenticated, audited and tested. What falsifies the recommendation: stale tokens accepted by the resource, hold or pause tails beyond the renewal budget, or an authority failover that admits two owners.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.the-protocol-before-the-product.q1"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.the-protocol-before-the-product.q2","surface":"track","en":"How are safety, liveness and fairness different in a lock protocol?","bodyEn":"Treat these as separate review questions instead of calling every successful acquire safe. Property Meaning Typical design evidence Safety Two valid owners must not perform a protected action at the same time. unique ownership, compare-and-delete, fencing checked by the resource Liveness A dead owner must not block everyone forever. lease expiry, session death, bounded wait, retry policy Fairness A waiting claimant should not be starved indefinitely. queueing or ordered acquisition; not guaranteed by a basic key lock Observability The team can tell whether the protocol is healthy. wait, hold, renewal, expiry and rejection metrics A protocol can be live but unsafe: a short TTL frees a key quickly while a paused old process is still writing. It can be safe but unusable: a lock that never expires preserves mutual exclusion by blocking the whole system. Fairness is an additional promise; SET NX or an advisory lock does not automatically provide FIFO ordering. Do not collapse the properties into one word Ask which property the requirement needs, which failure breaks it, and which component enforces it. A client-side timeout is not proof of safety, and a successful lock acquisition is not proof that the resource accepted the work. For each lock, write the failure contract before choosing a library: Safety breach: two owners run. Is the side effect rejected, deduplicated, or merely logged? Liveness breach: nobody runs. Who detects it, and how long until recovery? Fairness breach: one tenant waits forever. Is that acceptable, or does work need a durable queue? Visibility breach: the lock is contended but no one knows. Which alert fires before users notice?","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.the-protocol-before-the-product.q2"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.the-protocol-before-the-product.q3","surface":"track","en":"What is the difference between a mutex, a lease and leader election?","bodyEn":"The words describe different ownership lifecycles, even though products often expose similar APIs. Primitive Ownership ends Good fit Main trap Mutex Explicit unlock, or a server-side cleanup rule Short local or transactional critical sections A crashed client can leave a permanent lock Lease Expiry unless renewed Work that must recover after a dead process The old process may continue after expiry Leader election Session/lease loss or resignation One active scheduler/controller with standby replicas Leadership is not permission to write arbitrary state Use a mutex when the holder can reliably reach a release point. Use a lease when recovery from crash matters and the work is restartable. Use leader election when one replica should make decisions for a role, not when every resource key needs a single global owner. A lease is a time-bounded claim maintained by the coordinator. It is not a promise from the process that it will stop executing at the deadline. If the protected resource needs strict ordering, add a monotonically increasing fencing token and make the resource reject stale tokens. Choose by ownership shape Per-resource exclusivity points to a lock. One active controller points to election. A stream of independent work points to a durable queue or database claim. Do not turn all three into one Redis key. Kubernetes uses Lease objects for node heartbeats and leader election; etcd elections attach leadership to a lease. That pattern is strong for selecting an active controller because the coordinator owns the session. It still needs idempotent controller actions and a resource-side guard when a stale controller can issue writes.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.the-protocol-before-the-product.q3"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.the-protocol-before-the-product.q4","surface":"track","en":"Why can a lease expire while its holder is still running?","bodyEn":"A process can stop making progress without stopping. Common causes include a long garbage-collection pause, CPU starvation, a frozen VM, a blocked event loop, a broken network path or a partition can prevent renewals. The coordinator observes a missed deadline and grants the lease to B. A then resumes with stale beliefs. T0 A acquires lease L, expires at T0 + 30s T1 A pauses for 35s; its thread has not been killed T2 coordinator expires L and grants it to B T3 B writes the resource T4 A resumes and writes with its old ownership belief A TTL removed deadlock, but it did not remove stale work. The safe response is not to make the TTL enormous. Keep the critical section bounded, renew with a margin, stop when renewal is lost, and make the resource reject stale fencing tokens. If no resource can enforce that token, the lock is coordination only. The lease clock is not the worker's authority A worker must not decide that an expired local timer gives it permission to continue. The coordinator and the protected resource define ownership; the worker only holds a revocable claim. Redlock and other lock algorithms still need an explicit failure model. Ask what happens during clock skew, process pause, coordinator failover and network partition instead of treating a successful client call as a proof of mutual exclusion. The existing fintech note reaches the same conclusion: a lock may reduce contention, but the database must arbitrate money correctness (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q6).","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.the-protocol-before-the-product.q4"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q1","surface":"track","en":"When should a database constraint or transaction beat a distributed lock?","bodyEn":"When the invariant already lives in one database, serialize and validate it there before adding another coordinator. A database can combine the read, decision and write with the same transaction and recovery model that owns the data. Think in two planes: a coordinator (Redis, etcd, ZooKeeper or leader election) can decide who may try, while the resource must decide whether the write is valid. A database conditional write can own both planes; a separate lock usually cannot. UPDATE inventory SET remaining = remaining - 1 WHERE sku = :sku AND remaining > 0; -- affected rows = 1: this transaction won -- affected rows = 0: sold out or another writer won Other strong choices include a unique constraint for one logical operation, an optimistic version predicate, or a row lock inside a short transaction. A Redis lock around the same database write may lower contention, but it cannot replace the constraint: failover, expiry or a bypassing client can still reach the database. Invariant Prefer Why One order id once Unique key + idempotent result The database rejects duplicates Stock never below zero Conditional decrement The predicate and write are one decision One row's state transition Transaction + version/row lock Recovery follows the data External non-transactional device Lock + fencing + device-side reject The device must reject stale owners Boundary test If a second client can write the authoritative store without consulting the lock, the lock cannot be the only correctness mechanism. Put the invariant where every writer must pass. A misplaced lock often signals a misplaced service boundary. If two pieces of money or inventory must change atomically, keeping them behind one local ACID boundary is usually cheaper and safer than coordinating two services (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q5). Authority-first preflight: Invariant: name the row, object, role or external operation that must not conflict. Authority: identify the component that can reject an invalid or stale write. Failure model: state pause, partition, failover and bypass behaviour. Outcome policy: define acceptable duplicate work, blocked work, UNKNOWN state and recovery owner. Choose a product only after these four fields are explicit.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q1"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q2","surface":"track","en":"What do FOR UPDATE, NOWAIT and SKIP LOCKED mean, and when is each safe?","bodyEn":"These are database concurrency choices, not interchangeable lock flavours. Clause Behaviour Use it for FOR UPDATE Wait for and hold write-intent row locks until commit or rollback. A short transaction that must inspect then update the same rows. NOWAIT Return an error immediately if a requested row is locked. Fail-fast UI/API paths with an explicit retry or conflict response. SKIP LOCKED Skip rows currently locked by another transaction. Queue-like work where any eligible job is acceptable. A worker claim can look like this: BEGIN; SELECT id FROM jobs WHERE status = 'READY' ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 1; UPDATE jobs SET status = 'RUNNING', claimed_by = :worker WHERE id = :id; COMMIT; SKIP LOCKED deliberately returns an inconsistent view. It is useful for a queue, but not for a report, balance calculation or general transaction that needs a complete snapshot. The database still owns the claim; the worker needs a timeout, retry and recovery state. Do not use SKIP LOCKED to hide contention Skipping a locked row is a scheduling policy, not a correctness proof. If every row is skipped, expose backlog and retry rather than silently reporting that no work exists. Locking semantics depend on the engine, index and isolation level. For example, InnoDB range scans can take gap/next-key locks, while a unique indexed lookup may lock a narrower record. Measure the actual query plan and lock graph; never infer the footprint from the SQL keyword alone (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q1).","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q2"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q3","surface":"track","en":"How should PostgreSQL advisory locks be used without creating invisible ownership?","bodyEn":"PostgreSQL advisory locks are application-defined locks. They are useful when the key is not a row in a table, but PostgreSQL does not enforce that every code path respects the same key. The application owns the meaning. Choose the lifetime deliberately: Transaction-level advisory locks release automatically at commit or rollback and fit short critical sections. Session-level advisory locks survive a transaction rollback and remain until explicit unlock or session termination. They can be useful for a long-lived role, but a leaked session can retain them. Inspect held locks through pg_locks ; remember the server has a finite lock memory budget. BEGIN; SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended(:resource, 0)); -- read, validate and write the protected database state COMMIT; -- the transaction-level lock is released here Use a stable, namespaced key derivation and document the owner. Never mix a session-level acquire with a transaction-level release by accident, and do not assume an advisory lock protects a resource that another service can change directly. Database lock, database authority Advisory locks are attractive when the database is already the authority. They are not a general replacement for a unique constraint or conditional update, and they do not coordinate a remote API that cannot see PostgreSQL. The official PostgreSQL model distinguishes session and transaction lifetimes and exposes both through pg_locks . That detail matters during connection-pool reuse: a session-level lock belongs to the physical session, not to the request that happened to borrow it.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q3"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q4","surface":"track","en":"What is the safe single-instance Redis lock pattern, and what does it still not solve?","bodyEn":"For a short coordination lease, generate a unique owner token, acquire atomically with one SET , and release with an atomic compare-and-delete. Never release by blindly deleting the key: a new owner may have acquired it after the old TTL expired. SET lock:invoice:42 owner-token NX PX 30000 -- release only if this client still owns the key if redis.call('get', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then return redis.call('del', KEYS[1]) end return 0 The unique token prevents an old client from deleting a newer owner's lock. The TTL gives liveness after a crash. Neither proves that the old client stopped before expiry, and a replication/failover design may have its own acknowledged-write and lock-state semantics. A critical external write needs fencing or an authoritative transactional store. Control Why it exists What it does not guarantee Unique token Release only your own acquisition That you still own it after TTL NX One successful acquire at the key Fairness or durable business state PX Recovery from a dead holder Stale-work prevention Compare-and-delete Safe release under races Atomicity with another database/API Redis is not a magic correctness layer Use this pattern for coordination where occasional duplicate work is harmless or the resource enforces a stronger guard. For money, inventory and irreversible side effects, keep the database/idempotency/fencing check on the correctness path. The Redis documentation describes this as a canonical starting point and also documents Redlock as a multi-instance algorithm. Choosing the multi-instance algorithm does not remove the need to state the failure model, bound the work, monitor renewal and protect the downstream resource from stale owners. Redis evidence boundary: SET NX PX with a random owner token and compare-and-delete is the single-instance coordination pattern. Its safety scope ends at the Redis execution context; it does not fence a database or external provider. Replication and failover determine whether an acknowledged lock state survives. The official Redis description and independent Redlock critiques answer different questions, so state the selected failure model and do not present either one as external-write fencing.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q4"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q5","surface":"track","en":"When do etcd, ZooKeeper or Kubernetes Lease fit better than a cache lock?","bodyEn":"Use a coordination system with an explicit session, lease and quorum model when the job is leadership or cluster coordination rather than cache admission. System Strong fit Design question etcd Lease-backed mutex and leader election in a quorum-backed control plane. What happens when the client session or keepalive is lost? ZooKeeper Ephemeral/sequential coordination recipes and ordered waiters. Can clients watch only their predecessor instead of waking a herd? Kubernetes Lease Controller leader election and heartbeats through the Kubernetes API. Is the Lease name scoped and collision-free for every deployment? Redis Short, low-latency coordination close to an existing cache. Is the weaker failure/failover contract acceptable? The authority must match the decision. A Kubernetes Lease can select one controller, but it does not automatically fence writes to a database. An etcd election can select a leader, but the leader still needs idempotent reconciliation after restart. Do not choose by brand Write down quorum, session loss, failover, watch/retry semantics, fencing, latency and operational ownership. A more sophisticated coordinator is not automatically safer if the protected resource ignores its ownership signal. Kubernetes recommends product/component-specific Lease names to avoid collisions. etcd elections bind leadership to a lease, so expiry or revocation transfers leadership. These are useful operational contracts, but the controller should still stop issuing work after losing its session and should reconcile state on the next leadership term.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.choose-the-authority-before-the-mechanism.q5"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q1","surface":"track","en":"What must a lock acquire, release and renewal contract specify?","bodyEn":"A lock API is incomplete when it only says acquire(key) . Make ownership and failure visible in the contract. Part Minimum contract Key Canonical, namespaced resource identity; no accidental tenant collision. Owner Unique acquisition token; never identify ownership only by hostname or thread. Wait Bounded wait and an explicit busy result; no infinite retry hidden in a request thread. Release Compare owner before deleting or resigning; release is safe to retry. Renewal Server/session keepalive, deadline and behaviour when renewal fails. Loss Stop, fence, or transition to UNKNOWN; never continue silently. The caller should know whether it acquired the lock, timed out, lost the lease, or completed the protected action. Do not flatten all four outcomes into a generic exception and let a retry run the action again. Make ownership a value Carry owner_token , lease_id and, when needed, fencing_token through logs, database writes and downstream calls. A lock that cannot be correlated is hard to debug and harder to prove safe. In a pooled application, the owner is the logical execution, not merely the Redis connection or database session. On retry, create a new acquisition token; never reuse a token from an attempt whose outcome is unknown.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q1"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q2","surface":"track","en":"How should TTL, renewal and critical-section length be chosen?","bodyEn":"Start with a bounded work model, not a round number copied from a blog. A lease must cover the maximum expected critical work plus process pauses, network delay, coordinator failover and a renewal margin. A p99 duration is useful for capacity, but it is not a safety proof for the worst pause. critical work p99 = 2.0s worst measured pause = 1.5s network/failover margin = 1.0s renewal margin = 1.5s initial TTL = at least 6.0s, then validate with failure tests Renew before the deadline, preferably through the coordinator's session/keepalive mechanism. If renewal fails, stop starting new side effects, mark the attempt lost, and let the resource-side guard decide whether an in-flight write is accepted. If the work cannot be bounded, checkpoint it into restartable chunks instead of stretching a lease indefinitely. Renewal is not ownership forever A successful renewal extends a lease only while the coordinator accepts it. Treat a timeout, lost session or failed heartbeat as ownership loss; do not keep renewing from a process that no longer has a valid session. Monitor the distribution of hold time and renewal latency, not just the configured TTL. Alert when p99 hold time approaches the renewal budget, when renewals fail, or when expired owners still emit writes. Those signals reveal a protocol that is already unsafe before a duplicate becomes a customer incident. TTL worksheet: record p99 and maximum hold time, pause tail, network delay, authority failover time, renewal margin, and maximum recovery time. Treat the configured TTL as a hypothesis to validate, not as a proof. Use a monotonic deadline for the worker, renew before the margin is consumed, and attach a generation/fencing check to every protected write. If the work cannot fit the bound, checkpoint it instead of stretching the lease.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q2"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q3","surface":"track","en":"How do fencing tokens stop a stale lease holder from writing?","bodyEn":"A fencing token is a monotonically increasing number issued with each successful ownership term. Lease expiry answers when a new term may be issued; fencing answers which write may commit. They are complementary, not interchangeable: the protected resource stores the greatest token it has accepted and rejects a write carrying an older token. -- resource-side check: the storage enforces the ordering UPDATE device_command SET payload = :payload, last_fence = :token WHERE device_id = :device AND :token > last_fence; -- affected rows = 1: accepted -- affected rows = 0: stale owner or unknown device The lock service issuing token 42 does not make the device safe by itself. The database, object store, device gateway or other side-effect owner must compare the token on every write. Otherwise a paused worker can resume after token 43 and still mutate state. Layer Responsibility Coordinator Serialise terms and issue increasing tokens. Worker Attach the current token to every protected operation. Resource Reject a token lower than the one already accepted. Recovery Reconcile rejected or unknown operations idempotently. The token must reach the authority A fencing token in a log line is not fencing. It becomes a safety mechanism only when the final writer checks it atomically with the state change. For resources that cannot store a token, use their own version/ETag/sequence precondition if available. If there is no conditional write at all, describe the lock as best-effort coordination and do not promise strict mutual exclusion to the business.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q3"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q4","surface":"track","en":"What should a worker do after it loses the lock in the middle of work?","bodyEn":"Stop treating the result as success. The worker should stop new writes, record the ownership-loss reason, and move the operation to a state that can be reconciled. Whether already-sent work happened is often UNKNOWN, not FAILED. A robust workflow separates the coordination attempt from the business state: Create or load an idempotency record. Acquire a lease and record the owner term/token. Perform a bounded, idempotent step with the token. On renewal failure, stop and mark the attempt UNKNOWN or RETRYABLE. Query the resource or consume its callback before deciding the next transition. Reconcile later; never run a blind compensating action just because the lock was lost. A lock loss is not a reason to refund, decrement again, or publish a second event. Idempotency keys, conditional state transitions and reconciliation close the gap (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1). Do not convert timeout into failure A network timeout says the caller lacks evidence. It does not say the remote side did nothing. Keep PENDING/UNKNOWN visible until a status query, callback or reconciliation source proves the outcome. For batch work, checkpoint after each durable unit and make the unit safe to repeat. For external APIs, reuse the provider idempotency key. For database work, let a transaction commit or roll back locally and let an outbox publish the next step; do not hold a distributed lock across a slow remote call unless the failure contract justifies it. Recovery runbook: detect the stuck holder, stop or drain the old worker, inspect the current generation, and force release only with authorization and an audit record. Replay or reconcile the work, then verify that the resource rejects the stale token.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.design-for-expiry-and-stale-work.q4"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.apply-operate-and-test-it.q1","surface":"track","en":"Which pattern should a scheduler, resource worker or controller use?","bodyEn":"Choose the pattern from the unit of work and the authority that can observe completion. Need Preferred shape Why One active scheduler/controller Leader election + lease + idempotent reconciliation Standby replicas can take over the role. One operation per resource key Short lock or database serialization + fencing when needed Only conflicting keys block each other. Many independent jobs Durable queue or database claim with retry state Work is visible, retryable and recoverable. One invariant in one database Local transaction, constraint or conditional write The authority decides correctness directly. A queue claim is not a universal lock: it transfers work ownership with visibility, acknowledgement and redelivery semantics. Duplicate delivery remains possible, so the job needs idempotency. Keep the coordination scope as small as the conflict scope: a global lock around a queue often creates a bottleneck and hides backlog a leader election around per-order work makes one replica unnecessarily responsible for everything Name the conflict set Say exactly which two operations cannot overlap: inventory:sku-42 , scheduler:settlement , or tenant:17:rebuild . If the key is just global , the design probably has not found its real invariant. The same decision applies to Spring jobs: multiple replicas need election or a lock to avoid duplicate scheduling, but the job itself should be restartable and idempotent. A lease is not a substitute for a durable work state (25-microservice.08-operational-concerns.q2). Mechanism map: Category Use when Safety owner Database transaction/constraint Invariant fits one database Database commit or constraint Optimistic conditional write Conflicts are versionable Resource version/generation Coordination lock Reduce overlapping attempts Resource still guards correctness Lease/election Role needs takeover Session plus reconciliation Durable work claim Independent jobs need retry Claim state and idempotent effect","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.apply-operate-and-test-it.q1"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.apply-operate-and-test-it.q2","surface":"track","en":"Why do idempotency and outbox still matter when a distributed lock is present?","bodyEn":"A lock serializes overlapping attempts; it does not remember whether a previous attempt completed, and it does not make a retry after timeout safe. A process can lose its lease after committing the business change but before publishing an event or returning a response. Use a durable idempotency record for the logical operation, and write the record/business change/outbox event in one local transaction where possible: BEGIN; INSERT INTO idempotency(key, status) VALUES (:key, 'IN_PROGRESS'); -- unique conflict means another attempt owns this logical operation UPDATE order_state SET status = 'PAID' WHERE order_id = :id; INSERT INTO outbox(event_key, payload) VALUES (:key, :event); COMMIT; -- publisher retries the outbox; consumer deduplicates event_key The lock may reduce duplicate pressure, but the unique key and state transition remain the guard. This is why a lock belongs around coordination while outbox/idempotency belong around durable meaning (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3). Never make the lock your only deduplication record If the lock key expires, is evicted, or is deleted during failover, a second attempt must still be rejected or made harmless by the authoritative state. Keep the lock out of a long remote transaction when possible: reserve local state, commit, publish via outbox, and let the consumer perform an idempotent transition. If a remote call returns UNKNOWN, reconcile by idempotency key rather than acquiring a new lock and sending a new command.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.apply-operate-and-test-it.q2"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.apply-operate-and-test-it.q3","surface":"track","en":"Which metrics and logs reveal a distributed-lock problem before users do?","bodyEn":"Instrument the protocol as a queue with ownership, not just as a boolean success flag. Acquire: attempts, successes, busy/timeouts, wait duration and retry count by resource class. Hold: critical-section duration, p50/p95/p99, maximum hold and key cardinality. Lease: renew success/failure, remaining TTL at renewal, session loss and expired leases. Safety: fencing-token rejects, stale-owner writes, duplicate state transitions and UNKNOWN outcomes. Operations: owner churn, hot keys, dead-letter/reconciliation backlog and recovery time after coordinator loss. Log a correlation id, resource key, owner token hash, lease id and fencing token where safe; never log credentials or the full token if it is a bearer secret. Keep metrics low-cardinality by grouping keys into resource classes rather than putting an unbounded user id in a label. Signal Interpretation First action Wait p99 rising Contention or work duration is growing. Find hot keys and shorten/partition the critical section. Renew failures Pause, network or coordinator health is degrading. Stop new side effects and inspect session loss. Fencing rejects Stale work reached the resource. Preserve evidence; fix the caller, not just the TTL. Expired work with no retry Liveness recovered but business progress did not. Run reconciliation and expose backlog. Alert on absence and rejection A lock can fail silently by never being renewed or by skipping every job. Alert on missing completion, fencing rejects and reconciliation age, not only on exceptions thrown by the lock client. Correlate lock metrics with GC pauses, CPU throttling, database lock waits, network errors and deploys. The lock is often the first place a hidden saturation problem becomes visible, not the root cause.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.apply-operate-and-test-it.q3"},{"id":"28-distributed-lock-lease.apply-operate-and-test-it.q4","surface":"track","en":"How do you test a distributed lock so the test proves more than two clients can take turns?","bodyEn":"Test the failure protocol, not only the happy-path mutual exclusion. First verify that conflicting keys serialize while unrelated keys proceed; then run this minimum matrix: Fault injection Required assertion Holder crash before release Claim recovers after the declared lease/session deadline; no permanent lock. Stale holder after pause/expiry New term can proceed and the old token/write is rejected. Clock skew or wall-clock jump Deadline handling is monotonic; a local clock change cannot authorize stale work. Renewal loss or late renewal Worker stops new effects; a late heartbeat cannot extend the old term. Coordinator failover/partition Documented no-two-owner guarantee holds, or the system explicitly downgrades and reconciles. Fencing and unknown write Token N is rejected after N+1 is accepted; retry/idempotency creates no second business result. Also crash after business commit but before outbox publish, exercise hot-key contention and a long database transaction, and verify replay/reconciliation. assert acquire(A, key) == GRANTED pause(A) beyond lease assert acquire(B, key) == GRANTED assert write(resource, tokenA) == REJECTED assert write(resource, tokenB) == ACCEPTED assert retry(A) does not create a second business result A useful review checklist Before approving a lock, ask: What is the authority? What happens after expiry? Who checks the fencing token? What is the bounded wait? Which state records UNKNOWN? What metric catches a stale write? How does the system recover after coordinator loss? Run these tests against the real coordination and database implementations where possible. An in-memory fake can prove caller branches, but it cannot prove Redis failover, PostgreSQL lock lifetime, etcd session loss or Kubernetes API behavior. Inject faults at the boundary that owns the guarantee. Where the neighbouring tests live: Java-side interruption, lock ordering and starvation: (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q3) database lock/isolation: (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q1) idempotency/outbox recovery: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3) Add explicit cases for late release, a renewal race, starvation, and two locks acquired in opposite order. Assert that the test either preserves the declared fairness/deadlock policy or exposes a bounded timeout and recovery path.","contextEn":"Distributed Lock & Lease — coordination without false certainty","href":"/topics/28-distributed-lock-lease#question-28-distributed-lock-lease.apply-operate-and-test-it.q4"},{"id":"topic:01-java-core-jvm","surface":"track","weight":40,"en":"Java Core & JVM internals","bodyEn":"The ground every senior Java backend stands on: memory model, GC, concurrency, and how the JVM actually executes code. This is where your depth shows — build on the recent concurrency deep-dive. JMM GC: G1/ZGC Virtual threads ConcurrentHashMap JIT Escape analysis java.time Clock & skew","contextEn":"Topic 1 · Java Core & JVM","href":"/topics/01-java-core-jvm"},{"id":"topic:02-java-8-25-java-vs-go","surface":"track","weight":40,"en":"Java 8 → 25 (LTS) evolution & Java vs Go","bodyEn":"A senior should be able to tell the story of where the language went over ten years, and place Java against Go as of 2025–2026, now that both have converged on lightweight concurrency. LTS cadence Records/Sealed Pattern matching Virtual threads Java 25 Java vs Go GraalVM","contextEn":"Topic 2 · Java 8→25 & Java vs Go","href":"/topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go"},{"id":"topic:03-spring-boot-deep-build","surface":"track","weight":40,"en":"Spring & Spring Boot in depth + Maven/Gradle","bodyEn":"Your daily bread, but at the mechanism level: IoC, transactions, auto-configuration, and managing dependencies properly in the pom/gradle — where framework users part ways with people who understand the framework. IoC/DI @Transactional Auto-configuration POM/BOM Maven vs Gradle Actuator AOP Bean lifecycle DispatcherServlet JSON contract Application events Spring Cache","contextEn":"Topic 3 · Spring Boot deep dive + build","href":"/topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build"},{"id":"topic:04-rest-grpc-webflux","surface":"track","weight":40,"en":"API protocols (REST/gRPC) & reactive programming (WebFlux)","bodyEn":"How services talk to each other and handle I/O at a high level. The focus: picking the right protocol, reactive versus virtual threads, and designing a clean API. REST idempotency gRPC/Protobuf HTTP/2 Mono/Flux Backpressure Reactive vs VT Versioning","contextEn":"Topic 4 · REST/gRPC & WebFlux","href":"/topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux"},{"id":"topic:05-db-core-index-lock","surface":"track","weight":40,"en":"Database core — index internals, transactions, locking","bodyEn":"Database day one. Down to the algorithm behind an index and the mechanics of InnoDB. The goal: explain what an index is and why it is fast all the way down to the data structure, and read an execution plan fluently. B+Tree algorithm Clustered index MVCC Next-key lock Deadlock EXPLAIN LSM-tree Schema design Constraints","contextEn":"Topic 5 · DB core: indexes & locking","href":"/topics/05-db-core-index-lock"},{"id":"topic:06-db-scaling","surface":"track","weight":40,"en":"Database scaling — replication, sharding, partitioning","bodyEn":"Database day two. From one node to many: replicate for reads, shard for writes, and the consistency trade-offs in between. Builds on your AWS RDS experience. Replication lag Read-after-write Sharding Consistent hashing Snowflake ID CAP/PACELC CDC","contextEn":"Topic 6 · DB scaling","href":"/topics/06-db-scaling"},{"id":"topic:07-sql-nosql-db-engines","surface":"track","weight":40,"en":"SQL vs NoSQL — Oracle / MySQL / PostgreSQL / MongoDB","bodyEn":"The whole database landscape: when SQL and when NoSQL, why enterprises stay on Oracle, and a direct Oracle–MySQL comparison from physical storage and data integrity through ACID and CAP. SQL vs NoSQL Oracle RAC/PLSQL PostgreSQL MongoDB/WiredTiger ACID vs BASE Oracle vs MySQL Data integrity CAP (C/A/P) Redis Search